[[:db/cas 17592186054396 :question/problem "Calculate $(-3)^3 \\div 6$." "Calculate $(-3)^3 \\operatorname{div}  6$."] [:db/cas 17592186054398 :question/problem "What is the value of $\\left( 5 \\div (-2)^2 \\right) \\times \\left( -\\dfrac{4}{5} \\right).$" "What is the value of $\\left( 5 \\operatorname{div}  (-2)^2 \\right) \\times \\left( -\\dfrac{4}{5} \\right).$"] [:db/cas 17592186054440 :question/worked-solution "The cardinality of a finite set $A$ is the number of elements in $A.$ \n\nNotice that the elements of our set \n\n$$\n3, \\quad 21, \\quad 147, \\quad 1\\,029,\\quad \\ldots\n$$\n\nform a geometric sequence with a common ratio $r=7{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =7 \\\\  & =\\frac{21}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{147}{21} \\\\  & =⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $n$th term of our geometric sequence, denoted $a_n,$ is given by\n\n$$\na_n = a_1 r^{n-1} = 3(7^{n-1}) = \\dfrac37(7)^n.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express $A$ in set-builder notation as follows:\n\n$$\nA = \\left\\{\\dfrac37(7)^n : n\\in \\mathbb N,\\: 1\\leq n \\leq N \\right\\}\n$$\n\nwhere $N\\in \\mathbb N$ is to be determined.\n\nTo find $N,$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{7}(7)^{N} & =5\\,931\\,980\\,229 \\\\ {7}^{N} & =13\\,841\\,287\\,201 \\\\ N & ={log}_{7}⁡(13\\,841\\,287\\,201) \\\\ N & =\\frac{{log}_{10}⁡(13\\,841\\,287\\,201)}{{log}_{10}⁡(7)} \\\\ N & =12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our set $A$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\nA = \\left\\{\\dfrac37(7)^n : n\\in \\mathbb N,\\: 1\\leq n \\leq 12\\right\\}.\n$$\n\nFinally, since $A$ has $12$ elements, we must have $|A| = 12.$" "The cardinality of a finite set $A$ is the number of elements in $A.$ \n\nNotice that the elements of our set \n\n$$\n3, \\quad 21, \\quad 147, \\quad 1\\,029,\\quad \\ldots\n$$\n\nform a geometric sequence with a common ratio $r=7{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =7 \\\\  & =\\frac{21}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{147}{21} \\\\  & =⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $n$th term of our geometric sequence, denoted $a_n,$ is given by\n\n$$\na_n = a_1 r^{n-1} = 3(7^{n-1}) = \\dfrac37(7)^n.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express $A$ in set-builder notation as follows:\n\n$$\nA = \\left\\{\\dfrac37(7)^n : n\\in \\mathbb N,\\: 1\\leq n \\leq N \\right\\}\n$$\n\nwhere $N\\in \\mathbb N$ is to be determined.\n\nTo find $N,$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{7}(7)^{N} & =5\\,931\\,980\\,229 \\\\ {7}^{N} & =13\\,841\\,287\\,201 \\\\ N & ={\\log }_{7}⁡(13\\,841\\,287\\,201) \\\\ N & =\\frac{{\\log }_{10}⁡(13\\,841\\,287\\,201)}{{\\log }_{10}⁡(7)} \\\\ N & =12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our set $A$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\nA = \\left\\{\\dfrac37(7)^n : n\\in \\mathbb N,\\: 1\\leq n \\leq 12\\right\\}.\n$$\n\nFinally, since $A$ has $12$ elements, we must have $|A| = 12.$"] [:db/cas 17592186054526 :question/worked-solution "To find the value of $\\textrm{i}$ to some positive integer power $n$, we divide $n$ by $4$ and raise $\\textrm{i}$ to the remainder.\n\nIn our case, we have $n=15.$ Dividing the power by $4,$ we get\n\n$$\n15\\div 4 = 3\\,\\textrm{R} \\color{blue}{3}.\n$$\n\nSo the remainder is $\\color{blue}3.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\textrm{i}^{15} = \\textrm{i}^{\\color{blue}{3}} = -\\textrm{i}.\n$$" "To find the value of $\\textrm{i}$ to some positive integer power $n$, we divide $n$ by $4$ and raise $\\textrm{i}$ to the remainder.\n\nIn our case, we have $n=15.$ Dividing the power by $4,$ we get\n\n$$\n15\\operatorname{div}  4 = 3\\,\\textrm{R} \\color{blue}{3}.\n$$\n\nSo the remainder is $\\color{blue}3.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\textrm{i}^{15} = \\textrm{i}^{\\color{blue}{3}} = -\\textrm{i}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186054527 :question/worked-solution "Using the addition law for exponents, we have\n\n$$\nab =\\textrm{i}^9\\cdot\\textrm{i}^7 = \\textrm{i}^{9+7} = \\textrm{i}^{16}.\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\textrm{i}$ raised to some positive integer power $n,$ we divide $n$ by $4$ and raise $\\textrm{i}$ to the remainder.\n\nIn our case, we have $n=16.$ Dividing the power by $4,$ we get\n\n$$\n16\\div 4 = 4\\,\\textrm{R} \\color{blue}{0}.\n$$\n\nSo the remainder is $\\color{blue}0.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\textrm{i}^{16} = \\textrm{i}^{\\color{blue}{0}} =1.\n$$" "Using the addition law for exponents, we have\n\n$$\nab =\\textrm{i}^9\\cdot\\textrm{i}^7 = \\textrm{i}^{9+7} = \\textrm{i}^{16}.\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\textrm{i}$ raised to some positive integer power $n,$ we divide $n$ by $4$ and raise $\\textrm{i}$ to the remainder.\n\nIn our case, we have $n=16.$ Dividing the power by $4,$ we get\n\n$$\n16\\operatorname{div}  4 = 4\\,\\textrm{R} \\color{blue}{0}.\n$$\n\nSo the remainder is $\\color{blue}0.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\textrm{i}^{16} = \\textrm{i}^{\\color{blue}{0}} =1.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186054540 :question/worked-solution "We use the change of base formula:\n\n$$\n\\log_a X = \\dfrac {\\log_b X} {\\log_b a}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $a=16$ and $X =32.$ Since both $a$ and $X$ are powers of $2,$ we set $b=2.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{16}⁡(32) & =\\frac{{log}_{2}⁡(32)}{{log}_{2}⁡(16)} \\\\  & =\\frac{{log}_{2}⁡({2}^{5})}{{log}_{2}⁡({2}^{4})} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We use the change of base formula:\n\n$$\n\\log_a X = \\dfrac {\\log_b X} {\\log_b a}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $a=16$ and $X =32.$ Since both $a$ and $X$ are powers of $2,$ we set $b=2.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\log }_{16}⁡(32) & =\\frac{{\\log }_{2}⁡(32)}{{\\log }_{2}⁡(16)} \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\log }_{2}⁡({2}^{5})}{{\\log }_{2}⁡({2}^{4})} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186054541 :question/worked-solution "The change of base formula for logarithms is\n\n$$\n\\log_a X = \\dfrac {\\log_b X} {\\log_b a}.\n$$\n\nThe change of base formula works with any new base. In this example, the choices are written using base $2,$ so we rewrite $\\log x$ in terms of $\\log_2.$\nThis gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{log}⁡x & ={log}_{10}⁡x \\\\  & =\\frac{{log}_{2}⁡x}{{log}_{2}⁡10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" "The change of base formula for logarithms is\n\n$$\n\\log_a X = \\dfrac {\\log_b X} {\\log_b a}.\n$$\n\nThe change of base formula works with any new base. In this example, the choices are written using base $2,$ so we rewrite $\\log x$ in terms of $\\log_2.$\nThis gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{log}⁡x & ={\\log }_{10}⁡x \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\log }_{2}⁡x}{{\\log }_{2}⁡10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I only.\""] [:db/cas 17592186054543 :question/worked-solution "Let's calculate $\\log_3 (7)$ by changing the base $3$ to the common logarithm (base $10$).\n\nWe use the change of base formula:\n\n$$\n\\log_a X = \\dfrac {\\log_b X} {\\log_b a}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $a=3,$ $X =7,$ and $b=10.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{3}⁡(7) & =\\frac{{log}_{10}⁡(7)}{{log}_{10}⁡(3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(7)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(3)} \\\\  & ≈\\frac{0.845\\,10}{0.477\\,12} \\\\  & ≈1.77.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let's calculate $\\log_3 (7)$ by changing the base $3$ to the common logarithm (base $10$).\n\nWe use the change of base formula:\n\n$$\n\\log_a X = \\dfrac {\\log_b X} {\\log_b a}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $a=3,$ $X =7,$ and $b=10.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\log }_{3}⁡(7) & =\\frac{{\\log }_{10}⁡(7)}{{\\log }_{10}⁡(3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(7)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(3)} \\\\  & ≈\\frac{0.845\\,10}{0.477\\,12} \\\\  & ≈1.77.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186055040 :question/problem "$6 \\div 3 + (-3)^{3} =$ {{field-1}}" "$6 \\operatorname{div}  3 + (-3)^{3} =$ {{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186055042 :question/problem "$(4 \\div (-2) + 5)^{4} =$ {{field-1}}" "$(4 \\operatorname{div}  (-2) + 5)^{4} =$ {{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186055074 :question/worked-solution "The cardinality of a finite set $A$ is the number of elements in $A.$ \n\nNotice that the elements of our set \n\n$$\n3,\\,6,\\,12,\\,24,\\,…\n$$\n\nform a geometric sequence with a common ratio $r=2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =2 \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{12}{6} \\\\  & =⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $n$th term of our geometric sequence, denoted ${a}_{n},$ is given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={a}_{1}{r}^{n-1}=3({2}^{n-1})=\\frac{3}{2}(2)^{n}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express $A$ in set-builder notation as follows:\n\n$$\nA={\\frac{3}{2}(2)^{n}:n\\in N,\\,1\\le n\\le N}\n$$\n\nwhere $N\\in N$ is to be determined.\n\nTo find $N,$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2}(2)^{N} & =98\\,304 \\\\ {2}^{N} & =65\\,536 \\\\ N & ={log}_{2}⁡(65\\,536) \\\\ N & =\\frac{{log}_{10}⁡(65\\,536)}{{log}_{10}⁡(2)} \\\\ N & =16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our set $A$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\nA={\\frac{3}{2}(2)^{n}:n\\in N,\\,1\\le n\\le 16}.\n$$\n\nFinally, since $A$ has $16$ elements, we must have $|A|=16.$" "The cardinality of a finite set $A$ is the number of elements in $A.$ \n\nNotice that the elements of our set \n\n$$\n3,\\,6,\\,12,\\,24,\\,…\n$$\n\nform a geometric sequence with a common ratio $r=2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =2 \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{12}{6} \\\\  & =⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $n$th term of our geometric sequence, denoted ${a}_{n},$ is given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={a}_{1}{r}^{n-1}=3({2}^{n-1})=\\frac{3}{2}(2)^{n}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express $A$ in set-builder notation as follows:\n\n$$\nA={\\frac{3}{2}(2)^{n}:n\\in N,\\,1\\le n\\le N}\n$$\n\nwhere $N\\in N$ is to be determined.\n\nTo find $N,$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2}(2)^{N} & =98\\,304 \\\\ {2}^{N} & =65\\,536 \\\\ N & ={\\log }_{2}⁡(65\\,536) \\\\ N & =\\frac{{\\log }_{10}⁡(65\\,536)}{{\\log }_{10}⁡(2)} \\\\ N & =16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our set $A$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\nA={\\frac{3}{2}(2)^{n}:n\\in N,\\,1\\le n\\le 16}.\n$$\n\nFinally, since $A$ has $16$ elements, we must have $|A|=16.$"] [:db/cas 17592186055091 :question/problem "$((-4)^{2} + 5 \\cdot 2) - ((-2)^{3} \\div (-4)) =$ {{field-1}}" "$((-4)^{2} + 5 \\cdot 2) - ((-2)^{3} \\operatorname{div}  (-4)) =$ {{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186055183 :question/problem "Expressed as a fraction in its simplest form, ${log}_{16}⁡(64)$ equals{{field-1}}." "Expressed as a fraction in its simplest form, ${\\log }_{16}⁡(64)$ equals{{field-1}}."] [:db/cas 17592186055183 :question/worked-solution "We use the change of base formula:\n\n$$\n{log}_{a}⁡X=\\frac{{log}_{b}⁡X}{{log}_{b}⁡a}\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=16$ and $X=64.$ Since both $a$ and $X$ are powers of $2,$ we set $b=2.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{16}⁡(64) & =\\frac{{log}_{2}⁡(64)}{{log}_{2}⁡(16)} \\\\  & =\\frac{{log}_{2}⁡({2}^{6})}{{log}_{2}⁡({2}^{4})} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We use the change of base formula:\n\n$$\n{\\log }_{a}⁡X=\\frac{{\\log }_{b}⁡X}{{\\log }_{b}⁡a}\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=16$ and $X=64.$ Since both $a$ and $X$ are powers of $2,$ we set $b=2.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\log }_{16}⁡(64) & =\\frac{{\\log }_{2}⁡(64)}{{\\log }_{2}⁡(16)} \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\log }_{2}⁡({2}^{6})}{{\\log }_{2}⁡({2}^{4})} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186055462 :question/problem "${log}_{36}⁡(\\sqrt[3]{6})=$" "${\\log }_{36}⁡(\\sqrt[3]{6})=$"] [:db/cas 17592186055462 :question/worked-solution "We use the change of base formula:\n\n$$\n{log}_{a}⁡X=\\frac{{log}_{b}⁡X}{{log}_{b}⁡a}\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=36$ and $X=\\sqrt[3]{6}.$ Since both $a$ and $X$ can be presented as a power of $6,$ we set $b=6.$ So, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{36}⁡(\\sqrt[3]{6}) & =\\frac{{log}_{6}⁡(\\sqrt[3]{6})}{{log}_{6}⁡(36)} \\\\  & =\\frac{{log}_{6}⁡({6}^{1/3})}{{log}_{6}⁡({6}^{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We use the change of base formula:\n\n$$\n{\\log }_{a}⁡X=\\frac{{\\log }_{b}⁡X}{{\\log }_{b}⁡a}\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=36$ and $X=\\sqrt[3]{6}.$ Since both $a$ and $X$ can be presented as a power of $6,$ we set $b=6.$ So, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\log }_{36}⁡(\\sqrt[3]{6}) & =\\frac{{\\log }_{6}⁡(\\sqrt[3]{6})}{{\\log }_{6}⁡(36)} \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\log }_{6}⁡({6}^{1/3})}{{\\log }_{6}⁡({6}^{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186055516 :question/problem "Calculate ${log}_{9}⁡(27)$ without using a calculator." "Calculate ${\\log }_{9}⁡(27)$ without using a calculator."] [:db/cas 17592186055516 :question/worked-solution "We use the change of base formula:\n\n$$\n{log}_{a}⁡X=\\frac{{log}_{b}⁡X}{{log}_{b}⁡a}\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=9,$ and $X=27.$ Since both $a$ and $X$ are powers of $3,$ we set $b=3.$ So, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{9}⁡(27) & =\\frac{{log}_{3}⁡(27)}{{log}_{3}⁡(9)} \\\\  & =\\frac{{log}_{3}⁡({3}^{3})}{{log}_{3}⁡({3}^{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We use the change of base formula:\n\n$$\n{\\log }_{a}⁡X=\\frac{{\\log }_{b}⁡X}{{\\log }_{b}⁡a}\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=9,$ and $X=27.$ Since both $a$ and $X$ are powers of $3,$ we set $b=3.$ So, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\log }_{9}⁡(27) & =\\frac{{\\log }_{3}⁡(27)}{{\\log }_{3}⁡(9)} \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\log }_{3}⁡({3}^{3})}{{\\log }_{3}⁡({3}^{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186055970 :tutorial/content "Suppose we wish to evaluate $\\log_{4}9,$ but our calculator only has a key for the common logarithm $\\log$ (base $10$).\n\nIn such cases, we can use the **change of base formula**, which states that\n\n$$\n\\log_a X = \\dfrac {\\log_b X} {\\log_b a}.\n$$\n\nIt effectively allows us to change a logarithm from one base, $a,$ to another, $b.$ Note that $X,$ $a,$ and $b$ have to be all greater than zero and that $a \\ne 1,$ $b \\ne 1.$ \n\nWe usually choose the value of base $b$ that makes the problem easier. In particular, if we need to evaluate the expression using a calculator, we choose $b=10$ or $b=e$. \n\nFor example, to evaluate $\\log_{4}9$ using this formula, we can rewrite the logarithm from base $a=4$ to base $b=10.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{4}⁡9 & =\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡9}{\\operatorname{log}⁡4} \\\\  & ≈\\frac{0.954\\,242}{0.602\\,059} \\\\  & =1.585\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." "Suppose we wish to evaluate $\\log_{4}9,$ but our calculator only has a key for the common logarithm $\\log$ (base $10$).\n\nIn such cases, we can use the **change of base formula**, which states that\n\n$$\n\\log_a X = \\dfrac {\\log_b X} {\\log_b a}.\n$$\n\nIt effectively allows us to change a logarithm from one base, $a,$ to another, $b.$ Note that $X,$ $a,$ and $b$ have to be all greater than zero and that $a \\ne 1,$ $b \\ne 1.$ \n\nWe usually choose the value of base $b$ that makes the problem easier. In particular, if we need to evaluate the expression using a calculator, we choose $b=10$ or $b=e$. \n\nFor example, to evaluate $\\log_{4}9$ using this formula, we can rewrite the logarithm from base $a=4$ to base $b=10.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\log }_{4}⁡9 & =\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡9}{\\operatorname{log}⁡4} \\\\  & ≈\\frac{0.954\\,242}{0.602\\,059} \\\\  & =1.585\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."] [:db/cas 17592186056032 :tutorial/content "A **variable** is any letter or symbol that can be replaced by a number. For example, the letters\n\n$$\nx, \\quad y, \\quad z, \\quad a, \\quad b, \\quad c, \\quad k, \\quad m,\\quad n,\\quad p, \\quad q,\\quad r, \\quad \\ldots\n$$\n\nare commonly used as variables. But in general, we can use any letter we like.\n\nAn **algebraic expression** is a combination of variables with mathematical operations, like addition $(+)$, subtraction $(-)$, multiplication $(\\times)$, and division $(\\div).$ So \n\n$$\nx+2,\\qquad 6-y,\\qquad 2\\times p + 1,\\qquad q\\div r,\n$$\n\nare all examples of algebraic expressions." "A **variable** is any letter or symbol that can be replaced by a number. For example, the letters\n\n$$\nx, \\quad y, \\quad z, \\quad a, \\quad b, \\quad c, \\quad k, \\quad m,\\quad n,\\quad p, \\quad q,\\quad r, \\quad \\ldots\n$$\n\nare commonly used as variables. But in general, we can use any letter we like.\n\nAn **algebraic expression** is a combination of variables with mathematical operations, like addition $(+)$, subtraction $(-)$, multiplication $(\\times)$, and division $(\\operatorname{div} ).$ So \n\n$$\nx+2,\\qquad 6-y,\\qquad 2\\times p + 1,\\qquad q\\operatorname{div}  r,\n$$\n\nare all examples of algebraic expressions."] [:db/cas 17592186056074 :tutorial/content "Before we continue, we need a brief discussion on the concept of **integer divisibility.**\n\nSuppose $a$ and $b$ are integers. We write $a  \\mid b$ to mean that $\\boldsymbol a$ **divides** $\\boldsymbol b.$ Informally, this means the quotient of $\\dfrac b a$ is an *integer*, and there is *no remainder*. \n\nFor example, consider the following statement:\n\n$$\n5 \\,|\\, 20\n$$\n\nThis statement reads, \"$5$ divides $20$\". Moreover, this is a *true* statement because $20\\div 5 = 4$ is an integer. \n\nNow consider the following statement:\n\n$$\n6\\,  |\\, 20\n$$\n\nThis statement is *false* because $20\\div 6 = 3 {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac13}},$ which is *not* an integer. The correct statement is\n\n$$\n6 \\! \\not | \\: 20.\n$$\n\nThis statement reads, \"$6$ does not divide $20$\".\n\nFor now, this is all we need to understand the notation $a\\mid b.$ More formal discussions on integer divisibility will be given in future lessons." "Before we continue, we need a brief discussion on the concept of **integer divisibility.**\n\nSuppose $a$ and $b$ are integers. We write $a  \\mid b$ to mean that $\\boldsymbol a$ **divides** $\\boldsymbol b.$ Informally, this means the quotient of $\\dfrac b a$ is an *integer*, and there is *no remainder*. \n\nFor example, consider the following statement:\n\n$$\n5 \\,|\\, 20\n$$\n\nThis statement reads, \"$5$ divides $20$\". Moreover, this is a *true* statement because $20\\operatorname{div}  5 = 4$ is an integer. \n\nNow consider the following statement:\n\n$$\n6\\,  |\\, 20\n$$\n\nThis statement is *false* because $20\\operatorname{div}  6 = 3 {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac13}},$ which is *not* an integer. The correct statement is\n\n$$\n6 \\! \\not | \\: 20.\n$$\n\nThis statement reads, \"$6$ does not divide $20$\".\n\nFor now, this is all we need to understand the notation $a\\mid b.$ More formal discussions on integer divisibility will be given in future lessons."] [:db/cas 17592186059432 :question/worked-solution "We start by using the definition of modular congruence. Recall that\n\n$$\na \\equiv b \\: (\\textrm{mod} \\: n) \\quad\\Leftrightarrow\\quad n \\mid (a-b) \\quad\\Leftrightarrow\\quad a-b = nk\n$$\n\nfor some integer $k$.\n\nIn this case, we need to show the following:\n\n$$\n2a \\equiv 2b \\: (\\textrm{mod} \\: 14) \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad a \\equiv b \\: (\\textrm{mod} \\: 7)\n$$\n\nSo, we can reason as follows:\n\n> By the definition of modular congruence,\n> $2a≡2b(mod14) \\mid ⇔14∣(2a - 2b); ⇔14∣2(a - b)$\n\nNext, we use the definition of divisibility:\n\n> By the definition of divisibility in $\\mathbb{Z}$, $14 \\mid 2(a-b)$ means that there exists an integer $k$ such that\n> $2(a-b) = 14k$.\n\nNow, we can cancel out the factor of $2{:}$\n\n> Dividing this equation by $2$, we obtain\n> $a-b = 7k$,\n> which in turn implies\n> $7 \\mid (a-b)$.\n\nFinally, we use the definition of modular congruence again:\n\n> Therefore, according to the definition of modular congruence, we have\n> $a \\equiv b \\: (\\textrm{mod} \\: 7)$." "We start by using the definition of modular congruence. Recall that\n\n$$\na \\equiv b \\: (\\textrm{mod} \\: n) \\quad\\Leftrightarrow\\quad n \\mid (a-b) \\quad\\Leftrightarrow\\quad a-b = nk\n$$\n\nfor some integer $k$.\n\nIn this case, we need to show the following:\n\n$$\n2a \\equiv 2b \\: (\\textrm{mod} \\: 14) \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad a \\equiv b \\: (\\textrm{mod} \\: 7)\n$$\n\nSo, we can reason as follows:\n\n> By the definition of modular congruence,\n> $2a≡2b(\\bmod 14) \\mid ⇔14∣(2a - 2b); ⇔14∣2(a - b)$\n\nNext, we use the definition of divisibility:\n\n> By the definition of divisibility in $\\mathbb{Z}$, $14 \\mid 2(a-b)$ means that there exists an integer $k$ such that\n> $2(a-b) = 14k$.\n\nNow, we can cancel out the factor of $2{:}$\n\n> Dividing this equation by $2$, we obtain\n> $a-b = 7k$,\n> which in turn implies\n> $7 \\mid (a-b)$.\n\nFinally, we use the definition of modular congruence again:\n\n> Therefore, according to the definition of modular congruence, we have\n> $a \\equiv b \\: (\\textrm{mod} \\: 7)$."] [:db/cas 17592186059439 :question/worked-solution "To subtract mixed numbers with like denominators, we subtract\n\n- whole numbers from whole numbers, and\n- fractions from fractions.\n\nWith that in mind, let's subtract our mixed numbers.\n\n- Subtracting the whole numbers, we get\n$8 - 4 = {\\color{red}{4}}$.\n- Subtracting the fractions, we get\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac{7}{9} - \\dfrac{4}{9} = \\dfrac{7 - 4}{9} = \\dfrac{3}{9}.\n$$\nWe can simplify this fraction by dividing the numerator and denominator by $3{:}$\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac{3}{9} = \\dfrac{3 \\div 3}{9 \\div 3} = {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{1}{3}}}\n$$\n\nFinally, we combine the resulting whole number and fraction to create a mixed number:\n\n$$\n8 \\,\\dfrac{7}{9} - 4 \\,\\dfrac{4}{9} = {\\color{red}{4}}\\,{\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{1}{3}}}\n$$" "To subtract mixed numbers with like denominators, we subtract\n\n- whole numbers from whole numbers, and\n- fractions from fractions.\n\nWith that in mind, let's subtract our mixed numbers.\n\n- Subtracting the whole numbers, we get\n$8 - 4 = {\\color{red}{4}}$.\n- Subtracting the fractions, we get\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac{7}{9} - \\dfrac{4}{9} = \\dfrac{7 - 4}{9} = \\dfrac{3}{9}.\n$$\nWe can simplify this fraction by dividing the numerator and denominator by $3{:}$\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac{3}{9} = \\dfrac{3 \\operatorname{div}  3}{9 \\operatorname{div}  3} = {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{1}{3}}}\n$$\n\nFinally, we combine the resulting whole number and fraction to create a mixed number:\n\n$$\n8 \\,\\dfrac{7}{9} - 4 \\,\\dfrac{4}{9} = {\\color{red}{4}}\\,{\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{1}{3}}}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186059453 :question/worked-solution "Remember that a square matrix is singular if its determinant is equal to $0.$\n\nUsing the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 4 & 7\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 7\\end{vmatrix}-2\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 7\\end{vmatrix}+3\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1(0-4)-2(7-3)+3(4-0) \\\\  & =-4-2\\cdot 4+3\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =-4-8+12 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\det(A)=0$, the matrix is singular. \n\nTherefore, only statements I and III are true." "Remember that a square matrix is singular if its determinant is equal to $0.$\n\nUsing the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\det (A) & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 4 & 7\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 7\\end{vmatrix}-2\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 7\\end{vmatrix}+3\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1(0-4)-2(7-3)+3(4-0) \\\\  & =-4-2\\cdot 4+3\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =-4-8+12 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\det(A)=0$, the matrix is singular. \n\nTherefore, only statements I and III are true."] [:db/cas 17592186059469 :question/worked-solution "First, we factor $\\sin^2\\theta$ from the expression:\n\n$$\n\\sin^4{\\theta}+\\sin^2{\\theta}\\cos^2{\\theta} = \\sin^2{\\theta}\\cdot(\\sin^2{\\theta}+\\cos^2{\\theta})\n$$\n\nThen, we use $\\cos^2\\theta + \\sin^2\\theta = 1$ to simplify the expression in the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\cdot ({sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}) & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\cdot 1={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\sin^4{\\theta}+\\sin^2{\\theta}\\cos^2{\\theta} = \\sin^2\\theta.\n$$" "First, we factor $\\sin^2\\theta$ from the expression:\n\n$$\n\\sin^4{\\theta}+\\sin^2{\\theta}\\cos^2{\\theta} = \\sin^2{\\theta}\\cdot(\\sin^2{\\theta}+\\cos^2{\\theta})\n$$\n\nThen, we use $\\cos^2\\theta + \\sin^2\\theta = 1$ to simplify the expression in the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta \\cdot ({\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}) & ={\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta \\cdot 1={\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\sin^4{\\theta}+\\sin^2{\\theta}\\cos^2{\\theta} = \\sin^2\\theta.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186059470 :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the numerator:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\cos \\theta \\sin^2 \\theta + \\cos^3 \\theta} {\\sin \\theta}\t=\t\\dfrac {\\cos \\theta \\cdot (\\sin^2 \\theta + \\cos^2 \\theta)} {\\sin \\theta}\n$$\n\nNext, we use the Pythagorean identity to rewrite the expression in the parentheses, and then we simplify the resulting expression as much as possible.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot ({sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta )}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot (1)}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\cos \\theta \\sin^2 \\theta + \\cos^3 \\theta} {\\sin \\theta} = \\cot \\theta.\n$$" "First, we factor the numerator:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\cos \\theta \\sin^2 \\theta + \\cos^3 \\theta} {\\sin \\theta}\t=\t\\dfrac {\\cos \\theta \\cdot (\\sin^2 \\theta + \\cos^2 \\theta)} {\\sin \\theta}\n$$\n\nNext, we use the Pythagorean identity to rewrite the expression in the parentheses, and then we simplify the resulting expression as much as possible.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot ({\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta +{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta )}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot (1)}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\cos \\theta \\sin^2 \\theta + \\cos^3 \\theta} {\\sin \\theta} = \\cot \\theta.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186059471 :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the second term using the identity $\\cot \\theta = \\dfrac{\\cos\\theta}{\\sin\\theta},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\sin{\\theta}}{1+\\cos{\\theta}}+\\dfrac{\\cos{\\theta}}{\\sin{\\theta}}\n$$\n\nNext, we put both terms over a common denominator, and combine the two fractions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}+\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot (1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )} &= \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot (1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )} &= \\frac{({sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta )+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )} \\\\ &= \\frac{1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )} \\\\ &= \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } \\\\ &= \\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\sin{\\theta}}{1+\\cos{\\theta}}+\\cot{\\theta} = \\csc\\theta.\n$$" "First, we rewrite the second term using the identity $\\cot \\theta = \\dfrac{\\cos\\theta}{\\sin\\theta},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\sin{\\theta}}{1+\\cos{\\theta}}+\\dfrac{\\cos{\\theta}}{\\sin{\\theta}}\n$$\n\nNext, we put both terms over a common denominator, and combine the two fractions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}+\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot (1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )} &= \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot (1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )} &= \\frac{({\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta +{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta )+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )} \\\\ &= \\frac{1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )} \\\\ &= \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } \\\\ &= \\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\sin{\\theta}}{1+\\cos{\\theta}}+\\cot{\\theta} = \\csc\\theta.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186059475 :question/worked-solution "First, let's interpret the fraction as a division:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\cot \\theta + \\csc \\theta} {\\cos \\theta + 1} = \\left(\\cot \\theta + \\csc \\theta\\right)\\div \\left(\\cos \\theta + 1\\right)\n$$\n\nThen, we recall the identities\n\n$$\n\\cot \\theta = \\dfrac {\\cos \\theta} {\\sin \\theta}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\csc\\theta = \\dfrac{1}{\\sin\\theta}\n$$\n\nand we substitute them into the above expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(\\cot θ + \\csc θ) \\div (\\cos θ + 1) &= (\\frac{\\cos θ}{\\sin θ} + \\frac{1}{\\sin θ}) \\div (\\cos θ + 1) \\\\\n&= \\left(\\frac{\\cos θ + 1}{\\sin θ}\\right) \\div (\\cos θ + 1)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we turn the division into multiplication, and simplify the expression as much as possible:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle ( {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\cos   \\theta  + 1}{\\sin   \\theta }} ) \\div  ( \\cos   \\theta  + 1 ) & \\displaystyle  = ( {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\cos   \\theta  + 1}{\\sin   \\theta }} ) \\cdot  ( {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{\\cos   \\theta  + 1}} ) \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\cos   \\theta  + 1}{\\sin   \\theta  ( \\cos   \\theta  + 1 )}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\cancel{( \\cos   \\theta  + 1 )}}{\\sin   \\theta  \\cancel{( \\cos   \\theta  + 1 )}}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{\\sin   \\theta }} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = \\csc   \\theta \\end{array}}\n$$" "First, let's interpret the fraction as a division:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\cot \\theta + \\csc \\theta} {\\cos \\theta + 1} = \\left(\\cot \\theta + \\csc \\theta\\right)\\operatorname{div}  \\left(\\cos \\theta + 1\\right)\n$$\n\nThen, we recall the identities\n\n$$\n\\cot \\theta = \\dfrac {\\cos \\theta} {\\sin \\theta}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\csc\\theta = \\dfrac{1}{\\sin\\theta}\n$$\n\nand we substitute them into the above expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(\\cot θ + \\csc θ) \\operatorname{div}  (\\cos θ + 1) &= (\\frac{\\cos θ}{\\sin θ} + \\frac{1}{\\sin θ}) \\operatorname{div}  (\\cos θ + 1) \\\\\n&= \\left(\\frac{\\cos θ + 1}{\\sin θ}\\right) \\operatorname{div}  (\\cos θ + 1)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we turn the division into multiplication, and simplify the expression as much as possible:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle ( {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\cos   \\theta  + 1}{\\sin   \\theta }} ) \\operatorname{div}   ( \\cos   \\theta  + 1 ) & \\displaystyle  = ( {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\cos   \\theta  + 1}{\\sin   \\theta }} ) \\cdot  ( {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{\\cos   \\theta  + 1}} ) \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\cos   \\theta  + 1}{\\sin   \\theta  ( \\cos   \\theta  + 1 )}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\cancel{( \\cos   \\theta  + 1 )}}{\\sin   \\theta  \\cancel{( \\cos   \\theta  + 1 )}}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{\\sin   \\theta }} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = \\csc   \\theta \\end{array}}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186059479 :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the denominator using $1-\\cos^2 x = \\sin^2 x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\cos{x}}{1-\\cos^2{x}} = \\dfrac{\\cos{x}}{\\sin^2{x}}.\n$$\n\nNext, we simplify the expression by separating out the factors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{{sin}^{2}⁡x} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot 1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cot}⁡x\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\cos{x}}{1-\\cos^2{x}}=\\cot{x}\\csc{x}.\n$$" "First, we rewrite the denominator using $1-\\cos^2 x = \\sin^2 x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\cos{x}}{1-\\cos^2{x}} = \\dfrac{\\cos{x}}{\\sin^2{x}}.\n$$\n\nNext, we simplify the expression by separating out the factors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{{\\sin }^{2}⁡x} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot 1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cot}⁡x\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\cos{x}}{1-\\cos^2{x}}=\\cot{x}\\csc{x}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186059480 :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the numerator using $1-\\sin^2 x = \\cos^2 x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\cos^2 x}{1-\\sin x} = \\dfrac{1-\\sin^2 x}{1-\\sin x}\n$$\n\nThen, we factor the numerator as a difference of squares, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1-{sin}^{2}⁡x}{1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x} & =\\frac{(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}{1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}{\\cancel{1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{1} \\\\  & =1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\cos^2 x}{1-\\sin x} = 1 + \\sin x.\n$$" "First, we rewrite the numerator using $1-\\sin^2 x = \\cos^2 x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\cos^2 x}{1-\\sin x} = \\dfrac{1-\\sin^2 x}{1-\\sin x}\n$$\n\nThen, we factor the numerator as a difference of squares, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1-{\\sin }^{2}⁡x}{1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x} & =\\frac{(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}{1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}{\\cancel{1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{1} \\\\  & =1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\cos^2 x}{1-\\sin x} = 1 + \\sin x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186059486 :question/worked-solution "First, we expand the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-{sin}^{2}⁡x-{cos}^{2}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we group the $\\sin^2 x$ and $\\cos^2 x$ terms. \n\n$$\n2\\sin x\\cos x -(\\sin^2x + \\cos^2 x)\n$$\n\nThen, we use $\\sin^2{x} + \\cos^2{x} = 1$ to simplify the expression in the parentheses:\n\n$$\n2\\sin x\\cos x - 1\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\sin x(2\\cos x - \\sin x) - \\cos^2x = 2\\sin x\\cos x - 1.\n$$" "First, we expand the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-{\\sin }^{2}⁡x-{\\cos }^{2}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we group the $\\sin^2 x$ and $\\cos^2 x$ terms. \n\n$$\n2\\sin x\\cos x -(\\sin^2x + \\cos^2 x)\n$$\n\nThen, we use $\\sin^2{x} + \\cos^2{x} = 1$ to simplify the expression in the parentheses:\n\n$$\n2\\sin x\\cos x - 1\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\sin x(2\\cos x - \\sin x) - \\cos^2x = 2\\sin x\\cos x - 1.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186059487 :question/worked-solution "First, we expand the parentheses and simplify the resulting expression:\n\n$$\n3\\cos^2 x \\left(\\sec^2 x -1\\right) = 3\\cos^2 x \\sec^2 x - 3\\cos^2 x\n$$\n\nThen, we use the identity $\\sec x = \\dfrac{1}{\\cos x}$ to further simplify the expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{cos}^{2}⁡x{sec}^{2}⁡x-3{cos}^{2}⁡x & =3{cos}^{2}⁡x\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\right)}^{2}-3{cos}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & =3{cos}^{2}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡x}-3{cos}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & =\\frac{3{cos}^{2}⁡x}{{cos}^{2}⁡x}-3{cos}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\cancel{{cos}^{2}⁡x}}{\\cancel{{cos}^{2}⁡x}}-3{cos}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & =3-3{cos}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & =3(1-{cos}^{2}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can use the identity $\\sin^2 x = 1-\\cos^2 x$ to rewrite the expression in the parentheses, as follows:\n\n$$\n3(1-\\cos^2 x) = 3\\cdot \\sin^2 x = 3\\sin^2 x\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n3\\cos^2 x \\left(\\sec^2 x -1\\right)= 3\\sin^2 x.\n$$" "First, we expand the parentheses and simplify the resulting expression:\n\n$$\n3\\cos^2 x \\left(\\sec^2 x -1\\right) = 3\\cos^2 x \\sec^2 x - 3\\cos^2 x\n$$\n\nThen, we use the identity $\\sec x = \\dfrac{1}{\\cos x}$ to further simplify the expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{\\cos }^{2}⁡x{\\sec }^{2}⁡x-3{\\cos }^{2}⁡x & =3{\\cos }^{2}⁡x\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\right)}^{2}-3{\\cos }^{2}⁡x \\\\  & =3{\\cos }^{2}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{1}{{\\cos }^{2}⁡x}-3{\\cos }^{2}⁡x \\\\  & =\\frac{3{\\cos }^{2}⁡x}{{\\cos }^{2}⁡x}-3{\\cos }^{2}⁡x \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\cancel{{\\cos }^{2}⁡x}}{\\cancel{{\\cos }^{2}⁡x}}-3{\\cos }^{2}⁡x \\\\  & =3-3{\\cos }^{2}⁡x \\\\  & =3(1-{\\cos }^{2}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can use the identity $\\sin^2 x = 1-\\cos^2 x$ to rewrite the expression in the parentheses, as follows:\n\n$$\n3(1-\\cos^2 x) = 3\\cdot \\sin^2 x = 3\\sin^2 x\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n3\\cos^2 x \\left(\\sec^2 x -1\\right)= 3\\sin^2 x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186059503 :question/worked-solution "We know that $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x} ( \\sin x ) = \\cos x,$ and that $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x} ( \\cos x ) = -\\sin x.$ Applying these formulas, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3cos⁡x+4sin⁡x+2{x}^{3}) \\\\  & =3\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(cos⁡x)+4\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(sin⁡x)+2\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{3}) \\\\  & =-3sin⁡x+4cos⁡x+2(3){x}^{2} \\\\  & =-3sin⁡x+4cos⁡x+6{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We know that $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x} ( \\sin x ) = \\cos x,$ and that $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x} ( \\cos x ) = -\\sin x.$ Applying these formulas, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3\\cos ⁡x+4\\sin ⁡x+2{x}^{3}) \\\\  & =3\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\cos ⁡x)+4\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\sin ⁡x)+2\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{3}) \\\\  & =-3\\sin ⁡x+4\\cos ⁡x+2(3){x}^{2} \\\\  & =-3\\sin ⁡x+4\\cos ⁡x+6{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186059504 :question/worked-solution "The slope at $x=0$ is given by the value of the derivative $y'$ at $x=0.$ \n\nFirst, we compute the derivative, using the fact that $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x} (\\tan x) = \\sec^2 x.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4tan⁡x+5x) \\\\  & =4{sec}^{2}⁡x+5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(0) & =4{sec}^{2}⁡0+5 \\\\  & =4\\cdot 1+5 \\\\  & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope is $9.$" "The slope at $x=0$ is given by the value of the derivative $y'$ at $x=0.$ \n\nFirst, we compute the derivative, using the fact that $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x} (\\tan x) = \\sec^2 x.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4\\tan ⁡x+5x) \\\\  & =4{\\sec }^{2}⁡x+5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(0) & =4{\\sec }^{2}⁡0+5 \\\\  & =4\\cdot 1+5 \\\\  & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope is $9.$"] [:db/cas 17592186059509 :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the cofunction identity for sine and cosine:\n\n$$\n\\cos \\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2} - y \\right) = \\sin y\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n1 - \\cos(\\frac{π}{2} - y)\\sin y &= 1 - \\siny\\sin y \\\\\n&= 1 - \\sin^{2} y \\\\\n&= \\cos^{2} y\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we recall the cofunction identity for sine and cosine:\n\n$$\n\\cos \\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2} - y \\right) = \\sin y\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n1 - \\cos(\\frac{π}{2} - y)\\sin y &= 1 - \\sin y\\sin y \\\\\n&= 1 - \\sin^{2} y \\\\\n&= \\cos^{2} y\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186059518 :question/worked-solution "To remove the logarithm, we exponentiate both sides of the equation. The base of the logarithm is $7,$ so we use this base for the exponentiation as well:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{7}⁡(2x+1) & =1 \\\\ {7}^{{log}_{7}⁡(2x+1)} & ={7}^{1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since exponentiation is the inverse function of the logarithm, we know that $7^{\\log_7 (2x + 1)} = 2x+1.$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+1 & =7 \\\\ 2x & =6 \\\\ x & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "To remove the logarithm, we exponentiate both sides of the equation. The base of the logarithm is $7,$ so we use this base for the exponentiation as well:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\log }_{7}⁡(2x+1) & =1 \\\\ {7}^{{\\log }_{7}⁡(2x+1)} & ={7}^{1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since exponentiation is the inverse function of the logarithm, we know that $7^{\\log_7 (2x + 1)} = 2x+1.$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+1 & =7 \\\\ 2x & =6 \\\\ x & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186059557 :question/worked-solution "For a $2 \\times 2$ matrix, the determinant is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{vmatrix} = ad - bc.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 5 & 6\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =3\\cdot 6-2\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =18-10 \\\\  & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$" "For a $2 \\times 2$ matrix, the determinant is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{vmatrix} = ad - bc.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\det (A) & =\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 5 & 6\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =3\\cdot 6-2\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =18-10 \\\\  & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186059558 :question/worked-solution "For a $2 \\times 2$ matrix, the determinant is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{vmatrix} = ad - bc.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\sqrt{2} & -2 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{3}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{3}-(-2)\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "For a $2 \\times 2$ matrix, the determinant is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{vmatrix} = ad - bc.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\det (A) & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\sqrt{2} & -2 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{3}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{3}-(-2)\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186059559 :question/worked-solution "For a $2 \\times 2$ matrix, the determinant is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{vmatrix} = ad - bc.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ -3 & k\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\cdot k-(-1)\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =2k-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\det(A)=5,$ we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =5 \\\\ 2k-3 & =5 \\\\ 2k & =8 \\\\ k & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$" "For a $2 \\times 2$ matrix, the determinant is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{vmatrix} = ad - bc.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\det (A) & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ -3 & k\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\cdot k-(-1)\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =2k-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\det(A)=5,$ we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\det (A) & =5 \\\\ 2k-3 & =5 \\\\ 2k & =8 \\\\ k & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186059560 :question/worked-solution "The minor of $b_{21} = 3$ (second row, first column) is the determinant of the remaining entries when the column and row containing $3$ are removed: \n\n$$\nB= \n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{ccc}{\\color{gray}∗} & 1 & 5 \\\\ \\bbox[2pt,lightgray]{ {\\color{gray}3}} & {\\color{gray}∗} & {\\color{gray}∗} \\\\ {\\color{gray}∗} & 7 & 2\\end{array} \\right]}\n$$\n\nSo, the minor of $b_{21}=3$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 \\\\ 7 & 2\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 5 \\\\ 7 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\cdot 2-5\\cdot 7 \\\\  & =2-35 \\\\  & =-33.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The minor of $b_{21} = 3$ (second row, first column) is the determinant of the remaining entries when the column and row containing $3$ are removed: \n\n$$\nB= \n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{ccc}{\\color{gray}∗} & 1 & 5 \\\\ \\bbox[2pt,lightgray]{ {\\color{gray}3}} & {\\color{gray}∗} & {\\color{gray}∗} \\\\ {\\color{gray}∗} & 7 & 2\\end{array} \\right]}\n$$\n\nSo, the minor of $b_{21}=3$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\det \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 \\\\ 7 & 2\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 5 \\\\ 7 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\cdot 2-5\\cdot 7 \\\\  & =2-35 \\\\  & =-33.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186059561 :question/worked-solution "First, we remove the second row and second column: \n\n$$\nC=\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{ccc}7 x & {\\color{gray}∗} & - 3 y \\\\ {\\color{gray}∗} & \\bbox[2pt,lightgray]{ {\\color{gray}x + 2 y}} & {\\color{gray}∗} \\\\ - y & {\\color{gray}∗} & 5\\end{array} \\right]}\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det\\begin{bmatrix}7x & -3y \\\\ -y & 5\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{vmatrix}7x & -3y \\\\ -y & 5\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =7x\\cdot 5-(-3y)\\cdot (-y) \\\\  & =35x-3{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we remove the second row and second column: \n\n$$\nC=\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{ccc}7 x & {\\color{gray}∗} & - 3 y \\\\ {\\color{gray}∗} & \\bbox[2pt,lightgray]{ {\\color{gray}x + 2 y}} & {\\color{gray}∗} \\\\ - y & {\\color{gray}∗} & 5\\end{array} \\right]}\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\det \\begin{bmatrix}7x & -3y \\\\ -y & 5\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{vmatrix}7x & -3y \\\\ -y & 5\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =7x\\cdot 5-(-3y)\\cdot (-y) \\\\  & =35x-3{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186059562 :question/worked-solution "First, we remove the second row and third column:\n\n$$\nA= {\\left[ \\begin{array}{ccc}k & 3 & {\\color{gray}∗} \\\\ {\\color{gray}∗} & {\\color{gray}∗} & \\bbox[2pt,lightgray]{ {\\color{gray}7}} \\\\ - 1 & 2 k & {\\color{gray}∗}\\end{array} \\right]}\n$$\n\nThe minor of $a_{23}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det\\begin{bmatrix}k & 3 \\\\ -1 & 2k\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{vmatrix}k & 3 \\\\ -1 & 2k\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =k\\cdot 2k-3\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =2{k}^{2}+3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that the minor of $a_{23}$ is $5,$ so we must have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{k}^{2}+3 & =5 \\\\ 2{k}^{2} & =2 \\\\ {k}^{2} & =1 \\\\ k & =\\pm 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $k=-1$ and $k=1.$" "First, we remove the second row and third column:\n\n$$\nA= {\\left[ \\begin{array}{ccc}k & 3 & {\\color{gray}∗} \\\\ {\\color{gray}∗} & {\\color{gray}∗} & \\bbox[2pt,lightgray]{ {\\color{gray}7}} \\\\ - 1 & 2 k & {\\color{gray}∗}\\end{array} \\right]}\n$$\n\nThe minor of $a_{23}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\det \\begin{bmatrix}k & 3 \\\\ -1 & 2k\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{vmatrix}k & 3 \\\\ -1 & 2k\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =k\\cdot 2k-3\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =2{k}^{2}+3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that the minor of $a_{23}$ is $5,$ so we must have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{k}^{2}+3 & =5 \\\\ 2{k}^{2} & =2 \\\\ {k}^{2} & =1 \\\\ k & =\\pm 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $k=-1$ and $k=1.$"] [:db/cas 17592186059563 :question/worked-solution "Let's follow the standard five-step procedure.\n\n**Step 1:** Find the determinant of $A.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A)=\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 4 & -3 & 8\\end{vmatrix} & =0\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 8\\end{vmatrix}-1\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 8\\end{vmatrix}+2\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =0-(8-12)+2(-3-0) \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2:** Form the matrix of the minors of $A.$\nTo make this matrix, we replace each entry of $A$ by its minor.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 8\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 8\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 8\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 8\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 4 & -3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}9 & -4 & -3 \\\\ 14 & -8 & -4 \\\\ 3 & -2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Create the matrix of cofactors by changing the sign of some of the entries of $M$ according to the rule of alternating signs.\n\n$$\nC={\\left[ \\begin{array}{ccc}9 & - ( - 4 ) & - 3 \\\\ - ( 14 ) & - 8 & - ( - 4 ) \\\\ 3 & - ( - 2 ) & - 1\\end{array} \\right]}\n={\\left[ \\begin{array}{ccc}9 & 4 & - 3 \\\\ - 14 & - 8 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 2 & - 1\\end{array} \\right]}\n$$\n\n**Step 4:** Write down the transpose of the matrix of cofactors.\n\n$$\nC^{T}={\\left[ \\begin{array}{ccc}9 & - 14 & 3 \\\\ 4 & - 8 & 2 \\\\ - 3 & 4 & - 1\\end{array} \\right]}\n$$\n\n**Step 5:** Use the formula for the inverse of a $3\\times 3$ matrix.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{-2}\\begin{bmatrix}9 & -14 & 3 \\\\ 4 & -8 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 4 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}9 & -14 & 3 \\\\ 4 & -8 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 4 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{9}{2} & 7 & -\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ -2 & 4 & -1 \\\\ \\frac{3}{2} & -2 & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let's follow the standard five-step procedure.\n\n**Step 1:** Find the determinant of $A.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\det (A)=\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 4 & -3 & 8\\end{vmatrix} & =0\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 8\\end{vmatrix}-1\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 8\\end{vmatrix}+2\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =0-(8-12)+2(-3-0) \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2:** Form the matrix of the minors of $A.$\nTo make this matrix, we replace each entry of $A$ by its minor.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 8\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 8\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 8\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 8\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 4 & -3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}9 & -4 & -3 \\\\ 14 & -8 & -4 \\\\ 3 & -2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Create the matrix of cofactors by changing the sign of some of the entries of $M$ according to the rule of alternating signs.\n\n$$\nC={\\left[ \\begin{array}{ccc}9 & - ( - 4 ) & - 3 \\\\ - ( 14 ) & - 8 & - ( - 4 ) \\\\ 3 & - ( - 2 ) & - 1\\end{array} \\right]}\n={\\left[ \\begin{array}{ccc}9 & 4 & - 3 \\\\ - 14 & - 8 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 2 & - 1\\end{array} \\right]}\n$$\n\n**Step 4:** Write down the transpose of the matrix of cofactors.\n\n$$\nC^{T}={\\left[ \\begin{array}{ccc}9 & - 14 & 3 \\\\ 4 & - 8 & 2 \\\\ - 3 & 4 & - 1\\end{array} \\right]}\n$$\n\n**Step 5:** Use the formula for the inverse of a $3\\times 3$ matrix.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{\\det (A)}{C}^{T} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{-2}\\begin{bmatrix}9 & -14 & 3 \\\\ 4 & -8 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 4 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}9 & -14 & 3 \\\\ 4 & -8 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 4 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{9}{2} & 7 & -\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ -2 & 4 & -1 \\\\ \\frac{3}{2} & -2 & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186059564 :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the determinant of $A\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A)=\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & k & 0 \\\\ -1 & -3 & 3\\end{vmatrix} & =3\\begin{vmatrix}k & 0 \\\\ -3 & 3\\end{vmatrix}-2\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{vmatrix}+(-2)\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & k \\\\ -1 & -3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =3(3k-0)-2(-6-0)-2(6+k) \\\\  & =9k+12-12-2k \\\\  & =7k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we want the entry $a'_{31}$ in $A^{-1},$ we need to know the value of $c_{13}$ in the matrix of cofactors. For this, we use the formula to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{13} & =(-1)^{1+3}\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & k \\\\ -1 & -3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & k \\\\ -1 & -3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =6+k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since $A^{-1}=\\dfrac{1}{\\det (A)}C^{T},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{31}^{′} & =\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{13} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7k}\\cdot (6+k) \\\\  & =\\frac{6+k}{7k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since we are told that $a'_{31}=1$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{6+k}{7k} & =1 \\\\ 6+k & =7k \\\\ 6 & =6k \\\\ k & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we need to find the determinant of $A\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\det (A)=\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & k & 0 \\\\ -1 & -3 & 3\\end{vmatrix} & =3\\begin{vmatrix}k & 0 \\\\ -3 & 3\\end{vmatrix}-2\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{vmatrix}+(-2)\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & k \\\\ -1 & -3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =3(3k-0)-2(-6-0)-2(6+k) \\\\  & =9k+12-12-2k \\\\  & =7k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we want the entry $a'_{31}$ in $A^{-1},$ we need to know the value of $c_{13}$ in the matrix of cofactors. For this, we use the formula to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{13} & =(-1)^{1+3}\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & k \\\\ -1 & -3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & k \\\\ -1 & -3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =6+k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since $A^{-1}=\\dfrac{1}{\\det (A)}C^{T},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{31}^{′} & =\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{13} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7k}\\cdot (6+k) \\\\  & =\\frac{6+k}{7k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since we are told that $a'_{31}=1$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{6+k}{7k} & =1 \\\\ 6+k & =7k \\\\ 6 & =6k \\\\ k & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186059565 :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the determinant of $A\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A)=\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 3 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} & =-2\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}-3\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}+(-3)\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-2(2-0)-3(0-0)-3(0-1) \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we want the entry $a'_{23}$ in $A^{-1},$ we need to know the value of $c_{32}$ in the matrix of cofactors. For this, we use the formula to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{32} & =(-1)^{3+2}{M}_{32} \\\\  & =(-1)^{5}\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-1\\cdot (0) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since $A^{-1}=\\dfrac{1}{\\det (A)}C^{T},$ we have\n\n$$\na'_{23} = \\dfrac{1}{-1}c_{32} = -1\\cdot 0 = 0.\n$$" "First, we need to find the determinant of $A\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\det (A)=\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 3 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} & =-2\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}-3\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}+(-3)\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-2(2-0)-3(0-0)-3(0-1) \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we want the entry $a'_{23}$ in $A^{-1},$ we need to know the value of $c_{32}$ in the matrix of cofactors. For this, we use the formula to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{32} & =(-1)^{3+2}{M}_{32} \\\\  & =(-1)^{5}\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-1\\cdot (0) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since $A^{-1}=\\dfrac{1}{\\det (A)}C^{T},$ we have\n\n$$\na'_{23} = \\dfrac{1}{-1}c_{32} = -1\\cdot 0 = 0.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186059592 :question/worked-solution "First, we make $\\log_5 (x-3)$ the subject of the equation by moving everything else to the right-hand side. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{log}_{5}⁡(x-3)+1 & =5 \\\\ 2{log}_{5}⁡(x-3) & =4 \\\\ {log}_{5}⁡(x-3) & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo remove the logarithm, we exponentiate both sides of the equation. The base of the logarithm is $5,$ so we use a base of $5$ for the exponentiation as well:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{5}⁡(x-3) & =2 \\\\ {5}^{{log}_{5}⁡(x-3)} & ={5}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since exponentiation is the inverse function of the logarithm, we know that $5^{\\log_5 (x - 3)} = x-3.$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-3 & ={5}^{2} \\\\ x-3 & =25 \\\\ x & =28\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we make $\\log_5 (x-3)$ the subject of the equation by moving everything else to the right-hand side. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{\\log }_{5}⁡(x-3)+1 & =5 \\\\ 2{\\log }_{5}⁡(x-3) & =4 \\\\ {\\log }_{5}⁡(x-3) & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo remove the logarithm, we exponentiate both sides of the equation. The base of the logarithm is $5,$ so we use a base of $5$ for the exponentiation as well:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\log }_{5}⁡(x-3) & =2 \\\\ {5}^{{\\log }_{5}⁡(x-3)} & ={5}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since exponentiation is the inverse function of the logarithm, we know that $5^{\\log_5 (x - 3)} = x-3.$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-3 & ={5}^{2} \\\\ x-3 & =25 \\\\ x & =28\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186059593 :question/worked-solution "To remove the logarithm, we exponentiate both sides of the equation. The base of the logarithm is $2,$ so we use a base of $2$ for the exponentiation as well:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{2}⁡x & =5 \\\\ {2}^{{log}_{2}⁡x} & ={2}^{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since exponentiation is the inverse function of the logarithm, we know that $2^{\\log_2 x} = x.$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={2}^{5} \\\\ x & =32.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "To remove the logarithm, we exponentiate both sides of the equation. The base of the logarithm is $2,$ so we use a base of $2$ for the exponentiation as well:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\log }_{2}⁡x & =5 \\\\ {2}^{{\\log }_{2}⁡x} & ={2}^{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since exponentiation is the inverse function of the logarithm, we know that $2^{\\log_2 x} = x.$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={2}^{5} \\\\ x & =32.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186059594 :question/worked-solution "First, we simplify our equation by collecting the like logarithmic terms, and isolating the logarithm, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7{log}_{6}⁡x-3{log}_{6}⁡x & =2 \\\\ (7-3){log}_{6}⁡x & =2 \\\\ 4{log}_{6}⁡x & =2 \\\\ {log}_{6}⁡x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo remove the logarithm, we exponentiate both sides of the equation. The base of the logarithm is $6,$ so we use a base of $6$ for the exponentiation as well:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{6}⁡x & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {6}^{{log}_{6}⁡x} & ={6}^{1/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since exponentiation is the inverse function of the logarithm, we know that $6^{\\log_6 x} = x.$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={6}^{1/2} \\\\ x & =\\sqrt{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we simplify our equation by collecting the like logarithmic terms, and isolating the logarithm, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7{\\log }_{6}⁡x-3{\\log }_{6}⁡x & =2 \\\\ (7-3){\\log }_{6}⁡x & =2 \\\\ 4{\\log }_{6}⁡x & =2 \\\\ {\\log }_{6}⁡x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo remove the logarithm, we exponentiate both sides of the equation. The base of the logarithm is $6,$ so we use a base of $6$ for the exponentiation as well:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\log }_{6}⁡x & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {6}^{{\\log }_{6}⁡x} & ={6}^{1/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since exponentiation is the inverse function of the logarithm, we know that $6^{\\log_6 x} = x.$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={6}^{1/2} \\\\ x & =\\sqrt{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186059982 :question/problem "Calculate $det(Z)$, if\n $$\n\\displaystyle Z=\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{x} & y \\\\ {y}^{2} & \\sqrt{x}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" "Calculate $\\det (Z)$, if\n $$\n\\displaystyle Z=\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{x} & y \\\\ {y}^{2} & \\sqrt{x}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186059987 :question/worked-solution "The minor of ${y}_{13}=9$ (first row, third column) is the determinant of the remaining entries when the column and row containing $9$ are removed: \n\n$$\nY=\\begin{bmatrix}∗ & ∗ & 9 \\\\ 7 & 1 & ∗ \\\\ 0 & -1 & ∗\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSo, the minor of ${y}_{13}=9$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{vmatrix}7 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =7\\cdot (-1)-1\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =-7-0 \\\\  & =-7.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The minor of ${y}_{13}=9$ (first row, third column) is the determinant of the remaining entries when the column and row containing $9$ are removed: \n\n$$\nY=\\begin{bmatrix}∗ & ∗ & 9 \\\\ 7 & 1 & ∗ \\\\ 0 & -1 & ∗\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSo, the minor of ${y}_{13}=9$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\det \\begin{bmatrix}7 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{vmatrix}7 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =7\\cdot (-1)-1\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =-7-0 \\\\  & =-7.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186059988 :question/worked-solution "First, we remove the first row and second column:\n\n$$\nX=\\begin{bmatrix}∗ & -3a & ∗ \\\\ -b & ∗ & -2c \\\\ -a & ∗ & b\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det\\begin{bmatrix}-b & -2c \\\\ -a & b\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{vmatrix}-b & -2c \\\\ -a & b\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-b\\cdot b-(-2c)\\cdot (-a) \\\\  & =-{b}^{2}-2ac\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we remove the first row and second column:\n\n$$\nX=\\begin{bmatrix}∗ & -3a & ∗ \\\\ -b & ∗ & -2c \\\\ -a & ∗ & b\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\det \\begin{bmatrix}-b & -2c \\\\ -a & b\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{vmatrix}-b & -2c \\\\ -a & b\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-b\\cdot b-(-2c)\\cdot (-a) \\\\  & =-{b}^{2}-2ac\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186059989 :question/worked-solution "First, we remove the third row and second column:\n\n$$\nD=\\begin{bmatrix}3s & ∗ & 2t \\\\ -r & ∗ & r \\\\ ∗ & 4r & ∗\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det\\begin{bmatrix}3s & 2t \\\\ -r & r\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{vmatrix}3s & 2t \\\\ -r & r\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =3s\\cdot r-2t\\cdot (-r) \\\\  & =3rs-(-2rt) \\\\  & =3rs+2rt\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we remove the third row and second column:\n\n$$\nD=\\begin{bmatrix}3s & ∗ & 2t \\\\ -r & ∗ & r \\\\ ∗ & 4r & ∗\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\det \\begin{bmatrix}3s & 2t \\\\ -r & r\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{vmatrix}3s & 2t \\\\ -r & r\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =3s\\cdot r-2t\\cdot (-r) \\\\  & =3rs-(-2rt) \\\\  & =3rs+2rt\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186059991 :question/problem "If $\\frac{{log}_{7}⁡(x+1)}{2}=1$, then $x=$" "If $\\frac{{\\log }_{7}⁡(x+1)}{2}=1$, then $x=$"] [:db/cas 17592186059991 :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange our equation to isolate the logarithmic term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{log}_{7}⁡(x+1)}{2} & =1 \\\\ {log}_{7}⁡(x+1) & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo remove the logarithm, we exponentiate both sides of the equation. The base of the logarithm is $7,$ so we use this base for the exponentiation as well:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{7}⁡(x+1) & =2 \\\\ {7}^{{log}_{7}⁡(x+1)} & ={7}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since exponentiation is the inverse function of the logarithm, we get ${7}^{{log}_{7}⁡(x+1)}=x+1.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+1 & ={7}^{2} \\\\ x+1 & =49 \\\\ x & =48.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we rearrange our equation to isolate the logarithmic term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\log }_{7}⁡(x+1)}{2} & =1 \\\\ {\\log }_{7}⁡(x+1) & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo remove the logarithm, we exponentiate both sides of the equation. The base of the logarithm is $7,$ so we use this base for the exponentiation as well:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\log }_{7}⁡(x+1) & =2 \\\\ {7}^{{\\log }_{7}⁡(x+1)} & ={7}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since exponentiation is the inverse function of the logarithm, we get ${7}^{{\\log }_{7}⁡(x+1)}=x+1.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+1 & ={7}^{2} \\\\ x+1 & =49 \\\\ x & =48.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186060001 :question/problem "If\n$$\n\\displaystyle C = \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 5 & k\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nand\n$det(C) = 3$, what is the value of $k$?" "If\n$$\n\\displaystyle C = \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 5 & k\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nand\n$\\det (C) = 3$, what is the value of $k$?"] [:db/cas 17592186060020 :question/worked-solution "Remember that a square matrix is singular if its determinant is equal to $0.$\n\nUsing the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\sqrt{5} & 0 & \\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 0 & 3 & 5 \\\\ -3\\sqrt{5} & 3 & 4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{vmatrix}-0\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 5 \\\\ -3\\sqrt{5} & 4\\end{vmatrix}+\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 3 \\\\ -3\\sqrt{5} & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}(12-15)-0+\\frac{1}{3}(0+9\\sqrt{5}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}\\cdot (-3)+\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot 9\\sqrt{5} \\\\  & =-3\\sqrt{5}+3\\sqrt{5} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $det(M)=0$, the matrix is singular. \n\nTherefore, only statement “$M$ is a singular matrix” is true." "Remember that a square matrix is singular if its determinant is equal to $0.$\n\nUsing the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\det (M) & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\sqrt{5} & 0 & \\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 0 & 3 & 5 \\\\ -3\\sqrt{5} & 3 & 4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{vmatrix}-0\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 5 \\\\ -3\\sqrt{5} & 4\\end{vmatrix}+\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 3 \\\\ -3\\sqrt{5} & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}(12-15)-0+\\frac{1}{3}(0+9\\sqrt{5}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}\\cdot (-3)+\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot 9\\sqrt{5} \\\\  & =-3\\sqrt{5}+3\\sqrt{5} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $\\det (M)=0$, the matrix is singular. \n\nTherefore, only statement “$M$ is a singular matrix” is true."] [:db/cas 17592186060153 :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the determinant of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(A)=\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix} & =1\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix}-(-2)\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}+4\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1(6-0)+2(0+1)+4(0+3) \\\\  & =6+2+12 \\\\  & =20.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince we want the entry ${a}_{12}^{′}$ in ${A}^{-1},$ we need to know the value of ${c}_{21}$ in the matrix of cofactors. For this, we use the formula to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{21} & =(-1)^{2+1}\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-(-4-0) \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since ${A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{12}^{′}=\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{21}=\\frac{1}{20}\\cdot 4=\\frac{1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we need to find the determinant of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(A)=\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix} & =1\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix}-(-2)\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}+4\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1(6-0)+2(0+1)+4(0+3) \\\\  & =6+2+12 \\\\  & =20.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince we want the entry ${a}_{12}^{′}$ in ${A}^{-1},$ we need to know the value of ${c}_{21}$ in the matrix of cofactors. For this, we use the formula to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{21} & =(-1)^{2+1}\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-(-4-0) \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since ${A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{\\det (A)}{C}^{T},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{12}^{′}=\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{21}=\\frac{1}{20}\\cdot 4=\\frac{1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186060154 :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the determinant of $A:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}A=\\begin{vmatrix}5 & 0 & 3 \\\\ -2 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix} & =5\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}-0\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}+3\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =5(0-2)+0+3(-2+3) \\\\  & =-10+0+3 \\\\  & =-7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince we want the entry ${a}_{33}^{′}$ in ${A}^{-1},$ we need to know the value of ${c}_{33}$ in the matrix of cofactors. For this, we use the formula to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{33} & =(-1)^{3+3}\\begin{vmatrix}5 & 0 \\\\ -2 & -3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}5 & 0 \\\\ -2 & -3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-15-0 \\\\  & =-15.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since ${A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{33}^{′}=\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{33}=\\frac{1}{-7}\\cdot (-15)=\\frac{15}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we need to find the determinant of $A:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}A=\\begin{vmatrix}5 & 0 & 3 \\\\ -2 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix} & =5\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}-0\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}+3\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =5(0-2)+0+3(-2+3) \\\\  & =-10+0+3 \\\\  & =-7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince we want the entry ${a}_{33}^{′}$ in ${A}^{-1},$ we need to know the value of ${c}_{33}$ in the matrix of cofactors. For this, we use the formula to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{33} & =(-1)^{3+3}\\begin{vmatrix}5 & 0 \\\\ -2 & -3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}5 & 0 \\\\ -2 & -3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-15-0 \\\\  & =-15.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since ${A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{\\det (A)}{C}^{T},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{33}^{′}=\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{33}=\\frac{1}{-7}\\cdot (-15)=\\frac{15}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186060168 :question/problem "Let $a, b ∈ Z$. Prove that if $a≡b(mod13)$, then $a - 7≡b - 7(mod13)$.\n\nSince $a≡b(mod13)$, by the definition of {{field-1}},\n\n$(a + b)∣13$ {{field-2}} {{field-3}}\nfor some integer $k$." "Let $a, b ∈ Z$. Prove that if $a≡b(\\bmod 13)$, then $a - 7≡b - 7(\\bmod 13)$.\n\nSince $a≡b(\\bmod 13)$, by the definition of {{field-1}},\n\n$(a + b)∣13$ {{field-2}} {{field-3}}\nfor some integer $k$."] [:db/cas 17592186060169 :question/problem "Let $a, b ∈ Z$. Prove that if $a≡b(mod8)$, then $a + 3≡b + 3(mod8)$.\n\nSince $a≡b(mod8)$, by the definition of {{field-1}},\n\n$8∣(a - b)$ {{field-2}} {{field-3}}\nfor some integer $k$." "Let $a, b ∈ Z$. Prove that if $a≡b(\\bmod 8)$, then $a + 3≡b + 3(\\bmod 8)$.\n\nSince $a≡b(\\bmod 8)$, by the definition of {{field-1}},\n\n$8∣(a - b)$ {{field-2}} {{field-3}}\nfor some integer $k$."] [:db/cas 17592186060195 :question/problem "If\n$$\n\\displaystyle A = \\begin{bmatrix}1 + t & 2t \\\\ -3 & 1 - t\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nand $det(A) = 10$, what is the value of $t$?" "If\n$$\n\\displaystyle A = \\begin{bmatrix}1 + t & 2t \\\\ -3 & 1 - t\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nand $\\det (A) = 10$, what is the value of $t$?"] [:db/cas 17592186060215 :question/problem "Let $a, b ∈ Z$. Prove that $5a≡5b(mod7)$ if $a≡b(mod7)$.\n\nSince $a≡b(mod7)$, by the definition of {{field-1}},\n\n$(a + b)∣7$ {{field-2}} {{field-3}}\nfor some integer $k$." "Let $a, b ∈ Z$. Prove that $5a≡5b(\\bmod 7)$ if $a≡b(\\bmod 7)$.\n\nSince $a≡b(\\bmod 7)$, by the definition of {{field-1}},\n\n$(a + b)∣7$ {{field-2}} {{field-3}}\nfor some integer $k$."] [:db/cas 17592186060216 :question/problem "Let $a, b ∈ Z$. Prove that $5a≡5b(mod7)$ if $a≡b(mod7)$.\n\nSince $a≡b(mod7)$, by the definition of {{field-1}}, $7∣(a - b)$ {{field-2}} {{field-3}}\nfor some integer $k$.Now, multiplying both sides of $a - b =$ {{field-4}} by {{field-5}}, we get\n$5$ {{field-6}} {{field-7}} {{field-8}} {{field-9}} {{field-10}}\nwhere $m = 5k$ is an integer. According to the definition of {{field-11}}, the final equation implies that\n$5a≡5b(mod7)$." "Let $a, b ∈ Z$. Prove that $5a≡5b(\\bmod 7)$ if $a≡b(\\bmod 7)$.\n\nSince $a≡b(\\bmod 7)$, by the definition of {{field-1}}, $7∣(a - b)$ {{field-2}} {{field-3}}\nfor some integer $k$.Now, multiplying both sides of $a - b =$ {{field-4}} by {{field-5}}, we get\n$5$ {{field-6}} {{field-7}} {{field-8}} {{field-9}} {{field-10}}\nwhere $m = 5k$ is an integer. According to the definition of {{field-11}}, the final equation implies that\n$5a≡5b(\\bmod 7)$."] [:db/cas 17592186060217 :question/problem "Let $a, b ∈ Z$. Prove that if $3a≡3b(mod15)$, then $a≡b(mod5)$.\n\nUsing the definition of {{field-1}},\n\n$3(a - b)∣15$ {{field-2}}" "Let $a, b ∈ Z$. Prove that if $3a≡3b(\\bmod 15)$, then $a≡b(\\bmod 5)$.\n\nUsing the definition of {{field-1}},\n\n$3(a - b)∣15$ {{field-2}}"] [:db/cas 17592186060218 :question/problem "Let $a, b ∈ Z$. Prove that $a≡b(mod5)$ provided that $3a≡3b(mod5)$.\n\nUsing the definition of {{field-1}},\n\n$3(a - b)∣5$ {{field-2}}" "Let $a, b ∈ Z$. Prove that $a≡b(\\bmod 5)$ provided that $3a≡3b(\\bmod 5)$.\n\nUsing the definition of {{field-1}},\n\n$3(a - b)∣5$ {{field-2}}"] [:db/cas 17592186060249 :question/worked-solution "First, we expand the parenthesis and use the Pythagorean identity.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{12}\\right)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{12}\\right))}^{2} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{12}\\right)-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{12}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{12}\\right)+{cos}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{12}\\right) \\\\  & =[{sin}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{12}\\right)+{cos}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{12}\\right)]-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{12}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{12}\\right) \\\\  & =1-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{12}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{12}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's recall the double-angle formula for sine: \n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\frac{\\pi }{12}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{12}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{12}\\right) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{12}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{12}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{12}\\right) & =1-\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)=\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{12}\\right)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{12}\\right))}^{2}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$" "First, we expand the parenthesis and use the Pythagorean identity.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{12}\\right)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{12}\\right))}^{2} & ={\\sin }^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{12}\\right)-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{12}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{12}\\right)+{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{12}\\right) \\\\  & =[{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{12}\\right)+{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{12}\\right)]-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{12}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{12}\\right) \\\\  & =1-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{12}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{12}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's recall the double-angle formula for sine: \n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\frac{\\pi }{12}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{12}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{12}\\right) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{12}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{12}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{12}\\right) & =1-\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)=\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{12}\\right)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{12}\\right))}^{2}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186060347 :question/problem "Simplify the expression $\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+1}.$" "Simplify the expression $\\frac{{\\cos }^{2}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+1}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186060347 :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the numerator using $1-{sin}^{2}⁡x={cos}^{2}⁡x.$\n\n$$\n\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+1}=\\frac{1-{sin}^{2}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+1}\n$$\n\nNext, we factor the numerator as a difference of squares, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1-{sin}^{2}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+1} & =\\frac{(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}{\\cancel{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{1} \\\\  & =1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we rewrite the numerator using $1-{\\sin }^{2}⁡x={\\cos }^{2}⁡x.$\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\cos }^{2}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+1}=\\frac{1-{\\sin }^{2}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+1}\n$$\n\nNext, we factor the numerator as a difference of squares, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1-{\\sin }^{2}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+1} & =\\frac{(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}{\\cancel{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{1} \\\\  & =1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186060348 :question/problem "If $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{{sin}^{2}⁡x}=\\frac{1}{f(x)},\n$$ then $f(x)=$" "If $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{{\\sin }^{2}⁡x}=\\frac{1}{f(x)},\n$$ then $f(x)=$"] [:db/cas 17592186060348 :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the denominator using $1-{cos}^{2}⁡x={sin}^{2}⁡x.$\n\n$$\n\\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{{sin}^{2}⁡x}=\\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{1-{cos}^{2}⁡x}\n$$\n\nNext, we factor the denominator as difference of squares, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{1-{cos}^{2}⁡x} & =\\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}{\\cancel{(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)=1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$" "First, we rewrite the denominator using $1-{\\cos }^{2}⁡x={\\sin }^{2}⁡x.$\n\n$$\n\\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{{\\sin }^{2}⁡x}=\\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{1-{\\cos }^{2}⁡x}\n$$\n\nNext, we factor the denominator as difference of squares, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{1-{\\cos }^{2}⁡x} & =\\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}{\\cancel{(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)=1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$"] [:db/cas 17592186060363 :question/worked-solution "![](images/02/0204cfb247273efc20a31366dcb84096d630aa6dbe90c94a8802834205bbdd56.png)\n\nTo plot the given curve, we follow these steps:\n\n- Start with the graph of $y=sin⁡x.$\n\n- Then, reflect it in the $x$-axis to give $y=-sin⁡x.$\n\nTherefore, the given curve is the graph of $y=-sin⁡x.$" "![](images/02/0204cfb247273efc20a31366dcb84096d630aa6dbe90c94a8802834205bbdd56.png)\n\nTo plot the given curve, we follow these steps:\n\n- Start with the graph of $y=\\sin ⁡x.$\n\n- Then, reflect it in the $x$-axis to give $y=-\\sin ⁡x.$\n\nTherefore, the given curve is the graph of $y=-\\sin ⁡x.$"] [:db/cas 17592186060366 :question/worked-solution "The given curve is the graph of $y=cos⁡x.$\n\nNotice that, because the cosine function is even, it is symmetric about the $y$-axis, i.e.,\n\n$$\ncos⁡(-x)=cos⁡x\n$$\n\nSo the graph could also be viewed as $y=cos⁡(-x).$" "The given curve is the graph of $y=\\cos ⁡x.$\n\nNotice that, because the cosine function is even, it is symmetric about the $y$-axis, i.e.,\n\n$$\n\\cos ⁡(-x)=\\cos ⁡x\n$$\n\nSo the graph could also be viewed as $y=\\cos ⁡(-x).$"] [:db/cas 17592186060378 :question/worked-solution "![](images/c2/c27ff316601ece28946ef45711690d6aabca92a4c35f07b0b1cc1c0982fb5d8e.png)\n\nTo plot the given curve, we follow these steps:\n\n- Start with the graph of $y=cos⁡x.$\n\n- Then, reflect it in the $x$-axis to give $y=-cos⁡x.$\n\n- Finally, translate it by $1$ unit **up** to give $y=-cos⁡x+1$\n\nTherefore, the given curve is the graph of $y=-cos⁡x+1.$" "![](images/c2/c27ff316601ece28946ef45711690d6aabca92a4c35f07b0b1cc1c0982fb5d8e.png)\n\nTo plot the given curve, we follow these steps:\n\n- Start with the graph of $y=\\cos ⁡x.$\n\n- Then, reflect it in the $x$-axis to give $y=-\\cos ⁡x.$\n\n- Finally, translate it by $1$ unit **up** to give $y=-\\cos ⁡x+1$\n\nTherefore, the given curve is the graph of $y=-\\cos ⁡x+1.$"] [:db/cas 17592186060433 :question/problem "If ${log}_{4}⁡x=3$, then $x=$" "If ${\\log }_{4}⁡x=3$, then $x=$"] [:db/cas 17592186060433 :question/worked-solution "To remove the logarithm, we exponentiate both sides of the equation. The base of the logarithm is $4,$ so we use a base of $4$ for the exponentiation as well:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{4}⁡x & =3 \\\\ {4}^{{log}_{4}⁡x} & ={4}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since exponentiation is the inverse function of the logarithm, we get ${4}^{{log}_{4}⁡x}=x.$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={4}^{3} \\\\ x & =64.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "To remove the logarithm, we exponentiate both sides of the equation. The base of the logarithm is $4,$ so we use a base of $4$ for the exponentiation as well:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\log }_{4}⁡x & =3 \\\\ {4}^{{\\log }_{4}⁡x} & ={4}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since exponentiation is the inverse function of the logarithm, we get ${4}^{{\\log }_{4}⁡x}=x.$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={4}^{3} \\\\ x & =64.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186060450 :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the determinant of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A)=\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 4 \\\\ k & 6 & 0\\end{vmatrix} & =1\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 4 \\\\ 6 & 0\\end{vmatrix}-2\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 4 \\\\ k & 0\\end{vmatrix}+3\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ k & 6\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1(0-24)-2(0-4k)+3(0-k) \\\\  & =8k-3k-24 \\\\  & =5k-24.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we want the entry ${a}_{12}^{′}$ in ${A}^{-1},$ we need to know the value of ${c}_{21}$ in the matrix of cofactors. For this, we use the formula to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{21} & =(-1)^{2+1}\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 6 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 6 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-(0-18) \\\\  & =18.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since ${A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{12}^{′} & =\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{21} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5k-24}\\cdot 18 \\\\  & =\\frac{18}{5k-24}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since we are told that ${a}_{13}^{′}=18$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{18}{5k-24} & =18 \\\\ \\frac{1}{5k-24} & =1 \\\\ 1 & =5k-24 \\\\ 25 & =5k \\\\ k & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we need to find the determinant of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\det (A)=\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 4 \\\\ k & 6 & 0\\end{vmatrix} & =1\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 4 \\\\ 6 & 0\\end{vmatrix}-2\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 4 \\\\ k & 0\\end{vmatrix}+3\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ k & 6\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1(0-24)-2(0-4k)+3(0-k) \\\\  & =8k-3k-24 \\\\  & =5k-24.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we want the entry ${a}_{12}^{′}$ in ${A}^{-1},$ we need to know the value of ${c}_{21}$ in the matrix of cofactors. For this, we use the formula to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{21} & =(-1)^{2+1}\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 6 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 6 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-(0-18) \\\\  & =18.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since ${A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{\\det (A)}{C}^{T},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{12}^{′} & =\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{21} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5k-24}\\cdot 18 \\\\  & =\\frac{18}{5k-24}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since we are told that ${a}_{13}^{′}=18$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{18}{5k-24} & =18 \\\\ \\frac{1}{5k-24} & =1 \\\\ 1 & =5k-24 \\\\ 25 & =5k \\\\ k & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186060454 :question/worked-solution "Remember that a square matrix is singular if its determinant is equal to $0.$\n\nUsing the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(F) & =\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4 & 5 \\\\ 6 & 7 & 8\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =0\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 5 \\\\ 7 & 8\\end{vmatrix}-1\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 5 \\\\ 6 & 8\\end{vmatrix}+2\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 4 \\\\ 6 & 7\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =0-1(24-30)+2(21-24) \\\\  & =0-1\\cdot (-6)+2\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =6-6 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $det(F)=0$, the matrix is singular. \n\nTherefore, only statements $det(F)=0$ and “$F$ is a singular matrix” are true." "Remember that a square matrix is singular if its determinant is equal to $0.$\n\nUsing the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\det (F) & =\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4 & 5 \\\\ 6 & 7 & 8\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =0\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 5 \\\\ 7 & 8\\end{vmatrix}-1\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 5 \\\\ 6 & 8\\end{vmatrix}+2\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 4 \\\\ 6 & 7\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =0-1(24-30)+2(21-24) \\\\  & =0-1\\cdot (-6)+2\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =6-6 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\det (F)=0$, the matrix is singular. \n\nTherefore, only statements $\\det (F)=0$ and “$F$ is a singular matrix” are true."] [:db/cas 17592186060458 :question/problem "Find the inverse of the matrix $A = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\ngiven that $det(A) = 5$." "Find the inverse of the matrix $A = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\ngiven that $\\det (A) = 5$."] [:db/cas 17592186060459 :question/problem "The entry $a_{23}^{′}$ in the inverse of the matrix\n$$\n\\displaystyle A = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & k & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nequals $-1$ and $det(A) = 4 - 4k$. Find the value of $k$." "The entry $a_{23}^{′}$ in the inverse of the matrix\n$$\n\\displaystyle A = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & k & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nequals $-1$ and $\\det (A) = 4 - 4k$. Find the value of $k$."] [:db/cas 17592186060863 :tutorial/content "Let's now derive the law of cosines in the following form:\n\n$$\na^2 = b^2 + c^2 - 2bc\\cos A\n$$\n\nConsider $\\triangle ABC$ with sides of lengths $a,b,$ and $c,$ as shown below. \n\n![](images/7c/7cc2a0cbb6a02af829e070fc54cfdbe616683f86527b119a530053a34048e699.png)\n\nLet's now add the height $\\overline{CH}$ and assume it has length $h,$ as shown.\n\n![](images/d4/d4e80b2b54283ea353b687d959e9d8fc6c9303e85081c0a46b48703ee3dae7d5.png)\n\nThe remainder of this proof consists of two steps:\n\n- **Step 1**: Write down expressions for $\\cos A$ and $\\sin A.$\n\n- **Step 2**: Apply the Pythagorean theorem to $\\triangle BHC,$ and simplify.\n\nUsing elementary trigonometry, we have\n\n$$\n\\cos A = \\dfrac{AH}{b}, \\qquad \\sin A = \\dfrac{h}{b},\n$$\n\nwhich can be written as\n\n$$\nAH =  b\\cos A, \\qquad h = b\\sin A.\n$$\n\nApplying the Pythagorean theorem to $\\triangle BHC$ and using our expression for $h,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}^{2} & =(BH)^{2}+{h}^{2} \\\\ {a}^{2} & =(BH)^{2}+{b}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, consider the length of the side $\\overline{AB}.$ Since $\\overline{AB}$ consists of two segments, $\\overline{AH}$ and $\\overline{BH},$ we have\n\n$$\nc = AH + BH\\quad \\Longrightarrow\\quad BH = c - AH,\n$$\n\nand using our expression for $AH,$ we have\n\n$$\nBH = c - b\\cos A.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our expression for $a^2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}^{2} & =(BH)^{2}+{b}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡A \\\\  & =(c-b\\operatorname{cos}⁡A)^{2}+{b}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡A \\\\  & ={c}^{2}-2bc\\operatorname{cos}⁡A+{b}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡A+{b}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡A \\\\  & ={b}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡A+{b}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡A+{c}^{2}-2bc\\operatorname{cos}⁡A \\\\  & ={b}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡A+{sin}^{2}⁡A)+{c}^{2}-2bc\\operatorname{cos}⁡A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIt can be shown that $\\cos^2A + \\sin^2 A = 1$ for any angle $A$ (this is the Pythagorean theorem in trigonometric form). Therefore, we have\n\n$$\na^2 = b^2 + c^2 - 2bc\\cos A\n$$\n\nas required.\n\nWe can use a similar argument to prove the following results:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}^{2} &={a}^{2}+{c}^{2}-2ac\\operatorname{cos}⁡B \\\\ {c}^{2} &={a}^{2}+{b}^{2}-2ab\\operatorname{cos}⁡C\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let's now derive the law of cosines in the following form:\n\n$$\na^2 = b^2 + c^2 - 2bc\\cos A\n$$\n\nConsider $\\triangle ABC$ with sides of lengths $a,b,$ and $c,$ as shown below. \n\n![](images/7c/7cc2a0cbb6a02af829e070fc54cfdbe616683f86527b119a530053a34048e699.png)\n\nLet's now add the height $\\overline{CH}$ and assume it has length $h,$ as shown.\n\n![](images/d4/d4e80b2b54283ea353b687d959e9d8fc6c9303e85081c0a46b48703ee3dae7d5.png)\n\nThe remainder of this proof consists of two steps:\n\n- **Step 1**: Write down expressions for $\\cos A$ and $\\sin A.$\n\n- **Step 2**: Apply the Pythagorean theorem to $\\triangle BHC,$ and simplify.\n\nUsing elementary trigonometry, we have\n\n$$\n\\cos A = \\dfrac{AH}{b}, \\qquad \\sin A = \\dfrac{h}{b},\n$$\n\nwhich can be written as\n\n$$\nAH =  b\\cos A, \\qquad h = b\\sin A.\n$$\n\nApplying the Pythagorean theorem to $\\triangle BHC$ and using our expression for $h,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}^{2} & =(BH)^{2}+{h}^{2} \\\\ {a}^{2} & =(BH)^{2}+{b}^{2}{\\sin }^{2}⁡A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, consider the length of the side $\\overline{AB}.$ Since $\\overline{AB}$ consists of two segments, $\\overline{AH}$ and $\\overline{BH},$ we have\n\n$$\nc = AH + BH\\quad \\Longrightarrow\\quad BH = c - AH,\n$$\n\nand using our expression for $AH,$ we have\n\n$$\nBH = c - b\\cos A.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our expression for $a^2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}^{2} & =(BH)^{2}+{b}^{2}{\\sin }^{2}⁡A \\\\  & =(c-b\\operatorname{cos}⁡A)^{2}+{b}^{2}{\\sin }^{2}⁡A \\\\  & ={c}^{2}-2bc\\operatorname{cos}⁡A+{b}^{2}{\\cos }^{2}⁡A+{b}^{2}{\\sin }^{2}⁡A \\\\  & ={b}^{2}{\\cos }^{2}⁡A+{b}^{2}{\\sin }^{2}⁡A+{c}^{2}-2bc\\operatorname{cos}⁡A \\\\  & ={b}^{2}({\\cos }^{2}⁡A+{\\sin }^{2}⁡A)+{c}^{2}-2bc\\operatorname{cos}⁡A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIt can be shown that $\\cos^2A + \\sin^2 A = 1$ for any angle $A$ (this is the Pythagorean theorem in trigonometric form). Therefore, we have\n\n$$\na^2 = b^2 + c^2 - 2bc\\cos A\n$$\n\nas required.\n\nWe can use a similar argument to prove the following results:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}^{2} &={a}^{2}+{c}^{2}-2ac\\operatorname{cos}⁡B \\\\ {c}^{2} &={a}^{2}+{b}^{2}-2ab\\operatorname{cos}⁡C\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186060906 :tutorial/content "Suppose we are given the equation $\\log_3 x = 4.$ Can we determine $x?$\n\nRecall that the inverse operation of the logarithm is exponentiation. So, to isolate $x,$ we must exponentiate both sides of this equation. \n\nThe base of the logarithm is $3,$ so we use a base of $3$ for the exponentiation as well:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{3}⁡x & =4 \\\\ {3}^{({log}_{3}⁡x)} & ={3}^{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation above may look complicated, but since exponentiation is the inverse function of the logarithm, we know that $3^{(\\log_3{x})}=x.$ Therefore, we get   \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={3}^{4} \\\\ x & =81.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that $x = 81$ is the solution." "Suppose we are given the equation $\\log_3 x = 4.$ Can we determine $x?$\n\nRecall that the inverse operation of the logarithm is exponentiation. So, to isolate $x,$ we must exponentiate both sides of this equation. \n\nThe base of the logarithm is $3,$ so we use a base of $3$ for the exponentiation as well:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\log }_{3}⁡x & =4 \\\\ {3}^{({\\log }_{3}⁡x)} & ={3}^{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation above may look complicated, but since exponentiation is the inverse function of the logarithm, we know that $3^{(\\log_3{x})}=x.$ Therefore, we get   \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={3}^{4} \\\\ x & =81.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that $x = 81$ is the solution."] [:db/cas 17592186060995 :tutorial/content "Recall the following division properties of modular arithmetic for\n\n$$\nk\\neq 0{:}\n$$\n\n- The first division property states that\n$ka \\equiv kb \\quad(\\text{mod}\\,kn) \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad a \\equiv b \\quad(\\textrm{mod}\\,n)$.\n\n- The second division property states that\n$\\left(ka\\equiv kb\\:(\\textrm{mod} \\,n)\\right) \\:\\land\\: \\left(\\textrm{gcd}(k,n)=1\\right) \\quad \\Rightarrow\\quad a \\equiv b \\quad(\\textrm{mod}\\, n)$.\n\nLet's prove that the second property holds in the following case:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{3a \\equiv 3b}_{P(a,b)} \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad \\underbrace{a \\equiv b}_{Q(a,b)} \\qquad (\\textrm{mod} \\: 4)\n$$\n\nWe begin our proof by assuming $P$ is true.\n\n> *Assume* $3a \\equiv 3b\\:(\\textrm{mod} \\: 4)$.\n> *Then, by the definition of modular congruence, we have*\n> $3a≡3b(mod4) \\mid ⇔4∣(3a - 3b); ⇔4∣3(a - b)$.\n\nNow, we recall the following divisibility property of integers:\n\n$$\n(c \\mid ab) \\:\\land\\: (\\textrm{gcd}(a,c)=1) \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad (c \\mid b)\n$$\n\nUsing this fact, we have the following:\n\n> *Now, since $3(a-b)$ is divisible by $4$ and $\\textrm{gcd}(4,3)=1$, the difference $(a-b)$ must be divisible by $4$, i.e.,*\n> $4 \\mid (a-b)$.\n\nFinally, we use the definition of modular congruence one more:\n\n> *Therefore, according to the definition of modular congruence, we have*\n> $a \\equiv b \\qquad (\\textrm{mod} \\: 4)$.\n\nAnd we're done.\n\nLet's restate the full proof." "Recall the following division properties of modular arithmetic for\n\n$$\nk\\neq 0{:}\n$$\n\n- The first division property states that\n$ka \\equiv kb \\quad(\\text{mod}\\,kn) \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad a \\equiv b \\quad(\\textrm{mod}\\,n)$.\n\n- The second division property states that\n$\\left(ka\\equiv kb\\:(\\textrm{mod} \\,n)\\right) \\:\\land\\: \\left(\\textrm{gcd}(k,n)=1\\right) \\quad \\Rightarrow\\quad a \\equiv b \\quad(\\textrm{mod}\\, n)$.\n\nLet's prove that the second property holds in the following case:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{3a \\equiv 3b}_{P(a,b)} \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad \\underbrace{a \\equiv b}_{Q(a,b)} \\qquad (\\textrm{mod} \\: 4)\n$$\n\nWe begin our proof by assuming $P$ is true.\n\n> *Assume* $3a \\equiv 3b\\:(\\textrm{mod} \\: 4)$.\n> *Then, by the definition of modular congruence, we have*\n> $3a≡3b(\\bmod 4) \\mid ⇔4∣(3a - 3b); ⇔4∣3(a - b)$.\n\nNow, we recall the following divisibility property of integers:\n\n$$\n(c \\mid ab) \\:\\land\\: (\\textrm{gcd}(a,c)=1) \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad (c \\mid b)\n$$\n\nUsing this fact, we have the following:\n\n> *Now, since $3(a-b)$ is divisible by $4$ and $\\textrm{gcd}(4,3)=1$, the difference $(a-b)$ must be divisible by $4$, i.e.,*\n> $4 \\mid (a-b)$.\n\nFinally, we use the definition of modular congruence one more:\n\n> *Therefore, according to the definition of modular congruence, we have*\n> $a \\equiv b \\qquad (\\textrm{mod} \\: 4)$.\n\nAnd we're done.\n\nLet's restate the full proof."] [:db/cas 17592186061013 :tutorial/content "Proposition:\n\n$$\n3a \\equiv 3b\\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad a \\equiv b \\qquad (\\textrm{mod} \\: 4)\n$$\n\nProof:\n\n> *Assume* $3a \\equiv 3b\\:(\\textrm{mod} \\: 4)$. *Then, by the definition of modular congruence, we have*\n> $3a≡3b(mod4) \\mid ⇔4∣(3a - 3b); ⇔4∣3(a - b)$.\n> *Now, since $3(a-b)$ is divisible by $4$ and $\\textrm{gcd}(4,3)=1$, the difference $(a-b)$ must be divisible by $4$, i.e.,*\n> $4 \\mid (a-b)$.\n> *Therefore, according to the definition of modular congruence, we have*\n> $a \\equiv b \\qquad (\\textrm{mod} \\: 4)$.\n\nLet's now prove a particular instance of the first division property." "Proposition:\n\n$$\n3a \\equiv 3b\\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad a \\equiv b \\qquad (\\textrm{mod} \\: 4)\n$$\n\nProof:\n\n> *Assume* $3a \\equiv 3b\\:(\\textrm{mod} \\: 4)$. *Then, by the definition of modular congruence, we have*\n> $3a≡3b(\\bmod 4) \\mid ⇔4∣(3a - 3b); ⇔4∣3(a - b)$.\n> *Now, since $3(a-b)$ is divisible by $4$ and $\\textrm{gcd}(4,3)=1$, the difference $(a-b)$ must be divisible by $4$, i.e.,*\n> $4 \\mid (a-b)$.\n> *Therefore, according to the definition of modular congruence, we have*\n> $a \\equiv b \\qquad (\\textrm{mod} \\: 4)$.\n\nLet's now prove a particular instance of the first division property."] [:db/cas 17592186061430 :answer/value "11\\arcsecx + C" "11\\operatorname{arcsec} x + C"] [:db/cas 17592186061431 :answer/value "\\arcsecx + C" "\\operatorname{arcsec} x + C"] [:db/cas 17592186061432 :answer/value "\\arccsc(11x) + C" "\\operatorname{arccsc} (11x) + C"] [:db/cas 17592186061433 :answer/value "\\arcsec(11x) + C" "\\operatorname{arcsec} (11x) + C"] [:db/cas 17592186061434 :answer/value "11\\arccscx + C" "11\\operatorname{arccsc} x + C"] [:db/cas 17592186061436 :answer/value "3arccosx + C" "3\\arccos x + C"] [:db/cas 17592186061438 :answer/value "3arctanx + C" "3\\arctan x + C"] [:db/cas 17592186061935 :answer/value "4arcsecx + C" "4\\operatorname{arcsec} x + C"] [:db/cas 17592186061936 :answer/value "4arccotx + C" "4\\operatorname{arccot} x + C"] [:db/cas 17592186061937 :answer/value "4arccscx + C" "4\\operatorname{arccsc} x + C"] [:db/cas 17592186063703 :answer/value "(arcsec(8x))/(8) + C" "(\\operatorname{arcsec} (8x))/(8) + C"] [:db/cas 17592186063704 :answer/value "4arcsec(8x) + C" "4\\operatorname{arcsec} (8x) + C"] [:db/cas 17592186063705 :answer/value "arcsec(8x) + C" "\\operatorname{arcsec} (8x) + C"] [:db/cas 17592186063706 :answer/value "arcsec(64x) + C" "\\operatorname{arcsec} (64x) + C"] [:db/cas 17592186063707 :answer/value "(arcsec(4x))/(4) + C" "(\\operatorname{arcsec} (4x))/(4) + C"] [:db/cas 17592186063768 :answer/value "4arcsec(2x) + C" "4\\operatorname{arcsec} (2x) + C"] [:db/cas 17592186063769 :answer/value "arcsec(2x) + C" "\\operatorname{arcsec} (2x) + C"] [:db/cas 17592186063770 :answer/value "(arcsec(2x))/(2) + C" "(\\operatorname{arcsec} (2x))/(2) + C"] [:db/cas 17592186063771 :answer/value "2arcsec(2x) + C" "2\\operatorname{arcsec} (2x) + C"] [:db/cas 17592186063772 :answer/value "(arcsec(2x))/(4) + C" "(\\operatorname{arcsec} (2x))/(4) + C"] [:db/cas 17592186063941 :question/problem "Find the value of $-15 \\div 3.$" "Find the value of $-15 \\operatorname{div}  3.$"] [:db/cas 17592186063941 :question/worked-solution "Here, we are dividing a negative number $\\color{red}(-15)$ by a positive one $\\color{blue}(3).$ So the result will be negative:\n$$\n{\\color{red}(-15)} \\div {\\color{blue}3} = {\\color{red}\\mathbf{-} \\boxed{ {\\phantom{5}}} }\n$$\n\nNow, to compute the number that goes in the box, we divide $15$ by $3$ without any signs: $15 \\div 3 = 5.$ Therefore, we get\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}(-15)} \\div {\\color{blue}3} = {\\color{red}-5}.\n$$" "Here, we are dividing a negative number $\\color{red}(-15)$ by a positive one $\\color{blue}(3).$ So the result will be negative:\n$$\n{\\color{red}(-15)} \\operatorname{div}  {\\color{blue}3} = {\\color{red}\\mathbf{-} \\boxed{ {\\phantom{5}}} }\n$$\n\nNow, to compute the number that goes in the box, we divide $15$ by $3$ without any signs: $15 \\operatorname{div}  3 = 5.$ Therefore, we get\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}(-15)} \\operatorname{div}  {\\color{blue}3} = {\\color{red}-5}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186063942 :question/problem "Calculate the value of $50 \\div (-5).$" "Calculate the value of $50 \\operatorname{div}  (-5).$"] [:db/cas 17592186063942 :question/worked-solution "We have to divide a positive number $\\color{blue}(50)$ by a negative one $\\color{red}(-5).$ So the result will be negative:\n$$\n{\\color{blue}{50}} \\div {\\color{red}{(-5)}} = {\\color{red}{\\mathbf{-} \\boxed{ {\\phantom{10}}} }}\n$$\n\nNow, to compute the number that goes in the box, we divide $50$ by $5$ without any signs: $50 \\div 5 = 10.$ Therefore,  we get\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}{50}} \\div {\\color{red}{(-5)}} = {\\color{red}{-10}}.\n$$" "We have to divide a positive number $\\color{blue}(50)$ by a negative one $\\color{red}(-5).$ So the result will be negative:\n$$\n{\\color{blue}{50}} \\operatorname{div}  {\\color{red}{(-5)}} = {\\color{red}{\\mathbf{-} \\boxed{ {\\phantom{10}}} }}\n$$\n\nNow, to compute the number that goes in the box, we divide $50$ by $5$ without any signs: $50 \\operatorname{div}  5 = 10.$ Therefore,  we get\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}{50}} \\operatorname{div}  {\\color{red}{(-5)}} = {\\color{red}{-10}}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186063943 :question/problem "Calculate $-81\\div (-9).$" "Calculate $-81\\operatorname{div}  (-9).$"] [:db/cas 17592186063943 :question/worked-solution "We have to divide a negative number ${\\color{red}(-81)}$ by another negative number ${\\color{red}(-9)}.$ So the result will be positive:\n$$\n{\\color{red}(-81)} \\div {\\color{red}(-9)} = {\\color{blue} \\boxed{ {\\phantom{9}}} }\n$$\n\nNow, to compute the number that goes in the box, we divide $81$ by $9$ without any signs: $81 \\div 9 = 9.$ Therefore,  we get\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}(-81)} \\div {\\color{red}(-9)} = {\\color{blue}9}.\n$$" "We have to divide a negative number ${\\color{red}(-81)}$ by another negative number ${\\color{red}(-9)}.$ So the result will be positive:\n$$\n{\\color{red}(-81)} \\operatorname{div}  {\\color{red}(-9)} = {\\color{blue} \\boxed{ {\\phantom{9}}} }\n$$\n\nNow, to compute the number that goes in the box, we divide $81$ by $9$ without any signs: $81 \\operatorname{div}  9 = 9.$ Therefore,  we get\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}(-81)} \\operatorname{div}  {\\color{red}(-9)} = {\\color{blue}9}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186063949 :question/worked-solution "We can use either of the following two methods.\n\n**Method 1 - The Long Way** \n\nFirst, remember that a fraction represents a division:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\left(-\\dfrac 3 2\\right)}{6} = \\left(-\\dfrac 3 2\\right)  \\div 6\n$$\n\nNow, recall that dividing by $6$ is the same as multiplying by its reciprocal $\\dfrac{1}{6}.$ We can use this fact to simplify the fraction above: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left(-\\frac{3}{2}\\right)÷6 &= \\left(-\\frac{3}{2}\\right)\\times \\left(\\frac{1}{6}\\right) \\\\ &= -\\frac{3\\times 1}{2\\times 6} \\\\ &= -\\frac{3}{12}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify:\n\n$$\n- \\dfrac{3}{12}  = - \\dfrac{3 \\div 3}{12 \\div 3}  = -\\dfrac{1}{4}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\left(-\\dfrac 3 2\\right)}{6}= -\\dfrac14\n$$\n\n \n\n**Method 2 - The Quick Way**\n\nThe result will have the same numerator as the top fraction, and the denominator is just the product of the two denominators: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\left(-\\frac{3}{2}\\right)}{6} &= \\frac{-3}{2\\times 6} \\\\ &= -\\frac{3}{12} \\\\ &= -\\frac{3÷3}{12÷3} \\\\ &= -\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We can use either of the following two methods.\n\n**Method 1 - The Long Way** \n\nFirst, remember that a fraction represents a division:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\left(-\\dfrac 3 2\\right)}{6} = \\left(-\\dfrac 3 2\\right)  \\operatorname{div}  6\n$$\n\nNow, recall that dividing by $6$ is the same as multiplying by its reciprocal $\\dfrac{1}{6}.$ We can use this fact to simplify the fraction above: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left(-\\frac{3}{2}\\right)÷6 &= \\left(-\\frac{3}{2}\\right)\\times \\left(\\frac{1}{6}\\right) \\\\ &= -\\frac{3\\times 1}{2\\times 6} \\\\ &= -\\frac{3}{12}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify:\n\n$$\n- \\dfrac{3}{12}  = - \\dfrac{3 \\operatorname{div}  3}{12 \\operatorname{div}  3}  = -\\dfrac{1}{4}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\left(-\\dfrac 3 2\\right)}{6}= -\\dfrac14\n$$\n\n \n\n**Method 2 - The Quick Way**\n\nThe result will have the same numerator as the top fraction, and the denominator is just the product of the two denominators: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\left(-\\frac{3}{2}\\right)}{6} &= \\frac{-3}{2\\times 6} \\\\ &= -\\frac{3}{12} \\\\ &= -\\frac{3÷3}{12÷3} \\\\ &= -\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186064034 :question/worked-solution "From the definition of $h(x),$ we have that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 0}h(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}{log}_{3}⁡(x+c) \\\\  & ={log}_{3}⁡(0+c) \\\\  & ={log}_{3}⁡(c).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order for $h(x)$ to be continuous at $x=0,$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 0}h(x) & =h(0) \\\\ {log}_{3}⁡(c) & =2 \\\\ c & ={3}^{2} \\\\ c & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting $c=9$ removes the point discontinuity." "From the definition of $h(x),$ we have that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 0}h(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}{\\log }_{3}⁡(x+c) \\\\  & ={\\log }_{3}⁡(0+c) \\\\  & ={\\log }_{3}⁡(c).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order for $h(x)$ to be continuous at $x=0,$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 0}h(x) & =h(0) \\\\ {\\log }_{3}⁡(c) & =2 \\\\ c & ={3}^{2} \\\\ c & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting $c=9$ removes the point discontinuity."] [:db/cas 17592186064052 :question/worked-solution "Recall that\n\n$$\n\\int \\sin x \\, \\textrm dx = -\\cos x + C.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 3sin⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =3\\int sin⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3(-cos⁡x)+C \\\\  & =-3cos⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Recall that\n\n$$\n\\int \\sin x \\, \\textrm dx = -\\cos x + C.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 3\\sin ⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =3\\int \\sin ⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3(-\\cos ⁡x)+C \\\\  & =-3\\cos ⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186064053 :question/worked-solution "Recall that\n\n$$\n\\int \\csc x \\cot x \\, \\textrm dx = - \\csc x + C.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (-csc⁡xcot⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\int (csc⁡xcot⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-(-csc⁡x)+C \\\\  & =csc⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Recall that\n\n$$\n\\int \\csc x \\cot x \\, \\textrm dx = - \\csc x + C.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (-\\csc ⁡x\\cot ⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\int (\\csc ⁡x\\cot ⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-(-\\csc ⁡x)+C \\\\  & =\\csc ⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186064055 :question/worked-solution "Recall that \n\n$$\n\\int \\csc^2 x \\, \\textrm dx = -\\cot x + C\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\int \\csc x \\cot x \\, \\textrm dx = -\\csc x + C.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int csc⁡x(csc⁡x+3cot⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {csc}^{2}⁡x+3csc⁡xcot⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {csc}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x+3\\int csc⁡xcot⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-cot⁡x-3csc⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Recall that \n\n$$\n\\int \\csc^2 x \\, \\textrm dx = -\\cot x + C\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\int \\csc x \\cot x \\, \\textrm dx = -\\csc x + C.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\csc ⁡x(\\csc ⁡x+3\\cot ⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {\\csc }^{2}⁡x+3\\csc ⁡x\\cot ⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {\\csc }^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x+3\\int \\csc ⁡x\\cot ⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\cot ⁡x-3\\csc ⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186064059 :question/worked-solution "Recall that\n\n$$\n\\int \\dfrac{1}{|x|\\sqrt{x^2 - 1}} \\, \\textrm dx = \\textrm{arcsec} \\, x.\n$$\n\nThen we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\int {f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (\\frac{5}{|x|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}}-2x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{5}{|x|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x-\\int 2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =5\\int \\frac{1}{|x|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x-2\\int x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =5\\text{arcsec}\\,(x)-2\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+C \\\\  & =5\\text{arcsec}\\,(x)-{x}^{2}+C.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Recall that\n\n$$\n\\int \\dfrac{1}{|x|\\sqrt{x^2 - 1}} \\, \\textrm dx = \\operatorname{arcsec}  \\, x.\n$$\n\nThen we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\int {f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (\\frac{5}{|x|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}}-2x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{5}{|x|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x-\\int 2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =5\\int \\frac{1}{|x|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x-2\\int x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =5\\operatorname{arcsec} \\,(x)-2\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+C \\\\  & =5\\operatorname{arcsec} \\,(x)-{x}^{2}+C.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186064087 :question/problem "Calculate the value of $0 \\div 5$." "Calculate the value of $0 \\operatorname{div}  5$."] [:db/cas 17592186064087 :question/worked-solution "Zero divided by any number (other than zero) equals zero. Therefore,\n\n$$\n0 \\div 5 = 0\n$$" "Zero divided by any number (other than zero) equals zero. Therefore,\n\n$$\n0 \\operatorname{div}  5 = 0\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186064088 :question/problem "Compute the value of $0 \\div \\left(-\\dfrac 1 2\\right)$." "Compute the value of $0 \\operatorname{div}  \\left(-\\dfrac 1 2\\right)$."] [:db/cas 17592186064088 :question/worked-solution "Zero divided by any number (other than zero) equals zero. Therefore,\n\n$$\n0 \\div \\left(-\\dfrac 1 2\\right) = 0\n$$" "Zero divided by any number (other than zero) equals zero. Therefore,\n\n$$\n0 \\operatorname{div}  \\left(-\\dfrac 1 2\\right) = 0\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186064089 :question/problem "What is $-2 \\div 0$?" "What is $-2 \\operatorname{div}  0$?"] [:db/cas 17592186064089 :question/worked-solution "We cannot divide any number by zero. Therefore, $-2 \\div 0$ is undefined." "We cannot divide any number by zero. Therefore, $-2 \\operatorname{div}  0$ is undefined."] [:db/cas 17592186064093 :question/worked-solution "We multiply out the parentheses and then use the identity $\\dfrac{1}{\\sin x } = \\csc x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}(\\operatorname{cot}⁡x+{sin}^{2}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\cdot \\operatorname{cot}⁡x+\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\cdot {sin}^{2}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\cdot \\operatorname{cot}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\operatorname{cot}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We multiply out the parentheses and then use the identity $\\dfrac{1}{\\sin x } = \\csc x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}(\\operatorname{cot}⁡x+{\\sin }^{2}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\cdot \\operatorname{cot}⁡x+\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\cdot {\\sin }^{2}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\cdot \\operatorname{cot}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\operatorname{cot}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186064094 :question/worked-solution "We can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\int_0^{\\pi/3} \\dfrac{\\sin x}{\\cos^2 x}\\,\\textrm d x =  \\int_0^{\\pi/3} \\dfrac{1}{\\cos x}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\sin x}{\\cos x}\\,\\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nWe then use the fact that $\\dfrac{\\sin x }{\\cos x}  = \\tan x$ and $\\dfrac{1}{\\cos x} = \\sec x$ to express this as\n\n$$\n\\int_0^{\\pi/3} \\sec x\\tan x\\,\\textrm d x,\n$$\n\nwhich is a standard trigonometric integral. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡x{|}_{0}^{\\pi /3} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)-\\operatorname{sec}⁡(0) \\\\  & =2-1 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\int_0^{\\pi/3} \\dfrac{\\sin x}{\\cos^2 x}\\,\\textrm d x =  \\int_0^{\\pi/3} \\dfrac{1}{\\cos x}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\sin x}{\\cos x}\\,\\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nWe then use the fact that $\\dfrac{\\sin x }{\\cos x}  = \\tan x$ and $\\dfrac{1}{\\cos x} = \\sec x$ to express this as\n\n$$\n\\int_0^{\\pi/3} \\sec x\\tan x\\,\\textrm d x,\n$$\n\nwhich is a standard trigonometric integral. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{{\\cos }^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡x{|}_{0}^{\\pi /3} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)-\\operatorname{sec}⁡(0) \\\\  & =2-1 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186064101 :question/worked-solution "This resembles the basic integral for $\\textrm{arcsec},$\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{u^2-1}} \\textrm{d}u = \\textrm{arcsec}(u)+C.\n$$\n\nLet $u = 5x.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}=5\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n5\\,\\textrm{d}x = \\textrm{d}u.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{5\\,\\text{d}x}{|5x|\\sqrt{(5x)^{2}-1}} & =\\int \\frac{\\text{d}u}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}} \\\\  & =\\text{arcsec}(u)+C \\\\  & =\\text{arcsec}(5x)+C.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "This resembles the basic integral for $\\operatorname{arcsec} ,$\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{u^2-1}} \\textrm{d}u = \\operatorname{arcsec} (u)+C.\n$$\n\nLet $u = 5x.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}=5\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n5\\,\\textrm{d}x = \\textrm{d}u.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{5\\,\\text{d}x}{|5x|\\sqrt{(5x)^{2}-1}} & =\\int \\frac{\\text{d}u}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arcsec} (u)+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arcsec} (5x)+C.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186064127 :question/worked-solution "First, we write\n\n$$\nf(x) = (\\sin{x})^3.\n$$\n\nNow, we see that $f(x)$ can be expressed as the composite function\n\n$$\nf = u^3, \\qquad u=\\sin x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d f}{\\textrm d u} = 3u^2, \\qquad \\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = \\cos{x}.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =3{u}^{2}\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\  & =3(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\  & =3{sin}^{2}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we write\n\n$$\nf(x) = (\\sin{x})^3.\n$$\n\nNow, we see that $f(x)$ can be expressed as the composite function\n\n$$\nf = u^3, \\qquad u=\\sin x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d f}{\\textrm d u} = 3u^2, \\qquad \\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = \\cos{x}.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =3{u}^{2}\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\  & =3(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\  & =3{\\sin }^{2}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186064128 :question/worked-solution "![](images/11/113109cb44fd2d10cd3b88324ed58d2ff6455a25c81b4f62eb1862b7ced03b52.png)\n\nIn the figure, $y$ is the distance between the boat and the bank and $x$ is the horizontal distance traveled by that boat after passing Peter. The quantity $y=10$ meters does not change with time because the boat is at a constant distance from the bank.\n\nWe wish to calculate $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}\\theta}{\\textrm{d}t}.$\nTo do this, let's start by setting up a relation between $x$ and $y.$ Relative to the angle $\\theta,$ the sides $x$ and $y$ are the adjacent and opposite, respectively. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{y}{x} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{10}{x} \\\\ x & =10\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(10\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta ) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =(-10{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the given information, we have $\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t}=8$ and $\\theta = \\dfrac{\\pi}6.$ Substituting this into the above, we reach\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8 & =(-10{csc}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right))\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} \\\\ -\\frac{4}{5} & ={csc}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} \\\\ -\\frac{4}{5} & ={(2)}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} \\\\ -\\frac{4}{5} & =4\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the rate at which the angle $\\theta$ is decreasing is $\\dfrac{1}{5}$ radians per second." "![](images/11/113109cb44fd2d10cd3b88324ed58d2ff6455a25c81b4f62eb1862b7ced03b52.png)\n\nIn the figure, $y$ is the distance between the boat and the bank and $x$ is the horizontal distance traveled by that boat after passing Peter. The quantity $y=10$ meters does not change with time because the boat is at a constant distance from the bank.\n\nWe wish to calculate $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}\\theta}{\\textrm{d}t}.$\nTo do this, let's start by setting up a relation between $x$ and $y.$ Relative to the angle $\\theta,$ the sides $x$ and $y$ are the adjacent and opposite, respectively. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{y}{x} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{10}{x} \\\\ x & =10\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(10\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta ) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =(-10{\\csc }^{2}⁡\\theta )\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the given information, we have $\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t}=8$ and $\\theta = \\dfrac{\\pi}6.$ Substituting this into the above, we reach\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8 & =(-10{\\csc }^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right))\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} \\\\ -\\frac{4}{5} & ={\\csc }^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} \\\\ -\\frac{4}{5} & ={(2)}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} \\\\ -\\frac{4}{5} & =4\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the rate at which the angle $\\theta$ is decreasing is $\\dfrac{1}{5}$ radians per second."] [:db/cas 17592186064129 :question/worked-solution "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny = \\tan u, \\qquad u = \\sin x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d u} = \\sec^2 u,\\qquad \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm d x} = \\cos x.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={sec}^{2}⁡u\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\  & ={sec}^{2}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny = \\tan u, \\qquad u = \\sin x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d u} = \\sec^2 u,\\qquad \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm d x} = \\cos x.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={\\sec }^{2}⁡u\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\  & ={\\sec }^{2}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186064136 :question/problem "$\\newcommand{\\arsinh}{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcosh}{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\artanh}{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arsech}{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$\n\nCalculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int 8\\cosh t \\,\\textrm{d}t.\n$$" "$\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsinh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcosh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{artanh} }{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{sech} }{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{csch} }{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsech} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcsch} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcoth} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$\n\nCalculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int 8\\cosh t \\,\\textrm{d}t.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186064136 :question/worked-solution "Recall that\n\n$$\n\\newcommand{\\arsinh}{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcosh}{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\artanh}{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arsech}{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d}{\\textrm d t}(\\sinh t) = \\cosh{t} \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad  \\int \\cosh{t} \\, \\textrm dt = \\sinh{t} + C.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 8\\operatorname{cosh}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t=8\\int \\operatorname{cosh}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t=8\\operatorname{sinh}⁡t+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Recall that\n\n$$\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsinh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcosh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{artanh} }{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{sech} }{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{csch} }{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsech} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcsch} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcoth} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d}{\\textrm d t}(\\sinh t) = \\cosh{t} \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad  \\int \\cosh{t} \\, \\textrm dt = \\sinh{t} + C.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 8\\operatorname{cosh}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t=8\\int \\operatorname{cosh}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t=8\\operatorname{sinh}⁡t+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186064137 :question/problem "$\\newcommand{\\arsinh}{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcosh}{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\artanh}{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arsech}{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\left(-3\\sech x \\tanh x\\right) \\,\\textrm{d}x =\n$$" "$\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsinh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcosh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{artanh} }{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{sech} }{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{csch} }{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsech} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcsch} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcoth} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\left(-3\\operatorname{sech}  x \\tanh x\\right) \\,\\textrm{d}x =\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186064137 :question/worked-solution "$\\newcommand{\\arsinh}{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcosh}{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\artanh}{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arsech}{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$\n\nRecall that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d}{\\textrm d x}(\\sech x) = -\\sech{x}\\tanh x \n\\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad  \n\\int \\sech{x}\\tanh{x} \\, \\textrm dx = -\\sech{x} + C.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (-3\\operatorname{sech}⁡x\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-3\\int \\operatorname{sech}⁡x\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-3(-\\operatorname{sech}⁡x)+C \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{sech}⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "$\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsinh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcosh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{artanh} }{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{sech} }{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{csch} }{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsech} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcsch} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcoth} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$\n\nRecall that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d}{\\textrm d x}(\\operatorname{sech}  x) = -\\operatorname{sech} {x}\\tanh x \n\\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad  \n\\int \\operatorname{sech} {x}\\tanh{x} \\, \\textrm dx = -\\operatorname{sech} {x} + C.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (-3\\operatorname{sech}⁡x\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-3\\int \\operatorname{sech}⁡x\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-3(-\\operatorname{sech}⁡x)+C \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{sech}⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186064138 :question/worked-solution "$\\newcommand{\\arsinh}{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcosh}{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\artanh}{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arsech}{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$\n\nRecall that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d}{\\textrm d x}(\\tanh x) = \\sech^2{x} \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad  \\int \\sech^2{x} \\, \\textrm dx = \\tanh{x} + C.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 6{sech}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=6\\int {sech}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=6\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "$\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsinh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcosh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{artanh} }{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{sech} }{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{csch} }{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsech} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcsch} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcoth} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$\n\nRecall that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d}{\\textrm d x}(\\tanh x) = \\operatorname{sech} ^2{x} \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad  \\int \\operatorname{sech} ^2{x} \\, \\textrm dx = \\tanh{x} + C.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 6{\\operatorname{sech} }^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=6\\int {\\operatorname{sech} }^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=6\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186064138 :question/problem "$\\newcommand{\\arsinh}{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcosh}{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\artanh}{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arsech}{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int 6\\sech^2 x \\,\\textrm{d}x =\n$$" "$\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsinh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcosh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{artanh} }{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{sech} }{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{csch} }{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsech} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcsch} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcoth} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int 6\\operatorname{sech} ^2 x \\,\\textrm{d}x =\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186064139 :question/worked-solution "$\\newcommand{\\arsinh}{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcosh}{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\artanh}{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arsech}{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$\n\nRecall that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d}{\\textrm d x}(\\coth x) = -\\csch^2{x} \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad  \\int \\csch^2{x} \\, \\textrm dx = -\\coth{x} + C.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (-8{csch}^{2}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-8\\int {csch}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-(-8\\operatorname{coth}⁡x)+C \\\\  & =8\\operatorname{coth}⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "$\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsinh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcosh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{artanh} }{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{sech} }{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{csch} }{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsech} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcsch} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcoth} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$\n\nRecall that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d}{\\textrm d x}(\\coth x) = -\\operatorname{csch} ^2{x} \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad  \\int \\operatorname{csch} ^2{x} \\, \\textrm dx = -\\coth{x} + C.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (-8{\\operatorname{csch} }^{2}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-8\\int {\\operatorname{csch} }^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-(-8\\operatorname{coth}⁡x)+C \\\\  & =8\\operatorname{coth}⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186064139 :question/problem "$\\newcommand{\\arsinh}{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcosh}{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\artanh}{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arsech}{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int (-8\\csch^2 x )\\,\\textrm{d}x =\n$$" "$\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsinh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcosh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{artanh} }{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{sech} }{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{csch} }{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsech} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcsch} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcoth} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int (-8\\operatorname{csch} ^2 x )\\,\\textrm{d}x =\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186064489 :question/problem "Expressed as a mixed number, $(-25) \\div 6 =$" "Expressed as a mixed number, $(-25) \\operatorname{div}  6 =$"] [:db/cas 17592186064491 :question/problem "Expressed as a mixed number, $(-15) \\div (-4) =$" "Expressed as a mixed number, $(-15) \\operatorname{div}  (-4) =$"] [:db/cas 17592186064539 :question/worked-solution "Recall that\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{|x|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\text{arcsec}\\,x.\n$$\n\nThen we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{11}{|x|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x & =11\\int \\frac{1}{|x|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =11\\operatorname{arcsec} x+C.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Recall that\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{|x|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{arcsec} \\,x.\n$$\n\nThen we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{11}{|x|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x & =11\\int \\frac{1}{|x|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =11\\operatorname{arcsec} x+C.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186064549 :question/problem "What is $(-1) \\div (-1)$?" "What is $(-1) \\operatorname{div}  (-1)$?"] [:db/cas 17592186064551 :question/problem "What is $9 \\div (-3)$?" "What is $9 \\operatorname{div}  (-3)$?"] [:db/cas 17592186064552 :question/problem "What is $0 \\div 5$?" "What is $0 \\operatorname{div}  5$?"] [:db/cas 17592186064553 :question/problem "What is $1 \\div 0$?" "What is $1 \\operatorname{div}  0$?"] [:db/cas 17592186064638 :question/problem "${log}_{4}⁡1=$" "${\\log }_{4}⁡1=$"] [:db/cas 17592186064638 :question/worked-solution "We have the general rule that:\n\n$$\n{log}_{n}⁡1=0\n$$\n\nIn our case, $n=4.$ Therefore:\n\n$$\n{log}_{4}⁡1=0\n$$" "We have the general rule that:\n\n$$\n{\\log }_{n}⁡1=0\n$$\n\nIn our case, $n=4.$ Therefore:\n\n$$\n{\\log }_{4}⁡1=0\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186064763 :question/problem "Expressed as a fraction, $(-9) \\div (-4) =$" "Expressed as a fraction, $(-9) \\operatorname{div}  (-4) =$"] [:db/cas 17592186064764 :question/problem "Expressed as a fraction, $(-7) \\div 3 =$" "Expressed as a fraction, $(-7) \\operatorname{div}  3 =$"] [:db/cas 17592186064768 :question/problem "What is $0 \\div 16$?" "What is $0 \\operatorname{div}  16$?"] [:db/cas 17592186064770 :question/problem "Compute the value of $0 \\div \\frac{3}{5}$." "Compute the value of $0 \\operatorname{div}  \\frac{3}{5}$."] [:db/cas 17592186064771 :question/problem "Calculate the value of $0 \\div \\frac{3}{4}$." "Calculate the value of $0 \\operatorname{div}  \\frac{3}{4}$."] [:db/cas 17592186064857 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡x}({cot}^{2}⁡x+{cos}^{2}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{1}{{\\cos }^{2}⁡x}({\\cot }^{2}⁡x+{\\cos }^{2}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186064857 :question/worked-solution "We multiply out the parentheses and then use the identity ${cot}^{2}⁡x=\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡x}{{sin}^{2}⁡x},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡x}({cot}^{2}⁡x+{cos}^{2}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\cdot {cot}^{2}⁡x+\\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\cdot {cos}^{2}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (\\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\cdot \\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡x}{{sin}^{2}⁡x}+\\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\cdot {cos}^{2}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (\\frac{1}{{sin}^{2}⁡x}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int ({csc}^{2}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {csc}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cot}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We multiply out the parentheses and then use the identity ${\\cot }^{2}⁡x=\\frac{{\\cos }^{2}⁡x}{{\\sin }^{2}⁡x},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{{\\cos }^{2}⁡x}({\\cot }^{2}⁡x+{\\cos }^{2}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{1}{{\\cos }^{2}⁡x}\\cdot {\\cot }^{2}⁡x+\\frac{1}{{\\cos }^{2}⁡x}\\cdot {\\cos }^{2}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (\\frac{1}{{\\cos }^{2}⁡x}\\cdot \\frac{{\\cos }^{2}⁡x}{{\\sin }^{2}⁡x}+\\frac{1}{{\\cos }^{2}⁡x}\\cdot {\\cos }^{2}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (\\frac{1}{{\\sin }^{2}⁡x}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int ({\\csc }^{2}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {\\csc }^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cot}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186064858 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{{cos}^{3}⁡x+{cot}^{2}⁡x}{{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{{\\cos }^{3}⁡x+{\\cot }^{2}⁡x}{{\\cos }^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186064858 :question/worked-solution "We can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{{cos}^{3}⁡x+{cot}^{2}⁡x}{{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int \\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡x}({cos}^{3}⁡x+{cot}^{2}⁡x)\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe then use the fact that ${cot}^{2}⁡x=\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡x}{{sin}^{2}⁡x}$ to express this as\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡x}({cos}^{3}⁡x+\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡x}{{sin}^{2}⁡x})\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{cos}^{3}⁡x+{cot}^{2}⁡x}{{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡x}({cos}^{3}⁡x+\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡x}{{sin}^{2}⁡x})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (\\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\cdot {cos}^{3}⁡x+\\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\cdot \\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡x}{{sin}^{2}⁡x})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\frac{1}{{sin}^{2}⁡x})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+{csc}^{2}⁡x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{cot}⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{{\\cos }^{3}⁡x+{\\cot }^{2}⁡x}{{\\cos }^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int \\frac{1}{{\\cos }^{2}⁡x}({\\cos }^{3}⁡x+{\\cot }^{2}⁡x)\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe then use the fact that ${\\cot }^{2}⁡x=\\frac{{\\cos }^{2}⁡x}{{\\sin }^{2}⁡x}$ to express this as\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{{\\cos }^{2}⁡x}({\\cos }^{3}⁡x+\\frac{{\\cos }^{2}⁡x}{{\\sin }^{2}⁡x})\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{\\cos }^{3}⁡x+{\\cot }^{2}⁡x}{{\\cos }^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{{\\cos }^{2}⁡x}({\\cos }^{3}⁡x+\\frac{{\\cos }^{2}⁡x}{{\\sin }^{2}⁡x})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (\\frac{1}{{\\cos }^{2}⁡x}\\cdot {\\cos }^{3}⁡x+\\frac{1}{{\\cos }^{2}⁡x}\\cdot \\frac{{\\cos }^{2}⁡x}{{\\sin }^{2}⁡x})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\frac{1}{{\\sin }^{2}⁡x})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+{\\csc }^{2}⁡x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{cot}⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186064859 :question/problem "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{{tan}^{2}⁡x}{8{sin}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{{\\tan }^{2}⁡x}{8{\\sin }^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186064859 :question/worked-solution "We can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{{tan}^{2}⁡x}{8{sin}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{8}\\int {tan}^{2}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{1}{{sin}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe then use the fact that $$\n\\displaystyle \\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\n$$ and $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{tan}^{2}⁡x}{8{sin}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{8}\\int {tan}^{2}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{1}{{sin}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}\\int {\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\right)}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{sin}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}\\int \\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡x}{{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{sin}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}\\int \\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}\\int {\\left(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\right)}^{2}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}\\int {sec}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{{\\tan }^{2}⁡x}{8{\\sin }^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{8}\\int {\\tan }^{2}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{1}{{\\sin }^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe then use the fact that $$\n\\displaystyle \\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\n$$ and $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{\\tan }^{2}⁡x}{8{\\sin }^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{8}\\int {\\tan }^{2}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{1}{{\\sin }^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}\\int {\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\right)}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{\\sin }^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}\\int \\frac{{\\sin }^{2}⁡x}{{\\cos }^{2}⁡x}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{\\sin }^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}\\int \\frac{1}{{\\cos }^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}\\int {\\left(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\right)}^{2}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}\\int {\\sec }^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186064932 :question/worked-solution "To compute the integral of a general exponential function, we just need to divide by the natural logarithm of the base of the exponential function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {3}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{{3}^{x}}{ln⁡3}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" "To compute the integral of a general exponential function, we just need to divide by the natural logarithm of the base of the exponential function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {3}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{{3}^{x}}{\\ln ⁡3}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186064941 :question/worked-solution "Note that we can rewrite this integral as\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{8\\,\\text{d}x}{|8x|\\sqrt{(8x)^{2}-1}}=8\\int \\frac{\\text{d}x}{|8x|\\sqrt{(8x)^{2}-1}}.\n$$\n\nThis integral now resembles the basic one for $\\text{arcsec},$\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}}\\text{d}u=\\text{arcsec}(u)+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u$ as the variable, we have $8x.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=8x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=8\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{8}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}8\\int \\frac{1}{|8x|\\sqrt{(8x)^{2}-1}}\\text{d}x & =8\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{8}\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{\\text{d}u}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}} \\\\  & =\\text{arcsec }u+C \\\\  & =\\text{arcsec}(8x)+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Note that we can rewrite this integral as\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{8\\,\\text{d}x}{|8x|\\sqrt{(8x)^{2}-1}}=8\\int \\frac{\\text{d}x}{|8x|\\sqrt{(8x)^{2}-1}}.\n$$\n\nThis integral now resembles the basic one for $\\operatorname{arcsec} ,$\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}}\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arcsec} (u)+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u$ as the variable, we have $8x.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=8x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=8\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{8}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}8\\int \\frac{1}{|8x|\\sqrt{(8x)^{2}-1}}\\text{d}x & =8\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{8}\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{\\text{d}u}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arcsec} u+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arcsec} (8x)+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186064948 :question/problem "Find ${f}^{′}(x)$ when $f(x)=9{sin}^{2}⁡(x).$" "Find ${f}^{′}(x)$ when $f(x)=9{\\sin }^{2}⁡(x).$"] [:db/cas 17592186064952 :question/worked-solution "Note that we can rewrite this integral as\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{2\\,\\text{d}x}{|x|\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}-1}}=4\\int \\frac{\\text{d}x}{|2x|\\sqrt{(2x)^{2}-1}}.\n$$\n\nThis integral now resembles the basic one for $\\text{arcsec},$\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}}\\text{d}u=\\text{arcsec}(u)+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u$ as the variable, we have $2x.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=2x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2}{|x|\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x & =4\\int \\frac{1}{|2x|\\sqrt{(2x)^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =2\\int \\frac{\\text{d}u}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}} \\\\  & =2\\,\\text{arcsec}\\,u+C \\\\  & =2\\,\\text{arcsec}(2x)+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Note that we can rewrite this integral as\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{2\\,\\text{d}x}{|x|\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}-1}}=4\\int \\frac{\\text{d}x}{|2x|\\sqrt{(2x)^{2}-1}}.\n$$\n\nThis integral now resembles the basic one for $\\operatorname{arcsec} ,$\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}}\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arcsec} (u)+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u$ as the variable, we have $2x.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=2x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2}{|x|\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x & =4\\int \\frac{1}{|2x|\\sqrt{(2x)^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =2\\int \\frac{\\text{d}u}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}} \\\\  & =2\\,\\operatorname{arcsec} \\,u+C \\\\  & =2\\,\\operatorname{arcsec} (2x)+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186064969 :question/problem "$$\n\\int 2\\pi {sech}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$" "$$\n\\int 2\\pi {\\operatorname{sech} }^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186064969 :question/worked-solution "Recall that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x)={sech}^{2}⁡x\\,⇒\\,\\int {sech}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x+C.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 2\\pi {sech}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =2\\pi \\int {sech}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi \\operatorname{tanh}⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Recall that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x)={\\operatorname{sech} }^{2}⁡x\\,⇒\\,\\int {\\operatorname{sech} }^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x+C.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 2\\pi {\\operatorname{sech} }^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =2\\pi \\int {\\operatorname{sech} }^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi \\operatorname{tanh}⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186064971 :question/problem "$$\n\\int 4{csch}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$" "$$\n\\int 4{\\operatorname{csch} }^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186064971 :question/worked-solution "Recall that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{coth}⁡x)=-{csch}^{2}⁡x\\,⇒\\,\\int {csch}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=-\\operatorname{coth}⁡x+C.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 4{csch}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =4\\int {csch}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-4\\operatorname{coth}⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Recall that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{coth}⁡x)=-{\\operatorname{csch} }^{2}⁡x\\,⇒\\,\\int {\\operatorname{csch} }^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=-\\operatorname{coth}⁡x+C.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 4{\\operatorname{csch} }^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =4\\int {\\operatorname{csch} }^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-4\\operatorname{coth}⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186064973 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int (5{csch}^{2}⁡x-\\operatorname{sech}⁡x\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int (5{\\operatorname{csch} }^{2}⁡x-\\operatorname{sech}⁡x\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186064973 :question/worked-solution "Recall that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sech}⁡x)=-\\operatorname{sech}⁡x\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x & \\,⇒\\,\\int \\operatorname{sech}⁡x\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=-\\operatorname{sech}⁡x+C, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{coth}⁡x)=-{csch}^{2}⁡x & \\,⇒\\,\\int {csch}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=-\\operatorname{coth}⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (5{csch}^{2}⁡x-\\operatorname{sech}⁡x\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x & =5\\int {csch}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x-\\int \\operatorname{sech}⁡x\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =5(-\\operatorname{coth}⁡x)-(-\\operatorname{sech}⁡x)+C \\\\  & =-5\\operatorname{coth}⁡x+\\operatorname{sech}⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Recall that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sech}⁡x)=-\\operatorname{sech}⁡x\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x & \\,⇒\\,\\int \\operatorname{sech}⁡x\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=-\\operatorname{sech}⁡x+C, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{coth}⁡x)=-{\\operatorname{csch} }^{2}⁡x & \\,⇒\\,\\int {\\operatorname{csch} }^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=-\\operatorname{coth}⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (5{\\operatorname{csch} }^{2}⁡x-\\operatorname{sech}⁡x\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x & =5\\int {\\operatorname{csch} }^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x-\\int \\operatorname{sech}⁡x\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =5(-\\operatorname{coth}⁡x)-(-\\operatorname{sech}⁡x)+C \\\\  & =-5\\operatorname{coth}⁡x+\\operatorname{sech}⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186065360 :tutorial/content "Any number raised to the power of zero is equal to $1.$ For example, \n\n$$\n2^0 = 1 \\quad\\textrm{and}\\quad (-55)^0=1.\n$$\n\nTo understand why, let's try to convince ourselves that $2^0 = 1.$ First, we write down a table containing all of the powers of $2$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{4} & =16 \\\\ {2}^{3} & =8 \\\\ {2}^{2} & =4 \\\\ {2}^{1} & =2 \\\\ {2}^{0} & =◻\\,?\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs we go down the list, the number in blue is divided by $2$ each time. Following this pattern, we must have \n\n$$\n2^{\\color{red}{0}}  = {\\color{blue}{2}}\\div 2 = 1.\n$$\n\nIt doesn't matter whether the number is positive or negative, a fraction or a decimal, big or small. Raising any non-zero number to the power of zero always gives $1.$" "Any number raised to the power of zero is equal to $1.$ For example, \n\n$$\n2^0 = 1 \\quad\\textrm{and}\\quad (-55)^0=1.\n$$\n\nTo understand why, let's try to convince ourselves that $2^0 = 1.$ First, we write down a table containing all of the powers of $2$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{4} & =16 \\\\ {2}^{3} & =8 \\\\ {2}^{2} & =4 \\\\ {2}^{1} & =2 \\\\ {2}^{0} & =◻\\,?\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs we go down the list, the number in blue is divided by $2$ each time. Following this pattern, we must have \n\n$$\n2^{\\color{red}{0}}  = {\\color{blue}{2}}\\operatorname{div}  2 = 1.\n$$\n\nIt doesn't matter whether the number is positive or negative, a fraction or a decimal, big or small. Raising any non-zero number to the power of zero always gives $1.$"] [:db/cas 17592186065363 :tutorial/content "Dividing zero by any number (other than zero) always equals zero. For example:\n\n- $0 \\div 2 = 0$\n- $0\\div (-456) = 0$\n\nTo understand why dividing zero by any number (other than zero) always equals zero, think about it this way: if you have zero pizzas, and you want to divide zero pizzas among ten friends, then each friend will get exactly zero pizza." "Dividing zero by any number (other than zero) always equals zero. For example:\n\n- $0 \\operatorname{div}  2 = 0$\n- $0\\operatorname{div}  (-456) = 0$\n\nTo understand why dividing zero by any number (other than zero) always equals zero, think about it this way: if you have zero pizzas, and you want to divide zero pizzas among ten friends, then each friend will get exactly zero pizza."] [:db/cas 17592186065364 :tutorial/content "Division by zero is **undefined.** No number can be divided by zero, not even zero itself.\n\nTo see why, notice that if we divide $6$ by $3$, we get\n\n$$\n6\\div 3 = 2\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the relationship between multiplication and division, we have\n\n$$\n6 = 3\\times 2\n$$\n\nHowever, this argument does not work if we divide by zero. Suppose we have\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nThen, by the relationship between multiplication and division, we have\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nand this relationship cannot be true because any number multiplied by zero is zero! Therefore, ? is not a number." "Division by zero is **undefined.** No number can be divided by zero, not even zero itself.\n\nTo see why, notice that if we divide $6$ by $3$, we get\n\n$$\n6\\operatorname{div}  3 = 2\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the relationship between multiplication and division, we have\n\n$$\n6 = 3\\times 2\n$$\n\nHowever, this argument does not work if we divide by zero. Suppose we have\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nThen, by the relationship between multiplication and division, we have\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nand this relationship cannot be true because any number multiplied by zero is zero! Therefore, ? is not a number."] [:db/cas 17592186065381 :tutorial/content "In this lesson, we learned how to use substitution to evaluate integrals that give rise to inverse trigonometric functions.\n\nAlthough you can always solve these integrals by going through the substitution process, it is convenient to remember the following key results:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(ax)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{a}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(ax)+C \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{a}^{2}-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{a}\\right)+C \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{1+(ax)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{a}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(ax)+C \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{{a}^{2}+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{a}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{a}\\right)+C \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{|ax|\\sqrt{(ax)^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{a}\\text{arcsec}(ax)+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo remember the results above, note the following:\n\n- Whenever the integrand has an $ax$ term, the argument of the resulting inverse trigonometric function is $ax.$\n\n- Otherwise, if the $a$ and $x$ are separated, then the argument of the resulting inverse trigonometric function is $\\dfrac{x}{a}.$\n\n- All the results are multiplied by $\\dfrac{1}{a},$ except for $\\arcsin \\left( \\dfrac{x}{a} \\right) + C.$" "In this lesson, we learned how to use substitution to evaluate integrals that give rise to inverse trigonometric functions.\n\nAlthough you can always solve these integrals by going through the substitution process, it is convenient to remember the following key results:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(ax)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{a}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(ax)+C \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{a}^{2}-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{a}\\right)+C \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{1+(ax)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{a}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(ax)+C \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{{a}^{2}+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{a}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{a}\\right)+C \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{|ax|\\sqrt{(ax)^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{a}\\operatorname{arcsec} (ax)+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo remember the results above, note the following:\n\n- Whenever the integrand has an $ax$ term, the argument of the resulting inverse trigonometric function is $ax.$\n\n- Otherwise, if the $a$ and $x$ are separated, then the argument of the resulting inverse trigonometric function is $\\dfrac{x}{a}.$\n\n- All the results are multiplied by $\\dfrac{1}{a},$ except for $\\arcsin \\left( \\dfrac{x}{a} \\right) + C.$"] [:db/cas 17592186065390 :tutorial/content "$\\newcommand{\\arsinh}{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcosh}{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\artanh}{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arsech}{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$\n\nWe know that the derivative of the hyperbolic sine is the hyperbolic cosine:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d }{\\textrm d x}(\\sinh{x}) = \\cosh{x}\n$$\n\nSince integration is the reverse of differentiation, we have\n\n$$\n\\int \\cosh{x}\\,\\textrm d x = \\sinh{x} + C.\n$$\n\nSimilarly, by considering the derivative of the hyperbolic cosine, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d }{\\textrm d x}(\\cosh x) = \\sinh{x} \\qquad\\Rightarrow\\qquad \\int \\sinh x\\  \\textrm{d}x = \\cosh x + C.\n$$" "$\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsinh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcosh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{artanh} }{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{sech} }{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{csch} }{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsech} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcsch} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcoth} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$\n\nWe know that the derivative of the hyperbolic sine is the hyperbolic cosine:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d }{\\textrm d x}(\\sinh{x}) = \\cosh{x}\n$$\n\nSince integration is the reverse of differentiation, we have\n\n$$\n\\int \\cosh{x}\\,\\textrm d x = \\sinh{x} + C.\n$$\n\nSimilarly, by considering the derivative of the hyperbolic cosine, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d }{\\textrm d x}(\\cosh x) = \\sinh{x} \\qquad\\Rightarrow\\qquad \\int \\sinh x\\  \\textrm{d}x = \\cosh x + C.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186065392 :tutorial/content "$\\newcommand{\\arsinh}{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcosh}{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\artanh}{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arsech}{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$\n\nBy considering the derivatives of the hyperbolic tangent, secant, cosecant, and cotangent, we arrive at the following results:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {sech}^{2}⁡x\\text{ }\\text{d}x & =\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x+C \\\\ \\int \\operatorname{sech}⁡x\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x\\text{ }\\text{d}x & =-\\operatorname{sech}⁡x+C \\\\ \\int \\operatorname{csch}⁡x\\operatorname{coth}⁡x\\text{ }\\text{d}x & =-\\operatorname{csch}⁡x+C \\\\ \\int {csch}^{2}⁡x\\text{ }\\text{d}x & =-\\operatorname{coth}⁡x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" "$\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsinh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcosh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{artanh} }{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{sech} }{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{csch} }{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsech} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcsch} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcoth} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$\n\nBy considering the derivatives of the hyperbolic tangent, secant, cosecant, and cotangent, we arrive at the following results:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {\\operatorname{sech} }^{2}⁡x\\text{ }\\text{d}x & =\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x+C \\\\ \\int \\operatorname{sech}⁡x\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x\\text{ }\\text{d}x & =-\\operatorname{sech}⁡x+C \\\\ \\int \\operatorname{csch}⁡x\\operatorname{coth}⁡x\\text{ }\\text{d}x & =-\\operatorname{csch}⁡x+C \\\\ \\int {\\operatorname{csch} }^{2}⁡x\\text{ }\\text{d}x & =-\\operatorname{coth}⁡x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186065409 :tutorial/content "We know that the derivative of sine is cosine:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d }{\\textrm d x}(\\sin{x}) = \\cos{x}\n$$\n\nSince integration is the reverse of differentiation, we see that the integral of cosine is sine:\n\n$$\n\\int \\cos{x}\\,\\textrm d x = \\sin{x} + C\n$$\n\nWe can discover two other integrals by considering the derivatives of cosine and tangent:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\text{ }\\text{d}x & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+C \\\\ \\int {sec}^{2}⁡x\\text{ }\\text{d}x & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, by considering the derivatives of secant, cosecant, and cotangent, we arrive at\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\text{ }\\text{d}x & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡x+C \\\\ \\int \\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\operatorname{cot}⁡x\\text{ }\\text{d}x & =-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x+C \\\\ \\int {csc}^{2}⁡x\\text{ }\\text{d}x & =-\\operatorname{cot}⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith these six formulas, we can integrate many different trigonometric functions." "We know that the derivative of sine is cosine:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d }{\\textrm d x}(\\sin{x}) = \\cos{x}\n$$\n\nSince integration is the reverse of differentiation, we see that the integral of cosine is sine:\n\n$$\n\\int \\cos{x}\\,\\textrm d x = \\sin{x} + C\n$$\n\nWe can discover two other integrals by considering the derivatives of cosine and tangent:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\text{ }\\text{d}x & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+C \\\\ \\int {\\sec }^{2}⁡x\\text{ }\\text{d}x & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, by considering the derivatives of secant, cosecant, and cotangent, we arrive at\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\text{ }\\text{d}x & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡x+C \\\\ \\int \\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\operatorname{cot}⁡x\\text{ }\\text{d}x & =-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x+C \\\\ \\int {\\csc }^{2}⁡x\\text{ }\\text{d}x & =-\\operatorname{cot}⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith these six formulas, we can integrate many different trigonometric functions."] [:db/cas 17592186065410 :tutorial/content "Recall the following derivatives of the inverse trigonometric functions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arcsin}x) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arctan}x) & =\\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\text{arcsec}\\,x) & =\\frac{1}{|x|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince integration is the opposite of differentiation, we arrive at the following results:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}\\text{d}x & =\\operatorname{arcsin}(x)+C \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}}\\text{d}x & =\\operatorname{arctan}(x)+C \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{|x|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}}\\text{d}x & =\\text{arcsec}(x)+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Recall the following derivatives of the inverse trigonometric functions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arcsin}x) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arctan}x) & =\\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arcsec} \\,x) & =\\frac{1}{|x|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince integration is the opposite of differentiation, we arrive at the following results:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}\\text{d}x & =\\operatorname{arcsin}(x)+C \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}}\\text{d}x & =\\operatorname{arctan}(x)+C \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{|x|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}}\\text{d}x & =\\operatorname{arcsec} (x)+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186065416 :tutorial/content "Dividing with negative numbers follows similar rules as multiplying with negative numbers. \n\nFirst of all, a negative number divided by a positive number gives a negative number. We can picture this rule as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-)\\,÷\\,(+)\\,=\\,(-).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor example, let's use this rule to find the value of $(-6) \\div 2.$\n\nHere, since we are dividing a negative number $\\color{red}(-6)$ by a positive number ${\\color{blue}(2)},$ the result is going to be negative:\n$$\n{\\color{red}(-6)} \\div {\\color{blue}2} = {\\color{red} \\mathbf{-} \\boxed{ {\\phantom{3}}} }\n$$\n\nNow, to compute the number that goes in the box, we divide $6$ by $2$ *without* any signs: $6 \\div 2 = 3.$ So, we get\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}(-6)} \\div {\\color{blue}2} = {\\color{red} -3 }.\n$$" "Dividing with negative numbers follows similar rules as multiplying with negative numbers. \n\nFirst of all, a negative number divided by a positive number gives a negative number. We can picture this rule as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-)\\,÷\\,(+)\\,=\\,(-).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor example, let's use this rule to find the value of $(-6) \\operatorname{div}  2.$\n\nHere, since we are dividing a negative number $\\color{red}(-6)$ by a positive number ${\\color{blue}(2)},$ the result is going to be negative:\n$$\n{\\color{red}(-6)} \\operatorname{div}  {\\color{blue}2} = {\\color{red} \\mathbf{-} \\boxed{ {\\phantom{3}}} }\n$$\n\nNow, to compute the number that goes in the box, we divide $6$ by $2$ *without* any signs: $6 \\operatorname{div}  2 = 3.$ So, we get\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}(-6)} \\operatorname{div}  {\\color{blue}2} = {\\color{red} -3 }.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186065417 :tutorial/content "A positive number divided by a negative number is also a negative number. We can picture this rule as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(+)\\,÷\\,(-)\\,=\\,(-).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor instance, to find $8\\div (-2),$ we have to divide a positive number $\\color{blue}(8)$ by a negative one ${\\color{red}(-2)}.$ So the result will be negative:\n$$\n{\\color{blue}8} \\div {\\color{red}(-2)} = {\\color{red}\\mathbf{-} \\boxed{ {\\phantom{10}}} }\n$$\n\nNow, to compute the number that goes in the box, we divide $8$ by $2$ without any signs: $8 \\div 2 = 4.$ So, we get\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}8} \\div {\\color{red}(-2)} = {\\color{red}-4}.\n$$" "A positive number divided by a negative number is also a negative number. We can picture this rule as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(+)\\,÷\\,(-)\\,=\\,(-).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor instance, to find $8\\operatorname{div}  (-2),$ we have to divide a positive number $\\color{blue}(8)$ by a negative one ${\\color{red}(-2)}.$ So the result will be negative:\n$$\n{\\color{blue}8} \\operatorname{div}  {\\color{red}(-2)} = {\\color{red}\\mathbf{-} \\boxed{ {\\phantom{10}}} }\n$$\n\nNow, to compute the number that goes in the box, we divide $8$ by $2$ without any signs: $8 \\operatorname{div}  2 = 4.$ So, we get\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}8} \\operatorname{div}  {\\color{red}(-2)} = {\\color{red}-4}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186065418 :tutorial/content "Finally, the third rule is that a negative number divided by a negative number is a positive number:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-)\\,÷\\,(-)\\,=\\,(+)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo demonstrate, let's find $-12 \\div (-3).$ We have to divide a negative number $\\color{red}(-12)$ by another negative number ${\\color{red}(-3)}.$ So the result will be positive:\n$$\n{\\color{red}(-12)} \\div {\\color{red}(-3)} = {\\color{blue} \\boxed{ {\\phantom{4}}} }\n$$\n\nNow, to compute the number that goes in the box, we divide $12$ by $3$ without any signs: $12 \\div 3 = 4.$ Therefore,  we get\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}(-12)} \\div {\\color{red}(-3)} = {\\color{blue}4}.\n$$" "Finally, the third rule is that a negative number divided by a negative number is a positive number:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-)\\,÷\\,(-)\\,=\\,(+)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo demonstrate, let's find $-12 \\operatorname{div}  (-3).$ We have to divide a negative number $\\color{red}(-12)$ by another negative number ${\\color{red}(-3)}.$ So the result will be positive:\n$$\n{\\color{red}(-12)} \\operatorname{div}  {\\color{red}(-3)} = {\\color{blue} \\boxed{ {\\phantom{4}}} }\n$$\n\nNow, to compute the number that goes in the box, we divide $12$ by $3$ without any signs: $12 \\operatorname{div}  3 = 4.$ Therefore,  we get\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}(-12)} \\operatorname{div}  {\\color{red}(-3)} = {\\color{blue}4}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186065421 :tutorial/content "Let's think about how to simplify the following:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\left(\\dfrac{2}{3}\\right)}{\\left(\\dfrac{3}{4}\\right)}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe expression above is a fraction, but there are additional fractions in the numerator and denominator.\nSuch expressions are called **fractions of fractions**.\nWe use parentheses to make it easier to read.\n\nThe trick to working with fractions of fractions is to remember that a fraction represents a division. So, a fraction of fractions is a division of two fractions:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\left(\\dfrac{2}{3}\\right)}{\\left(\\dfrac{\\color{blue}{3}}{\\color{red}{4}}\\right)} = \\left(\\dfrac{2}{3}\\right) \\boldsymbol{\\div} \\left(\\dfrac{\\color{blue}{3}}{\\color{red}{4}}\\right)\n$$\n\nNow, recall that dividing by $\\dfrac{\\color{blue}{3}}{\\color{red}{4}}$ is the same as multiplying by its reciprocal $\\dfrac{\\color{red}{4}}{\\color{blue}{3}}.$ We can use this fact to simplify the fraction above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)÷\\left(\\frac{3}{4}\\right) &= \\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)\\times \\left(\\frac{4}{3}\\right) \\\\ &= \\frac{2\\times 4}{3\\times 3} \\\\ &= \\frac{8}{9}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\left(\\dfrac{2}{3}\\right)}{\\left(\\dfrac{3}{4}\\right)} = \\dfrac{8}{9}.\n$$" "Let's think about how to simplify the following:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\left(\\dfrac{2}{3}\\right)}{\\left(\\dfrac{3}{4}\\right)}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe expression above is a fraction, but there are additional fractions in the numerator and denominator.\nSuch expressions are called **fractions of fractions**.\nWe use parentheses to make it easier to read.\n\nThe trick to working with fractions of fractions is to remember that a fraction represents a division. So, a fraction of fractions is a division of two fractions:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\left(\\dfrac{2}{3}\\right)}{\\left(\\dfrac{\\color{blue}{3}}{\\color{red}{4}}\\right)} = \\left(\\dfrac{2}{3}\\right) \\boldsymbol{\\operatorname{div} } \\left(\\dfrac{\\color{blue}{3}}{\\color{red}{4}}\\right)\n$$\n\nNow, recall that dividing by $\\dfrac{\\color{blue}{3}}{\\color{red}{4}}$ is the same as multiplying by its reciprocal $\\dfrac{\\color{red}{4}}{\\color{blue}{3}}.$ We can use this fact to simplify the fraction above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)÷\\left(\\frac{3}{4}\\right) &= \\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)\\times \\left(\\frac{4}{3}\\right) \\\\ &= \\frac{2\\times 4}{3\\times 3} \\\\ &= \\frac{8}{9}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\left(\\dfrac{2}{3}\\right)}{\\left(\\dfrac{3}{4}\\right)} = \\dfrac{8}{9}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186065435 :tutorial/content "Suppose we want to calculate the integral\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int\\dfrac{1}{\\sin^2x}\\,\\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nAt first glance, this looks kind of tricky. However, if we use the identity $\\dfrac{1}{\\sin x} = \\csc x,$ we can rewrite the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{{sin}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x &=\\int {\\left(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\right)}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int {csc}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand we see that this is just a standard trigonometric integral:\n\n$$\n\\int\\dfrac{1}{\\sin^2x}\\,\\textrm d x  =  \\int\\csc^2 x\\,\\textrm d x =-\\cot x + C\n$$\n\nWhen confronted with a tricky-looking trigonometric integral, we can often reduce it to a standard integral using a trigonometric identity." "Suppose we want to calculate the integral\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int\\dfrac{1}{\\sin^2x}\\,\\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nAt first glance, this looks kind of tricky. However, if we use the identity $\\dfrac{1}{\\sin x} = \\csc x,$ we can rewrite the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{{\\sin }^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x &=\\int {\\left(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\right)}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int {\\csc }^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand we see that this is just a standard trigonometric integral:\n\n$$\n\\int\\dfrac{1}{\\sin^2x}\\,\\textrm d x  =  \\int\\csc^2 x\\,\\textrm d x =-\\cot x + C\n$$\n\nWhen confronted with a tricky-looking trigonometric integral, we can often reduce it to a standard integral using a trigonometric identity."] [:db/cas 17592186066869 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}arcsec(x + 3) + C" "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arcsec} (x + 3) + C"] [:db/cas 17592186066870 :answer/value "arcsec(x - 2) + C" "\\operatorname{arcsec} (x - 2) + C"] [:db/cas 17592186066871 :answer/value "arcsec(x + 2) + C" "\\operatorname{arcsec} (x + 2) + C"] [:db/cas 17592186066872 :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}arcsec(2x - 4) + C" "\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{arcsec} (2x - 4) + C"] [:db/cas 17592186066873 :answer/value "arcsec\\left(\\frac{x - 2}{2}\\right) + C" "\\operatorname{arcsec} \\left(\\frac{x - 2}{2}\\right) + C"] [:db/cas 17592186067457 :answer/value "arcsec\\left(\\frac{x + 5}{4}\\right) + C" "\\operatorname{arcsec} \\left(\\frac{x + 5}{4}\\right) + C"] [:db/cas 17592186067458 :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}arcsec(x + 1) + C" "\\frac{4}{5}\\operatorname{arcsec} (x + 1) + C"] [:db/cas 17592186067459 :answer/value "4arcsec(x + 5) + C" "4\\operatorname{arcsec} (x + 5) + C"] [:db/cas 17592186067460 :answer/value "4arcsec\\left(\\frac{x}{5}\\right) + C" "4\\operatorname{arcsec} \\left(\\frac{x}{5}\\right) + C"] [:db/cas 17592186067461 :answer/value "5arcsec(x + 4) + C" "5\\operatorname{arcsec} (x + 4) + C"] [:db/cas 17592186068506 :question/worked-solution "First, we complete the square for the expression inside the square root:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n    x^2-2x&= x^2 -2x +(1-1)\\\\[3pt]\n              &= (x^2 -2x +1)-1\\\\[3pt]\n         &= (x-1)^2-1\n         \n  \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above in the given integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\int_{1+2/\\sqrt{3}}^{3} \\dfrac{1}{|x-1|\\sqrt{x^2-2x}}\\, \\textrm{d}x \n= \\int_{1+2/\\sqrt{3}}^{3} \\dfrac{1}{|x-1|\\sqrt{(x-1)^2-1}}\\, \\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis resembles the basic integral for inverse secant:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{u^2-1}} \\, \\textrm{d}u = \\textrm{arcsec} \\, u +C\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u$ as the variable, we have $x-1$.\n\nSo, let's perform the substitution\n\n$$\nu=x-1.\n$$\n\n \nDifferentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}=1\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n\\textrm{d}x = \\textrm{d}u.\n$$\n\nLet's use the table below to change the limits from $x$ to $u$.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1+\\dfrac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$u$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n    \\int_{1+2/\\sqrt{3}}^{3} \\dfrac{1}{|x-1|\\sqrt{x^2-2x}}\\, \\textrm{d}x \n&=  \\int_{1+2/\\sqrt{3}}^{3}\\dfrac{1}{|x-1|\\sqrt{(x-1)^2-1}}\\,\\textrm{d}x \\\\[5pt]\n&=\\int_{2/\\sqrt{3}}^{2} \\dfrac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{u^2-1}}\\, \\textrm{d}u\\\\[5pt]\n&=  \\textrm{arcsec}\\, {u}\\Big|_{2/\\sqrt{3}}^{2}\\\\[5pt]\n&= \\textrm{arcsec} \\left(2\\right)  - \\textrm{arcsec}\\left(\\dfrac{2}{\\sqrt{3}} \\right)\\\\[5pt]\n&=\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}-\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\\right)\\\\[5pt]\n&=\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}.\n\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we complete the square for the expression inside the square root:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n    x^2-2x&= x^2 -2x +(1-1)\\\\[3pt]\n              &= (x^2 -2x +1)-1\\\\[3pt]\n         &= (x-1)^2-1\n         \n  \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above in the given integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\int_{1+2/\\sqrt{3}}^{3} \\dfrac{1}{|x-1|\\sqrt{x^2-2x}}\\, \\textrm{d}x \n= \\int_{1+2/\\sqrt{3}}^{3} \\dfrac{1}{|x-1|\\sqrt{(x-1)^2-1}}\\, \\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis resembles the basic integral for inverse secant:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{u^2-1}} \\, \\textrm{d}u = \\operatorname{arcsec}  \\, u +C\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u$ as the variable, we have $x-1$.\n\nSo, let's perform the substitution\n\n$$\nu=x-1.\n$$\n\n \nDifferentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}=1\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n\\textrm{d}x = \\textrm{d}u.\n$$\n\nLet's use the table below to change the limits from $x$ to $u$.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1+\\dfrac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$u$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n    \\int_{1+2/\\sqrt{3}}^{3} \\dfrac{1}{|x-1|\\sqrt{x^2-2x}}\\, \\textrm{d}x \n&=  \\int_{1+2/\\sqrt{3}}^{3}\\dfrac{1}{|x-1|\\sqrt{(x-1)^2-1}}\\,\\textrm{d}x \\\\[5pt]\n&=\\int_{2/\\sqrt{3}}^{2} \\dfrac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{u^2-1}}\\, \\textrm{d}u\\\\[5pt]\n&=  \\operatorname{arcsec} \\, {u}\\Big|_{2/\\sqrt{3}}^{2}\\\\[5pt]\n&= \\operatorname{arcsec}  \\left(2\\right)  - \\operatorname{arcsec} \\left(\\dfrac{2}{\\sqrt{3}} \\right)\\\\[5pt]\n&=\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}-\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\\right)\\\\[5pt]\n&=\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}.\n\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186068569 :question/worked-solution "Let $u = x^3 - \\dfrac{\\pi}{6}.$ Differentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d} u}{\\textrm{d}x } = 3x^2 \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\dfrac 1 3 \\textrm d u = x^2\\,\\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {x}^{2}{sec}^{2}⁡({x}^{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {sec}^{2}⁡({x}^{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\cdot {x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {sec}^{2}⁡(u)\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int {sec}^{2}⁡(u)\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{tan}⁡u+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{tan}⁡({x}^{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{6})+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let $u = x^3 - \\dfrac{\\pi}{6}.$ Differentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d} u}{\\textrm{d}x } = 3x^2 \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\dfrac 1 3 \\textrm d u = x^2\\,\\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {x}^{2}{\\sec }^{2}⁡({x}^{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {\\sec }^{2}⁡({x}^{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\cdot {x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {\\sec }^{2}⁡(u)\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int {\\sec }^{2}⁡(u)\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{tan}⁡u+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{tan}⁡({x}^{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{6})+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186068570 :question/worked-solution "Notice that the numerator is proportional to the derivative of the denominator, because\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d}{\\textrm d x}(1+\\tan^2 x) = 2\\tan{x}\\sec^2 x.\n$$\n\nSo, let $u = 1+\\tan^2 x.$ Differentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d} u}{\\textrm{d}x } = 2\\tan x\\sec^2 x \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\dfrac 1 2 \\textrm d u = \\tan x\\sec^2 x\\,\\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nCalculating the limits for $u$ gives\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\pi/4$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$u$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nWe now substitute and evaluate, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x{sec}^{2}⁡x}{1+{tan}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{1}{1+{tan}^{2}⁡x}\\cdot \\operatorname{tan}⁡x{sec}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\operatorname{ln}⁡1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Notice that the numerator is proportional to the derivative of the denominator, because\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d}{\\textrm d x}(1+\\tan^2 x) = 2\\tan{x}\\sec^2 x.\n$$\n\nSo, let $u = 1+\\tan^2 x.$ Differentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d} u}{\\textrm{d}x } = 2\\tan x\\sec^2 x \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\dfrac 1 2 \\textrm d u = \\tan x\\sec^2 x\\,\\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nCalculating the limits for $u$ gives\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\pi/4$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$u$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nWe now substitute and evaluate, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x{\\sec }^{2}⁡x}{1+{\\tan }^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{1}{1+{\\tan }^{2}⁡x}\\cdot \\operatorname{tan}⁡x{\\sec }^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\operatorname{ln}⁡1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186068580 :question/worked-solution "First, we let\n\n$$\nI = \\int \\dfrac{\\ln^2 x}{x} \\, \\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo use integration by parts, let's suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={ln}^{2}⁡x & \\,⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x} & \\,⇒\\,v=\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the integration by parts formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\int \\frac{{ln}^{2}⁡x}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =({ln}^{2}⁡x)(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)-\\int (\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)\\left(\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x}\\right)\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={ln}^{3}⁡x-2\\int \\frac{{ln}^{2}⁡x}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={ln}^{3}⁡x-2I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $I,$ remembering to add a constant of integration $C$ at the very end:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & ={ln}^{3}⁡x-2I \\\\ 3I & ={ln}^{3}⁡x \\\\ I & =\\frac{{ln}^{3}⁡x}{3}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we let\n\n$$\nI = \\int \\dfrac{\\ln^2 x}{x} \\, \\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo use integration by parts, let's suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={\\ln }^{2}⁡x & \\,⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x} & \\,⇒\\,v=\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the integration by parts formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\int \\frac{{\\ln }^{2}⁡x}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =({\\ln }^{2}⁡x)(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)-\\int (\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)\\left(\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x}\\right)\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\ln }^{3}⁡x-2\\int \\frac{{\\ln }^{2}⁡x}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\ln }^{3}⁡x-2I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $I,$ remembering to add a constant of integration $C$ at the very end:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & ={\\ln }^{3}⁡x-2I \\\\ 3I & ={\\ln }^{3}⁡x \\\\ I & =\\frac{{\\ln }^{3}⁡x}{3}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186068597 :question/worked-solution "To compute\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{x} \\div \\dfrac{y}{6x^2},\n$$\n\nwe just need to multiply by the reciprocal of the divisor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\dfrac 3 x \\div  \\dfrac y {6x^2} &= \\dfrac 3 x \\cdot  \\dfrac {6x^2} y \\\\ &= \\dfrac {18x^2} {xy} \\\\ &= \\dfrac {18x^\\cancel{2}} {\\cancel{x}y} \\\\ &= \\dfrac {18x} {y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that in the final step, we canceled out a common factor of $x$ in the numerator and denominator." "To compute\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{x} \\operatorname{div}  \\dfrac{y}{6x^2},\n$$\n\nwe just need to multiply by the reciprocal of the divisor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\dfrac 3 x \\operatorname{div}   \\dfrac y {6x^2} &= \\dfrac 3 x \\cdot  \\dfrac {6x^2} y \\\\ &= \\dfrac {18x^2} {xy} \\\\ &= \\dfrac {18x^\\cancel{2}} {\\cancel{x}y} \\\\ &= \\dfrac {18x} {y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that in the final step, we canceled out a common factor of $x$ in the numerator and denominator."] [:db/cas 17592186068598 :question/problem "Given that $\\dfrac {2a^2(1+b)} {3b}  =x$ and $\\dfrac {1+b}{5b^2} =y ,$ then what is $x \\div y?$" "Given that $\\dfrac {2a^2(1+b)} {3b}  =x$ and $\\dfrac {1+b}{5b^2} =y ,$ then what is $x \\operatorname{div}  y?$"] [:db/cas 17592186068598 :question/worked-solution "To compute $x \\div y,$ i.e., \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2{a}^{2}(1+b)}{3b} & ÷\\frac{1+b}{5{b}^{2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe just need to multiply by the reciprocal of the divisor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\dfrac {2a^2(1+b)} {3b}  \\div  \\dfrac{1+b}{5b^2} &= \\dfrac {2a^2(1+b)} {3b}  \\cdot  \\dfrac {5b^2} {1+b} \\\\[5pt] &= \\dfrac {2a^2\\cancel{(1+b)}} {3\\cancel{b}}  \\cdot  \\dfrac {5b^{\\cancel{2}}} {\\cancel{1+b}} \\\\[5pt] &= \\dfrac {10a^2b} {3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "To compute $x \\operatorname{div}  y,$ i.e., \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2{a}^{2}(1+b)}{3b} & ÷\\frac{1+b}{5{b}^{2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe just need to multiply by the reciprocal of the divisor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\dfrac {2a^2(1+b)} {3b}  \\operatorname{div}   \\dfrac{1+b}{5b^2} &= \\dfrac {2a^2(1+b)} {3b}  \\cdot  \\dfrac {5b^2} {1+b} \\\\[5pt] &= \\dfrac {2a^2\\cancel{(1+b)}} {3\\cancel{b}}  \\cdot  \\dfrac {5b^{\\cancel{2}}} {\\cancel{1+b}} \\\\[5pt] &= \\dfrac {10a^2b} {3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186068604 :question/worked-solution "To compute\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{x} \\div \\dfrac{5}{y},\n$$\n\nwe just need to multiply by the reciprocal of the divisor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\dfrac 3 x \\div  \\dfrac 5 y &= \\dfrac 3 x \\cdot  \\dfrac y 5 \\\\ &= \\dfrac {3y} {5x}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "To compute\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{x} \\operatorname{div}  \\dfrac{5}{y},\n$$\n\nwe just need to multiply by the reciprocal of the divisor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\dfrac 3 x \\operatorname{div}   \\dfrac 5 y &= \\dfrac 3 x \\cdot  \\dfrac y 5 \\\\ &= \\dfrac {3y} {5x}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186068605 :question/problem "Given that $\\dfrac {2a^3 - 10a} {7b}  =x$ and $\\dfrac {2a} {14b^2}=y,$ then what is $x \\div y?$" "Given that $\\dfrac {2a^3 - 10a} {7b}  =x$ and $\\dfrac {2a} {14b^2}=y,$ then what is $x \\operatorname{div}  y?$"] [:db/cas 17592186068605 :question/worked-solution "To compute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\n\t\t\\dfrac {2a^3 - 10a} {7b}  &\\div  \\dfrac {2a} {14b^2},\n\t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe just need to multiply by the reciprocal of the divisor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\dfrac {2a^3 - 10a} {7b}  \\div  \\dfrac {2a} {14b^2} &= \\dfrac {2a^3 - 10a} {7b}  \\cdot \\dfrac {14b^2} {2a} \\\\ &= \\dfrac {2a(a^2 - 5)} {7b}  \\cdot  \\dfrac {2b \\cdot 7b} {2a} \\\\ &= \\dfrac {\\cancel{2a}(a^2 - 5)} {\\cancel{7b}}  \\cdot  \\dfrac {2b \\cdot \\cancel{7b}} {\\cancel{2a}} \\\\ &= \\dfrac {2b(a^2 - 5)} 1 \\\\ &= 2a^2b - 10b\\end{aligned}\n$$" "To compute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\n\t\t\\dfrac {2a^3 - 10a} {7b}  &\\operatorname{div}   \\dfrac {2a} {14b^2},\n\t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe just need to multiply by the reciprocal of the divisor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\dfrac {2a^3 - 10a} {7b}  \\operatorname{div}   \\dfrac {2a} {14b^2} &= \\dfrac {2a^3 - 10a} {7b}  \\cdot \\dfrac {14b^2} {2a} \\\\ &= \\dfrac {2a(a^2 - 5)} {7b}  \\cdot  \\dfrac {2b \\cdot 7b} {2a} \\\\ &= \\dfrac {\\cancel{2a}(a^2 - 5)} {\\cancel{7b}}  \\cdot  \\dfrac {2b \\cdot \\cancel{7b}} {\\cancel{2a}} \\\\ &= \\dfrac {2b(a^2 - 5)} 1 \\\\ &= 2a^2b - 10b\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186068622 :question/worked-solution "To compute $\\dfrac{\\textrm d^2 y }{\\textrm d x^2},$ we can use the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{dt}\\left(\\frac{dy}{dx}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{dx}{dt}\\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}= -2 \\tan(2t),$ and we can calculate $\\dfrac{\\mathrm dx}{\\mathrm dt}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm dx}{\\mathrm dt} = \\dfrac{\\mathrm d}{\\mathrm dt} ( \\sin 2t ) = 2 \\cos(2t).\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{dt}(-2\\operatorname{tan}⁡2t)}{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-4{sec}^{2}⁡(2t)}{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)} \\\\  & =-4{sec}^{2}⁡(2t)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡(2t) \\\\  & =-2{sec}^{3}⁡(2t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate at $t=\\dfrac{\\pi}{8},$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}{|}_{t=\\pi /8} & =-2{sec}^{3}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{8}) \\\\  & =-2{sec}^{3}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) \\\\  & =-2{(\\sqrt{2})}^{3} \\\\  & =-4\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "To compute $\\dfrac{\\textrm d^2 y }{\\textrm d x^2},$ we can use the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{dt}\\left(\\frac{dy}{dx}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{dx}{dt}\\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}= -2 \\tan(2t),$ and we can calculate $\\dfrac{\\mathrm dx}{\\mathrm dt}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm dx}{\\mathrm dt} = \\dfrac{\\mathrm d}{\\mathrm dt} ( \\sin 2t ) = 2 \\cos(2t).\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{dt}(-2\\operatorname{tan}⁡2t)}{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-4{\\sec }^{2}⁡(2t)}{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)} \\\\  & =-4{\\sec }^{2}⁡(2t)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡(2t) \\\\  & =-2{\\sec }^{3}⁡(2t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate at $t=\\dfrac{\\pi}{8},$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}{|}_{t=\\pi /8} & =-2{\\sec }^{3}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{8}) \\\\  & =-2{\\sec }^{3}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) \\\\  & =-2{(\\sqrt{2})}^{3} \\\\  & =-4\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186069046 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int 4{x}^{2}{sec}^{2}⁡({x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int 4{x}^{2}{\\sec }^{2}⁡({x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186069046 :question/worked-solution "Let $u={x}^{3}.$ Differentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=3{x}^{2}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{3}\\text{d}u={x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 4{x}^{2}{sec}^{2}⁡({x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x & =4\\int {sec}^{2}⁡({x}^{3})\\cdot {x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4\\int {sec}^{2}⁡(u)\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}\\int {sec}^{2}⁡u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}\\operatorname{tan}⁡u+C \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}\\operatorname{tan}⁡({x}^{3})+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let $u={x}^{3}.$ Differentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=3{x}^{2}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{3}\\text{d}u={x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 4{x}^{2}{\\sec }^{2}⁡({x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x & =4\\int {\\sec }^{2}⁡({x}^{3})\\cdot {x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4\\int {\\sec }^{2}⁡(u)\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}\\int {\\sec }^{2}⁡u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}\\operatorname{tan}⁡u+C \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}\\operatorname{tan}⁡({x}^{3})+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186069064 :question/problem "What is $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{7}{7s + 14} \\div \\frac{1}{s^{2} - 4}\n$$?" "What is $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{7}{7s + 14} \\operatorname{div}  \\frac{1}{s^{2} - 4}\n$$?"] [:db/cas 17592186069191 :question/problem "If the equation ${log}_{25}⁡5=\\frac{1}{2}$ is equivalent to $\\sqrt{b}=5,$  then $b=${{field-1}}." "If the equation ${\\log }_{25}⁡5=\\frac{1}{2}$ is equivalent to $\\sqrt{b}=5,$  then $b=${{field-1}}."] [:db/cas 17592186069191 :question/worked-solution "The logarithm of $a$ with base $b$ is defined as follows:\n\n${log}_{b}⁡a=x$ is equivalent to $a={b}^{x}$\n\nIf we compare ${log}_{25}⁡5=\\frac{1}{2}$ with ${log}_{b}⁡a=x,$ we get $a=5,$ $b=25,$ and $x=\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the given equation is equivalent to ${25}^{1/2}=5,$ which is also equivalent to $\\sqrt{25}=5.$ Consequently, $b=25.$" "The logarithm of $a$ with base $b$ is defined as follows:\n\n${\\log }_{b}⁡a=x$ is equivalent to $a={b}^{x}$\n\nIf we compare ${\\log }_{25}⁡5=\\frac{1}{2}$ with ${\\log }_{b}⁡a=x,$ we get $a=5,$ $b=25,$ and $x=\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the given equation is equivalent to ${25}^{1/2}=5,$ which is also equivalent to $\\sqrt{25}=5.$ Consequently, $b=25.$"] [:db/cas 17592186069214 :question/problem "Given that $\\frac{1}{3z + 18} = a$ and $\\frac{1}{z^{2} + 6z} = b$, what is the value of $a \\div b$?" "Given that $\\frac{1}{3z + 18} = a$ and $\\frac{1}{z^{2} + 6z} = b$, what is the value of $a \\operatorname{div}  b$?"] [:db/cas 17592186069262 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{{ln}^{2}⁡x}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{{\\ln }^{2}⁡x}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186069262 :question/worked-solution "We need to use integration by parts.\n\nIn this case, we define\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={ln}^{2}⁡(x)\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)}{x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x=-\\frac{1}{2{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{ln}^{2}⁡x}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =({ln}^{2}⁡(x))\\left(-\\frac{1}{2{x}^{2}}\\right)-\\int \\left(-\\frac{1}{2{x}^{2}}\\right)\\left(\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)}{x}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{{ln}^{2}⁡(x)}{2{x}^{2}}+\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe use integration by parts once again to evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)}{{x}^{3}}\\text{d}x.\n$$ We take\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x=-\\frac{1}{2{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the formula for integration by parts gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x))\\left(-\\frac{1}{2{x}^{2}}\\right)-\\int \\left(-\\frac{1}{2{x}^{2}}\\right)\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)}{2{x}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)}{2{x}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{4{x}^{2}}+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an integral constant.\n\nSo we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{ln}^{2}⁡x}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\frac{{ln}^{2}⁡(x)}{2{x}^{2}}+(-\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)}{2{x}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{4{x}^{2}}+C) \\\\  & =-\\frac{{ln}^{2}⁡(x)}{2{x}^{2}}-\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)}{2{x}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{4{x}^{2}}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4{x}^{2}}(2{ln}^{2}⁡(x)+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+1)+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We need to use integration by parts.\n\nIn this case, we define\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={\\ln }^{2}⁡(x)\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)}{x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x=-\\frac{1}{2{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{\\ln }^{2}⁡x}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =({\\ln }^{2}⁡(x))\\left(-\\frac{1}{2{x}^{2}}\\right)-\\int \\left(-\\frac{1}{2{x}^{2}}\\right)\\left(\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)}{x}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{{\\ln }^{2}⁡(x)}{2{x}^{2}}+\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe use integration by parts once again to evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)}{{x}^{3}}\\text{d}x.\n$$ We take\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x=-\\frac{1}{2{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the formula for integration by parts gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x))\\left(-\\frac{1}{2{x}^{2}}\\right)-\\int \\left(-\\frac{1}{2{x}^{2}}\\right)\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)}{2{x}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)}{2{x}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{4{x}^{2}}+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an integral constant.\n\nSo we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{\\ln }^{2}⁡x}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\frac{{\\ln }^{2}⁡(x)}{2{x}^{2}}+(-\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)}{2{x}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{4{x}^{2}}+C) \\\\  & =-\\frac{{\\ln }^{2}⁡(x)}{2{x}^{2}}-\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)}{2{x}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{4{x}^{2}}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4{x}^{2}}(2{\\ln }^{2}⁡(x)+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+1)+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186069401 :question/problem "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{{ln}^{3}⁡z}{z}\\,\\text{d}z\n$$ using integration by parts." "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{{\\ln }^{3}⁡z}{z}\\,\\text{d}z\n$$ using integration by parts."] [:db/cas 17592186069401 :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the indefinite integral by $$\n\\displaystyle I=\\int \\frac{{ln}^{3}⁡z}{z}\\,\\text{d}z.\n$$\n\nTo use integration by parts, let's suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={ln}^{3}⁡z\\, & ⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}z}=\\frac{3{ln}^{2}⁡z}{z}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}z}=\\frac{1}{z}\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\int \\frac{1}{z}\\,\\text{d}z=\\operatorname{ln}⁡z.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the integration by parts formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\int \\frac{{ln}^{3}⁡z}{z}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}z}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}z}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={ln}^{3}⁡z(\\operatorname{ln}⁡z)-\\int (\\operatorname{ln}⁡z)\\left(\\frac{3{ln}^{2}⁡z}{z}\\right)\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={ln}^{4}⁡z-3\\int \\frac{{ln}^{3}⁡z}{z}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={ln}^{4}⁡z-3I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for $I.$ We can ignore the constant of integration in this case, because it is insignificant for further evaluation of the definite integral.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & ={ln}^{4}⁡z-3I \\\\ 4I & ={ln}^{4}⁡z \\\\ I & =\\frac{{ln}^{4}⁡z}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate our definite integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{{ln}^{3}⁡z}{z}\\,\\text{d}z & =\\frac{1}{4}({ln}^{4}⁡z){|}_{1}^{e} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}({ln}^{4}⁡(e)-{ln}^{4}⁡(1)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let's denote the indefinite integral by $$\n\\displaystyle I=\\int \\frac{{\\ln }^{3}⁡z}{z}\\,\\text{d}z.\n$$\n\nTo use integration by parts, let's suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={\\ln }^{3}⁡z\\, & ⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}z}=\\frac{3{\\ln }^{2}⁡z}{z}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}z}=\\frac{1}{z}\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\int \\frac{1}{z}\\,\\text{d}z=\\operatorname{ln}⁡z.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the integration by parts formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\int \\frac{{\\ln }^{3}⁡z}{z}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}z}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}z}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\ln }^{3}⁡z(\\operatorname{ln}⁡z)-\\int (\\operatorname{ln}⁡z)\\left(\\frac{3{\\ln }^{2}⁡z}{z}\\right)\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\ln }^{4}⁡z-3\\int \\frac{{\\ln }^{3}⁡z}{z}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\ln }^{4}⁡z-3I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for $I.$ We can ignore the constant of integration in this case, because it is insignificant for further evaluation of the definite integral.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & ={\\ln }^{4}⁡z-3I \\\\ 4I & ={\\ln }^{4}⁡z \\\\ I & =\\frac{{\\ln }^{4}⁡z}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate our definite integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{{\\ln }^{3}⁡z}{z}\\,\\text{d}z & =\\frac{1}{4}({\\ln }^{4}⁡z){|}_{1}^{e} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}({\\ln }^{4}⁡(e)-{\\ln }^{4}⁡(1)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186069412 :question/problem "Given that $r(t)=⟨{cos}^{2}⁡t,\\,{sin}^{2}⁡t⟩,$ calculate $r\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right).$" "Given that $r(t)=⟨{\\cos }^{2}⁡t,\\,{\\sin }^{2}⁡t⟩,$ calculate $r\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right).$"] [:db/cas 17592186069412 :question/worked-solution "We substitute $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ into our function, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) & =⟨{cos}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right),\\,{sin}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)}^{2},\\,{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)}^{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{2},\\,\\frac{1}{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We substitute $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ into our function, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) & =⟨{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right),\\,{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)}^{2},\\,{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)}^{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{2},\\,\\frac{1}{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186069477 :question/problem "The equation ${log}_{5}⁡5=1$ is equivalent to ${b}^{1}=5.$ What is the value of $b?$" "The equation ${\\log }_{5}⁡5=1$ is equivalent to ${b}^{1}=5.$ What is the value of $b?$"] [:db/cas 17592186069477 :question/worked-solution "The logarithm of $a$ with base $b$ is defined as follows:\n\n${log}_{b}⁡a=x$ is equivalent to $a={b}^{x}$\n\nIf we compare ${log}_{5}⁡5=1$ with ${log}_{b}⁡a=x,$ we get $a=5,$ $b=5,$ and $x=1.$\n\nTherefore, the given equation is equivalent to ${5}^{1}=5.$ Consequently, $b=5.$" "The logarithm of $a$ with base $b$ is defined as follows:\n\n${\\log }_{b}⁡a=x$ is equivalent to $a={b}^{x}$\n\nIf we compare ${\\log }_{5}⁡5=1$ with ${\\log }_{b}⁡a=x,$ we get $a=5,$ $b=5,$ and $x=1.$\n\nTherefore, the given equation is equivalent to ${5}^{1}=5.$ Consequently, $b=5.$"] [:db/cas 17592186069502 :question/problem "What is $\\frac{2x}{5} \\div \\frac{3y}{2}$?" "What is $\\frac{2x}{5} \\operatorname{div}  \\frac{3y}{2}$?"] [:db/cas 17592186069973 :tutorial/content "To divide one rational expression by another, we multiply by the reciprocal of the divisor. Just like with fractions!\n\nFor example, let's divide $x$ by $\\dfrac{3}{y}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\nx \\div \\dfrac{3}{y}\n$$\n\nTo do this, we just need to multiply by the reciprocal of the divisor. The divisor is $\\dfrac{3}{y},$ and to get the reciprocal, we just flip the fraction upside down: $\\dfrac{y}{3}.$ Multiplying by the reciprocal of the divisor, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x÷\\frac{3}{y} &= x\\cdot \\frac{y}{3} \\\\ &= \\frac{xy}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's see some more examples!" "To divide one rational expression by another, we multiply by the reciprocal of the divisor. Just like with fractions!\n\nFor example, let's divide $x$ by $\\dfrac{3}{y}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\nx \\operatorname{div}  \\dfrac{3}{y}\n$$\n\nTo do this, we just need to multiply by the reciprocal of the divisor. The divisor is $\\dfrac{3}{y},$ and to get the reciprocal, we just flip the fraction upside down: $\\dfrac{y}{3}.$ Multiplying by the reciprocal of the divisor, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x÷\\frac{3}{y} &= x\\cdot \\frac{y}{3} \\\\ &= \\frac{xy}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's see some more examples!"] [:db/cas 17592186070032 :tutorial/content "In some cases, applying integration by parts can lead to a cyclic pattern, where the original integral reappears after a few steps. This cyclic behavior allows us to solve the integral by forming an equation that can be solved algebraically.\n\nTo illustrate, let's consider the following example:\n\n$$\nI = \\int \\dfrac{\\ln x}{x} \\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nLet's try using the method of integration by parts. We suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x & \\,⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x} & \\,⇒\\,v=\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the integration by parts formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡x(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)-\\int (\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right)\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={ln}^{2}⁡x-\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={ln}^{2}⁡x-I\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now have the same integral $I$ on both sides of the equality. Thinking about $I$ as some unknown variable, we can easily solve this equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & ={ln}^{2}⁡x-I \\\\ I+I & ={ln}^{2}⁡x \\\\ 2I & ={ln}^{2}⁡x \\\\ I & =\\frac{{ln}^{2}⁡x}{2}+C\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we added a constant of integration right at the very end of the calculation." "In some cases, applying integration by parts can lead to a cyclic pattern, where the original integral reappears after a few steps. This cyclic behavior allows us to solve the integral by forming an equation that can be solved algebraically.\n\nTo illustrate, let's consider the following example:\n\n$$\nI = \\int \\dfrac{\\ln x}{x} \\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nLet's try using the method of integration by parts. We suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x & \\,⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x} & \\,⇒\\,v=\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the integration by parts formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡x(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)-\\int (\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right)\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\ln }^{2}⁡x-\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\ln }^{2}⁡x-I\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now have the same integral $I$ on both sides of the equality. Thinking about $I$ as some unknown variable, we can easily solve this equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & ={\\ln }^{2}⁡x-I \\\\ I+I & ={\\ln }^{2}⁡x \\\\ 2I & ={\\ln }^{2}⁡x \\\\ I & =\\frac{{\\ln }^{2}⁡x}{2}+C\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we added a constant of integration right at the very end of the calculation."] [:db/cas 17592186070221 :answer/value "3 \\div \\frac{1}{4}" "3 \\operatorname{div}  \\frac{1}{4}"] [:db/cas 17592186070222 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4} \\div 3" "\\frac{1}{4} \\operatorname{div}  3"] [:db/cas 17592186070225 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3} \\div 7" "\\frac{1}{3} \\operatorname{div}  7"] [:db/cas 17592186070228 :answer/value "7 \\div \\frac{1}{3}" "7 \\operatorname{div}  \\frac{1}{3}"] [:db/cas 17592186072405 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3} \\div 5 = \\frac{5}{15}" "\\frac{1}{3} \\operatorname{div}  5 = \\frac{5}{15}"] [:db/cas 17592186072406 :answer/value "\\frac{5}{3} \\div 5 = \\frac{1}{3}" "\\frac{5}{3} \\operatorname{div}  5 = \\frac{1}{3}"] [:db/cas 17592186072407 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5} \\div 3 = \\frac{1}{15}" "\\frac{1}{5} \\operatorname{div}  3 = \\frac{1}{15}"] [:db/cas 17592186072408 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5} \\div 3 = \\frac{3}{15}" "\\frac{1}{5} \\operatorname{div}  3 = \\frac{3}{15}"] [:db/cas 17592186072409 :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5} \\div 3 = \\frac{1}{5}" "\\frac{3}{5} \\operatorname{div}  3 = \\frac{1}{5}"] [:db/cas 17592186073415 :question/worked-solution "To find out the height of the pile of boxes, we have to multiply $\\dfrac{3}{4}$ by $5.$\n\nTo multiply a fraction by a whole number, we multiply the fraction's numerator by the whole number and keep the denominator the same:\n\n$$\n5 \\times \\dfrac34 = \\dfrac{5\\times 3}{4} = \\dfrac{15}{4}\n$$\n\nFinally, we then convert $\\dfrac {15}{4}$ to a mixed number:\n\n$$\n15 \\div 4 = 3\\,\\textrm{R}\\,3 = 3\\,\\dfrac{3}{4}\n$$\n\nSo, Greg's stack of boxes was $3\\,\\dfrac{3}{4}\\,\\textrm{ft}$ high." "To find out the height of the pile of boxes, we have to multiply $\\dfrac{3}{4}$ by $5.$\n\nTo multiply a fraction by a whole number, we multiply the fraction's numerator by the whole number and keep the denominator the same:\n\n$$\n5 \\times \\dfrac34 = \\dfrac{5\\times 3}{4} = \\dfrac{15}{4}\n$$\n\nFinally, we then convert $\\dfrac {15}{4}$ to a mixed number:\n\n$$\n15 \\operatorname{div}  4 = 3\\,\\textrm{R}\\,3 = 3\\,\\dfrac{3}{4}\n$$\n\nSo, Greg's stack of boxes was $3\\,\\dfrac{3}{4}\\,\\textrm{ft}$ high."] [:db/cas 17592186073416 :question/worked-solution "To find out how long Mr. Stewart watches the show each week, we need to multiply $\\dfrac{5}{6}$ by $5.$ \n\nTo multiply a fraction by a whole number, we multiply the fraction's numerator by the whole number and keep the denominator the same:\n\n$$\n5\\times\\dfrac56 = \\dfrac{5\\times 5}{6} = \\dfrac{25}6\n$$\n\nFinally, we then convert $\\dfrac {25}{6}$ to a mixed number:\n\n$$\n25 \\div 6 = 4\\,\\textrm{R}\\,1 = 4\\,\\dfrac{1}{6}\n$$\n\nSo, Mr. Stewart spends a total of  $4\\,\\dfrac{1}{6}$ hours watching the show, which lies between $4$ and $5$ hours." "To find out how long Mr. Stewart watches the show each week, we need to multiply $\\dfrac{5}{6}$ by $5.$ \n\nTo multiply a fraction by a whole number, we multiply the fraction's numerator by the whole number and keep the denominator the same:\n\n$$\n5\\times\\dfrac56 = \\dfrac{5\\times 5}{6} = \\dfrac{25}6\n$$\n\nFinally, we then convert $\\dfrac {25}{6}$ to a mixed number:\n\n$$\n25 \\operatorname{div}  6 = 4\\,\\textrm{R}\\,1 = 4\\,\\dfrac{1}{6}\n$$\n\nSo, Mr. Stewart spends a total of  $4\\,\\dfrac{1}{6}$ hours watching the show, which lies between $4$ and $5$ hours."] [:db/cas 17592186073421 :question/problem "![](images/f5/f5a23469d826e99bc4975a4f2901a18b8af46fb1afbe4315d34a1d82d075ddb2.png)\n\nUse the area model above to find the value of $9\\div\\dfrac 1 3$." "![](images/f5/f5a23469d826e99bc4975a4f2901a18b8af46fb1afbe4315d34a1d82d075ddb2.png)\n\nUse the area model above to find the value of $9\\operatorname{div} \\dfrac 1 3$."] [:db/cas 17592186073421 :question/worked-solution "The area of a rectangle equals its length multiplied by its width.\n\nFor the given rectangle:\n\n- its length is $27$\n- its width is $\\dfrac{1}{3}$\n- its area is ${9}$\n\nTherefore, this area model shows the following multiplication problem:\n\n$$\n27\\times\\dfrac 1 {3} = 9\n$$\n\nUsing the relationship between multiplication and division, we can turn this multiplication problem into the following division problem:\n\n$$\n27 = 9\\div \\dfrac 13\n$$\n\nSo, the answer is $27$." "The area of a rectangle equals its length multiplied by its width.\n\nFor the given rectangle:\n\n- its length is $27$\n- its width is $\\dfrac{1}{3}$\n- its area is ${9}$\n\nTherefore, this area model shows the following multiplication problem:\n\n$$\n27\\times\\dfrac 1 {3} = 9\n$$\n\nUsing the relationship between multiplication and division, we can turn this multiplication problem into the following division problem:\n\n$$\n27 = 9\\operatorname{div}  \\dfrac 13\n$$\n\nSo, the answer is $27$."] [:db/cas 17592186073422 :question/problem "Draw an area model that could be used to represent the division problem $\\dfrac 1 {8}\\div 5 = \\dfrac 1 {40}$." "Draw an area model that could be used to represent the division problem $\\dfrac 1 {8}\\operatorname{div}  5 = \\dfrac 1 {40}$."] [:db/cas 17592186073422 :question/worked-solution "Our division problem is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 1 {8} \\div 5 = \\dfrac 1{40}\n$$\n\nUsing the relationship between multiplication and division, we can turn this division problem into the following multiplication problems:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 1{8} = \\dfrac 1 {40} \\times 5 \\qquad\\textrm{or}\\qquad \\dfrac 1{8} = 5 \\times \\dfrac 1 {40}\n$$\n\nTo describe these multiplication problems using an area model, we need a rectangle with the following properties:\n\n- its area is $\\dfrac 1{8}$\n- its sides have lengths $\\dfrac1{40}$ and $5$\n\nAn area model that represents this multiplication problem could be the following:\n\n![](images/08/08643d8599a91b4976fefc2a0c89eefa794e29e25256851220aaea1d40622891.png)" "Our division problem is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 1 {8} \\operatorname{div}  5 = \\dfrac 1{40}\n$$\n\nUsing the relationship between multiplication and division, we can turn this division problem into the following multiplication problems:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 1{8} = \\dfrac 1 {40} \\times 5 \\qquad\\textrm{or}\\qquad \\dfrac 1{8} = 5 \\times \\dfrac 1 {40}\n$$\n\nTo describe these multiplication problems using an area model, we need a rectangle with the following properties:\n\n- its area is $\\dfrac 1{8}$\n- its sides have lengths $\\dfrac1{40}$ and $5$\n\nAn area model that represents this multiplication problem could be the following:\n\n![](images/08/08643d8599a91b4976fefc2a0c89eefa794e29e25256851220aaea1d40622891.png)"] [:db/cas 17592186073423 :question/worked-solution "The area of a rectangle equals its length multiplied by its width.\n\nFor the given rectangle:\n\n- its length is $3$\n- its width is $\\dfrac{1}{24}$\n- its area is $\\dfrac{1}{8}$\n\nTherefore, the area model represents the following multiplication problems:\n\n$$\n3 \\times {\\color{blue}\\dfrac{1}{24}} = \\dfrac{1}{8} \\qquad\\textrm{or}\\qquad {\\color{blue}\\dfrac{1}{24}} \\times 3 = \\dfrac{1}{8}\n$$\n\nUsing the relationship between multiplication and division, we can turn this multiplication problem into a division problem where the quotient equals\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}\\dfrac{1}{24}}{:}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\bbox[5pt, border: 1pt solid black]{\\dfrac{1}{8} \\div 3} = {\\color{blue}\\dfrac{1}{24}}\n$$\n\nSo, the answer is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{8} \\div 3\n$$" "The area of a rectangle equals its length multiplied by its width.\n\nFor the given rectangle:\n\n- its length is $3$\n- its width is $\\dfrac{1}{24}$\n- its area is $\\dfrac{1}{8}$\n\nTherefore, the area model represents the following multiplication problems:\n\n$$\n3 \\times {\\color{blue}\\dfrac{1}{24}} = \\dfrac{1}{8} \\qquad\\textrm{or}\\qquad {\\color{blue}\\dfrac{1}{24}} \\times 3 = \\dfrac{1}{8}\n$$\n\nUsing the relationship between multiplication and division, we can turn this multiplication problem into a division problem where the quotient equals\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}\\dfrac{1}{24}}{:}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\bbox[5pt, border: 1pt solid black]{\\dfrac{1}{8} \\operatorname{div}  3} = {\\color{blue}\\dfrac{1}{24}}\n$$\n\nSo, the answer is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{8} \\operatorname{div}  3\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186073447 :question/worked-solution "All of the answer choices have the second number equal to $3.$ So our equivalent ratio is of the form $\\,\\boxed{ {\\phantom{0}}} : 3.$  \n\nTo find a ratio that's equivalent to $12 : 9,$ we divide both numbers by the same value. In particular, we need to get $3$ from ${\\color{red}{9}},$ so we divide ${\\color{red}{9}}$ by $3.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12 &  & : & \\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,9 &  \\\\ \\,\\,↓÷\\,\\,3 &  &  &  & \\,\\,\\,↓÷\\,\\,3 &  \\\\ \\,\\boxed{\\text{\\phantom{0}}} &  & : &  & \\,\\,\\,3 & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n So to get $\\,\\boxed{ {\\phantom{0}}}$ from ${\\color{blue}{12}},$ we divide $12$ by $3$ as well:\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}12} \\div 3 =4\n$$\n\nTherefore, $12:9$ is equivalent to $4:3.$ \n\nSo, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" "All of the answer choices have the second number equal to $3.$ So our equivalent ratio is of the form $\\,\\boxed{ {\\phantom{0}}} : 3.$  \n\nTo find a ratio that's equivalent to $12 : 9,$ we divide both numbers by the same value. In particular, we need to get $3$ from ${\\color{red}{9}},$ so we divide ${\\color{red}{9}}$ by $3.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12 &  & : & \\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,9 &  \\\\ \\,\\,↓÷\\,\\,3 &  &  &  & \\,\\,\\,↓÷\\,\\,3 &  \\\\ \\,\\boxed{\\text{\\phantom{0}}} &  & : &  & \\,\\,\\,3 & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n So to get $\\,\\boxed{ {\\phantom{0}}}$ from ${\\color{blue}{12}},$ we divide $12$ by $3$ as well:\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}12} \\operatorname{div}  3 =4\n$$\n\nTherefore, $12:9$ is equivalent to $4:3.$ \n\nSo, the correct answer is \"II only.\""] [:db/cas 17592186073454 :question/worked-solution "We start with the ratio ${\\color{blue}6}:{\\color{red}18}$ and write it as a fraction:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{{\\color{blue}6}}{{\\color{red}18}}\n$$\n\nSo the numerator represents the left-hand entry (${\\color{blue}6}$), and the denominator represents the right-hand entry (${\\color{red}18}$).\n\nNow we divide the numerator and the denominator by $6$ to find an equivalent ratio:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{{\\color{blue}{6}}\\div 6}{{\\color{red}{18}}\\div 6} = \\dfrac{{\\color{blue}1}}{{\\color{red}3}}\n$$\n\nWe can generate equivalent ratios by finding equivalent fractions:\n\n- To create a denominator of $24,$ we multiply the numerator and denominator of the previous fraction by $8{:}$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{{\\color{blue}{1}}\\times 8}{{\\color{red}{3}}\\times 8} = \\dfrac{\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}8}}{{\\color{red}24}}\n$$\n\n- To create a numerator of $20,$ we multiply the numerator and denominator of our fraction by $20{:}$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{{\\color{blue}{1}}\\times 20}{{\\color{red}{3}}\\times 20} = \\dfrac{{\\color{blue}20}}{\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{red}60}}\n$$\n\nFinally, we fill in the ratio table:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{blue}6}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{red}18}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}8}$</td> <td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{red}24}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{blue}20}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{red}60}$</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nSo, from top to bottom, the missing numbers are $8$ and $60.$" "We start with the ratio ${\\color{blue}6}:{\\color{red}18}$ and write it as a fraction:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{{\\color{blue}6}}{{\\color{red}18}}\n$$\n\nSo the numerator represents the left-hand entry (${\\color{blue}6}$), and the denominator represents the right-hand entry (${\\color{red}18}$).\n\nNow we divide the numerator and the denominator by $6$ to find an equivalent ratio:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{{\\color{blue}{6}}\\operatorname{div}  6}{{\\color{red}{18}}\\operatorname{div}  6} = \\dfrac{{\\color{blue}1}}{{\\color{red}3}}\n$$\n\nWe can generate equivalent ratios by finding equivalent fractions:\n\n- To create a denominator of $24,$ we multiply the numerator and denominator of the previous fraction by $8{:}$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{{\\color{blue}{1}}\\times 8}{{\\color{red}{3}}\\times 8} = \\dfrac{\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}8}}{{\\color{red}24}}\n$$\n\n- To create a numerator of $20,$ we multiply the numerator and denominator of our fraction by $20{:}$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{{\\color{blue}{1}}\\times 20}{{\\color{red}{3}}\\times 20} = \\dfrac{{\\color{blue}20}}{\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{red}60}}\n$$\n\nFinally, we fill in the ratio table:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{blue}6}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{red}18}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}8}$</td> <td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{red}24}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{blue}20}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{red}60}$</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nSo, from top to bottom, the missing numbers are $8$ and $60.$"] [:db/cas 17592186073456 :question/worked-solution "We start with the ratio ${\\color{blue}12}:{\\color{red}4}$ and write it as a fraction:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{{\\color{blue}12}}{{\\color{red}4}}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow we divide the numerator and the denominator by $4$ to find an equivalent ratio:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{{\\color{blue}{12}}\\div 4}{{\\color{red}{4}}\\div 4} = \\dfrac{{\\color{blue}3}}{{\\color{red}1}}\n$$\n\nWe can generate ratios that are equivalent to $3:1$ by finding more equivalent fractions: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3\\times 3}{1\\times 3} & =\\frac{9}{3} \\\\ \\frac{3\\times 5}{1\\times 5} & =\\frac{15}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we fill in the ratio table:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Reward (in dollars)</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Hours of work</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}9}$</td> <td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$12$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$15$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{red}5}$</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nSo, from top to bottom, the missing numbers are $9$ and $5.$" "We start with the ratio ${\\color{blue}12}:{\\color{red}4}$ and write it as a fraction:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{{\\color{blue}12}}{{\\color{red}4}}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow we divide the numerator and the denominator by $4$ to find an equivalent ratio:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{{\\color{blue}{12}}\\operatorname{div}  4}{{\\color{red}{4}}\\operatorname{div}  4} = \\dfrac{{\\color{blue}3}}{{\\color{red}1}}\n$$\n\nWe can generate ratios that are equivalent to $3:1$ by finding more equivalent fractions: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3\\times 3}{1\\times 3} & =\\frac{9}{3} \\\\ \\frac{3\\times 5}{1\\times 5} & =\\frac{15}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we fill in the ratio table:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Reward (in dollars)</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Hours of work</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}9}$</td> <td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$12$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$15$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{red}5}$</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nSo, from top to bottom, the missing numbers are $9$ and $5.$"] [:db/cas 17592186073462 :question/problem "What model could represent the division problem below?\n\n$$\n3 \\div \\dfrac{1}{6} = 18\n$$" "What model could represent the division problem below?\n\n$$\n3 \\operatorname{div}  \\dfrac{1}{6} = 18\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186073462 :question/worked-solution "Consider the following $\\color{red}3$ wholes:\n\n![](images/23/235e19c4e3231eb22e6d43ccadcd90c5694077ec4ce562e6c4f188cfff77545f.png)\n\nTo calculate ${\\color{red}3} \\div \\dfrac{1}{\\color{blue}6}$, we need to divide each of $\\color{red}3$ wholes into $\\color{blue}6$ equal parts, as shown below. This gives us the corresponding model for the given division problem.\n\n![](images/3b/3b3551ad59a9d5abe2bf03e350f373412959293c68a4f059123fafcbabf72936.png)\n\nWe get ${\\color{red}3} \\times {\\color{blue}6}= 18$ pieces in total." "Consider the following $\\color{red}3$ wholes:\n\n![](images/23/235e19c4e3231eb22e6d43ccadcd90c5694077ec4ce562e6c4f188cfff77545f.png)\n\nTo calculate ${\\color{red}3} \\operatorname{div}  \\dfrac{1}{\\color{blue}6}$, we need to divide each of $\\color{red}3$ wholes into $\\color{blue}6$ equal parts, as shown below. This gives us the corresponding model for the given division problem.\n\n![](images/3b/3b3551ad59a9d5abe2bf03e350f373412959293c68a4f059123fafcbabf72936.png)\n\nWe get ${\\color{red}3} \\times {\\color{blue}6}= 18$ pieces in total."] [:db/cas 17592186073463 :question/problem "What is the value of $\\dfrac{1}{4} \\div 3 ?$" "What is the value of $\\dfrac{1}{4} \\operatorname{div}  3 ?$"] [:db/cas 17592186073463 :question/worked-solution "To divide a unit fraction by a whole number, we multiply the fraction's denominator by that whole number:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{4} \\div 3  = \\dfrac{1}{4 \\times 3} = \\dfrac{1}{12}\n$$" "To divide a unit fraction by a whole number, we multiply the fraction's denominator by that whole number:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{4} \\operatorname{div}  3  = \\dfrac{1}{4 \\times 3} = \\dfrac{1}{12}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186073465 :question/problem "What is the value of $\\dfrac{1}{16} \\div 5 ?$" "What is the value of $\\dfrac{1}{16} \\operatorname{div}  5 ?$"] [:db/cas 17592186073465 :question/worked-solution "To divide a unit fraction by a whole number, we multiply the fraction's denominator by that whole number:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{16} \\div 5  = \\dfrac{1}{16 \\times 5} = \\dfrac{1}{80}\n$$" "To divide a unit fraction by a whole number, we multiply the fraction's denominator by that whole number:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{16} \\operatorname{div}  5  = \\dfrac{1}{16 \\times 5} = \\dfrac{1}{80}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186073466 :question/worked-solution "To determine the amount of ham on each pizza, we must divide the amount of ham by the number of pizzas:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{2} \\div 4\n$$\n\nTo divide a unit fraction by a whole number, we multiply the fraction's denominator by that whole number:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{2} \\div 4  = \\dfrac{1}{2 \\times 4} = \\dfrac{1}{8}\n$$\n\nTherefore, Joshua puts $\\dfrac{1}{8} \\, \\textrm{kg}$ of ham on each pizza." "To determine the amount of ham on each pizza, we must divide the amount of ham by the number of pizzas:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{2} \\operatorname{div}  4\n$$\n\nTo divide a unit fraction by a whole number, we multiply the fraction's denominator by that whole number:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{2} \\operatorname{div}  4  = \\dfrac{1}{2 \\times 4} = \\dfrac{1}{8}\n$$\n\nTherefore, Joshua puts $\\dfrac{1}{8} \\, \\textrm{kg}$ of ham on each pizza."] [:db/cas 17592186073467 :question/problem "Each of the three shapes in the model below represents one whole.\n\n![](images/22/222af9ec253a6624ece646225db926069cd7be3ac416fe846a9216360981f8cb.png)\n\n \n\nFrom left to right, what numbers are missing from the division problem below?\n$$\n\\boxed{ {\\hspace{0.167em} \\phantom{0} \\hspace{0.167em}}} \\div \\dfrac{1}{4} = \\boxed{ {\\hspace{0.167em} \\phantom{0} \\hspace{0.167em}}}\n$$" "Each of the three shapes in the model below represents one whole.\n\n![](images/22/222af9ec253a6624ece646225db926069cd7be3ac416fe846a9216360981f8cb.png)\n\n \n\nFrom left to right, what numbers are missing from the division problem below?\n$$\n\\boxed{ {\\hspace{0.167em} \\phantom{0} \\hspace{0.167em}}} \\operatorname{div}  \\dfrac{1}{4} = \\boxed{ {\\hspace{0.167em} \\phantom{0} \\hspace{0.167em}}}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186073467 :question/worked-solution "In the model, there are $\\color{red}3$ shapes. Each shape consists of $4$ pieces that each represent $\\dfrac{1}{4}$ of a whole. There are $\\color{blue}12$ pieces in total.\n\nThe model tells us that when we divide $\\color{red}3$ wholes into quarters, we will be left with $\\color{blue}12$ pieces.\n\nTherefore, the division problem is:\n$$\n\\boxed{ {\\hspace{0.167em} {\\color{red}3} \\hspace{0.167em}}} \\div \\dfrac{1}{4} = \\boxed{ {\\hspace{0.167em} {\\color{blue}12} \\hspace{0.167em}}}\n$$\n\nSo, the missing numbers are $\\color{red}3$ and $\\color{blue}12.$" "In the model, there are $\\color{red}3$ shapes. Each shape consists of $4$ pieces that each represent $\\dfrac{1}{4}$ of a whole. There are $\\color{blue}12$ pieces in total.\n\nThe model tells us that when we divide $\\color{red}3$ wholes into quarters, we will be left with $\\color{blue}12$ pieces.\n\nTherefore, the division problem is:\n$$\n\\boxed{ {\\hspace{0.167em} {\\color{red}3} \\hspace{0.167em}}} \\operatorname{div}  \\dfrac{1}{4} = \\boxed{ {\\hspace{0.167em} {\\color{blue}12} \\hspace{0.167em}}}\n$$\n\nSo, the missing numbers are $\\color{red}3$ and $\\color{blue}12.$"] [:db/cas 17592186073468 :question/problem "![](images/46/4644be3ee3652c8a52d62e9d849d107e691dbf1f184ea247f02552af108b2e04.png)\n\n \n\nThe model above represents $3$ wholes. Use this model to calculate the value of $3 \\div \\dfrac{1}{5}.$" "![](images/46/4644be3ee3652c8a52d62e9d849d107e691dbf1f184ea247f02552af108b2e04.png)\n\n \n\nThe model above represents $3$ wholes. Use this model to calculate the value of $3 \\operatorname{div}  \\dfrac{1}{5}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186073468 :question/worked-solution "To calculate ${\\color{red}3} \\div \\dfrac{1}{\\color{blue}5}$, we need to divide each of $\\color{red}3$ wholes into $\\color{blue}5$ equal parts, as shown below.\n\n![](images/32/327833c4954c43c2093cb31a03cef01e66389e7cafc3bb2d4cd174e0f7e64f5d.png)\n\n \n\nWe get ${\\color{red}3} \\times {\\color{blue}5} = 15$ pieces in total. \n\nTherefore:\n\n$$\n3 \\div \\dfrac{1}{5} = 15\n$$" "To calculate ${\\color{red}3} \\operatorname{div}  \\dfrac{1}{\\color{blue}5}$, we need to divide each of $\\color{red}3$ wholes into $\\color{blue}5$ equal parts, as shown below.\n\n![](images/32/327833c4954c43c2093cb31a03cef01e66389e7cafc3bb2d4cd174e0f7e64f5d.png)\n\n \n\nWe get ${\\color{red}3} \\times {\\color{blue}5} = 15$ pieces in total. \n\nTherefore:\n\n$$\n3 \\operatorname{div}  \\dfrac{1}{5} = 15\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186073469 :question/problem "Divide $14 \\div \\dfrac{1}{3}.$" "Divide $14 \\operatorname{div}  \\dfrac{1}{3}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186073469 :question/worked-solution "To divide a whole number by a unit fraction, we multiply the whole number by the fraction's denominator:\n\n$$\n14 \\div \\dfrac{1}{3} = 14 \\times 3 = 42\n$$" "To divide a whole number by a unit fraction, we multiply the whole number by the fraction's denominator:\n\n$$\n14 \\operatorname{div}  \\dfrac{1}{3} = 14 \\times 3 = 42\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186073470 :question/problem "What is the value of $5 \\div \\dfrac{1}{15} ?$" "What is the value of $5 \\operatorname{div}  \\dfrac{1}{15} ?$"] [:db/cas 17592186073470 :question/worked-solution "To divide a whole number by a unit fraction, we multiply the whole number by the fraction's denominator:\n\n$$\n5 \\div \\dfrac{1}{15} = 5 \\times 15 = 75\n$$" "To divide a whole number by a unit fraction, we multiply the whole number by the fraction's denominator:\n\n$$\n5 \\operatorname{div}  \\dfrac{1}{15} = 5 \\times 15 = 75\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186073471 :question/worked-solution "To determine the number of pizzas that Johnny can make, we divide the amount of wheat flour by the amount of wheat flour each pizza needs.\n\n$$\n5 \\div  \\dfrac{1}{10}\n$$\n\nTo divide a whole number by a unit fraction, we multiply the whole number by the fraction's denominator:\n\n$$\n5 \\div  \\dfrac{1}{10} = 5 \\times 10 = 50\n$$\n\nTherefore, Johnny can make $50$ pizzas." "To determine the number of pizzas that Johnny can make, we divide the amount of wheat flour by the amount of wheat flour each pizza needs.\n\n$$\n5 \\operatorname{div}   \\dfrac{1}{10}\n$$\n\nTo divide a whole number by a unit fraction, we multiply the whole number by the fraction's denominator:\n\n$$\n5 \\operatorname{div}   \\dfrac{1}{10} = 5 \\times 10 = 50\n$$\n\nTherefore, Johnny can make $50$ pizzas."] [:db/cas 17592186073476 :question/problem "By rounding the dividend to the nearest whole number, estimate the value of $94.8 \\div 5$." "By rounding the dividend to the nearest whole number, estimate the value of $94.8 \\operatorname{div}  5$."] [:db/cas 17592186073476 :question/worked-solution "First, we round the dividend to the nearest whole number:\n$94.8 \\rightarrow 95$\n\nHence, we need to find the value of $95 \\div 5$. Computing the quotient, we get:\n\n| $1$$9$$5$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{9 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 5}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$5$$4$$5$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$4$$5$$0$ |  |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n95 \\div 5 = 19\n$$\n\nTherefore, $94.8 \\div 5$ is approximately equal to $19$." "First, we round the dividend to the nearest whole number:\n$94.8 \\rightarrow 95$\n\nHence, we need to find the value of $95 \\operatorname{div}  5$. Computing the quotient, we get:\n\n| $1$$9$$5$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{9 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 5}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$5$$4$$5$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$4$$5$$0$ |  |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n95 \\operatorname{div}  5 = 19\n$$\n\nTherefore, $94.8 \\operatorname{div}  5$ is approximately equal to $19$."] [:db/cas 17592186073477 :question/problem "Estimate $12.3 \\div 106.8$ by rounding the divisor to the nearest hundred." "Estimate $12.3 \\operatorname{div}  106.8$ by rounding the divisor to the nearest hundred."] [:db/cas 17592186073477 :question/worked-solution "First, we round the divisor to the nearest hundred:\n$106.8 \\rightarrow 100$\n\nNow, we need to compute\n\n$$\n12.3 \\div 100\n$$\n\nThere are two zeros in $100$, so we move the decimal point two places to the left:\n\n$$\n12.3 \\rightarrow 0.123\n$$\n\nTherefore, $12.3 \\div 106.8$ is approximately equal to $0.123 \\,$." "First, we round the divisor to the nearest hundred:\n$106.8 \\rightarrow 100$\n\nNow, we need to compute\n\n$$\n12.3 \\operatorname{div}  100\n$$\n\nThere are two zeros in $100$, so we move the decimal point two places to the left:\n\n$$\n12.3 \\rightarrow 0.123\n$$\n\nTherefore, $12.3 \\operatorname{div}  106.8$ is approximately equal to $0.123 \\,$."] [:db/cas 17592186073478 :question/problem "Estimate the value of $206.6 \\div 3.43$ by rounding each number to the nearest whole number." "Estimate the value of $206.6 \\operatorname{div}  3.43$ by rounding each number to the nearest whole number."] [:db/cas 17592186073478 :question/worked-solution "We round both numbers to the nearest whole number:\n$$\n\\begin{array}{ccc}206.6 & ÷ & 3.43 \\\\ ↓ &  & ↓ \\\\ {\\color{blue}{207}} & ÷ & {\\color{blue}{3}}\\end{array}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the value of\n\n$$\n207 \\div 3\n$$\n\nSo, we go through the long division procedure as usual:\n\n| $6$$9$$3$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{2 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 0 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 7}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$1$$8$$2$$7$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$2$$7$$\\fbox{0}$ |\n| --- |\n\nTherefore, $206.6 \\div 3.43$ is approximately equal to $69$." "We round both numbers to the nearest whole number:\n$$\n\\begin{array}{ccc}206.6 & ÷ & 3.43 \\\\ ↓ &  & ↓ \\\\ {\\color{blue}{207}} & ÷ & {\\color{blue}{3}}\\end{array}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the value of\n\n$$\n207 \\operatorname{div}  3\n$$\n\nSo, we go through the long division procedure as usual:\n\n| $6$$9$$3$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{2 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 0 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 7}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$1$$8$$2$$7$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$2$$7$$\\fbox{0}$ |\n| --- |\n\nTherefore, $206.6 \\operatorname{div}  3.43$ is approximately equal to $69$."] [:db/cas 17592186073479 :question/problem "Estimate the value of $116.8 \\div 5.15$ by rounding each number to the nearest whole number. Round your final answer to the nearest whole number." "Estimate the value of $116.8 \\operatorname{div}  5.15$ by rounding each number to the nearest whole number. Round your final answer to the nearest whole number."] [:db/cas 17592186073479 :question/worked-solution "We round both numbers to the nearest whole number:\n$$\n\\begin{array}{ccc}116.8 & ÷ & 5.15 \\\\ ↓ &  & ↓ \\\\ {\\color{blue}{117}} & ÷ & {\\color{blue}{5}}\\end{array}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the value of\n$117\\div 5$.\n\nSo, we go through the long division procedure as usual:\n\n|  |  | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $5$ | $\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{1 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 1 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 7}$ |  |  |\n| $-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$ | $1$ | $0$ |  |\n|  |  | $1$ | $7$ |\n|  | $-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$ | $1$ | $5$ |\n|  |  |  | $\\fbox{2}$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n117 \\div 5 = 23\\,\\text{R}2\n$$\n\nThe remainder $2$ is closer to $0$ than to the divisor $5$. So, to round to the nearest whole number, we must round down:\n\n$$\n117 \\div 5 \\rightarrow 23\n$$\n\nTherefore, $116.8 \\div 5.15$ is approximately equal to $23$." "We round both numbers to the nearest whole number:\n$$\n\\begin{array}{ccc}116.8 & ÷ & 5.15 \\\\ ↓ &  & ↓ \\\\ {\\color{blue}{117}} & ÷ & {\\color{blue}{5}}\\end{array}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the value of\n$117\\operatorname{div}  5$.\n\nSo, we go through the long division procedure as usual:\n\n|  |  | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $5$ | $\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{1 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 1 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 7}$ |  |  |\n| $-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$ | $1$ | $0$ |  |\n|  |  | $1$ | $7$ |\n|  | $-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$ | $1$ | $5$ |\n|  |  |  | $\\fbox{2}$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n117 \\operatorname{div}  5 = 23\\,\\text{R}2\n$$\n\nThe remainder $2$ is closer to $0$ than to the divisor $5$. So, to round to the nearest whole number, we must round down:\n\n$$\n117 \\operatorname{div}  5 \\rightarrow 23\n$$\n\nTherefore, $116.8 \\operatorname{div}  5.15$ is approximately equal to $23$."] [:db/cas 17592186073486 :question/worked-solution "We have $\\dfrac{1}{4}$ on the left and $\\dfrac{1}{8}$ on the right.\n\n![](images/78/785deacac749a6ed43e5e6a2638677e8ff9e9b58087cb4e471ea85fd50609ce6.png)\n\nThe division operation splits the shaded region on the left into $2$ equal parts. So the shaded part on the left is $2$ times larger than the shaded part on the right.\n\nTherefore, the division problem shown is:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{4} \\div 2 = \\dfrac{1}{8}\n$$" "We have $\\dfrac{1}{4}$ on the left and $\\dfrac{1}{8}$ on the right.\n\n![](images/78/785deacac749a6ed43e5e6a2638677e8ff9e9b58087cb4e471ea85fd50609ce6.png)\n\nThe division operation splits the shaded region on the left into $2$ equal parts. So the shaded part on the left is $2$ times larger than the shaded part on the right.\n\nTherefore, the division problem shown is:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{4} \\operatorname{div}  2 = \\dfrac{1}{8}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186073487 :question/worked-solution "We have $\\dfrac{1}{3}$ on the left and $\\dfrac{1}{15}$ on the right.\n\n![](images/ed/ed5517a9242aed1c5b521ca07772dacbb7c96a73c69d4d9c6214a098742358e2.png)\n\nThe division operation splits the shaded region on the left into $5$ equal parts. So the shaded part on the left is $5$ times larger than the shaded part on the right. \n\nTherefore, the division problem shown is:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{3} \\div {\\color{blue}{5}} = \\dfrac{1}{15}\n$$\n\nSo, the missing number is $\\color{blue}5.$" "We have $\\dfrac{1}{3}$ on the left and $\\dfrac{1}{15}$ on the right.\n\n![](images/ed/ed5517a9242aed1c5b521ca07772dacbb7c96a73c69d4d9c6214a098742358e2.png)\n\nThe division operation splits the shaded region on the left into $5$ equal parts. So the shaded part on the left is $5$ times larger than the shaded part on the right. \n\nTherefore, the division problem shown is:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{3} \\operatorname{div}  {\\color{blue}{5}} = \\dfrac{1}{15}\n$$\n\nSo, the missing number is $\\color{blue}5.$"] [:db/cas 17592186073489 :question/problem "Use the model below to calculate $\\dfrac{1}{4} \\div 4.$\n\n![](images/8e/8eccf163bffabb43593188590a1f995544f3d1ac0aebcdd87dce638b230f8165.png)" "Use the model below to calculate $\\dfrac{1}{4} \\operatorname{div}  4.$\n\n![](images/8e/8eccf163bffabb43593188590a1f995544f3d1ac0aebcdd87dce638b230f8165.png)"] [:db/cas 17592186073489 :question/worked-solution "Since we need to divide by $\\color{blue}4$, we split the whole into $\\color{blue}4$ equal parts horizontally.\n\n![](images/a3/a34a3fb198ef2ea79f8baf8f0acbdbdc179c6259c614824315d3d96e5ea4e921.png)\n\nFrom the picture on the right, we obtain that the shaded part represents $\\dfrac{1}{16}$ of the whole.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{4} \\div 4 = \\dfrac{1}{16}\\,.\n$$" "Since we need to divide by $\\color{blue}4$, we split the whole into $\\color{blue}4$ equal parts horizontally.\n\n![](images/a3/a34a3fb198ef2ea79f8baf8f0acbdbdc179c6259c614824315d3d96e5ea4e921.png)\n\nFrom the picture on the right, we obtain that the shaded part represents $\\dfrac{1}{16}$ of the whole.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{4} \\operatorname{div}  4 = \\dfrac{1}{16}\\,.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186073503 :question/worked-solution "To know how many kilograms they bought, we have to calculate the sum:\n\n$$\n3 + \\dfrac{11}{3}\n$$\n\nTo add\n\n$$\n3 + \\dfrac{11}{\\color{blue}{3}}\n$$\n\n, we need to express the whole number $3$ as an equivalent fraction with a denominator of ${\\color{blue}{3}}$.\n\nFirst, we write $3$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{1}\n$$\n\nThen, we put this fraction over a denominator of $\\color{blue}3$ by multiplying the numerator and denominator by ${\\color{blue}{3}}$:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3 \\times {\\color{blue}{3}}}{1 \\times{\\color{blue}{3}}} = \\dfrac{9}{3}\n$$\n\nSo now, we have to find the following sum:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{9}{3} + \\dfrac{11}{3}\n$$\n\nTo add two fractions with like denominators, we add the numerators and keep the denominators the same.\n\n$$\n\\frac{9}{3} + \\frac{11}{3} = \\frac{9 + 11}{3} = \\frac{20}{3}\n$$\n\nFinally, we convert this to a mixed number:\n\n$$\n20 \\div 3 = 6 \\, \\textrm{R} \\, 2 = 6 \\, \\dfrac{2}{3}\n$$\n\nThus, Tom and Margaret bought\n\n$$\n6 \\, \\dfrac{2}{3}\n$$\n\nkilograms of fruit." "To know how many kilograms they bought, we have to calculate the sum:\n\n$$\n3 + \\dfrac{11}{3}\n$$\n\nTo add\n\n$$\n3 + \\dfrac{11}{\\color{blue}{3}}\n$$\n\n, we need to express the whole number $3$ as an equivalent fraction with a denominator of ${\\color{blue}{3}}$.\n\nFirst, we write $3$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{1}\n$$\n\nThen, we put this fraction over a denominator of $\\color{blue}3$ by multiplying the numerator and denominator by ${\\color{blue}{3}}$:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3 \\times {\\color{blue}{3}}}{1 \\times{\\color{blue}{3}}} = \\dfrac{9}{3}\n$$\n\nSo now, we have to find the following sum:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{9}{3} + \\dfrac{11}{3}\n$$\n\nTo add two fractions with like denominators, we add the numerators and keep the denominators the same.\n\n$$\n\\frac{9}{3} + \\frac{11}{3} = \\frac{9 + 11}{3} = \\frac{20}{3}\n$$\n\nFinally, we convert this to a mixed number:\n\n$$\n20 \\operatorname{div}  3 = 6 \\, \\textrm{R} \\, 2 = 6 \\, \\dfrac{2}{3}\n$$\n\nThus, Tom and Margaret bought\n\n$$\n6 \\, \\dfrac{2}{3}\n$$\n\nkilograms of fruit."] [:db/cas 17592186073522 :question/worked-solution "To subtract $2$ from\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{31}{\\color{blue}{9}}\n$$\n\n, we need to express the whole number $2$ as an equivalent fraction with a denominator of ${\\color{blue}{9}}$.\n\nFirst, we write $2$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2}{1}\n$$\n\nThen, we put this fraction over a denominator of $\\color{blue}9$ by multiplying the numerator and denominator by ${\\color{blue}{9}}$:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2 \\times {\\color{blue}{9}}}{1\\times{\\color{blue}{9}}} = \\dfrac{18}{9}\n$$\n\nSo now, we have to find:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{31}{9} - \\dfrac{18}{9}\n$$\n\nTo subtract two fractions with like denominators, we subtract the numerators and keep the denominators the same:\n\n$$\n\\frac{31}{9} - \\frac{18}{9} = \\frac{31 - 18}{9} = \\frac{13}{9}\n$$\n\nFinally, we convert to a mixed number:\n\n$$\n13 \\div 9 = 1 \\, \\textrm{R} \\, 4\n$$\n\nSo our final answer is\n\n$$\n1 \\, \\dfrac{4}{9}\n$$" "To subtract $2$ from\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{31}{\\color{blue}{9}}\n$$\n\n, we need to express the whole number $2$ as an equivalent fraction with a denominator of ${\\color{blue}{9}}$.\n\nFirst, we write $2$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2}{1}\n$$\n\nThen, we put this fraction over a denominator of $\\color{blue}9$ by multiplying the numerator and denominator by ${\\color{blue}{9}}$:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2 \\times {\\color{blue}{9}}}{1\\times{\\color{blue}{9}}} = \\dfrac{18}{9}\n$$\n\nSo now, we have to find:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{31}{9} - \\dfrac{18}{9}\n$$\n\nTo subtract two fractions with like denominators, we subtract the numerators and keep the denominators the same:\n\n$$\n\\frac{31}{9} - \\frac{18}{9} = \\frac{31 - 18}{9} = \\frac{13}{9}\n$$\n\nFinally, we convert to a mixed number:\n\n$$\n13 \\operatorname{div}  9 = 1 \\, \\textrm{R} \\, 4\n$$\n\nSo our final answer is\n\n$$\n1 \\, \\dfrac{4}{9}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186073523 :question/worked-solution "To find out how much juice is left, we have to calculate the difference\n\n$$\n4 - \\dfrac{15}{8}\n$$\n\nTo subtract\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{15}{\\color{blue}{8}}\n$$\n\nfrom $4$, we need to express the whole number $4$ as an equivalent fraction with a denominator of ${\\color{blue}{8}}$.\n\nFirst, we write $4$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4}{1}\n$$\n\nThen, we put this fraction over a denominator of $\\color{blue}8$ by multiplying the numerator and denominator by ${\\color{blue}{8}}$:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4 \\times {\\color{blue}{8}}}{1\\times{\\color{blue}{8}}} = \\dfrac{32}{8}\n$$\n\nSo now, we have to find:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{32}{8} - \\dfrac{15}{8}\n$$\n\nTo subtract two fractions with like denominators, we subtract the numerators and keep the denominators the same:\n\n$$\n\\frac{32}{8} - \\frac{15}{8} = \\frac{32 - 15}{8} = \\frac{17}{8}\n$$\n\nFinally, we convert to a mixed number:\n\n$$\n17 \\div 8 = 2 \\, \\textrm{R} \\, 1\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n2 \\, \\dfrac{1}{8}\n$$\n\nliters of juice is left." "To find out how much juice is left, we have to calculate the difference\n\n$$\n4 - \\dfrac{15}{8}\n$$\n\nTo subtract\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{15}{\\color{blue}{8}}\n$$\n\nfrom $4$, we need to express the whole number $4$ as an equivalent fraction with a denominator of ${\\color{blue}{8}}$.\n\nFirst, we write $4$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4}{1}\n$$\n\nThen, we put this fraction over a denominator of $\\color{blue}8$ by multiplying the numerator and denominator by ${\\color{blue}{8}}$:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4 \\times {\\color{blue}{8}}}{1\\times{\\color{blue}{8}}} = \\dfrac{32}{8}\n$$\n\nSo now, we have to find:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{32}{8} - \\dfrac{15}{8}\n$$\n\nTo subtract two fractions with like denominators, we subtract the numerators and keep the denominators the same:\n\n$$\n\\frac{32}{8} - \\frac{15}{8} = \\frac{32 - 15}{8} = \\frac{17}{8}\n$$\n\nFinally, we convert to a mixed number:\n\n$$\n17 \\operatorname{div}  8 = 2 \\, \\textrm{R} \\, 1\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n2 \\, \\dfrac{1}{8}\n$$\n\nliters of juice is left."] [:db/cas 17592186073524 :question/worked-solution "To find out how long each of the pieces is, we need to divide $85$ by $5$.\n\nWe start by writing down the question using long division notation:\n\n| $5$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{8 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 5}$ |  |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nFirst, we consider the tens:\n\n| $\\color{blue}1$$5$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{{\\color{red}8} {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 5}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$5$$\\color{lightgray}\\downarrow$$3$$\\color{lightgray}5$ | Divide: ${\\color{red}8} \\div 5 = {\\color{blue}1}\\,\\text{R}\\,3$Write over tens: $\\color{blue}1$Multiply: $5 \\times {\\color{blue}1} = 5$Subtract: ${\\color{red}8} - 5 = 3$Bring down: $5$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nNext, we consider the ones:\n\n| $1$$\\color{blue}7$$5$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{8 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 5}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$5$$\\color{red}3$$\\color{red}5$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$3$$5$$\\fbox{0}$ | Divide: ${\\color{red}35} \\div 5 = {\\color{blue}7}$Write over ones: $\\color{blue}7$Multiply: $5 \\times {\\color{blue}7} = 35$Subtract: ${\\color{red}35} - 35 = \\fbox{0}$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nWe've gone through all the digits in the number $85$, so the division is done.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n85 \\div 5 = 17\n$$\n\nThis means that each piece of rope is $17$ inches long." "To find out how long each of the pieces is, we need to divide $85$ by $5$.\n\nWe start by writing down the question using long division notation:\n\n| $5$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{8 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 5}$ |  |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nFirst, we consider the tens:\n\n| $\\color{blue}1$$5$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{{\\color{red}8} {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 5}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$5$$\\color{lightgray}\\downarrow$$3$$\\color{lightgray}5$ | Divide: ${\\color{red}8} \\operatorname{div}  5 = {\\color{blue}1}\\,\\text{R}\\,3$Write over tens: $\\color{blue}1$Multiply: $5 \\times {\\color{blue}1} = 5$Subtract: ${\\color{red}8} - 5 = 3$Bring down: $5$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nNext, we consider the ones:\n\n| $1$$\\color{blue}7$$5$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{8 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 5}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$5$$\\color{red}3$$\\color{red}5$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$3$$5$$\\fbox{0}$ | Divide: ${\\color{red}35} \\operatorname{div}  5 = {\\color{blue}7}$Write over ones: $\\color{blue}7$Multiply: $5 \\times {\\color{blue}7} = 35$Subtract: ${\\color{red}35} - 35 = \\fbox{0}$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nWe've gone through all the digits in the number $85$, so the division is done.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n85 \\operatorname{div}  5 = 17\n$$\n\nThis means that each piece of rope is $17$ inches long."] [:db/cas 17592186073525 :question/worked-solution "We start by writing down the question using long division notation:\n\n| $7$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{8 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 5}$ |  |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nFirst, we consider the tens:\n\n| $\\color{blue}1$$7$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{{\\color{red}8} {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 5}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$7$$\\color{lightgray}\\downarrow$$1$$\\color{lightgray}5$ | Divide: ${\\color{red}8} \\div 7 = {\\color{blue}1}\\,\\text{R}\\,1$Write over tens: $\\color{blue}1$Multiply: $7 \\times {\\color{blue}1} = 7$Subtract: ${\\color{red}8} - 7 = 1$Bring down: $5$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nNext, we consider the ones:\n\n| $1$$\\color{blue}2$$7$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{8 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 5}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$7$$\\color{red}1$$\\color{red}5$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$1$$4$$\\fbox{1}$ | Divide: ${\\color{red}15} \\div 7 = {\\color{blue}2}\\,\\text{R}\\,1$Write over ones: $\\color{blue}2$Multiply: $7 \\times {\\color{blue}2} = 14$Subtract: ${\\color{red}15} - 14 = \\fbox{1}$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nWe've gone through all the digits in the number $85$, so the division is done. However, we still have a remainder of $1$, so we include this in our final answer.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n85 \\div 7 = 12\\,\\text{R}\\,1\n$$\n\nThe missing digits are $1$, $7$ and $1$:\n\n| $\\fbox{1}$$2$$7$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{8 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 5}$$-\\!\\!\\!$$\\fbox{7}$$\\fbox{1}$$5$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$1$$4$$1$ |\n| --- |" "We start by writing down the question using long division notation:\n\n| $7$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{8 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 5}$ |  |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nFirst, we consider the tens:\n\n| $\\color{blue}1$$7$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{{\\color{red}8} {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 5}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$7$$\\color{lightgray}\\downarrow$$1$$\\color{lightgray}5$ | Divide: ${\\color{red}8} \\operatorname{div}  7 = {\\color{blue}1}\\,\\text{R}\\,1$Write over tens: $\\color{blue}1$Multiply: $7 \\times {\\color{blue}1} = 7$Subtract: ${\\color{red}8} - 7 = 1$Bring down: $5$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nNext, we consider the ones:\n\n| $1$$\\color{blue}2$$7$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{8 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 5}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$7$$\\color{red}1$$\\color{red}5$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$1$$4$$\