d number:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{11}{10} = 1 \\, \\text{R} \\, 1 = 1 \\, \\dfrac{1}{10}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our model could be the following:\n\n![](images/c9/c951b3f3f45c7d502d447d5371286f095b060cade366c01723ea3118ee55da4a.png)" "First, recall that $110\\%$ is the same as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{110}{100}\n$$\n\nNow, we simplify the fraction. Dividing the numerator and denominator by $10$, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{110}{100} = \\dfrac{110 \\operatorname{div}  10}{100 \\operatorname{div}  10} = \\dfrac{11}{10}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{11}{10}\n$$\n\nas a mixed number:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{11}{10} = 1 \\, \\text{R} \\, 1 = 1 \\, \\dfrac{1}{10}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our model could be the following:\n\n![](images/c9/c951b3f3f45c7d502d447d5371286f095b060cade366c01723ea3118ee55da4a.png)"] [:db/cas 17592186073636 :question/worked-solution "To find the number of minutes that the pump takes to empty $5$ pools, we can first find the unit rate of minutes per pool, and then multiply the unit rate by $5$ pools.\n\nTo find the unit rate of minutes per pool, we divide the number of minutes, $15,$ by the total pool volume emptied, $\\dfrac{3}{2},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n15 \\div \\dfrac{3}{2}\n$$\n\nTo divide by a fraction, we multiply by the fraction's reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}15÷\\frac{3}{2} & =15\\times \\frac{2}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{1}\\times \\frac{2}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{15\\times 2}{1\\times 3} \\\\  & =\\frac{30}{3} \\\\  & =30÷3 \\\\  & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the unit rate is $10$ minutes per pool. To find the number of minutes that the pump takes to empty $5$ pools, we multiply the unit rate by $5$ pools and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10\\times 5 & =50.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the pump will take $50$ minutes to empty $5$ pools." "To find the number of minutes that the pump takes to empty $5$ pools, we can first find the unit rate of minutes per pool, and then multiply the unit rate by $5$ pools.\n\nTo find the unit rate of minutes per pool, we divide the number of minutes, $15,$ by the total pool volume emptied, $\\dfrac{3}{2},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n15 \\operatorname{div}  \\dfrac{3}{2}\n$$\n\nTo divide by a fraction, we multiply by the fraction's reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}15÷\\frac{3}{2} & =15\\times \\frac{2}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{1}\\times \\frac{2}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{15\\times 2}{1\\times 3} \\\\  & =\\frac{30}{3} \\\\  & =30÷3 \\\\  & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the unit rate is $10$ minutes per pool. To find the number of minutes that the pump takes to empty $5$ pools, we multiply the unit rate by $5$ pools and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10\\times 5 & =50.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the pump will take $50$ minutes to empty $5$ pools."] [:db/cas 17592186073637 :question/worked-solution "To find the number of dollars that Ava earns if she sells $18$ liters of coffee, we can first find the unit rate of dollars per liter, and then multiply the unit rate by $18$ liters.\n\nTo find the unit rate of dollars per liter, we divide the number of dollars, $\\dfrac{3}{4},$ by the number of liters, $\\dfrac{3}{8},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{4} \\div \\dfrac{3}{8}\n$$\n\nDividing one fraction by another is equivalent to multiplying the fraction by the reciprocal of the second fraction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{4}÷\\frac{3}{8} & =\\frac{3}{4}\\times \\frac{8}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\times 8}{4\\times 3} \\\\  & =\\frac{24}{12} \\\\  & =24÷12 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the unit rate is $2$ dollars per liter. To find the number of dollars that Ava earns selling $18$ liters, we multiply the unit rate by $18$ liters and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\times 18 & =36.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, Ava earns $36$ dollars if she sells $18$ liters of coffee." "To find the number of dollars that Ava earns if she sells $18$ liters of coffee, we can first find the unit rate of dollars per liter, and then multiply the unit rate by $18$ liters.\n\nTo find the unit rate of dollars per liter, we divide the number of dollars, $\\dfrac{3}{4},$ by the number of liters, $\\dfrac{3}{8},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{4} \\operatorname{div}  \\dfrac{3}{8}\n$$\n\nDividing one fraction by another is equivalent to multiplying the fraction by the reciprocal of the second fraction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{4}÷\\frac{3}{8} & =\\frac{3}{4}\\times \\frac{8}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\times 8}{4\\times 3} \\\\  & =\\frac{24}{12} \\\\  & =24÷12 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the unit rate is $2$ dollars per liter. To find the number of dollars that Ava earns selling $18$ liters, we multiply the unit rate by $18$ liters and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\times 18 & =36.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, Ava earns $36$ dollars if she sells $18$ liters of coffee."] [:db/cas 17592186073638 :question/problem "Calculate the quotient of $681\\div 32$ using the relationship between multiplication and division." "Calculate the quotient of $681\\operatorname{div}  32$ using the relationship between multiplication and division."] [:db/cas 17592186073638 :question/worked-solution "Using our knowledge of multiplication:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n32 \\cdot 20 &= 640 \\\\\n32 \\cdot 21 &= 640 + 32 = 672✓ \\\\\n32 \\cdot 22 &= 672 + 32 = 704\\cdot(too big)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $681\\div 32$ has a quotient of ${\\color{blue}{21}}$." "Using our knowledge of multiplication:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n32 \\cdot 20 &= 640 \\\\\n32 \\cdot 21 &= 640 + 32 = 672✓ \\\\\n32 \\cdot 22 &= 672 + 32 = 704\\cdot(too big)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $681\\operatorname{div}  32$ has a quotient of ${\\color{blue}{21}}$."] [:db/cas 17592186073639 :question/problem "Calculate $99 \\div 14$ using the relationship between multiplication and division." "Calculate $99 \\operatorname{div}  14$ using the relationship between multiplication and division."] [:db/cas 17592186073639 :question/worked-solution "Using our knowledge of multiplication:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n14 \\cdot 5 &= 70 \\\\\n14 \\cdot 6 &= 70 + 14 = 84 \\\\\n14 \\cdot 7 &= 84 + 14 = 98✓ \\\\\n14 \\cdot 8 &= 98 + 14 = 112\\cdot(too big)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $99 \\div 14$ has a whole number part of ${\\color{blue}{7}}$.\n\nWe now find the remainder:\n\n$$\n99 - 98 = {\\color{red}{1}}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n99 \\div 14 = {\\color{blue}{7}}\\,\\textrm{R}\\,{\\color{red}{1}}\n$$" "Using our knowledge of multiplication:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n14 \\cdot 5 &= 70 \\\\\n14 \\cdot 6 &= 70 + 14 = 84 \\\\\n14 \\cdot 7 &= 84 + 14 = 98✓ \\\\\n14 \\cdot 8 &= 98 + 14 = 112\\cdot(too big)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $99 \\operatorname{div}  14$ has a whole number part of ${\\color{blue}{7}}$.\n\nWe now find the remainder:\n\n$$\n99 - 98 = {\\color{red}{1}}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n99 \\operatorname{div}  14 = {\\color{blue}{7}}\\,\\textrm{R}\\,{\\color{red}{1}}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186073640 :question/worked-solution "To find how many boxes of $15$ the factory can fill, we need to find the quotient of $460 \\div 15$.\n\nUsing our knowledge of multiplication:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n15 \\cdot 30 &= 450✓ \\\\\n15 \\cdot 31 &= 450 + 15 = 465\\cdot(too big)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $465$ is greater than $460$, the factory can't fill $31$ boxes.\n\nTherefore, the factory can produce $\\color{blue}30$ full boxes of toy cars each day." "To find how many boxes of $15$ the factory can fill, we need to find the quotient of $460 \\operatorname{div}  15$.\n\nUsing our knowledge of multiplication:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n15 \\cdot 30 &= 450✓ \\\\\n15 \\cdot 31 &= 450 + 15 = 465\\cdot(too big)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $465$ is greater than $460$, the factory can't fill $31$ boxes.\n\nTherefore, the factory can produce $\\color{blue}30$ full boxes of toy cars each day."] [:db/cas 17592186073641 :question/worked-solution "The total number of cupcakes to be distributed is\n\n$$\n35+46=81\n$$\n\nTo calculate the number of plates that can be filled, we need to figure out $81\\div 11$.\n\nUsing our knowledge of multiplication:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n11 \\cdot 7 &= 77✓ \\\\\n11 \\cdot 8 &= 77 + 11 = 88\\cdot(too big)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $88$ is greater than $81$, Sean and Martin can't fill $8$ plates. Therefore, $7$ plates can be completely filled with cupcakes.\n\nSince $\\color{blue}7$ plates will carry $77$ cupcakes in total, the number of cupcakes that will remain is\n\n$$\n81 - 77 ={\\color{red}4}\n$$\n\nSo we conclude that $7$ plates can be filled completely, and $4$ cupcakes will remain." "The total number of cupcakes to be distributed is\n\n$$\n35+46=81\n$$\n\nTo calculate the number of plates that can be filled, we need to figure out $81\\operatorname{div}  11$.\n\nUsing our knowledge of multiplication:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n11 \\cdot 7 &= 77✓ \\\\\n11 \\cdot 8 &= 77 + 11 = 88\\cdot(too big)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $88$ is greater than $81$, Sean and Martin can't fill $8$ plates. Therefore, $7$ plates can be completely filled with cupcakes.\n\nSince $\\color{blue}7$ plates will carry $77$ cupcakes in total, the number of cupcakes that will remain is\n\n$$\n81 - 77 ={\\color{red}4}\n$$\n\nSo we conclude that $7$ plates can be filled completely, and $4$ cupcakes will remain."] [:db/cas 17592186073657 :question/worked-solution "We start by writing down the question using long division notation:\n\n| $4$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{4 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 6 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 3}$ |  |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nFirst, we consider the hundreds:\n\n| $\\color{blue}1$$4$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{{\\color{red}4} {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 6 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 3}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$4$$\\color{lightgray}\\downarrow$$0$$\\color{lightgray}6$ | Divide: ${\\color{red}4} \\div 4 = {\\color{blue}1}$Write over hundreds: $\\color{blue}1$Multiply: $4 \\times {\\color{blue}1} = 4$Subtract: ${\\color{red}4} - 4 = 0$Bring down: $6$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nNext, we consider the tens:\n\n| $1$$\\color{blue}1$$4$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{4 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 6 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}}