"![](images/75/759e8da58095015929acb94fa4eb7ca1b981839dc2c0bfe19166f5cbf480dd29.png)\n\nUse the area model above to find the value of $\\frac{1}{6} \\div 3$." "![](images/75/759e8da58095015929acb94fa4eb7ca1b981839dc2c0bfe19166f5cbf480dd29.png)\n\nUse the area model above to find the value of $\\frac{1}{6} \\operatorname{div}  3$."] [:db/cas 17592186074433 :question/problem "Which of the following area models could be used to represent the division problem $4 \\div \\frac{1}{3} = 12$?" "Which of the following area models could be used to represent the division problem $4 \\operatorname{div}  \\frac{1}{3} = 12$?"] [:db/cas 17592186074436 :question/problem "Which of the following area models could be used to represent the division problem $\\frac{1}{10} \\div 3 = \\frac{1}{30}$?" "Which of the following area models could be used to represent the division problem $\\frac{1}{10} \\operatorname{div}  3 = \\frac{1}{30}$?"] [:db/cas 17592186074522 :question/problem "Estimate $6.4 \\div 97.7$ by rounding the divisor to the nearest hundred." "Estimate $6.4 \\operatorname{div}  97.7$ by rounding the divisor to the nearest hundred."] [:db/cas 17592186074942 :tutorial/content "When we compare two ratios, we are comparing how much one quantity is associated with another quantity.\n\nOne way to compare ratios is to write each ratio as a fraction and simplify.\n\nFor example, suppose Store A has $12$ pens and $3$ notebooks, and Store B has $15$ pens and $5$ notebooks.\n\nThe ratio of pens to notebooks at Store A is $12:3,$ which can be written as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{12}{3}.\n$$\n\nWe can simplify this ratio by dividing the numerator and denominator by $3{:}$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{12 \\div 3}{3 \\div 3} = \\dfrac{4}{1} = 4\n$$\n\nSo the ratio $12:3$ is equivalent to $4:1.$\n\nThe ratio of pens to notebooks at Store B is $15:5,$ which can be written as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{15}{5}.\n$$\n\nWe can simplify this ratio by dividing the numerator and denominator by $5{:}$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{15 \\div 5}{5 \\div 5} = \\dfrac{3}{1} = 3\n$$\n\nSo the ratio $15:5$ is equivalent to $3:1.$\n\nSince $4 > 3,$ Store A has a larger ratio of pens to notebooks than Store B." "When we compare two ratios, we are comparing how much one quantity is associated with another quantity.\n\nOne way to compare ratios is to write each ratio as a fraction and simplify.\n\nFor example, suppose Store A has $12$ pens and $3$ notebooks, and Store B has $15$ pens and $5$ notebooks.\n\nThe ratio of pens to notebooks at Store A is $12:3,$ which can be written as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{12}{3}.\n$$\n\nWe can simplify this ratio by dividing the numerator and denominator by $3{:}$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{12 \\operatorname{div}  3}{3 \\operatorname{div}  3} = \\dfrac{4}{1} = 4\n$$\n\nSo the ratio $12:3$ is equivalent to $4:1.$\n\nThe ratio of pens to notebooks at Store B is $15:5,$ which can be written as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{15}{5}.\n$$\n\nWe can simplify this ratio by dividing the numerator and denominator by $5{:}$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{15 \\operatorname{div}  5}{5 \\operatorname{div}  5} = \\dfrac{3}{1} = 3\n$$\n\nSo the ratio $15:5$ is equivalent to $3:1.$\n\nSince $4 > 3,$ Store A has a larger ratio of pens to notebooks than Store B."] [:db/cas 17592186074944 :tutorial/content "Sometimes it is helpful to compare two ratios by writing them as fractions.\n\nTo do this, write each ratio as a fraction, simplify, and then compare the simplified fractions.\n\nFor example, suppose Jar A has $3$ blue marbles and $24$ red marbles, and Jar B has $5$ blue marbles and $20$ red marbles.\n\nThe ratio of blue marbles to red marbles in Jar A is $3:24,$ which can be written as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{24}.\n$$\n\nWe can simplify this fraction by dividing both the numerator and the denominator by $3{:}$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3 \\div 3}{24 \\div 3} = \\dfrac{1}{8}\n$$\n\nThe ratio of blue marbles to red marbles in Jar B is $5:20,$ which can be written as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{5}{20}.\n$$\n\nWe can simplify this fraction by dividing both the numerator and the denominator by $5{:}$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{5 \\div 5}{20 \\div 5} = \\dfrac{1}{4}\n$$\n\nTo compare $\\dfrac{1}{8}$ and $\\dfrac{1}{4},$ we look at the denominators. Since $8$ is larger than $4,$ the fraction $\\dfrac{1}{8}$ represents a smaller portion.\n\nSince $\\dfrac{1}{8} < \\dfrac{1}{4},$ Jar A has a smaller ratio of blue marbles to red marbles than Jar B." "Sometimes it is helpful to compare two ratios by writing them as fractions.\n\nTo do this, write each ratio as a fraction, simplify, and then compare the simplified fractions.\n\nFor example, suppose Jar A has $3$ blue marbles and $24$ red marbles, and Jar B has $5$ blue marbles and $20$ red marbles.\n\nThe ratio of blue marbles to red marbles in Jar A is $3:24,$ which can be written as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{24}.\n$$\n\nWe can simplify this fraction by dividing both the numerator and the denominator by $3{:}$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3 \\operatorname{div}  3}{24 \\operatorname{div}  3} = \\dfrac{1}{8}\n$$\n\nThe ratio of blue marbles to red marbles in Jar B is $5:20,$ which can be written as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{5}{20}.\n$$\n\nWe can simplify this fraction by dividing both the numerator and the denominator by $5{:}$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{5 \\operatorname{div}  5}{20 \\operatorname{div}  5} = \\dfrac{1}{4}\n$$\n\nTo compare $\\dfrac{1}{8}$ and $\\dfrac{1}{4},$ we look at the denominators. Since $8$ is larger than $4,$ the fraction $\\dfrac{1}{8}$ represents a smaller portion.\n\nSince $\\dfrac{1}{8} < \\dfrac{1}{4},$ Jar A has a smaller ratio of blue marbles to red marbles than Jar B."] [:db/cas 17592186074950 :tutorial/content "We use the **standard algorithm** to divide a two-digit number by a one-digit number. To illustrate, let's divide $92$ by $4$.\n\nWe start by writing down the question using long division notation, as follows:\n\n| $4$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{9 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 2}$ |  |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nFirst, we consider the tens. How many times does $4$ go into ${\\color{red}9}$? It goes in ${\\color{blue}2}$ times, and\n\n$$\n4\\times {\\color{blue}2} =8\n$$\n\nso there is a remainder of\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}9} - 8 = 1\n$$\n\nWe write the ${\\color{blue}2}$ above the tens, the $8$ at the bottom, and subtract to get the remainder of $1$. Then we bring the ones digit ($2$) down next to the remainder.\n\n| $\\color{blue}2$$4$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{{\\color{red}9} {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 2}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$8$$\\color{lightgray}\\downarrow$$1$$\\color{lightgray}2$ | Divide: ${\\color{red}9} \\div 4 = {\\color{blue}2}\\,\\text{R}\\,1$Write over tens: $\\color{blue}2$Multiply: $4 \\times {\\color{blue}2} = 8$Subtract: ${\\color{red}9} - 8 = 1$Bring down: $2$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nNext, we consider the ones. How many times does $4$ go into ${\\color{red}12}$? It goes in ${\\color{blue}3}$ times, and\n\n$$\n4\\times {\\color{blue}3} =12\n$$\n\nso there is no remainder (\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}12} - 12 = 0\n$$\n\n).\n\nWe write the ${\\color{blue}3}$ above the ones, the $12$ at the bottom, and subtract to get the final remainder of $0$.\n\n| $2$$\\color{blue}3$$4$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{9 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 2}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$8$$\\color{red}1$$\\color{red}2$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$1$$2$$\\fbox{0}$ | Divide: ${\\color{red}12} \\div 4 = {\\color{blue}3}$Write over ones: $\\color{blue}3$Multiply: $4 \\times {\\color{blue}3} = 12$Subtract: ${\\color{red}12} - 12 = \\fbox{0}$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nWe've gone through all the digits in the number $92$, so the division is done. The number we have written at the top is the answer.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n92 \\div 4 = 23\n$$" "We use the **standard algorithm** to divide a two-digit number by a one-digit number. To illustrate, let's divide $92$ by $4$.\n\nWe start by writing down the question using long division notation, as follows:\n\n| $4$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{9 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 2}$ |  |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nFirst, we consider the tens. How many times does $4$ go into ${\\color{red}9}$? It goes in ${\\color{blue}2}$ times, and\n\n$$\n4\\times {\\color{blue}2} =8\n$$\n\nso there is a remainder of\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}9} - 8 = 1\n$$\n\nWe write the ${\\color{blue}2}$ above the tens, the $8$ at the bottom, and subtract to get the remainder of $1$. Then we bring the ones digit ($2$) down next to the remainder.\n\n| $\\color{blue}2$$4$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{{\\color{red}9} {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 2}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$8$$\\color{lightgray}\\downarrow$$1$$\\color{lightgray}2$ | Divide: ${\\color{red}9} \\operatorname{div}  4 = {\\color{blue}2}\\,\\text{R}\\,1$Write over tens: $\\color{blue}2$Multiply: $4 \\times {\\color{blue}2} = 8$Subtract: ${\\color{red}9} - 8 = 1$Bring down: $2$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nNext, we consider the ones. How many times does $4$ go into ${\\color{red}12}$? It goes in ${\\color{blue}3}$ times, and\n\n$$\n4\\times {\\color{blue}3} =12\n$$\n\nso there is no remainder (\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}12} - 12 = 0\n$$\n\n).\n\nWe write the ${\\color{blue}3}$ above the ones, the $12$ at the bottom, and subtract to get the final remainder of $0$.\n\n| $2$$\\color{blue}3$$4$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{9 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 2}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$8$$\\color{red}1$$\\color{red}2$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$1$$2$$\\fbox{0}$ | Divide: ${\\color{red}12} \\operatorname{div}  4 = {\\color{blue}3}$Write over ones: $\\color{blue}3$Multiply: $4 \\times {\\color{blue}3} = 12$Subtract: ${\\color{red}12} - 12 = \\fbox{0}$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nWe've gone through all the digits in the number $92$, so the division is done. The number we have written at the top is the answer.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n92 \\operatorname{div}  4 = 23\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186074951 :tutorial/content "If there is a remainder after using the standard algorithm, we write the remainder in the answer. To illustrate, let's find the remainder of $95\\div 8\\,$.\n\nWe start by writing down the question using long division notation:\n\n| $8$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{9 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 5}$ |  |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nFirst, we consider the tens:\n\n| $\\color{blue}1$$8$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{{\\color{red}9} {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 5}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$8$$\\color{lightgray}\\downarrow$$1$$\\color{lightgray}5$ | Divide: ${\\color{red}9} \\div 8 = {\\color{blue}1}\\,\\text{R}\\,1$Write over tens: $\\color{blue}1$Multiply: $8 \\times {\\color{blue}1} = 8$Subtract: ${\\color{red}9} - 8 = 1$Bring down: $5$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nNext, we consider the ones:\n\n| $1$$\\color{blue}1$$8$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{9 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 5}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$8$$\\color{red}1$$\\color{red}5$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$8$$\\fbox{7}$ | Divide: ${\\color{red}15} \\div 8 = {\\color{blue}1}\\,\\text{R}\\,7$Write over ones: $\\color{blue}1$Multiply: $8 \\times {\\color{blue}1} = 8$Subtract: ${\\color{red}15} - 8 = \\fbox{7}$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nWe've gone through all the digits in the number $95$, so the division is done. However, we still have a **remainder** of $\\color{blue}7$, so we include this in our final answer.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n95 \\div 8 = {\\color{red}11}\\,\\text{R}\\,{\\color{blue}7}\n$$\n\nHere, $\\color{red}11$ is called the **quotient**." "If there is a remainder after using the standard algorithm, we write the remainder in the answer. To illustrate, let's find the remainder of $95\\operatorname{div}  8\\,$.\n\nWe start by writing down the question using long division notation:\n\n| $8$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{9 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 5}$ |  |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nFirst, we consider the tens:\n\n| $\\color{blue}1$$8$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{{\\color{red}9} {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 5}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$8$$\\color{lightgray}\\downarrow$$1$$\\color{lightgray}5$ | Divide: ${\\color{red}9} \\operatorname{div}  8 = {\\color{blue}1}\\,\\text{R}\\,1$Write over tens: $\\color{blue}1$Multiply: $8 \\times {\\color{blue}1} = 8$Subtract: ${\\color{red}9} - 8 = 1$Bring down: $5$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nNext, we consider the ones:\n\n| $1$$\\color{blue}1$$8$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{9 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}}