undreds. How many times does $2$ go into ${\\color{red}4}$? It goes in ${\\color{blue}2}$ times, and\n\n$$\n2\\times {\\color{blue}2} =4\n$$\n\nso there is a remainder of\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}4} - 4 = 0\n$$\n\nWe write the ${\\color{blue}2}$ above the hundreds, the $4$ at the bottom, and subtract to get the remainder of $0$. Then we bring the tens digit $(6)$ down next to the remainder.\n\n| $\\color{blue}2$$2$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{{\\color{red}4} {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 6 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 8}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$4$$\\color{lightgray}\\downarrow$$0$$\\color{lightgray}6$ | Divide: ${\\color{red}4} \\operatorname{div}  2 = {\\color{blue}2}$Write over hundreds: $\\color{blue}2$Multiply: $2 \\times {\\color{blue}2} = 4$Subtract: ${\\color{red}4} - 4 = 0$Bring down: $6$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nNext, we consider the tens. How many times does $2$ go into ${\\color{red}6}$? It goes in ${\\color{blue}3}$ times, and\n\n$$\n2\\times {\\color{blue}3} =6\n$$\n\nso there is a remainder of\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}6} - 6 = 0\n$$\n\nWe write the ${\\color{blue}3}$ above the tens, the $6$ at the bottom, and subtract to get the remainder of $0$. Then, we bring the ones digit $(8)$ down next to the remainder.\n\n| $2$$\\color{blue}3$$2$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{4 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 6 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 8}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$4$$\\color{lightgray}\\downarrow$$0$$\\color{red}6$$\\color{lightgray}\\downarrow$$-\\!\\!\\!$$6$$\\color{lightgray}\\downarrow$$0$$\\color{lightgray}8$ | Divide: ${\\color{red}6} \\operatorname{div}  2 = {\\color{blue}3}$Write over tens: $\\color{blue}3$Multiply: $2 \\times {\\color{blue}3} = 6$Subtract: ${\\color{red}6} - 6 = 0$Bring down: $8$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nFinally, we consider the ones. How many times does $2$ go into ${\\color{red}8}$? It goes in ${\\color{blue}4}$ times, and\n\n$$\n2\\times {\\color{blue}4} =8\n$$\n\nso there is a remainder of\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}8} - 8 = 0\n$$\n\nWe write the ${\\color{blue}4}$ above the ones, the $8$ at the bottom, and subtract to get the final remainder of $0$.\n\n| $2$$3$$\\color{blue}4$$2$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{4 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 6 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 8}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$4$$0$$6$$-\\!\\!\\!$$6$$0$$\\color{red}8$$-\\!\\!\\!$$8$$\\fbox{0}$ | Divide: ${\\color{red}8} \\operatorname{div}  2 = {\\color{blue}4}$Write over ones: $\\color{blue}4$Multiply: $2 \\times {\\color{blue}4} = 8$Subtract: ${\\color{red}8} - 8 = \\fbox{0}$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nWe've gone through all the digits in the number $468$, so the division is done. The number we have written at the top is the answer. There is no remainder.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n468 \\operatorname{div}  2 = 234\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186074953 :tutorial/content "We can also use the standard algorithm to find the value of $305 \\div 5$. However, because the hundreds digit ($3$) is less than the divisor ($5$), the process will be slightly different.\n\nWe start by writing down the question using long division notation:\n\n| $5$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{3 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 0 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 5}$ |  |\n| --- | ---: |\n\n**Warning**: Notice that $\\color{red}3$ (the number of hundreds in ${\\color{red}3}05$) is less than the divisor $5$, so it doesn't divide. To counter this, we should consider both the hundreds *and* tens. That is, we should divide $\\color{red}30$ by $5$, as follows:\n\n| $\\color{blue}6$$5$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{{\\color{red}3} {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} {\\color{red}0} {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 5}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$3$$0$$\\color{lightgray}\\downarrow$$0$$\\color{lightgray}5$ | Divide: ${\\color{red}30} \\div 5 = {\\color{blue}6}$Write over tens: $\\color{blue}6$Multiply: $5 \\times {\\color{blue}6} = 30$Subtract: ${\\color{red}30} - 30 = 0$Bring down: $5$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nNext, we consider the ones:\n\n| $6$$\\color{blue}1$$5$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{3 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 0 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 5}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$3$$0$$0$$\\color{red}5$$-\\!\\!\\!$$5$$\\fbox{0}$ | Divide: ${\\color{red}5} \\div 5 = {\\color{blue}1}$Write over ones: $\\color{blue}1$Multiply: $5 \\times {\\color{blue}1} = 5$Subtract: ${\\color{red}5} - 5 = 0$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nWe've gone through all the digits in the number $305$, so the division is done. The number we have written at the top is the answer. There is no remainder.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n305 \\div 5 = 61\n$$" "We can also use the standard algorithm to find the value of $305 \\operatorname{div}  5$. However, because the hundreds digit ($3$) is less than the divisor ($5$), the process will be slightly different.\n\nWe start by writing down the question using long division notation:\n\n| $5$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{3 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 0 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 5}$ |  |\n| --- | ---: |\n\n**Warning**: Notice that $\\color{red}3$ (the number of hundreds in ${\\color{red}3}05$) is less than the divisor $5$, so it doesn't divide. To counter this, we should consider both the hundreds *and* tens. That is, we should divide $\\color{red}30$ by $5$, as follows:\n\n| $\\color{blue}6$$5$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{{\\color{red}3} {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} {\\color{red}0} {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 5}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$3$$0$$\\color{lightgray}\\downarrow$$0$$\\color{lightgray}5$ | Divide: ${\\color{red}30} \\operatorname{div}  5 = {\\color{blue}6}$Write over tens: $\\color{blue}6$Multiply: $5 \\times {\\color{blue}6} = 30$Subtract: ${\\color{red}30} - 30 = 0$Bring down: $5$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nNext, we consider the ones:\n\n| $6$$\\color{blue}1$$5$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{3 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 0 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 5}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$3$$0$$0$$\\color{red}5$$-\\!\\!\\!$$5$$\\fbox{0}$ | Divide: ${\\color{red}5} \\operatorname{div}  5 = {\\color{blue}1}$Write over ones: $\\color{blue}1$Multiply: $5 \\times {\\color{blue}1} = 5$Subtract: ${\\color{red}5} - 5 = 0$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nWe've gone through all the digits in the number $305$, so the division is done. The number we have written at the top is the answer. There is no remainder.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n305 \\operatorname{div}  5 = 61\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186074954 :tutorial/content "We can also use the standard algorithm to find the value of $408 \\div 4$. We will see that the situation here is slightly different. Let's work through it.\n\nAs usual, we start by writing down the question using long division notation:\n\n| $4$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{4 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 0 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 8}$ |  |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nFirst, we consider the hundreds:\n\n| $\\color{blue}1$$4$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{{\\color{red}4} {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 0 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 8}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$4$$\\color{lightgray}\\downarrow$$0$$\\color{lightgray}0$ | Divide: ${\\color{red}4} \\div 4 = {\\color{blue}1}$Write over hundreds: $\\color{blue}1$Multiply: $4 \\times {\\color{blue}1} = 4$Subtract: ${\\color{red}4} - 4 = 0$Bring down: $0$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nNext, we consider the tens:\n\n| $1$$\\color{blue}0$$4$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{4 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 0 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 8}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$4$$0$$\\color{red}0$ | Divide: ${\\color{red}0} \\div 4 = {\\color{blue}0}$Write over tens: $\\color{blue}0$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nWait! Notice that $\\color{red}0$ is less than the divisor $4$, but not all numbers of the dividend have yet been used! In this case, we need to bring down one more digit:\n\n| $1$$0$$4$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{4 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 0 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 8}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$4$$\\color{lightgray}\\downarrow$$0$$0$$\\color{lightgray}8$ | Bring down: $8$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nFinally, we consider the ones:\n\n| $1$$0$$\\color{blue}2$$4$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{4 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 0 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 8}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$4$$0$$0$$\\color{red}8$$-\\!\\!\\!$$8$$\\fbox{0}$ | Divide: ${\\color{red}8} \\div 4 = {\\color{blue}2}$Write over ones: $\\color{blue}2$Multiply: $4 \\times {\\color{blue}2} = 8$Subtract: ${\\color{red}8} - 8 = \\fbox{0}$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nWe've gone through all the digits in the number $408$, so the division is done. The number we have written at the top is the answer. There is no remainder.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n408 \\div 4 = 102\n$$" "We can also use the standard algorithm to find the value of $408 \\operatorname{div}  4$. We will see that the situation here is slightly different. Let's work through it.\n\nAs usual, we start by writing down the question using long division notation:\n\n| $4$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{4 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 0 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 8}$ |  |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nFirst, we consider the hundreds:\n\n| $\\color{blue}1$$4$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{{\\color{red}4} {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 0 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 8}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$4$$\\color{lightgray}\\downarrow$$0$$\\color{lightgray}0$ | Divide: ${\\color{red}4} \\operatorname{div}  4 = {\\color{blue}1}$Write over hundreds: $\\color{blue}1$Multiply: $4 \\times {\\color{blue}1} = 4$Subtract: ${\\color{red}4} - 4 = 0$Bring down: $0$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nNext, we consider the tens:\n\n| $1$$\\color{blue}0$$4$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{4 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 0 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 8}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$4$$0$$\\color{red}0$ | Divide: ${\\color{red}0} \\operatorname{div}  4 = {\\color{blue}0}$Write over tens: $\\color{blue}0$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nWait! Notice that $\\color{red}0$ is less than the divisor $4$, but not all numbers of the dividend have yet been used! In this case, we need to bring down one more digit:\n\n| $1$$0$$4$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{4 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 0 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 8}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$4$$\\color{lightgray}\\downarrow$$0$$0$$\\color{lightgray}8$ | Bring down: $8$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nFinally, we consider the ones:\n\n| $1$$0$$\\color{blue}2$$4$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{4 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 0 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 8}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$4$$0$$0$$\\color{red}8$$-\\!\\!\\!$$8$$\\fbox{0}$ | Divide: ${\\color{red}8} \\operatorname{div}  4 = {\\color{blue}2}$Write over ones: $\\color{blue}2$Multiply: $4 \\times {\\color{blue}2} = 8$Subtract: ${\\color{red}8} - 8 = \\fbox{0}$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nWe've gone through all the digits in the number $408$, so the division is done. The number we have written at the top is the answer. There is no remainder.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n408 \\operatorname{div}  4 = 102\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186074955 :tutorial/content "We can also use the standard algorithm to divide a four-digit number by a one-digit number.\n\nTo illustrate, let's use the algorithm to divide $4,236$ by $3$. We start by writing down the question using long division notation:\n\n| $3$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{4 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 2 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 3 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 6}$ |  |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nFirst, we consider the thousands:\n\n| $\\color{blue}1$$3$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{{\\color{red}4} {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 2 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 3 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 6}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$3$$\\color{lightgray}\\downarrow$$1$$\\color{lightgray}2$ | Divide: ${\\color{red}4} \\div 3 = {\\color{blue}1}\\,\\text{R}\\,1$Write over thousands: $\\color{blue}1$Multiply: $3 \\times {\\color{blue}1} = 3$Subtract: ${\\color{red}4} - 3 = 1$Bring down: $2$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nNext, we consider the hundreds:\n\n| $1$$\\color{blue}4$$3$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{4 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 2 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 3 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 6}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$3$$\\color{lightgray}\\downarrow$$\\color{red}1$$\\color{red}2$$\\color{lightgray}\\downarrow$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$1$$2$$\\color{lightgray}\\downarrow$$0$$\\color{lightgray}3$ | Divide: ${\\color{red}12} \\div 3 = {\\color{blue}4}$Write over hundreds: $\\color{blue}4$Multiply: $3 \\times {\\color{blue}4} = 12$Subtract: ${\\color{red}12} - 12 = 0$Bring down: $3$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nNext, we consider the tens:\n\n| $1$$4$$\\color{blue}1$$3$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{4 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 2 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 3 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 6}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$3$$\\color{lightgray}\\downarrow$$1$$2$$\\color{lightgray}\\downarrow$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$1$$2$$\\color{lightgray}\\downarrow$$0$$\\color{red}3$$\\color{lightgray}\\downarrow$$-\\!\\!\\!$$3$$\\color{lightgray}\\downarrow$$0$$\\color{lightgray}6$ | Divide: ${\\color{red}3} \\div 3 = {\\color{blue}1}$Write over tens: $\\color{blue}1$Multiply: $3 \\times {\\color{blue}1} = 3$Subtract: ${\\color{red}3} - 3 = 0$Bring down: $6$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nFinally, we consider the ones:\n\n| $1$$4$$1$$\\color{blue}2$$3$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{4 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 2 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 3 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 6}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$3$$1$$2$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$1$$2$$0$$3$$-\\!\\!\\!$$3$$0$$\\color{red}6$$-\\!\\!\\!$$6$$\\fbox{0}$ | Divide: ${\\color{red}6} \\div 3 = {\\color{blue}2}$Write over ones: $\\color{blue}2$Multiply: $3 \\times {\\color{blue}2} = 6$Subtract: ${\\color{red}6} - 6 = \\fbox{0}$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nWe've gone through all the digits in the number $4,236$, so the division is done. The number we have written at the top is the answer. There is no remainder.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n4,236 \\div 3 = 1,412\n$$" "We can also use the standard algorithm to divide a four-digit number by a one-digit number.\n\nTo illustrate, let's use the algorithm to divide $4,236$ by $3$. We start by writing down the question using long division notation:\n\n| $3$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{4 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 2 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 3 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 6}$ |  |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nFirst, we consider the thousands:\n\n| $\\color{blue}1$$3$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{{\\color{red}4} {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 2 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 3 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 6}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$3$$\\color{lightgray}\\downarrow$$1$$\\color{lightgray}2$ | Divide: ${\\color{red}4} \\operatorname{div}  3 = {\\color{blue}1}\\,\\text{R}\\,1$Write over thousands: $\\color{blue}1$Multiply: $3 \\times {\\color{blue}1} = 3$Subtract: ${\\color{red}4} - 3 = 1$Bring down: $2$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nNext, we consider the hundreds:\n\n| $1$$\\color{blue}4$$3$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{4 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 2 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 3 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 6}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$3$$\\color{lightgray}\\downarrow$$\\color{red}1$$\\color{red}2$$\\color{lightgray}\\downarrow$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$1$$2$$\\color{lightgray}\\downarrow$$0$$\\color{lightgray}3$ | Divide: ${\\color{red}12} \\operatorname{div}  3 = {\\color{blue}4}$Write over hundreds: $\\color{blue}4$Multiply: $3 \\times {\\color{blue}4} = 12$Subtract: ${\\color{red}12} - 12 = 0$Bring down: $3$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nNext, we consider the tens:\n\n| $1$$4$$\\color{blue}1$$3$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{4 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 2 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 3 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 6}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$3$$\\color{lightgray}\\downarrow$$1$$2$$\\color{lightgray}\\downarrow$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$1$$2$$\\color{lightgray}\\downarrow$$0$$\\color{red}3$$\\color{lightgray}\\downarrow$$-\\!\\!\\!$$3$$\\color{lightgray}\\downarrow$$0$$\\color{lightgray}6$ | Divide: ${\\color{red}3} \\operatorname{div}  3 = {\\color{blue}1}$Write over tens: $\\color{blue}1$Multiply: $3 \\times {\\color{blue}1} = 3$Subtract: ${\\color{red}3} - 3 = 0$Bring down: $6$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nFinally, we consider the ones:\n\n| $1$$4$$1$$\\color{blue}2$$3$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{4 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 2 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 3 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 6}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$3$$1$$2$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$1$$2$$0$$3$$-\\!\\!\\!$$3$$0$$\\color{red}6$$-\\!\\!\\!$$6$$\\fbox{0}$ | Divide: ${\\color{red}6} \\operatorname{div}  3 = {\\color{blue}2}$Write over ones: $\\color{blue}2$Multiply: $3 \\times {\\color{blue}2} = 6$Subtract: ${\\color{red}6} - 6 = \\fbox{0}$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nWe've gone through all the digits in the number $4,236$, so the division is done. The number we have written at the top is the answer. There is no remainder.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n4,236 \\operatorname{div}  3 = 1,412\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186074956 :tutorial/content "We can use box models to divide numbers by two-digit numbers.\n\nLet's see how to use a box model by finding the value of $300 \\div 25$.\n\nWe start by writing our division in the following way. We place the dividend in a box and the divisor to the left of the box.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"right\" data-cell-valign=\"top\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"><div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td data-fill=\"#EBF5FB\">$25$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-cell-valign=\"top\"><div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-cell-valign=\"top\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-cell-valign=\"top\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nWe want to find how many times $25$ goes into $300$. But instead of trying to figure this out in a single step, let's break this problem up into a series of simpler steps.\n\nIn our first step, we will pick a multiple of $25$ that is easy to compute and is fairly large but no larger than $300$. We will choose the multiple $25 \\times {\\color{blue}10}$ because we can compute\n\n$$\n25 \\times {\\color{blue}10} = 250\n$$\n\nquickly and the result is no larger than $300$.\n\nTo organize our work so far, we write $\\color{blue}10$ above the box and subtract $25 \\times {\\color{blue}10}$ from $300$ inside the box.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#EBF5FB\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$\\color{blue}10$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"right\" data-cell-valign=\"top\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"><div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td data-fill=\"#EBF5FB\">$25$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-cell-valign=\"top\"><div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-t