n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#EBF5FB\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$10$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#EBF5FB\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$\\color{blue}2$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"right\" data-cell-valign=\"top\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"><div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td data-fill=\"#EBF5FB\">$25$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-cell-valign=\"top\"><div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$-\\!$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-left=\"none\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-left=\"none\">$5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-left=\"none\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-left=\"none\">$\\color{red}5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-left=\"none\">$\\color{red}0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-cell-valign=\"top\"><div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$\\color{red}5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$\\color{red}0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$-\\!\\!\\!$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-left=\"none\">$5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-left=\"none\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-left=\"none\">$\\fbox{0}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-cell-valign=\"top\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nWe get $\\fbox{0}$, so the division is done.\n\nTo find the quotient $300 \\operatorname{div}  25$, all we need to do is add the numbers on top of the boxes:\n\n$$\n10+2=12\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n300 \\operatorname{div}  25 = 12\\,\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186074957 :tutorial/content "In this lesson, we'll consider real-life situations where we have to multiply fractions by whole numbers.\n\nSuppose that Justin, a chef, has $8$ large tins of tomatoes. If each tin of tomatoes weighs $\\dfrac{5}{4}\\,\\textrm{lb},$ how many pounds of tomatoes does Justin have in total?\n\nTo find out the total weight of tomatoes, we need to multiply $\\dfrac{5}{4}\\,\\textrm{lb}$ by $8.$  \n\nTo multiply a fraction by a whole number, we multiply the fraction's numerator by the whole number and keep the denominator the same:\n\n$$\n8 \\times \\dfrac54= \\dfrac{8\\times 5}{4} = \\dfrac{40}4\n$$\n\nWe can simplify this fraction by interpreting the fraction as a division:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{40}{4} =  40 \\div 4 = 10\n$$\n\nSo, in total, Justin has $10\\,\\textrm{lb}$ of tomatoes." "In this lesson, we'll consider real-life situations where we have to multiply fractions by whole numbers.\n\nSuppose that Justin, a chef, has $8$ large tins of tomatoes. If each tin of tomatoes weighs $\\dfrac{5}{4}\\,\\textrm{lb},$ how many pounds of tomatoes does Justin have in total?\n\nTo find out the total weight of tomatoes, we need to multiply $\\dfrac{5}{4}\\,\\textrm{lb}$ by $8.$  \n\nTo multiply a fraction by a whole number, we multiply the fraction's numerator by the whole number and keep the denominator the same:\n\n$$\n8 \\times \\dfrac54= \\dfrac{8\\times 5}{4} = \\dfrac{40}4\n$$\n\nWe can simplify this fraction by interpreting the fraction as a division:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{40}{4} =  40 \\operatorname{div}  4 = 10\n$$\n\nSo, in total, Justin has $10\\,\\textrm{lb}$ of tomatoes."] [:db/cas 17592186074962 :tutorial/content "There are many ways to divide decimals. One such way is to write the division as a fraction and then multiply the numerator and denominator by a power of $10$ so that both become whole numbers. Then, we carry out the division as usual.\n\nTo illustrate, let's use this method to find the value of $4.5\\div 0.5$.\n\n**Step 1.** First, we write the division problem as a fraction:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4.5}{0.5}\n$$\n\n**Step 2.** Then, we multiply the numerator and denominator by $10$:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4.5\\times 10}{0.5\\times 10} = \\dfrac{45}{5}\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Finally, we solve the resulting division problem. In this case, we have\n\n$$\n45 \\div 5 = 9\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n4.5 \\div 0.5 = 9 \\,\n$$" "There are many ways to divide decimals. One such way is to write the division as a fraction and then multiply the numerator and denominator by a power of $10$ so that both become whole numbers. Then, we carry out the division as usual.\n\nTo illustrate, let's use this method to find the value of $4.5\\operatorname{div}  0.5$.\n\n**Step 1.** First, we write the division problem as a fraction:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4.5}{0.5}\n$$\n\n**Step 2.** Then, we multiply the numerator and denominator by $10$:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4.5\\times 10}{0.5\\times 10} = \\dfrac{45}{5}\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Finally, we solve the resulting division problem. In this case, we have\n\n$$\n45 \\operatorname{div}  5 = 9\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n4.5 \\operatorname{div}  0.5 = 9 \\,\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186074963 :tutorial/content "When using long division to divide whole numbers, it's often appropriate to round the final answer.\n\nFor instance, let's find the value of $58 \\div 7$, rounded to the nearest tenth. We start by going through the long division procedure as usual:\n\n| $\\,\\, \\phantom{0} {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 8 {\\,. \\,} 2 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} {\\color{black}8} \\: \\ldots$$7$$\\phantom{\\mid}\\!\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{5 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 8 {\\,. \\,} 0 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 0}$$-\\!\\!\\!$$5$$6$$2$$0$$-\\!\\!\\!$$1$$4$$6$$0$$-\\!\\!\\!$$5$$6$$4$$\\vdots$ |\n| --- |\n\nWe could divide further, but since we want to round to $1$ decimal place (the nearest tenth), we stop after finding the quotient to $2$ decimal places:\n\n$$\n58 \\div 7 = 8.28 \\ldots\n$$\n\nFinally, we round the quotient to the nearest tenth. The hundredths place is $8$, which is greater than $5$, so we round up:\n$8.28 \\ldots \\rightarrow 8.3$" "When using long division to divide whole numbers, it's often appropriate to round the final answer.\n\nFor instance, let's find the value of $58 \\operatorname{div}  7$, rounded to the nearest tenth. We start by going through the long division procedure as usual:\n\n| $\\,\\, \\phantom{0} {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 8 {\\,. \\,} 2 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} {\\color{black}8} \\: \\ldots$$7$$\\phantom{\\mid}\\!\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{5 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 8 {\\,. \\,} 0 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 0}$$-\\!\\!\\!$$5$$6$$2$$0$$-\\!\\!\\!$$1$$4$$6$$0$$-\\!\\!\\!$$5$$6$$4$$\\vdots$ |\n| --- |\n\nWe could divide further, but since we want to round to $1$ decimal place (the nearest tenth), we stop after finding the quotient to $2$ decimal places:\n\n$$\n58 \\operatorname{div}  7 = 8.28 \\ldots\n$$\n\nFinally, we round the quotient to the nearest tenth. The hundredths place is $8$, which is greater than $5$, so we round up:\n$8.28 \\ldots \\rightarrow 8.3$"] [:db/cas 17592186074964 :tutorial/content "We can use the standard algorithm to divide decimals by whole numbers.\n\nTo illustrate, let's compute $7.2 \\div 3$.\n\nFirst, we write down the problem using long division notation, keeping the decimal point in the dividend:\n\n| $3$$\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{7 {\\,\\bbox[2px, lightgray]{\\color{blue}.}\\,} 2}{\\:\\phantom{\\mid}}$ |\n| --- |\n\nWe also bring the decimal point up into the quotient, placing it directly above the decimal point in the dividend:\n\n| $\\,\\phantom{0} \\bbox[2px, lightgray]{\\color{blue}.}$$3$$\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{7 {\\bbox[2px, lightgray]{\\color{blue}.} 2{\\:\\phantom{\\mid}}}}$ |\n| --- |\n\nNow, we carry out the long division process as usual:\n\n| $\\,2 {\\,\\,. \\,} 4$$3$$\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{7 {\\,\\,. \\,} 2}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$6$$1$$2$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$1$$2$$0$ |\n| --- |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n7.2 \\div 3 = 2.4 \\,\n$$\n\n**Note:** The \"standard algorithm\" is the same as \"long division.\" The two phrases can be used interchangeably." "We can use the standard algorithm to divide decimals by whole numbers.\n\nTo illustrate, let's compute $7.2 \\operatorname{div}  3$.\n\nFirst, we write down the problem using long division notation, keeping the decimal point in the dividend:\n\n| $3$$\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{7 {\\,\\bbox[2px, lightgray]{\\color{blue}.}\\,} 2}{\\:\\phantom{\\mid}}$ |\n| --- |\n\nWe also bring the decimal point up into the quotient, placing it directly above the decimal point in the dividend:\n\n| $\\,\\phantom{0} \\bbox[2px, lightgray]{\\color{blue}.}$$3$$\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{7 {\\bbox[2px, lightgray]{\\color{blue}.} 2{\\:\\phantom{\\mid}}}}$ |\n| --- |\n\nNow, we carry out the long division process as usual:\n\n| $\\,2 {\\,\\,. \\,} 4$$3$$\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{7 {\\,\\,. \\,} 2}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$6$$1$$2$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$1$$2$$0$ |\n| --- |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n7.2 \\operatorname{div}  3 = 2.4 \\,\n$$\n\n**Note:** The \"standard algorithm\" is the same as \"long division.\" The two phrases can be used interchangeably."] [:db/cas 17592186074965 :tutorial/content "We can also use the standard algorithm to divide decimals by other decimals. The only difference is that we first need to find an equivalent division problem where the divisor is a whole number.\n\nTo illustrate, let's compute $1.11\\div 0.3 \\,$. First, we write this division problem as a fraction:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1.11}{0.3}\n$$\n\nThen, we multiply the numerator and denominator by $10$ so that we get a whole number in the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1.11 \\times 10}{0.3\\times 10} = \\dfrac{11.1}{3}\n$$\n\nNow we see that the division problem is equivalent to $11.1 \\div 3$. We can compute this using long division as usual:\n\n| $\\,\\, 0 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 3 {\\,\\,. \\,} 7$$3$$\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{1 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 1 {\\,\\,. \\,} 1}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$9$$2$$1$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$2$$1$$0$ |\n| --- |\n\nFrom the long division, we find that\n\n$$\n11.1 \\div 3 = 3.7\n$$\n\nso we conclude that\n\n$$\n1.11 \\div 0.3 = 3.7 \\,\n$$" "We can also use the standard algorithm to divide decimals by other decimals. The only difference is that we first need to find an equivalent division problem where the divisor is a whole number.\n\nTo illustrate, let's compute $1.11\\operatorname{div}  0.3 \\,$. First, we write this division problem as a fraction:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1.11}{0.3}\n$$\n\nThen, we multiply the numerator and denominator by $10$ so that we get a whole number in the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1.11 \\times 10}{0.3\\times 10} = \\dfrac{11.1}{3}\n$$\n\nNow we see that the division problem is equivalent to $11.1 \\operatorname{div}  3$. We can compute this using long division as usual:\n\n| $\\,\\, 0 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 3 {\\,\\,. \\,} 7$$3$$\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{1 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 1 {\\,\\,. \\,} 1}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$9$$2$$1$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$2$$1$$0$ |\n| --- |\n\nFrom the long division, we find that\n\n$$\n11.1 \\operatorname{div}  3 = 3.7\n$$\n\nso we conclude that\n\n$$\n1.11 \\operatorname{div}  0.3 = 3.7 \\,\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186074970 :tutorial/content "A **unit rate** is a comparison of two quantities where one of the terms has a quantity of $1.$ This means the ratio, in fraction form, has a denominator of $1.$\n\nFor instance, suppose Michael earned $\\$ 200$ by selling $4$ identical tires.\n\nThe ratio of dollars to tires is ${\\color{blue}200}:{\\color{red}4}.$ Writing the ratio as a fraction, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{{\\color{blue}200}}{{\\color{red}4}} \\, .\n$$\n\nThe unit rate is how many dollars Michael got for $1$ tire.\n\nWe can make a denominator of $1$ by dividing the numerator and denominator by $4\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{{\\color{blue}200} \\div 4}{{\\color{red}4} \\div 4} = \\dfrac{{\\color{blue}50}}{{\\color{red}1}}\n$$\n\nThis is the unit rate. In this case, we say that Michael got $\\color{blue}50$ dollars *per* tire." "A **unit rate** is a comparison of two quantities where one of the terms has a quantity of $1.$ This means the ratio, in fraction form, has a denominator of $1.$\n\nFor instance, suppose Michael earned $\\$ 200$ by selling $4$ identical tires.\n\nThe ratio of dollars to tires is ${\\color{blue}200}:{\\color{red}4}.$ Writing the ratio as a fraction, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{{\\color{blue}200}}{{\\color{red}4}} \\, .\n$$\n\nThe unit rate is how many dollars Michael got for $1$ tire.\n\nWe can make a denominator of $1$ by dividing the numerator and denominator by $4\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{{\\color{blue}200} \\operatorname{div}  4}{{\\color{red}4} \\operatorname{div}  4} = \\dfrac{{\\color{blue}50}}{{\\color{red}1}}\n$$\n\nThis is the unit rate. In this case, we say that Michael got $\\color{blue}50$ dollars *per* tire."] [:db/cas 17592186074971 :tutorial/content "In some tape diagrams, each box can represent more than one unit.\n\nFor instance, the ratio of pins to paper clips in a case is given by the tape diagram below. If there are $15$ pins in the case, how many paper clips are there in total?\n\n![](images/82/82f94d7377ea3923ffdd825de324b128cd8b4f102070bf6890dee2cbc619657e.png)\n\nWe are told that there are $15$ pins in the case, but the tape diagram for pins shows $3$ boxes. Therefore, each box in the diagram above represents $15 \\div 3 = 5$ units. \n\nNow, we fill the number $5$ in each box of both tapes:\n\n![](images/7d/7d8d1e22ee067588593b7b08442dd2a44142f9f684d84809056ee0ffb4b2dfc0.png)\n\nSince there are $4$ boxes corresponding to paper clips, and each box represents $5$ units, the total number of paper clips is\n\n$$\n4 \\times 5 = 20 \\, .\n$$\n\nSo, there are $20$ paper clips in Mrs. Robinson's case." "In some tape diagrams, each box can represent more than one unit.\n\nFor instance, the ratio of pins to paper clips in a case is given by the tape diagram below. If there are $15$ pins in the case, how many paper clips are there in total?\n\n![](images/82/82f94d7377ea3923ffdd825de324b128cd8b4f102070bf6890dee2cbc619657e.png)\n\nWe are told that there are $15$ pins in the case, but the tape diagram for pins shows $3$ boxes. Therefore, each box in the diagram above represents $15 \\operatorname{div}  3 = 5$ units. \n\nNow, we fill the number $5$ in each box of both tapes:\n\n![](images/7d/7d8d1e22ee067588593b7b08442dd2a44142f9f684d84809056ee0ffb4b2dfc0.png)\n\nSince there are $4$ boxes corresponding to paper clips, and each box represents $5$ units, the total number of paper clips is\n\n$$\n4 \\times 5 = 20 \\, .\n$$\n\nSo, there are $20$ paper clips in Mrs. Robinson's case."] [:db/cas 17592186075004 :tutorial/content "In this lesson, we will learn how to divide larger numbers by two-digit numbers *with remainders* using the relationship between multiplication and division.\n\nTo begin with, let's figure out how to compute $47\\div 13$.\n\nWe will start by computing products of $13$ and other whole numbers, starting from $1$, until we get to a number that is greater than $47$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n13 \\cdot 1 &= 13 \\\\\n13 \\cdot 2 &= 13 + 13 = 26 \\\\\n13 \\cdot 3 &= 26 + 13 = 39✓ \\\\\n13 \\cdot 4 &= 39 + 13 = 52\\cdot(> {47}, so too big)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $13 \\times {\\color{blue}3}$ is *smaller than* $47$, but $13 \\times 4$ is *greater than* $47$. This means that ${47}\\div {13}$ has a whole number part (or quotient) of ${\\color{blue}3}$.\n\nTo find the remainder, we subtract the product from the dividend:\n\n$$\n{47} - {39} = {\\color{red}8}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{47}\\div {13} = {\\color{blue}3}\\,\\textrm{R}\\,{\\color{red}8}\n$$" "In this lesson, we will learn how to divide larger numbers by two-digit numbers *with remainders* using the relationship between multiplication and division.\n\nTo begin with, let's figure out how to compute $47\\operatorname{div}  13$.\n\nWe will start by computing products of $13$ and other whole numbers, starting from $1$, until we get to a number that is greater than $47$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n13 \\cdot 1 &= 13 \\\\\n13 \\cdot 2 &= 13 + 13 = 26 \\\\\n13 \\cdot 3 &= 26 + 13 = 39✓ \\\\\n13 \\cdot 4 &= 39 + 13 = 52\\cdot(> {47}, so too big)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $13 \\times {\\color{blue}3}$ is *smaller than* $47$, but $13 \\times 4$ is *greater than* $47$. This means that ${47}\\operatorname{div}  {13}$ has a whole number part (or quotient) of ${\\color{blue}3}$.\n\nTo find the remainder, we subtract the product from the dividend:\n\n$$\n{47} - {39} = {\\color{red}8}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{47}\\operatorname{div}  {13} = {\\color{blue}3}\\,\\textrm{R}\\,{\\color{red}8}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186075018 :tutorial/content "We can use area models to understand what it means to divide a fraction by a whole number.\n\nFor example, let's consider the area model below:\n\n![](images/66/664911a4bae7edacdf2b68f8fddb7dd54af3da02ab995c003e9001cd8b27aaff.png)\n\nThe area of a rectangle equals its length multiplied by its width.\n\nFor the given rectangle:\n\n- its length is $3$\n- its width is $\\dfrac{1}{12}$\n- its area is $\\dfrac{1}{4}$\n\nTherefore, this area model shows the following multiplication problem:\n\n$$\n3\\times \\dfrac{1}{12} = \\dfrac{1}{4}\n$$\n\nUsing the relationship between multiplication and division, we can turn this multiplication problem into the following division problem:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{12} = \\dfrac14\\div 3\n$$\n\nWe can also use area models to interpret the division of a whole number by a fraction. Let's see an example." "We can use area models to understand what it means to divide a fraction by a whole number.\n\nFor example, let's consider the area model below:\n\n![](images/66/664911a4bae7edacdf2b68f8fddb7dd54af3da02ab995c003e9001cd8b27aaff.png)\n\nThe area of a rectangle equals its length multiplied by its width.\n\nFor the given rectangle:\n\n- its length is $3$\n- its width is $\\dfrac{1}{12}$\n- its area is $\\dfrac{1}{4}$\n\nTherefore, this area model shows the following multiplication problem:\n\n$$\n3\\times \\dfrac{1}{12} = \\dfrac{1}{4}\n$$\n\nUsing the relationship between multiplication and division, we can turn this multiplication problem into the following division problem:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{12} = \\dfrac14\\operatorname{div}  3\n$$\n\nWe can also use area models to interpret the division of a whole number by a fraction. Let's see an example."] [:db/cas 17592186075044 :tutorial/content "The model below represents $2$ wholes. Let's use it to calculate the value of $2 \\div \\dfrac{1}{5}.$\n\n![](images/bc/bceafee7d9ca5e92e69b5d33d9504f4290526b80059ad62ed26229d02712a278.png)\n\n \n\nTo calculate ${\\color{red}2} \\div \\dfrac{1}{\\color{blue}5}$, we need to divide each of $\\color{red}2$ wholes into $\\color{blue}5$ equal parts, as shown below.\n\n![](images/cf/cf608ce8f5d8e2a47a4bebe21f198d193b10dd8f6e95436986bc954c89c60d19.png)\n\n \n\nWe get ${\\color{red}2} \\times {\\color{blue}5} = 10$ pieces in total. \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n2 \\div \\dfrac{1}{5} = 10\\,.\n$$" "The model below represents $2$ wholes. Let's use it to calculate the value of $2 \\operatorname{div}  \\dfrac{1}{5}.$\n\n![](images/bc/bceafee7d9ca5e92e69b5d33d9504f4290526b80059ad62ed26229d02712a278.png)\n\n \n\nTo calculate ${\\color{red}2} \\operatorname{div}  \\dfrac{1}{\\color{blue}5}$, we need to divide each of $\\color{red}2$ wholes into $\\color{blue}5$ equal parts, as shown below.\n\n![](images/cf/cf608ce8f5d8e2a47a4bebe21f198d193b10dd8f6e95436986bc954c89c60d19.png)\n\n \n\nWe get ${\\color{red}2} \\times {\\color{blue}5} = 10$ pieces in total. \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n2 \\operatorname{div}  \\dfrac{1}{5} = 10\\,.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186075045 :tutorial/content "To divide a unit fraction by a whole number, we multiply the fraction's denominator by the whole number.\n\nFor example, to calculate $\\dfrac{1}{5} \\div {\\color{blue}2},$ we multiply the fraction's denominator by ${\\color{blue}2}\\!:$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{5} \\div {\\color{blue}2}  = \\dfrac{1}{5 \\times {\\color{blue}2}} = \\dfrac{1}{10}\n$$\n\nNotice that we get the same answer using a fraction model, and the process is similar.\n\n![](images/3f/3f1f0dddd115c0d820de4d79624bd87492e215a6c2de43071fda73a4a0684d77.png)" "To divide a unit fraction by a whole number, we multiply the fraction's denominator by the whole number.\n\nFor example, to calculate $\\dfrac{1}{5} \\operatorname{div}  {\\color{blue}2},$ we multiply the fraction's denominator by ${\\color{blue}2}\\!:$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{5} \\operatorname{div}  {\\color{blue}2}  = \\dfrac{1}{5 \\times {\\color{blue}2}} = \\dfrac{1}{10}\n$$\n\nNotice that we get the same answer using a fraction model, and the process is similar.\n\n![](images/3f/3f1f0dddd115c0d820de4d79624bd87492e215a6c2de43071fda73a4a0684d77.png)"] [:db/cas 17592186075046 :tutorial/content "To divide a whole number by a unit fraction, we can multiply the whole number by the fraction's denominator.\n\nSo, to divide $2 \\div \\dfrac{1}{\\color{blue}5},$ we multiply the $2$ by ${\\color{blue}5}\\!:$\n\n$$\n2 \\div \\dfrac{1}{\\color{blue}5} = 2 \\times {\\color{blue}5} = 10\n$$\n\nAnd that's the answer! Notice that this method does the same job as the fraction model method we used before:\n\n![](images/5f/5f2eb6ab39ea67466d7a42a8809b8a039a84f9a20a13cca3659d81f669a772a5.png)" "To divide a whole number by a unit fraction, we can multiply the whole number by the fraction's denominator.\n\nSo, to divide $2 \\operatorname{div}  \\dfrac{1}{\\color{blue}5},$ we multiply the $2$ by ${\\color{blue}5}\\!:$\n\n$$\n2 \\operatorname{div}  \\dfrac{1}{\\color{blue}5} = 2 \\times {\\color{blue}5} = 10\n$$\n\nAnd that's the answer! Notice that this method does the same job as the fraction model method we used before:\n\n![](images/5f/5f2eb6ab39ea67466d7a42a8809b8a039a84f9a20a13cca3659d81f669a772a5.png)"] [:db/cas 17592186075048 :tutorial/content "We can estimate decimal division by rounding each decimal to the nearest whole number and dividing the resulting numbers.\n\nFor example, suppose we wish to estimate the quotient of the following division problem.\n$19.7 \\div 5.3$\n\nOne way to proceed is first to round both quantities to the nearest whole number:\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{ccc}19.7 & ÷ & 5.3 \\\\ ↓ &  & ↓ \\\\ {\\color{blue}{20}} & ÷ & {\\color{blue}{5}}\\end{array}\n$$\n\nSo, we can form an estimate of $19.7 \\div 5.3$ by evaluating $20 \\div 5$.\n\nComputing the quotient of this simplified problem gives\n\n$$\n20 \\div 5 = 4\n$$\n\nTherefore, $19.7 \\div 5.3$ is approximately equal to $4$. We can write this symbolically as follows:\n\n$$\n19.7 \\div 5.3 \\approx 4\n$$\n\nThe symbol\n\n$$\n\\approx\n$$\n\nmeans \"is approximately equal to.\"" "We can estimate decimal division by rounding each decimal to the nearest whole number and dividing the resulting numbers.\n\nFor example, suppose we wish to estimate the quotient of the following division problem.\n$19.7 \\operatorname{div}  5.3$\n\nOne way to proceed is first to round both quantities to the nearest whole number:\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{ccc}19.7 & ÷ & 5.3 \\\\ ↓ &  & ↓ \\\\ {\\color{blue}{20}} & ÷ & {\\color{blue}{5}}\\end{array}\n$$\n\nSo, we can form an estimate of $19.7 \\operatorname{div}  5.3$ by evaluating $20 \\operatorname{div}  5$.\n\nComputing the quotient of this simplified problem gives\n\n$$\n20 \\operatorname{div}  5 = 4\n$$\n\nTherefore, $19.7 \\operatorname{div}  5.3$ is approximately equal to $4$. We can write this symbolically as follows:\n\n$$\n19.7 \\operatorname{div}  5.3 \\approx 4\n$$\n\nThe symbol\n\n$$\n\\approx\n$$\n\nmeans \"is approximately equal to.\""] [:db/cas 17592186075052 :tutorial/content "Remember that a *unit* fraction is a fraction containing $\\color{blue}1$ in the numerator. So, for example, the fractions \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{blue}{1}}2, \\qquad\\dfrac{\\color{blue}{1}}{12}\n$$\nare both unit fractions, whereas \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{red}{2}}9, \\qquad \\dfrac{\\color{red}{3}}{5}\n$$\nare *not* unit fractions.\n\nIn this lesson, we will use models to divide unit fractions by whole numbers. \n\nLet's use a fraction model to find the value of \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{2} \\div 2.\n$$\nWe start with a square model of $\\dfrac12,$ split *vertically*.\n\n![](images/cc/ccfe0f457342c456200c98cfc46e63bdc1153698282085164eeafae5c2092c45.png)\n\nTo divide $\\dfrac12$ by ${\\color{blue}2},$ we split the above whole into $\\color{blue}2$ equal parts *horizontally*. Then, we remove any shaded parts that are not in the bottom row.\n\n![](images/79/7940073ba05d3136ee1d465530a60bc3624b7aa06fd7484d30797758a28976d7.png)\n\nFrom the picture on the right, we obtain that the shaded part represents $\\dfrac14$ of the whole.\n\nTherefore,\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{2} \\div 2 = \\dfrac{1}{4}.\n$$" "Remember that a *unit* fraction is a fraction containing $\\color{blue}1$ in the numerator. So, for example, the fractions \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{blue}{1}}2, \\qquad\\dfrac{\\color{blue}{1}}{12}\n$$\nare both unit fractions, whereas \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{red}{2}}9, \\qquad \\dfrac{\\color{red}{3}}{5}\n$$\nare *not* unit fractions.\n\nIn this lesson, we will use models to divide unit fractions by whole numbers. \n\nLet's use a fraction model to find the value of \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{2} \\operatorname{div}  2.\n$$\nWe start with a square model of $\\dfrac12,$ split *vertically*.\n\n![](images/cc/ccfe0f457342c456200c98cfc46e63bdc1153698282085164eeafae5c2092c45.png)\n\nTo divide $\\dfrac12$ by ${\\color{blue}2},$ we split the above whole into $\\color{blue}2$ equal parts *horizontally*. Then, we remove any shaded parts that are not in the bottom row.\n\n![](images/79/7940073ba05d3136ee1d465530a60bc3624b7aa06fd7484d30797758a28976d7.png)\n\nFrom the picture on the right, we obtain that the shaded part represents $\\dfrac14$ of the whole.\n\nTherefore,\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{2} \\operatorname{div}  2 = \\dfrac{1}{4}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186075058 :tutorial/content "How do we write\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{7}{\\color{blue}4} + 5\n$$\n\nas a mixed number?\n\nNotice that we cannot write\n\n$$\n5\\,\\dfrac 7 4\n$$\n\nbecause\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 7 4\n$$\n\nis an improper fraction. Instead, let's find this sum using the same method as before.\n\nFirst, we write $5$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{5}{1}\n$$\n\nThen, we put this fraction over a denominator of $\\color{blue}4$ by multiplying the numerator and denominator by ${\\color{blue}{4}}$:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{5 \\times {\\color{blue}{4}}}{1 \\times{\\color{blue}{4}}} = \\dfrac{20}{4}\n$$\n\nSo now, we have to find the following sum:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{7}{4} + \\dfrac{20}{4}\n$$\n\nTo add two fractions with like denominators, we add the numerators and keep the denominators the same:\n\n$$\n\\frac{7}{4} + \\frac{20}{4} = \\frac{7 + 20}{4} = \\frac{27}{4}\n$$\n\nFinally, we convert this to a mixed number:\n\n$$\n27 \\div 4 = 6 \\, \\textrm{R} \\, 3 = 6 \\, \\dfrac{3}{4}\n$$" "How do we write\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{7}{\\color{blue}4} + 5\n$$\n\nas a mixed number?\n\nNotice that we cannot write\n\n$$\n5\\,\\dfrac 7 4\n$$\n\nbecause\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 7 4\n$$\n\nis an improper fraction. Instead, let's find this sum using the same method as before.\n\nFirst, we write $5$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{5}{1}\n$$\n\nThen, we put this fraction over a denominator of $\\color{blue}4$ by multiplying the numerator and denominator by ${\\color{blue}{4}}$:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{5 \\times {\\color{blue}{4}}}{1 \\times{\\color{blue}{4}}} = \\dfrac{20}{4}\n$$\n\nSo now, we have to find the following sum:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{7}{4} + \\dfrac{20}{4}\n$$\n\nTo add two fractions with like denominators, we add the numerators and keep the denominators the same:\n\n$$\n\\frac{7}{4} + \\frac{20}{4} = \\frac{7 + 20}{4} = \\frac{27}{4}\n$$\n\nFinally, we convert this to a mixed number:\n\n$$\n27 \\operatorname{div}  4 = 6 \\, \\textrm{R} \\, 3 = 6 \\, \\dfrac{3}{4}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186078225 :question/worked-solution "At $x=1,$ the left-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{-}}g(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{-}}{log}_{2}⁡(ax+1) \\\\  & ={log}_{2}⁡(a(1)+1) \\\\  & ={log}_{2}⁡(a+1),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nand the right-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{+}}g(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{+}}({log}_{3}⁡(2x+1)) \\\\  & ={log}_{3}⁡(2(1)+1) \\\\  & ={log}_{3}⁡(3) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order for $g(x)$ to be continuous at $x=1,$ we require that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{-}}g(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{+}}g(x) \\\\ {log}_{2}⁡(a+1) & =1 \\\\ a+1 & ={2}^{1} \\\\ a & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "At $x=1,$ the left-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{-}}g(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{-}}{\\log }_{2}⁡(ax+1) \\\\  & ={\\log }_{2}⁡(a(1)+1) \\\\  & ={\\log }_{2}⁡(a+1),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nand the right-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{+}}g(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{+}}({\\log }_{3}⁡(2x+1)) \\\\  & ={\\log }_{3}⁡(2(1)+1) \\\\  & ={\\log }_{3}⁡(3) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order for $g(x)$ to be continuous at $x=1,$ we require that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{-}}g(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{+}}g(x) \\\\ {\\log }_{2}⁡(a+1) & =1 \\\\ a+1 & ={2}^{1} \\\\ a & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186078288 :question/worked-solution "Notice that the terms of the corresponding sequence\n\n$$\n4, \\quad 8, \\quad 12, \\quad , \\ldots, \\quad 56, \\quad 60.\n$$\n\n are multiples of $4,$ and since $60\\div 4 = 15,$ there are $15$ terms in total. \n\nTherefore, the $n$th term of the sequence has the form\n\n$$\na_n =4n,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ ranges from $n=1$ to $n=15\\mathbin{.}$ Indeed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} & =4(1)=4 \\\\ {a}_{2} & =4(2)=8 \\\\ {a}_{3} & =4(3)=12 \\\\  & \\,\\,⋮ \\\\ {a}_{14} & =4(14)=56 \\\\ {a}_{15} & =4(15)=60\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nS=4+8+12+\\cdots+56+60=\\sum_{n=1}^{15} 4n.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Notice that the terms of the corresponding sequence\n\n$$\n4, \\quad 8, \\quad 12, \\quad , \\ldots, \\quad 56, \\quad 60.\n$$\n\n are multiples of $4,$ and since $60\\operatorname{div}  4 = 15,$ there are $15$ terms in total. \n\nTherefore, the $n$th term of the sequence has the form\n\n$$\na_n =4n,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ ranges from $n=1$ to $n=15\\mathbin{.}$ Indeed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} & =4(1)=4 \\\\ {a}_{2} & =4(2)=8 \\\\ {a}_{3} & =4(3)=12 \\\\  & \\,\\,⋮ \\\\ {a}_{14} & =4(14)=56 \\\\ {a}_{15} & =4(15)=60\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nS=4+8+12+\\cdots+56+60=\\sum_{n=1}^{15} 4n.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186078318 :question/problem "Find the value of the expression $\\sqrt{ (3^4+7^2-2) \\div 2}.$" "Find the value of the expression $\\sqrt{ (3^4+7^2-2) \\operatorname{div}  2}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186078319 :question/problem "Simplify the expression $\\sqrt{((5^2-4) \\div 3)-2}.$" "Simplify the expression $\\sqrt{((5^2-4) \\operatorname{div}  3)-2}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186078329 :question/worked-solution "First, we can simplify the limit using trigonometric identities, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\theta \\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta }{4{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta } & =\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}(\\frac{\\theta }{4{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}(\\frac{\\theta }{4{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }) \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}(\\frac{\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\cdot \\frac{1}{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }) \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\cdot {lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{1}{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now evaluate the limits separately:\n\n- Consider the first limit. Notice that as $\\theta \\to 0,$ both numerator and denominator approach $0.$ \n\nSo, if we attempt to evaluate the limit directly, we get\n\n$$\n\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}\\: \\dfrac{\\theta}{\\sin\\theta} = \\dfrac{0}{0},\n$$\n\nwhich is an indeterminate form. \n\nRewriting the given limit using the algebra of limits and applying our special limit, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } & =\\frac{1}{(\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\theta })} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Consider the second limit. Evaluating it directly, we get\n\n$$\n\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0} \\:\\dfrac{1}{4 \\cos \\theta} = \\dfrac{1}{4\\cos 0} = \\dfrac14.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\cdot {lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{1}{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta } & =1\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}=\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we can simplify the limit using trigonometric identities, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\theta \\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta }{4{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta } & =\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}(\\frac{\\theta }{4{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}(\\frac{\\theta }{4{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta }\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }) \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}(\\frac{\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\cdot \\frac{1}{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }) \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\cdot {lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{1}{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now evaluate the limits separately:\n\n- Consider the first limit. Notice that as $\\theta \\to 0,$ both numerator and denominator approach $0.$ \n\nSo, if we attempt to evaluate the limit directly, we get\n\n$$\n\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}\\: \\dfrac{\\theta}{\\sin\\theta} = \\dfrac{0}{0},\n$$\n\nwhich is an indeterminate form. \n\nRewriting the given limit using the algebra of limits and applying our special limit, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } & =\\frac{1}{(\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\theta })} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Consider the second limit. Evaluating it directly, we get\n\n$$\n\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0} \\:\\dfrac{1}{4 \\cos \\theta} = \\dfrac{1}{4\\cos 0} = \\dfrac14.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\cdot {lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{1}{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta } & =1\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}=\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186078910 :question/problem "Find the value of $(\\sqrt{81} + 7) \\div (2 - 6)$." "Find the value of $(\\sqrt{81} + 7) \\operatorname{div}  (2 - 6)$."] [:db/cas 17592186078956 :question/worked-solution "Here, both the lower and upper limits contain a variable. However, to apply FTC II, we need the variable to be on the upper limit only. So, we can split the integral into two integrals, and rewrite them so that in both cases the variable is on the upper limit. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{x}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}\\sqrt[3]{1+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{x}^{a}\\sqrt[3]{1+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{a}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}\\sqrt[3]{1+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{a}^{x}\\sqrt[3]{1+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{a}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}\\sqrt[3]{1+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is any constant number between $x$ and $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$\n\nThe integrand $\\sqrt[3]{1+{t}^{2}}$ is continuous for all $t,$ so applying FTC II gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-{\\int }_{a}^{x}\\sqrt[3]{1+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{a}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}\\sqrt[3]{1+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({\\int }_{a}^{x}\\sqrt[3]{1+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({\\int }_{a}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\sqrt[3]{1+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =-\\sqrt[3]{1+{x}^{2}}+\\frac{\\text{du}}{\\text{d}x}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}({\\int }_{a}^{u}\\sqrt[3]{1+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =-\\sqrt[3]{1+{x}^{2}}+(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x))\\sqrt[3]{1+{u}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\sqrt[3]{1+(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x))^{2}}-\\sqrt[3]{1+{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\sqrt[3]{1+{sin}^{2}⁡(x)}-\\sqrt[3]{1+{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we used the substitution $u=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$\n\nFinally, we can compute ${F}^{′}(0).$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(0) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)\\sqrt[3]{1+{sin}^{2}⁡(0)}-\\sqrt[3]{1+(0)^{2}} \\\\  & =1\\sqrt[3]{1}-\\sqrt[3]{1} \\\\  & =1-1 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Here, both the lower and upper limits contain a variable. However, to apply FTC II, we need the variable to be on the upper limit only. So, we can split the integral into two integrals, and rewrite them so that in both cases the variable is on the upper limit. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{x}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}\\sqrt[3]{1+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{x}^{a}\\sqrt[3]{1+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{a}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}\\sqrt[3]{1+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{a}^{x}\\sqrt[3]{1+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{a}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}\\sqrt[3]{1+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is any constant number between $x$ and $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$\n\nThe integrand $\\sqrt[3]{1+{t}^{2}}$ is continuous for all $t,$ so applying FTC II gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-{\\int }_{a}^{x}\\sqrt[3]{1+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{a}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}\\sqrt[3]{1+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({\\int }_{a}^{x}\\sqrt[3]{1+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({\\int }_{a}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\sqrt[3]{1+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =-\\sqrt[3]{1+{x}^{2}}+\\frac{\\text{du}}{\\text{d}x}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}({\\int }_{a}^{u}\\sqrt[3]{1+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =-\\sqrt[3]{1+{x}^{2}}+(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x))\\sqrt[3]{1+{u}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\sqrt[3]{1+(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x))^{2}}-\\sqrt[3]{1+{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\sqrt[3]{1+{\\sin }^{2}⁡(x)}-\\sqrt[3]{1+{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we used the substitution $u=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$\n\nFinally, we can compute ${F}^{′}(0).$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(0) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)\\sqrt[3]{1+{\\sin }^{2}⁡(0)}-\\sqrt[3]{1+(0)^{2}} \\\\  & =1\\sqrt[3]{1}-\\sqrt[3]{1} \\\\  & =1-1 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186079032 :question/problem "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{\\pi /8}^{\\pi /8}{sin}^{3}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡2x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{\\pi /8}^{\\pi /8}{\\sin }^{3}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡2x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186079032 :question/worked-solution "Notice that the lower and upper limits are the same. So, the integral evaluates to zero:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{\\pi /8}^{\\pi /8}{sin}^{3}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡2x)\\,\\text{d}x=0\n$$" "Notice that the lower and upper limits are the same. So, the integral evaluates to zero:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{\\pi /8}^{\\pi /8}{\\sin }^{3}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡2x)\\,\\text{d}x=0\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186079184 :question/problem "What is the value of $\\sqrt{(24 \\div 2) + 12} - 3$ rounded to $3$ decimal places?\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." "What is the value of $\\sqrt{(24 \\operatorname{div}  2) + 12} - 3$ rounded to $3$ decimal places?\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question."] [:db/cas 17592186079665 :tutorial/content "Suppose we want to simplify the following expression:\n\n$$\n-3\\sqrt{\\left(2^4-1\\right) \\div 3}\\,.\n$$\n\nThe first thing to realize is that there is an invisible multiplication symbol here. The above expression is short for\n\n$$\n-3\\,{\\color{red}{\\times}}\\,\\sqrt{\\left(2^4-1\\right) \\div 3}\\,.\n$$\n\nWhen multiplying a radical by a number, we usually omit the additional multiplication symbol.\n\nNow, to simplify the given expression, we use PEMDAS.  In terms of the order of operations:\n\n- Expressions underneath radicals are treated as if they are contained within parentheses (the **P** in **P**EMDAS), so we always deal with this first.\n\n- Radicals themselves are treated as exponents (the **E** in P**E**MDAS).\n\nTherefore, following PEMDAS, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3\\sqrt{({2}^{4}-1)÷3} &= -3\\sqrt{(16-1)÷3} \\\\ &= -3\\sqrt{15÷3} \\\\ &= -3\\sqrt{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd that's our answer! \n\nTo get a numerical approximation, we need to use a calculator. In this case, we get\n\n$$\n-3 \\sqrt{5} = -3\\times \\sqrt{5} \\approx -6.708\\,204\n$$\n\nrounded to six decimal places." "Suppose we want to simplify the following expression:\n\n$$\n-3\\sqrt{\\left(2^4-1\\right) \\operatorname{div}  3}\\,.\n$$\n\nThe first thing to realize is that there is an invisible multiplication symbol here. The above expression is short for\n\n$$\n-3\\,{\\color{red}{\\times}}\\,\\sqrt{\\left(2^4-1\\right) \\operatorname{div}  3}\\,.\n$$\n\nWhen multiplying a radical by a number, we usually omit the additional multiplication symbol.\n\nNow, to simplify the given expression, we use PEMDAS.  In terms of the order of operations:\n\n- Expressions underneath radicals are treated as if they are contained within parentheses (the **P** in **P**EMDAS), so we always deal with this first.\n\n- Radicals themselves are treated as exponents (the **E** in P**E**MDAS).\n\nTherefore, following PEMDAS, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3\\sqrt{({2}^{4}-1)÷3} &= -3\\sqrt{(16-1)÷3} \\\\ &= -3\\sqrt{15÷3} \\\\ &= -3\\sqrt{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd that's our answer! \n\nTo get a numerical approximation, we need to use a calculator. In this case, we get\n\n$$\n-3 \\sqrt{5} = -3\\times \\sqrt{5} \\approx -6.708\\,204\n$$\n\nrounded to six decimal places."] [:db/cas 17592186079667 :tutorial/content "Suppose we need to evaluate an expression like\n\n$$\n1+\\sqrt{4},\n$$\n\nwhere we have a square root sign. What would be the order of operations? It turns out that the root signs ($\\sqrt{\\phantom{x}}$ or $\\sqrt[3]{\\phantom{x}}$) can be thought of as parentheses. \nIn other words, we find the value under the square root first:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1+\\sqrt{4} &= 1+2 \\\\ &= 3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSimilarly, let's evaluate $\\big(1-\\sqrt[3]{-27}\\big) \\div 2$.\n\nTo evaluate expressions with radicals, we use PEMDAS as before, but with the following additional rules:\n\n- expressions underneath radicals are treated the same as parentheses\n\n- radicals themselves are treated as exponents\n\nFollowing the order of operations, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(1-\\sqrt[3]{-27})÷2 &= (1-\\sqrt[3]{(-3)^{3}})÷2 \\\\ &= (1-(-3))÷2 \\\\ &= 4÷2 \\\\ &= 2\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Suppose we need to evaluate an expression like\n\n$$\n1+\\sqrt{4},\n$$\n\nwhere we have a square root sign. What would be the order of operations? It turns out that the root signs ($\\sqrt{\\phantom{x}}$ or $\\sqrt[3]{\\phantom{x}}$) can be thought of as parentheses. \nIn other words, we find the value under the square root first:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1+\\sqrt{4} &= 1+2 \\\\ &= 3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSimilarly, let's evaluate $\\big(1-\\sqrt[3]{-27}\\big) \\operatorname{div}  2$.\n\nTo evaluate expressions with radicals, we use PEMDAS as before, but with the following additional rules:\n\n- expressions underneath radicals are treated the same as parentheses\n\n- radicals themselves are treated as exponents\n\nFollowing the order of operations, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(1-\\sqrt[3]{-27})÷2 &= (1-\\sqrt[3]{(-3)^{3}})÷2 \\\\ &= (1-(-3))÷2 \\\\ &= 4÷2 \\\\ &= 2\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186079710 :tutorial/content "We know how to evaluate expressions like $2^3, 2^2, 2^1$, and even $2^0.$ But what if the power is negative? How would we evaluate $2^{-1},$ for example?\n\nTo help us with that, let's go back to the results table for powers of $2.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{4} & =16 \\\\ {2}^{3} & =8 \\\\ {2}^{2} & =4 \\\\ {2}^{1} & =2 \\\\ {2}^{0} & =1 \\\\ {2}^{-1} & =◻\\,?\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs we go down the list, the number in blue is divided by $2$ every time. If we follow the same pattern, we find that \n\n$$\n2^{\\color{red}{-1}} = {\\color{blue}{1}}\\div 2 = \\dfrac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, $\\dfrac{1}{2}$ is just the reciprocal of $2.$ So, raising a number to the power of $-1$ is the *same* as finding its reciprocal!" "We know how to evaluate expressions like $2^3, 2^2, 2^1$, and even $2^0.$ But what if the power is negative? How would we evaluate $2^{-1},$ for example?\n\nTo help us with that, let's go back to the results table for powers of $2.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{4} & =16 \\\\ {2}^{3} & =8 \\\\ {2}^{2} & =4 \\\\ {2}^{1} & =2 \\\\ {2}^{0} & =1 \\\\ {2}^{-1} & =◻\\,?\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs we go down the list, the number in blue is divided by $2$ every time. If we follow the same pattern, we find that \n\n$$\n2^{\\color{red}{-1}} = {\\color{blue}{1}}\\operatorname{div}  2 = \\dfrac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, $\\dfrac{1}{2}$ is just the reciprocal of $2.$ So, raising a number to the power of $-1$ is the *same* as finding its reciprocal!"] [:db/cas 17592186082908 :question/worked-solution "The limit comparison test states that if $a_n$ and $b_n$ are both positive, and\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\rightarrow\\infty} \\frac{a_n}{b_n} = L\n$$\n\nwhere $L$ is positive and finite, then the series\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty a_n\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty b_n\n$$\n\neither *both* converge or *both* diverge.\n\nThe given series looks as though it will behave like the convergent series\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{1}{2n^2} = \\dfrac 1 2\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{1}{n^2}\n$$\n\nSo, we let\n\n$$\na_n = \\dfrac{1}{2n^2+n}, \\qquad b_n = \\dfrac{1}{n^2}\n$$\n\nComputing the limit of the ratio, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\lim_{n \\to  \\infty}\\frac{a_{n}}{b_{n}} &= \\lim_{n \\to  \\infty}(\\frac{1}{2n^{2} + n} \\div \\frac{1}{n^{2}}) \\\\\n&= \\lim_{n \\to  \\infty}\\left(\\frac{n^{2}}{2n^{2} + n}\\right) \\\\\n&= \\lim_{n \\to  \\infty}(1)/((2 + \\frac{1}{n})) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2 + 0} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $a_n$ and $b_n$ are both positive and\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\lim_{n\\rightarrow\\infty} \\frac{a_n}{b_n} = \\dfrac12\n$$\n\nis positive and finite, we can use the limit comparison test.\n\nSo, since\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty b_n\n$$\n\nconverges, then\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty a_n\n$$\n\nconverges as well. Therefore, the series\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{1}{2n^2+n}\n$$\n\nconverges." "The limit comparison test states that if $a_n$ and $b_n$ are both positive, and\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\rightarrow\\infty} \\frac{a_n}{b_n} = L\n$$\n\nwhere $L$ is positive and finite, then the series\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty a_n\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty b_n\n$$\n\neither *both* converge or *both* diverge.\n\nThe given series looks as though it will behave like the convergent series\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{1}{2n^2} = \\dfrac 1 2\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{1}{n^2}\n$$\n\nSo, we let\n\n$$\na_n = \\dfrac{1}{2n^2+n}, \\qquad b_n = \\dfrac{1}{n^2}\n$$\n\nComputing the limit of the ratio, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\lim_{n \\to  \\infty}\\frac{a_{n}}{b_{n}} &= \\lim_{n \\to  \\infty}(\\frac{1}{2n^{2} + n} \\operatorname{div}  \\frac{1}{n^{2}}) \\\\\n&= \\lim_{n \\to  \\infty}\\left(\\frac{n^{2}}{2n^{2} + n}\\right) \\\\\n&= \\lim_{n \\to  \\infty}(1)/((2 + \\frac{1}{n})) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2 + 0} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $a_n$ and $b_n$ are both positive and\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\lim_{n\\rightarrow\\infty} \\frac{a_n}{b_n} = \\dfrac12\n$$\n\nis positive and finite, we can use the limit comparison test.\n\nSo, since\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty b_n\n$$\n\nconverges, then\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty a_n\n$$\n\nconverges as well. Therefore, the series\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{1}{2n^2+n}\n$$\n\nconverges."] [:db/cas 17592186082909 :question/worked-solution "The limit comparison test states that if $a_n$ and $b_n$ are both positive, and\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\rightarrow\\infty} \\frac{a_n}{b_n} = L\n$$\n\nwhere $L$ is positive and finite, then the series\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty a_n\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty b_n\n$$\n\neither *both* converge or *both* diverge.\n\nThe given series looks as though it will behave like the divergent series\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{2n^3}{4n^4} = \\dfrac 2 4\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{n^3}{n^4} = \\dfrac 1 2\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{1}{n}\n$$\n\nSo, we let\n\n$$\na_n = \\dfrac{2n^3 - n^2}{4n^4+n}, \\qquad b_n = \\dfrac{1}{n}\n$$\n\nComputing the limit of the ratio, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\lim_{n \\to  \\infty}\\frac{a_{n}}{b_{n}} &= \\lim_{n \\to  \\infty}(\\frac{2n^{3} - n^{2}}{4n^{4} + n} \\div \\frac{1}{n}) \\\\\n&= \\lim_{n \\to  \\infty}(\\frac{2n^{3} - n^{2}}{4n^{4} + n} \\cdot n) \\\\\n&= \\lim_{n \\to  \\infty}\\left(\\frac{2n^{4} - n^{3}}{4n^{4} + n}\\right) \\\\\n&= \\lim_{n \\to  \\infty}(((2 - \\frac{1}{n}))/((4 + \\frac{1}{n^{3}}))) \\\\\n&= \\frac{2 - 0}{4 + 0} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $a_n$ and $b_n$ are both positive, and\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\lim_{n\\rightarrow\\infty} \\frac{a_n}{b_n} = \\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nis positive and finite, we can use the limit comparison test.\n\nSo, since\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty b_n\n$$\n\ndiverges, then\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty a_n\n$$\n\ndiverges as well. Therefore, the series\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{2n^3-n^2}{4n^4+n}\n$$\n\ndiverges." "The limit comparison test states that if $a_n$ and $b_n$ are both positive, and\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\rightarrow\\infty} \\frac{a_n}{b_n} = L\n$$\n\nwhere $L$ is positive and finite, then the series\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty a_n\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty b_n\n$$\n\neither *both* converge or *both* diverge.\n\nThe given series looks as though it will behave like the divergent series\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{2n^3}{4n^4} = \\dfrac 2 4\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{n^3}{n^4} = \\dfrac 1 2\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{1}{n}\n$$\n\nSo, we let\n\n$$\na_n = \\dfrac{2n^3 - n^2}{4n^4+n}, \\qquad b_n = \\dfrac{1}{n}\n$$\n\nComputing the limit of the ratio, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\lim_{n \\to  \\infty}\\frac{a_{n}}{b_{n}} &= \\lim_{n \\to  \\infty}(\\frac{2n^{3} - n^{2}}{4n^{4} + n} \\operatorname{div}  \\frac{1}{n}) \\\\\n&= \\lim_{n \\to  \\infty}(\\frac{2n^{3} - n^{2}}{4n^{4} + n} \\cdot n) \\\\\n&= \\lim_{n \\to  \\infty}\\left(\\frac{2n^{4} - n^{3}}{4n^{4} + n}\\right) \\\\\n&= \\lim_{n \\to  \\infty}(((2 - \\frac{1}{n}))/((4 + \\frac{1}{n^{3}}))) \\\\\n&= \\frac{2 - 0}{4 + 0} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $a_n$ and $b_n$ are both positive, and\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\lim_{n\\rightarrow\\infty} \\frac{a_n}{b_n} = \\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nis positive and finite, we can use the limit comparison test.\n\nSo, since\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty b_n\n$$\n\ndiverges, then\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty a_n\n$$\n\ndiverges as well. Therefore, the series\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{2n^3-n^2}{4n^4+n}\n$$\n\ndiverges."] [:db/cas 17592186082910 :question/worked-solution "The limit comparison test states that if $a_n$ and $b_n$ are both positive, and\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\rightarrow\\infty} \\frac{a_n}{b_n} = L\n$$\n\nwhere $L$ is positive and finite, then the series\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty a_n\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty b_n\n$$\n\neither *both* converge or *both* diverge.\n\nThe given series looks as though it will behave like the convergent geometric series\n\n$$\n\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{2^n}{3^n} = \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\left(\\dfrac{2}{3} \\right)^n\n$$\n\nWe know the above series converges because it is a geometric series with common ratio\n\n$$\n\\mid r \\mid = \\dfrac{2}{3} < 1\n$$\n\nSo, we let\n\n$$\na_n = \\dfrac{3(2^n)+1}{2(3^n)+1}, \\qquad b_n = \\left(\\dfrac{2}{3} \\right)^n\n$$\n\nComputing the limit of the ratio, we get\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle \\lim_{n \\to  \\mathrm{\\infty }} {\\displaystyle \\frac{ {a}_{n}}{ {b}_{n}}} & \\displaystyle  = \\lim_{n \\to  \\mathrm{\\infty }} ( {\\displaystyle \\frac{3 ( {2}^{n} ) + 1}{2 ( {3}^{n} ) + 1}} \\div  {\\displaystyle \\frac{ {2}^{n}}{ {3}^{n}}} ) \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = \\lim_{n \\to  \\mathrm{\\infty }} ( {\\displaystyle \\frac{3 ( {2}^{n} ) + 1}{2 ( {3}^{n} ) + 1}} \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{ {3}^{n}}{ {2}^{n}}} ) \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = \\lim_{n \\to  \\mathrm{\\infty }} ( {\\displaystyle \\frac{ {2}^{n} ( 3 + {2}^{- n} )}{ {3}^{n} ( 2 + {3}^{- n} )}} \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{ {3}^{n}}{ {2}^{n}}} ) \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = \\lim_{n \\to  \\mathrm{\\infty }} ( {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\cancel{ {2}^{n}} ( 3 + {2}^{- n} )}{\\cancel{ {3}^{n}} ( 2 + {3}^{- n} )}} \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\cancel{ {3}^{n}}}{\\cancel{ {2}^{n}}}} ) \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = \\lim_{n \\to  \\mathrm{\\infty }} ( {\\displaystyle \\frac{3 + {2}^{- n}}{2 + {3}^{- n}}} ) \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{3 + 2 ( 0 )}{2 + 3 ( 0 )}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{3}{2}} .\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nSince $a_n$ and $b_n$ are both positive and\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\lim_{n\\rightarrow\\infty} \\frac{a_n}{b_n} = \\dfrac{3}{2}\n$$\n\nis positive and finite, we can use the limit comparison test.\n\nSo, since\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty b_n\n$$\n\nconverges, then\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty a_n\n$$\n\nconverges as well. Therefore, the series\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{3(2^n)+1}{2(3^n)+1}\n$$\n\nconverges." "The limit comparison test states that if $a_n$ and $b_n$ are both positive, and\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\rightarrow\\infty} \\frac{a_n}{b_n} = L\n$$\n\nwhere $L$ is positive and finite, then the series\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty a_n\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty b_n\n$$\n\neither *both* converge or *both* diverge.\n\nThe given series looks as though it will behave like the convergent geometric series\n\n$$\n\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{2^n}{3^n} = \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\left(\\dfrac{2}{3} \\right)^n\n$$\n\nWe know the above series converges because it is a geometric series with common ratio\n\n$$\n\\mid r \\mid = \\dfrac{2}{3} < 1\n$$\n\nSo, we let\n\n$$\na_n = \\dfrac{3(2^n)+1}{2(3^n)+1}, \\qquad b_n = \\left(\\dfrac{2}{3} \\right)^n\n$$\n\nComputing the limit of the ratio, we get\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle \\lim_{n \\to  \\mathrm{\\infty }} {\\displaystyle \\frac{ {a}_{n}}{ {b}_{n}}} & \\displaystyle  = \\lim_{n \\to  \\mathrm{\\infty }} ( {\\displaystyle \\frac{3 ( {2}^{n} ) + 1}{2 ( {3}^{n} ) + 1}} \\operatorname{div}   {\\displaystyle \\frac{ {2}^{n}}{ {3}^{n}}} ) \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = \\lim_{n \\to  \\mathrm{\\infty }} ( {\\displaystyle \\frac{3 ( {2}^{n} ) + 1}{2 ( {3}^{n} ) + 1}} \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{ {3}^{n}}{ {2}^{n}}} ) \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = \\lim_{n \\to  \\mathrm{\\infty }} ( {\\displaystyle \\frac{ {2}^{n} ( 3 + {2}^{- n} )}{ {3}^{n} ( 2 + {3}^{- n} )}} \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{ {3}^{n}}{ {2}^{n}}} ) \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = \\lim_{n \\to  \\mathrm{\\infty }} ( {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\cancel{ {2}^{n}} ( 3 + {2}^{- n} )}{\\cancel{ {3}^{n}} ( 2 + {3}^{- n} )}} \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\cancel{ {3}^{n}}}{\\cancel{ {2}^{n}}}} ) \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = \\lim_{n \\to  \\mathrm{\\infty }} ( {\\displaystyle \\frac{3 + {2}^{- n}}{2 + {3}^{- n}}} ) \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{3 + 2 ( 0 )}{2 + 3 ( 0 )}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{3}{2}} .\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nSince $a_n$ and $b_n$ are both positive and\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\lim_{n\\rightarrow\\infty} \\frac{a_n}{b_n} = \\dfrac{3}{2}\n$$\n\nis positive and finite, we can use the limit comparison test.\n\nSo, since\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty b_n\n$$\n\nconverges, then\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty a_n\n$$\n\nconverges as well. Therefore, the series\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{3(2^n)+1}{2(3^n)+1}\n$$\n\nconverges."] [:db/cas 17592186082911 :question/worked-solution "Let $a_n$ and $b_n$ be two positive sequences. The one-sided limit comparison tests state the following:\n\n- If $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{n\\rightarrow\\infty} \\frac{a_n}{b_n} = 0,\n$$ and it is known that $$\n\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty b_n\n$$ converges, then $$\n\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty a_n\n$$ also converges.\n- If $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{n\\rightarrow\\infty} \\frac{a_n}{b_n} = \\infty,\n$$ and it is known that $$\n\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty b_n\n$$ diverges, then $$\n\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty a_n\n$$ also diverges.\n\nFirst, notice that\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty b_n\n$$\n\nis a divergent $p$-series.\n\nComputing the limit of the ratio, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL &= \\lim_{n \\to  \\infty}\\frac{a_{n}}{b_{n}} \\\\\n&= \\lim_{n \\to  \\infty}(\\frac{\\sqrt[3]{n^{2}}}{3n + 4} \\div \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{n}}) \\\\\n&= \\lim_{n \\to  \\infty}(\\frac{\\sqrt[3]{n^{2}}}{3n + 4} \\cdot \\sqrt{n}) \\\\\n&= \\lim_{n \\to  \\infty}(\\frac{n^{2/3}}{3n + 4} \\cdot n^{1/2}) \\\\\n&= \\lim_{n \\to  \\infty}\\left(\\frac{n^{7/6}}{3n + 4}\\right) \\\\\n&= \\lim_{n \\to  \\infty}\\left(\\frac{n^{1/6}}{3 + 4/n}\\right) \\\\\n&= \\frac{∞}{3 + 0} \\\\\n&= ∞\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $a_n$ and $b_n$ are both positive and\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{n \\to\\infty} \\dfrac{a_n}{b_n}=\\infty\n$$\n\nwe can use the one-sided comparison test.\n\nSo, since\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty b_n\n$$\n\ndiverges, then\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty a_n\n$$\n\ndiverges as well. Therefore, the series\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{\\sqrt[3]{n^2}}{3n+4}\n$$\n\ndiverges." "Let $a_n$ and $b_n$ be two positive sequences. The one-sided limit comparison tests state the following:\n\n- If $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{n\\rightarrow\\infty} \\frac{a_n}{b_n} = 0,\n$$ and it is known that $$\n\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty b_n\n$$ converges, then $$\n\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty a_n\n$$ also converges.\n- If $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{n\\rightarrow\\infty} \\frac{a_n}{b_n} = \\infty,\n$$ and it is known that $$\n\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty b_n\n$$ diverges, then $$\n\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty a_n\n$$ also diverges.\n\nFirst, notice that\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty b_n\n$$\n\nis a divergent $p$-series.\n\nComputing the limit of the ratio, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL &= \\lim_{n \\to  \\infty}\\frac{a_{n}}{b_{n}} \\\\\n&= \\lim_{n \\to  \\infty}(\\frac{\\sqrt[3]{n^{2}}}{3n + 4} \\operatorname{div}  \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{n}}) \\\\\n&= \\lim_{n \\to  \\infty}(\\frac{\\sqrt[3]{n^{2}}}{3n + 4} \\cdot \\sqrt{n}) \\\\\n&= \\lim_{n \\to  \\infty}(\\frac{n^{2/3}}{3n + 4} \\cdot n^{1/2}) \\\\\n&= \\lim_{n \\to  \\infty}\\left(\\frac{n^{7/6}}{3n + 4}\\right) \\\\\n&= \\lim_{n \\to  \\infty}\\left(\\frac{n^{1/6}}{3 + 4/n}\\right) \\\\\n&= \\frac{∞}{3 + 0} \\\\\n&= ∞\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $a_n$ and $b_n$ are both positive and\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{n \\to\\infty} \\dfrac{a_n}{b_n}=\\infty\n$$\n\nwe can use the one-sided comparison test.\n\nSo, since\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty b_n\n$$\n\ndiverges, then\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty a_n\n$$\n\ndiverges as well. Therefore, the series\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{\\sqrt[3]{n^2}}{3n+4}\n$$\n\ndiverges."] [:db/cas 17592186082950 :question/worked-solution "The profit is equal to the revenue minus the cost:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(x) &= R(x) - C(x) \\\\\n&= (130x - 0.4x^{2}) - (6700 + 26x) \\\\\n&=-0.4x^{2} + 104x - 6700\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since we want a profit of $\\$20\\,000$, and $P(x)$ is measured in thousands of dollars, we set $P(x)$ equal to\n\n$$\n20\\,000\\div 1\\,000 = 20\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(x) &= 20 \\\\\n- 0.4x^{2} + 104x - 6700 &= 20\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can tidy up the equation so that it becomes a quadratic equation in standard form with a leading coefficient of $1$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-0.4x^{2} + 104x - 6700 &= 20 \\\\\n0.4x^{2} - 104x + 6720 &= 0 \\\\\n\\frac{0.4x^{2} - 104x + 6720}{0.4} &= \\frac{0}{0.4} \\\\\nx^{2} - 260x + 16800 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to solve for $x$, we apply the quadratic formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= (- (-260) ± \\sqrt{(-260)^{2} - 4(1)(16800)})/(2) \\\\\n&= \\frac{260 ± \\sqrt{400}}{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{260 ± 20}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solutions are $x=120$ and $x=140$. Therefore, the company will make a profit of $\\$20\\,000$ if it produces $120$ or $140$ units." "The profit is equal to the revenue minus the cost:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(x) &= R(x) - C(x) \\\\\n&= (130x - 0.4x^{2}) - (6700 + 26x) \\\\\n&=-0.4x^{2} + 104x - 6700\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since we want a profit of $\\$20\\,000$, and $P(x)$ is measured in thousands of dollars, we set $P(x)$ equal to\n\n$$\n20\\,000\\operatorname{div}  1\\,000 = 20\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(x) &= 20 \\\\\n- 0.4x^{2} + 104x - 6700 &= 20\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can tidy up the equation so that it becomes a quadratic equation in standard form with a leading coefficient of $1$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-0.4x^{2} + 104x - 6700 &= 20 \\\\\n0.4x^{2} - 104x + 6720 &= 0 \\\\\n\\frac{0.4x^{2} - 104x + 6720}{0.4} &= \\frac{0}{0.4} \\\\\nx^{2} - 260x + 16800 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to solve for $x$, we apply the quadratic formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= (- (-260) ± \\sqrt{(-260)^{2} - 4(1)(16800)})/(2) \\\\\n&= \\frac{260 ± \\sqrt{400}}{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{260 ± 20}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solutions are $x=120$ and $x=140$. Therefore, the company will make a profit of $\\$20\\,000$ if it produces $120$ or $140$ units."] [:db/cas 17592186082957 :question/worked-solution "The angles $30^\\circ$ and $60^\\circ$ are special angles, and their trigonometric ratios have the particular values\n\n$$\n\\sin{30^\\circ} = \\dfrac{1}{2}, \\qquad \\cos 60^\\circ = \\dfrac{1}{2} , \\qquad \\tan{30^\\circ} = \\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}.\n$$\n\nAlso, note that $\\tan^2{30^\\circ}$ simply means $(\\tan{30^\\circ})^2.$ \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡{30}^{∘}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡{60}^{∘}+{tan}^{2}⁡{30}^{∘} &= \\operatorname{sin}⁡{30}^{∘}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡{60}^{∘}+(\\operatorname{tan}⁡{30}^{∘})^{2} \\\\ &= \\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\right)}^{2} \\\\ &= 1+\\frac{3}{9} \\\\ &= 1+\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ &= \\frac{4}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The angles $30^\\circ$ and $60^\\circ$ are special angles, and their trigonometric ratios have the particular values\n\n$$\n\\sin{30^\\circ} = \\dfrac{1}{2}, \\qquad \\cos 60^\\circ = \\dfrac{1}{2} , \\qquad \\tan{30^\\circ} = \\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}.\n$$\n\nAlso, note that $\\tan^2{30^\\circ}$ simply means $(\\tan{30^\\circ})^2.$ \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡{30}^{∘}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡{60}^{∘}+{\\tan }^{2}⁡{30}^{∘} &= \\operatorname{sin}⁡{30}^{∘}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡{60}^{∘}+(\\operatorname{tan}⁡{30}^{∘})^{2} \\\\ &= \\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\right)}^{2} \\\\ &= 1+\\frac{3}{9} \\\\ &= 1+\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ &= \\frac{4}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186083097 :question/worked-solution "Let\n\n$$\na_n = \\dfrac{n}{3^n}\n$$\n\nIf we replace $n$ with $n+1$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} a_{n+1}= \\frac{n+1}{3^{n+1}}= \\frac{n+1}{3\\cdot 3^n}. \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that we have expressions for the consecutive terms $a_n$ and $a_{n+1}$, we can work out the necessary limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} L &=\\lim_{n\\to\\infty}\\left|\\frac{a_{n+1}}{a_n} \\right| \\\\[5pt] &=\\lim_{n\\to\\infty}\\left(\\frac{n+1}{3\\cdot 3^n} \\div \\frac{n}{3^n}\\right) \\\\[5pt] &= \\lim_{n\\to\\infty}\\left(\\frac{n+1}{3\\cdot 3^n} \\cdot \\frac{3^n}{n}\\right) \\\\[5pt] &= \\lim_{n\\to\\infty}\\left(\\frac{n+1}{3n} \\right) \\\\[5pt] &= \\lim_{n\\to\\infty}\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\frac{n+1}{n}\\right) \\\\[5pt] &= \\frac{1}{3}\\lim_{n\\to\\infty}\\left(1 + \\frac{1}{n}\\right) \\\\[5pt] &=\\frac{1}{3}\\left(1 + 0\\right) \\\\[5pt] &=\\frac{1}{3}. \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we dropped the absolute value bars because the sequence is positive for all\n\n$$\nn\\geq 1\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\nL = \\dfrac{1}{3}<1\n$$\n\nwe conclude that the series is convergent by the ratio test." "Let\n\n$$\na_n = \\dfrac{n}{3^n}\n$$\n\nIf we replace $n$ with $n+1$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} a_{n+1}= \\frac{n+1}{3^{n+1}}= \\frac{n+1}{3\\cdot 3^n}. \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that we have expressions for the consecutive terms $a_n$ and $a_{n+1}$, we can work out the necessary limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} L &=\\lim_{n\\to\\infty}\\left|\\frac{a_{n+1}}{a_n} \\right| \\\\[5pt] &=\\lim_{n\\to\\infty}\\left(\\frac{n+1}{3\\cdot 3^n} \\operatorname{div}  \\frac{n}{3^n}\\right) \\\\[5pt] &= \\lim_{n\\to\\infty}\\left(\\frac{n+1}{3\\cdot 3^n} \\cdot \\frac{3^n}{n}\\right) \\\\[5pt] &= \\lim_{n\\to\\infty}\\left(\\frac{n+1}{3n} \\right) \\\\[5pt] &= \\lim_{n\\to\\infty}\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\frac{n+1}{n}\\right) \\\\[5pt] &= \\frac{1}{3}\\lim_{n\\to\\infty}\\left(1 + \\frac{1}{n}\\right) \\\\[5pt] &=\\frac{1}{3}\\left(1 + 0\\right) \\\\[5pt] &=\\frac{1}{3}. \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we dropped the absolute value bars because the sequence is positive for all\n\n$$\nn\\geq 1\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\nL = \\dfrac{1}{3}<1\n$$\n\nwe conclude that the series is convergent by the ratio test."] [:db/cas 17592186083098 :question/worked-solution "This is an example of an **alternating series**. The $(-1)^n$ causes the signs of the successive terms to alternate between positive and negative.\n\nLet\n\n$$\na_n = \\dfrac{(-1)^n\\,n!}{3^n}\n$$\n\nIf we replace $n$ with $n+1$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} a_{n+1}= \\dfrac{(-1)^{n+1}(n+1)!}{3^{n+1}}= \\frac{(-1)^{n+1}\\,(n+1)!}{3\\cdot 3^n}. \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that we have expressions for the consecutive terms $a_n$ and $a_{n+1}$, we can work out the necessary limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} L &=\\lim_{n\\to\\infty}\\left|\\frac{a_{n+1}}{a_n} \\right| \\\\[5pt] &=\\lim_{n\\to\\infty}\\left| \\dfrac{(-1)^{n+1}\\,(n+1)!}{3\\cdot 3^n} \\div \\dfrac{(-1)^n\\,n!}{3^n}\\right| \\\\[5pt] &=\\lim_{n\\to\\infty}\\left| \\dfrac{(-1)^{n+1}\\,(n+1)!}{3\\cdot 3^n} \\cdot \\dfrac{3^n}{(-1)^n\\,n!}\\right| \\\\[5pt] &=\\lim_{n\\to\\infty}\\left|-\\dfrac{n+1}{3}\\right| \\\\[5pt] &=\\dfrac 1 3\\lim_{n\\to\\infty}\\left(n+1\\right) \\\\[5pt] &=\\infty. \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\nL=\\infty > 1\n$$\n\nwe conclude that the series is divergent by the ratio test." "This is an example of an **alternating series**. The $(-1)^n$ causes the signs of the successive terms to alternate between positive and negative.\n\nLet\n\n$$\na_n = \\dfrac{(-1)^n\\,n!}{3^n}\n$$\n\nIf we replace $n$ with $n+1$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} a_{n+1}= \\dfrac{(-1)^{n+1}(n+1)!}{3^{n+1}}= \\frac{(-1)^{n+1}\\,(n+1)!}{3\\cdot 3^n}. \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that we have expressions for the consecutive terms $a_n$ and $a_{n+1}$, we can work out the necessary limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} L &=\\lim_{n\\to\\infty}\\left|\\frac{a_{n+1}}{a_n} \\right| \\\\[5pt] &=\\lim_{n\\to\\infty}\\left| \\dfrac{(-1)^{n+1}\\,(n+1)!}{3\\cdot 3^n} \\operatorname{div}  \\dfrac{(-1)^n\\,n!}{3^n}\\right| \\\\[5pt] &=\\lim_{n\\to\\infty}\\left| \\dfrac{(-1)^{n+1}\\,(n+1)!}{3\\cdot 3^n} \\cdot \\dfrac{3^n}{(-1)^n\\,n!}\\right| \\\\[5pt] &=\\lim_{n\\to\\infty}\\left|-\\dfrac{n+1}{3}\\right| \\\\[5pt] &=\\dfrac 1 3\\lim_{n\\to\\infty}\\left(n+1\\right) \\\\[5pt] &=\\infty. \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\nL=\\infty > 1\n$$\n\nwe conclude that the series is divergent by the ratio test."] [:db/cas 17592186083099 :question/worked-solution "Let\n\n$$\na_n = \\dfrac{1}{2n+1}\n$$\n\nIf we replace $n$ with $n+1$, we get\n\n$$\na_{n+1} = \\frac{1}{2(n+1)+1}= \\frac{1}{2n+3}\n$$\n\nNow that we have expressions for the consecutive terms $a_n$ and $a_{n+1}$, we can work out the necessary limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL &= \\lim_{n \\to  \\infty} \\mid \\frac{a_{n + 1}}{a_{n}} \\mid \\\\\n&= \\lim_{n \\to  \\infty} \\mid \\frac{a_{n + 1}}{a_{n}} \\mid \\\\\n&= \\lim_{n \\to  \\infty} \\mid \\frac{1}{2n + 3} \\div \\frac{1}{2n + 1} \\mid \\\\\n&= \\lim_{n \\to  \\infty}(\\frac{1}{2n + 3} \\cdot \\frac{2n + 1}{1}) \\\\\n&= \\lim_{n \\to  \\infty}\\left(\\frac{2n + 1}{2n + 3}\\right) \\\\\n&= \\lim_{n \\to  \\infty}\\left(\\frac{2 + \\frac{1}{n}}{2 + \\frac{3}{n}}\\right) \\\\\n&= \\frac{2}{2} \\\\\n&= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $L=1$, the ratio test gives *no conclusion* about whether the series is convergent or divergent.\n\n**Note:** Although the ratio test gave no conclusion, the series\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\frac{1}{2n+1}\n$$\n\nis actually divergent." "Let\n\n$$\na_n = \\dfrac{1}{2n+1}\n$$\n\nIf we replace $n$ with $n+1$, we get\n\n$$\na_{n+1} = \\frac{1}{2(n+1)+1}= \\frac{1}{2n+3}\n$$\n\nNow that we have expressions for the consecutive terms $a_n$ and $a_{n+1}$, we can work out the necessary limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL &= \\lim_{n \\to  \\infty} \\mid \\frac{a_{n + 1}}{a_{n}} \\mid \\\\\n&= \\lim_{n \\to  \\infty} \\mid \\frac{a_{n + 1}}{a_{n}} \\mid \\\\\n&= \\lim_{n \\to  \\infty} \\mid \\frac{1}{2n + 3} \\operatorname{div}  \\frac{1}{2n + 1} \\mid \\\\\n&= \\lim_{n \\to  \\infty}(\\frac{1}{2n + 3} \\cdot \\frac{2n + 1}{1}) \\\\\n&= \\lim_{n \\to  \\infty}\\left(\\frac{2n + 1}{2n + 3}\\right) \\\\\n&= \\lim_{n \\to  \\infty}\\left(\\frac{2 + \\frac{1}{n}}{2 + \\frac{3}{n}}\\right) \\\\\n&= \\frac{2}{2} \\\\\n&= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $L=1$, the ratio test gives *no conclusion* about whether the series is convergent or divergent.\n\n**Note:** Although the ratio test gave no conclusion, the series\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\frac{1}{2n+1}\n$$\n\nis actually divergent."] [:db/cas 17592186083541 :question/worked-solution "The arc length of a curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=a$ and $x=b$ can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{csc}⁡x),$ $a=1,$ and $b=3.$\n\nFirst, we find the derivative of $f(x):$\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\cdot \\operatorname{cot}⁡x}{\\operatorname{csc}⁡x}=-\\operatorname{cot}⁡x\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${f}^{′}(x)=-\\operatorname{cot}⁡x$ into the arc length formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}\\sqrt{1+{[{f}^{′}(x)]}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}\\sqrt{1+{(-\\operatorname{cot}⁡x)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}\\sqrt{{csc}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}|\\operatorname{csc}⁡x|\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote, that we use $|\\operatorname{csc}⁡x|=\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$, which is valid due to that $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x>0$ on $[1,3].$" "The arc length of a curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=a$ and $x=b$ can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{csc}⁡x),$ $a=1,$ and $b=3.$\n\nFirst, we find the derivative of $f(x):$\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\cdot \\operatorname{cot}⁡x}{\\operatorname{csc}⁡x}=-\\operatorname{cot}⁡x\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${f}^{′}(x)=-\\operatorname{cot}⁡x$ into the arc length formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}\\sqrt{1+{[{f}^{′}(x)]}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}\\sqrt{1+{(-\\operatorname{cot}⁡x)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}\\sqrt{{\\csc }^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}|\\operatorname{csc}⁡x|\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote, that we use $|\\operatorname{csc}⁡x|=\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$, which is valid due to that $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x>0$ on $[1,3].$"] [:db/cas 17592186084434 :tutorial/content "A **complex number** is a combination of a real number and an imaginary number.\nIf we add the real number $\\color{blue}2$ and the imaginary number ${\\color{red}3\\textrm{i}}$, we get the complex number ${\\color{blue}2} +{\\color{red}3\\textrm{i}}$.\n\nThis complex number has **real part** equal to $\\color{blue}2$ and **imaginary part** equal to ${\\color{red}3}$, which we write as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nRe(2 + 3\\textrm{i}) &= 2, \\\\\nIm(2 + 3\\textrm{i}) &= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn general, we write a complex number as ${\\color{blue}a} + {\\color{red}b\\textrm{i}}$, where $\\color{blue}a$ is the real part and $\\color{red}b$ is the imaginary part." "A **complex number** is a combination of a real number and an imaginary number.\nIf we add the real number $\\color{blue}2$ and the imaginary number ${\\color{red}3\\textrm{i}}$, we get the complex number ${\\color{blue}2} +{\\color{red}3\\textrm{i}}$.\n\nThis complex number has **real part** equal to $\\color{blue}2$ and **imaginary part** equal to ${\\color{red}3}$, which we write as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\Re (2 + 3\\textrm{i}) &= 2, \\\\\n\\Im (2 + 3\\textrm{i}) &= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn general, we write a complex number as ${\\color{blue}a} + {\\color{red}b\\textrm{i}}$, where $\\color{blue}a$ is the real part and $\\color{red}b$ is the imaginary part."] [:db/cas 17592186084503 :tutorial/content "Let's use the limit comparison test to determine whether the following series converges or diverges:\n\n$$\n\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{1}{n + 2}\n$$\n\nThis looks a bit like the diverging $p$-series\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{1}{n}\n$$\n\nHowever, we cannot use the comparison test because\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{n + 2} < \\dfrac{1}{n}\n$$\n\nLet's instead try the limit comparison test. Let\n\n$$\na_n = \\dfrac{1}{n + 2}, \\qquad b_n = \\dfrac{1}{n}\n$$\n\nComputing the limit of the ratio, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\lim_{n \\to  \\infty}\\frac{a_{n}}{b_{n}} &= \\lim_{n \\to  \\infty}\\frac{1}{n + 2} \\div \\frac{1}{n} \\\\\n&= \\lim_{n \\to  \\infty}\\frac{n}{n + 2} \\\\\n&= \\lim_{n \\to  \\infty}(\\left(\\frac{n}{n}\\right))/((\\frac{n}{n} + \\frac{2}{n})) \\\\\n&= \\lim_{n \\to  \\infty}(1)/((1 + \\frac{2}{n})) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{1 + 0} \\\\\n&= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $a_n$ and $b_n$ are both positive and\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\lim_{n\\rightarrow\\infty} \\frac{a_n}{b_n} = 1\n$$\n\nis positive and finite, we can use the limit comparison test.\n\nSo, since\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty b_n\n$$\n\ndiverges, then\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty a_n\n$$\n\ndiverges as well. Therefore, the series\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{1}{n + 2}\n$$\n\ndiverges." "Let's use the limit comparison test to determine whether the following series converges or diverges:\n\n$$\n\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{1}{n + 2}\n$$\n\nThis looks a bit like the diverging $p$-series\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{1}{n}\n$$\n\nHowever, we cannot use the comparison test because\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{n + 2} < \\dfrac{1}{n}\n$$\n\nLet's instead try the limit comparison test. Let\n\n$$\na_n = \\dfrac{1}{n + 2}, \\qquad b_n = \\dfrac{1}{n}\n$$\n\nComputing the limit of the ratio, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\lim_{n \\to  \\infty}\\frac{a_{n}}{b_{n}} &= \\lim_{n \\to  \\infty}\\frac{1}{n + 2} \\operatorname{div}  \\frac{1}{n} \\\\\n&= \\lim_{n \\to  \\infty}\\frac{n}{n + 2} \\\\\n&= \\lim_{n \\to  \\infty}(\\left(\\frac{n}{n}\\right))/((\\frac{n}{n} + \\frac{2}{n})) \\\\\n&= \\lim_{n \\to  \\infty}(1)/((1 + \\frac{2}{n})) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{1 + 0} \\\\\n&= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $a_n$ and $b_n$ are both positive and\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\lim_{n\\rightarrow\\infty} \\frac{a_n}{b_n} = 1\n$$\n\nis positive and finite, we can use the limit comparison test.\n\nSo, since\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty b_n\n$$\n\ndiverges, then\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty a_n\n$$\n\ndiverges as well. Therefore, the series\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{1}{n + 2}\n$$\n\ndiverges."] [:db/cas 17592186087429 :question/worked-solution "The prime factors of a number are the primes that divide the number with no remainder.\n\nThe first few prime numbers are given below.\n\n$$\n2, \\quad 3, \\quad 5, \\quad 7, \\quad 11, \\quad \\ldots\n$$\n\nLet's see which of the given numbers are prime factors of $12{:}$\n\n- $3$ is prime. Also, $3$ is a factor of $12$ since $12 \\div 3 = 4. \\quad \\color{green} \\checkmark$\n- $4$ is **not** prime.\n\n- $5$ is prime. However, $5$ is **not** a factor of $12$ since dividing $12$ by\n$5$ gives a remainder of $2.\\quad\\color{red} \\times$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$3$ only.\"" "The prime factors of a number are the primes that divide the number with no remainder.\n\nThe first few prime numbers are given below.\n\n$$\n2, \\quad 3, \\quad 5, \\quad 7, \\quad 11, \\quad \\ldots\n$$\n\nLet's see which of the given numbers are prime factors of $12{:}$\n\n- $3$ is prime. Also, $3$ is a factor of $12$ since $12 \\operatorname{div}  3 = 4. \\quad \\color{green} \\checkmark$\n- $4$ is **not** prime.\n\n- $5$ is prime. However, $5$ is **not** a factor of $12$ since dividing $12$ by\n$5$ gives a remainder of $2.\\quad\\color{red} \\times$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$3$ only.\""] [:db/cas 17592186087477 :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the double-angle formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\cos^2 x - \\sin^2 x = \\cos 2x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x={\\dfrac{\\pi}{24}}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cos}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{24}\\right)-{sin}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{24}\\right) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{24}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{12}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, let's recall the double-angle formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\cos^2 x - \\sin^2 x = \\cos 2x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x={\\dfrac{\\pi}{24}}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{24}\\right)-{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{24}\\right) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{24}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{12}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186087478 :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the double-angle formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n2 \\cos^2 x - 1 = \\cos 2x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x={\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{cos}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)-1 & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, let's recall the double-angle formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n2 \\cos^2 x - 1 = \\cos 2x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x={\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)-1 & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186087479 :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the double-angle formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\cos^2 x - \\sin^2 x = \\cos 2x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x={22.5^\\circ}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cos}^{2}⁡({22.5}^{∘})-{sin}^{2}⁡({22.5}^{∘}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot {22.5}^{∘}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡{45}^{∘} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, let's recall the double-angle formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\cos^2 x - \\sin^2 x = \\cos 2x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x={22.5^\\circ}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\cos }^{2}⁡({22.5}^{∘})-{\\sin }^{2}⁡({22.5}^{∘}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot {22.5}^{∘}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡{45}^{∘} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186087508 :question/worked-solution "Given a $2 \\times 2$ matrix\n\n<div class=\"matrix-operands matrix-operands-stacked\">\n<div>$A =\\begin{bmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n</div>\n\nthe inverse of $A$ can be found using the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{det(A)}\\begin{bmatrix}d & -b \\\\ -c & a\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\det(A) = 1 \\cdot 1 - 0 \\cdot (-2) = 1\n$$\n\nUsing the formula, then, the inverse is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{det(A)}\\begin{bmatrix}d & -b \\\\ -c & a\\end{bmatrix}. \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -0 \\\\ -(-2) & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can verify the result as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A{A}^{-1} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={I}_{2}\\,✓ \\\\ {A}^{-1}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={I}_{2}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Given a $2 \\times 2$ matrix\n\n<div class=\"matrix-operands matrix-operands-stacked\">\n<div>$A =\\begin{bmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n</div>\n\nthe inverse of $A$ can be found using the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{\\det (A)}\\begin{bmatrix}d & -b \\\\ -c & a\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\det(A) = 1 \\cdot 1 - 0 \\cdot (-2) = 1\n$$\n\nUsing the formula, then, the inverse is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{\\det (A)}\\begin{bmatrix}d & -b \\\\ -c & a\\end{bmatrix}. \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -0 \\\\ -(-2) & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can verify the result as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A{A}^{-1} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={I}_{2}\\,✓ \\\\ {A}^{-1}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={I}_{2}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186087510 :question/worked-solution "The matrix $A$ is singular if and only if $\\det(A)=0.$ Calculating the determinant, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =(1-k)(1-k)-4 \\\\  & ={k}^{2}-2k-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we set the determinant equal to $0$ and solve for $k\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{k}^{2}-2k-3 & =0 \\\\ (k-3)(k+1) & =0 \\\\ k & =3,-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the values of $k$ are $k=3$ and $k=-1.$" "The matrix $A$ is singular if and only if $\\det(A)=0.$ Calculating the determinant, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\det (A) & =(1-k)(1-k)-4 \\\\  & ={k}^{2}-2k-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we set the determinant equal to $0$ and solve for $k\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{k}^{2}-2k-3 & =0 \\\\ (k-3)(k+1) & =0 \\\\ k & =3,-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the values of $k$ are $k=3$ and $k=-1.$"] [:db/cas 17592186087557 :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the hyperbola, we need to eliminate the parameter $t.$ We can do this using the secant-tangent identity.\n\n- Isolating the $\\sec t$ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =6\\operatorname{sec}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{x}{6} & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡t \\\\ {\\left(\\frac{x}{6}\\right)}^{2} & ={(\\operatorname{sec}⁡t)}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{6}^{2}} & ={sec}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{36} & ={sec}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Isolating the $\\tan{t}$ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =4\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{y}{4\\sqrt{2}} & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡t \\\\ {\\left(\\frac{y}{4\\sqrt{2}}\\right)}^{2} & ={(\\operatorname{tan}⁡t)}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{{(4\\sqrt{2})}^{2}} & ={tan}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{32} & ={tan}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the two equations into the identity $\\tan^2{t} + 1 = \\sec^2{t}$ and rearranging, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{tan}^{2}⁡t+1 & ={sec}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{32}+1 & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{36} \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{36}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{32} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "To find the Cartesian equation of the hyperbola, we need to eliminate the parameter $t.$ We can do this using the secant-tangent identity.\n\n- Isolating the $\\sec t$ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =6\\operatorname{sec}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{x}{6} & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡t \\\\ {\\left(\\frac{x}{6}\\right)}^{2} & ={(\\operatorname{sec}⁡t)}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{6}^{2}} & ={\\sec }^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{36} & ={\\sec }^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Isolating the $\\tan{t}$ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =4\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{y}{4\\sqrt{2}} & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡t \\\\ {\\left(\\frac{y}{4\\sqrt{2}}\\right)}^{2} & ={(\\operatorname{tan}⁡t)}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{{(4\\sqrt{2})}^{2}} & ={\\tan }^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{32} & ={\\tan }^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the two equations into the identity $\\tan^2{t} + 1 = \\sec^2{t}$ and rearranging, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\tan }^{2}⁡t+1 & ={\\sec }^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{32}+1 & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{36} \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{36}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{32} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186087616 :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the circle, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta.$ We can do this using the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n\\cos^2 \\theta + \\sin^2 \\theta = 1\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\cos\\theta$ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-2}{3} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{{3}^{2}} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{9} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\sin\\theta$ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y+4}{3} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(y+4)^{2}}{{3}^{2}} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(y+4)^{2}}{9} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these values into the Pythagorean identity, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{9}+\\frac{(y+4)^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ (x-2)^{2}+(y+4)^{2} & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the Cartesian equation is $(x-2)^2 + (y+4)^2 = 9.$" "To find the Cartesian equation of the circle, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta.$ We can do this using the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n\\cos^2 \\theta + \\sin^2 \\theta = 1\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\cos\\theta$ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-2}{3} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{{3}^{2}} & ={\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{9} & ={\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\sin\\theta$ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y+4}{3} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(y+4)^{2}}{{3}^{2}} & ={\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(y+4)^{2}}{9} & ={\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these values into the Pythagorean identity, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta +{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{9}+\\frac{(y+4)^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ (x-2)^{2}+(y+4)^{2} & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the Cartesian equation is $(x-2)^2 + (y+4)^2 = 9.$"] [:db/cas 17592186087989 :question/worked-solution "- For the matrix $A$, we have\n\n$$\ndet(A)=0\\cdot 0-1\\cdot 1=-1.\n$$\n\nBecause $det(A)\\ne 0,$ we conclude that $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The matrix $B$ is a $3\\times 1$ matrix. So, it is not invertible. Since the matrix is not square, we can't say that it's singular.\n\n- For the matrix $C$, we have\n\n$$\ndet(C)=1\\cdot 0-0\\cdot 1=0.\n$$\n\nBecause $det(C)=0,$ we conclude that $C$ is not invertible. Since $C$ is a square matrix that is not invertible, we also say that $C$ is singular.\n\nTherefore, only $C$ is singular." "- For the matrix $A$, we have\n\n$$\n\\det (A)=0\\cdot 0-1\\cdot 1=-1.\n$$\n\nBecause $\\det (A)\\ne 0,$ we conclude that $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The matrix $B$ is a $3\\times 1$ matrix. So, it is not invertible. Since the matrix is not square, we can't say that it's singular.\n\n- For the matrix $C$, we have\n\n$$\n\\det (C)=1\\cdot 0-0\\cdot 1=0.\n$$\n\nBecause $\\det (C)=0,$ we conclude that $C$ is not invertible. Since $C$ is a square matrix that is not invertible, we also say that $C$ is singular.\n\nTherefore, only $C$ is singular."] [:db/cas 17592186087991 :question/worked-solution "The matrix $A$ is singular if and only if $det(A)=0.$ Calculating the determinant, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =k\\cdot 1-k\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =k-2k \\\\  & =-k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we set the determinant equal to $0$ and solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-k & =0 \\\\ k & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the value of $k$ is $k=0.$" "The matrix $A$ is singular if and only if $\\det (A)=0.$ Calculating the determinant, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\det (A) & =k\\cdot 1-k\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =k-2k \\\\  & =-k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we set the determinant equal to $0$ and solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-k & =0 \\\\ k & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the value of $k$ is $k=0.$"] [:db/cas 17592186087993 :question/worked-solution "- The matrix $A$ is a $2\\times 3$ matrix. So, it is not invertible.\n\n- For the matrix $B$, we have\n\n$$\ndet(B)=6\\cdot 4-8\\cdot 3=0.\n$$\n\nBecause $det(B)=0,$ we conclude that $B$ is not invertible.\n\n- For the matrix $C$, we have\n\n$$\ndet(C)=(-1)\\cdot 5-1\\cdot 5=-10.\n$$\n\nBecause $det(C)\\ne 0,$ we conclude that $C$ is invertible.\n\nTherefore, only $A$ and $B$ do not have an inverse." "- The matrix $A$ is a $2\\times 3$ matrix. So, it is not invertible.\n\n- For the matrix $B$, we have\n\n$$\n\\det (B)=6\\cdot 4-8\\cdot 3=0.\n$$\n\nBecause $\\det (B)=0,$ we conclude that $B$ is not invertible.\n\n- For the matrix $C$, we have\n\n$$\n\\det (C)=(-1)\\cdot 5-1\\cdot 5=-10.\n$$\n\nBecause $\\det (C)\\ne 0,$ we conclude that $C$ is invertible.\n\nTherefore, only $A$ and $B$ do not have an inverse."] [:db/cas 17592186088094 :question/problem "Express $$\n\\displaystyle {cos}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\right)-{sin}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\right)\n$$ as a single trigonometric ratio." "Express $$\n\\displaystyle {\\cos }^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\right)-{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\right)\n$$ as a single trigonometric ratio."] [:db/cas 17592186088094 :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the double-angle formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n{cos}^{2}⁡x-{sin}^{2}⁡x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cos}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\right)-{sin}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\right) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{3\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, let's recall the double-angle formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n{\\cos }^{2}⁡x-{\\sin }^{2}⁡x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\right)-{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\right) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{3\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186088095 :question/problem "Express $2{cos}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{3}\\right)-1$ as a single trigonometric ratio." "Express $2{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{3}\\right)-1$ as a single trigonometric ratio."] [:db/cas 17592186088095 :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the double-angle formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n2{cos}^{2}⁡x-1=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\frac{5\\pi }{3}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{cos}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{3}\\right)-1 & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{5\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{10\\pi }{3}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, let's recall the double-angle formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n2{\\cos }^{2}⁡x-1=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\frac{5\\pi }{3}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{3}\\right)-1 & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{5\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{10\\pi }{3}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186088096 :question/problem "Find the exact value of $$\n\\displaystyle {cos}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{12}\\right)-{sin}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{12}\\right).\n$$" "Find the exact value of $$\n\\displaystyle {\\cos }^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{12}\\right)-{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{12}\\right).\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186088096 :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the double-angle formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n{cos}^{2}⁡x-{sin}^{2}⁡x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\frac{\\pi }{12}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cos}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{12}\\right)-{sin}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{12}\\right) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{12}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, let's recall the double-angle formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n{\\cos }^{2}⁡x-{\\sin }^{2}⁡x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\frac{\\pi }{12}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{12}\\right)-{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{12}\\right) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{12}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186088111 :question/problem "Find the exact value of $1-2{sin}^{2}⁡({67.5}^{∘}).$" "Find the exact value of $1-2{\\sin }^{2}⁡({67.5}^{∘}).$"] [:db/cas 17592186088111 :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the double-angle formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n1-2{sin}^{2}⁡x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x={67.5}^{∘}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-2{sin}^{2}⁡({67.5}^{∘}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot {67.5}^{∘}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡{135}^{∘} \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡{45}^{∘} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, let's recall the double-angle formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n1-2{\\sin }^{2}⁡x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x={67.5}^{∘}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-2{\\sin }^{2}⁡({67.5}^{∘}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot {67.5}^{∘}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡{135}^{∘} \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡{45}^{∘} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186088264 :question/worked-solution "First, we take $ln$ of both sides of the equation and solve for $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{1-2x} & =5 \\\\ ln⁡({e}^{1-2x}) & =ln⁡(5) \\\\ 1-2x & =ln⁡(5) \\\\ -2x & =ln⁡(5)-1 \\\\ x & =\\frac{1-ln⁡(5)}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the above expression using a calculator gives\n\n$$\nx≈-0.305\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." "First, we take $ln$ of both sides of the equation and solve for $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{1-2x} & =5 \\\\ \\ln ⁡({e}^{1-2x}) & =\\ln ⁡(5) \\\\ 1-2x & =\\ln ⁡(5) \\\\ -2x & =\\ln ⁡(5)-1 \\\\ x & =\\frac{1-\\ln ⁡(5)}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the above expression using a calculator gives\n\n$$\nx≈-0.305\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."] [:db/cas 17592186088339 :question/problem "Express $5-10{sin}^{2}⁡{20}^{∘}$ as a single trigonometric ratio." "Express $5-10{\\sin }^{2}⁡{20}^{∘}$ as a single trigonometric ratio."] [:db/cas 17592186088339 :question/worked-solution "Our expression can be factored as follows:\n\n$$\n5(1-2{sin}^{2}⁡{20}^{∘})\n$$\n\nNow, let's recall the double-angle formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n1-2{sin}^{2}⁡x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x={20}^{∘}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-2{sin}^{2}⁡{20}^{∘} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot {20}^{∘})=\\operatorname{cos}⁡{40}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n5(1-2{sin}^{2}⁡{20}^{∘})=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡{40}^{∘}.\n$$" "Our expression can be factored as follows:\n\n$$\n5(1-2{\\sin }^{2}⁡{20}^{∘})\n$$\n\nNow, let's recall the double-angle formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n1-2{\\sin }^{2}⁡x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x={20}^{∘}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-2{\\sin }^{2}⁡{20}^{∘} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot {20}^{∘})=\\operatorname{cos}⁡{40}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n5(1-2{\\sin }^{2}⁡{20}^{∘})=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡{40}^{∘}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186088376 :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the hyperbola, we need to eliminate the parameter $t.$ We can do this using the secant-tangent identity.\n\n- Isolating the $\\operatorname{sec}⁡t$ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sec}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{x}{\\sqrt{5}} & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{(\\sqrt{5})^{2}} & ={sec}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{5} & ={sec}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Isolating the $\\operatorname{tan}⁡t$ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =3\\operatorname{tan}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{y}{3} & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{{3}^{2}} & ={tan}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{9} & ={tan}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the two equations into the identity ${tan}^{2}⁡t+1={sec}^{2}⁡t$ and rearranging, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{tan}^{2}⁡t+1 & ={sec}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}+1 & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{5} \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{5}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "To find the Cartesian equation of the hyperbola, we need to eliminate the parameter $t.$ We can do this using the secant-tangent identity.\n\n- Isolating the $\\operatorname{sec}⁡t$ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sec}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{x}{\\sqrt{5}} & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{(\\sqrt{5})^{2}} & ={\\sec }^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{5} & ={\\sec }^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Isolating the $\\operatorname{tan}⁡t$ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =3\\operatorname{tan}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{y}{3} & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{{3}^{2}} & ={\\tan }^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{9} & ={\\tan }^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the two equations into the identity ${\\tan }^{2}⁡t+1={\\sec }^{2}⁡t$ and rearranging, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\tan }^{2}⁡t+1 & ={\\sec }^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}+1 & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{5} \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{5}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186088434 :question/worked-solution "Let's plot $z$ in the complex plane.\n\n![](images/d0/d096d76118bc06b2d5c79d5017c0178d96fc84bff6f97d563b7b872d26e75d20.png)\n\nThe complex number $z$ lies in the fourth quadrant of the complex plane. Therefore $\\frac{3\\pi }{2}<arg⁡(z)<2\\pi .$ \n\nIf $\\alpha $ is the angle that $z$ makes with the positive real axis, then\n\n$$\n\\alpha =arctan⁡\\left(\\frac{5}{7}\\right)≈0.620\\,25.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta =arg⁡(z)=2\\pi -0.620\\,25≈5.66\n$$\n\nto two decimal places." "Let's plot $z$ in the complex plane.\n\n![](images/d0/d096d76118bc06b2d5c79d5017c0178d96fc84bff6f97d563b7b872d26e75d20.png)\n\nThe complex number $z$ lies in the fourth quadrant of the complex plane. Therefore $\\frac{3\\pi }{2}<\\arg ⁡(z)<2\\pi .$ \n\nIf $\\alpha $ is the angle that $z$ makes with the positive real axis, then\n\n$$\n\\alpha =\\arctan ⁡\\left(\\frac{5}{7}\\right)≈0.620\\,25.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\arg ⁡(z)=2\\pi -0.620\\,25≈5.66\n$$\n\nto two decimal places."] [:db/cas 17592186088448 :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the hyperbola, we need to eliminate the parameter $t.$ We can do this using the secant-tangent identity.\n\n- Isolating the $\\operatorname{sec}⁡t$ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{x}{\\sqrt{2}} & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{(\\sqrt{2})^{2}} & ={sec}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{2} & ={sec}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Isolating the $\\operatorname{tan}⁡t$ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\sqrt{6}\\operatorname{tan}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{y}{\\sqrt{6}} & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{(\\sqrt{6})^{2}} & ={tan}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{6} & ={tan}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the two equations into the identity ${tan}^{2}⁡t+1={sec}^{2}⁡t$ and rearranging, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{tan}^{2}⁡t+1 & ={sec}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{6}+1 & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{6} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "To find the Cartesian equation of the hyperbola, we need to eliminate the parameter $t.$ We can do this using the secant-tangent identity.\n\n- Isolating the $\\operatorname{sec}⁡t$ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{x}{\\sqrt{2}} & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{(\\sqrt{2})^{2}} & ={\\sec }^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{2} & ={\\sec }^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Isolating the $\\operatorname{tan}⁡t$ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\sqrt{6}\\operatorname{tan}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{y}{\\sqrt{6}} & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{(\\sqrt{6})^{2}} & ={\\tan }^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{6} & ={\\tan }^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the two equations into the identity ${\\tan }^{2}⁡t+1={\\sec }^{2}⁡t$ and rearranging, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\tan }^{2}⁡t+1 & ={\\sec }^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{6}+1 & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{6} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186088881 :tutorial/content "It's possible to write the double angle formula for cosine in two alternative forms.\n\nFrom the Pythagorean identity $\\sin^2{x}+\\cos^2{x}=1,$ we obtain $\\cos^2{x}=1-\\sin^2{x}.$\nSubstituting this into the double-angle formula for cosine, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x & ={cos}^{2}⁡x-{sin}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & =(1-{sin}^{2}⁡x)-{sin}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & =1-2{sin}^{2}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives an alternative form of the double-angle formula for cosine. We'll restate it below:\n\n$$\n\\cos{2x} =1 - 2\\sin^2{x}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, from the same Pythagorean identity, we obtain that $\\sin^2{x}=1-\\cos^2{x}.$ Substituting into the double-angle formula for cosine, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x & ={cos}^{2}⁡x-{sin}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡x-(1-{cos}^{2}⁡x) \\\\  & =2{cos}^{2}⁡x-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is another alternative form of the double-angle formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\cos{2x} =2\\cos^2{x}-1\n$$" "It's possible to write the double angle formula for cosine in two alternative forms.\n\nFrom the Pythagorean identity $\\sin^2{x}+\\cos^2{x}=1,$ we obtain $\\cos^2{x}=1-\\sin^2{x}.$\nSubstituting this into the double-angle formula for cosine, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x & ={\\cos }^{2}⁡x-{\\sin }^{2}⁡x \\\\  & =(1-{\\sin }^{2}⁡x)-{\\sin }^{2}⁡x \\\\  & =1-2{\\sin }^{2}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives an alternative form of the double-angle formula for cosine. We'll restate it below:\n\n$$\n\\cos{2x} =1 - 2\\sin^2{x}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, from the same Pythagorean identity, we obtain that $\\sin^2{x}=1-\\cos^2{x}.$ Substituting into the double-angle formula for cosine, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x & ={\\cos }^{2}⁡x-{\\sin }^{2}⁡x \\\\  & ={\\cos }^{2}⁡x-(1-{\\cos }^{2}⁡x) \\\\  & =2{\\cos }^{2}⁡x-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is another alternative form of the double-angle formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\cos{2x} =2\\cos^2{x}-1\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186088895 :tutorial/content "Given a $2 \\times 2$ matrix\n\n<div class=\"matrix-operands matrix-operands-stacked\">\n<div>$A =\\begin{bmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n</div>\n\nthe **inverse** of $A$ can be found using the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{det(A)}\\begin{bmatrix}d & -b \\\\ -c & a\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Given a $2 \\times 2$ matrix\n\n<div class=\"matrix-operands matrix-operands-stacked\">\n<div>$A =\\begin{bmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n</div>\n\nthe **inverse** of $A$ can be found using the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{\\det (A)}\\begin{bmatrix}d & -b \\\\ -c & a\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186088916 :tutorial/content "When we add a complex number and its conjugate, the result is always twice the real part of the complex number. That is to say,\n\n$$\nz + \\overline{z} = 2\\operatorname{Re}(z),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\operatorname{Re}(z)$ is the real part of $z.$\n\nFor example, suppose we have a complex number $z = 3+4\\textrm{i}.$ To obtain the conjugate, we just flip the sign of the imaginary part and get $\\overline{z} = 3 - 4 \\textrm{i}.$\n\nNow, adding $z$ and its conjugate $\\overline{z},$ we find that the real part $(3)$ is doubled while the imaginary part $(4)$ is canceled out:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z+\\overset{―}{z} & =(3+4\\text{i})+(3-4\\text{i}) \\\\  & =(3+3)+(4\\text{i}-4\\text{i}) \\\\  & =6+0\\text{i} \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis result holds in general. For any complex number $z=x+\\textrm{i}y,$ the conjugate is $\\overline{z} = x-\\textrm{i}y,$ and when we add, we find that the real part $(x)$ is doubled while the imaginary part $(y)$ is canceled out:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z+\\overset{―}{z} & =(x+\\text{i}y)+(x-\\text{i}y) \\\\  & =(x+x)+(\\text{i}y-\\text{i}y) \\\\  & =2x+\\text{i}0 \\\\  & =2x \\\\  & =2Re(z).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "When we add a complex number and its conjugate, the result is always twice the real part of the complex number. That is to say,\n\n$$\nz + \\overline{z} = 2\\operatorname{Re}(z),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\operatorname{Re}(z)$ is the real part of $z.$\n\nFor example, suppose we have a complex number $z = 3+4\\textrm{i}.$ To obtain the conjugate, we just flip the sign of the imaginary part and get $\\overline{z} = 3 - 4 \\textrm{i}.$\n\nNow, adding $z$ and its conjugate $\\overline{z},$ we find that the real part $(3)$ is doubled while the imaginary part $(4)$ is canceled out:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z+\\overset{―}{z} & =(3+4\\text{i})+(3-4\\text{i}) \\\\  & =(3+3)+(4\\text{i}-4\\text{i}) \\\\  & =6+0\\text{i} \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis result holds in general. For any complex number $z=x+\\textrm{i}y,$ the conjugate is $\\overline{z} = x-\\textrm{i}y,$ and when we add, we find that the real part $(x)$ is doubled while the imaginary part $(y)$ is canceled out:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z+\\overset{―}{z} & =(x+\\text{i}y)+(x-\\text{i}y) \\\\  & =(x+x)+(\\text{i}y-\\text{i}y) \\\\  & =2x+\\text{i}0 \\\\  & =2x \\\\  & =2\\Re (z).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186088917 :tutorial/content "When we multiply a complex number and its conjugate, the result is always the sum of squares of the real part and the imaginary part. That is to say,\n\n$$\nz\\overline{z} = x^2 + y^2 = (\\operatorname{Re}(z))^2 + (\\operatorname{Im}(z))^2.\n$$\n\nFor example, suppose we have a complex number $z=3+4\\textrm{i}.$  To obtain the conjugate, we just flip the sign of the imaginary part and get $\\overline{z} = 3 - 4 \\textrm{i}.$\n\nNow, multiplying $z$ and its conjugate $\\overline{z},$ we find that the result is the sum of squares of the real part $(3^2=9)$ and the imaginary part $(4^2=16)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z\\overset{―}{z} & =(3+4\\text{i})(3-4\\text{i}) \\\\  & ={3}^{2}-3(4\\text{i})+3(4\\text{i})-{4}^{2}{\\text{i}}^{2} \\\\  & =9-12\\text{i}+12\\text{i}-16(-1) \\\\  & =9+16 \\\\  & =25\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis result holds in general. For any complex number $z=x+\\textrm{i}y,$ the conjugate is $\\overline{z} = x-\\textrm{i}y,$ and when we multiply, we find that the result is the sum of squares of the real part $(x^2)$ and the imaginary part $(y^2)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z\\overset{―}{z} & =(x+\\text{i}y)(x-\\text{i}y) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-\\text{i}xy+\\text{i}xy-{\\text{i}}^{2}{y}^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-(-1){y}^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2} \\\\  & =(Re(z))^{2}+(Im(z))^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "When we multiply a complex number and its conjugate, the result is always the sum of squares of the real part and the imaginary part. That is to say,\n\n$$\nz\\overline{z} = x^2 + y^2 = (\\operatorname{Re}(z))^2 + (\\operatorname{Im}(z))^2.\n$$\n\nFor example, suppose we have a complex number $z=3+4\\textrm{i}.$  To obtain the conjugate, we just flip the sign of the imaginary part and get $\\overline{z} = 3 - 4 \\textrm{i}.$\n\nNow, multiplying $z$ and its conjugate $\\overline{z},$ we find that the result is the sum of squares of the real part $(3^2=9)$ and the imaginary part $(4^2=16)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z\\overset{―}{z} & =(3+4\\text{i})(3-4\\text{i}) \\\\  & ={3}^{2}-3(4\\text{i})+3(4\\text{i})-{4}^{2}{\\text{i}}^{2} \\\\  & =9-12\\text{i}+12\\text{i}-16(-1) \\\\  & =9+16 \\\\  & =25\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis result holds in general. For any complex number $z=x+\\textrm{i}y,$ the conjugate is $\\overline{z} = x-\\textrm{i}y,$ and when we multiply, we find that the result is the sum of squares of the real part $(x^2)$ and the imaginary part $(y^2)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z\\overset{―}{z} & =(x+\\text{i}y)(x-\\text{i}y) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-\\text{i}xy+\\text{i}xy-{\\text{i}}^{2}{y}^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-(-1){y}^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2} \\\\  & =(\\Re (z))^{2}+(\\Im (z))^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186088973 :tutorial/content "We've been told that the parametric equations of a horizontal hyperbola centered at the origin are\n\n$$\nx=a\\sec{t},\\quad y=b\\tan{t}\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are positive constants. Can we be sure that these equations do indeed describe a hyperbola?\n\nFirst, let's make the trigonometric functions the subject of each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =a\\operatorname{sec}⁡t & ⟹ &  & \\operatorname{sec}⁡t & =\\frac{x}{a}, \\\\ y & =b\\operatorname{tan}⁡t & ⟹ &  & \\operatorname{tan}⁡t & =\\frac{y}{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we recall the secant-tangent identity:\n\n$$\n\\tan^2{t} + 1 = \\sec^2{t}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the secant-tangent identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{tan}^{2}⁡t+1 & ={sec}^{2}⁡t \\\\ {\\left(\\frac{y}{b}\\right)}^{2}+1 & ={\\left(\\frac{x}{a}\\right)}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}+1 & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is the Cartesian equation of a horizontal hyperbola centered at the origin. Therefore, the parametric equations do indeed describe a horizontal hyperbola." "We've been told that the parametric equations of a horizontal hyperbola centered at the origin are\n\n$$\nx=a\\sec{t},\\quad y=b\\tan{t}\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are positive constants. Can we be sure that these equations do indeed describe a hyperbola?\n\nFirst, let's make the trigonometric functions the subject of each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =a\\operatorname{sec}⁡t & ⟹ &  & \\operatorname{sec}⁡t & =\\frac{x}{a}, \\\\ y & =b\\operatorname{tan}⁡t & ⟹ &  & \\operatorname{tan}⁡t & =\\frac{y}{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we recall the secant-tangent identity:\n\n$$\n\\tan^2{t} + 1 = \\sec^2{t}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the secant-tangent identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\tan }^{2}⁡t+1 & ={\\sec }^{2}⁡t \\\\ {\\left(\\frac{y}{b}\\right)}^{2}+1 & ={\\left(\\frac{x}{a}\\right)}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}+1 & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is the Cartesian equation of a horizontal hyperbola centered at the origin. Therefore, the parametric equations do indeed describe a horizontal hyperbola."] [:db/cas 17592186088988 :tutorial/content "The first few prime numbers are\n\n$$\n2,\\quad 3, \\quad 5, \\quad 7, \\quad 11, \\quad 13, \\quad 17, \\quad 19, \\quad 23, \\quad 29, \\quad \\ldots\\,.\n$$\n\nRemember that $1$ is *not* a prime number.\n\nA number's **prime factors** are the prime numbers that divide the number with no remainder. \n\nLet's list the prime factors of $45{:}$\n\n- $2$ is *not* a prime factor of $45$ since dividing $45$ by $2$ gives a remainder of $1. \\quad{\\color{red}{\\times}}$\n\n- $3$ *is* a prime factor of $45$ since $45 \\div 3 = 15.\\quad{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$\n\n- $5$ *is* a prime factor of $45$ since $45 \\div 5 = 9.\\quad{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$\n\n- $7$ is *not* a prime factor of $45$ since dividing $45$ by $7$ gives a remainder of $3. \\quad{\\color{red}{\\times}}$\n\nThere are no other prime numbers that divide $45.$ Therefore, the prime factors of $45$ are $3$ and $5.$\n\nWe often wish to find a number's prime factors. We'll discuss this more in a future lesson." "The first few prime numbers are\n\n$$\n2,\\quad 3, \\quad 5, \\quad 7, \\quad 11, \\quad 13, \\quad 17, \\quad 19, \\quad 23, \\quad 29, \\quad \\ldots\\,.\n$$\n\nRemember that $1$ is *not* a prime number.\n\nA number's **prime factors** are the prime numbers that divide the number with no remainder. \n\nLet's list the prime factors of $45{:}$\n\n- $2$ is *not* a prime factor of $45$ since dividing $45$ by $2$ gives a remainder of $1. \\quad{\\color{red}{\\times}}$\n\n- $3$ *is* a prime factor of $45$ since $45 \\operatorname{div}  3 = 15.\\quad{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$\n\n- $5$ *is* a prime factor of $45$ since $45 \\operatorname{div}  5 = 9.\\quad{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$\n\n- $7$ is *not* a prime factor of $45$ since dividing $45$ by $7$ gives a remainder of $3. \\quad{\\color{red}{\\times}}$\n\nThere are no other prime numbers that divide $45.$ Therefore, the prime factors of $45$ are $3$ and $5.$\n\nWe often wish to find a number's prime factors. We'll discuss this more in a future lesson."] [:db/cas 17592186091934 :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $\\mathbf{X} = [X_1, X_2]^T$ is a bivariate normal vector with mean vector $\\boldsymbol{\\mu}$ and covariance matrix $\\Sigma,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu =\\begin{pmatrix}{\\mu }_{1} \\\\ {\\mu }_{2}\\end{pmatrix},\\,Σ=\\begin{bmatrix}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2} & {\\sigma }_{12} \\\\ {\\sigma }_{21} & {\\sigma }_{2}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2} & \\rho {\\sigma }_{1}{\\sigma }_{2} \\\\ \\rho {\\sigma }_{1}{\\sigma }_{2} & {\\sigma }_{2}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\sigma^2_i = \\textrm{Var}[X_i],$ $\\sigma_{12} = \\textrm{Cov}[X_1,X_2],$ and $\\rho$ is the correlation, then the joint probability density function of $\\mathbf X$ is\n\n$$\nf(\\mathbf x) = \\dfrac{1}{2\\pi\\sqrt{|\\Sigma|}} \\mathrm{exp}\\left( -\\dfrac 12(\\mathbf x - \\boldsymbol \\mu)^T \\,\\Sigma^{-1} \\,(\\mathbf x - \\boldsymbol \\mu)\\right)\n$$\n\nand $|\\Sigma|$ denotes the determinant of $\\Sigma.$\n\nUsing the given values of $\\sigma^2_1, \\sigma^2_2$, and $\\rho,$ our covariance matrix is\n\n$$\n\\Sigma \n= \n\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}9 & \\frac{1}{3} \\cdot  \\sqrt{9} \\cdot  \\sqrt{1} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} \\cdot  \\sqrt{9} \\cdot  \\sqrt{1} & 1\\end{array} \\right]}\n\n\n=\n\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}9 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n|\\Sigma| = 9\\cdot 1 - 1\\cdot 1 = 8.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this value into our expression for the joint PDF, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{1}{2\\pi \\sqrt{8}}exp(-\\frac{1}{2}(x-\\mu )^{T}\\,{Σ}^{-1}\\,(x-\\mu )) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4\\pi \\sqrt{2}}exp(-\\frac{1}{2}(x-\\mu )^{T}\\,{Σ}^{-1}\\,(x-\\mu )).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $k=4\\pi\\sqrt 2.$" "Recall that if $\\mathbf{X} = [X_1, X_2]^T$ is a bivariate normal vector with mean vector $\\boldsymbol{\\mu}$ and covariance matrix $\\Sigma,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu =\\begin{pmatrix}{\\mu }_{1} \\\\ {\\mu }_{2}\\end{pmatrix},\\,Σ=\\begin{bmatrix}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2} & {\\sigma }_{12} \\\\ {\\sigma }_{21} & {\\sigma }_{2}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2} & \\rho {\\sigma }_{1}{\\sigma }_{2} \\\\ \\rho {\\sigma }_{1}{\\sigma }_{2} & {\\sigma }_{2}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\sigma^2_i = \\textrm{Var}[X_i],$ $\\sigma_{12} = \\textrm{Cov}[X_1,X_2],$ and $\\rho$ is the correlation, then the joint probability density function of $\\mathbf X$ is\n\n$$\nf(\\mathbf x) = \\dfrac{1}{2\\pi\\sqrt{|\\Sigma|}} \\mathrm{exp}\\left( -\\dfrac 12(\\mathbf x - \\boldsymbol \\mu)^T \\,\\Sigma^{-1} \\,(\\mathbf x - \\boldsymbol \\mu)\\right)\n$$\n\nand $|\\Sigma|$ denotes the determinant of $\\Sigma.$\n\nUsing the given values of $\\sigma^2_1, \\sigma^2_2$, and $\\rho,$ our covariance matrix is\n\n$$\n\\Sigma \n= \n\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}9 & \\frac{1}{3} \\cdot  \\sqrt{9} \\cdot  \\sqrt{1} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} \\cdot  \\sqrt{9} \\cdot  \\sqrt{1} & 1\\end{array} \\right]}\n\n\n=\n\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}9 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n|\\Sigma| = 9\\cdot 1 - 1\\cdot 1 = 8.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this value into our expression for the joint PDF, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{1}{2\\pi \\sqrt{8}}\\exp (-\\frac{1}{2}(x-\\mu )^{T}\\,{Σ}^{-1}\\,(x-\\mu )) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4\\pi \\sqrt{2}}\\exp (-\\frac{1}{2}(x-\\mu )^{T}\\,{Σ}^{-1}\\,(x-\\mu )).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $k=4\\pi\\sqrt 2.$"] [:db/cas 17592186091935 :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $\\mathbf{X} = [X_1, X_2]^T$ is a bivariate normal random vector with mean vector $\\boldsymbol{\\mu}$ and covariance matrix $\\Sigma,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu =\\begin{bmatrix}{\\mu }_{1} \\\\ {\\mu }_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,Σ=\\begin{bmatrix}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2} & {\\sigma }_{12} \\\\ {\\sigma }_{21} & {\\sigma }_{2}^{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthen the random variable $Y,$ defined as\n\n$$\nY= aX_1 + bX_2\n$$\n\nis a normally distributed random variable for all values of $a$ and $b,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}E[Y] & =a{\\mu }_{1}+b{\\mu }_{2}, \\\\ Var[Y] & ={a}^{2}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}+{b}^{2}{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}+2ab{\\sigma }_{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $\\mu_1 = 2$ and $\\mu_2 = -1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y] & =\\text{E}[3{X}_{1}+{X}_{2}] \\\\  & =3\\text{E}[{X}_{1}]+\\text{E}[{X}_{2}] \\\\  & =3\\cdot {\\mu }_{1}+{\\mu }_{2} \\\\  & =3\\cdot 2+(-1) \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, we have $\\sigma_1^2 = 4, \\sigma_2^2 = 7,$ and $\\sigma_{12} = \\sigma_{21} = -3.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Var[Y] & =Var[3{X}_{1}+{X}_{2}] \\\\  & ={3}^{2}\\cdot 4+{1}^{2}\\cdot 7+2\\cdot 3\\cdot 1\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =36+7-18 \\\\  & =25.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nY \\sim N(5,25).\n$$" "Recall that if $\\mathbf{X} = [X_1, X_2]^T$ is a bivariate normal random vector with mean vector $\\boldsymbol{\\mu}$ and covariance matrix $\\Sigma,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu =\\begin{bmatrix}{\\mu }_{1} \\\\ {\\mu }_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,Σ=\\begin{bmatrix}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2} & {\\sigma }_{12} \\\\ {\\sigma }_{21} & {\\sigma }_{2}^{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthen the random variable $Y,$ defined as\n\n$$\nY= aX_1 + bX_2\n$$\n\nis a normally distributed random variable for all values of $a$ and $b,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}E[Y] & =a{\\mu }_{1}+b{\\mu }_{2}, \\\\ \\operatorname{Var} [Y] & ={a}^{2}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}+{b}^{2}{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}+2ab{\\sigma }_{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $\\mu_1 = 2$ and $\\mu_2 = -1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y] & =\\text{E}[3{X}_{1}+{X}_{2}] \\\\  & =3\\text{E}[{X}_{1}]+\\text{E}[{X}_{2}] \\\\  & =3\\cdot {\\mu }_{1}+{\\mu }_{2} \\\\  & =3\\cdot 2+(-1) \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, we have $\\sigma_1^2 = 4, \\sigma_2^2 = 7,$ and $\\sigma_{12} = \\sigma_{21} = -3.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{Var} [Y] & =\\operatorname{Var} [3{X}_{1}+{X}_{2}] \\\\  & ={3}^{2}\\cdot 4+{1}^{2}\\cdot 7+2\\cdot 3\\cdot 1\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =36+7-18 \\\\  & =25.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nY \\sim N(5,25).\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186091936 :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $\\mathbf{X} =  [X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_k]^T$ is a multivariate normal random vector with mean vector $\\boldsymbol{\\mu}$ and covariance matrix $\\Sigma,$ then the random variable $Y,$ defined as\n\n$$\nY= \\mathbf c^T \\mathbf X = \\sum_{i=1}^k c_i X_i\n$$\n\nis a normally distributed random variable, where $\\mathbf c$ is any $k\\times 1$ column vector, and  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}E[Y] & ={c}^{T}\\mu , \\\\ Var[Y] & ={c}^{T}Σc.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, since we wish to know the distribution of $Y = X_1 -2X_2 +4 X_3,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\mathbf c = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1 \\\\ - 2 \\\\ 4\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, for the mean of $Y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y] & ={c}^{T}\\mu  \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =1-4+4 \\\\  & =1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand for the variance of $Y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[Y] & ={c}^{T}Σc \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}12 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 14\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =12-6+56 \\\\  & =62.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nY\\sim N(1,62).\n$$" "Recall that if $\\mathbf{X} =  [X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_k]^T$ is a multivariate normal random vector with mean vector $\\boldsymbol{\\mu}$ and covariance matrix $\\Sigma,$ then the random variable $Y,$ defined as\n\n$$\nY= \\mathbf c^T \\mathbf X = \\sum_{i=1}^k c_i X_i\n$$\n\nis a normally distributed random variable, where $\\mathbf c$ is any $k\\times 1$ column vector, and  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}E[Y] & ={c}^{T}\\mu , \\\\ \\operatorname{Var} [Y] & ={c}^{T}Σc.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, since we wish to know the distribution of $Y = X_1 -2X_2 +4 X_3,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\mathbf c = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1 \\\\ - 2 \\\\ 4\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, for the mean of $Y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y] & ={c}^{T}\\mu  \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =1-4+4 \\\\  & =1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand for the variance of $Y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[Y] & ={c}^{T}Σc \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}12 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 14\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =12-6+56 \\\\  & =62.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nY\\sim N(1,62).\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186091945 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the indicator functions of $\\overline{A},$ $A \\cap B,$ and $A \\cup B$ have the following properties:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{1}_{\\overset{―}{A}}\\,(x) & =1-{1}_{A}(x) \\\\ {1}_{A\\,\\cap \\,B}\\,(x) & =min\\{{1}_{A}(x),{1}_{B}(x)\\} \\\\ {1}_{A\\,\\cup \\,B}\\,(x) & =max\\{{1}_{A}(x),{1}_{B}(x)\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true while statement II is false. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{1}_{A\\,\\cup \\,B}\\,(-1) & =max\\{{1}_{A}(-1),{1}_{B}(-1)\\} \\\\  & =max\\{0,1\\} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $\\mathbf{1}_{A \\,\\cup\\, B}\\,(-1) = 1,$ we conclude that $-1$ belongs to $A \\cup B.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" "Recall that the indicator functions of $\\overline{A},$ $A \\cap B,$ and $A \\cup B$ have the following properties:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{1}_{\\overset{―}{A}}\\,(x) & =1-{1}_{A}(x) \\\\ {1}_{A\\,\\cap \\,B}\\,(x) & =\\min \\{{1}_{A}(x),{1}_{B}(x)\\} \\\\ {1}_{A\\,\\cup \\,B}\\,(x) & =\\max \\{{1}_{A}(x),{1}_{B}(x)\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true while statement II is false. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{1}_{A\\,\\cup \\,B}\\,(-1) & =\\max \\{{1}_{A}(-1),{1}_{B}(-1)\\} \\\\  & =\\max \\{0,1\\} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $\\mathbf{1}_{A \\,\\cup\\, B}\\,(-1) = 1,$ we conclude that $-1$ belongs to $A \\cup B.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""] [:db/cas 17592186091946 :question/problem "Which of the following expressions are polynomials?\n\nI. $(5x^2-10x^3) \\div (5x)$\n\nII. $(1-x^3) \\div (x^3)$\n\nIII. $(2x^5) \\cdot (-x^3-7)$" "Which of the following expressions are polynomials?\n\nI. $(5x^2-10x^3) \\operatorname{div}  (5x)$\n\nII. $(1-x^3) \\operatorname{div}  (x^3)$\n\nIII. $(2x^5) \\cdot (-x^3-7)$"] [:db/cas 17592186092026 :question/worked-solution "First, we organize the system so that the variables are in the correct order and every variable has a coefficient:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+3y+z &=0 \\\\ 0x+4y+z &=7 \\\\ 2x-y+0z &=-4\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNext, we  write the system in the form $A\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{b}$, where $A$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix and $\\mathbf{x}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ are $3\\times 1$ column vectors. We can do this as follows:\n\n\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 7 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n\n\nNow, the solution to $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ is given by $\\mathbf{x}= A^{-1}\\mathbf{b}.$ So, we compute $A^{-1}$ as follows:\n\n*Step 1*: Find the determinant of $A.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A)=\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 0\\end{vmatrix} & =1\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}-3\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{vmatrix}+1\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(1)-3(-2)+(-8) \\\\  & =1+6-8 \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*Step 2*: Compute the matrix of minors.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{vmatrix}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & -8 \\\\ 1 & -2 & -7 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*Step 3*:  Find the matrix of cofactors.\n\n\n\n$$\nC=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -8 \\\\ -1 & -2 & 7 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n\n\n*Step 4*: Transpose the matrix of cofactors.\n\n\n\n$$\nC^T=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -2 & -1 \\\\ -8 & 7 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n\n\n*Step 5*: Apply the inverse matrix formula.\n\n\n\n$$\nA^{-1}=\\dfrac{1}{\\det (A)}C^T=\\dfrac{1}{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -2 & -1 \\\\ -8 & 7 & 4\\end{bmatrix} =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 8 & -7 & -4\\end{bmatrix} .\n$$\n\n\n\nFinally, we can calculate the solution as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 8 & -7 & -4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 7 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 10 \\\\ -33\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x=3,$ $y=10,$ $z=-33.$" "First, we organize the system so that the variables are in the correct order and every variable has a coefficient:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+3y+z &=0 \\\\ 0x+4y+z &=7 \\\\ 2x-y+0z &=-4\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNext, we  write the system in the form $A\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{b}$, where $A$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix and $\\mathbf{x}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ are $3\\times 1$ column vectors. We can do this as follows:\n\n\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 7 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n\n\nNow, the solution to $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ is given by $\\mathbf{x}= A^{-1}\\mathbf{b}.$ So, we compute $A^{-1}$ as follows:\n\n*Step 1*: Find the determinant of $A.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\det (A)=\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 0\\end{vmatrix} & =1\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}-3\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{vmatrix}+1\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(1)-3(-2)+(-8) \\\\  & =1+6-8 \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*Step 2*: Compute the matrix of minors.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{vmatrix}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & -8 \\\\ 1 & -2 & -7 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*Step 3*:  Find the matrix of cofactors.\n\n\n\n$$\nC=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -8 \\\\ -1 & -2 & 7 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n\n\n*Step 4*: Transpose the matrix of cofactors.\n\n\n\n$$\nC^T=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -2 & -1 \\\\ -8 & 7 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n\n\n*Step 5*: Apply the inverse matrix formula.\n\n\n\n$$\nA^{-1}=\\dfrac{1}{\\det (A)}C^T=\\dfrac{1}{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -2 & -1 \\\\ -8 & 7 & 4\\end{bmatrix} =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 8 & -7 & -4\\end{bmatrix} .\n$$\n\n\n\nFinally, we can calculate the solution as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 8 & -7 & -4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 7 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 10 \\\\ -33\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x=3,$ $y=10,$ $z=-33.$"] [:db/cas 17592186092037 :question/worked-solution "We use the identity\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n[rcis(θ)]^{n} &= r^{n}cis(nθ)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwith $n=3$, $r=3$, and\n\n$$\n\\theta=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\n$$\n\nSubstituting in these values, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n[3cis\\left(\\frac{π}{4}\\right)]^{3} &= 3^{3}cis(3 \\cdot \\frac{π}{4}) = 27cis\\left(\\frac{3π}{4}\\right)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, we know that\n\n$$\nr\\,\\textrm{cis}(\\theta) = r \\left[ \\cos (\\theta) +\\textrm{i}\\sin(\\theta) \\right]\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n27cis\\left(\\frac{3π}{4}\\right) &= 27[\\cos \\left(\\frac{3π}{4}\\right) + \\textrm{i}\\sin \\left(\\frac{3π}{4}\\right)] \\\\\n&= 27(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} + \\textrm{i}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\\n&=-\\frac{27\\sqrt{2}}{2} + \\frac{27\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\textrm{i}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We use the identity\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n[r\\operatorname{cis} (θ)]^{n} &= r^{n}\\operatorname{cis} (nθ)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwith $n=3$, $r=3$, and\n\n$$\n\\theta=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\n$$\n\nSubstituting in these values, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n[3\\operatorname{cis} \\left(\\frac{π}{4}\\right)]^{3} &= 3^{3}\\operatorname{cis} (3 \\cdot \\frac{π}{4}) = 27\\operatorname{cis} \\left(\\frac{3π}{4}\\right)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, we know that\n\n$$\nr\\,\\textrm{cis}(\\theta) = r \\left[ \\cos (\\theta) +\\textrm{i}\\sin(\\theta) \\right]\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n27\\operatorname{cis} \\left(\\frac{3π}{4}\\right) &= 27[\\cos \\left(\\frac{3π}{4}\\right) + \\textrm{i}\\sin \\left(\\frac{3π}{4}\\right)] \\\\\n&= 27(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} + \\textrm{i}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\\n&=-\\frac{27\\sqrt{2}}{2} + \\frac{27\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\textrm{i}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186092072 :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any linear transformation $\\mathbf{T},$ we have the following connection between the area of $D$ and the area of the image $\\mathbf{T}(D)$ under the action of $\\mathbf{T}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(\\mathbf{T}(D)) = |\\det(T)| \\cdot \\text{Area}(D)\n$$\n\nFrom the diagram, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D) = \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 3 \\cdot 4 = 6.\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant of $T,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\det(T) = 4 \\cdot 2 -  2 \\cdot (-1) = 10.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(T(D)) & =|det(T)|\\cdot \\text{Area}(D) \\\\  & =|\\,10\\,|\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =60.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Recall that for any linear transformation $\\mathbf{T},$ we have the following connection between the area of $D$ and the area of the image $\\mathbf{T}(D)$ under the action of $\\mathbf{T}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(\\mathbf{T}(D)) = |\\det(T)| \\cdot \\text{Area}(D)\n$$\n\nFrom the diagram, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D) = \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 3 \\cdot 4 = 6.\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant of $T,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\det(T) = 4 \\cdot 2 -  2 \\cdot (-1) = 10.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(T(D)) & =|\\det (T)|\\cdot \\text{Area}(D) \\\\  & =|\\,10\\,|\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =60.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186092073 :question/worked-solution "First, let's sketch our region:\n\n![](images/ee/ee8f0cf16983d87fa505303ed6372a3f389518d05b3e13f2e9e2121038792b84.png)\n\nRecall that for any linear transformation $\\mathbf{T},$ we have the following connection between the area of $D$ and the area of the image $\\mathbf{T}(D)$ under the action of $\\mathbf{T}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(\\mathbf{T}(D)) = |\\det(T)| \\cdot \\text{Area}(D)\n$$\n\nFrom the diagram, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D) = \\dfrac{1}{2} \\cdot 3 \\cdot 4 = 6.\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant of $T,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\det(T) = (-7) \\cdot 3 -  5 \\cdot (-2) = -11.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(T(D)) & =|det(T)|\\cdot \\text{Area}(D) \\\\  & =|-11|\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =66.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, let's sketch our region:\n\n![](images/ee/ee8f0cf16983d87fa505303ed6372a3f389518d05b3e13f2e9e2121038792b84.png)\n\nRecall that for any linear transformation $\\mathbf{T},$ we have the following connection between the area of $D$ and the area of the image $\\mathbf{T}(D)$ under the action of $\\mathbf{T}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(\\mathbf{T}(D)) = |\\det(T)| \\cdot \\text{Area}(D)\n$$\n\nFrom the diagram, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D) = \\dfrac{1}{2} \\cdot 3 \\cdot 4 = 6.\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant of $T,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\det(T) = (-7) \\cdot 3 -  5 \\cdot (-2) = -11.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(T(D)) & =|\\det (T)|\\cdot \\text{Area}(D) \\\\  & =|-11|\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =66.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186092074 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the matrix $\\, A-\\lambda I\\mathbin{:}$\n﻿﻿﻿$A - \\lambda I = \n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}2 - \\lambda  & 4 \\\\ 3 & 2 - \\lambda \\end{array} \\right]}.$\n\nThen, the characteristic equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}2-\\lambda  & 4 \\\\ 3 & 2-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )(2-\\lambda )-4\\cdot 3 & =0 \\\\ 4-4\\lambda +{\\lambda }^{2}-12 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda -8 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we find the matrix $\\, A-\\lambda I\\mathbin{:}$\n﻿﻿﻿$A - \\lambda I = \n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}2 - \\lambda  & 4 \\\\ 3 & 2 - \\lambda \\end{array} \\right]}.$\n\nThen, the characteristic equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\det (A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}2-\\lambda  & 4 \\\\ 3 & 2-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )(2-\\lambda )-4\\cdot 3 & =0 \\\\ 4-4\\lambda +{\\lambda }^{2}-12 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda -8 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186092075 :question/worked-solution "Let's start by writing the matrix $B-\\lambda I\\mathbin{:}$\n$$\nB - \\lambda I = \n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}4 - \\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 - \\lambda \\end{array} \\right]}\n$$\n\nNow, let's find and solve the characteristic equation $\\det(B-\\lambda I) = 0\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(B-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}4-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (4-\\lambda )(4-\\lambda )-0\\cdot 0 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -4)^{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the matrix $B$ has only one eigenvalue $\\lambda=4,$ which is a double root of the characteristic equation. \n\n**Note:** We also say that $\\lambda=4$ has an **algebraic multiplicity** of $2.$" "Let's start by writing the matrix $B-\\lambda I\\mathbin{:}$\n$$\nB - \\lambda I = \n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}4 - \\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 - \\lambda \\end{array} \\right]}\n$$\n\nNow, let's find and solve the characteristic equation $\\det(B-\\lambda I) = 0\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\det (B-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}4-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (4-\\lambda )(4-\\lambda )-0\\cdot 0 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -4)^{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the matrix $B$ has only one eigenvalue $\\lambda=4,$ which is a double root of the characteristic equation. \n\n**Note:** We also say that $\\lambda=4$ has an **algebraic multiplicity** of $2.$"] [:db/cas 17592186092076 :question/worked-solution "Let's start by writing the matrix $A - \\lambda I\\mathbin{:}$\n﻿﻿$A - \\lambda I = \n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}10 - \\lambda  & 3 \\\\ 6 & 7 - \\lambda \\end{array} \\right]}$\n\nNow, let's find and solve the characteristic equation $\\det(A-\\lambda I) = 0\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}10-\\lambda  & 3 \\\\ 6 & 7-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (10-\\lambda )(7-\\lambda )-3\\cdot 6 & =0 \\\\ 70-7\\lambda -10\\lambda +{\\lambda }^{2}-18 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-17\\lambda +52 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the quadratic formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lambda  & =\\frac{17\\pm \\sqrt{(-17)^{2}-4\\cdot 1\\cdot 52}}{2\\cdot 1} \\\\  & =\\frac{17\\pm \\sqrt{81}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{17\\pm 9}{2} \\\\  & =13,4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues are $\\lambda=13$ and $\\lambda=4.$" "Let's start by writing the matrix $A - \\lambda I\\mathbin{:}$\n﻿﻿$A - \\lambda I = \n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}10 - \\lambda  & 3 \\\\ 6 & 7 - \\lambda \\end{array} \\right]}$\n\nNow, let's find and solve the characteristic equation $\\det(A-\\lambda I) = 0\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\det (A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}10-\\lambda  & 3 \\\\ 6 & 7-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (10-\\lambda )(7-\\lambda )-3\\cdot 6 & =0 \\\\ 70-7\\lambda -10\\lambda +{\\lambda }^{2}-18 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-17\\lambda +52 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the quadratic formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lambda  & =\\frac{17\\pm \\sqrt{(-17)^{2}-4\\cdot 1\\cdot 52}}{2\\cdot 1} \\\\  & =\\frac{17\\pm \\sqrt{81}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{17\\pm 9}{2} \\\\  & =13,4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues are $\\lambda=13$ and $\\lambda=4.$"] [:db/cas 17592186092077 :question/worked-solution "Let's start by writing the matrix $A - \\lambda I \\mathbin{:}$\n﻿﻿﻿$A - \\lambda I = \n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}3 - \\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 9 & - 2 \\sqrt{3} - \\lambda \\end{array} \\right]}$\n\nNow, let's find and solve the characteristic equation $\\det (A - \\lambda I) = 0\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}3-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 9 & -2\\sqrt{3}-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (3-\\lambda )(-2\\sqrt{3}-\\lambda )-9\\cdot 0 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -3)(\\lambda +2\\sqrt{3}) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we get the eigenvalues $\\lambda={\\color{red}3}$ and $\\lambda={\\color{blue}-2\\sqrt{3}}.$\n\nIf we look again at the matrix $\\,A={\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}{\\color{red}3} & 0 \\\\ 9 & {\\color{blue}- 2 \\sqrt{3}}\\end{array} \\right]},$\nwe see that the eigenvalues are the entries on the main diagonal. This is true in general: the eigenvalues of a *triangular* or *diagonal* matrix are *always* given by the entries on the main diagonal!\n\n**Watch out!** For a general matrix (which is neither triangular nor diagonal), this might not be the case!" "Let's start by writing the matrix $A - \\lambda I \\mathbin{:}$\n﻿﻿﻿$A - \\lambda I = \n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}3 - \\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 9 & - 2 \\sqrt{3} - \\lambda \\end{array} \\right]}$\n\nNow, let's find and solve the characteristic equation $\\det (A - \\lambda I) = 0\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\det (A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}3-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 9 & -2\\sqrt{3}-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (3-\\lambda )(-2\\sqrt{3}-\\lambda )-9\\cdot 0 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -3)(\\lambda +2\\sqrt{3}) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we get the eigenvalues $\\lambda={\\color{red}3}$ and $\\lambda={\\color{blue}-2\\sqrt{3}}.$\n\nIf we look again at the matrix $\\,A={\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}{\\color{red}3} & 0 \\\\ 9 & {\\color{blue}- 2 \\sqrt{3}}\\end{array} \\right]},$\nwe see that the eigenvalues are the entries on the main diagonal. This is true in general: the eigenvalues of a *triangular* or *diagonal* matrix are *always* given by the entries on the main diagonal!\n\n**Watch out!** For a general matrix (which is neither triangular nor diagonal), this might not be the case!"] [:db/cas 17592186092082 :question/worked-solution "Computing the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)) \\\\  & ={e}^{t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+{e}^{t}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t) \\\\  & =2{e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)) \\\\  & ={e}^{t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+{e}^{t}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t) \\\\  & =-2{e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute these derivatives in the formula for arc length, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{{(2{e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{2}+{(-2{e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{4{e}^{2t}({cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}2{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2{e}^{t}{|}_{0}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =2({e}^{\\pi /4}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Computing the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)) \\\\  & ={e}^{t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+{e}^{t}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t) \\\\  & =2{e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)) \\\\  & ={e}^{t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+{e}^{t}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t) \\\\  & =-2{e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute these derivatives in the formula for arc length, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{{(2{e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{2}+{(-2{e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{4{e}^{2t}({\\cos }^{2}⁡t+{\\sin }^{2}⁡t)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}2{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2{e}^{t}{|}_{0}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =2({e}^{\\pi /4}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186092089 :question/worked-solution "The area bounded by a polar curve is given by\n\n$$\nA = \\dfrac{1}{2} \\int_{\\theta_1}^{\\theta_2} r^2(\\theta) \\, \\textrm{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\nWhen $r(\\theta) = \\cos(3\\theta),$ $\\theta_1=-\\dfrac{\\pi}{6},$ and $\\theta_2=\\dfrac{\\pi}{6},$ this gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{\\pi /6}{cos}^{2}⁡(3\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{\\pi /6}(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\theta ))\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(\\theta {|}_{-\\pi /6}^{\\pi /6}+\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6\\theta ){|}_{-\\pi /6}^{\\pi /6}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}((\\frac{\\pi }{6}-\\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right))+\\frac{1}{6}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\pi ))) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(\\frac{\\pi }{3}+0) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The area bounded by a polar curve is given by\n\n$$\nA = \\dfrac{1}{2} \\int_{\\theta_1}^{\\theta_2} r^2(\\theta) \\, \\textrm{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\nWhen $r(\\theta) = \\cos(3\\theta),$ $\\theta_1=-\\dfrac{\\pi}{6},$ and $\\theta_2=\\dfrac{\\pi}{6},$ this gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{\\pi /6}{\\cos }^{2}⁡(3\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{\\pi /6}(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\theta ))\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(\\theta {|}_{-\\pi /6}^{\\pi /6}+\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6\\theta ){|}_{-\\pi /6}^{\\pi /6}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}((\\frac{\\pi }{6}-\\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right))+\\frac{1}{6}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\pi ))) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(\\frac{\\pi }{3}+0) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186092090 :question/worked-solution "The area bounded by a polar curve is given by\n\n$$\nA = \\dfrac{1}{2} \\int_{\\theta_1}^{\\theta_2} r^2(\\theta) \\, \\textrm{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\nWhen $r(\\theta) = 1+2\\cos(\\theta),$ $\\theta_1=\\dfrac{2\\pi}{3},$ and $\\theta_2=\\dfrac{4\\pi}{3},$ this gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{2\\pi /3}^{4\\pi /3}(1+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta ))^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{2\\pi /3}^{4\\pi /3}(1+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +4{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\theta \\,{|}_{2\\pi /3}^{4\\pi /3}+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,{|}_{2\\pi /3}^{4\\pi /3}+4\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{2\\pi /3}^{4\\pi /3}(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ))\\,\\text{d}\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\theta \\,{|}_{2\\pi /3}^{4\\pi /3}+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,{|}_{2\\pi /3}^{4\\pi /3}+2\\theta \\,{|}_{2\\pi /3}^{4\\pi /3}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )\\,{|}_{2\\pi /3}^{4\\pi /3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(3\\theta \\,{|}_{2\\pi /3}^{4\\pi /3}+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,{|}_{2\\pi /3}^{4\\pi /3}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )\\,{|}_{2\\pi /3}^{4\\pi /3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(2\\pi -4\\sqrt{3}+\\sqrt{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(2\\pi -3\\sqrt{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The area bounded by a polar curve is given by\n\n$$\nA = \\dfrac{1}{2} \\int_{\\theta_1}^{\\theta_2} r^2(\\theta) \\, \\textrm{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\nWhen $r(\\theta) = 1+2\\cos(\\theta),$ $\\theta_1=\\dfrac{2\\pi}{3},$ and $\\theta_2=\\dfrac{4\\pi}{3},$ this gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{2\\pi /3}^{4\\pi /3}(1+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta ))^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{2\\pi /3}^{4\\pi /3}(1+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +4{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\theta \\,{|}_{2\\pi /3}^{4\\pi /3}+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,{|}_{2\\pi /3}^{4\\pi /3}+4\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{2\\pi /3}^{4\\pi /3}(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ))\\,\\text{d}\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\theta \\,{|}_{2\\pi /3}^{4\\pi /3}+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,{|}_{2\\pi /3}^{4\\pi /3}+2\\theta \\,{|}_{2\\pi /3}^{4\\pi /3}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )\\,{|}_{2\\pi /3}^{4\\pi /3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(3\\theta \\,{|}_{2\\pi /3}^{4\\pi /3}+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,{|}_{2\\pi /3}^{4\\pi /3}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )\\,{|}_{2\\pi /3}^{4\\pi /3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(2\\pi -4\\sqrt{3}+\\sqrt{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(2\\pi -3\\sqrt{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186092091 :question/worked-solution "Consider the rightmost pair of petals. First, we find the values of $\\theta$ for which the two curves intersect:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ) & =1 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ) & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us two solutions for $2\\theta \\in [-\\pi,\\pi]$:\n\n$$\n2\\theta = -\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}, \\dfrac{\\pi}{3} \\qquad \\Longrightarrow \\qquad \\theta= -\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}, \\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\n$$\n\nTo determine which is the inner function and which is the outer, let's draw an arrow from the origin that passes through the shaded region.\n\n![](images/1d/1dc9ce9add3d7135f69c92590ebde003d4e5ee31446d6202222ac890e2f42a9c.png)\n\nThe arrow\n\n- *enters* the region through the inner function $r=1,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the outer function $r=2\\cos(2\\theta).$\n\nTo find the area, we use the formula\n\n$$\nA = \\dfrac{1}{2} \\int_{\\theta_1}^{\\theta_2} \\Big( \\big[ {\\color{brown}\\text{outer}} \\big]^2 - \\big[ {\\color{blue}\\text{inner}} \\big]^2 \\Big) \\:\\textrm{d}\\theta,\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$\\qquad$ $\\theta_1=-\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}, \\quad$ and $\\quad \\theta_2=\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}.$ \n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{\\pi /6}((2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ))^{2}-{1}^{2})\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{\\pi /6}(4{cos}^{2}⁡(2\\theta )-1)\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{\\pi /6}(2(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta ))-1)\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{\\pi /6}(1+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta ))\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\theta {|}_{-\\pi /6}^{\\pi /6}+\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\theta ){|}_{-\\pi /6}^{\\pi /6} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{\\pi }{6}-\\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right))+\\frac{1}{4}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{2\\pi }{3}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(-\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\right)) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}+\\frac{1}{4}(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}-\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the total area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =4{A}_{1}=4(\\frac{\\pi }{6}+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4})=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}+\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Consider the rightmost pair of petals. First, we find the values of $\\theta$ for which the two curves intersect:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ) & =1 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ) & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us two solutions for $2\\theta \\in [-\\pi,\\pi]$:\n\n$$\n2\\theta = -\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}, \\dfrac{\\pi}{3} \\qquad \\Longrightarrow \\qquad \\theta= -\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}, \\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\n$$\n\nTo determine which is the inner function and which is the outer, let's draw an arrow from the origin that passes through the shaded region.\n\n![](images/1d/1dc9ce9add3d7135f69c92590ebde003d4e5ee31446d6202222ac890e2f42a9c.png)\n\nThe arrow\n\n- *enters* the region through the inner function $r=1,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the outer function $r=2\\cos(2\\theta).$\n\nTo find the area, we use the formula\n\n$$\nA = \\dfrac{1}{2} \\int_{\\theta_1}^{\\theta_2} \\Big( \\big[ {\\color{brown}\\text{outer}} \\big]^2 - \\big[ {\\color{blue}\\text{inner}} \\big]^2 \\Big) \\:\\textrm{d}\\theta,\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$\\qquad$ $\\theta_1=-\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}, \\quad$ and $\\quad \\theta_2=\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}.$ \n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{\\pi /6}((2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ))^{2}-{1}^{2})\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{\\pi /6}(4{\\cos }^{2}⁡(2\\theta )-1)\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{\\pi /6}(2(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta ))-1)\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{\\pi /6}(1+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta ))\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\theta {|}_{-\\pi /6}^{\\pi /6}+\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\theta ){|}_{-\\pi /6}^{\\pi /6} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{\\pi }{6}-\\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right))+\\frac{1}{4}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{2\\pi }{3}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(-\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\right)) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}+\\frac{1}{4}(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}-\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the total area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =4{A}_{1}=4(\\frac{\\pi }{6}+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4})=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}+\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186092092 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the values of $\\theta$ for which the two curves intersect:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us two solutions for $\\theta \\in \\left[0,\\pi\\right]\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\theta = 0, \\pi\n$$\n\nTo determine the inner function and the outer, let's draw an arrow from the origin that passes through the shaded region.\n\n![](images/4c/4ce437c0d6b1f196269c5c1e56e687a6dca21fd407834dce20bfa7ac76298002.png)\n\nThe arrow\n\n- *enters* the region through the inner function $r=\\sin\\theta,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the outer function $r=2\\sin\\theta.$\n\nTo find the area, we use the formula\n\n$$\nA = \\dfrac{1}{2} \\int_{\\theta_1}^{\\theta_2} \\Big( \\big[ {\\color{brown}\\text{outer}} \\big]^2 - \\big[ {\\color{blue}\\text{inner}} \\big]^2 \\Big) \\:\\textrm{d}\\theta,\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$\\qquad$ $\\theta_1=0, \\quad$ and $\\quad \\theta_2=\\pi.$ \n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }((2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}-(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2})\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }3{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}(\\theta -\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }{2}){|}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}[(\\pi -\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi )}{2})-(0-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡0}{2})] \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we find the values of $\\theta$ for which the two curves intersect:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us two solutions for $\\theta \\in \\left[0,\\pi\\right]\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\theta = 0, \\pi\n$$\n\nTo determine the inner function and the outer, let's draw an arrow from the origin that passes through the shaded region.\n\n![](images/4c/4ce437c0d6b1f196269c5c1e56e687a6dca21fd407834dce20bfa7ac76298002.png)\n\nThe arrow\n\n- *enters* the region through the inner function $r=\\sin\\theta,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the outer function $r=2\\sin\\theta.$\n\nTo find the area, we use the formula\n\n$$\nA = \\dfrac{1}{2} \\int_{\\theta_1}^{\\theta_2} \\Big( \\big[ {\\color{brown}\\text{outer}} \\big]^2 - \\big[ {\\color{blue}\\text{inner}} \\big]^2 \\Big) \\:\\textrm{d}\\theta,\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$\\qquad$ $\\theta_1=0, \\quad$ and $\\quad \\theta_2=\\pi.$ \n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }((2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}-(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2})\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }3{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}(\\theta -\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }{2}){|}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}[(\\pi -\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi )}{2})-(0-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡0}{2})] \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186092093 :question/worked-solution "The arc length $L$ of a polar curve $r=r(\\theta)$ is given by\n\n$$\nL =  \\int_{\\theta_1}^{\\theta_2} \\sqrt{r^2 +\\left(\\frac{\\textrm{d}r}{\\textrm{d}\\theta}\\right)^2 }\\,\\textrm{d}\\theta.\n$$\n\nSince $r=2(1-\\cos\\theta),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}r}{\\textrm{d}\\theta} = 2\\sin\\theta.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{(2-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+{(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{4-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +4{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +4{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{4-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +4}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{8-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now use the half-angle formula $1-\\cos\\theta =2\\sin^2\\left(\\dfrac{\\theta}{2}\\right)$ to give\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & =2\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{2{sin}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\theta }{2}\\right)}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{2}\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\theta }{2}\\right)\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={-4\\cdot 2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\theta }{2}\\right))|}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =-8(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡0) \\\\  & =-8(0-1) \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The arc length $L$ of a polar curve $r=r(\\theta)$ is given by\n\n$$\nL =  \\int_{\\theta_1}^{\\theta_2} \\sqrt{r^2 +\\left(\\frac{\\textrm{d}r}{\\textrm{d}\\theta}\\right)^2 }\\,\\textrm{d}\\theta.\n$$\n\nSince $r=2(1-\\cos\\theta),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}r}{\\textrm{d}\\theta} = 2\\sin\\theta.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{(2-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+{(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{4-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +4{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta +4{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{4-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +4}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{8-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now use the half-angle formula $1-\\cos\\theta =2\\sin^2\\left(\\dfrac{\\theta}{2}\\right)$ to give\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & =2\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{2{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\theta }{2}\\right)}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{2}\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\theta }{2}\\right)\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={-4\\cdot 2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\theta }{2}\\right))|}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =-8(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡0) \\\\  & =-8(0-1) \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186092105 :question/worked-solution "The area bounded by a polar curve is given by\n\n$$\nA = \\dfrac{1}{2} \\int_{\\theta_1}^{\\theta_2} r^2(\\theta) \\, \\textrm{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\nWhen $r(\\theta) = 2\\sin\\theta + 2\\cos\\theta,$ $\\theta_1=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2},$ and $\\theta_2=\\dfrac{3\\pi}{4},$ this gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /4}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /4}(4{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +8\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +4{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /4}(4+8\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{2}{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /4}(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ))\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2(\\theta \\,{|}_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /4}-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}{2}\\,{|}_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /4}) \\\\  & =2\\theta \\,{|}_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /4}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )\\,{|}_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /4} \\\\  & =2(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}-\\frac{\\pi }{2})-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\right)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}-1 \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi -2}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The area bounded by a polar curve is given by\n\n$$\nA = \\dfrac{1}{2} \\int_{\\theta_1}^{\\theta_2} r^2(\\theta) \\, \\textrm{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\nWhen $r(\\theta) = 2\\sin\\theta + 2\\cos\\theta,$ $\\theta_1=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2},$ and $\\theta_2=\\dfrac{3\\pi}{4},$ this gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /4}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /4}(4{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta +8\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +4{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /4}(4+8\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{2}{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /4}(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ))\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2(\\theta \\,{|}_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /4}-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}{2}\\,{|}_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /4}) \\\\  & =2\\theta \\,{|}_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /4}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )\\,{|}_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /4} \\\\  & =2(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}-\\frac{\\pi }{2})-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\right)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}-1 \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi -2}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186092113 :question/worked-solution "To compute the arc length $L$ of a parametrically defined curve, we use the formula\n\n$$\nL = \\int_{t_1}^{t_2} \\sqrt{ \\left( \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}x}{\\textrm{d}t}\\right)^2 + \\left( \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}t}\\right)^2}\\:\\textrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t))=-\\operatorname{tan}⁡t\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute these derivatives in the formula for arc length, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{{(1)}^{2}+{(-\\operatorname{tan}⁡t)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{1+{tan}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{{sec}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{sec}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "To compute the arc length $L$ of a parametrically defined curve, we use the formula\n\n$$\nL = \\int_{t_1}^{t_2} \\sqrt{ \\left( \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}x}{\\textrm{d}t}\\right)^2 + \\left( \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}t}\\right)^2}\\:\\textrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t))=-\\operatorname{tan}⁡t\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute these derivatives in the formula for arc length, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{{(1)}^{2}+{(-\\operatorname{tan}⁡t)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{1+{\\tan }^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{{\\sec }^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{sec}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186092123 :question/worked-solution "Consider the rightmost petal, oriented along the $x$-axis. First, we find the values of $\\theta$ for which the two curves intersect:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\theta ) & =1 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\theta ) & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us two solutions for $3\\theta \\in \\left[-\\pi,\\pi\\right]\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n3\\theta = -\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}, \\dfrac{\\pi}{3} \\qquad \\Longrightarrow \\qquad \\theta= -\\dfrac{\\pi}{9}, \\dfrac{\\pi}{9}\n$$\n\nTo determine the inner function and the outer, let's draw an arrow from the origin that passes through the shaded region.\n\n![](images/89/8950c8f5e8ea54736714e83f584ccc4b164cdee53cf3c3f64a8e0c9f81f16e6a.png)\n\nThe arrow\n\n- *enters* the region through the inner function $r=1,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the outer function $r=2\\cos(3\\theta).$\n\nTo find the area, we use the formula\n\n$$\nA = \\dfrac{1}{2} \\int_{\\theta_1}^{\\theta_2} \\Big( \\big[ {\\color{brown}\\text{outer}} \\big]^2 - \\big[ {\\color{blue}\\text{inner}} \\big]^2 \\Big) \\:\\textrm{d}\\theta,\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$\\qquad$ $\\theta_1=-\\dfrac{\\pi}{9}, \\quad$ and $\\quad \\theta_2=\\dfrac{\\pi}{9}.$ \n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /9}^{\\pi /9}[{(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\theta ))}^{2}-{1}^{2}]\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /9}^{\\pi /9}(4{cos}^{2}⁡(3\\theta )-1)\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Consider the rightmost petal, oriented along the $x$-axis. First, we find the values of $\\theta$ for which the two curves intersect:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\theta ) & =1 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\theta ) & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us two solutions for $3\\theta \\in \\left[-\\pi,\\pi\\right]\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n3\\theta = -\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}, \\dfrac{\\pi}{3} \\qquad \\Longrightarrow \\qquad \\theta= -\\dfrac{\\pi}{9}, \\dfrac{\\pi}{9}\n$$\n\nTo determine the inner function and the outer, let's draw an arrow from the origin that passes through the shaded region.\n\n![](images/89/8950c8f5e8ea54736714e83f584ccc4b164cdee53cf3c3f64a8e0c9f81f16e6a.png)\n\nThe arrow\n\n- *enters* the region through the inner function $r=1,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the outer function $r=2\\cos(3\\theta).$\n\nTo find the area, we use the formula\n\n$$\nA = \\dfrac{1}{2} \\int_{\\theta_1}^{\\theta_2} \\Big( \\big[ {\\color{brown}\\text{outer}} \\big]^2 - \\big[ {\\color{blue}\\text{inner}} \\big]^2 \\Big) \\:\\textrm{d}\\theta,\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$\\qquad$ $\\theta_1=-\\dfrac{\\pi}{9}, \\quad$ and $\\quad \\theta_2=\\dfrac{\\pi}{9}.$ \n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /9}^{\\pi /9}[{(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\theta ))}^{2}-{1}^{2}]\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /9}^{\\pi /9}(4{\\cos }^{2}⁡(3\\theta )-1)\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186092124 :question/worked-solution "We are given that our curves intersect when $\\theta_1= \\dfrac{\\pi}{4}$ and $\\theta_2=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}.$ \n\nTo determine the inner function and the outer, 