{2}\\ln\\left(\\dfrac{1+\\dfrac{1}{2}}{1-\\dfrac{1}{2}}\\right)\\\\[5pt]\n\t\t\n&=\\dfrac{1}{2}\\ln\\left(\\dfrac{\\dfrac{3}{2}}{\\dfrac{1}{2}}\\right)\\\\[5pt]\n\t\t\n&=\\dfrac{1}{2}\\ln{3}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" "$\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsinh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcosh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{artanh} }{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{sech} }{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{csch} }{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsech} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcsch} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcoth} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$\n\nThe definition of $\\operatorname{artanh} {x}$ is \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{artanh} {x} = \\dfrac{1}{2}\\ln\\left(\\dfrac{1+x}{1-x}\\right).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\operatorname{artanh} \\left ( \\dfrac{1}{2} \\right )\n                \n& = \\dfrac{1}{2}\\ln\\left(\\dfrac{1+\\dfrac{1}{2}}{1-\\dfrac{1}{2}}\\right)\\\\[5pt]\n\t\t\n&=\\dfrac{1}{2}\\ln\\left(\\dfrac{\\dfrac{3}{2}}{\\dfrac{1}{2}}\\right)\\\\[5pt]\n\t\t\n&=\\dfrac{1}{2}\\ln{3}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186092140 :question/problem "$\\newcommand{\\arsinh}{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcosh}{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\artanh}{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arsech}{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$\n\nUsing the definition of the hyperbolic sine, calculate $f^{-1}(x)$ given that $f(x) = \\sinh{\\left(x^2\\right)}$ and $x \\geq 0.$" "$\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsinh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcosh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{artanh} }{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{sech} }{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{csch} }{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsech} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcsch} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcoth} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$\n\nUsing the definition of the hyperbolic sine, calculate $f^{-1}(x)$ given that $f(x) = \\sinh{\\left(x^2\\right)}$ and $x \\geq 0.$"] [:db/cas 17592186092140 :question/worked-solution "$\\newcommand{\\arsinh}{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcosh}{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\artanh}{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arsech}{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$\n\nLet $y = \\sinh{\\left(x^2\\right)}.$\nSince $x \\geq 0,$ we have $y \\geq 0.$\n\nTo find the inverse function we swap $x$ and $y{:}$\n\n$$\nx = \\sinh{\\left(y^2\\right)},\n$$\n\nwhere $x\\in[0,\\infty)$ and $y\\in[0,\\infty).$\n\nNext, we apply the definition of $\\sinh x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{{y}^{2}}-{e}^{-{y}^{2}}) \\\\ 2x & ={e}^{{y}^{2}}-{e}^{-{y}^{2}} \\\\ 2x{e}^{{y}^{2}} & ={e}^{2{y}^{2}}-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBringing everything to the left-hand side, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2{y}^{2}}-2x{e}^{{y}^{2}}-1 & =0 \\\\ {({e}^{{y}^{2}})}^{2}-2x({e}^{{y}^{2}})-1 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $Y = e^{y^2}.$ We can form a quadratic equation in $Y$ and solve it by completing the square, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Y}^{2}-2xY-1 & =0 \\\\ (Y-x)^{2}-{x}^{2}-1 & =0 \\\\ (Y-x)^{2} & ={x}^{2}+1 \\\\ Y-x & =\\pm \\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1} \\\\ Y & =x\\pm \\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1} \\\\ {e}^{{y}^{2}} & =x\\pm \\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $e^{y^2} >0$ for all real $y,$ and $x -\\sqrt{x^2+1} <0,$ we must select the positive branch:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{{y}^{2}} & =x+\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{{y}^{2}}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}) \\\\ {y}^{2} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}) \\\\ y & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since we require $y\\ge 0,$ we select the positive solution. Therefore,\n\n$$\nf^{-1}(x) = \\sqrt{\\ln\\left(x+\\sqrt{x^2+1}\\right)}.\n$$" "$\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsinh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcosh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{artanh} }{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{sech} }{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{csch} }{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsech} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcsch} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcoth} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$\n\nLet $y = \\sinh{\\left(x^2\\right)}.$\nSince $x \\geq 0,$ we have $y \\geq 0.$\n\nTo find the inverse function we swap $x$ and $y{:}$\n\n$$\nx = \\sinh{\\left(y^2\\right)},\n$$\n\nwhere $x\\in[0,\\infty)$ and $y\\in[0,\\infty).$\n\nNext, we apply the definition of $\\sinh x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{{y}^{2}}-{e}^{-{y}^{2}}) \\\\ 2x & ={e}^{{y}^{2}}-{e}^{-{y}^{2}} \\\\ 2x{e}^{{y}^{2}} & ={e}^{2{y}^{2}}-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBringing everything to the left-hand side, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2{y}^{2}}-2x{e}^{{y}^{2}}-1 & =0 \\\\ {({e}^{{y}^{2}})}^{2}-2x({e}^{{y}^{2}})-1 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $Y = e^{y^2}.$ We can form a quadratic equation in $Y$ and solve it by completing the square, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Y}^{2}-2xY-1 & =0 \\\\ (Y-x)^{2}-{x}^{2}-1 & =0 \\\\ (Y-x)^{2} & ={x}^{2}+1 \\\\ Y-x & =\\pm \\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1} \\\\ Y & =x\\pm \\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1} \\\\ {e}^{{y}^{2}} & =x\\pm \\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $e^{y^2} >0$ for all real $y,$ and $x -\\sqrt{x^2+1} <0,$ we must select the positive branch:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{{y}^{2}} & =x+\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{{y}^{2}}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}) \\\\ {y}^{2} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}) \\\\ y & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since we require $y\\ge 0,$ we select the positive solution. Therefore,\n\n$$\nf^{-1}(x) = \\sqrt{\\ln\\left(x+\\sqrt{x^2+1}\\right)}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186092471 :question/worked-solution "To compute the arc length $L$ of a parametrically defined curve, we use the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute these derivatives in the formula for arc length, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{{(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{2}+{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{2-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to the double-angle formula for cosine, $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )=1-2{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .$ Substituting $\\theta =\\frac{t}{2},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{t}{2}) & =1-2{sin}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{t}{2}\\right) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t & =1-2{sin}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{t}{2}\\right) \\\\ 1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & =2{sin}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{t}{2}\\right) \\\\ 2-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & =4{sin}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{t}{2}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{2-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{4{sin}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{t}{2}\\right)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{t}{2}\\right)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-2\\cdot 2\\cdot {\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{t}{2}\\right)|}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =-4(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡0) \\\\  & =-4(0-1) \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "To compute the arc length $L$ of a parametrically defined curve, we use the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute these derivatives in the formula for arc length, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{{(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{2}+{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+{\\cos }^{2}⁡t+{\\sin }^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{2-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to the double-angle formula for cosine, $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )=1-2{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta .$ Substituting $\\theta =\\frac{t}{2},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{t}{2}) & =1-2{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{t}{2}\\right) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t & =1-2{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{t}{2}\\right) \\\\ 1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & =2{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{t}{2}\\right) \\\\ 2-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & =4{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{t}{2}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{2-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{4{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{t}{2}\\right)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{t}{2}\\right)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-2\\cdot 2\\cdot {\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{t}{2}\\right)|}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =-4(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡0) \\\\  & =-4(0-1) \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186092516 :question/worked-solution "Let's start by writing the matrix $A-\\lambda I:$\n﻿﻿﻿\n\n$$\nA-\\lambda I=\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{3}-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 4 & 2-\\lambda \\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, let's find and solve the characteristic equation $det(A-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}\\sqrt{3}-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 4 & 2-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (\\sqrt{3}-\\lambda )(2-\\lambda )-0\\cdot 4 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -\\sqrt{3})(\\lambda -2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues are $\\lambda =\\sqrt{3}$ and $\\lambda =2.$\n\n**Note:** The eigenvalues of a triangular (or diagonal) matrix are the entries on the main diagonal." "Let's start by writing the matrix $A-\\lambda I:$\n﻿﻿﻿\n\n$$\nA-\\lambda I=\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{3}-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 4 & 2-\\lambda \\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, let's find and solve the characteristic equation $\\det (A-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\det (A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}\\sqrt{3}-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 4 & 2-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (\\sqrt{3}-\\lambda )(2-\\lambda )-0\\cdot 4 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -\\sqrt{3})(\\lambda -2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues are $\\lambda =\\sqrt{3}$ and $\\lambda =2.$\n\n**Note:** The eigenvalues of a triangular (or diagonal) matrix are the entries on the main diagonal."] [:db/cas 17592186092554 :question/problem "Which of the following expressions are polynomials?\n\n1. $(-7x) + (2x^{2} + x)$\n2. $(2x - x^{3}) \\div (x)$\n3. $(2x^{7}) \\cdot (1 + 2x^{5})$" "Which of the following expressions are polynomials?\n\n1. $(-7x) + (2x^{2} + x)$\n2. $(2x - x^{3}) \\operatorname{div}  (x)$\n3. $(2x^{7}) \\cdot (1 + 2x^{5})$"] [:db/cas 17592186092560 :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $X=[{X}_{1},{X}_{2}]^{T}$ is a bivariate normal random vector with mean vector $\\mu $ and covariance matrix $Σ,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu =\\begin{bmatrix}{\\mu }_{1} \\\\ {\\mu }_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,Σ=\\begin{bmatrix}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2} & {\\sigma }_{12} \\\\ {\\sigma }_{21} & {\\sigma }_{2}^{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthen the random variable $Y,$ defined as\n\n$$\nY=a{X}_{1}+b{X}_{2}\n$$\n\nis a normally distributed random variable for all values of $a$ and $b,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}E[Y] & =a{\\mu }_{1}+b{\\mu }_{2}, \\\\ Var[Y] & ={a}^{2}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}+{b}^{2}{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}+2ab{\\sigma }_{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have ${\\mu }_{1}=0$ and ${\\mu }_{2}=1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y] & =\\text{E}[{X}_{1}-2{X}_{2}] \\\\  & =\\text{E}[{X}_{1}]-2\\text{E}[{X}_{2}] \\\\  & ={\\mu }_{1}-2\\cdot {\\mu }_{2} \\\\  & =0-2\\cdot (1) \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, we have ${\\sigma }_{1}^{2}=1,{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}=4,$ and ${\\sigma }_{12}={\\sigma }_{21}=1.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Var[Y] & =Var[{X}_{1}-2{X}_{2}] \\\\  & ={1}^{2}\\cdot 1+(-2)^{2}\\cdot 4+2\\cdot 1\\cdot (-2)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =1+16-4 \\\\  & =13.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nY∼N(-2,13).\n$$" "Recall that if $X=[{X}_{1},{X}_{2}]^{T}$ is a bivariate normal random vector with mean vector $\\mu $ and covariance matrix $Σ,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu =\\begin{bmatrix}{\\mu }_{1} \\\\ {\\mu }_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,Σ=\\begin{bmatrix}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2} & {\\sigma }_{12} \\\\ {\\sigma }_{21} & {\\sigma }_{2}^{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthen the random variable $Y,$ defined as\n\n$$\nY=a{X}_{1}+b{X}_{2}\n$$\n\nis a normally distributed random variable for all values of $a$ and $b,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}E[Y] & =a{\\mu }_{1}+b{\\mu }_{2}, \\\\ \\operatorname{Var} [Y] & ={a}^{2}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}+{b}^{2}{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}+2ab{\\sigma }_{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have ${\\mu }_{1}=0$ and ${\\mu }_{2}=1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y] & =\\text{E}[{X}_{1}-2{X}_{2}] \\\\  & =\\text{E}[{X}_{1}]-2\\text{E}[{X}_{2}] \\\\  & ={\\mu }_{1}-2\\cdot {\\mu }_{2} \\\\  & =0-2\\cdot (1) \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, we have ${\\sigma }_{1}^{2}=1,{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}=4,$ and ${\\sigma }_{12}={\\sigma }_{21}=1.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{Var} [Y] & =\\operatorname{Var} [{X}_{1}-2{X}_{2}] \\\\  & ={1}^{2}\\cdot 1+(-2)^{2}\\cdot 4+2\\cdot 1\\cdot (-2)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =1+16-4 \\\\  & =13.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nY∼N(-2,13).\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186092570 :question/problem "What is $[4cis\\left(\\frac{π}{3}\\right)]^{2}$?" "What is $[4\\operatorname{cis} \\left(\\frac{π}{3}\\right)]^{2}$?"] [:db/cas 17592186092572 :question/problem "What is $[2cis\\left(\\frac{π}{24}\\right)]^{6}$?" "What is $[2\\operatorname{cis} \\left(\\frac{π}{24}\\right)]^{6}$?"] [:db/cas 17592186092575 :question/worked-solution "To compute the arc length $L$ of a parametrically defined curve, we use the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t})={e}^{t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)={e}^{t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute these derivatives in the formula for arc length, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-3}^{1}\\sqrt{{e}^{2t}+{e}^{2t}{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-3}^{1}{e}^{t}\\sqrt{1+{sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-3}^{1}{e}^{t}\\sqrt{2+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)}\\,\\text{d}t\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "To compute the arc length $L$ of a parametrically defined curve, we use the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t})={e}^{t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)={e}^{t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute these derivatives in the formula for arc length, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-3}^{1}\\sqrt{{e}^{2t}+{e}^{2t}{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-3}^{1}{e}^{t}\\sqrt{1+{\\sin }^{2}⁡t+{\\cos }^{2}⁡t+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-3}^{1}{e}^{t}\\sqrt{2+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)}\\,\\text{d}t\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186092579 :question/problem "Let $X=[{X}_{1},{X}_{2}]^{T}$ be a bivariate normal random vector. The variances of ${X}_{1}$ and ${X}_{2}$ and their covariance are given by\n\n$$\n{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}=10,\\,{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}=5,\\,{\\sigma }_{12}=5.\n$$\n\nGiven that the joint probability density function of $X$ is\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{1}{k}\\,exp(-\\frac{1}{2}(x-\\mu )^{T}\\,{Σ}^{-1}\\,(x-\\mu )),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mu $ is the mean vector and $Σ$ is the corresponding covariance matrix, find the value of $k.$" "Let $X=[{X}_{1},{X}_{2}]^{T}$ be a bivariate normal random vector. The variances of ${X}_{1}$ and ${X}_{2}$ and their covariance are given by\n\n$$\n{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}=10,\\,{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}=5,\\,{\\sigma }_{12}=5.\n$$\n\nGiven that the joint probability density function of $X$ is\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{1}{k}\\,\\exp (-\\frac{1}{2}(x-\\mu )^{T}\\,{Σ}^{-1}\\,(x-\\mu )),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mu $ is the mean vector and $Σ$ is the corresponding covariance matrix, find the value of $k.$"] [:db/cas 17592186092579 :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $X=[{X}_{1},{X}_{2}]^{T}$ is a bivariate normal vector with mean vector $\\mu $ and covariance matrix $Σ,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu =\\begin{pmatrix}{\\mu }_{1} \\\\ {\\mu }_{2}\\end{pmatrix},\\,Σ=\\begin{bmatrix}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2} & {\\sigma }_{12} \\\\ {\\sigma }_{21} & {\\sigma }_{2}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2} & \\rho {\\sigma }_{1}{\\sigma }_{2} \\\\ \\rho {\\sigma }_{1}{\\sigma }_{2} & {\\sigma }_{2}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\sigma }_{i}^{2}=\\text{Var}[{X}_{i}],$ ${\\sigma }_{12}=\\text{Cov}[{X}_{1},{X}_{2}],$ and $\\rho $ is the correlation, then the joint probability density function of $X$ is\n\n$$\nf({x}_{1},{x}_{2})=\\frac{1}{2\\pi \\sqrt{|Σ|}}exp(-\\frac{1}{2}(X-\\mu )^{T}\\,{Σ}^{-1}\\,(X-\\mu ))\n$$\n\nand $|Σ|$ denotes the determinant of $Σ.$\n\nUsing the given values of ${\\sigma }_{1}^{2},{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}$, and ${\\sigma }_{12},$ our covariance matrix is\n\n$$\nΣ=\\begin{bmatrix}10 & 5 \\\\ 5 & 5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n|Σ|=10\\cdot 5-5\\cdot 5=25.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this value into our expression for the joint PDF, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{1}{2\\pi \\sqrt{25}}exp(-\\frac{1}{2}(x-\\mu )^{T}\\,{Σ}^{-1}\\,(x-\\mu )) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10\\pi }exp(-\\frac{1}{2}(x-\\mu )^{T}\\,{Σ}^{-1}\\,(x-\\mu )).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $k=10\\pi .$" "Recall that if $X=[{X}_{1},{X}_{2}]^{T}$ is a bivariate normal vector with mean vector $\\mu $ and covariance matrix $Σ,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu =\\begin{pmatrix}{\\mu }_{1} \\\\ {\\mu }_{2}\\end{pmatrix},\\,Σ=\\begin{bmatrix}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2} & {\\sigma }_{12} \\\\ {\\sigma }_{21} & {\\sigma }_{2}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2} & \\rho {\\sigma }_{1}{\\sigma }_{2} \\\\ \\rho {\\sigma }_{1}{\\sigma }_{2} & {\\sigma }_{2}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\sigma }_{i}^{2}=\\text{Var}[{X}_{i}],$ ${\\sigma }_{12}=\\text{Cov}[{X}_{1},{X}_{2}],$ and $\\rho $ is the correlation, then the joint probability density function of $X$ is\n\n$$\nf({x}_{1},{x}_{2})=\\frac{1}{2\\pi \\sqrt{|Σ|}}\\exp (-\\frac{1}{2}(X-\\mu )^{T}\\,{Σ}^{-1}\\,(X-\\mu ))\n$$\n\nand $|Σ|$ denotes the determinant of $Σ.$\n\nUsing the given values of ${\\sigma }_{1}^{2},{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}$, and ${\\sigma }_{12},$ our covariance matrix is\n\n$$\nΣ=\\begin{bmatrix}10 & 5 \\\\ 5 & 5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n|Σ|=10\\cdot 5-5\\cdot 5=25.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this value into our expression for the joint PDF, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{1}{2\\pi \\sqrt{25}}\\exp (-\\frac{1}{2}(x-\\mu )^{T}\\,{Σ}^{-1}\\,(x-\\mu )) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10\\pi }\\exp (-\\frac{1}{2}(x-\\mu )^{T}\\,{Σ}^{-1}\\,(x-\\mu )).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $k=10\\pi .$"] [:db/cas 17592186092580 :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $X=[{X}_{1},{X}_{2}]^{T}$ is a bivariate normal random vector with mean vector $\\mu $ and covariance matrix $Σ,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu =\\begin{bmatrix}{\\mu }_{1} \\\\ {\\mu }_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,Σ=\\begin{bmatrix}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2} & {\\sigma }_{12} \\\\ {\\sigma }_{21} & {\\sigma }_{2}^{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthen the random variable $Y,$ defined as\n\n$$\nY=a{X}_{1}+b{X}_{2}\n$$\n\nis a normally distributed random variable for all values of $a$ and $b,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}E[Y] & =a{\\mu }_{1}+b{\\mu }_{2}, \\\\ Var[Y] & ={a}^{2}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}+{b}^{2}{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}+2ab{\\sigma }_{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have ${\\mu }_{1}=0$ and ${\\mu }_{2}=1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y] & =\\text{E}[{X}_{1}-2{X}_{2}] \\\\  & =\\text{E}[{X}_{1}]-2\\text{E}[{X}_{2}] \\\\  & ={\\mu }_{1}-2\\cdot {\\mu }_{2} \\\\  & =0-2\\cdot (1) \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, we have ${\\sigma }_{1}^{2}=1,{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}=4,$ and ${\\sigma }_{12}={\\sigma }_{21}=1.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Var[Y] & =Var[{X}_{1}-2{X}_{2}] \\\\  & ={1}^{2}\\cdot 1+(-2)^{2}\\cdot 4+2\\cdot 1\\cdot (-2)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =1+16-4 \\\\  & =13.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\nY∼N(-2,13).\n$$\n\nNow, we need to compute $P(Y\\ge 1).$ First, transform $Y$ into a standard normal random variable $Z$ by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y\\ge 1) & =P(Z\\ge \\frac{1-(-2)}{\\sqrt{13}}) \\\\  & =P(Z\\ge 0.832)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we express the required probability in terms of $Φ(z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Z\\ge 0.83) & =1-P(Z<0.83) \\\\  & =1-P(Z\\le 0.83) \\\\  & =1-Φ(0.83)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we know that\n\n$$\nΦ(0.83)=0.7967.\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Z\\ge 0.83) & =1-Φ(0.83) \\\\  & =1-0.7967 \\\\  & =0.2033.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nP(Y\\ge 1)=0.2033.\n$$" "Recall that if $X=[{X}_{1},{X}_{2}]^{T}$ is a bivariate normal random vector with mean vector $\\mu $ and covariance matrix $Σ,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu =\\begin{bmatrix}{\\mu }_{1} \\\\ {\\mu }_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,Σ=\\begin{bmatrix}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2} & {\\sigma }_{12} \\\\ {\\sigma }_{21} & {\\sigma }_{2}^{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthen the random variable $Y,$ defined as\n\n$$\nY=a{X}_{1}+b{X}_{2}\n$$\n\nis a normally distributed random variable for all values of $a$ and $b,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}E[Y] & =a{\\mu }_{1}+b{\\mu }_{2}, \\\\ \\operatorname{Var} [Y] & ={a}^{2}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}+{b}^{2}{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}+2ab{\\sigma }_{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have ${\\mu }_{1}=0$ and ${\\mu }_{2}=1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y] & =\\text{E}[{X}_{1}-2{X}_{2}] \\\\  & =\\text{E}[{X}_{1}]-2\\text{E}[{X}_{2}] \\\\  & ={\\mu }_{1}-2\\cdot {\\mu }_{2} \\\\  & =0-2\\cdot (1) \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, we have ${\\sigma }_{1}^{2}=1,{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}=4,$ and ${\\sigma }_{12}={\\sigma }_{21}=1.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{Var} [Y] & =\\operatorname{Var} [{X}_{1}-2{X}_{2}] \\\\  & ={1}^{2}\\cdot 1+(-2)^{2}\\cdot 4+2\\cdot 1\\cdot (-2)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =1+16-4 \\\\  & =13.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\nY∼N(-2,13).\n$$\n\nNow, we need to compute $P(Y\\ge 1).$ First, transform $Y$ into a standard normal random variable $Z$ by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y\\ge 1) & =P(Z\\ge \\frac{1-(-2)}{\\sqrt{13}}) \\\\  & =P(Z\\ge 0.832)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we express the required probability in terms of $Φ(z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Z\\ge 0.83) & =1-P(Z<0.83) \\\\  & =1-P(Z\\le 0.83) \\\\  & =1-Φ(0.83)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we know that\n\n$$\nΦ(0.83)=0.7967.\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Z\\ge 0.83) & =1-Φ(0.83) \\\\  & =1-0.7967 \\\\  & =0.2033.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nP(Y\\ge 1)=0.2033.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186092581 :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $X=[{X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{k}]^{T}$ is a multivariate normal random vector with mean vector $\\mu $ and covariance matrix $Σ,$ then the random variable $Y,$ defined as\n\n$$\nY={c}^{T}X={\\sum }_{i=1}^{k}{c}_{i}{X}_{i}\n$$\n\nis a normally distributed random variable, where $c$ is any $k\\times 1$ column vector, and  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}E[Y] & ={c}^{T}\\mu , \\\\ Var[Y] & ={c}^{T}Σc.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, since we wish to know the distribution of $Y={X}_{1}+2{X}_{2}-{X}_{3},$ we have \n\n$$\nc=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, for the mean of $Y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y] & ={c}^{T}\\mu  \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =3+2-2 \\\\  & =3,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand for the variance of $Y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[Y] & ={c}^{T}Σc \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =3+10-1 \\\\  & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\nY∼N(3,12).\n$$\n\nNow, we need to compute $P(Y<1).$ First, transform $Y$ into a standard normal random variable $Z$ by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y<1) & =P(Z<\\frac{1-3}{\\sqrt{12}}) \\\\  & =P(Z<-0.58)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we express the required probability in terms of $Φ(z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Z<-0.58) & =P(Z\\le -0.58) \\\\  & =Φ(-0.58).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we know that\n\n$$\nΦ(0.58)=0.7190.\n$$\n\nBy the symmetry of the normal distribution, we have\n\n$$\nΦ(-0.58)=1-Φ(0.58)=1-0.7190=0.2810.\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Z<-0.58)=0.2810.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nP(Y<1)=0.2810.\n$$" "Recall that if $X=[{X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{k}]^{T}$ is a multivariate normal random vector with mean vector $\\mu $ and covariance matrix $Σ,$ then the random variable $Y,$ defined as\n\n$$\nY={c}^{T}X={\\sum }_{i=1}^{k}{c}_{i}{X}_{i}\n$$\n\nis a normally distributed random variable, where $c$ is any $k\\times 1$ column vector, and  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}E[Y] & ={c}^{T}\\mu , \\\\ \\operatorname{Var} [Y] & ={c}^{T}Σc.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, since we wish to know the distribution of $Y={X}_{1}+2{X}_{2}-{X}_{3},$ we have \n\n$$\nc=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, for the mean of $Y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y] & ={c}^{T}\\mu  \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =3+2-2 \\\\  & =3,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand for the variance of $Y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[Y] & ={c}^{T}Σc \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =3+10-1 \\\\  & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\nY∼N(3,12).\n$$\n\nNow, we need to compute $P(Y<1).$ First, transform $Y$ into a standard normal random variable $Z$ by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y<1) & =P(Z<\\frac{1-3}{\\sqrt{12}}) \\\\  & =P(Z<-0.58)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we express the required probability in terms of $Φ(z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Z<-0.58) & =P(Z\\le -0.58) \\\\  & =Φ(-0.58).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we know that\n\n$$\nΦ(0.58)=0.7190.\n$$\n\nBy the symmetry of the normal distribution, we have\n\n$$\nΦ(-0.58)=1-Φ(0.58)=1-0.7190=0.2810.\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Z<-0.58)=0.2810.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nP(Y<1)=0.2810.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186092602 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the indicator functions of $\\overset{―}{A},$ $A\\cap B,$ and $A\\cup B$ have the following properties:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{1}_{\\overset{―}{A}}\\,(x) & =1-{1}_{A}(x) \\\\ {1}_{A\\,\\cap \\,B}\\,(x) & =min\\{{1}_{A}(x),{1}_{B}(x)\\} \\\\ {1}_{A\\,\\cup \\,B}\\,(x) & =max\\{{1}_{A}(x),{1}_{B}(x)\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement ${1}_{A\\,\\cap \\,B}\\,(5)=1$ is false while statement ${1}_{A\\,\\cap \\,B}\\,(5)=0$ is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{1}_{A\\,\\cap \\,B}\\,(5) & =min\\{{1}_{A}(5),{1}_{B}(5)\\} \\\\  & =min\\{1,0\\} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement $5\\in A\\cap B$ is false. Since ${1}_{A\\,\\cap \\,B}\\,(5)=0,$ we conclude that $5$ does not belong to $A\\cap B.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${1}_{A\\,\\cap \\,B}\\,(5)=0$ only." "Recall that the indicator functions of $\\overset{―}{A},$ $A\\cap B,$ and $A\\cup B$ have the following properties:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{1}_{\\overset{―}{A}}\\,(x) & =1-{1}_{A}(x) \\\\ {1}_{A\\,\\cap \\,B}\\,(x) & =\\min \\{{1}_{A}(x),{1}_{B}(x)\\} \\\\ {1}_{A\\,\\cup \\,B}\\,(x) & =\\max \\{{1}_{A}(x),{1}_{B}(x)\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement ${1}_{A\\,\\cap \\,B}\\,(5)=1$ is false while statement ${1}_{A\\,\\cap \\,B}\\,(5)=0$ is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{1}_{A\\,\\cap \\,B}\\,(5) & =\\min \\{{1}_{A}(5),{1}_{B}(5)\\} \\\\  & =\\min \\{1,0\\} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement $5\\in A\\cap B$ is false. Since ${1}_{A\\,\\cap \\,B}\\,(5)=0,$ we conclude that $5$ does not belong to $A\\cap B.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${1}_{A\\,\\cap \\,B}\\,(5)=0$ only."] [:db/cas 17592186092604 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the indicator functions of $\\overset{―}{A},$ $A\\cap B,$ and $A\\cup B$ have the following properties:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{1}_{\\overset{―}{A}}\\,(x) & =1-{1}_{A}(x) \\\\ {1}_{A\\,\\cap \\,B}\\,(x) & =min\\{{1}_{A}(x),{1}_{B}(x)\\} \\\\ {1}_{A\\,\\cup \\,B}\\,(x) & =max\\{{1}_{A}(x),{1}_{B}(x)\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true while statement II is false. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{1}_{A\\,\\cup \\,B}\\,(5) & =max\\{{1}_{A}(5),{1}_{B}(5)\\} \\\\  & =max\\{1,0\\} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since ${1}_{A\\,\\cup \\,B}\\,(5)=1,$ we conclude that $5$ belongs to $A\\cup B.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" "Recall that the indicator functions of $\\overset{―}{A},$ $A\\cap B,$ and $A\\cup B$ have the following properties:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{1}_{\\overset{―}{A}}\\,(x) & =1-{1}_{A}(x) \\\\ {1}_{A\\,\\cap \\,B}\\,(x) & =\\min \\{{1}_{A}(x),{1}_{B}(x)\\} \\\\ {1}_{A\\,\\cup \\,B}\\,(x) & =\\max \\{{1}_{A}(x),{1}_{B}(x)\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true while statement II is false. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{1}_{A\\,\\cup \\,B}\\,(5) & =\\max \\{{1}_{A}(5),{1}_{B}(5)\\} \\\\  & =\\max \\{1,0\\} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since ${1}_{A\\,\\cup \\,B}\\,(5)=1,$ we conclude that $5$ belongs to $A\\cup B.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""] [:db/cas 17592186092628 :question/worked-solution "The area bounded by a polar curve is given by\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}(\\theta )\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\nWhen $r(\\theta )=2+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $, ${\\theta }_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and ${\\theta }_{2}=\\pi $, this gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }(2+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }(4+8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +4{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{2}{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }(1+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2(\\theta \\,{|}_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,{|}_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }+\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ))\\,\\text{d}\\theta ) \\\\  & =2(\\theta \\,{|}_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,{|}_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }+\\frac{1}{2}(\\theta \\,{|}_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )}{2}\\,{|}_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi })) \\\\  & =2(\\theta \\,{|}_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,{|}_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }+\\frac{1}{2}\\theta \\,{|}_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )}{4}\\,{|}_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }) \\\\  & =3\\theta \\,{|}_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,{|}_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )}{2}\\,{|}_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =3(\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{2})+4(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right))+\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )) \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{2}-4+\\frac{1}{2}(0) \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi -8}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The area bounded by a polar curve is given by\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}(\\theta )\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\nWhen $r(\\theta )=2+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $, ${\\theta }_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and ${\\theta }_{2}=\\pi $, this gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }(2+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }(4+8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +4{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{2}{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }(1+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2(\\theta \\,{|}_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,{|}_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }+\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ))\\,\\text{d}\\theta ) \\\\  & =2(\\theta \\,{|}_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,{|}_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }+\\frac{1}{2}(\\theta \\,{|}_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )}{2}\\,{|}_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi })) \\\\  & =2(\\theta \\,{|}_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,{|}_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }+\\frac{1}{2}\\theta \\,{|}_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )}{4}\\,{|}_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }) \\\\  & =3\\theta \\,{|}_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,{|}_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )}{2}\\,{|}_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =3(\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{2})+4(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right))+\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )) \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{2}-4+\\frac{1}{2}(0) \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi -8}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186092689 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the matrix $\\,A-\\lambda I:$\n﻿﻿﻿\n\n$$\nA-\\lambda I=\\begin{bmatrix}2-\\lambda  & 2 \\\\ -8 & -6-\\lambda \\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThen, the characteristic equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}2-\\lambda  & 2 \\\\ -8 & -6-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )(-6-\\lambda )-(-8)\\cdot 2 & =0 \\\\ -12-2\\lambda +6\\lambda +{\\lambda }^{2}+16 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+4\\lambda +4 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we find the matrix $\\,A-\\lambda I:$\n﻿﻿﻿\n\n$$\nA-\\lambda I=\\begin{bmatrix}2-\\lambda  & 2 \\\\ -8 & -6-\\lambda \\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThen, the characteristic equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\det (A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}2-\\lambda  & 2 \\\\ -8 & -6-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )(-6-\\lambda )-(-8)\\cdot 2 & =0 \\\\ -12-2\\lambda +6\\lambda +{\\lambda }^{2}+16 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+4\\lambda +4 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186092691 :question/worked-solution "Let's start by writing the matrix $Q-\\lambda I:$\n﻿﻿﻿\n\n$$\nQ-\\lambda I=\\begin{bmatrix}-6-\\lambda  & -3 \\\\ 2 & -10-\\lambda \\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, let's find and solve the characteristic equation $det(Q-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(Q-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}-6-\\lambda  & -3 \\\\ 2 & -10-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (-6-\\lambda )(-10-\\lambda )-(-3)\\cdot 2 & =0 \\\\ 60+6\\lambda +10\\lambda +{\\lambda }^{2}+6 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+16\\lambda +66 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the discriminant of the quadratic equation above, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & ={16}^{2}-4\\cdot 1\\cdot 66 \\\\  & =256-264 \\\\  & =-8 \\\\  & <0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the characteristic equation does not have real solutions, and therefore, $Q$ does not have real eigenvalues." "Let's start by writing the matrix $Q-\\lambda I:$\n﻿﻿﻿\n\n$$\nQ-\\lambda I=\\begin{bmatrix}-6-\\lambda  & -3 \\\\ 2 & -10-\\lambda \\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, let's find and solve the characteristic equation $\\det (Q-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\det (Q-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}-6-\\lambda  & -3 \\\\ 2 & -10-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (-6-\\lambda )(-10-\\lambda )-(-3)\\cdot 2 & =0 \\\\ 60+6\\lambda +10\\lambda +{\\lambda }^{2}+6 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+16\\lambda +66 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the discriminant of the quadratic equation above, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & ={16}^{2}-4\\cdot 1\\cdot 66 \\\\  & =256-264 \\\\  & =-8 \\\\  & <0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the characteristic equation does not have real solutions, and therefore, $Q$ does not have real eigenvalues."] [:db/cas 17592186092709 :question/worked-solution "First, let's rewrite the system in the correct order:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x+z &=3 \\\\ 3x+5y-2z &=2 \\\\ 2x-2y+3z &=1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNext, we  write the system in the form $Ax=b$, where $A$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix and $x$ and $b$ are $3\\times 1$ column vectors. We can do this as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 5 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, the solution to $Ax=b$ is given by $x={A}^{-1}b.$ So, we compute ${A}^{-1}$ as follows:\n\n*Step 1:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 5 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\begin{vmatrix}5 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{vmatrix}-0+\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 5 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2(15-4)+(-6-10) \\\\  & =22-16 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\n*Step 2:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\begin{vmatrix}5 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}3 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}3 & 5 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 5 & -2\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 5\\end{vmatrix}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}11 & 13 & -16 \\\\ 2 & 4 & -4 \\\\ -5 & -7 & 10\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*Step 3:*\n\n$$\nC=\\begin{bmatrix}11 & -13 & -16 \\\\ -2 & 4 & 4 \\\\ -5 & 7 & 10\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n*Step 4:*\n\n$$\n{C}^{T}=\\begin{bmatrix}11 & -2 & -5 \\\\ -13 & 4 & 7 \\\\ -16 & 4 & 10\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n*Step 5:*\n\n$$\n{A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T}=\\frac{1}{6}\\begin{bmatrix}11 & -2 & -5 \\\\ -13 & 4 & 7 \\\\ -16 & 4 & 10\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the solution to the given system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\begin{bmatrix}11 & -2 & -5 \\\\ -13 & 4 & 7 \\\\ -16 & 4 & 10\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\begin{bmatrix}24 \\\\ -24 \\\\ -30\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, $x+y+z=4-4-5=-5.$" "First, let's rewrite the system in the correct order:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x+z &=3 \\\\ 3x+5y-2z &=2 \\\\ 2x-2y+3z &=1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNext, we  write the system in the form $Ax=b$, where $A$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix and $x$ and $b$ are $3\\times 1$ column vectors. We can do this as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 5 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, the solution to $Ax=b$ is given by $x={A}^{-1}b.$ So, we compute ${A}^{-1}$ as follows:\n\n*Step 1:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\det (A) & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 5 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\begin{vmatrix}5 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{vmatrix}-0+\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 5 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2(15-4)+(-6-10) \\\\  & =22-16 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\n*Step 2:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\begin{vmatrix}5 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}3 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}3 & 5 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 5 & -2\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 5\\end{vmatrix}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}11 & 13 & -16 \\\\ 2 & 4 & -4 \\\\ -5 & -7 & 10\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*Step 3:*\n\n$$\nC=\\begin{bmatrix}11 & -13 & -16 \\\\ -2 & 4 & 4 \\\\ -5 & 7 & 10\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n*Step 4:*\n\n$$\n{C}^{T}=\\begin{bmatrix}11 & -2 & -5 \\\\ -13 & 4 & 7 \\\\ -16 & 4 & 10\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n*Step 5:*\n\n$$\n{A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{\\det (A)}{C}^{T}=\\frac{1}{6}\\begin{bmatrix}11 & -2 & -5 \\\\ -13 & 4 & 7 \\\\ -16 & 4 & 10\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the solution to the given system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\begin{bmatrix}11 & -2 & -5 \\\\ -13 & 4 & 7 \\\\ -16 & 4 & 10\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\begin{bmatrix}24 \\\\ -24 \\\\ -30\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, $x+y+z=4-4-5=-5.$"] [:db/cas 17592186092732 :question/problem "Calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for $y={log}_{3}⁡(5x-2).$" "Calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for $y={\\log }_{3}⁡(5x-2).$"] [:db/cas 17592186092732 :question/worked-solution "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny={log}_{3}⁡(u),\\,u=5x-2.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=\\frac{1}{u\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=5.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{u\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)}\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(5x-2)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)}\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{(5x-2)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny={\\log }_{3}⁡(u),\\,u=5x-2.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=\\frac{1}{u\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=5.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{u\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)}\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(5x-2)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)}\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{(5x-2)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186092764 :question/worked-solution "The arc length $L$ of a polar curve $r=r(\\theta )$ is given by\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\sqrt{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ))^{2}+(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ))^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\sqrt{{cos}^{2}⁡(2\\theta )+4{sin}^{2}⁡(2\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The arc length $L$ of a polar curve $r=r(\\theta )$ is given by\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\sqrt{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ))^{2}+(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ))^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\sqrt{{\\cos }^{2}⁡(2\\theta )+4{\\sin }^{2}⁡(2\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186092765 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the values of $\\theta $ for which the two curves intersect:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe have two solutions: \n\n$\\,$ $\\theta =-\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3}$    \n\nTo determine the inner function and the outer, let's draw an arrow from the origin that passes through the shaded region.\n\n![](images/d8/d802b734480b75e6e8e16f49c84fb4685cbcd6ddbdc33c8c40b29de7dc7c3824.png)\n\nThe arrow\n\n- *enters* the region through the inner function $r=1,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the outer function $r=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$\n\nTo find the area, we use the formula\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}([\\text{outer}]^{2}-[\\text{inner}]^{2})\\,\\text{d}\\theta ,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere\n\n$\\,$ ${\\theta }_{1}=-\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\,$ and $\\,{\\theta }_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$    \n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /3}^{\\pi /3}(4{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -1))\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /3}^{\\pi /3}(1+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ))\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we find the values of $\\theta $ for which the two curves intersect:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe have two solutions: \n\n$\\,$ $\\theta =-\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3}$    \n\nTo determine the inner function and the outer, let's draw an arrow from the origin that passes through the shaded region.\n\n![](images/d8/d802b734480b75e6e8e16f49c84fb4685cbcd6ddbdc33c8c40b29de7dc7c3824.png)\n\nThe arrow\n\n- *enters* the region through the inner function $r=1,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the outer function $r=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$\n\nTo find the area, we use the formula\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}([\\text{outer}]^{2}-[\\text{inner}]^{2})\\,\\text{d}\\theta ,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere\n\n$\\,$ ${\\theta }_{1}=-\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\,$ and $\\,{\\theta }_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$    \n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /3}^{\\pi /3}(4{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta -1))\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /3}^{\\pi /3}(1+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ))\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186092898 :question/worked-solution "The area scale factor of a linear transformation $T$ with standard matrix $T$ is equal to $|det(T)|.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|det(T)| & =|1\\cdot (-2)-5\\cdot 0| \\\\  & =|-2-0| \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The area scale factor of a linear transformation $T$ with standard matrix $T$ is equal to $|\\det (T)|.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\det (T)| & =|1\\cdot (-2)-5\\cdot 0| \\\\  & =|-2-0| \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186092899 :question/worked-solution "First, let's sketch our region:\n\n![](images/3c/3cbf4ce4422e043cedb73e9b10bba11e9cb1e64352ff182eff8aaa0046c4e8f3.png)\n\nRecall that for any linear transformation $T,$ we have the following connection between the area of $D$ and the area of the image $T(D)$ under the action of $T:$\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(T(D))=|det(T)|\\cdot \\text{Area}(D).\n$$\n\nFrom the diagram, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D)=1\\cdot 1=1.\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant of $T,$ we get \n\n$$\ndet(T)=7\\cdot (-1)-0\\cdot 0=-7.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(T(D)) & =|det(T)|\\cdot \\text{Area}(D) \\\\  & =|-7|\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, let's sketch our region:\n\n![](images/3c/3cbf4ce4422e043cedb73e9b10bba11e9cb1e64352ff182eff8aaa0046c4e8f3.png)\n\nRecall that for any linear transformation $T,$ we have the following connection between the area of $D$ and the area of the image $T(D)$ under the action of $T:$\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(T(D))=|\\det (T)|\\cdot \\text{Area}(D).\n$$\n\nFrom the diagram, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D)=1\\cdot 1=1.\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant of $T,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\det (T)=7\\cdot (-1)-0\\cdot 0=-7.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(T(D)) & =|\\det (T)|\\cdot \\text{Area}(D) \\\\  & =|-7|\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186092900 :question/worked-solution "The area scale factor of a linear transformation $T$ with standard matrix $T$ is equal to $|det(T)|.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|det(T)| & =|7\\cdot 5-1\\cdot 5| \\\\  & =|35-5| \\\\  & =30.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The area scale factor of a linear transformation $T$ with standard matrix $T$ is equal to $|\\det (T)|.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\det (T)| & =|7\\cdot 5-1\\cdot 5| \\\\  & =|35-5| \\\\  & =30.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186092901 :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any linear transformation $T,$ we have the following connection between the area of $D$ and the area of the image $T(D)$ under the action of $T:$\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(T(D))=|det(T)|\\cdot \\text{Area}(D).\n$$\n\nFrom the diagram, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D)=\\pi \\cdot {2}^{2}=4\\pi .\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant of $T,$ we get \n\n$$\ndet(T)=(-2)\\cdot 5-(-1)\\cdot 0=-10.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(T(D)) & =|det(T)|\\cdot \\text{Area}(D) \\\\  & =|-10|\\cdot 4\\pi  \\\\  & =40\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Recall that for any linear transformation $T,$ we have the following connection between the area of $D$ and the area of the image $T(D)$ under the action of $T:$\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(T(D))=|\\det (T)|\\cdot \\text{Area}(D).\n$$\n\nFrom the diagram, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D)=\\pi \\cdot {2}^{2}=4\\pi .\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant of $T,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\det (T)=(-2)\\cdot 5-(-1)\\cdot 0=-10.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(T(D)) & =|\\det (T)|\\cdot \\text{Area}(D) \\\\  & =|-10|\\cdot 4\\pi  \\\\  & =40\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186092902 :question/worked-solution "Let's start by writing the matrix $A-\\lambda I:$\n﻿﻿﻿\n\n$$\nA-\\lambda I=\\begin{bmatrix}-4-\\lambda  & 2 \\\\ 6 & 7-\\lambda \\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, let's find and solve the characteristic equation $det(A-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}-4-\\lambda  & 2 \\\\ 6 & 7-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (-4-\\lambda )(7-\\lambda )-2\\cdot 6 & =0 \\\\ -28-7\\lambda +4\\lambda +{\\lambda }^{2}-12 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-3\\lambda -40 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the quadratic formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lambda  & =\\frac{3\\pm \\sqrt{(-3)^{2}+4\\cdot 1\\cdot 40}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pm \\sqrt{169}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pm 13}{2} \\\\  & =8,-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues are $\\lambda =8$ and $\\lambda =-5.$" "Let's start by writing the matrix $A-\\lambda I:$\n﻿﻿﻿\n\n$$\nA-\\lambda I=\\begin{bmatrix}-4-\\lambda  & 2 \\\\ 6 & 7-\\lambda \\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, let's find and solve the characteristic equation $\\det (A-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\det (A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}-4-\\lambda  & 2 \\\\ 6 & 7-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (-4-\\lambda )(7-\\lambda )-2\\cdot 6 & =0 \\\\ -28-7\\lambda +4\\lambda +{\\lambda }^{2}-12 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-3\\lambda -40 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the quadratic formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lambda  & =\\frac{3\\pm \\sqrt{(-3)^{2}+4\\cdot 1\\cdot 40}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pm \\sqrt{169}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pm 13}{2} \\\\  & =8,-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues are $\\lambda =8$ and $\\lambda =-5.$"] [:db/cas 17592186092903 :question/worked-solution "Let's start by writing the matrix $A-\\lambda I:$\n﻿﻿﻿\n\n$$\nA-\\lambda I=\\begin{bmatrix}3-\\lambda  & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1-\\lambda \\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, let's find and solve the characteristic equation $det(A-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}3-\\lambda  & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (3-\\lambda )(1-\\lambda )+1\\cdot 1 & =0 \\\\ 3-3\\lambda -\\lambda +{\\lambda }^{2}+1 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda +4 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -2)^{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the matrix $A$ has only one eigenvalue $\\lambda =2,$ which is a double root of the characteristic equation." "Let's start by writing the matrix $A-\\lambda I:$\n﻿﻿﻿\n\n$$\nA-\\lambda I=\\begin{bmatrix}3-\\lambda  & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1-\\lambda \\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, let's find and solve the characteristic equation $\\det (A-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\det (A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}3-\\lambda  & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (3-\\lambda )(1-\\lambda )+1\\cdot 1 & =0 \\\\ 3-3\\lambda -\\lambda +{\\lambda }^{2}+1 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda +4 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -2)^{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the matrix $A$ has only one eigenvalue $\\lambda =2,$ which is a double root of the characteristic equation."] [:db/cas 17592186092922 :question/worked-solution "The arc length $L$ of a polar curve $r=r(\\theta )$ is given by\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{5\\pi /6}\\sqrt{(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}+{(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{5\\pi /6}\\sqrt{4({sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{5\\pi /6}1\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\theta {|}_{\\pi /2}^{5\\pi /6} \\\\  & =2(\\frac{5\\pi }{6}-\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The arc length $L$ of a polar curve $r=r(\\theta )$ is given by\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{5\\pi /6}\\sqrt{(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}+{(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{5\\pi /6}\\sqrt{4({\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta +{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{5\\pi /6}1\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\theta {|}_{\\pi /2}^{5\\pi /6} \\\\  & =2(\\frac{5\\pi }{6}-\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186092924 :question/problem "Given that $w \\ge 0$, which of the following is equivalent to $arsinh \\sqrt{w}$?" "Given that $w \\ge 0$, which of the following is equivalent to $\\operatorname{arsinh}  \\sqrt{w}$?"] [:db/cas 17592186092926 :question/problem "Given that $u ∈ (-7, 7)$, which of the following is equivalent to $artanh \\left(-\\frac{u}{7}\\right)$?" "Given that $u ∈ (-7, 7)$, which of the following is equivalent to $\\operatorname{artanh}  \\left(-\\frac{u}{7}\\right)$?"] [:db/cas 17592186092927 :question/problem "What is $arcosh \\sqrt{2}$?" "What is $\\operatorname{arcosh}  \\sqrt{2}$?"] [:db/cas 17592186092928 :question/problem "What is $arsinh (-1)$?" "What is $\\operatorname{arsinh}  (-1)$?"] [:db/cas 17592186092929 :question/problem "What is $artanh \\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)$?" "What is $\\operatorname{artanh}  \\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)$?"] [:db/cas 17592186092935 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the values of $\\theta $ for which the two curves intersect:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 2+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us two solutions for $$\n\\displaystyle \\theta \\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{2}]:\n$$\n\n$$\n\\theta =-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\n$$\n\nTo determine the inner function and the outer, let's draw an arrow from the origin that passes through the shaded region.\n\n![](images/d0/d0ebb3b99f66e8c1af93f1a15ff4284f5fb9a83fb4d91bad1027e18c8b447939.png)\n\nThe arrow\n\n- *enters* the region through the inner function $r=1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the outer function $r=2(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ).$\n\nTo find the area, we use the formula\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}([\\text{outer}]^{2}-[\\text{inner}]^{2})\\,\\text{d}\\theta ,\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$\\,$ ${\\theta }_{1}=-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,$ and $\\,{\\theta }_{2}=\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}((2(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ))^{2}-(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2})\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}(1+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}(\\theta {|}_{-\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {|}_{-\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}+\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ))\\,\\text{d}\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}((\\frac{3\\pi }{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{2})-2(0-0)+\\frac{1}{2}\\theta {|}_{-\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ){|}_{-\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}(2\\pi +\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{2})-\\frac{1}{4}(0-0)) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}(2\\pi +\\pi ) \\\\  & =\\frac{9\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we find the values of $\\theta $ for which the two curves intersect:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 2+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us two solutions for $$\n\\displaystyle \\theta \\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{2}]:\n$$\n\n$$\n\\theta =-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\n$$\n\nTo determine the inner function and the outer, let's draw an arrow from the origin that passes through the shaded region.\n\n![](images/d0/d0ebb3b99f66e8c1af93f1a15ff4284f5fb9a83fb4d91bad1027e18c8b447939.png)\n\nThe arrow\n\n- *enters* the region through the inner function $r=1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the outer function $r=2(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ).$\n\nTo find the area, we use the formula\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}([\\text{outer}]^{2}-[\\text{inner}]^{2})\\,\\text{d}\\theta ,\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$\\,$ ${\\theta }_{1}=-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,$ and $\\,{\\theta }_{2}=\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}((2(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ))^{2}-(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2})\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}(1+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}(\\theta {|}_{-\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {|}_{-\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}+\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ))\\,\\text{d}\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}((\\frac{3\\pi }{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{2})-2(0-0)+\\frac{1}{2}\\theta {|}_{-\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ){|}_{-\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}(2\\pi +\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{2})-\\frac{1}{4}(0-0)) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}(2\\pi +\\pi ) \\\\  & =\\frac{9\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186092937 :question/worked-solution "We organize the system so that it’s in the correct order first:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+2y+3z &=-1 \\\\ y+4z &=1 \\\\ 5x+6y &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe wish to write this system in the form $Ax=b$, where $A$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix and $x$ and $b$ are $3\\times 1$ column vectors. We can do this as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 5 & 6 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, the solution to $Ax=b$ is given by $x={A}^{-1}b.$ So, we compute ${A}^{-1}$ as follows:\n\n*Step 1:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 5 & 6 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 4 \\\\ 6 & 0\\end{vmatrix}-2\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 4 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{vmatrix}+3\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 6\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1(-24)-2(-20)+3(-5) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*Step 2:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 4 \\\\ 6 & 0\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 4 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 6\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 6 & 0\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 5 & 6\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-24 & -20 & -5 \\\\ -18 & -15 & -4 \\\\ 5 & 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*Step 3:*\n\n$$\nC=\\begin{bmatrix}-24 & 20 & -5 \\\\ 18 & -15 & 4 \\\\ 5 & -4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n*Step 4:*\n\n$$\n{C}^{T}=\\begin{bmatrix}-24 & 18 & 5 \\\\ 20 & -15 & -4 \\\\ -5 & 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n*Step 5:*\n\n$$\n{A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T}=\\frac{1}{1}\\begin{bmatrix}-24 & 18 & 5 \\\\ 20 & -15 & -4 \\\\ -5 & 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nHence\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-24 & 18 & 5 \\\\ 20 & -15 & -4 \\\\ -5 & 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}42 \\\\ -35 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence the solution is $x=42,y=-35,z=9.$" "We organize the system so that it’s in the correct order first:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+2y+3z &=-1 \\\\ y+4z &=1 \\\\ 5x+6y &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe wish to write this system in the form $Ax=b$, where $A$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix and $x$ and $b$ are $3\\times 1$ column vectors. We can do this as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 5 & 6 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, the solution to $Ax=b$ is given by $x={A}^{-1}b.$ So, we compute ${A}^{-1}$ as follows:\n\n*Step 1:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\det (A) & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 5 & 6 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 4 \\\\ 6 & 0\\end{vmatrix}-2\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 4 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{vmatrix}+3\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 6\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1(-24)-2(-20)+3(-5) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*Step 2:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 4 \\\\ 6 & 0\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 4 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 6\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 6 & 0\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 5 & 6\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-24 & -20 & -5 \\\\ -18 & -15 & -4 \\\\ 5 & 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*Step 3:*\n\n$$\nC=\\begin{bmatrix}-24 & 20 & -5 \\\\ 18 & -15 & 4 \\\\ 5 & -4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n*Step 4:*\n\n$$\n{C}^{T}=\\begin{bmatrix}-24 & 18 & 5 \\\\ 20 & -15 & -4 \\\\ -5 & 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n*Step 5:*\n\n$$\n{A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{\\det (A)}{C}^{T}=\\frac{1}{1}\\begin{bmatrix}-24 & 18 & 5 \\\\ 20 & -15 & -4 \\\\ -5 & 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nHence\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-24 & 18 & 5 \\\\ 20 & -15 & -4 \\\\ -5 & 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}42 \\\\ -35 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence the solution is $x=42,y=-35,z=9.$"] [:db/cas 17592186093352 :tutorial/content "Suppose we would like to find the arc length of the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=2\\cos{t}, \\quad y=2\\sin{t}\n$$\n\nfrom $t=0$ to $t=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2},$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/83/836757696669a0852812a63c8cafcf9ac3bddd73558f7b3e67c8427ec39c008a.png)\n\nIt can be shown that the arc length $L$ of a curve defined parametrically by\n\n$$\nx=x(t), \\quad y=y(t), \\qquad t\\in [t_1,t_2],\n$$\n\nis given by the formula\n\n$$\nL = \\int_{t_1}^{t_2} \\sqrt{\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}x}{\\textrm{d}t}\\right)^2+\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}t}\\right)^2}\\,\\textrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nThe intuition behind this formula is given at the end of this tutorial.\n\nAccording to the formula, the length of the blue arc is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t))}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t))}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}+{(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{4({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}2\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2t{|}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =2(\\frac{\\pi }{2}-0) \\\\  & =\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Suppose we would like to find the arc length of the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=2\\cos{t}, \\quad y=2\\sin{t}\n$$\n\nfrom $t=0$ to $t=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2},$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/83/836757696669a0852812a63c8cafcf9ac3bddd73558f7b3e67c8427ec39c008a.png)\n\nIt can be shown that the arc length $L$ of a curve defined parametrically by\n\n$$\nx=x(t), \\quad y=y(t), \\qquad t\\in [t_1,t_2],\n$$\n\nis given by the formula\n\n$$\nL = \\int_{t_1}^{t_2} \\sqrt{\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}x}{\\textrm{d}t}\\right)^2+\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}t}\\right)^2}\\,\\textrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nThe intuition behind this formula is given at the end of this tutorial.\n\nAccording to the formula, the length of the blue arc is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t))}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t))}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}+{(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{4({\\sin }^{2}⁡t+{\\cos }^{2}⁡t)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}2\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2t{|}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =2(\\frac{\\pi }{2}-0) \\\\  & =\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186093357 :tutorial/content "Suppose we want to find the area of the region bounded by the polar curve $r = 3\\cos\\theta$ and the rays $\\theta_1 = 0$ and $\\theta_2 = \\dfrac{\\pi}{2},$ as shown in blue in the picture below. Can we calculate this area using polar coordinates?\n\n![](images/2c/2c7e638a338aed2155ef5bf388be1f83a3a67d2296d5be09987b099a07edacbd.png)\n\nIn general, the area of the region bounded by a polar curve $r=r(\\theta)$ and the rays $\\theta=\\theta_1$ and $\\theta=\\theta_2$ is given by the formula\n\n$$\nA = \\dfrac{1}{2} \\int_{\\theta_1}^{\\theta_2} r^2 \\:\\textrm{d}\\theta.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the half-circle above can be obtained as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{1}{2}(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ))\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{4}(\\theta {|}_{0}^{\\pi /2}+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ){|}_{0}^{\\pi /2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{4}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}+0) \\\\  & =\\frac{9\\pi }{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Suppose we want to find the area of the region bounded by the polar curve $r = 3\\cos\\theta$ and the rays $\\theta_1 = 0$ and $\\theta_2 = \\dfrac{\\pi}{2},$ as shown in blue in the picture below. Can we calculate this area using polar coordinates?\n\n![](images/2c/2c7e638a338aed2155ef5bf388be1f83a3a67d2296d5be09987b099a07edacbd.png)\n\nIn general, the area of the region bounded by a polar curve $r=r(\\theta)$ and the rays $\\theta=\\theta_1$ and $\\theta=\\theta_2$ is given by the formula\n\n$$\nA = \\dfrac{1}{2} \\int_{\\theta_1}^{\\theta_2} r^2 \\:\\textrm{d}\\theta.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the half-circle above can be obtained as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{1}{2}(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ))\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{4}(\\theta {|}_{0}^{\\pi /2}+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ){|}_{0}^{\\pi /2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{4}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}+0) \\\\  & =\\frac{9\\pi }{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186093360 :tutorial/content "Consider the polar curves $r=2(1+\\cos\\theta)$ and $r=2.$ What is the area of the shaded region shown below?\n\n![](images/c0/c011bf3b40639e10ec151541d0892d5095b2d983b6ed03247824f06f225c10d9.png)\n\nFirst, we find the values of $\\theta$ for which the two curves intersect:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ) & =2 \\\\ 1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us two solutions for $$\n\\displaystyle \\theta \\in \\left[-\\dfrac{\\pi}{2},\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right],\n$$ namely\n\n$$\n\\theta = -\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}, \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\n$$\n\nTo find the area, we use the formula\n\n$$\nA = \\dfrac{1}{2} \\int_{\\theta_1}^{\\theta_2} \\Big( \\big[ {\\color{brown}\\text{outer curve}} \\big]^2 - \\big[ {\\color{blue}\\text{inner curve}} \\big]^2 \\Big) \\:\\textrm{d}\\theta,\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$\\qquad$ $\\theta_1=-\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}, \\quad$ and $\\quad \\theta_2=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}.$ \n\nTo determine the inner function and the outer, let's draw an arrow from the origin that passes through the shaded region.\n\n![](images/7b/7b77af383d0d05d00c91db364e17675dfd45445eb5ac8e2926f8bb90220bc0fe.png)\n\nThe arrow\n\n- *enters* the region through the inner function $r=2,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the outer function $r=2(1+\\cos\\theta).$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}({(2(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ))}^{2}-{2}^{2})\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}(4(1+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta )-4)\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Consider the polar curves $r=2(1+\\cos\\theta)$ and $r=2.$ What is the area of the shaded region shown below?\n\n![](images/c0/c011bf3b40639e10ec151541d0892d5095b2d983b6ed03247824f06f225c10d9.png)\n\nFirst, we find the values of $\\theta$ for which the two curves intersect:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ) & =2 \\\\ 1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us two solutions for $$\n\\displaystyle \\theta \\in \\left[-\\dfrac{\\pi}{2},\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right],\n$$ namely\n\n$$\n\\theta = -\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}, \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\n$$\n\nTo find the area, we use the formula\n\n$$\nA = \\dfrac{1}{2} \\int_{\\theta_1}^{\\theta_2} \\Big( \\big[ {\\color{brown}\\text{outer curve}} \\big]^2 - \\big[ {\\color{blue}\\text{inner curve}} \\big]^2 \\Big) \\:\\textrm{d}\\theta,\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$\\qquad$ $\\theta_1=-\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}, \\quad$ and $\\quad \\theta_2=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}.$ \n\nTo determine the inner function and the outer, let's draw an arrow from the origin that passes through the shaded region.\n\n![](images/7b/7b77af383d0d05d00c91db364e17675dfd45445eb5ac8e2926f8bb90220bc0fe.png)\n\nThe arrow\n\n- *enters* the region through the inner function $r=2,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the outer function $r=2(1+\\cos\\theta).$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}({(2(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ))}^{2}-{2}^{2})\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}(4(1+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta )-4)\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta )\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186093364 :tutorial/content "We've been using the following formula to compute the arc length bounded by a polar curve:\n\n$$\nL = \\int_{\\theta_1}^{\\theta_2} \\sqrt{ r^2 + \\left( \\frac{\\textrm{d}r}{\\textrm{d}\\theta} \\right)^2} \\:\\textrm{d}\\theta\n$$\n\nBut where does this formula come from? Let's start with the familiar formula for the arc length of a parametric curve:\n\n$$\nL = \\int_{t_1}^{t_2} \\sqrt{ \\left(\\frac{\\textrm{d}x}{\\textrm{d}t}\\right)^2 +\\left(\\frac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}t}\\right)^2 }\\textrm{d}t\n$$\n\nA polar curve is really just a parametric curve with parameter $\\theta$ and parametric equations given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the arc length formula for a parametric function with parameter $\\theta,$ we have\n\n$$\nL = \\int_{\\theta_1}^{\\theta_2} \\sqrt{ \\left(\\frac{\\textrm{d}x}{\\textrm{d}\\theta}\\right)^2 +\\left(\\frac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}\\theta}\\right)^2 }\\textrm{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $x = r \\cos \\theta,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +r\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLikewise, since $y=r\\sin \\theta,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +r\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our expression for $L,$ we reach the desired formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We've been using the following formula to compute the arc length bounded by a polar curve:\n\n$$\nL = \\int_{\\theta_1}^{\\theta_2} \\sqrt{ r^2 + \\left( \\frac{\\textrm{d}r}{\\textrm{d}\\theta} \\right)^2} \\:\\textrm{d}\\theta\n$$\n\nBut where does this formula come from? Let's start with the familiar formula for the arc length of a parametric curve:\n\n$$\nL = \\int_{t_1}^{t_2} \\sqrt{ \\left(\\frac{\\textrm{d}x}{\\textrm{d}t}\\right)^2 +\\left(\\frac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}t}\\right)^2 }\\textrm{d}t\n$$\n\nA polar curve is really just a parametric curve with parameter $\\theta$ and parametric equations given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the arc length formula for a parametric function with parameter $\\theta,$ we have\n\n$$\nL = \\int_{\\theta_1}^{\\theta_2} \\sqrt{ \\left(\\frac{\\textrm{d}x}{\\textrm{d}\\theta}\\right)^2 +\\left(\\frac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}\\theta}\\right)^2 }\\textrm{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $x = r \\cos \\theta,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +r\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLikewise, since $y=r\\sin \\theta,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +r\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our expression for $L,$ we reach the desired formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta +{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}({\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta +{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta )+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}({\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta +{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186093413 :tutorial/content "Remember that the\n\n$$\n\\textrm{cis}\\left(\\theta\\right)\n$$\n\nnotation is a compact notation for expressing complex numbers:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{cis}\\left(\\theta\\right) = \\cos(\\theta) + \\textrm{i} \\sin(\\theta)\n$$\n\nWe can write De Moivre's theorem in\n\n$$\n\\textrm{cis}\\left(\\theta\\right)\n$$\n\nnotation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n[\\cos (θ) + \\textrm{i}\\sin (θ)]^{n} &= \\cos (nθ) + \\textrm{i}\\sin (nθ) \\\\\n&= ⇓ \\mid ⇓ \\\\\n[cis(θ)]^{n} &= cis(nθ)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, to raise a complex number in the form\n\n$$\nz=r\\textrm{cis}\\left(\\theta\\right)\n$$\n\nto the power of $n$, we can use the identity\n\n$$\n\\left[r\\,\\textrm{cis}\\left(\\theta\\right)\\right]^n = r^n\\,\\textrm{cis}\\left(n\\theta\\right)\n$$" "Remember that the\n\n$$\n\\textrm{cis}\\left(\\theta\\right)\n$$\n\nnotation is a compact notation for expressing complex numbers:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{cis}\\left(\\theta\\right) = \\cos(\\theta) + \\textrm{i} \\sin(\\theta)\n$$\n\nWe can write De Moivre's theorem in\n\n$$\n\\textrm{cis}\\left(\\theta\\right)\n$$\n\nnotation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n[\\cos (θ) + \\textrm{i}\\sin (θ)]^{n} &= \\cos (nθ) + \\textrm{i}\\sin (nθ) \\\\\n&= ⇓ \\mid ⇓ \\\\\n[\\operatorname{cis} (θ)]^{n} &= \\operatorname{cis} (nθ)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, to raise a complex number in the form\n\n$$\nz=r\\textrm{cis}\\left(\\theta\\right)\n$$\n\nto the power of $n$, we can use the identity\n\n$$\n\\left[r\\,\\textrm{cis}\\left(\\theta\\right)\\right]^n = r^n\\,\\textrm{cis}\\left(n\\theta\\right)\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186093437 :tutorial/content "Consider the shaded region $D$ shown below.\n\n![](images/ca/cae19e62492782b42b69675ec0babd005407c7db8ce4dc6d77a14da06beedfc1.png)\n\nHow can we calculate the area of the image of $D$ under the action of the linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ that is given by its standard matrix $$T=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}?$$\n\nFor any linear transformation $\\mathbf{T},$ we have the following connection between the area of $D$ and the area of the image $\\mathbf{T}(D)$ under the action of $\\mathbf{T}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\\text{Area}(\\mathbf{T}(D)) = |\\det(T)| \\cdot \\text{Area}(D)$$\n\nThis equation is important. It tells us that $|\\det(T)|$ is the **area scale factor** of the transformation.\n\nFrom the diagram, we have\n\n$$\\text{Area}(D) = 2 \\cdot 3 = 6.$$\n\nComputing the determinant of $T,$ we get\n\n$$\\det(T) = 0 \\cdot 0 - (-2) \\cdot 2 = 4.$$\n\nTherefore, $$\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(T(D)) & =|det(T)|\\cdot \\text{Area}(D) \\\\ & =|\\,4\\,|\\cdot 6 \\\\ & =24.\\end{aligned}$$" "Consider the shaded region $D$ shown below.\n\n![](images/ca/cae19e62492782b42b69675ec0babd005407c7db8ce4dc6d77a14da06beedfc1.png)\n\nHow can we calculate the area of the image of $D$ under the action of the linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ that is given by its standard matrix $$T=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}?$$\n\nFor any linear transformation $\\mathbf{T},$ we have the following connection between the area of $D$ and the area of the image $\\mathbf{T}(D)$ under the action of $\\mathbf{T}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\\text{Area}(\\mathbf{T}(D)) = |\\det(T)| \\cdot \\text{Area}(D)$$\n\nThis equation is important. It tells us that $|\\det(T)|$ is the **area scale factor** of the transformation.\n\nFrom the diagram, we have\n\n$$\\text{Area}(D) = 2 \\cdot 3 = 6.$$\n\nComputing the determinant of $T,$ we get\n\n$$\\det(T) = 0 \\cdot 0 - (-2) \\cdot 2 = 4.$$\n\nTherefore, $$\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(T(D)) & =|\\det (T)|\\cdot \\text{Area}(D) \\\\ & =|\\,4\\,|\\cdot 6 \\\\ & =24.\\end{aligned}$$"] [:db/cas 17592186093438 :tutorial/content "Consider the matrix $A= {\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}2 & 5 \\\\ - 1 & - 4\\end{array} \\right]}.$ How can we find its eigenvalues?\n\nBy definition, we need to find the value(s) of $\\,\\lambda\\,$ such that $\\,A\\mathbf{\\color{blue}v} = \\lambda \\mathbf{\\color{blue}v}\\,$ is true for some *non-zero* vector $\\mathbf{\\color{blue}v}.$ Let's re-write this equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =\\lambda v \\\\ Av-\\lambda v & =0 \\\\ Av-\\lambda Iv & =0 \\\\ (A-\\lambda I)v & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe final equation above tells us that there should be at least one non-zero vector $\\mathbf{\\color{blue}v}$ that lies in $\\textrm{Null}(A-\\lambda I)$, the null space of the matrix $A-\\lambda I.$ As a result, the invertible matrix theorem tells us that the matrix $\\, A-\\lambda I \\,$ must be singular, meaning $\\det(A-\\lambda I) = 0.$\n\nWe've reduced the problem to finding the values of $\\lambda$ that satisfy the equation\n\n$$\n\\det(A-\\lambda I) = 0,\n$$\n\nwhich is called the **characteristic equation** of the matrix $A$. So, let's solve it!\n\nFirst, we write down the matrix $A-\\lambda I\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A\\,-\\,\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 5 \\\\ -1 & -4\\end{bmatrix}\\,-\\,\\lambda \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2\\,-\\,\\lambda  & 5 \\\\ -1 & -4\\,-\\,\\lambda \\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we solve the characteristic equation $\\det(A-\\lambda I) = 0\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}2-\\lambda  & 5 \\\\ -1 & -4-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )(-4-\\lambda )-5\\cdot (-1) & =0 \\\\ -8-2\\lambda +4\\lambda +{\\lambda }^{2}+5 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda -3 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +3)(\\lambda -1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues of $A$ are $\\lambda=-3$ and $\\lambda=1.$" "Consider the matrix $A= {\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}2 & 5 \\\\ - 1 & - 4\\end{array} \\right]}.$ How can we find its eigenvalues?\n\nBy definition, we need to find the value(s) of $\\,\\lambda\\,$ such that $\\,A\\mathbf{\\color{blue}v} = \\lambda \\mathbf{\\color{blue}v}\\,$ is true for some *non-zero* vector $\\mathbf{\\color{blue}v}.$ Let's re-write this equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =\\lambda v \\\\ Av-\\lambda v & =0 \\\\ Av-\\lambda Iv & =0 \\\\ (A-\\lambda I)v & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe final equation above tells us that there should be at least one non-zero vector $\\mathbf{\\color{blue}v}$ that lies in $\\textrm{Null}(A-\\lambda I)$, the null space of the matrix $A-\\lambda I.$ As a result, the invertible matrix theorem tells us that the matrix $\\, A-\\lambda I \\,$ must be singular, meaning $\\det(A-\\lambda I) = 0.$\n\nWe've reduced the problem to finding the values of $\\lambda$ that satisfy the equation\n\n$$\n\\det(A-\\lambda I) = 0,\n$$\n\nwhich is called the **characteristic equation** of the matrix $A$. So, let's solve it!\n\nFirst, we write down the matrix $A-\\lambda I\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A\\,-\\,\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 5 \\\\ -1 & -4\\end{bmatrix}\\,-\\,\\lambda \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2\\,-\\,\\lambda  & 5 \\\\ -1 & -4\\,-\\,\\lambda \\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we solve the characteristic equation $\\det(A-\\lambda I) = 0\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\det (A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}2-\\lambda  & 5 \\\\ -1 & -4-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )(-4-\\lambda )-5\\cdot (-1) & =0 \\\\ -8-2\\lambda +4\\lambda +{\\lambda }^{2}+5 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda -3 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +3)(\\lambda -1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues of $A$ are $\\lambda=-3$ and $\\lambda=1.$"] [:db/cas 17592186093454 :tutorial/content "The hyperbolic functions have inverse functions that are easy to define. The **inverse hyperbolic functions** are the following:\n$\\newcommand{\\arsinh}{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcosh}{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\artanh}{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arsech}{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$\n\n- The **inverse hyperbolic sine**, defined as\n\n$$\n\\sinh^{-1}{x} = \\arsinh{x} = \\ln\\big(x+\\sqrt{x^2+1}\\big), \\qquad x \\in (-\\infty, \\infty).\n$$\n\nThe graph of the inverse hyperbolic sine function is given below.\n\n![](images/1f/1f07f38b11cf297e099341df2ab062d4691c4502098c060de565b70f1ea7e360.png)\n\n- The **inverse hyperbolic cosine**, defined as\n\n$$\n\\cosh^{-1}{x} = \\arcosh{x} = \\ln\\big(x+\\sqrt{x^2-1}\\big), \\qquad x \\in [1, \\infty).\n$$\n\nThe graph of the inverse hyperbolic cosine function is given below.\n\n![](images/60/60607a764c50807dcb0f4f05328d900c626079654995cffbaa84fa2214788835.png)\n\n- The **inverse hyperbolic tangent**, defined as\n\n$$\n\\tanh^{-1}{x} = \\artanh{x} = \\dfrac12\\ln\\left(\\dfrac{1+x}{1-x}\\right), \\qquad x \\in (-1, 1).\n$$\n\nThe graph of the inverse hyperbolic tangent function is given below.\n\n![](images/96/96b4f7aa06ee165751dcaa2101e9dea104d95d5bebdaea7bc4f317d9160b5a7a.png)\n\nFor example, to calculate $\\arcosh(\\sqrt2)$ we substitute $x$ for $\\sqrt2$ in the formula for $\\arcosh$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡(\\sqrt{2}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\sqrt{2}+\\sqrt{{(\\sqrt{2})}^{2}-1}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\sqrt{2}+\\sqrt{2-1}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\sqrt{2}+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The hyperbolic functions have inverse functions that are easy to define. The **inverse hyperbolic functions** are the following:\n$\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsinh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcosh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{artanh} }{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{sech} }{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{csch} }{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsech} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcsch} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcoth} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$\n\n- The **inverse hyperbolic sine**, defined as\n\n$$\n\\sinh^{-1}{x} = \\operatorname{arsinh} {x} = \\ln\\big(x+\\sqrt{x^2+1}\\big), \\qquad x \\in (-\\infty, \\infty).\n$$\n\nThe graph of the inverse hyperbolic sine function is given below.\n\n![](images/1f/1f07f38b11cf297e099341df2ab062d4691c4502098c060de565b70f1ea7e360.png)\n\n- The **inverse hyperbolic cosine**, defined as\n\n$$\n\\cosh^{-1}{x} = \\operatorname{arcosh} {x} = \\ln\\big(x+\\sqrt{x^2-1}\\big), \\qquad x \\in [1, \\infty).\n$$\n\nThe graph of the inverse hyperbolic cosine function is given below.\n\n![](images/60/60607a764c50807dcb0f4f05328d900c626079654995cffbaa84fa2214788835.png)\n\n- The **inverse hyperbolic tangent**, defined as\n\n$$\n\\tanh^{-1}{x} = \\operatorname{artanh} {x} = \\dfrac12\\ln\\left(\\dfrac{1+x}{1-x}\\right), \\qquad x \\in (-1, 1).\n$$\n\nThe graph of the inverse hyperbolic tangent function is given below.\n\n![](images/96/96b4f7aa06ee165751dcaa2101e9dea104d95d5bebdaea7bc4f317d9160b5a7a.png)\n\nFor example, to calculate $\\operatorname{arcosh} (\\sqrt2)$ we substitute $x$ for $\\sqrt2$ in the formula for $\\operatorname{arcosh} $ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡(\\sqrt{2}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\sqrt{2}+\\sqrt{{(\\sqrt{2})}^{2}-1}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\sqrt{2}+\\sqrt{2-1}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\sqrt{2}+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186093455 :tutorial/content "We can derive the formulas from inverse hyperbolic sine, cosine, and tangent from the definition of these base functions. \n\nFor example, let's show that\n$\\newcommand{\\arsinh}{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcosh}{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\artanh}{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arsech}{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$\n\n$$\n\\cosh^{-1}{x} = \\arcosh{x} = \\ln\\big(x+\\sqrt{x^2-1}\\big), \\qquad x \\in [1, \\infty).\n$$\n\nRecall that\n\n$$\n\\cosh{x} = \\dfrac12 \\left( e^x + e^{-x} \\right).\n$$\n\nFirst, let $y = \\cosh{x}$ and $x \\geq 0.$ Then we have $y\\geq 1.$\n\nTo find the inverse function we swap $x$ and $y{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\operatorname{cosh}⁡y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, now we have that $x \\geq 1$ and $y \\geq 0.$\n\nNext, we use the definition of $\\cosh x$ and bring everything to the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{y}+{e}^{-y}) \\\\ 2x & ={e}^{y}+{e}^{-y} \\\\ 2x{e}^{y} & ={e}^{2y}+1 \\\\ {e}^{2y}-2x{e}^{y}+1 & =0 \\\\ {({e}^{y})}^{2}-2x({e}^{y})+1 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLetting $Y = e^{y},$ we form a quadratic equation in $Y$ and solve it by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Y}^{2}-2xY+1 & =0 \\\\ (Y-x)^{2}-{x}^{2}+1 & =0 \\\\ (Y-x)^{2} & ={x}^{2}-1 \\\\ Y-x & =\\pm \\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1} \\\\ Y & =x\\pm \\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1} \\\\ {e}^{y} & =x\\pm \\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1} \\\\ y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x\\pm \\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that for  $x\\ge 1,$ we have $x-\\sqrt{x^{2}-1}\\leq 1$ and  $x+\\sqrt{x^{2}-1}\\geq 1.$ Since $y \\ge 0,$ the logarithm in the above equality must be positive, so we reject the alternative with the negative sign. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain our formula for $\\arcosh{:}$\n\n$$\nf^{-1}(x)=\n\\arcosh{x} = \\ln\\big(x+\\sqrt{x^2-1}\\big).\n$$" "We can derive the formulas from inverse hyperbolic sine, cosine, and tangent from the definition of these base functions. \n\nFor example, let's show that\n$\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsinh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcosh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{artanh} }{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{sech} }{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{csch} }{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsech} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcsch} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcoth} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$\n\n$$\n\\cosh^{-1}{x} = \\operatorname{arcosh} {x} = \\ln\\big(x+\\sqrt{x^2-1}\\big), \\qquad x \\in [1, \\infty).\n$$\n\nRecall that\n\n$$\n\\cosh{x} = \\dfrac12 \\left( e^x + e^{-x} \\right).\n$$\n\nFirst, let $y = \\cosh{x}$ and $x \\geq 0.$ Then we have $y\\geq 1.$\n\nTo find the inverse function we swap $x$ and $y{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\operatorname{cosh}⁡y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, now we have that $x \\geq 1$ and $y \\geq 0.$\n\nNext, we use the definition of $\\cosh x$ and bring everything to the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{y}+{e}^{-y}) \\\\ 2x & ={e}^{y}+{e}^{-y} \\\\ 2x{e}^{y} & ={e}^{2y}+1 \\\\ {e}^{2y}-2x{e}^{y}+1 & =0 \\\\ {({e}^{y})}^{2}-2x({e}^{y})+1 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLetting $Y = e^{y},$ we form a quadratic equation in $Y$ and solve it by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Y}^{2}-2xY+1 & =0 \\\\ (Y-x)^{2}-{x}^{2}+1 & =0 \\\\ (Y-x)^{2} & ={x}^{2}-1 \\\\ Y-x & =\\pm \\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1} \\\\ Y & =x\\pm \\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1} \\\\ {e}^{y} & =x\\pm \\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1} \\\\ y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x\\pm \\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that for  $x\\ge 1,$ we have $x-\\sqrt{x^{2}-1}\\leq 1$ and  $x+\\sqrt{x^{2}-1}\\geq 1.$ Since $y \\ge 0,$ the logarithm in the above equality must be positive, so we reject the alternative with the negative sign. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain our formula for $\\operatorname{arcosh} {:}$\n\n$$\nf^{-1}(x)=\n\\operatorname{arcosh} {x} = \\ln\\big(x+\\sqrt{x^2-1}\\big).\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186093482 :tutorial/content "Earlier, we compared the PDF of $X\\sim N(\\mu,\\sigma^2),$ given by \n\n$$\nf(x) = (2\\pi)^{-{\\color{red}1}/2 } ({\\color{blue}\\sigma^2})^{-1/2} \\: \\mathrm{exp}\\left( -\\frac{1}{2} (x-\\mu) ({\\color{blue}\\sigma^2})^{-1} (x-\\mu) \\right).\n$$\n\nand the joint PDF of a bivariate normal distribution $\\mathbf X = [X_1,X_2]^T$ with mean vector $\\boldsymbol\\mu$ and covariance matrix $\\Sigma,$ given by\n\n$$\nf(x_1, x_2) = (2\\pi)^{-{\\color{red}2}/2} {\\color{blue}|\\Sigma|}^{-1/2} \\: \\mathrm{exp}\\left( -\\dfrac 12(\\mathbf X - \\boldsymbol \\mu)^T \\,{\\color{blue}\\Sigma}^{-1} \\,(\\mathbf X - \\boldsymbol \\mu)\\right).\n$$\n\nWe can generalize this for an arbitrary number of variables ${\\color{red}p},$ as follows:\n\n$$\nf(x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_p) = (2\\pi)^{-{\\color{red}p}/2} {\\color{blue}|\\Sigma|}^{-1/2} \\: \\mathrm{exp}\\left( -\\dfrac 12(\\mathbf X - \\boldsymbol \\mu)^T \\,{\\color{blue}\\Sigma}^{-1} \\,(\\mathbf X - \\boldsymbol \\mu)\\right)\n$$\n\nThe function $f(x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_p)$ is called the **multivariate normal** density function. A random vector $\\mathbf X = [X_1,X_2,\\ldots,X_p]^T$ with this joint density function is said to follow a **multivariate normal distribution**.\n\nSimilar to the bivariate case, we can consider linear combinations of coordinates of multivariate normal random vectors: \n\n*If $\\mathbf{X} =  [X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_k]^T$ is a multivariate normal random vector with mean vector $\\boldsymbol{\\mu}$ and covariance matrix $\\Sigma,$ then the random variable $Y,$ defined as \n\n$$\nY= \\mathbf c^T \\mathbf X = \\sum_{i=1}^k c_i X_i\n$$\n\n is a normally distributed random variable, where $\\mathbf c$ is any $k\\times 1$ column vector, and*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}E[Y] & ={c}^{T}\\mu , \\\\ Var[Y] & ={c}^{T}Σc.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the expression for the variance is a quadratic form." "Earlier, we compared the PDF of $X\\sim N(\\mu,\\sigma^2),$ given by \n\n$$\nf(x) = (2\\pi)^{-{\\color{red}1}/2 } ({\\color{blue}\\sigma^2})^{-1/2} \\: \\mathrm{exp}\\left( -\\frac{1}{2} (x-\\mu) ({\\color{blue}\\sigma^2})^{-1} (x-\\mu) \\right).\n$$\n\nand the joint PDF of a bivariate normal distribution $\\mathbf X = [X_1,X_2]^T$ with mean vector $\\boldsymbol\\mu$ and covariance matrix $\\Sigma,$ given by\n\n$$\nf(x_1, x_2) = (2\\pi)^{-{\\color{red}2}/2} {\\color{blue}|\\Sigma|}^{-1/2} \\: \\mathrm{exp}\\left( -\\dfrac 12(\\mathbf X - \\boldsymbol \\mu)^T \\,{\\color{blue}\\Sigma}^{-1} \\,(\\mathbf X - \\boldsymbol \\mu)\\right).\n$$\n\nWe can generalize this for an arbitrary number of variables ${\\color{red}p},$ as follows:\n\n$$\nf(x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_p) = (2\\pi)^{-{\\color{red}p}/2} {\\color{blue}|\\Sigma|}^{-1/2} \\: \\mathrm{exp}\\left( -\\dfrac 12(\\mathbf X - \\boldsymbol \\mu)^T \\,{\\color{blue}\\Sigma}^{-1} \\,(\\mathbf X - \\boldsymbol \\mu)\\right)\n$$\n\nThe function $f(x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_p)$ is called the **multivariate normal** density function. A random vector $\\mathbf X = [X_1,X_2,\\ldots,X_p]^T$ with this joint density function is said to follow a **multivariate normal distribution**.\n\nSimilar to the bivariate case, we can consider linear combinations of coordinates of multivariate normal random vectors: \n\n*If $\\mathbf{X} =  [X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_k]^T$ is a multivariate normal random vector with mean vector $\\boldsymbol{\\mu}$ and covariance matrix $\\Sigma,$ then the random variable $Y,$ defined as \n\n$$\nY= \\mathbf c^T \\mathbf X = \\sum_{i=1}^k c_i X_i\n$$\n\n is a normally distributed random variable, where $\\mathbf c$ is any $k\\times 1$ column vector, and*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}E[Y] & ={c}^{T}\\mu , \\\\ \\operatorname{Var} [Y] & ={c}^{T}Σc.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the expression for the variance is a quadratic form."] [:db/cas 17592186093483 :tutorial/content "Recall that a normally distributed random variable $X\\sim N(\\mu,\\sigma^2)$ has the probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\dfrac{1}{\\sigma \\sqrt{2\\pi}} e^{-\\frac{1}{2} \\left( \\frac{x-\\mu}{\\sigma} \\right)^2}.\n$$\n\nThis probability density function can be written as \n\n$$\nf(x) = (2\\pi)^{-{\\color{red}1}/2 } ({\\color{blue}\\sigma^2})^{-1/2} \\: \\mathrm{exp}\\left( -\\frac{1}{2} (x-\\mu) ({\\color{blue}\\sigma^2})^{-1} (x-\\mu) \\right).\n$$\n\nLet's compare this to the density function of a bivariate normal vector. First, we rewrite the joint density $f(x_1,x_2)$ of the bivariate normal distribution as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f({x}_{1},{x}_{2}) & =\\frac{1}{2\\pi \\sqrt{|Σ|}}\\,exp(-\\frac{1}{2}(X-\\mu )^{T}\\,{Σ}^{-1}\\,(X-\\mu )) \\\\  & =(2\\pi )^{-1}|Σ{|}^{-1/2}\\,exp(-\\frac{1}{2}(X-\\mu )^{T}\\,{Σ}^{-1}\\,(X-\\mu )) \\\\  & =(2\\pi )^{-2/2}{|Σ|}^{-1/2}\\,exp(-\\frac{1}{2}(X-\\mu )^{T}\\,{Σ}^{-1}\\,(X-\\mu ))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy comparing the expressions for $f(x)$ and $f(x_1,x_2),$ we see that the *bivariate* normal distribution can be considered as a generalization of the *normal distribution* from the ${\\color{red}1}$-dimensional case (when we have only one variable, $x$) to the ${\\color{red}2}$-dimensional case (when we have two variables, $x_1$ and $x_2$).\n\nThis analogy will be particularly helpful when extending the bivariate normal distribution to more than two random variables." "Recall that a normally distributed random variable $X\\sim N(\\mu,\\sigma^2)$ has the probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\dfrac{1}{\\sigma \\sqrt{2\\pi}} e^{-\\frac{1}{2} \\left( \\frac{x-\\mu}{\\sigma} \\right)^2}.\n$$\n\nThis probability density function can be written as \n\n$$\nf(x) = (2\\pi)^{-{\\color{red}1}/2 } ({\\color{blue}\\sigma^2})^{-1/2} \\: \\mathrm{exp}\\left( -\\frac{1}{2} (x-\\mu) ({\\color{blue}\\sigma^2})^{-1} (x-\\mu) \\right).\n$$\n\nLet's compare this to the density function of a bivariate normal vector. First, we rewrite the joint density $f(x_1,x_2)$ of the bivariate normal distribution as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f({x}_{1},{x}_{2}) & =\\frac{1}{2\\pi \\sqrt{|Σ|}}\\,\\exp (-\\frac{1}{2}(X-\\mu )^{T}\\,{Σ}^{-1}\\,(X-\\mu )) \\\\  & =(2\\pi )^{-1}|Σ{|}^{-1/2}\\,\\exp (-\\frac{1}{2}(X-\\mu )^{T}\\,{Σ}^{-1}\\,(X-\\mu )) \\\\  & =(2\\pi )^{-2/2}{|Σ|}^{-1/2}\\,\\exp (-\\frac{1}{2}(X-\\mu )^{T}\\,{Σ}^{-1}\\,(X-\\mu ))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy comparing the expressions for $f(x)$ and $f(x_1,x_2),$ we see that the *bivariate* normal distribution can be considered as a generalization of the *normal distribution* from the ${\\color{red}1}$-dimensional case (when we have only one variable, $x$) to the ${\\color{red}2}$-dimensional case (when we have two variables, $x_1$ and $x_2$).\n\nThis analogy will be particularly helpful when extending the bivariate normal distribution to more than two random variables."] [:db/cas 17592186093484 :tutorial/content "A linear combination of coordinates of a bivariate normal random vector generates a (one-dimensional) normal distribution:\n\n*If $\\mathbf{X} = [X_1, X_2]^T$ is a bivariate normal random vector with mean vector $\\boldsymbol{\\mu}$ and covariance matrix $\\Sigma,$ given by*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu =\\begin{bmatrix}{\\mu }_{1} \\\\ {\\mu }_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,Σ=\\begin{bmatrix}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2} & {\\sigma }_{12} \\\\ {\\sigma }_{21} & {\\sigma }_{2}^{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*then the random variable $Y,$ defined as* \n\n$$\nY= aX_1 + bX_2\n$$\n\n*is a normally distributed random variable for all values of $a$ and $b.$ Furthermore, we have that*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}E[Y] & =a{\\mu }_{1}+b{\\mu }_{2}, \\\\ Var[Y] & ={a}^{2}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}+{b}^{2}{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}+2ab{\\sigma }_{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "A linear combination of coordinates of a bivariate normal random vector generates a (one-dimensional) normal distribution:\n\n*If $\\mathbf{X} = [X_1, X_2]^T$ is a bivariate normal random vector with mean vector $\\boldsymbol{\\mu}$ and covariance matrix $\\Sigma,$ given by*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu =\\begin{bmatrix}{\\mu }_{1} \\\\ {\\mu }_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,Σ=\\begin{bmatrix}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2} & {\\sigma }_{12} \\\\ {\\sigma }_{21} & {\\sigma }_{2}^{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*then the random variable $Y,$ defined as* \n\n$$\nY= aX_1 + bX_2\n$$\n\n*is a normally distributed random variable for all values of $a$ and $b.$ Furthermore, we have that*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}E[Y] & =a{\\mu }_{1}+b{\\mu }_{2}, \\\\ \\operatorname{Var} [Y] & ={a}^{2}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}+{b}^{2}{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}+2ab{\\sigma }_{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186093496 :tutorial/content "Recall that the indicator function of a set $A \\subseteq X$ is the function\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{1}_A(x) =\n{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{ll}1 , & \\text{if } {x \\in A} , \\\\ 0 , & \\text{if } {x \\notin A} .\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$\n\nTherefore, the indicator function of the complement $\\overline{A}$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{1}_{\\overline{A}}(x) =\n{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{ll}1 , & \\text{if } {x \\in \\overline{A}} , \\\\ 0 , & \\text{if } {x \\notin \\overline{A}} .\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$\n\nThis means that the indicator function of $A$ and its complement are related as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{1}_{\\overset{―}{A}}\\,(x) & =1-{1}_{A}(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, the indicator functions of $A \\cap B$ and union $A \\cup B$ can be expressed through the indicator functions of $A$ and $B.$\n\n- For the indicator function of the *intersection*, we have: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{1}_{A\\,\\cap \\,B}\\,(x) & =min\\{{1}_{A}(x),{1}_{B}(x)\\} \\\\  & ={1}_{A}(x)\\cdot {1}_{B}(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the indicator function of the *union*, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{1}_{A\\,\\cup \\,B}\\,(x) & =max\\{{1}_{A}(x),{1}_{B}(x)\\} \\\\  & ={1}_{A}(x)+{1}_{B}(x)-{1}_{A}(x)\\cdot {1}_{B}(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can prove these results by splitting them into cases." "Recall that the indicator function of a set $A \\subseteq X$ is the function\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{1}_A(x) =\n{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{ll}1 , & \\text{if } {x \\in A} , \\\\ 0 , & \\text{if } {x \\notin A} .\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$\n\nTherefore, the indicator function of the complement $\\overline{A}$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{1}_{\\overline{A}}(x) =\n{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{ll}1 , & \\text{if } {x \\in \\overline{A}} , \\\\ 0 , & \\text{if } {x \\notin \\overline{A}} .\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$\n\nThis means that the indicator function of $A$ and its complement are related as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{1}_{\\overset{―}{A}}\\,(x) & =1-{1}_{A}(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, the indicator functions of $A \\cap B$ and union $A \\cup B$ can be expressed through the indicator functions of $A$ and $B.$\n\n- For the indicator function of the *intersection*, we have: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{1}_{A\\,\\cap \\,B}\\,(x) & =\\min \\{{1}_{A}(x),{1}_{B}(x)\\} \\\\  & ={1}_{A}(x)\\cdot {1}_{B}(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the indicator function of the *union*, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{1}_{A\\,\\cup \\,B}\\,(x) & =\\max \\{{1}_{A}(x),{1}_{B}(x)\\} \\\\  & ={1}_{A}(x)+{1}_{B}(x)-{1}_{A}(x)\\cdot {1}_{B}(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can prove these results by splitting them into cases."] [:db/cas 17592186096404 :question/worked-solution "This question requires the product rule. Let $u(x) = e^{-2x}$ and $v(x)=\\tan{(x^2)}.$ Then\n\n$$\nu'(x) = -2e^{-2x}, \\qquad v'(x) = 2x\\sec^2(x^2).\n$$\n\nNote that we used the chain rule to differentiate both $u$ and $v.$\n\nNow, applying the product rule and simplifying, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)+u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x) \\\\  & =(-2{e}^{-2x})\\cdot \\operatorname{tan}⁡({x}^{2})+{e}^{-2x}\\cdot 2x{sec}^{2}⁡({x}^{2}) \\\\  & =2{e}^{-2x}(-\\operatorname{tan}⁡({x}^{2})+x{sec}^{2}⁡({x}^{2})) \\\\  & =2{e}^{-2x}(x{sec}^{2}⁡({x}^{2})-\\operatorname{tan}⁡({x}^{2})).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "This question requires the product rule. Let $u(x) = e^{-2x}$ and $v(x)=\\tan{(x^2)}.$ Then\n\n$$\nu'(x) = -2e^{-2x}, \\qquad v'(x) = 2x\\sec^2(x^2).\n$$\n\nNote that we used the chain rule to differentiate both $u$ and $v.$\n\nNow, applying the product rule and simplifying, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)+u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x) \\\\  & =(-2{e}^{-2x})\\cdot \\operatorname{tan}⁡({x}^{2})+{e}^{-2x}\\cdot 2x{\\sec }^{2}⁡({x}^{2}) \\\\  & =2{e}^{-2x}(-\\operatorname{tan}⁡({x}^{2})+x{\\sec }^{2}⁡({x}^{2})) \\\\  & =2{e}^{-2x}(x{\\sec }^{2}⁡({x}^{2})-\\operatorname{tan}⁡({x}^{2})).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186096407 :question/worked-solution "The sum formula for sine gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+ϕ) & =R(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{2}\\sin x + \\sqrt{7}\\cos x   = R\\sin(x+\\phi),\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{2}\\sin x + \\sqrt{7}\\cos x    = (R \\cos \\phi ) \\sin x + (R \\sin \\phi ) \\cos x\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\sin x$ and $\\cos x,$ we have the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ &=\\sqrt{2} \\\\ R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ &=\\sqrt{7}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSquaring the first and the second equations of the system and adding the results together, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)}^{2}+{(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)}^{2} & ={\\sqrt{2}}^{2}+{\\sqrt{7}}^{2} \\\\ {R}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡ϕ+{sin}^{2}⁡ϕ) & =2+7 \\\\ {R}^{2} & =9 \\\\ R & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we only consider the positive root since $R>0.$" "The sum formula for sine gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+ϕ) & =R(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{2}\\sin x + \\sqrt{7}\\cos x   = R\\sin(x+\\phi),\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{2}\\sin x + \\sqrt{7}\\cos x    = (R \\cos \\phi ) \\sin x + (R \\sin \\phi ) \\cos x\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\sin x$ and $\\cos x,$ we have the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ &=\\sqrt{2} \\\\ R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ &=\\sqrt{7}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSquaring the first and the second equations of the system and adding the results together, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)}^{2}+{(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)}^{2} & ={\\sqrt{2}}^{2}+{\\sqrt{7}}^{2} \\\\ {R}^{2}({\\cos }^{2}⁡ϕ+{\\sin }^{2}⁡ϕ) & =2+7 \\\\ {R}^{2} & =9 \\\\ R & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we only consider the positive root since $R>0.$"] [:db/cas 17592186096408 :question/worked-solution "The difference formula for sine gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-ϕ) & =R(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n3 \\sin x - 2 \\cos x    = R\\sin(x-\\phi) ,\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n3 \\sin x - 2 \\cos x   = (R \\cos \\phi ) \\sin x - (R \\sin \\phi )\\cos x\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\sin x$ and $\\cos x,$ we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ &=3 \\\\ R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ &=2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nDividing the second equation by the first, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ} & =\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡ϕ & =\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ ϕ & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right) \\\\  & ≈0.588\\,\\text{rad}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places.\n\nNow, we find the amplitude $R.$ Squaring the first and the second equations of the system and adding the results together, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)}^{2}+{(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)}^{2} & =(3)^{2}+(2)^{2} \\\\ {R}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡ϕ+{sin}^{2}⁡ϕ) & =9+4 \\\\ {R}^{2} & =13 \\\\ R & =\\sqrt{13}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we only considered the positive root, since $R>0.$\n\nFinally, we can write the initial expression in amplitude-phase form as follows:\n\n$$\n3\\sin(x)-2\\cos(x) \\approx \\sqrt{13} \\sin \\left(x - 0.588 \\right)\n$$" "The difference formula for sine gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-ϕ) & =R(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n3 \\sin x - 2 \\cos x    = R\\sin(x-\\phi) ,\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n3 \\sin x - 2 \\cos x   = (R \\cos \\phi ) \\sin x - (R \\sin \\phi )\\cos x\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\sin x$ and $\\cos x,$ we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ &=3 \\\\ R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ &=2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nDividing the second equation by the first, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ} & =\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡ϕ & =\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ ϕ & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right) \\\\  & ≈0.588\\,\\text{rad}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places.\n\nNow, we find the amplitude $R.$ Squaring the first and the second equations of the system and adding the results together, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)}^{2}+{(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)}^{2} & =(3)^{2}+(2)^{2} \\\\ {R}^{2}({\\cos }^{2}⁡ϕ+{\\sin }^{2}⁡ϕ) & =9+4 \\\\ {R}^{2} & =13 \\\\ R & =\\sqrt{13}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we only considered the positive root, since $R>0.$\n\nFinally, we can write the initial expression in amplitude-phase form as follows:\n\n$$\n3\\sin(x)-2\\cos(x) \\approx \\sqrt{13} \\sin \\left(x - 0.588 \\right)\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186096409 :question/worked-solution "The difference formula for cosine gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+ϕ) & =R(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n3\\cos x - \\sqrt{3}\\sin x   = R\\cos(x+\\phi)\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n3\\cos x - \\sqrt{3}\\sin x   = (R \\cos \\phi) \\cos x -( R \\sin \\phi )\\sin x\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\sin x$ and $\\cos x,$ we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ &=3 \\\\ R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ &=\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nDividing the second equation by the first, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡ϕ & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ ϕ & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we find the amplitude $R.$ Squaring the first and the second equations of the system and adding the results together, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)}^{2}+{(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)}^{2} & ={3}^{2}+(\\sqrt{3})^{2} \\\\ {R}^{2}({sin}^{2}⁡ϕ+{cos}^{2}⁡ϕ) & =9+3 \\\\ {R}^{2} & =12 \\\\ R & =\\sqrt{12} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we only consider the positive root since $R>0.$\n\nFinally, we can write the initial expression in amplitude-phase form as follows:\n\n$$\n3\\cos x - \\sqrt{3}\\sin x = 2\\sqrt{3}\\cos\\left(x + \\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\\right)\n$$" "The difference formula for cosine gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+ϕ) & =R(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n3\\cos x - \\sqrt{3}\\sin x   = R\\cos(x+\\phi)\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n3\\cos x - \\sqrt{3}\\sin x   = (R \\cos \\phi) \\cos x -( R \\sin \\phi )\\sin x\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\sin x$ and $\\cos x,$ we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ &=3 \\\\ R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ &=\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nDividing the second equation by the first, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡ϕ & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ ϕ & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we find the amplitude $R.$ Squaring the first and the second equations of the system and adding the results together, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)}^{2}+{(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)}^{2} & ={3}^{2}+(\\sqrt{3})^{2} \\\\ {R}^{2}({\\sin }^{2}⁡ϕ+{\\cos }^{2}⁡ϕ) & =9+3 \\\\ {R}^{2} & =12 \\\\ R & =\\sqrt{12} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we only consider the positive root since $R>0.$\n\nFinally, we can write the initial expression in amplitude-phase form as follows:\n\n$$\n3\\cos x - \\sqrt{3}\\sin x = 2\\sqrt{3}\\cos\\left(x + \\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\\right)\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186096418 :question/worked-solution "Here, we have the product of two functions $u(x) = x^2$ and $v(x)=\\ln x.$ So, we apply the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={({x}^{2})}^{′}ln⁡x+{x}^{2}{(ln⁡x)}^{′} \\\\  & =2x\\cdot ln⁡x+{x}^{2}\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right) \\\\  & =2xln⁡x+x \\\\  & =x(2ln⁡x+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Here, we have the product of two functions $u(x) = x^2$ and $v(x)=\\ln x.$ So, we apply the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={({x}^{2})}^{′}\\ln ⁡x+{x}^{2}{(\\ln ⁡x)}^{′} \\\\  & =2x\\cdot \\ln ⁡x+{x}^{2}\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right) \\\\  & =2x\\ln ⁡x+x \\\\  & =x(2\\ln ⁡x+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186096419 :question/worked-solution "We differentiate using the product rule. Let $u(x) = e^x$ and $v(x)=\\sin{x}.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = e^x,\\qquad \\dfrac{\\textrm d v}{\\textrm d x} = \\cos x.\n$$\n\nApplying the product rule gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{x}\\cdot cos⁡x+sin⁡x\\cdot {e}^{x} \\\\  & ={e}^{x}(cos⁡x+sin⁡x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we now substitute $x=\\dfrac\\pi 2$ into the formula and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=\\pi /2} & ={e}^{\\pi /2}(cos⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)+sin⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)) \\\\  & ={e}^{\\pi /2}(0+1) \\\\  & ={e}^{\\pi /2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent line is $e^{\\pi/2}.$" "We differentiate using the product rule. Let $u(x) = e^x$ and $v(x)=\\sin{x}.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = e^x,\\qquad \\dfrac{\\textrm d v}{\\textrm d x} = \\cos x.\n$$\n\nApplying the product rule gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{x}\\cdot \\cos ⁡x+\\sin ⁡x\\cdot {e}^{x} \\\\  & ={e}^{x}(\\cos ⁡x+\\sin ⁡x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we now substitute $x=\\dfrac\\pi 2$ into the formula and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=\\pi /2} & ={e}^{\\pi /2}(\\cos ⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)+\\sin ⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)) \\\\  & ={e}^{\\pi /2}(0+1) \\\\  & ={e}^{\\pi /2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent line is $e^{\\pi/2}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186096467 :question/worked-solution "We use a variation of the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n\\sin^2 x + \\cos^2 x = 1\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad  \\cos^2 x = 1-\\sin^2 x.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we rewrite the denominator and calculate the resulting integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{1-{sin}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {\\left(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\right)}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {sec}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We use a variation of the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n\\sin^2 x + \\cos^2 x = 1\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad  \\cos^2 x = 1-\\sin^2 x.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we rewrite the denominator and calculate the resulting integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{1-{\\sin }^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{{\\cos }^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {\\left(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\right)}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {\\sec }^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186096473 :question/worked-solution "Let's recall the double-angle formula for cosine that involves the $\\sin^2$ term: \n\n$$\n\\cos {2\\theta}=1-2\\sin^2\\theta.\n$$\n\nWe can rearrange the above formula as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{2}\\left(1-\\cos {2\\theta} \\right )=\\sin^2\\theta.\n$$\n\nLetting $\\theta = 3x$, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{2}\\left(1-\\cos {6x} \\right )=\\sin^2(3x).\n$$\n\nWe can now integrate as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {sin}^{2}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{2}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡6x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int (1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡6x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(x-\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{sin}⁡6x)+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}x-\\frac{1}{12}\\operatorname{sin}⁡6x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let's recall the double-angle formula for cosine that involves the $\\sin^2$ term: \n\n$$\n\\cos {2\\theta}=1-2\\sin^2\\theta.\n$$\n\nWe can rearrange the above formula as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{2}\\left(1-\\cos {2\\theta} \\right )=\\sin^2\\theta.\n$$\n\nLetting $\\theta = 3x$, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{2}\\left(1-\\cos {6x} \\right )=\\sin^2(3x).\n$$\n\nWe can now integrate as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {\\sin }^{2}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{2}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡6x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int (1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡6x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(x-\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{sin}⁡6x)+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}x-\\frac{1}{12}\\operatorname{sin}⁡6x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186096476 :question/worked-solution "We define\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={log}_{4}⁡x\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡4} \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x=-\\,\\frac{1}{2{x}^{2}}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{log}_{4}⁡x}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u{v}^{′}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={log}_{4}⁡x(-\\,\\frac{1}{2{x}^{2}})-\\int (-\\,\\frac{1}{2{x}^{2}})\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2{x}^{2}}\\,{log}_{4}⁡x+\\frac{1}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{2{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}+\\frac{1}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}(-\\,\\frac{1}{2{x}^{2}})+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{2{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}-\\frac{1}{4{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1}{4{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that in the above calculation we used the change of base formula $\\log_4{x}=\\dfrac{\\ln{x}}{\\ln{4}}.$" "We define\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={\\log }_{4}⁡x\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡4} \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x=-\\,\\frac{1}{2{x}^{2}}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{\\log }_{4}⁡x}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u{v}^{′}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\log }_{4}⁡x(-\\,\\frac{1}{2{x}^{2}})-\\int (-\\,\\frac{1}{2{x}^{2}})\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2{x}^{2}}\\,{\\log }_{4}⁡x+\\frac{1}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{2{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}+\\frac{1}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}(-\\,\\frac{1}{2{x}^{2}})+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{2{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}-\\frac{1}{4{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1}{4{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that in the above calculation we used the change of base formula $\\log_4{x}=\\dfrac{\\ln{x}}{\\ln{4}}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186096541 :question/worked-solution "First, we express $y$ as a composite function\n\n$$\ny = u^4, \\qquad u = \\cos{v}, \\qquad v = 2x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d u} = 4u^3,\\qquad \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm d v} = -\\sin{v},\\qquad \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}v}{\\textrm d x} = 2.\n$$\n\nApplying now the chain rule twice, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}v}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =4{u}^{3}\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡v)\\cdot (2) \\\\  & =4(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)^{3}\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)\\cdot (2) \\\\  & =-8{cos}^{3}⁡2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we express $y$ as a composite function\n\n$$\ny = u^4, \\qquad u = \\cos{v}, \\qquad v = 2x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d u} = 4u^3,\\qquad \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm d v} = -\\sin{v},\\qquad \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}v}{\\textrm d x} = 2.\n$$\n\nApplying now the chain rule twice, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}v}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =4{u}^{3}\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡v)\\cdot (2) \\\\  & =4(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)^{3}\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)\\cdot (2) \\\\  & =-8{\\cos }^{3}⁡2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186096542 :question/worked-solution "Note that \n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(t)=\\int \\mathbf F'(t)\\, \\textrm d t=\\int \\left\\langle 6t^5+2t,\\, \\sec^2 t\\right\\rangle \\, \\textrm d t.\n$$\n\nTo integrate a vector-valued function, we integrate each component separately. So, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int ⟨6{t}^{5}+2t,\\,{sec}^{2}⁡t⟩\\,\\text{d}t & =⟨\\int (6{t}^{5}+2t)\\,\\text{d}t,\\,\\int {sec}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{t}^{6}+{t}^{2}+{c}_{1},\\,\\operatorname{tan}⁡t+{c}_{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{t}^{6}+{t}^{2},\\,\\operatorname{tan}⁡t⟩+⟨{c}_{1},\\,{c}_{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{t}^{6}+{t}^{2},\\,\\operatorname{tan}⁡t⟩+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf C$ is an arbitrary (vector) constant of integration." "Note that \n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(t)=\\int \\mathbf F'(t)\\, \\textrm d t=\\int \\left\\langle 6t^5+2t,\\, \\sec^2 t\\right\\rangle \\, \\textrm d t.\n$$\n\nTo integrate a vector-valued function, we integrate each component separately. So, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int ⟨6{t}^{5}+2t,\\,{\\sec }^{2}⁡t⟩\\,\\text{d}t & =⟨\\int (6{t}^{5}+2t)\\,\\text{d}t,\\,\\int {\\sec }^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{t}^{6}+{t}^{2}+{c}_{1},\\,\\operatorname{tan}⁡t+{c}_{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{t}^{6}+{t}^{2},\\,\\operatorname{tan}⁡t⟩+⟨{c}_{1},\\,{c}_{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{t}^{6}+{t}^{2},\\,\\operatorname{tan}⁡t⟩+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf C$ is an arbitrary (vector) constant of integration."] [:db/cas 17592186096934 :question/problem "Calculate\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int (4{cos}^{2}⁡x-2)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" "Calculate\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int (4{\\cos }^{2}⁡x-2)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186096934 :question/worked-solution "Here, we use the double-angle formula for cosine that involves the ${cos}^{2}⁡x$ term, given by\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x=2{cos}^{2}⁡x-1.\n$$\n\n \n\nRearranging, we have\n\n$$\n{cos}^{2}⁡x=\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+1).\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (4{cos}^{2}⁡x-2)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (4(\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+1))-2)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+2-2)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Here, we use the double-angle formula for cosine that involves the ${\\cos }^{2}⁡x$ term, given by\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x=2{\\cos }^{2}⁡x-1.\n$$\n\n \n\nRearranging, we have\n\n$$\n{\\cos }^{2}⁡x=\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+1).\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (4{\\cos }^{2}⁡x-2)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (4(\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+1))-2)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+2-2)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186096936 :question/problem "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int 100{tan}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int 100{\\tan }^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186096936 :question/worked-solution "First, we select the identity that contains a ${tan}^{2}⁡x$ term. So we can use the identity \n\n$$\n1+{tan}^{2}⁡x={sec}^{2}⁡x.\n$$\n\nRearranging the above identity gives\n\n$$\n{tan}^{2}⁡x={sec}^{2}⁡x-1.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we compute the given integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 100{tan}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 100({sec}^{2}⁡x-1)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (100{sec}^{2}⁡x-100)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =100\\operatorname{tan}⁡x-100x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we select the identity that contains a ${\\tan }^{2}⁡x$ term. So we can use the identity \n\n$$\n1+{\\tan }^{2}⁡x={\\sec }^{2}⁡x.\n$$\n\nRearranging the above identity gives\n\n$$\n{\\tan }^{2}⁡x={\\sec }^{2}⁡x-1.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we compute the given integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 100{\\tan }^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 100({\\sec }^{2}⁡x-1)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (100{\\sec }^{2}⁡x-100)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =100\\operatorname{tan}⁡x-100x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186096940 :question/problem "A course teacher wants to check the strength of the linear correlation between the following variables:\n\n- $x$, the final grade a student received on finishing the course, and\n- $y$, the student's score in the exam.\n\nYou are given that $Cov(x, y) = 140$, $σ_{x} = 5$, and $σ_{y} = 30$. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $ρ \\approx 0.93$\n2. There is a strong positive correlation between $x$ and $y$.\n3. There is a weak positive correlation between $x$ and $y$.\n\n*Hint: $\\mid ρ \\mid > 0.7$ indicates a strong correlation, and $0.3 \\le \\mid ρ \\mid \\le 0.7$ indicates a weak correlation.*\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." "A course teacher wants to check the strength of the linear correlation between the following variables:\n\n- $x$, the final grade a student received on finishing the course, and\n- $y$, the student's score in the exam.\n\nYou are given that $\\operatorname{Cov} (x, y) = 140$, $σ_{x} = 5$, and $σ_{y} = 30$. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $ρ \\approx 0.93$\n2. There is a strong positive correlation between $x$ and $y$.\n3. There is a weak positive correlation between $x$ and $y$.\n\n*Hint: $\\mid ρ \\mid > 0.7$ indicates a strong correlation, and $0.3 \\le \\mid ρ \\mid \\le 0.7$ indicates a weak correlation.*\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question."] [:db/cas 17592186096964 :question/worked-solution "The sum formula for sine gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+ϕ) & =R(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+ϕ)\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,$ we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ &=4 \\\\ R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ &=3\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSquaring the first and the second equations of the system and adding the results together, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)}^{2}+{(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)}^{2} & ={4}^{2}+{3}^{2} \\\\ {R}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡ϕ+{sin}^{2}⁡ϕ) & =16+9 \\\\ {R}^{2} & =25 \\\\ R & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we only consider the positive root since $R>0.$" "The sum formula for sine gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+ϕ) & =R(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+ϕ)\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,$ we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ &=4 \\\\ R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ &=3\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSquaring the first and the second equations of the system and adding the results together, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)}^{2}+{(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)}^{2} & ={4}^{2}+{3}^{2} \\\\ {R}^{2}({\\cos }^{2}⁡ϕ+{\\sin }^{2}⁡ϕ) & =16+9 \\\\ {R}^{2} & =25 \\\\ R & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we only consider the positive root since $R>0.$"] [:db/cas 17592186097056 :question/problem "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int x{log}_{2}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int x{\\log }_{2}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186097056 :question/worked-solution "We will solve this problem using integration by parts. We start by defining $u$ and ${v}^{′},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={log}_{2}⁡(3x)\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\ {v}^{′}=x\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int x\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x{log}_{2}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={log}_{2}⁡(3x)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}\\right)-\\int \\left(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}\\right)\\left(\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}{log}_{2}⁡(3x)}{2}-\\frac{1}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\int x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)-1)+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an integral constant.\n\nNote that in the above calculation we used the change of base formula ${log}_{2}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}.$" "We will solve this problem using integration by parts. We start by defining $u$ and ${v}^{′},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={\\log }_{2}⁡(3x)\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\ {v}^{′}=x\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int x\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x{\\log }_{2}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\log }_{2}⁡(3x)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}\\right)-\\int \\left(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}\\right)\\left(\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}{\\log }_{2}⁡(3x)}{2}-\\frac{1}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\int x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)-1)+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an integral constant.\n\nNote that in the above calculation we used the change of base formula ${\\log }_{2}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186097057 :question/problem "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{{log}_{3}⁡x}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{{\\log }_{3}⁡x}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186097057 :question/worked-solution "We will solve this problem using integration by parts. We start by defining $u$ and ${v}^{′},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={log}_{3}⁡(x)\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡3} \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x=-\\frac{1}{2{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{log}_{3}⁡x}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={log}_{3}⁡x\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{2{x}^{2}}\\right)-\\int \\left(-\\frac{1}{2{x}^{2}}\\right)\\left(\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{{log}_{3}⁡x}{2{x}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{{log}_{3}⁡x}{2{x}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{4{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{2{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}-\\frac{1}{4{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1}{4{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an integral constant.\n\nNote that in the above calculation we used the change of base formula ${log}_{3}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}.$" "We will solve this problem using integration by parts. We start by defining $u$ and ${v}^{′},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={\\log }_{3}⁡(x)\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡3} \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x=-\\frac{1}{2{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{\\log }_{3}⁡x}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\log }_{3}⁡x\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{2{x}^{2}}\\right)-\\int \\left(-\\frac{1}{2{x}^{2}}\\right)\\left(\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{{\\log }_{3}⁡x}{2{x}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{{\\log }_{3}⁡x}{2{x}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{4{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{2{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}-\\frac{1}{4{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1}{4{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an integral constant.\n\nNote that in the above calculation we used the change of base formula ${\\log }_{3}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186097201 :question/worked-solution "We use the quotient rule with the numerator $u(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(7x-2)$ and the denominator $v(x)=4x.$ \n\nDifferentiating $u(x)$ and $v(x),$ we have\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}(x)=7{sec}^{2}⁡(7x-2),\\,{v}^{′}(x)=4.\n$$\n\nNote that we used the chain rule to differentiate $u.$\n\nNow, applying the quotient rule and simplifying, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)-u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x)}{[v(x)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(7{sec}^{2}⁡(7x-2))(4x)-\\operatorname{tan}⁡(7x-2)(4)}{(4x)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4(7x{sec}^{2}⁡(7x-2)-\\operatorname{tan}⁡(7x-2))}{16{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{⧸}4(7x{sec}^{2}⁡(7x-2)-\\operatorname{tan}⁡(7x-2))}{\\text{⧸}4(4{x}^{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{7x{sec}^{2}⁡(7x-2)-\\operatorname{tan}⁡(7x-2)}{4{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We use the quotient rule with the numerator $u(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(7x-2)$ and the denominator $v(x)=4x.$ \n\nDifferentiating $u(x)$ and $v(x),$ we have\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}(x)=7{\\sec }^{2}⁡(7x-2),\\,{v}^{′}(x)=4.\n$$\n\nNote that we used the chain rule to differentiate $u.$\n\nNow, applying the quotient rule and simplifying, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)-u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x)}{[v(x)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(7{\\sec }^{2}⁡(7x-2))(4x)-\\operatorname{tan}⁡(7x-2)(4)}{(4x)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4(7x{\\sec }^{2}⁡(7x-2)-\\operatorname{tan}⁡(7x-2))}{16{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{⧸}4(7x{\\sec }^{2}⁡(7x-2)-\\operatorname{tan}⁡(7x-2))}{\\text{⧸}4(4{x}^{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{7x{\\sec }^{2}⁡(7x-2)-\\operatorname{tan}⁡(7x-2)}{4{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186097216 :question/worked-solution "The difference formula for cosine gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-ϕ) & =R(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-ϕ)\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,$ we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ &=\\sqrt{3} \\\\ R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ &=1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nDividing the second equation by the first, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ} & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡ϕ & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ ϕ & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the amplitude $R.$ Squaring the first and the second equations of the system and adding the results together, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)}^{2}+{(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)}^{2} & ={1}^{2}+(\\sqrt{3})^{2} \\\\ {R}^{2}({sin}^{2}⁡ϕ+{cos}^{2}⁡ϕ) & =1+3 \\\\ {R}^{2} & =4 \\\\ R & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we only consider the positive root since $R>0.$\n\nFinally, we can write the initial expression in amplitude-phase form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{6})\n$$" "The difference formula for cosine gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-ϕ) & =R(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-ϕ)\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,$ we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ &=\\sqrt{3} \\\\ R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ &=1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nDividing the second equation by the first, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ} & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡ϕ & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ ϕ & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the amplitude $R.$ Squaring the first and the second equations of the system and adding the results together, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)}^{2}+{(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)}^{2} & ={1}^{2}+(\\sqrt{3})^{2} \\\\ {R}^{2}({\\sin }^{2}⁡ϕ+{\\cos }^{2}⁡ϕ) & =1+3 \\\\ {R}^{2} & =4 \\\\ R & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we only consider the positive root since $R>0.$\n\nFinally, we can write the initial expression in amplitude-phase form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{6})\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186097272 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int {tan}^{2}⁡(2x+\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\text{d}x=\n$$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int {\\tan }^{2}⁡(2x+\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186097272 :question/worked-solution "From the identity  $1+{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta ={sec}^{2}⁡\\theta $ with $\\theta =2x+\\frac{\\pi }{6},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1+{tan}^{2}⁡(2x+\\frac{\\pi }{6}) & ={sec}^{2}⁡(2x+\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\ {tan}^{2}⁡(2x+\\frac{\\pi }{6}) & ={sec}^{2}⁡(2x+\\frac{\\pi }{6})-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our integral gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {tan}^{2}⁡(2x+\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\text{d}x & =\\int ({sec}^{2}⁡(2x+\\frac{\\pi }{6})-1)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {sec}^{2}⁡(2x+\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\text{d}x-\\int 1\\cdot \\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x+\\frac{\\pi }{6})-x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "From the identity  $1+{\\tan }^{2}⁡\\theta ={\\sec }^{2}⁡\\theta $ with $\\theta =2x+\\frac{\\pi }{6},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1+{\\tan }^{2}⁡(2x+\\frac{\\pi }{6}) & ={\\sec }^{2}⁡(2x+\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\ {\\tan }^{2}⁡(2x+\\frac{\\pi }{6}) & ={\\sec }^{2}⁡(2x+\\frac{\\pi }{6})-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our integral gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {\\tan }^{2}⁡(2x+\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\text{d}x & =\\int ({\\sec }^{2}⁡(2x+\\frac{\\pi }{6})-1)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {\\sec }^{2}⁡(2x+\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\text{d}x-\\int 1\\cdot \\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x+\\frac{\\pi }{6})-x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186097273 :question/problem "Evaluate \n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }(2{cos}^{2}⁡2x-{x}^{2})\\text{d}x.\n$$" "Evaluate \n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }(2{\\cos }^{2}⁡2x-{x}^{2})\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186097273 :question/worked-solution "Here, we use the double-angle formula for cosine that involves the ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta $ term, given by\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta =2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -1.\n$$\n\n \n\nRearranging, we have\n\n$$\n{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta +1).\n$$\n\nLetting $\\theta =2x$, we have\n\n$$\n2{cos}^{2}⁡2x=2[\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+1)]=\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+1.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }(2{cos}^{2}⁡2x-{x}^{2})\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+1-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x+x-\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3})|}_{\\pi }^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =[\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\pi +2\\pi -\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot 2{\\pi }^{3}]-[\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\pi +\\pi -\\frac{1}{3}{\\pi }^{3}] \\\\  & =2\\pi -\\frac{8{\\pi }^{3}}{3}-\\pi +\\frac{{\\pi }^{3}}{3} \\\\  & =\\pi -\\frac{7{\\pi }^{3}}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi -7{\\pi }^{3}}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi (3-7{\\pi }^{2})}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Here, we use the double-angle formula for cosine that involves the ${\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta $ term, given by\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta =2{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta -1.\n$$\n\n \n\nRearranging, we have\n\n$$\n{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta +1).\n$$\n\nLetting $\\theta =2x$, we have\n\n$$\n2{\\cos }^{2}⁡2x=2[\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+1)]=\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+1.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }(2{\\cos }^{2}⁡2x-{x}^{2})\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+1-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x+x-\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3})|}_{\\pi }^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =[\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\pi +2\\pi -\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot 2{\\pi }^{3}]-[\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\pi +\\pi -\\frac{1}{3}{\\pi }^{3}] \\\\  & =2\\pi -\\frac{8{\\pi }^{3}}{3}-\\pi +\\frac{{\\pi }^{3}}{3} \\\\  & =\\pi -\\frac{7{\\pi }^{3}}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi -7{\\pi }^{3}}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi (3-7{\\pi }^{2})}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186097275 :question/problem "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{\\pi /6}\\frac{9-9{tan}^{4}⁡x}{1-{tan}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{\\pi /6}\\frac{9-9{\\tan }^{4}⁡x}{1-{\\tan }^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186097275 :question/worked-solution "First, we select the identity that contains a ${tan}^{2}⁡x$ term. We can use the identity \n\n$$\n1+{tan}^{2}⁡x={sec}^{2}⁡x.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we compute the given integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{\\pi /6}\\frac{9-9{tan}^{4}⁡x}{1-{tan}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & =9{\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{\\pi /6}\\frac{1-{tan}^{4}⁡x}{1-{tan}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =9{\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{\\pi /6}\\frac{(1-{tan}^{2}⁡x)(1+{tan}^{2}⁡x)}{1-{tan}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =9{\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{\\pi /6}(1+{tan}^{2}⁡x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =9{\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{\\pi /6}{sec}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =9\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\,{|}_{-\\pi /6}^{\\pi /6} \\\\  & =9\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)-9\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right) \\\\  & =9\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}-9\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\right) \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{3}+3\\sqrt{3} \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we select the identity that contains a ${\\tan }^{2}⁡x$ term. We can use the identity \n\n$$\n1+{\\tan }^{2}⁡x={\\sec }^{2}⁡x.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we compute the given integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{\\pi /6}\\frac{9-9{\\tan }^{4}⁡x}{1-{\\tan }^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & =9{\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{\\pi /6}\\frac{1-{\\tan }^{4}⁡x}{1-{\\tan }^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =9{\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{\\pi /6}\\frac{(1-{\\tan }^{2}⁡x)(1+{\\tan }^{2}⁡x)}{1-{\\tan }^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =9{\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{\\pi /6}(1+{\\tan }^{2}⁡x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =9{\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{\\pi /6}{\\sec }^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =9\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\,{|}_{-\\pi /6}^{\\pi /6} \\\\  & =9\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)-9\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right) \\\\  & =9\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}-9\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\right) \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{3}+3\\sqrt{3} \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186097276 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int {cot}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}-x)\\text{d}x=\n$$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int {\\cot }^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}-x)\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186097276 :question/worked-solution "From the identity $1+{cot}^{2}⁡\\theta ={csc}^{2}⁡\\theta $ with $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}-x,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1+{cot}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}-x) & ={csc}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}-x) \\\\ {cot}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}-x) & ={csc}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}-x)-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our integral gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {cot}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}-x)\\text{d}x & =\\int ({csc}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}-x)-1)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {csc}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}-x)\\text{d}x-\\int 1\\cdot \\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}-x)-x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "From the identity $1+{\\cot }^{2}⁡\\theta ={\\csc }^{2}⁡\\theta $ with $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}-x,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1+{\\cot }^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}-x) & ={\\csc }^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}-x) \\\\ {\\cot }^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}-x) & ={\\csc }^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}-x)-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our integral gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {\\cot }^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}-x)\\text{d}x & =\\int ({\\csc }^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}-x)-1)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {\\csc }^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}-x)\\text{d}x-\\int 1\\cdot \\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}-x)-x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186097279 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(10{cos}^{2}⁡x-4{x}^{3}+10{sin}^{2}⁡x)\\text{d}x=\n$$" "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(10{\\cos }^{2}⁡x-4{x}^{3}+10{\\sin }^{2}⁡x)\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186097279 :question/worked-solution "We use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n{cos}^{2}⁡x+{sin}^{2}⁡x=1\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we compute the given integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(10{cos}^{2}⁡x-4{x}^{3}+10{sin}^{2}⁡x)\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(10({sin}^{2}⁡x+{cos}^{2}⁡x)-4{x}^{3})\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(10\\cdot 1-4{x}^{3})\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(10-4{x}^{3})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(10x-{x}^{4}){|}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =[10\\pi -{\\pi }^{4}]-[10\\cdot 0-{0}^{4}] \\\\  & =10\\pi -{\\pi }^{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n{\\cos }^{2}⁡x+{\\sin }^{2}⁡x=1\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we compute the given integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(10{\\cos }^{2}⁡x-4{x}^{3}+10{\\sin }^{2}⁡x)\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(10({\\sin }^{2}⁡x+{\\cos }^{2}⁡x)-4{x}^{3})\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(10\\cdot 1-4{x}^{3})\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(10-4{x}^{3})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(10x-{x}^{4}){|}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =[10\\pi -{\\pi }^{4}]-[10\\cdot 0-{0}^{4}] \\\\  & =10\\pi -{\\pi }^{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186097313 :question/worked-solution "We use a variation of the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n{sin}^{2}⁡x+{cos}^{2}⁡x=1\\,⟹\\,{cos}^{2}⁡x=1-{sin}^{2}⁡x.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we rewrite the denominator and calculate the resulting integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{1}{1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\cdot \\frac{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{1-{sin}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (\\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡x}+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{{cos}^{2}⁡x})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int ({sec}^{2}⁡x+\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+\\operatorname{sec}⁡x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We use a variation of the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n{\\sin }^{2}⁡x+{\\cos }^{2}⁡x=1\\,⟹\\,{\\cos }^{2}⁡x=1-{\\sin }^{2}⁡x.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we rewrite the denominator and calculate the resulting integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{1}{1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\cdot \\frac{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{1-{\\sin }^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{{\\cos }^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (\\frac{1}{{\\cos }^{2}⁡x}+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{{\\cos }^{2}⁡x})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int ({\\sec }^{2}⁡x+\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+\\operatorname{sec}⁡x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186097314 :question/problem "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int {sin}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int {\\sin }^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186097314 :question/worked-solution "Let's recall the double-angle formula for cosine that involves the ${sin}^{2}$ term: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x=1-2{sin}^{2}⁡x.\n$$\n\nWe can rearrange the above formula as\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)={sin}^{2}⁡x.\n$$\n\nWe can now integrate as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {sin}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{2}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int (1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(x-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}x-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let's recall the double-angle formula for cosine that involves the ${sin}^{2}$ term: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x=1-2{\\sin }^{2}⁡x.\n$$\n\nWe can rearrange the above formula as\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)={\\sin }^{2}⁡x.\n$$\n\nWe can now integrate as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {\\sin }^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{2}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int (1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(x-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}x-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186097817 :tutorial/content "For a general logarithm of base $a,$ the derivative is given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{ \\textrm{d} x}\\left( \\log_a x \\right)= \\dfrac{1}{x\\ln a}.\n$$\n\nAlso, recall that whenever $\\log$ is written without a base, we can assume that it represents the base $10.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\log x =  \\log_{10} x.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{dx}(\\operatorname{log}⁡x) & =\\frac{d}{dx}({log}_{10}⁡x)=\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** To see where this rule comes from, we can use the change-of-base formula as shown below.\n\n$$\n\\log_a x = \\dfrac{\\ln x}{\\ln a}\n$$\n\nTaking the derivative and using the fact that $\\ln a$ is a constant, then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({log}_{a}⁡x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡a}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡a}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡a}\\cdot \\frac{1}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡a}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "For a general logarithm of base $a,$ the derivative is given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{ \\textrm{d} x}\\left( \\log_a x \\right)= \\dfrac{1}{x\\ln a}.\n$$\n\nAlso, recall that whenever $\\log$ is written without a base, we can assume that it represents the base $10.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\log x =  \\log_{10} x.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{dx}(\\operatorname{log}⁡x) & =\\frac{d}{dx}({\\log }_{10}⁡x)=\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** To see where this rule comes from, we can use the change-of-base formula as shown below.\n\n$$\n\\log_a x = \\dfrac{\\ln x}{\\ln a}\n$$\n\nTaking the derivative and using the fact that $\\ln a$ is a constant, then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({\\log }_{a}⁡x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡a}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡a}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡a}\\cdot \\frac{1}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡a}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186097822 :tutorial/content "We can now prove that $(\\tan x)' = \\sec^2{x}$ using the quotient rule. Here's how! \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)^{′}\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)^{′}}{{cos}^{2}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}{{cos}^{2}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡x+{sin}^{2}⁡x}{{cos}^{2}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡x} \\\\  & ={sec}^{2}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We can now prove that $(\\tan x)' = \\sec^2{x}$ using the quotient rule. Here's how! \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)^{′}\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)^{′}}{{\\cos }^{2}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}{{\\cos }^{2}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\cos }^{2}⁡x+{\\sin }^{2}⁡x}{{\\cos }^{2}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{\\cos }^{2}⁡x} \\\\  & ={\\sec }^{2}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186097923 :tutorial/content "Let's recall the three double-angle formulas for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) & ={cos}^{2}⁡x-{sin}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & =2{cos}^{2}⁡x-1 \\\\  & =1-2{sin}^{2}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can use the double-angle formulas for cosine to integrate $\\sin^2{x}$ and $\\cos^2{x}.$ Let's take a look at some examples." "Let's recall the three double-angle formulas for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) & ={\\cos }^{2}⁡x-{\\sin }^{2}⁡x \\\\  & =2{\\cos }^{2}⁡x-1 \\\\  & =1-2{\\sin }^{2}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can use the double-angle formulas for cosine to integrate $\\sin^2{x}$ and $\\cos^2{x}.$ Let's take a look at some examples."] [:db/cas 17592186098464 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}cis\\left(\\frac{π}{3}\\right)" "\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{cis} \\left(\\frac{π}{3}\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186098465 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}cis\\left(\\frac{5π}{3}\\right)" "\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{cis} \\left(\\frac{5π}{3}\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186098466 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}cis\\left(\\frac{4π}{3}\\right)" "\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{cis} \\left(\\frac{4π}{3}\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186098467 :answer/value "3cis\\left(\\frac{5π}{3}\\right)" "3\\operatorname{cis} \\left(\\frac{5π}{3}\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186098468 :answer/value "3cis\\left(\\frac{π}{3}\\right)" "3\\operatorname{cis} \\left(\\frac{π}{3}\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186098470 :answer/value "15cis\\left(\\frac{π}{4}\\right)" "15\\operatorname{cis} \\left(\\frac{π}{4}\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186098471 :answer/value "15cis\\left(\\frac{3π}{4}\\right)" "15\\operatorname{cis} \\left(\\frac{3π}{4}\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186098472 :answer/value "\\frac{15}{2}cis\\left(\\frac{7π}{4}\\right)" "\\frac{15}{2}\\operatorname{cis} \\left(\\frac{7π}{4}\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186098473 :answer/value "\\frac{15}{2}cis\\left(\\frac{π}{4}\\right)" "\\frac{15}{2}\\operatorname{cis} \\left(\\frac{π}{4}\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186098474 :answer/value "15cis\\left(\\frac{5π}{4}\\right)" "15\\operatorname{cis} \\left(\\frac{5π}{4}\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186099580 :answer/value "2cis\\left(\\frac{π}{4}\\right)" "2\\operatorname{cis} \\left(\\frac{π}{4}\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186099581 :answer/value "2cis\\left(\\frac{7π}{4}\\right)" "2\\operatorname{cis} \\left(\\frac{7π}{4}\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186099582 :answer/value "2cis\\left(\\frac{5π}{4}\\right)" "2\\operatorname{cis} \\left(\\frac{5π}{4}\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186099583 :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}cis\\left(\\frac{5π}{4}\\right)" "\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cis} \\left(\\frac{5π}{4}\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186099584 :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}cis\\left(\\frac{π}{4}\\right)" "\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cis} \\left(\\frac{π}{4}\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186099586 :answer/value "4cis\\left(\\frac{π}{3}\\right)" "4\\operatorname{cis} \\left(\\frac{π}{3}\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186099587 :answer/value "8cis\\left(\\frac{2π}{3}\\right)" "8\\operatorname{cis} \\left(\\frac{2π}{3}\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186099588 :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}cis\\left(\\frac{π}{3}\\right)" "2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cis} \\left(\\frac{π}{3}\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186099589 :answer/value "8cis\\left(-\\frac{π}{3}\\right)" "8\\operatorname{cis} \\left(-\\frac{π}{3}\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186099590 :answer/value "8cis\\left(\\frac{π}{3}\\right)" "8\\operatorname{cis} \\left(\\frac{π}{3}\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186100600 :question/worked-solution "First, we write the fraction as a division:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{(-5)^{7}}{(-5)^3} = (-5)^{7} \\div (-5)^3\n$$\n\nNow, to divide powers of the same base, we simply subtract the exponents:\n\n$$\n(-5)^{7} \\div (-5)^3 = (-5)^{7-3} = (-5)^{4}\n$$" "First, we write the fraction as a division:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{(-5)^{7}}{(-5)^3} = (-5)^{7} \\operatorname{div}  (-5)^3\n$$\n\nNow, to divide powers of the same base, we simply subtract the exponents:\n\n$$\n(-5)^{7} \\operatorname{div}  (-5)^3 = (-5)^{7-3} = (-5)^{4}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186100601 :question/problem "Express $$\n\\displaystyle \\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^4 \\div \\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^2\n$$ as a base raised to a single exponent." "Express $$\n\\displaystyle \\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^4 \\operatorname{div}  \\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^2\n$$ as a base raised to a single exponent."] [:db/cas 17592186100601 :question/worked-solution "To divide powers of the same base, we simply subtract the exponents:\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^4 \\div \\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^2 = \\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^{4-2}= \\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^{2}\n$$" "To divide powers of the same base, we simply subtract the exponents:\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^4 \\operatorname{div}  \\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^2 = \\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^{4-2}= \\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^{2}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186100602 :question/worked-solution "First, we write the fraction as a division:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{0.2^5}{0.2^2} = 0.2^5 \\div 0.2^2\n$$\n\nNow, to divide powers of the same base, we simply subtract the exponents:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{0.2}^{5}÷{0.2}^{2}={0.2}^{5-2}={0.2}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we write the fraction as a division:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{0.2^5}{0.2^2} = 0.2^5 \\operatorname{div}  0.2^2\n$$\n\nNow, to divide powers of the same base, we simply subtract the exponents:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{0.2}^{5}÷{0.2}^{2}={0.2}^{5-2}={0.2}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186100609 :question/worked-solution "Let's rewrite our integral as\n\n$$\n\\int \\dfrac{(\\log_3 x)^2 }{x} \\,\\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nLet $u=\\log_3 x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = \\dfrac{1}{x\\ln 3}\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\\ln 3\\,\\textrm d u = \\dfrac 1 x \\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{({log}_{3}⁡x)^{2}}{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int ({log}_{3}⁡x)^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {u}^{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡3\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡3\\int {u}^{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡3\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}{u}^{3}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡3({log}_{3}⁡x)^{3}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡3{log}_{3}^{3}⁡x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let's rewrite our integral as\n\n$$\n\\int \\dfrac{(\\log_3 x)^2 }{x} \\,\\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nLet $u=\\log_3 x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = \\dfrac{1}{x\\ln 3}\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\\ln 3\\,\\textrm d u = \\dfrac 1 x \\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{({\\log }_{3}⁡x)^{2}}{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int ({\\log }_{3}⁡x)^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {u}^{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡3\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡3\\int {u}^{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡3\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}{u}^{3}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡3({\\log }_{3}⁡x)^{3}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡3{\\log }_{3}^{3}⁡x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186100701 :question/worked-solution "First, we construct a matrix where the columns are made up of the components of the corresponding vectors:\n\n$$\nM =\n\t{\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}3 & 6 \\\\ - 2 & - 4\\end{array} \\right]}\n$$\n\nThen, we find the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 6 \\\\ -2 & -4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =3\\cdot (-4)-6\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe absolute value of $\\det(M)$ gives the area $A$ of the parallelogram:\n\n$$\nA = |\\,0\\,| = 0\n$$" "First, we construct a matrix where the columns are made up of the components of the corresponding vectors:\n\n$$\nM =\n\t{\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}3 & 6 \\\\ - 2 & - 4\\end{array} \\right]}\n$$\n\nThen, we find the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\det (M) & =\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 6 \\\\ -2 & -4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =3\\cdot (-4)-6\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe absolute value of $\\det(M)$ gives the area $A$ of the parallelogram:\n\n$$\nA = |\\,0\\,| = 0\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186100714 :question/worked-solution "First, let's make the trigonometric functions the subject of each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{csc}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{csc}⁡t=x \\\\ y=2\\operatorname{cot}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{cot}⁡t=\\frac{y}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing the Pythagorean identity $\\sin^{2}{t}+\\cos^{2}{t}=1$ by $\\sin^{2}{t},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡t}{{sin}^{2}⁡t}+\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡t}{{sin}^{2}⁡t} & =\\frac{1}{{sin}^{2}⁡t} \\\\ 1+{\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}\\right)}^{2} & ={\\left(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}\\right)}^{2} \\\\ 1+{cot}^{2}⁡t & ={csc}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\csc{t} = x$ and $\\cot t =\\dfrac{y}{2}$ into this formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1+{\\left(\\frac{y}{2}\\right)}^{2} & ={x}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{4} & ={x}^{2}-1 \\\\ {y}^{2} & =4({x}^{2}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $y^2 = 4\\left( x^2-1 \\right).$\n\nFinally, since $0 < t < \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$ and $x=\\csc t,$ the interval on the $x$-axis is $x \\geq 1.$" "First, let's make the trigonometric functions the subject of each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{csc}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{csc}⁡t=x \\\\ y=2\\operatorname{cot}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{cot}⁡t=\\frac{y}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing the Pythagorean identity $\\sin^{2}{t}+\\cos^{2}{t}=1$ by $\\sin^{2}{t},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\sin }^{2}⁡t}{{\\sin }^{2}⁡t}+\\frac{{\\cos }^{2}⁡t}{{\\sin }^{2}⁡t} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sin }^{2}⁡t} \\\\ 1+{\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}\\right)}^{2} & ={\\left(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}\\right)}^{2} \\\\ 1+{\\cot }^{2}⁡t & ={\\csc }^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\csc{t} = x$ and $\\cot t =\\dfrac{y}{2}$ into this formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1+{\\left(\\frac{y}{2}\\right)}^{2} & ={x}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{4} & ={x}^{2}-1 \\\\ {y}^{2} & =4({x}^{2}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $y^2 = 4\\left( x^2-1 \\right).$\n\nFinally, since $0 < t < \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$ and $x=\\csc t,$ the interval on the $x$-axis is $x \\geq 1.$"] [:db/cas 17592186100730 :question/worked-solution "To compute the product of two complex numbers\n\n$$\nz_1 = r_1\\textrm{cis}\\,\\theta_1\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nz_2 = r_2\\textrm{cis}\\,\\theta_2\n$$\n\nwe use the formula\n\n$$\nz_1z_2 = r_1 r_2\\,\\textrm{cis}\\left(\\theta_1 + \\theta_2\\right)\n$$\n\nSubstituting $r_1 = 5$, $r_2 = 3$,\n\n$$\n\\theta_1 = \\dfrac{5\\pi}{6}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\theta_2 = \\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nz_{1}z_{2} &= 5 \\cdot 3 \\cdot cis(\\frac{5π}{6} + \\frac{π}{3}) \\\\\n&= 15cis\\left(\\frac{7π}{6}\\right)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy expanding the $\\textrm{cis}$ notation and evaluating the sine and cosine, we can get the expression in Cartesian form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nz_{1}z_{2} &= 15cis\\left(\\frac{7π}{6}\\right) \\\\\n&= 15[\\cos \\left(\\frac{7π}{6}\\right) + \\textrm{i}\\sin \\left(\\frac{7π}{6}\\right)] \\\\\n&= 15[- \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} + \\textrm{i}\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)] \\\\\n&=-\\frac{15}{2}(\\sqrt{3} + \\textrm{i})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" "To compute the product of two complex numbers\n\n$$\nz_1 = r_1\\textrm{cis}\\,\\theta_1\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nz_2 = r_2\\textrm{cis}\\,\\theta_2\n$$\n\nwe use the formula\n\n$$\nz_1z_2 = r_1 r_2\\,\\textrm{cis}\\left(\\theta_1 + \\theta_2\\right)\n$$\n\nSubstituting $r_1 = 5$, $r_2 = 3$,\n\n$$\n\\theta_1 = \\dfrac{5\\pi}{6}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\theta_2 = \\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nz_{1}z_{2} &= 5 \\cdot 3 \\cdot \\operatorname{cis} (\\frac{5π}{6} + \\frac{π}{3}) \\\\\n&= 15\\operatorname{cis} \\left(\\frac{7π}{6}\\right)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy expanding the $\\textrm{cis}$ notation and evaluating the sine and cosine, we can get the expression in Cartesian form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nz_{1}z_{2} &= 15\\operatorname{cis} \\left(\\frac{7π}{6}\\right) \\\\\n&= 15[\\cos \\left(\\frac{7π}{6}\\right) + \\textrm{i}\\sin \\left(\\frac{7π}{6}\\right)] \\\\\n&= 15[- \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} + \\textrm{i}\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)] \\\\\n&=-\\frac{15}{2}(\\sqrt{3} + \\textrm{i})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186100731 :question/worked-solution "To compute the quotient of two complex numbers\n\n$$\nz_1 = r_1\\textrm{cis}\\,\\theta_1\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nz_2 = r_2\\textrm{cis}\\,\\theta_2\n$$\n\nwe use the formula\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{z_1}{z_2} = \\dfrac{r_1}{r_2}\\,\\textrm{cis}\\left(\\theta_1 - \\theta_2\\right)\n$$\n\nSubstituting $r_1 = 3$, $r_2 = 9$,\n\n$$\n\\theta_1 = \\dfrac{3\\pi}{4}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\theta_2 = \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{z_{1}}{z_{2}} &= \\frac{3}{9}cis(\\frac{3π}{4} - \\frac{π}{2}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3}cis\\left(\\frac{π}{4}\\right)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy expanding the $\\textrm{cis}$ notation and evaluating the sine and cosine, we can get the expression in Cartesian form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{z_{1}}{z_{2}} &= \\frac{1}{3}cis\\left(\\frac{π}{4}\\right) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3}[\\cos \\left(\\frac{π}{4}\\right) + \\textrm{i}\\sin \\left(\\frac{π}{4}\\right)] \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3}[\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} + \\textrm{i}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}] \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{6}(\\sqrt{2} + \\sqrt{2}\\textrm{i})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" "To compute the quotient of two complex numbers\n\n$$\nz_1 = r_1\\textrm{cis}\\,\\theta_1\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nz_2 = r_2\\textrm{cis}\\,\\theta_2\n$$\n\nwe use the formula\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{z_1}{z_2} = \\dfrac{r_1}{r_2}\\,\\textrm{cis}\\left(\\theta_1 - \\theta_2\\right)\n$$\n\nSubstituting $r_1 = 3$, $r_2 = 9$,\n\n$$\n\\theta_1 = \\dfrac{3\\pi}{4}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\theta_2 = \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{z_{1}}{z_{2}} &= \\frac{3}{9}\\operatorname{cis} (\\frac{3π}{4} - \\frac{π}{2}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{cis} \\left(\\frac{π}{4}\\right)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy expanding the $\\textrm{cis}$ notation and evaluating the sine and cosine, we can get the expression in Cartesian form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{z_{1}}{z_{2}} &= \\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{cis} \\left(\\frac{π}{4}\\right) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3}[\\cos \\left(\\frac{π}{4}\\right) + \\textrm{i}\\sin \\left(\\frac{π}{4}\\right)] \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3}[\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} + \\textrm{i}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}] \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{6}(\\sqrt{2} + \\sqrt{2}\\textrm{i})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186100737 :question/worked-solution "The number $\\textrm{cis}\\,\\theta$ corresponds to\n\n$$\n\\textrm{cis}\\,\\theta = \\cos \\theta + \\textrm{i}\\sin \\theta\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4cis\\left(\\frac{π}{6}\\right) &= 4[\\cos \\left(\\frac{π}{6}\\right) + \\textrm{i}\\sin \\left(\\frac{π}{6}\\right)] \\\\\n&= 4[\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} + \\textrm{i}\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)] \\\\\n&= 2\\sqrt{3} + 2\\textrm{i}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The number $\\textrm{cis}\\,\\theta$ corresponds to\n\n$$\n\\textrm{cis}\\,\\theta = \\cos \\theta + \\textrm{i}\\sin \\theta\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4\\operatorname{cis} \\left(\\frac{π}{6}\\right) &= 4[\\cos \\left(\\frac{π}{6}\\right) + \\textrm{i}\\sin \\left(\\frac{π}{6}\\right)] \\\\\n&= 4[\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} + \\textrm{i}\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)] \\\\\n&= 2\\sqrt{3} + 2\\textrm{i}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186101145 :question/problem "Calculate the integral $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{5}{x{ln}^{2}⁡(3x)}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" "Calculate the integral $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{5}{x{\\ln }^{2}⁡(3x)}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186101145 :question/worked-solution "Let's set $u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x).$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{3x}\\cdot 3=\\frac{1}{x}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{x}.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{5}{x{ln}^{2}⁡(3x)}\\,\\text{d}x & =5\\int \\frac{1}{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x))^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{x} \\\\  & =5\\int \\frac{1}{{u}^{2}}\\cdot \\text{d}u \\\\  & =-5\\cdot {u}^{-1}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{u}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let's set $u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x).$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{3x}\\cdot 3=\\frac{1}{x}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{x}.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{5}{x{\\ln }^{2}⁡(3x)}\\,\\text{d}x & =5\\int \\frac{1}{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x))^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{x} \\\\  & =5\\int \\frac{1}{{u}^{2}}\\cdot \\text{d}u \\\\  & =-5\\cdot {u}^{-1}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{u}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186101160 :question/worked-solution "According to the candidates test, the maximum value of $f(x)$ on $[-2,2]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval. \n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-2,2),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)\\ne 0$ on this interval. Therefore, the candidates are $x=-2,2.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-2) & =2 \\\\ f(2) & =f(-2)+{\\int }_{-2}^{2}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2+7 \\\\  & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the largest value of $f$ from our candidates. \n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{max}}=max\\{2,9\\}=9.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{max}}=9,$ and the value of $x$ that maximizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{max}}=2.$" "According to the candidates test, the maximum value of $f(x)$ on $[-2,2]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval. \n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-2,2),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)\\ne 0$ on this interval. Therefore, the candidates are $x=-2,2.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-2) & =2 \\\\ f(2) & =f(-2)+{\\int }_{-2}^{2}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2+7 \\\\  & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the largest value of $f$ from our candidates. \n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{max}}=\\max \\{2,9\\}=9.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{max}}=9,$ and the value of $x$ that maximizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{max}}=2.$"] [:db/cas 17592186101169 :question/worked-solution "According to the candidates test, the minimum value of $f(x)$ on $[-1,5]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval.\n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-1,5),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)\\ne 0$ on this interval. Therefore, the candidates are $x=-1,5.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-1) & =1 \\\\ f(5) & =f(-1)+{\\int }_{-1}^{5}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =1+6 \\\\  & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the smallest value of $f$ from our candidates.\n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{min}}=min\\{1,7\\}=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{min}}=1,$ and the value of $x$ that minimizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{min}}=-1.$" "According to the candidates test, the minimum value of $f(x)$ on $[-1,5]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval.\n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-1,5),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)\\ne 0$ on this interval. Therefore, the candidates are $x=-1,5.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-1) & =1 \\\\ f(5) & =f(-1)+{\\int }_{-1}^{5}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =1+6 \\\\  & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the smallest value of $f$ from our candidates.\n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{min}}=\\min \\{1,7\\}=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{min}}=1,$ and the value of $x$ that minimizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{min}}=-1.$"] [:db/cas 17592186101176 :question/problem "If $z_{1} = 2cis\\left(\\frac{π}{6}\\right)$ and $z_{2} = 3cis\\left(\\frac{π}{3}\\right)$, calculate the product $z_{1}z_{2}$." "If $z_{1} = 2\\operatorname{cis} \\left(\\frac{π}{6}\\right)$ and $z_{2} = 3\\operatorname{cis} \\left(\\frac{π}{3}\\right)$, calculate the product $z_{1}z_{2}$."] [:db/cas 17592186101188 :question/problem "What is $$\n\\displaystyle (3cis\\left(\\frac{2π}{3}\\right))/(9cis\\left(\\frac{π}{3}\\right))\n$$?" "What is $$\n\\displaystyle (3\\operatorname{cis} \\left(\\frac{2π}{3}\\right))/(9\\operatorname{cis} \\left(\\frac{π}{3}\\right))\n$$?"] [:db/cas 17592186101189 :question/problem "What is $$\n\\displaystyle 5cis\\left(\\frac{3π}{4}\\right) \\cdot 3cis\\left(\\frac{π}{2}\\right)\n$$?" "What is $$\n\\displaystyle 5\\operatorname{cis} \\left(\\frac{3π}{4}\\right) \\cdot 3\\operatorname{cis} \\left(\\frac{π}{2}\\right)\n$$?"] [:db/cas 17592186101190 :question/problem "If $z_{1} = 6cis\\left(\\frac{5π}{12}\\right)$ and $z_{2} = 9cis\\left(\\frac{π}{12}\\right)$, calculate the quotient $\\frac{z_{1}}{z_{2}}$." "If $z_{1} = 6\\operatorname{cis} \\left(\\frac{5π}{12}\\right)$ and $z_{2} = 9\\operatorname{cis} \\left(\\frac{π}{12}\\right)$, calculate the quotient $\\frac{z_{1}}{z_{2}}$."] [:db/cas 17592186101284 :question/worked-solution "According to the candidates test, the maximum value of $f(x)$ on $[-3,3]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval. \n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-3,3),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ at $x=0.$ Therefore, the candidates are $x=-3,0,3.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-3) & =-6 \\\\ f(0) & =f(-3)+{\\int }_{-3}^{0}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-6-4 \\\\  & =-10 \\\\ f(3) & =f(-3)+{\\int }_{-3}^{3}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-6-4+2 \\\\  & =-8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the largest value of $f$ from our candidates. \n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{max}}=max\\{-6,-10,-8\\}=-6.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{max}}=-6,$ and the value of $x$ that maximizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{max}}=-3.$" "According to the candidates test, the maximum value of $f(x)$ on $[-3,3]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval. \n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-3,3),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ at $x=0.$ Therefore, the candidates are $x=-3,0,3.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-3) & =-6 \\\\ f(0) & =f(-3)+{\\int }_{-3}^{0}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-6-4 \\\\  & =-10 \\\\ f(3) & =f(-3)+{\\int }_{-3}^{3}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-6-4+2 \\\\  & =-8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the largest value of $f$ from our candidates. \n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{max}}=\\max \\{-6,-10,-8\\}=-6.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{max}}=-6,$ and the value of $x$ that maximizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{max}}=-3.$"] [:db/cas 17592186101371 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{{log}_{2}⁡x}{x}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{{\\log }_{2}⁡x}{x}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186101371 :question/worked-solution "Let $u={log}_{2}⁡x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\,⟹\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\,\\text{d}u=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{x}.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{log}_{2}⁡x}{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {log}_{2}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{x} \\\\  & =\\int u\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\int u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\cdot \\frac{{u}^{2}}{2}+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\cdot \\frac{{log}_{2}^{2}⁡x}{2}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let $u={\\log }_{2}⁡x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\,⟹\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\,\\text{d}u=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{x}.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{\\log }_{2}⁡x}{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {\\log }_{2}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{x} \\\\  & =\\int u\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\int u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\cdot \\frac{{u}^{2}}{2}+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\cdot \\frac{{\\log }_{2}^{2}⁡x}{2}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186101374 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{{log}_{3}⁡({x}^{2})}{x}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{{\\log }_{3}⁡({x}^{2})}{x}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186101374 :question/worked-solution "Let $u={log}_{3}⁡({x}^{2}).$Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{2}\\,\\text{d}u=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{x}.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{log}_{3}⁡({x}^{2})}{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {log}_{3}⁡({x}^{2})\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{x} \\\\  & =\\int u\\cdot (\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{2}\\,\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{2}\\int u\\cdot \\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{2}\\cdot \\frac{{u}^{2}}{2}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3{log}_{3}^{2}⁡({x}^{2})}{4}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let $u={\\log }_{3}⁡({x}^{2}).$Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{2}\\,\\text{d}u=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{x}.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{\\log }_{3}⁡({x}^{2})}{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {\\log }_{3}⁡({x}^{2})\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{x} \\\\  & =\\int u\\cdot (\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{2}\\,\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{2}\\int u\\cdot \\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{2}\\cdot \\frac{{u}^{2}}{2}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3{\\log }_{3}^{2}⁡({x}^{2})}{4}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186101405 :question/problem "Express $3cis(2)$ in the form $x + \\textrm{i}y$. Round your answer to $2$ decimal places.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." "Express $3\\operatorname{cis} (2)$ in the form $x + \\textrm{i}y$. Round your answer to $2$ decimal places.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question."] [:db/cas 17592186101406 :question/problem "$6cis\\left(\\frac{π}{4}\\right) =$\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." "$6\\operatorname{cis} \\left(\\frac{π}{4}\\right) =$\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question."] [:db/cas 17592186101475 :question/worked-solution "According to the candidates test, the minimum value of $f(x)$ on $[-2,2]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval.\n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-2,2),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ at $x=-1.$ Therefore, the candidates are $x=-2,-1,2.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-2) & =-5 \\\\ f(-1) & =f(-2)+{\\int }_{-2}^{-1}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-5-1 \\\\  & =-6 \\\\ f(2) & =f(-2)+{\\int }_{-2}^{2}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-5-1+5 \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the smallest value of $f$ from our candidates.\n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{min}}=min\\{-5,-6,-1\\}=-6.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{min}}=-6,$ and the value of $x$ that minimizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{min}}=-1.$" "According to the candidates test, the minimum value of $f(x)$ on $[-2,2]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval.\n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-2,2),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ at $x=-1.$ Therefore, the candidates are $x=-2,-1,2.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-2) & =-5 \\\\ f(-1) & =f(-2)+{\\int }_{-2}^{-1}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-5-1 \\\\  & =-6 \\\\ f(2) & =f(-2)+{\\int }_{-2}^{2}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-5-1+5 \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the smallest value of $f$ from our candidates.\n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{min}}=\\min \\{-5,-6,-1\\}=-6.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{min}}=-6,$ and the value of $x$ that minimizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{min}}=-1.$"] [:db/cas 17592186101510 :question/problem "What is $3^{8} \\div 3^{4}$?" "What is $3^{8} \\operatorname{div}  3^{4}$?"] [:db/cas 17592186101513 :question/problem "What is $$\n\\displaystyle \\left(-\\frac{1}{3}\\right)^{7} \\div \\left(-\\frac{1}{3}\\right)^{5}\n$$?" "What is $$\n\\displaystyle \\left(-\\frac{1}{3}\\right)^{7} \\operatorname{div}  \\left(-\\frac{1}{3}\\right)^{5}\n$$?"] [:db/cas 17592186101515 :question/problem "What is $(-0.5)^{8} \\div (-0.5)^{2}$?" "What is $(-0.5)^{8} \\operatorname{div}  (-0.5)^{2}$?"] [:db/cas 17592186101517 :question/problem "What is $3^{-5} \\div 3^{-2}$?" "What is $3^{-5} \\operatorname{div}  3^{-2}$?"] [:db/cas 17592186101527 :question/worked-solution "First, we construct a matrix where the columns are made up of the components of the corresponding vectors:\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & \\sqrt{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThen, we find the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & \\sqrt{2}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =0\\cdot \\sqrt{2}-0\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe absolute value of $det(M)$ gives the area $A$ of the parallelogram:\n\n$$\nA=|\\,0\\,|=0\n$$" "First, we construct a matrix where the columns are made up of the components of the corresponding vectors:\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & \\sqrt{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThen, we find the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\det (M) & =\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & \\sqrt{2}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =0\\cdot \\sqrt{2}-0\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe absolute value of $\\det (M)$ gives the area $A$ of the parallelogram:\n\n$$\nA=|\\,0\\,|=0\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186101535 :question/problem "What is the value of $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{2}^{4}\\frac{4}{x{log}_{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x?\n$$" "What is the value of $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{2}^{4}\\frac{4}{x{\\log }_{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x?\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186101535 :question/worked-solution "Let $u={log}_{2}⁡x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\,⟹\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\,\\text{d}u=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{x}.\n$$\n\nCalculating the limits for $u$ gives the following:\n\n| $x$ | $2$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n\nWe now substitute and evaluate as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2}^{4}\\frac{4}{x{log}_{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & =4{\\int }_{2}^{4}\\frac{1}{{log}_{2}⁡x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{x} \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{u}\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|){|}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\cdot (\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\operatorname{ln}⁡1) \\\\  & =4{ln}^{2}⁡2\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let $u={\\log }_{2}⁡x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\,⟹\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\,\\text{d}u=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{x}.\n$$\n\nCalculating the limits for $u$ gives the following:\n\n| $x$ | $2$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n\nWe now substitute and evaluate as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2}^{4}\\frac{4}{x{\\log }_{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & =4{\\int }_{2}^{4}\\frac{1}{{\\log }_{2}⁡x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{x} \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{u}\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|){|}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\cdot (\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\operatorname{ln}⁡1) \\\\  & =4{\\ln }^{2}⁡2\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186101536 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{1}{x{log}_{7}⁡(4x)}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{1}{x{\\log }_{7}⁡(4x)}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186101536 :question/worked-solution "Let $u={log}_{7}⁡(4x).$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡7}\\,⟹\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡7\\,\\text{d}u=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{x}.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{x{log}_{7}⁡(4x)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{{log}_{7}⁡(4x)}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{x} \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡7\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡7\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\cdot \\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡7\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡7\\operatorname{ln}⁡|{log}_{7}⁡(4x)|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let $u={\\log }_{7}⁡(4x).$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡7}\\,⟹\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡7\\,\\text{d}u=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{x}.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{x{\\log }_{7}⁡(4x)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{{\\log }_{7}⁡(4x)}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{x} \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡7\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡7\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\cdot \\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡7\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡7\\operatorname{ln}⁡|{\\log }_{7}⁡(4x)|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186101963 :tutorial/content "Suppose we have two vectors $\\textbf{u}=\\langle 1, 3 \\rangle$ and $\\textbf{v}=\\langle 2,-1 \\rangle.$ The parallelogram spanned by $\\textbf{u}$ and $\\textbf{v}$ is shown in the picture below:\n\n![](images/10/102f72ba6730d1b07ca0d01186a1ff7851954193f00dc74eb7150981844b0222.png)\n\nNow, we construct a new matrix\n\n<div class=\"matrix-operands matrix-operands-stacked\">\n<div>$M =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n</div>\n\nwhere the columns are made up of the components of the corresponding vectors. Let's find the determinant of $M\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-1)-2\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =-7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIt turns out that the absolute value of $\\det(M)$ gives the area $A$ of the parallelogram:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|det(M)| \\\\  & =|-7| \\\\  & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Suppose we have two vectors $\\textbf{u}=\\langle 1, 3 \\rangle$ and $\\textbf{v}=\\langle 2,-1 \\rangle.$ The parallelogram spanned by $\\textbf{u}$ and $\\textbf{v}$ is shown in the picture below:\n\n![](images/10/102f72ba6730d1b07ca0d01186a1ff7851954193f00dc74eb7150981844b0222.png)\n\nNow, we construct a new matrix\n\n<div class=\"matrix-operands matrix-operands-stacked\">\n<div>$M =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n</div>\n\nwhere the columns are made up of the components of the corresponding vectors. Let's find the determinant of $M\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\det (M) & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-1)-2\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =-7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIt turns out that the absolute value of $\\det(M)$ gives the area $A$ of the parallelogram:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\det (M)| \\\\  & =|-7| \\\\  & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186101964 :tutorial/content "Why does the absolute value of the determinant represent the area of the parallelogram? Let's prove this result.\n\nFirst, let's state the result precisely. Consider two vectors $\\textbf{u}=\\langle a,b \\rangle$ and $\\textbf{v}=\\langle c,d \\rangle.$ The parallelogram spanned by $\\textbf{u}$ and $\\textbf{v}$ is shown in the picture below:\n\n![](images/3a/3a466fd3c52ea345d80b9ba67ac104efef29bfd7eeed0e4567364c80f3212c0d.png)\n\nWe construct a new matrix\n\n<div class=\"matrix-operands matrix-operands-stacked\">\n<div>$M =\\begin{bmatrix}a & c \\\\ b & d\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n</div>\n\nwhere the columns are made up of the components of the corresponding vectors. Then the absolute value of $\\det(M)$ gives the area $A$ of the parallelogram spanned by $\\textbf{u}$ and $\\textbf{v}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\nA = |\\det(M)|\n$$\n\nTo prove the formula, we use the following picture:\n\n![](images/3d/3da110b114d2038738bd4ef06cb031bb8b66aee062ffc83955dfdaade72584e1.png)\n\nWe can compute the area by starting with the area of the outer rectangle, and then subtracting off the area of the smaller triangles and rectangles surrounding the parallelogram:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|(a+c)(b+d)-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}cd-2\\cdot ad-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}ab| \\\\  & =|ab+ad+bc+cd-cd-2ad-ab| \\\\  & =|bc-ad| \\\\  & =|ad-bc| \\\\  & =|det(M)|\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Why does the absolute value of the determinant represent the area of the parallelogram? Let's prove this result.\n\nFirst, let's state the result precisely. Consider two vectors $\\textbf{u}=\\langle a,b \\rangle$ and $\\textbf{v}=\\langle c,d \\rangle.$ The parallelogram spanned by $\\textbf{u}$ and $\\textbf{v}$ is shown in the picture below:\n\n![](images/3a/3a466fd3c52ea345d80b9ba67ac104efef29bfd7eeed0e4567364c80f3212c0d.png)\n\nWe construct a new matrix\n\n<div class=\"matrix-operands matrix-operands-stacked\">\n<div>$M =\\begin{bmatrix}a & c \\\\ b & d\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n</div>\n\nwhere the columns are made up of the components of the corresponding vectors. Then the absolute value of $\\det(M)$ gives the area $A$ of the parallelogram spanned by $\\textbf{u}$ and $\\textbf{v}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\nA = |\\det(M)|\n$$\n\nTo prove the formula, we use the following picture:\n\n![](images/3d/3da110b114d2038738bd4ef06cb031bb8b66aee062ffc83955dfdaade72584e1.png)\n\nWe can compute the area by starting with the area of the outer rectangle, and then subtracting off the area of the smaller triangles and rectangles surrounding the parallelogram:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|(a+c)(b+d)-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}cd-2\\cdot ad-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}ab| \\\\  & =|ab+ad+bc+cd-cd-2ad-ab| \\\\  & =|bc-ad| \\\\  & =|ad-bc| \\\\  & =|\\det (M)|\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186101966 :tutorial/content "When two vectors are parallel, the parallelogram does not exist. So, it has zero area.\n\n![](images/b6/b65c64ccf16764f99c0c30dbf6b37ce7b6254b83496512a5d207dddab095dac7.png)\n\nAs a result, the determinant of a matrix whose columns are parallel vectors must also be zero. \n\nFor example, in the diagram above, we have the vectors $\\textbf{u} = \\left< 1,1 \\right>$ and $\\textbf{v} = \\left< 2,2 \\right>.$ Constructing a matrix where the columns are made up of the components of the corresponding vectors, we have\n\n<div class=\"matrix-operands matrix-operands-stacked\">\n<div>$M =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n</div>\n\nIndeed, the determinant does come out to zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\cdot 2-2\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" "When two vectors are parallel, the parallelogram does not exist. So, it has zero area.\n\n![](images/b6/b65c64ccf16764f99c0c30dbf6b37ce7b6254b83496512a5d207dddab095dac7.png)\n\nAs a result, the determinant of a matrix whose columns are parallel vectors must also be zero. \n\nFor example, in the diagram above, we have the vectors $\\textbf{u} = \\left< 1,1 \\right>$ and $\\textbf{v} = \\left< 2,2 \\right>.$ Constructing a matrix where the columns are made up of the components of the corresponding vectors, we have\n\n<div class=\"matrix-operands matrix-operands-stacked\">\n<div>$M =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n</div>\n\nIndeed, the determinant does come out to zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\det (M) & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\cdot 2-2\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186102023 :tutorial/content "To divide powers of the same base, we simply *subtract* the exponents. This is known as the **quotient rule for exponents**. For instance, in the expression \n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}3}^5 \\div {\\color{blue}3}^2,\n$$\n\nboth powers have the same base $({\\color{blue}3})$. Therefore, we can simplify this expression by subtracting the exponents:\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}3}^5 \\div {\\color{blue}3}^2 = {\\color{blue}3}^{5-2} = {\\color{blue}3}^3.\n$$\n\n**Note:** The quotient rule for exponents is really just a quicker alternative to the following process:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{5}÷{3}^{2} &= {3}^{3+2}÷{3}^{2} \\\\ &= {3}^{3}\\times \\underbrace{{3}^{2}÷{3}^{2}}_{=1} \\\\ &= {3}^{3}\\times 1 \\\\ &= {3}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "To divide powers of the same base, we simply *subtract* the exponents. This is known as the **quotient rule for exponents**. For instance, in the expression \n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}3}^5 \\operatorname{div}  {\\color{blue}3}^2,\n$$\n\nboth powers have the same base $({\\color{blue}3})$. Therefore, we can simplify this expression by subtracting the exponents:\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}3}^5 \\operatorname{div}  {\\color{blue}3}^2 = {\\color{blue}3}^{5-2} = {\\color{blue}3}^3.\n$$\n\n**Note:** The quotient rule for exponents is really just a quicker alternative to the following process:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{5}÷{3}^{2} &= {3}^{3+2}÷{3}^{2} \\\\ &= {3}^{3}\\times \\underbrace{{3}^{2}÷{3}^{2}}_{=1} \\\\ &= {3}^{3}\\times 1 \\\\ &= {3}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186102024 :tutorial/content "We can use the quotient rule for exponents with negative exponents too. For example, to simplify $3^{-5}\\div 3^{-3},$ we subtract the exponents:\n\n$$\n3^{-5} \\div 3^{-3} = 3^{-5-(-3)} = 3^{-2}\n$$\n\nNow, to evaluate $3^{-2},$ we find the reciprocal of the base $(3)$ and then raise that reciprocal to the same power but with the opposite sign:\n\n$$\n3^{-2}  = \\left(\\dfrac{1}{3}\\right)^2 =\\dfrac{1}{3} \\times \\dfrac{1}{3} = \\dfrac{1}{9}\n$$" "We can use the quotient rule for exponents with negative exponents too. For example, to simplify $3^{-5}\\operatorname{div}  3^{-3},$ we subtract the exponents:\n\n$$\n3^{-5} \\operatorname{div}  3^{-3} = 3^{-5-(-3)} = 3^{-2}\n$$\n\nNow, to evaluate $3^{-2},$ we find the reciprocal of the base $(3)$ and then raise that reciprocal to the same power but with the opposite sign:\n\n$$\n3^{-2}  = \\left(\\dfrac{1}{3}\\right)^2 =\\dfrac{1}{3} \\times \\dfrac{1}{3} = \\dfrac{1}{9}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186102029 :tutorial/content "Let's think about how to calculate an integral like $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\dfrac {\\ln x} {x} \\,\\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nNotice that this integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\int \\dfrac {\\ln x} {x} \\,\\textrm d x = \\int \\underbrace{\\ln x}_{u} \\cdot\\overbrace{\\dfrac {1} {x}}^{u'} \\,\\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nWe've seen integrals like this before, where the integrand consists of a function $u(x)$ multiplied by its derivative $u'(x).$ Such integrals can be solved by substitution.\n\nSo, we let $u = \\ln x,$ which gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}= \\dfrac 1 x\\quad   \\Longrightarrow \\quad \\dfrac 1 x \\,\\textrm{d}x={\\textrm{d}u}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we can calculate our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n       \\int \\dfrac{\\ln x}{x}\\, \\textrm{d}x \t&=\\int \\ln x\\cdot \\dfrac {1} {x} \\,\\textrm d x \\\\[5pt]\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t& = \\int u \\, \\textrm{d} u \\\\[5pt]\n                                                                &=  \\dfrac{1}{2}u^2  +C \\\\[5pt]   \n                                                                &= \\dfrac{1}{2}\\ln^2 x +C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAfter solving an integral using substitution, we should always double-check that our result is correct. If we differentiate the result, then it should come out to the original integrand:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{ln}^{2}⁡x+C) & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({ln}^{2}⁡x)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)^{2}]+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+0 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{1}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let's think about how to calculate an integral like $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\dfrac {\\ln x} {x} \\,\\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nNotice that this integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\int \\dfrac {\\ln x} {x} \\,\\textrm d x = \\int \\underbrace{\\ln x}_{u} \\cdot\\overbrace{\\dfrac {1} {x}}^{u'} \\,\\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nWe've seen integrals like this before, where the integrand consists of a function $u(x)$ multiplied by its derivative $u'(x).$ Such integrals can be solved by substitution.\n\nSo, we let $u = \\ln x,$ which gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}= \\dfrac 1 x\\quad   \\Longrightarrow \\quad \\dfrac 1 x \\,\\textrm{d}x={\\textrm{d}u}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we can calculate our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n       \\int \\dfrac{\\ln x}{x}\\, \\textrm{d}x \t&=\\int \\ln x\\cdot \\dfrac {1} {x} \\,\\textrm d x \\\\[5pt]\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t& = \\int u \\, \\textrm{d} u \\\\[5pt]\n                                                                &=  \\dfrac{1}{2}u^2  +C \\\\[5pt]   \n                                                                &= \\dfrac{1}{2}\\ln^2 x +C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAfter solving an integral using substitution, we should always double-check that our result is correct. If we differentiate the result, then it should come out to the original integrand:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{\\ln }^{2}⁡x+C) & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({\\ln }^{2}⁡x)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)^{2}]+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+0 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{1}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186104963 :answer/value "4 \\div (11 \\cdot 3)" "4 \\operatorname{div}  (11 \\cdot 3)"] [:db/cas 17592186104964 :answer/value "4^{3} \\div 11" "4^{3} \\operatorname{div}  11"] [:db/cas 17592186104965 :answer/value "4 \\div (11 + 3)" "4 \\operatorname{div}  (11 + 3)"] [:db/cas 17592186104966 :answer/value "4^{3} \\div 11^{3}" "4^{3} \\operatorname{div}  11^{3}"] [:db/cas 17592186104967 :answer/value "4 \\div 11^{3}" "4 \\operatorname{div}  11^{3}"] [:db/cas 17592186105458 :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S f(x,y,z) \\, \\textrm{d}S \n=\n\\iint\\limits_D f\\left(x,y,g(x,y)\\right) \\sqrt{1+\\left(\\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}\\right)^2+\\left(\\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial y}\\right)^2} \\: \\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial x} = \\dfrac{\\partial}{\\partial x}(\\sqrt2x-y) = \\sqrt2,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial y} = \\dfrac{\\partial}{\\partial y}(\\sqrt2x-y) = -1.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂x}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂y}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}y\\cdot \\sqrt{1+(\\sqrt{2})^{2}+(-1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}y\\cdot \\sqrt{4}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =2{∬}_{D}\\,{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that in polar coordinates $(r, \\theta),$ our domain can be expressed as \n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\left\\{ (r,\\theta) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq r \\leq 1, \\: 0 \\leq \\theta \\leq \\pi \\right\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula for polar coordinates, where $x=r\\cos\\theta$ and $y=r\\sin\\theta,$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{∬}_{D}\\,{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}A & =2{∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta  \\\\  & =2{∬}_{Δ}{r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\cdot r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{∬}_{Δ}{r}^{4}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{1}{r}^{4}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,[\\frac{{r}^{5}}{5}]_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{15}{[-{cos}^{3}⁡\\theta ]}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{15}(1+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{15}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S f(x,y,z) \\, \\textrm{d}S \n=\n\\iint\\limits_D f\\left(x,y,g(x,y)\\right) \\sqrt{1+\\left(\\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}\\right)^2+\\left(\\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial y}\\right)^2} \\: \\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial x} = \\dfrac{\\partial}{\\partial x}(\\sqrt2x-y) = \\sqrt2,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial y} = \\dfrac{\\partial}{\\partial y}(\\sqrt2x-y) = -1.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂x}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂y}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}y\\cdot \\sqrt{1+(\\sqrt{2})^{2}+(-1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}y\\cdot \\sqrt{4}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =2{∬}_{D}\\,{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that in polar coordinates $(r, \\theta),$ our domain can be expressed as \n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\left\\{ (r,\\theta) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq r \\leq 1, \\: 0 \\leq \\theta \\leq \\pi \\right\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula for polar coordinates, where $x=r\\cos\\theta$ and $y=r\\sin\\theta,$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{∬}_{D}\\,{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}A & =2{∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta  \\\\  & =2{∬}_{Δ}{r}^{2}{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta \\cdot r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{∬}_{Δ}{r}^{4}{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{1}{r}^{4}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,[\\frac{{r}^{5}}{5}]_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{15}{[-{\\cos }^{3}⁡\\theta ]}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{15}(1+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{15}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186105573 :question/worked-solution "First, we apply the constant factor rule and take the coefficient $2$ out of the integral. Then we compute the integral of $\\dfrac 1 x,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\left(\\frac{2}{x}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x & =2\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2ln⁡|x|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we apply the constant factor rule and take the coefficient $2$ out of the integral. Then we compute the integral of $\\dfrac 1 x,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\left(\\frac{2}{x}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x & =2\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\ln ⁡|x|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186105574 :question/worked-solution "Applying the sum and constant factor rules, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (\\frac{1}{2x}+3x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{2x}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int 3x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x+3\\int x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}ln⁡|x|+3\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{1+1}}{1+1}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{ln⁡|x|}{2}+\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{2}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Applying the sum and constant factor rules, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (\\frac{1}{2x}+3x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{2x}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int 3x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x+3\\int x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\ln ⁡|x|+3\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{1+1}}{1+1}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{\\ln ⁡|x|}{2}+\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{2}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186105575 :question/worked-solution "We separate the integrand into the sum of two fractions so that we can simplify each term. So, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{3+2x}{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{3}{x}+\\frac{2x}{x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (\\frac{3}{x}+2)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x+2\\int \\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3ln⁡|x|+2x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, let's rewrite the constant so that we can combine it with the natural logarithm. For any constant $C,$ we can always write $C =3 \\ln K$ where $K$ is some other constant. Doing this allows us to combine the constant with $3\\ln|x|$ term, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{3+2x}{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =3ln⁡|x|+2x+C \\\\  & =3ln⁡|x|+2x+3ln⁡K \\\\  & =3ln⁡(K|x|)+2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We separate the integrand into the sum of two fractions so that we can simplify each term. So, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{3+2x}{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{3}{x}+\\frac{2x}{x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (\\frac{3}{x}+2)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x+2\\int \\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3\\ln ⁡|x|+2x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, let's rewrite the constant so that we can combine it with the natural logarithm. For any constant $C,$ we can always write $C =3 \\ln K$ where $K$ is some other constant. Doing this allows us to combine the constant with $3\\ln|x|$ term, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{3+2x}{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =3\\ln ⁡|x|+2x+C \\\\  & =3\\ln ⁡|x|+2x+3\\ln ⁡K \\\\  & =3\\ln ⁡(K|x|)+2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186105580 :question/worked-solution "The function $\\log_2 (x+4)$ is defined for $x>-4,$ so the limit as $x \\to 4$ exists.\n\nTo find this limit, we substitute $x=4$ into the expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 4}{log}_{2}⁡(x+4) & ={log}_{2}⁡(4+4) \\\\  & ={log}_{2}⁡(8) \\\\  & ={log}_{2}⁡({2}^{3}) \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The function $\\log_2 (x+4)$ is defined for $x>-4,$ so the limit as $x \\to 4$ exists.\n\nTo find this limit, we substitute $x=4$ into the expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 4}{\\log }_{2}⁡(x+4) & ={\\log }_{2}⁡(4+4) \\\\  & ={\\log }_{2}⁡(8) \\\\  & ={\\log }_{2}⁡({2}^{3}) \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186105646 :question/worked-solution "The area bounded by a polar curve is given by\n\n$$\nA = \\dfrac{1}{2} \\int_{\\theta_1}^{\\theta_2} r^2(\\theta) \\: \\textrm{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r(\\theta) = \\sin(5\\theta)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\theta ))}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{sin}^{2}⁡(5\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the integration limits.  \n\nThe curve bounding the region crosses itself at the origin. Therefore, we want to find the values of $\\theta$ for which $r=0$, or \n\n$$\n\\sin(5\\theta) = 0.\n$$\n\nThis gives us two solutions for $5\\theta\\in[0,2\\pi)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n5\\theta = 0, \\pi \\qquad \\Longrightarrow \\qquad \\theta=0,\\dfrac{\\pi}{5}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the first petal is traversed for $\\theta \\in \\left[0,\\dfrac{\\pi}{5}\\right].$  Notice that this interval lies in the first quadrant, the same as our petal.\n\nTherefore, our expression for the area is given by\n\n$$\nA = \\dfrac{1}{2} \\int_{0}^{\\pi/5} \\sin^2(5\\theta) \\: \\textrm{d}\\theta.\n$$" "The area bounded by a polar curve is given by\n\n$$\nA = \\dfrac{1}{2} \\int_{\\theta_1}^{\\theta_2} r^2(\\theta) \\: \\textrm{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r(\\theta) = \\sin(5\\theta)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\theta ))}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{\\sin }^{2}⁡(5\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the integration limits.  \n\nThe curve bounding the region crosses itself at the origin. Therefore, we want to find the values of $\\theta$ for which $r=0$, or \n\n$$\n\\sin(5\\theta) = 0.\n$$\n\nThis gives us two solutions for $5\\theta\\in[0,2\\pi)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n5\\theta = 0, \\pi \\qquad \\Longrightarrow \\qquad \\theta=0,\\dfrac{\\pi}{5}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the first petal is traversed for $\\theta \\in \\left[0,\\dfrac{\\pi}{5}\\right].$  Notice that this interval lies in the first quadrant, the same as our petal.\n\nTherefore, our expression for the area is given by\n\n$$\nA = \\dfrac{1}{2} \\int_{0}^{\\pi/5} \\sin^2(5\\theta) \\: \\textrm{d}\\theta.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186105684 :question/worked-solution "$\\newcommand{\\arsinh}{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcosh}{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\artanh}{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arsech}{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$\n\nFirst, we recall the results for the derivatives of the basic hyperbolic functions:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x} ( \\sinh x ) = \\cosh x,\n\\quad \n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x} ( \\cosh x ) = \\sinh x,\n\\quad \n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x} ( \\tanh x ) = \\sech^2 x\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the given function using these results, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x-\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x) \\\\  & =3\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x)-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x) \\\\  & =3{sech}^{2}⁡x-\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "$\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsinh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcosh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{artanh} }{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{sech} }{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{csch} }{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsech} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcsch} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcoth} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$\n\nFirst, we recall the results for the derivatives of the basic hyperbolic functions:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x} ( \\sinh x ) = \\cosh x,\n\\quad \n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x} ( \\cosh x ) = \\sinh x,\n\\quad \n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x} ( \\tanh x ) = \\operatorname{sech} ^2 x\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the given function using these results, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x-\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x) \\\\  & =3\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x)-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x) \\\\  & =3{\\operatorname{sech} }^{2}⁡x-\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186105684 :question/problem "$\\newcommand{\\arsinh}{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcosh}{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\artanh}{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arsech}{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$\n\nFind the derivative of $y = 3\\tanh{x}- \\sinh{x}.$" "$\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsinh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcosh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{artanh} }{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{sech} }{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{csch} }{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsech} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcsch} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcoth} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$\n\nFind the derivative of $y = 3\\tanh{x}- \\sinh{x}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186105685 :question/worked-solution "$\\newcommand{\\arsinh}{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcosh}{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\artanh}{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arsech}{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$\n\nHere, we have the product of two functions $u(x) = 2x$ and $v(x)=\\tanh x.$ So, we apply the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =(2x)^{′}\\cdot \\operatorname{tanh}⁡x+2x\\cdot (\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x)^{′} \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\operatorname{tanh}⁡x+2x\\cdot {sech}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x+2x{sech}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & =2(\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x+x{sech}^{2}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$" "$\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsinh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcosh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{artanh} }{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{sech} }{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{csch} }{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsech} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcsch} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcoth} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$\n\nHere, we have the product of two functions $u(x) = 2x$ and $v(x)=\\tanh x.$ So, we apply the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =(2x)^{′}\\cdot \\operatorname{tanh}⁡x+2x\\cdot (\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x)^{′} \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\operatorname{tanh}⁡x+2x\\cdot {\\operatorname{sech} }^{2}⁡x \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x+2x{\\operatorname{sech} }^{2}⁡x \\\\  & =2(\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x+x{\\operatorname{sech} }^{2}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186105685 :question/problem "$\\newcommand{\\arsinh}{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcosh}{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\artanh}{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arsech}{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$\n\nFind the derivative of $f(x) = 2x\\tanh{x}.$" "$\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsinh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcosh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{artanh} }{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{sech} }{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{csch} }{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsech} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcsch} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcoth} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$\n\nFind the derivative of $f(x) = 2x\\tanh{x}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186105686 :question/worked-solution "$\\newcommand{\\arsinh}{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcosh}{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\artanh}{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arsech}{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$\n\nIn this case, the expression for $w(x)$ is a quotient $\\dfrac{u(x)}{v(x)}$ with $u(x) = x^3$ and $v(x) = \\cosh{x}.$ \n\nTherefore, we apply the quotient rule as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{w}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{{u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)-u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x)}{[v(x)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{({x}^{3})^{′}\\cdot \\operatorname{cosh}⁡x-{x}^{3}\\cdot (\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x)^{′}}{(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3{x}^{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{cosh}⁡x-{x}^{3}\\cdot \\operatorname{sinh}⁡x}{{cosh}^{2}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}(3\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x-x\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x)}{{cosh}^{2}⁡x}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "$\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsinh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcosh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{artanh} }{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{sech} }{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{csch} }{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsech} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcsch} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcoth} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$\n\nIn this case, the expression for $w(x)$ is a quotient $\\dfrac{u(x)}{v(x)}$ with $u(x) = x^3$ and $v(x) = \\cosh{x}.$ \n\nTherefore, we apply the quotient rule as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{w}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{{u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)-u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x)}{[v(x)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{({x}^{3})^{′}\\cdot \\operatorname{cosh}⁡x-{x}^{3}\\cdot (\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x)^{′}}{(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3{x}^{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{cosh}⁡x-{x}^{3}\\cdot \\operatorname{sinh}⁡x}{{\\cosh }^{2}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}(3\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x-x\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x)}{{\\cosh }^{2}⁡x}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186105686 :question/problem "$\\newcommand{\\arsinh}{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcosh}{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\artanh}{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arsech}{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$\n\nFind the derivative of $w(x) = \\dfrac{x^3}{\\cosh{x}}.$" "$\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsinh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcosh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{artanh} }{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{sech} }{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{csch} }{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsech} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcsch} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcoth} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$\n\nFind the derivative of $w(x) = \\dfrac{x^3}{\\cosh{x}}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186105687 :question/problem "$\\newcommand{\\arsinh}{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcosh}{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\artanh}{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arsech}{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$\n\nFind the derivative of $f(x) = \\cosh{\\sqrt x}.$" "$\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsinh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcosh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{artanh} }{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{sech} }{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{csch} }{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsech} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcsch} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcoth} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$\n\nFind the derivative of $f(x) = \\cosh{\\sqrt x}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186105690 :question/worked-solution "The area bounded by a polar curve is given by\n\n$$\nA = \\dfrac{1}{2} \\int_{\\theta_1}^{\\theta_2} r^2(\\theta) \\: \\textrm{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r(\\theta) = \\sqrt{2}\\cos(6\\theta)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\theta ))}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{cos}^{2}⁡(6\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the integration limits. \n\nThe curve bounding the region crosses itself at the origin. Therefore, we want to find the values of $\\theta$ for which $r=0$, or \n\n$$\n\\sqrt{2}\\cos(6\\theta) = 0.\n$$\n\nNotice that the first petal lies between the first and fourth quadrants. So it's convenient to assume that $\\theta$ can be negative.\n\nWe now solve the above equation for $6\\theta\\in[-\\pi,\\pi)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n6\\theta = -\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}, \\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\qquad \\Longrightarrow \\qquad \\theta=-\\dfrac{\\pi}{12},\\dfrac{\\pi}{12}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the first petal is traversed for $$\n\\displaystyle \\theta \\in \\left[-\\dfrac{\\pi}{12},\\dfrac{\\pi}{12}\\right].\n$$ Notice that this interval lies between the first and fourth quadrants, the same as our petal.\n\nTherefore, our expression for the area is given by\n\n$$\n\\int_{-\\pi/12}^{\\pi/12} \\cos^2(6\\theta) \\: \\textrm{d}\\theta.\n$$" "The area bounded by a polar curve is given by\n\n$$\nA = \\dfrac{1}{2} \\int_{\\theta_1}^{\\theta_2} r^2(\\theta) \\: \\textrm{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r(\\theta) = \\sqrt{2}\\cos(6\\theta)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\theta ))}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{\\cos }^{2}⁡(6\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the integration limits. \n\nThe curve bounding the region crosses itself at the origin. Therefore, we want to find the values of $\\theta$ for which $r=0$, or \n\n$$\n\\sqrt{2}\\cos(6\\theta) = 0.\n$$\n\nNotice that the first petal lies between the first and fourth quadrants. So it's convenient to assume that $\\theta$ can be negative.\n\nWe now solve the above equation for $6\\theta\\in[-\\pi,\\pi)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n6\\theta = -\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}, \\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\qquad \\Longrightarrow \\qquad \\theta=-\\dfrac{\\pi}{12},\\dfrac{\\pi}{12}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the first petal is traversed for $$\n\\displaystyle \\theta \\in \\left[-\\dfrac{\\pi}{12},\\dfrac{\\pi}{12}\\right].\n$$ Notice that this interval lies between the first and fourth quadrants, the same as our petal.\n\nTherefore, our expression for the area is given by\n\n$$\n\\int_{-\\pi/12}^{\\pi/12} \\cos^2(6\\theta) \\: \\textrm{d}\\theta.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186106037 :question/worked-solution "The area bounded by a polar curve is given by\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}(\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r(\\theta )=\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\theta )$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\theta ))}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{cos}^{2}⁡(3\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the integration limits. \n\nThe curve bounding the region crosses itself at the origin. Therefore, we want to find the values of $\\theta $ for which $r=0$, or \n\n$$\n\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\theta )=0.\n$$\n\nNotice that the first petal lies between the first and fourth quadrants. So it's convenient to assume that $\\theta $ can be negative.\n\nWe now solve the above equation for $3\\theta \\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ):$\n\n$$\n3\\theta =-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,⟹\\,\\theta =-\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\frac{\\pi }{6}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the first petal is traversed for $$\n\\displaystyle \\theta \\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\frac{\\pi }{6}].\n$$ Notice that this interval lies between the first and fourth quadrants, the same as our petal.\n\nTherefore, our expression for the area is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{\\pi /6}{cos}^{2}⁡(3\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$" "The area bounded by a polar curve is given by\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}(\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r(\\theta )=\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\theta )$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\theta ))}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{\\cos }^{2}⁡(3\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the integration limits. \n\nThe curve bounding the region crosses itself at the origin. Therefore, we want to find the values of $\\theta $ for which $r=0$, or \n\n$$\n\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\theta )=0.\n$$\n\nNotice that the first petal lies between the first and fourth quadrants. So it's convenient to assume that $\\theta $ can be negative.\n\nWe now solve the above equation for $3\\theta \\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ):$\n\n$$\n3\\theta =-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,⟹\\,\\theta =-\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\frac{\\pi }{6}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the first petal is traversed for $$\n\\displaystyle \\theta \\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\frac{\\pi }{6}].\n$$ Notice that this interval lies between the first and fourth quadrants, the same as our petal.\n\nTherefore, our expression for the area is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{\\pi /6}{\\cos }^{2}⁡(3\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186106062 :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂x}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂y}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x-y)=2,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x-y)=-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂x}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂y}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\cdot \\sqrt{1+{2}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\cdot \\sqrt{6}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}{∬}_{D}\\,({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ our domain can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 2,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula for polar coordinates, where $x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{6}{∬}_{D}\\,({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A & =\\sqrt{6}{∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}{∬}_{Δ}({r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\cdot r\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{[\\frac{{r}^{4}}{4}]}_{0}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }4\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{6}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{6}[\\theta ]_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =8\\sqrt{6}\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂x}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂y}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x-y)=2,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x-y)=-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂x}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂y}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\cdot \\sqrt{1+{2}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\cdot \\sqrt{6}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}{∬}_{D}\\,({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ our domain can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 2,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula for polar coordinates, where $x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{6}{∬}_{D}\\,({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A & =\\sqrt{6}{∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}{∬}_{Δ}({r}^{2}{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta )\\cdot r\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{[\\frac{{r}^{4}}{4}]}_{0}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }4\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{6}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{6}[\\theta ]_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =8\\sqrt{6}\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186106064 :question/problem "Evaluate the surface integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\frac{z}{\\sqrt{1+2{cos}^{2}⁡(x+y)}}\\,\\text{d}S\n$$\n\nwhere $S$ is defined by $z=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)$ for $0\\le x\\le \\pi $ and $-x\\le y\\le x.$" "Evaluate the surface integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\frac{z}{\\sqrt{1+2{\\cos }^{2}⁡(x+y)}}\\,\\text{d}S\n$$\n\nwhere $S$ is defined by $z=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)$ for $0\\le x\\le \\pi $ and $-x\\le y\\le x.$"] [:db/cas 17592186106064 :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂x}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂y}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y),\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be evaluated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\frac{z}{\\sqrt{1+2{cos}^{2}⁡(x+y)}}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂x}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂y}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)}{\\sqrt{1+2{cos}^{2}⁡(x+y)}}\\sqrt{1+2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y))^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)}{\\cancel{\\sqrt{1+2{cos}^{2}⁡(x+y)}}}\\cancel{\\sqrt{1+2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y))^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{-x}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }[-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y)]_{y=-x}^{y=x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[x-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{2}]}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$" "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂x}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂y}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y),\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be evaluated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\frac{z}{\\sqrt{1+2{\\cos }^{2}⁡(x+y)}}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂x}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂y}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)}{\\sqrt{1+2{\\cos }^{2}⁡(x+y)}}\\sqrt{1+2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y))^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)}{\\cancel{\\sqrt{1+2{\\cos }^{2}⁡(x+y)}}}\\cancel{\\sqrt{1+2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y))^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{-x}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }[-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y)]_{y=-x}^{y=x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[x-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{2}]}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186106067 :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any linear transformation $T$ with associated $2\\times 2$ matrix $T,$ we have one of the following three possibilities:\n\n- If $T$ is non-singular, i.e., $det(T)\\ne 0,$ then the image of a two-dimensional object is a two-dimensional object. In particular, the image of a polygon with $n$ vertices is a polygon with $n$ vertices.\n\n- If $T$ is singular, i.e., $det(T)=0,$ we have two additional subcases:\n\n- If $T$ has at least one nonzero entry, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a one-dimensional line segment.\n\n- If $T$ is the zero matrix, then the image of a two-dimensional object is the point $(0,0),$ the origin of the coordinate system.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our transformation.\n\nComputing the determinant of $T,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(T) & =\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 6 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-2)\\cdot (-3)-6\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a singular nonzero matrix. Therefore, the image of the triangle under the action of $T$ is a line segment." "Recall that for any linear transformation $T$ with associated $2\\times 2$ matrix $T,$ we have one of the following three possibilities:\n\n- If $T$ is non-singular, i.e., $\\det (T)\\ne 0,$ then the image of a two-dimensional object is a two-dimensional object. In particular, the image of a polygon with $n$ vertices is a polygon with $n$ vertices.\n\n- If $T$ is singular, i.e., $\\det (T)=0,$ we have two additional subcases:\n\n- If $T$ has at least one nonzero entry, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a one-dimensional line segment.\n\n- If $T$ is the zero matrix, then the image of a two-dimensional object is the point $(0,0),$ the origin of the coordinate system.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our transformation.\n\nComputing the determinant of $T,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\det (T) & =\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 6 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-2)\\cdot (-3)-6\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a singular nonzero matrix. Therefore, the image of the triangle under the action of $T$ is a line segment."] [:db/cas 17592186106068 :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any linear transformation $T$ with associated $2\\times 2$ matrix $T,$ we have one of the following three possibilities:\n\n- If $T$ is non-singular, i.e., $det(T)\\ne 0,$ then the image of a two-dimensional object is a two-dimensional object. In particular, the image of a polygon with $n$ vertices is a polygon with $n$ vertices.\n\n- If $T$ is singular, i.e., $det(T)=0,$ we have two additional subcases:\n\n- If $T$ has at least one nonzero entry, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a one-dimensional line segment.\n\n- If $T$ is the zero matrix, then the image of a two-dimensional object is the point $(0,0),$ the origin of the coordinate system.\n\nSince $T$ is the zero matrix, the image of our object under the action of $T$ is the origin." "Recall that for any linear transformation $T$ with associated $2\\times 2$ matrix $T,$ we have one of the following three possibilities:\n\n- If $T$ is non-singular, i.e., $\\det (T)\\ne 0,$ then the image of a two-dimensional object is a two-dimensional object. In particular, the image of a polygon with $n$ vertices is a polygon with $n$ vertices.\n\n- If $T$ is singular, i.e., $\\det (T)=0,$ we have two additional subcases:\n\n- If $T$ has at least one nonzero entry, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a one-dimensional line segment.\n\n- If $T$ is the zero matrix, then the image of a two-dimensional object is the point $(0,0),$ the origin of the coordinate system.\n\nSince $T$ is the zero matrix, the image of our object under the action of $T$ is the origin."] [:db/cas 17592186106069 :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any linear transformation $T$ with associated $2\\times 2$ matrix $T,$ we have one of the following three possibilities:\n\n- If $T$ is non-singular, i.e., $det(T)\\ne 0,$ then the image of a two-dimensional object is a two-dimensional object. In particular, the image of a polygon with $n$ vertices is a polygon with $n$ vertices.\n\n- If $T$ is singular, i.e., $det(T)=0,$ we have two additional subcases:\n\n- If $T$ has at least one nonzero entry, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a one-dimensional line segment.\n\n- If $T$ is the zero matrix, then the image of a two-dimensional object is the point $(0,0),$ the origin of the coordinate system.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our transformation.\n\nComputing the determinant of $T,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(T) & =\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-1\\cdot 1-0\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a nonsingular matrix. Therefore, the image of our object under the action of  $T$ is a quadrilateral." "Recall that for any linear transformation $T$ with associated $2\\times 2$ matrix $T,$ we have one of the following three possibilities:\n\n- If $T$ is non-singular, i.e., $\\det (T)\\ne 0,$ then the image of a two-dimensional object is a two-dimensional object. In particular, the image of a polygon with $n$ vertices is a polygon with $n$ vertices.\n\n- If $T$ is singular, i.e., $\\det (T)=0,$ we have two additional subcases:\n\n- If $T$ has at least one nonzero entry, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a one-dimensional line segment.\n\n- If $T$ is the zero matrix, then the image of a two-dimensional object is the point $(0,0),$ the origin of the coordinate system.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our transformation.\n\nComputing the determinant of $T,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\det (T) & =\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-1\\cdot 1-0\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a nonsingular matrix. Therefore, the image of our object under the action of  $T$ is a quadrilateral."] [:db/cas 17592186106148 :question/worked-solution "The area bounded by a polar curve is given by\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}(\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r(\\theta )=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\theta )$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\theta ))}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{sin}^{2}⁡(3\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the integration limits.  \n\nThe curve bounding the region crosses itself at the origin. Therefore, we want to find the values of $\\theta $ for which $r=0$, or \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\theta )=0.\n$$\n\nThis gives us two solutions for $3\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ):$\n\n$$\n3\\theta =0,\\pi \\,⟹\\,\\theta =0,\\frac{\\pi }{3}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the first petal is traversed for $\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{3}].$ Notice that this interval lies in the first quadrant, the same as our petal.\n\nTherefore, our expression for the area is given by\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}{sin}^{2}⁡(3\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$" "The area bounded by a polar curve is given by\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}(\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r(\\theta )=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\theta )$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\theta ))}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{\\sin }^{2}⁡(3\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the integration limits.  \n\nThe curve bounding the region crosses itself at the origin. Therefore, we want to find the values of $\\theta $ for which $r=0$, or \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\theta )=0.\n$$\n\nThis gives us two solutions for $3\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ):$\n\n$$\n3\\theta =0,\\pi \\,⟹\\,\\theta =0,\\frac{\\pi }{3}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the first petal is traversed for $\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{3}].$ Notice that this interval lies in the first quadrant, the same as our petal.\n\nTherefore, our expression for the area is given by\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}{\\sin }^{2}⁡(3\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186106366 :question/worked-solution "First, we apply the constant factor rule and take the coefficient $\\frac{1}{9}$ out of the integral. Then we compute the integral of $\\frac{1}{x},$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}x}{9x} & =\\frac{1}{9}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}x}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}ln⁡|x|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we apply the constant factor rule and take the coefficient $\\frac{1}{9}$ out of the integral. Then we compute the integral of $\\frac{1}{x},$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}x}{9x} & =\\frac{1}{9}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}x}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}\\ln ⁡|x|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186106502 :question/problem "Let $X$ be the outcome of rolling an unfair six-sided die that follows the probability mass function $f(x)$ shown in the table below. What is $Var[X]$?\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$f(x)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\frac{1}{4}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\frac{1}{4}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\frac{1}{8}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\frac{1}{8}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\frac{1}{8}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\frac{1}{8}$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." "Let $X$ be the outcome of rolling an unfair six-sided die that follows the probability mass function $f(x)$ shown in the table below. What is $\\operatorname{Var} [X]$?\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$f(x)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\frac{1}{4}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\frac{1}{4}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\frac{1}{8}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\frac{1}{8}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\frac{1}{8}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\frac{1}{8}$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question."] [:db/cas 17592186106506 :question/problem "Let $X$ be the outcome of rolling an unfair six-sided die that follows the probability mass function $f(x)$ shown in the table below. What is $Var[X]$?\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$f(x)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\frac{3}{10}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\frac{1}{10}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\frac{1}{5}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\frac{1}{5}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\frac{1}{10}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\frac{1}{10}$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." "Let $X$ be the outcome of rolling an unfair six-sided die that follows the probability mass function $f(x)$ shown in the table below. What is $\\operatorname{Var} [X]$?\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$f(x)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\frac{3}{10}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\frac{1}{10}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\frac{1}{5}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\frac{1}{5}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\frac{1}{10}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\frac{1}{10}$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question."] [:db/cas 17592186106536 :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the results for the derivatives of the basic hyperbolic functions:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x)=\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x)=\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x)={sech}^{2}⁡x\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the given function using these results, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x+3\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x) \\\\  & =2\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x)+3\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x+3\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we recall the results for the derivatives of the basic hyperbolic functions:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x)=\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x)=\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x)={\\operatorname{sech} }^{2}⁡x\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the given function using these results, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x+3\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x) \\\\  & =2\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x)+3\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x+3\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186106539 :question/worked-solution "In this case, the expression for $f(x)$ is a quotient $\\frac{u(x)}{v(x)}$ with $u(x)={x}^{2}$ and $v(x)=\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x.$ \n\nTherefore, we apply the quotient rule as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{{u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)-u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x)}{[v(x)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{({x}^{2})^{′}\\cdot \\operatorname{sinh}⁡x-{x}^{2}\\cdot (\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x)^{′}}{(\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2x\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x-{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}{{sinh}^{2}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\frac{x(2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x-x\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x)}{{sinh}^{2}⁡x}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "In this case, the expression for $f(x)$ is a quotient $\\frac{u(x)}{v(x)}$ with $u(x)={x}^{2}$ and $v(x)=\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x.$ \n\nTherefore, we apply the quotient rule as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{{u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)-u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x)}{[v(x)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{({x}^{2})^{′}\\cdot \\operatorname{sinh}⁡x-{x}^{2}\\cdot (\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x)^{′}}{(\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2x\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x-{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}{{\\sinh }^{2}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\frac{x(2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x-x\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x)}{{\\sinh }^{2}⁡x}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186106541 :question/problem "If $w(x)=\\frac{{e}^{x}}{\\operatorname{tanh}⁡2x},$ then ${w}^{′}(x)=\\frac{{e}^{x}\\cdot g(x)}{{tanh}^{2}⁡2x}.$ What is $g(x)?$" "If $w(x)=\\frac{{e}^{x}}{\\operatorname{tanh}⁡2x},$ then ${w}^{′}(x)=\\frac{{e}^{x}\\cdot g(x)}{{\\tanh }^{2}⁡2x}.$ What is $g(x)?$"] [:db/cas 17592186106541 :question/worked-solution "In this case, the expression for $w(x)$ is a quotient $\\frac{u(x)}{v(x)}$ with $u(x)={e}^{x}$ and $v(x)=\\operatorname{tanh}⁡2x.$ \n\nTherefore, we apply the quotient rule as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{w}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{{u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)-u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x)}{[v(x)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{({e}^{x})^{′}\\cdot \\operatorname{tanh}⁡2x-{e}^{x}\\cdot (\\operatorname{tanh}⁡2x)^{′}}{(\\operatorname{tanh}⁡2x)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{x}\\operatorname{tanh}⁡2x-{e}^{x}\\cdot 2{sech}^{2}⁡2x}{{tanh}^{2}⁡2x} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{x}\\overbrace{(\\operatorname{tanh}⁡2x-2{sech}^{2}⁡2x)}^{g(x)}}{{tanh}^{2}⁡2x}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $g(x)=\\operatorname{tanh}⁡2x-2{sech}^{2}⁡2x.$" "In this case, the expression for $w(x)$ is a quotient $\\frac{u(x)}{v(x)}$ with $u(x)={e}^{x}$ and $v(x)=\\operatorname{tanh}⁡2x.$ \n\nTherefore, we apply the quotient rule as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{w}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{{u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)-u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x)}{[v(x)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{({e}^{x})^{′}\\cdot \\operatorname{tanh}⁡2x-{e}^{x}\\cdot (\\operatorname{tanh}⁡2x)^{′}}{(\\operatorname{tanh}⁡2x)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{x}\\operatorname{tanh}⁡2x-{e}^{x}\\cdot 2{\\operatorname{sech} }^{2}⁡2x}{{\\tanh }^{2}⁡2x} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{x}\\overbrace{(\\operatorname{tanh}⁡2x-2{\\operatorname{sech} }^{2}⁡2x)}^{g(x)}}{{\\tanh }^{2}⁡2x}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $g(x)=\\operatorname{tanh}⁡2x-2{\\operatorname{sech} }^{2}⁡2x.$"] [:db/cas 17592186106542 :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the results for the derivatives of the basic hyperbolic functions:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x)=\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x)=\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x)={sech}^{2}⁡x\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the given function using these results, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-14\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x) \\\\  & =-14\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x) \\\\  & =-14{sech}^{2}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we recall the results for the derivatives of the basic hyperbolic functions:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x)=\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x)=\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x)={\\operatorname{sech} }^{2}⁡x\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the given function using these results, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-14\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x) \\\\  & =-14\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x) \\\\  & =-14{\\operatorname{sech} }^{2}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186106543 :question/problem "Find the derivative of $f(x)=4{sinh}^{3}⁡x.$" "Find the derivative of $f(x)=4{\\sinh }^{3}⁡x.$"] [:db/cas 17592186106543 :question/worked-solution "First, we express $f(x)$ as a composite function:\n\n$$\nf=4{g}^{3},\\,g=\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x\n$$\n\nThe chain rule states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}g}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}g}=12{g}^{2}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x.\n$$\n\nFinally, multiplying the two results together and substituting for $g$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =12{g}^{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{cosh}⁡x \\\\  & =12{sinh}^{2}⁡x\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we express $f(x)$ as a composite function:\n\n$$\nf=4{g}^{3},\\,g=\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x\n$$\n\nThe chain rule states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}g}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}g}=12{g}^{2}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x.\n$$\n\nFinally, multiplying the two results together and substituting for $g$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =12{g}^{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{cosh}⁡x \\\\  & =12{\\sinh }^{2}⁡x\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186107022 :tutorial/content "The area bounded by a polar curve $r = r(\\theta)$ and the rays $\\theta = \\theta_1$ and $\\theta = \\theta_2$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 1 2\\int_{\\theta_1}^{\\theta_2} r^2\\,\\textrm d \\theta \\, .\n$$\n\nCalculating the integral is often straightforward if we know the integration limits $\\theta_1$ and $\\theta_2$. But what if we don't know these values?\n\nFor example, suppose we want to calculate the total area of the first petal of the curve $r=\\sin(3\\theta),$ shown below.\n\n![](images/e0/e0c9059613162af33791e7edb7413dfbac9739cf24eeabd01b0033acba2b772a.png)\n\nThe curve bounding the blue petal crosses itself at the origin. When it passes through the origin, we must have $r=0,$ and since $r=\\sin(3\\theta),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\sin(3\\theta)=0 \\, .\n$$\n\nThis gives us two solutions for $3\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n3\\theta = 0,\\pi \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\theta=0,\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\theta_1 = 0, \\theta_2 = \\dfrac\\pi 3,$ and the area of the first petal is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}{sin}^{2}⁡(3\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\theta ))\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(\\theta {|}_{0}^{\\pi /3}-\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6\\theta ){|}_{0}^{\\pi /3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(\\frac{\\pi }{3}+0) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{12}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The area bounded by a polar curve $r = r(\\theta)$ and the rays $\\theta = \\theta_1$ and $\\theta = \\theta_2$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 1 2\\int_{\\theta_1}^{\\theta_2} r^2\\,\\textrm d \\theta \\, .\n$$\n\nCalculating the integral is often straightforward if we know the integration limits $\\theta_1$ and $\\theta_2$. But what if we don't know these values?\n\nFor example, suppose we want to calculate the total area of the first petal of the curve $r=\\sin(3\\theta),$ shown below.\n\n![](images/e0/e0c9059613162af33791e7edb7413dfbac9739cf24eeabd01b0033acba2b772a.png)\n\nThe curve bounding the blue petal crosses itself at the origin. When it passes through the origin, we must have $r=0,$ and since $r=\\sin(3\\theta),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\sin(3\\theta)=0 \\, .\n$$\n\nThis gives us two solutions for $3\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n3\\theta = 0,\\pi \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\theta=0,\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\theta_1 = 0, \\theta_2 = \\dfrac\\pi 3,$ and the area of the first petal is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}{\\sin }^{2}⁡(3\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\theta ))\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(\\theta {|}_{0}^{\\pi /3}-\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6\\theta ){|}_{0}^{\\pi /3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(\\frac{\\pi }{3}+0) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{12}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186107119 :tutorial/content "$\\newcommand{\\arsinh}{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcosh}{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\artanh}{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arsech}{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$\n\nSince the hyperbolic functions are defined in terms of exponential functions, we can easily calculate their derivatives. \n\nThe derivatives of the basic hyperbolic functions are as follows:  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x) & =\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x) & =\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x) & ={sech}^{2}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's use these results to find the derivative of the following function:\n\n$$\ny = \\sinh{x} - 2\\cosh{x}\n$$\n\nUsing the usual properties of derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x-2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x)-2\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x-2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "$\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsinh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcosh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{artanh} }{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{sech} }{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{csch} }{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsech} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcsch} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcoth} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$\n\nSince the hyperbolic functions are defined in terms of exponential functions, we can easily calculate their derivatives. \n\nThe derivatives of the basic hyperbolic functions are as follows:  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x) & =\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x) & =\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x) & ={\\operatorname{sech} }^{2}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's use these results to find the derivative of the following function:\n\n$$\ny = \\sinh{x} - 2\\cosh{x}\n$$\n\nUsing the usual properties of derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x-2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x)-2\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x-2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186107120 :tutorial/content "$\\newcommand{\\arsinh}{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcosh}{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\artanh}{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arsech}{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}\n%$\n\nWe would like the prove the following result:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}(\\sinh{x}) = \\cosh x\n$$\n\nTo do this, we revert to the definition of the hyperbolic sine:\n\n$$\n\\sinh x = \\dfrac{1}{2}(e^x-e^{-x}).\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above equation with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{x}-{e}^{-x})) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{x}-{e}^{-x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe proof for the derivative of the hyperbolic cosine is very similar. You may wish to try it for yourself." "$\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsinh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcosh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{artanh} }{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{sech} }{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{csch} }{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsech} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcsch} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcoth} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}\n%$\n\nWe would like the prove the following result:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}(\\sinh{x}) = \\cosh x\n$$\n\nTo do this, we revert to the definition of the hyperbolic sine:\n\n$$\n\\sinh x = \\dfrac{1}{2}(e^x-e^{-x}).\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above equation with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{x}-{e}^{-x})) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{x}-{e}^{-x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe proof for the derivative of the hyperbolic cosine is very similar. You may wish to try it for yourself."] [:db/cas 17592186107121 :tutorial/content "$\\newcommand{\\arsinh}{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcosh}{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\artanh}{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arsech}{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$\n\nWe would like the prove the following result:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d}{\\textrm d x}(\\tanh{x}) = \\sech^2{x}\n$$\n\nWe do this using the definition of the hyperbolic tangent. The most convenient definition to work with for this purpose is\n\n$$\n\\tanh{x} = \\dfrac{e^{2x}-1}{e^{2x}+1}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above equation with respect to $x$ using the quotient rule, we arrive at\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{{e}^{2x}-1}{{e}^{2x}+1}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{({e}^{2x}-1)^{′}\\cdot ({e}^{2x}+1)-({e}^{2x}-1)\\cdot ({e}^{2x}+1)^{′}}{({e}^{2x}+1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(2{e}^{2x})({e}^{2x}+1)-({e}^{2x}-1)(2{e}^{2x})}{({e}^{2x}+1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2{e}^{4x}+2{e}^{2x}-2{e}^{4x}+2{e}^{2x}}{({e}^{2x}+1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4{e}^{2x}}{({e}^{2x}+1)^{2}} \\\\  & ={\\left(\\frac{2{e}^{x}}{{e}^{2x}+1}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & ={\\left(\\frac{2}{{e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & ={sech}^{2}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "$\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsinh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcosh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{artanh} }{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{sech} }{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{csch} }{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsech} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcsch} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcoth} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$\n\nWe would like the prove the following result:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d}{\\textrm d x}(\\tanh{x}) = \\operatorname{sech} ^2{x}\n$$\n\nWe do this using the definition of the hyperbolic tangent. The most convenient definition to work with for this purpose is\n\n$$\n\\tanh{x} = \\dfrac{e^{2x}-1}{e^{2x}+1}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above equation with respect to $x$ using the quotient rule, we arrive at\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{{e}^{2x}-1}{{e}^{2x}+1}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{({e}^{2x}-1)^{′}\\cdot ({e}^{2x}+1)-({e}^{2x}-1)\\cdot ({e}^{2x}+1)^{′}}{({e}^{2x}+1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(2{e}^{2x})({e}^{2x}+1)-({e}^{2x}-1)(2{e}^{2x})}{({e}^{2x}+1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2{e}^{4x}+2{e}^{2x}-2{e}^{4x}+2{e}^{2x}}{({e}^{2x}+1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4{e}^{2x}}{({e}^{2x}+1)^{2}} \\\\  & ={\\left(\\frac{2{e}^{x}}{{e}^{2x}+1}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & ={\\left(\\frac{2}{{e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & ={\\operatorname{sech} }^{2}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186107155 :tutorial/content "Given a linear transformation $\\mathbf T$ with an associated $2\\times 2$ matrix $T,$ there are three possibilities regarding the type of object formed under the action of $\\mathbf{T}$ by a two-dimensional preimage. \n\nTo demonstrate, consider the three-sided polygon $\\mathcal P$ with vertices $(0,1)$, $(2,0)$ and $(2,1),$ as shown below. \n\n![](images/97/97689c7aaf7f6d42314fd01cb36b557df8081880659bcea08905e6c57f358ac1.png)\n\nLet's describe the three possible types of object formed by the image of $\\mathcal P{:}$\n\n- Case where $T$ is non-singular:\n\nLet $\\mathbf T$ be the linear transformation with the following matrix representation:\n\n<div class=\"matrix-operands matrix-operands-stacked\">\n<div>$T =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n</div>\n\nWe can easily check that $T$ is non-singular by computing its determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(T) & =\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\cdot 0-1\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the image of $\\mathcal P$ under the action of $\\mathbf T$, we create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $\\mathcal P$ and then compute the product $TX{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the image of $\\mathcal P$ under the action of $\\mathbf{T}$ is a triangle.\n\n![](images/14/145753e4f69dfa67bb45f59b7e9ed8bca789c589d4c5340ce2112363fa70cd7b.png)\n\n- Case where $\\mathbf{T}$ is singular and has at least one nonzero entry:\n\nLet $\\mathbf T$ be the linear transformation with the following matrix representation:\n\n$$\nT = \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWe can easily check that $T$ is singular by computing its determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(T) & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\cdot 3-6\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, computing the image of $\\mathcal P$ under the action of $\\mathbf T,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 4 & 10 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 5\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the image of $\\mathcal P$ under the action of  $\\mathbf{T}$ is a line segment.\n\n![](images/46/46d387dbf6678d324135dbbb3439688c0bdbf9f9567baef56f34aa4aa9ee22e3.png)\n\n- Case where $T$ is the zero matrix:\n\nLet $\\mathbf T$ be the linear transformation represented by the zero matrix:\n\n$$\nT = \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nClearly, $T$ is singular. Now, computing the image of $\\mathcal P$ under the action of $\\mathbf T$, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the image's vertices are all $(0,0)$, the origin $O$ of the coordinate system. \n\n![](images/db/dbb9fce92da1f231a31e110f6cabb6faaf1bb43961a6c1de012c7c302c7af928.png)" "Given a linear transformation $\\mathbf T$ with an associated $2\\times 2$ matrix $T,$ there are three possibilities regarding the type of object formed under the action of $\\mathbf{T}$ by a two-dimensional preimage. \n\nTo demonstrate, consider the three-sided polygon $\\mathcal P$ with vertices $(0,1)$, $(2,0)$ and $(2,1),$ as shown below. \n\n![](images/97/97689c7aaf7f6d42314fd01cb36b557df8081880659bcea08905e6c57f358ac1.png)\n\nLet's describe the three possible types of object formed by the image of $\\mathcal P{:}$\n\n- Case where $T$ is non-singular:\n\nLet $\\mathbf T$ be the linear transformation with the following matrix representation:\n\n<div class=\"matrix-operands matrix-operands-stacked\">\n<div>$T =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n</div>\n\nWe can easily check that $T$ is non-singular by computing its determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\det (T) & =\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\cdot 0-1\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the image of $\\mathcal P$ under the action of $\\mathbf T$, we create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $\\mathcal P$ and then compute the product $TX{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the image of $\\mathcal P$ under the action of $\\mathbf{T}$ is a triangle.\n\n![](images/14/145753e4f69dfa67bb45f59b7e9ed8bca789c589d4c5340ce2112363fa70cd7b.png)\n\n- Case where $\\mathbf{T}$ is singular and has at least one nonzero entry:\n\nLet $\\mathbf T$ be the linear transformation with the following matrix representation:\n\n$$\nT = \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWe can easily check that $T$ is singular by computing its determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\det (T) & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\cdot 3-6\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, computing the image of $\\mathcal P$ under the action of $\\mathbf T,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 4 & 10 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 5\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the image of $\\mathcal P$ under the action of  $\\mathbf{T}$ is a line segment.\n\n![](images/46/46d387dbf6678d324135dbbb3439688c0bdbf9f9567baef56f34aa4aa9ee22e3.png)\n\n- Case where $T$ is the zero matrix:\n\nLet $\\mathbf T$ be the linear transformation represented by the zero matrix:\n\n$$\nT = \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nClearly, $T$ is singular. Now, computing the image of $\\mathcal P$ under the action of $\\mathbf T$, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the image's vertices are all $(0,0)$, the origin $O$ of the coordinate system. \n\n![](images/db/dbb9fce92da1f231a31e110f6cabb6faaf1bb43961a6c1de012c7c302c7af928.png)"] [:db/cas 17592186110287 :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the secant-tangent identity:\n\n$$\n1+\\tan^2 x = \\sec^2{x}\n$$\n\nAlso, recall the following identity:\n\n$$\n\\cot x = \\dfrac{1}{\\tan x}\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\tan x\\cdot \\cot x = 1\n$$\n\nUsing the above identities, we can simplify our expression as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\cdot (\\operatorname{cot}⁡x+\\operatorname{tan}⁡x) & =2\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\cdot \\operatorname{cot}⁡x+2{tan}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & =2\\cdot 1+2{tan}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & =2\\text{ }\\,(1+{tan}^{2}⁡x) \\\\  & =2\\,{sec}^{2}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n2\\tan x\\cdot(\\cot x+\\tan{x})= 2\\sec^2x.\n$$" "First, let's recall the secant-tangent identity:\n\n$$\n1+\\tan^2 x = \\sec^2{x}\n$$\n\nAlso, recall the following identity:\n\n$$\n\\cot x = \\dfrac{1}{\\tan x}\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\tan x\\cdot \\cot x = 1\n$$\n\nUsing the above identities, we can simplify our expression as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\cdot (\\operatorname{cot}⁡x+\\operatorname{tan}⁡x) & =2\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\cdot \\operatorname{cot}⁡x+2{\\tan }^{2}⁡x \\\\  & =2\\cdot 1+2{\\tan }^{2}⁡x \\\\  & =2\\text{ }\\,(1+{\\tan }^{2}⁡x) \\\\  & =2\\,{\\sec }^{2}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n2\\tan x\\cdot(\\cot x+\\tan{x})= 2\\sec^2x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186110288 :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the secant-tangent identity:\n\n$$\n1+\\tan^2 x = \\sec^2{x}\n$$\n\nFirst, we expand the parentheses using the difference of squares formula:\n\n$$\n(1-\\sec x )(1+\\sec x ) = 1 - \\sec^2x\n$$\n\nThen, we rewrite the expression using the secant-tangent identity as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-{sec}^{2}⁡x & =1-(1+{tan}^{2}⁡x) \\\\  & =1-1-{tan}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & =-{tan}^{2}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n(1-\\sec x )(1+\\sec x )= -\\tan^2 x.\n$$" "First, let's recall the secant-tangent identity:\n\n$$\n1+\\tan^2 x = \\sec^2{x}\n$$\n\nFirst, we expand the parentheses using the difference of squares formula:\n\n$$\n(1-\\sec x )(1+\\sec x ) = 1 - \\sec^2x\n$$\n\nThen, we rewrite the expression using the secant-tangent identity as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-{\\sec }^{2}⁡x & =1-(1+{\\tan }^{2}⁡x) \\\\  & =1-1-{\\tan }^{2}⁡x \\\\  & =-{\\tan }^{2}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n(1-\\sec x )(1+\\sec x )= -\\tan^2 x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186110442 :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the secant-tangent identity:\n\n$$\n1+\\tan^2 \\theta = \\sec^2{\\theta}\n$$\n\nWe rewrite the given expression using the secant-tangent identity as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2\\sec^2 \\theta}{\\color{blue}1+\\tan^2 \\theta} = \\dfrac{2\\sec^2 \\theta}{\\color{blue}\\sec^2 \\theta}\n$$\n\nWe now cancel the common factor of $\\sec^2 \\theta$ in the numerator and denominator and simplify the fraction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta }{{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta } & =\\frac{2\\cancel{{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta }}{\\cancel{{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta }} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\cdot 1}{1} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2\\sec^2 \\theta}{1+\\tan^2 \\theta} = 2.\n$$" "First, let's recall the secant-tangent identity:\n\n$$\n1+\\tan^2 \\theta = \\sec^2{\\theta}\n$$\n\nWe rewrite the given expression using the secant-tangent identity as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2\\sec^2 \\theta}{\\color{blue}1+\\tan^2 \\theta} = \\dfrac{2\\sec^2 \\theta}{\\color{blue}\\sec^2 \\theta}\n$$\n\nWe now cancel the common factor of $\\sec^2 \\theta$ in the numerator and denominator and simplify the fraction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2{\\sec }^{2}⁡\\theta }{{\\sec }^{2}⁡\\theta } & =\\frac{2\\cancel{{\\sec }^{2}⁡\\theta }}{\\cancel{{\\sec }^{2}⁡\\theta }} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\cdot 1}{1} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2\\sec^2 \\theta}{1+\\tan^2 \\theta} = 2.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186110453 :question/worked-solution "First, using the identity $1 + \\cot^2 \\theta = \\csc^2 \\theta,$ we rewrite the expression in the parentheses: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,(1+{cot}^{2}⁡\\theta )=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot {csc}^{2}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the fact that $\\csc {\\theta} = \\dfrac{1}{\\sin{\\theta}},$ we can further simplify the expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot {csc}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\frac{1}{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\cancel{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\cancel{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, using the identity $1 + \\cot^2 \\theta = \\csc^2 \\theta,$ we rewrite the expression in the parentheses: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,(1+{\\cot }^{2}⁡\\theta )=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot {\\csc }^{2}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the fact that $\\csc {\\theta} = \\dfrac{1}{\\sin{\\theta}},$ we can further simplify the expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot {\\csc }^{2}⁡\\theta  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\frac{1}{{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\cancel{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\cancel{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186110454 :question/worked-solution "Note that we can simplify this expression by transforming the denominator into a form where we can apply a known trigonometric identity.\n\nFirst, we multiply the numerator and the denominator of our expression by $\\csc \\theta + \\cot \\theta.$ This gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta } & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta }\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta }{{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta -{cot}^{2}⁡\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the identity $\\csc^2 \\theta - \\cot^2 \\theta=1$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta }{{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta -{cot}^{2}⁡\\theta } & =\\frac{\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta }{1} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Note that we can simplify this expression by transforming the denominator into a form where we can apply a known trigonometric identity.\n\nFirst, we multiply the numerator and the denominator of our expression by $\\csc \\theta + \\cot \\theta.$ This gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta } & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta }\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta }{{\\csc }^{2}⁡\\theta -{\\cot }^{2}⁡\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the identity $\\csc^2 \\theta - \\cot^2 \\theta=1$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta }{{\\csc }^{2}⁡\\theta -{\\cot }^{2}⁡\\theta } & =\\frac{\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta }{1} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186110455 :question/worked-solution "First, we factor $\\csc x$ from the first two terms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{csc}⁡x+\\operatorname{csc}⁡x{cot}^{2}⁡x-{csc}^{3}⁡x & =\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\,(1+{cot}^{2}⁡x)-{csc}^{3}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the cotangent-cosecant identity,\n\n$$\n1+ \\cot^2 x=\\csc^2 x ,\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\,(1+{cot}^{2}⁡x)-{csc}^{3}⁡x & =\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\cdot ({csc}^{2}⁡x)-{csc}^{3}⁡x \\\\  & ={csc}^{3}⁡x-{csc}^{3}⁡x \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we factor $\\csc x$ from the first two terms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{csc}⁡x+\\operatorname{csc}⁡x{\\cot }^{2}⁡x-{\\csc }^{3}⁡x & =\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\,(1+{\\cot }^{2}⁡x)-{\\csc }^{3}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the cotangent-cosecant identity,\n\n$$\n1+ \\cot^2 x=\\csc^2 x ,\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\,(1+{\\cot }^{2}⁡x)-{\\csc }^{3}⁡x & =\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\cdot ({\\csc }^{2}⁡x)-{\\csc }^{3}⁡x \\\\  & ={\\csc }^{3}⁡x-{\\csc }^{3}⁡x \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186110457 :question/worked-solution "Any point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta$ as follows:\n\n$$\nx = \\cos\\theta, \\qquad y = \\sin\\theta.\n$$\n\nWe're given that $x=\\dfrac{2}{3}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\cos\\theta = \\dfrac{2}{3}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\sin\\theta\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\cos\\theta = \\dfrac{2}{3}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{4}{9}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{5}{9}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we select the positive answer since $\\sin\\theta$ is positive in the first quadrant. \n\nSo our final answer is $\\sin\\theta = \\dfrac{\\sqrt 5}{3}.$" "Any point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta$ as follows:\n\n$$\nx = \\cos\\theta, \\qquad y = \\sin\\theta.\n$$\n\nWe're given that $x=\\dfrac{2}{3}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\cos\\theta = \\dfrac{2}{3}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\sin\\theta\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta +{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\cos\\theta = \\dfrac{2}{3}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{4}{9}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{5}{9}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we select the positive answer since $\\sin\\theta$ is positive in the first quadrant. \n\nSo our final answer is $\\sin\\theta = \\dfrac{\\sqrt 5}{3}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186110458 :question/worked-solution "Again, any point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta$ as follows:\n\n$$\nx = \\cos\\theta, \\qquad y = \\sin\\theta.\n$$\n\nWe're given that $x=\\dfrac{2\\sqrt 6}{7}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\cos\\theta = \\dfrac{2\\sqrt 6}{7}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\sin\\theta\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\cos\\theta = \\dfrac{2\\sqrt 6}{7}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{\\left(\\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{7}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{24}{49}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{25}{49}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{5}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the positive answer since $\\sin\\theta$ is positive in the first quadrant. So, $\\sin\\theta = \\dfrac{5}{7}.$\n\nFinally, knowing that $\\csc\\theta = \\dfrac{1}{\\sin\\theta},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\csc\\theta = \\dfrac{1}{\\left(\\dfrac{5}{7}\\right)} = \\dfrac{7}{5}.\n$$" "Again, any point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta$ as follows:\n\n$$\nx = \\cos\\theta, \\qquad y = \\sin\\theta.\n$$\n\nWe're given that $x=\\dfrac{2\\sqrt 6}{7}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\cos\\theta = \\dfrac{2\\sqrt 6}{7}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\sin\\theta\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta +{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\cos\\theta = \\dfrac{2\\sqrt 6}{7}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{\\left(\\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{7}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{24}{49}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{25}{49}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{5}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the positive answer since $\\sin\\theta$ is positive in the first quadrant. So, $\\sin\\theta = \\dfrac{5}{7}.$\n\nFinally, knowing that $\\csc\\theta = \\dfrac{1}{\\sin\\theta},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\csc\\theta = \\dfrac{1}{\\left(\\dfrac{5}{7}\\right)} = \\dfrac{7}{5}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186110459 :question/worked-solution "We're given that $x=\\dfrac{4}{5}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\cos\\theta = \\dfrac{4}{5}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\sin\\theta\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\cos\\theta = \\dfrac{4}{5}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{\\left(\\frac{4}{5}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{16}{25}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{9}{25}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{3}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the positive answer since $\\sin\\theta$ is positive in the first quadrant. Therefore $\\sin\\theta = \\dfrac{3}{5}.$\n\nUsing the fact that\n\n$$\n\\tan\\theta = \\dfrac{\\sin\\theta}{\\cos\\theta},\n$$\n\nwe finally get\n\n$$\n\\tan\\theta = \\dfrac{\\left(\\dfrac{3}{5}\\right)}{\\left(\\dfrac{4}{5}\\right)} = \\dfrac {3}{4}.\n$$" "We're given that $x=\\dfrac{4}{5}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\cos\\theta = \\dfrac{4}{5}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\sin\\theta\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta +{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\cos\\theta = \\dfrac{4}{5}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{\\left(\\frac{4}{5}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{16}{25}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{9}{25}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{3}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the positive answer since $\\sin\\theta$ is positive in the first quadrant. Therefore $\\sin\\theta = \\dfrac{3}{5}.$\n\nUsing the fact that\n\n$$\n\\tan\\theta = \\dfrac{\\sin\\theta}{\\cos\\theta},\n$$\n\nwe finally get\n\n$$\n\\tan\\theta = \\dfrac{\\left(\\dfrac{3}{5}\\right)}{\\left(\\dfrac{4}{5}\\right)} = \\dfrac {3}{4}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186110460 :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the following identity: \n\n$$\n1= \\csc^2{\\theta} -\\cot^2{\\theta}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above identity into the given expression, and then factoring it as a difference of squares, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta }+\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta -{cot}^{2}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta }+\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta )\\,(\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta )}{\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta }+\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta )\\,\\cancel{(\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta )}}{\\cancel{\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta }}+\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta )+\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we recall the following identity: \n\n$$\n1= \\csc^2{\\theta} -\\cot^2{\\theta}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above identity into the given expression, and then factoring it as a difference of squares, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta }+\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{{\\csc }^{2}⁡\\theta -{\\cot }^{2}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta }+\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta )\\,(\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta )}{\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta }+\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta )\\,\\cancel{(\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta )}}{\\cancel{\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta }}+\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta )+\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186110486 :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the product rule for logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\log_n a + \\log_n b = \\log_n(ab)\n$$\n\nUsing the product rule for logarithms, the given expression can be rewritten as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{3}⁡2+{log}_{3}⁡5 & ={log}_{3}⁡(2\\cdot 5) \\\\  & ={log}_{3}⁡10.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, let's recall the product rule for logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\log_n a + \\log_n b = \\log_n(ab)\n$$\n\nUsing the product rule for logarithms, the given expression can be rewritten as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\log }_{3}⁡2+{\\log }_{3}⁡5 & ={\\log }_{3}⁡(2\\cdot 5) \\\\  & ={\\log }_{3}⁡10.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186110487 :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the product rule for logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\log_n a + \\log_n b = \\log_n(ab)\n$$\n\nIf we factor the given expression and use the product rule for logarithms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{log}_{5}⁡7+3{log}_{5}⁡3 & =3({log}_{5}⁡7+{log}_{5}⁡3) \\\\  & =3{log}_{5}⁡(7\\cdot 3) \\\\  & =3{log}_{5}⁡21.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n3\\log_{5}{7}+3\\log_{5}{3} = 3\\log_{5} 21 .\n$$" "First, let's recall the product rule for logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\log_n a + \\log_n b = \\log_n(ab)\n$$\n\nIf we factor the given expression and use the product rule for logarithms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{\\log }_{5}⁡7+3{\\log }_{5}⁡3 & =3({\\log }_{5}⁡7+{\\log }_{5}⁡3) \\\\  & =3{\\log }_{5}⁡(7\\cdot 3) \\\\  & =3{\\log }_{5}⁡21.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n3\\log_{5}{7}+3\\log_{5}{3} = 3\\log_{5} 21 .\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186110489 :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the product rule for logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\log_n a + \\log_n b = \\log_n(ab)\n$$\n\nUsing the product rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{2}⁡(3x) & ={log}_{2}⁡3+{log}_{2}⁡x \\\\  & =k+p.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, let's recall the product rule for logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\log_n a + \\log_n b = \\log_n(ab)\n$$\n\nUsing the product rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\log }_{2}⁡(3x) & ={\\log }_{2}⁡3+{\\log }_{2}⁡x \\\\  & =k+p.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186110490 :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the quotient rule for logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\log_n(a) - \\log_n(b) = \\log_n\\left( \\dfrac{a}{b} \\right)\n$$\n\nUsing the quotient rule for logarithms, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{3}⁡\\left(\\frac{2}{5}\\right) & ={log}_{3}⁡(2)-{log}_{3}⁡(5).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, let's recall the quotient rule for logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\log_n(a) - \\log_n(b) = \\log_n\\left( \\dfrac{a}{b} \\right)\n$$\n\nUsing the quotient rule for logarithms, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\log }_{3}⁡\\left(\\frac{2}{5}\\right) & ={\\log }_{3}⁡(2)-{\\log }_{3}⁡(5).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186110491 :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the quotient rule for logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\log_n(a) - \\log_n(b) = \\log_n\\left( \\dfrac{a}{b} \\right)\n$$\n\nUsing the quotient rule for logarithms, the given expression can be rewritten as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{5}⁡(16)-{log}_{5}⁡(8) & ={log}_{5}⁡\\left(\\frac{16}{8}\\right) \\\\  & ={log}_{5}⁡(2).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, let's recall the quotient rule for logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\log_n(a) - \\log_n(b) = \\log_n\\left( \\dfrac{a}{b} \\right)\n$$\n\nUsing the quotient rule for logarithms, the given expression can be rewritten as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\log }_{5}⁡(16)-{\\log }_{5}⁡(8) & ={\\log }_{5}⁡\\left(\\frac{16}{8}\\right) \\\\  & ={\\log }_{5}⁡(2).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186110492 :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the quotient rule for logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\log_n(a) - \\log_n(b) = \\log_n\\left( \\dfrac{a}{b} \\right)\n$$\n\nIf we factor the given expression and use the quotient rule for logarithms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{log}_{2}⁡(25)-2{log}_{2}⁡(5) & =2({log}_{2}⁡(25)-{log}_{2}⁡(5)) \\\\  & =2({log}_{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{25}{5}\\right)) \\\\  & =2{log}_{2}⁡(5).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n2\\log_2(25) - 2\\log_2(5) = 2\\log_2(5).\n$$" "First, let's recall the quotient rule for logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\log_n(a) - \\log_n(b) = \\log_n\\left( \\dfrac{a}{b} \\right)\n$$\n\nIf we factor the given expression and use the quotient rule for logarithms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{\\log }_{2}⁡(25)-2{\\log }_{2}⁡(5) & =2({\\log }_{2}⁡(25)-{\\log }_{2}⁡(5)) \\\\  & =2({\\log }_{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{25}{5}\\right)) \\\\  & =2{\\log }_{2}⁡(5).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n2\\log_2(25) - 2\\log_2(5) = 2\\log_2(5).\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186110494 :question/worked-solution "First, we take $\\log_2$ of both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{x+1} & =3 \\\\ {log}_{2}⁡({2}^{x+1}) & ={log}_{2}⁡(3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that $\\log_b \\left( b^c \\right) = c$ to simplify the left-hand side. This gives\n\n$$\nx+1 = \\log_2 \\left( 3 \\right).\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+1 & ={log}_{2}⁡(3) \\\\ x & ={log}_{2}⁡(3)-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x= \\log_2 \\left( 3 \\right) - 1.$" "First, we take $\\log_2$ of both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{x+1} & =3 \\\\ {\\log }_{2}⁡({2}^{x+1}) & ={\\log }_{2}⁡(3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that $\\log_b \\left( b^c \\right) = c$ to simplify the left-hand side. This gives\n\n$$\nx+1 = \\log_2 \\left( 3 \\right).\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+1 & ={\\log }_{2}⁡(3) \\\\ x & ={\\log }_{2}⁡(3)-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x= \\log_2 \\left( 3 \\right) - 1.$"] [:db/cas 17592186110498 :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the power rule for logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\log_n(c^m)= m\\log_n\\left( c \\right)\n$$\n\nUsing the power rule for logarithms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{log}_{3}⁡x & ={log}_{3}⁡({x}^{1/2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $k = \\dfrac{1}{2}.$" "First, let's recall the power rule for logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\log_n(c^m)= m\\log_n\\left( c \\right)\n$$\n\nUsing the power rule for logarithms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{\\log }_{3}⁡x & ={\\log }_{3}⁡({x}^{1/2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $k = \\dfrac{1}{2}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186110502 :question/worked-solution "To find $f^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2{log}_{4}⁡(3x)-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we swap $x$ and $y{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =2{log}_{4}⁡(3y)-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we make $y$ the subject of the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =2{log}_{4}⁡(3y)-1 \\\\ 2{log}_{4}⁡(3y) & =x+1 \\\\ {log}_{4}⁡(3y) & =\\frac{x+1}{2} \\\\ {4}^{{log}_{4}⁡(3y)} & ={4}^{(x+1)/2} \\\\ 3y & ={4}^{(x+1)/2} \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{3}{4}^{(x+1)/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nTherefore, the inverse function is $f^{-1}(x) =\\dfrac13 4^{(x+1)/2}.$" "To find $f^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2{\\log }_{4}⁡(3x)-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we swap $x$ and $y{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =2{\\log }_{4}⁡(3y)-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we make $y$ the subject of the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =2{\\log }_{4}⁡(3y)-1 \\\\ 2{\\log }_{4}⁡(3y) & =x+1 \\\\ {\\log }_{4}⁡(3y) & =\\frac{x+1}{2} \\\\ {4}^{{\\log }_{4}⁡(3y)} & ={4}^{(x+1)/2} \\\\ 3y & ={4}^{(x+1)/2} \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{3}{4}^{(x+1)/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nTherefore, the inverse function is $f^{-1}(x) =\\dfrac13 4^{(x+1)/2}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186110899 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta }-\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta =\n$$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{{\\csc }^{2}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta }-\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta =\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186110899 :question/worked-solution "Using the identity $1+{cot}^{2}⁡\\theta ={csc}^{2}⁡\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta }-\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta -{cot}^{2}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{1+{cot}^{2}⁡\\theta -{cot}^{2}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Using the identity $1+{\\cot }^{2}⁡\\theta ={\\csc }^{2}⁡\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\csc }^{2}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta }-\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{{\\csc }^{2}⁡\\theta -{\\cot }^{2}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{1+{\\cot }^{2}⁡\\theta -{\\cot }^{2}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186110900 :question/problem "![](images/bd/bd8650a856fe3136759d81a886b2d4622cf3eb463b75e6023b44083c72f63a1a.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $y$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\frac{1}{5},$ use the Pythagorean identity to find $sec⁡\\theta .$" "![](images/bd/bd8650a856fe3136759d81a886b2d4622cf3eb463b75e6023b44083c72f63a1a.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $y$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\frac{1}{5},$ use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\sec ⁡\\theta .$"] [:db/cas 17592186110900 :question/worked-solution "![](images/73/734eef45985ddfb6450666e1dd32c6d3beab9ca3aef66c87c6ba393d6960b08b.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=cos⁡\\theta ,\\,y=sin⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $y=\\frac{1}{5}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\nsin⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{5}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $cos⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ cos⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{5}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}cos⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{\\left(\\frac{1}{5}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{1}{25}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{24}{25}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the positive answer since $cos⁡\\theta $ is positive in the first quadrant. So, $cos⁡\\theta =\\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{5}.$\n\nKnowing that $sec⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{cos⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\nsec⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{\\left(\\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{5}\\right)}=\\frac{5}{2\\sqrt{6}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we rationalize the denominator.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sec⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{5}{2\\sqrt{6}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2\\sqrt{6}}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\sqrt{6}}{2\\cdot 6} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\sqrt{6}}{12}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "![](images/73/734eef45985ddfb6450666e1dd32c6d3beab9ca3aef66c87c6ba393d6960b08b.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=\\cos ⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\sin ⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $y=\\frac{1}{5}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\sin ⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{5}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\cos ⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta +{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\cos ⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\sin ⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{5}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\cos ⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{\\left(\\frac{1}{5}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{1}{25}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{24}{25}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the positive answer since $\\cos ⁡\\theta $ is positive in the first quadrant. So, $\\cos ⁡\\theta =\\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{5}.$\n\nKnowing that $\\sec ⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{\\cos ⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\sec ⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{\\left(\\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{5}\\right)}=\\frac{5}{2\\sqrt{6}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we rationalize the denominator.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sec ⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{5}{2\\sqrt{6}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2\\sqrt{6}}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\sqrt{6}}{2\\cdot 6} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\sqrt{6}}{12}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186110985 :question/problem "The inverse of the function $f(x)=5{log}_{4}⁡(10x-1)$ is ${f}^{-1}(x)=${{field-1}}." "The inverse of the function $f(x)=5{\\log }_{4}⁡(10x-1)$ is ${f}^{-1}(x)=${{field-1}}."] [:db/cas 17592186110985 :question/worked-solution "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =5{log}_{4}⁡(10x-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =5{log}_{4}⁡(10y-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we make $y$ the subject of the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =5{log}_{4}⁡(10y-1) \\\\ {log}_{4}⁡(10y-1) & =\\frac{x}{5} \\\\ {4}^{{log}_{4}⁡(10y-1)} & ={4}^{x/5} \\\\ 10y-1 & ={4}^{x/5} \\\\ 10y & ={4}^{x/5}+1 \\\\ y & =\\frac{{4}^{x/5}+1}{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is ${f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{{4}^{x/5}+1}{10}.$" "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =5{\\log }_{4}⁡(10x-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =5{\\log }_{4}⁡(10y-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we make $y$ the subject of the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =5{\\log }_{4}⁡(10y-1) \\\\ {\\log }_{4}⁡(10y-1) & =\\frac{x}{5} \\\\ {4}^{{\\log }_{4}⁡(10y-1)} & ={4}^{x/5} \\\\ 10y-1 & ={4}^{x/5} \\\\ 10y & ={4}^{x/5}+1 \\\\ y & =\\frac{{4}^{x/5}+1}{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is ${f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{{4}^{x/5}+1}{10}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186110994 :question/problem "Simplify $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta }{1+{cot}^{2}⁡\\theta }.\n$$" "Simplify $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta }{1+{\\cot }^{2}⁡\\theta }.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186110994 :question/worked-solution "Using the identity $1+{cot}^{2}⁡\\theta ={csc}^{2}⁡\\theta ,$ we rewrite and simplify the expression as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta }{1+{cot}^{2}⁡\\theta } & =\\frac{\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta }{{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$" "Using the identity $1+{\\cot }^{2}⁡\\theta ={\\csc }^{2}⁡\\theta ,$ we rewrite and simplify the expression as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta }{1+{\\cot }^{2}⁡\\theta } & =\\frac{\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta }{{\\csc }^{2}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186110995 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }{1+{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta }=\n$$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }{1+{\\tan }^{2}⁡\\theta }=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186110995 :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the secant-tangent identity:\n\n$$\n1+{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta ={sec}^{2}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe rewrite the given expression using the secant-tangent identity as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }{1+{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta }=\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }{{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta }\n$$\n\nWe now simplify the expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }{{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta } & =\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\cancel{\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }}{\\cancel{\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we recall the following identities:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta },\\,\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\n \n\nUsing the above identities, we can further simplify our expression as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta } & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }}\\cdot \\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }{1+{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta }=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$" "First, let's recall the secant-tangent identity:\n\n$$\n1+{\\tan }^{2}⁡\\theta ={\\sec }^{2}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe rewrite the given expression using the secant-tangent identity as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }{1+{\\tan }^{2}⁡\\theta }=\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }{{\\sec }^{2}⁡\\theta }\n$$\n\nWe now simplify the expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }{{\\sec }^{2}⁡\\theta } & =\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\cancel{\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }}{\\cancel{\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we recall the following identities:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta },\\,\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\n \n\nUsing the above identities, we can further simplify our expression as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta } & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }}\\cdot \\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }{1+{\\tan }^{2}⁡\\theta }=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186111031 :question/problem "Express ${log}_{5}⁡2+{log}_{5}⁡4+{log}_{5}⁡6$ as a single logarithm." "Express ${\\log }_{5}⁡2+{\\log }_{5}⁡4+{\\log }_{5}⁡6$ as a single logarithm."] [:db/cas 17592186111031 :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the product rule for logarithms:\n\n$$\n{log}_{n}⁡a+{log}_{n}⁡b={log}_{n}⁡(ab)\n$$\n\nUsing the product rule for logarithms, we can combine the first two terms as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{5}⁡2+{log}_{5}⁡4+{log}_{5}⁡6 & ={log}_{5}⁡(2\\cdot 4)+{log}_{5}⁡6 \\\\  & ={log}_{5}⁡8+{log}_{5}⁡6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the product rule for logarithms once more, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{5}⁡8+{log}_{5}⁡6 & ={log}_{5}⁡(8\\cdot 6) \\\\  & ={log}_{5}⁡48.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, let's recall the product rule for logarithms:\n\n$$\n{\\log }_{n}⁡a+{\\log }_{n}⁡b={\\log }_{n}⁡(ab)\n$$\n\nUsing the product rule for logarithms, we can combine the first two terms as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\log }_{5}⁡2+{\\log }_{5}⁡4+{\\log }_{5}⁡6 & ={\\log }_{5}⁡(2\\cdot 4)+{\\log }_{5}⁡6 \\\\  & ={\\log }_{5}⁡8+{\\log }_{5}⁡6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the product rule for logarithms once more, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\log }_{5}⁡8+{\\log }_{5}⁡6 & ={\\log }_{5}⁡(8\\cdot 6) \\\\  & ={\\log }_{5}⁡48.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186111055 :question/problem "Express ${log}_{2}⁡({x}^{3})-{log}_{2}⁡(x)$ as a single logarithm." "Express ${\\log }_{2}⁡({x}^{3})-{\\log }_{2}⁡(x)$ as a single logarithm."] [:db/cas 17592186111055 :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the quotient rule for logarithms:\n\n$$\n{log}_{n}⁡(a)-{log}_{n}⁡(b)={log}_{n}⁡\\left(\\frac{a}{b}\\right)\n$$\n\nUsing the quotient rule for logarithms, the given expression can be rewritten as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{2}⁡({x}^{3})-{log}_{2}⁡(x) & ={log}_{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{x}\\right) \\\\  & ={log}_{2}⁡({x}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, let's recall the quotient rule for logarithms:\n\n$$\n{\\log }_{n}⁡(a)-{\\log }_{n}⁡(b)={\\log }_{n}⁡\\left(\\frac{a}{b}\\right)\n$$\n\nUsing the quotient rule for logarithms, the given expression can be rewritten as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\log }_{2}⁡({x}^{3})-{\\log }_{2}⁡(x) & ={\\log }_{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{x}\\right) \\\\  & ={\\log }_{2}⁡({x}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186111158 :question/worked-solution "![](images/43/438ce908c902696b480bf9791cae089be40f9e7b2552208e13e08405496b696c.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $x=\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{4}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{4}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{4}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{4}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{7}{16}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{9}{16}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we select the positive answer since $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ is positive in the first quadrant. So our final answer is $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{3}{4}.$" "![](images/43/438ce908c902696b480bf9791cae089be40f9e7b2552208e13e08405496b696c.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $x=\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{4}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{4}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta +{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{4}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{4}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{7}{16}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{9}{16}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we select the positive answer since $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ is positive in the first quadrant. So our final answer is $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{3}{4}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186111178 :question/problem "$\\frac{1+{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta }{{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta }=$" "$\\frac{1+{\\tan }^{2}⁡\\theta }{{\\sec }^{2}⁡\\theta }=$"] [:db/cas 17592186111178 :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the secant-tangent identity:\n\n$$\n1+{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta ={sec}^{2}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe rewrite the given expression using the secant-tangent identity as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1+{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta }{{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta } & =\\frac{{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta }{{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta }}{\\cancel{{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta }} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\frac{1+{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta }{{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta }=1.\n$$" "First, let's recall the secant-tangent identity:\n\n$$\n1+{\\tan }^{2}⁡\\theta ={\\sec }^{2}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe rewrite the given expression using the secant-tangent identity as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1+{\\tan }^{2}⁡\\theta }{{\\sec }^{2}⁡\\theta } & =\\frac{{\\sec }^{2}⁡\\theta }{{\\sec }^{2}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{{\\sec }^{2}⁡\\theta }}{\\cancel{{\\sec }^{2}⁡\\theta }} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\frac{1+{\\tan }^{2}⁡\\theta }{{\\sec }^{2}⁡\\theta }=1.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186111212 :question/problem "By expressing $\\frac{5}{2}{log}_{2}⁡t$ in the form ${log}_{2}⁡({t}^{k}),$ determine the value of $k.$" "By expressing $\\frac{5}{2}{\\log }_{2}⁡t$ in the form ${\\log }_{2}⁡({t}^{k}),$ determine the value of $k.$"] [:db/cas 17592186111212 :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the power rule for logarithms:\n\n$$\n{log}_{n}⁡({c}^{m})=m{log}_{n}⁡(c)\n$$\n\nUsing the power rule for logarithms, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{2}{log}_{2}⁡t & ={log}_{2}⁡({t}^{5/2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence $k=\\frac{5}{2}.$" "First, let's recall the power rule for logarithms:\n\n$$\n{\\log }_{n}⁡({c}^{m})=m{\\log }_{n}⁡(c)\n$$\n\nUsing the power rule for logarithms, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{2}{\\log }_{2}⁡t & ={\\log }_{2}⁡({t}^{5/2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence $k=\\frac{5}{2}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186111342 :question/problem "![](images/96/96b22799c75a4b19df4c6bddf8b63d11a7d0cab8a0cc481b8466a29eb1a2af15.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $y$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\frac{3\\sqrt{5}}{7},$ use the Pythagorean identity to find $tan⁡\\theta .$" "![](images/96/96b22799c75a4b19df4c6bddf8b63d11a7d0cab8a0cc481b8466a29eb1a2af15.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $y$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\frac{3\\sqrt{5}}{7},$ use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\tan ⁡\\theta .$"] [:db/cas 17592186111342 :question/worked-solution "![](images/21/21170c11aa5ff0ab39b86f94588e5e8a0fa178d0c34b9aa1774ee9f07a4d0812.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=cos⁡\\theta ,\\,y=sin⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $y=\\frac{3\\sqrt{5}}{7}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\nsin⁡\\theta =\\frac{3\\sqrt{5}}{7}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $cos⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ cos⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{3\\sqrt{5}}{7}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}cos⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{\\left(\\frac{3\\sqrt{5}}{7}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{45}{49}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{4}{49}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{2}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the positive answer since $cos⁡\\theta $ is positive in the first quadrant. So, $cos⁡\\theta =\\frac{2}{7}.$\n\nFinally, knowing that $tan⁡\\theta =\\frac{sin⁡\\theta }{cos⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\ntan⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\left(\\frac{3\\sqrt{5}}{7}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{2}{7}\\right)}=\\frac{3\\sqrt{5}}{2}.\n$$" "![](images/21/21170c11aa5ff0ab39b86f94588e5e8a0fa178d0c34b9aa1774ee9f07a4d0812.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=\\cos ⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\sin ⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $y=\\frac{3\\sqrt{5}}{7}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\sin ⁡\\theta =\\frac{3\\sqrt{5}}{7}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\cos ⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta +{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\cos ⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\sin ⁡\\theta =\\frac{3\\sqrt{5}}{7}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\cos ⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{\\left(\\frac{3\\sqrt{5}}{7}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{45}{49}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{4}{49}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{2}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the positive answer since $\\cos ⁡\\theta $ is positive in the first quadrant. So, $\\cos ⁡\\theta =\\frac{2}{7}.$\n\nFinally, knowing that $\\tan ⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sin ⁡\\theta }{\\cos ⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\tan ⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\left(\\frac{3\\sqrt{5}}{7}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{2}{7}\\right)}=\\frac{3\\sqrt{5}}{2}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186111352 :question/problem "${sec}^{2}⁡x-2{tan}^{2}⁡x=$" "${\\sec }^{2}⁡x-2{\\tan }^{2}⁡x=$"] [:db/cas 17592186111352 :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the secant-tangent identity:\n\n$$\n1+{tan}^{2}⁡x={sec}^{2}⁡x\n$$\n\nWe rewrite the given expression using the secant-tangent identity as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sec}^{2}⁡x-2{tan}^{2}⁡x & =1+{tan}^{2}⁡x-2{tan}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & =1-{tan}^{2}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{sec}^{2}⁡x-2{tan}^{2}⁡x=1-{tan}^{2}⁡x.\n$$" "First, let's recall the secant-tangent identity:\n\n$$\n1+{\\tan }^{2}⁡x={\\sec }^{2}⁡x\n$$\n\nWe rewrite the given expression using the secant-tangent identity as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sec }^{2}⁡x-2{\\tan }^{2}⁡x & =1+{\\tan }^{2}⁡x-2{\\tan }^{2}⁡x \\\\  & =1-{\\tan }^{2}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\sec }^{2}⁡x-2{\\tan }^{2}⁡x=1-{\\tan }^{2}⁡x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186111353 :question/problem "Simplify $\\frac{\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta }{{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta -1}.$" "Simplify $\\frac{\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta }{{\\csc }^{2}⁡\\theta -1}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186111353 :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the following identity:\n\n$$\n{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta -1={cot}^{2}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nSubstituting the above identity into the given expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta }{{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta -1} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta }{{cot}^{2}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cot}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, let's recall the following identity:\n\n$$\n{\\csc }^{2}⁡\\theta -1={\\cot }^{2}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nSubstituting the above identity into the given expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta }{{\\csc }^{2}⁡\\theta -1} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta }{{\\cot }^{2}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cot}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186111382 :question/problem "Which of the following is a zero of $y={log}_{5}⁡x?$" "Which of the following is a zero of $y={\\log }_{5}⁡x?$"] [:db/cas 17592186111382 :question/worked-solution "Let's recall the graph of $y={log}_{5}⁡x.$\n\n![](images/f0/f095d09fd04a982ad51ff3859f6d789083ed7528b382457954e45cf259cc06a5.png)\n\nNote that the graph passes through the point $(1,0).$ \n\nTherefore, the zero of the function $y={log}_{5}⁡x$ is $x=1.$" "Let's recall the graph of $y={\\log }_{5}⁡x.$\n\n![](images/f0/f095d09fd04a982ad51ff3859f6d789083ed7528b382457954e45cf259cc06a5.png)\n\nNote that the graph passes through the point $(1,0).$ \n\nTherefore, the zero of the function $y={\\log }_{5}⁡x$ is $x=1.$"] [:db/cas 17592186111860 :tutorial/content "Let's recall the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cos}^{2}⁡x+{sin}^{2}⁡x &=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we divide both sides of this identity by ${\\color{black}\\cos^2 x},$ we get the equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\cos^2 x} {\\color{black}\\cos^2 x} + \\dfrac {\\sin^2 x}{\\color{black}\\cos^2 x}\t = \\dfrac 1 {\\color{black}\\cos^2 x}\n$$\n\nwhich can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n1+ \\left(\\dfrac {\\sin x}{\\cos x}\\right)^2 = \\left(\\dfrac 1 {\\cos x}\\right)^2 \\qquad (\\ast)\n$$\n\nNow, recall the following identities:\n\n$$\n\\tan x = \\dfrac{\\sin x}{\\cos x}, \\qquad \\sec x = \\dfrac{1}{\\cos x}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above identities into $(\\ast)$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1+{\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\right)}^{2} & ={\\left(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\right)}^{2} \\\\ 1+(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)^{2} & =(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n1 + \\tan^2 x  = \\sec^2 x.\n$$\n\nThe final identity is called the **secant-tangent identity**. We often use it to simplify trigonometric expressions containing both tangent and secant functions." "Let's recall the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\cos }^{2}⁡x+{\\sin }^{2}⁡x &=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we divide both sides of this identity by ${\\color{black}\\cos^2 x},$ we get the equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\cos^2 x} {\\color{black}\\cos^2 x} + \\dfrac {\\sin^2 x}{\\color{black}\\cos^2 x}\t = \\dfrac 1 {\\color{black}\\cos^2 x}\n$$\n\nwhich can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n1+ \\left(\\dfrac {\\sin x}{\\cos x}\\right)^2 = \\left(\\dfrac 1 {\\cos x}\\right)^2 \\qquad (\\ast)\n$$\n\nNow, recall the following identities:\n\n$$\n\\tan x = \\dfrac{\\sin x}{\\cos x}, \\qquad \\sec x = \\dfrac{1}{\\cos x}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above identities into $(\\ast)$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1+{\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\right)}^{2} & ={\\left(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\right)}^{2} \\\\ 1+(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)^{2} & =(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n1 + \\tan^2 x  = \\sec^2 x.\n$$\n\nThe final identity is called the **secant-tangent identity**. We often use it to simplify trigonometric expressions containing both tangent and secant functions."] [:db/cas 17592186111865 :tutorial/content "When simplifying expressions containing $\\cot$ and $\\csc,$ we can use the following **cotangent-cosecant identity**:\n\n$$\n1 + \\cot^2 x= \\csc^2 x\n$$\n\nThe identity above can be obtained by starting with the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡x+{cos}^{2}⁡x &=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe divide both sides by $\\color{black}\\sin^2 x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡x}{{sin}^{2}⁡x}+\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡x}{{sin}^{2}⁡x} & =\\frac{1}{{sin}^{2}⁡x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimplifying the above using $\\cot x = \\dfrac{\\cos x}{\\sin x}$ and $\\csc x = \\dfrac{1}{\\sin x}$ gives\n\n$$\n1 + \\cot^2 x  = \\csc^2 x,\n$$\n\nas required." "When simplifying expressions containing $\\cot$ and $\\csc,$ we can use the following **cotangent-cosecant identity**:\n\n$$\n1 + \\cot^2 x= \\csc^2 x\n$$\n\nThe identity above can be obtained by starting with the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sin }^{2}⁡x+{\\cos }^{2}⁡x &=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe divide both sides by $\\color{black}\\sin^2 x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\sin }^{2}⁡x}{{\\sin }^{2}⁡x}+\\frac{{\\cos }^{2}⁡x}{{\\sin }^{2}⁡x} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sin }^{2}⁡x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimplifying the above using $\\cot x = \\dfrac{\\cos x}{\\sin x}$ and $\\csc x = \\dfrac{1}{\\sin x}$ gives\n\n$$\n1 + \\cot^2 x  = \\csc^2 x,\n$$\n\nas required."] [:db/cas 17592186111866 :tutorial/content "We can rewrite the cotangent-cosecant identity in other useful forms.\n\nStarting with $1 + \\cot^2\\theta = \\csc^2\\theta,$ if we subtract $1$ from both sides, we get\n\n$$\n\\cot^2\\theta = \\csc^2\\theta - 1.\n$$\n\nThis is one alternate form of the cotangent-cosecant identity.\n\nWe can also rewrite the identity another way. Starting again with $1 + \\cot^2\\theta = \\csc^2\\theta,$ if we subtract $\\cot^2\\theta$ from both sides, we get\n\n$$\n\\csc^2\\theta - \\cot^2\\theta = 1.\n$$\n\nFor example, let's simplify $\\dfrac{1}{\\csc^2 \\theta - 1}.$\n\nFirst, we recognize that the denominator matches one of the identities above.\n\nSubstituting the identity into the given expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta -1} & =\\frac{1}{{cot}^{2}⁡\\theta }={tan}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We can rewrite the cotangent-cosecant identity in other useful forms.\n\nStarting with $1 + \\cot^2\\theta = \\csc^2\\theta,$ if we subtract $1$ from both sides, we get\n\n$$\n\\cot^2\\theta = \\csc^2\\theta - 1.\n$$\n\nThis is one alternate form of the cotangent-cosecant identity.\n\nWe can also rewrite the identity another way. Starting again with $1 + \\cot^2\\theta = \\csc^2\\theta,$ if we subtract $\\cot^2\\theta$ from both sides, we get\n\n$$\n\\csc^2\\theta - \\cot^2\\theta = 1.\n$$\n\nFor example, let's simplify $\\dfrac{1}{\\csc^2 \\theta - 1}.$\n\nFirst, we recognize that the denominator matches one of the identities above.\n\nSubstituting the identity into the given expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{{\\csc }^{2}⁡\\theta -1} & =\\frac{1}{{\\cot }^{2}⁡\\theta }={\\tan }^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186111867 :tutorial/content "Recall that the coordinates of a point on the unit circle with a central angle $\\theta$ have the property\n\n$$\nx = \\cos\\theta, \\qquad y = \\sin\\theta.\n$$\n\nIf this point is in the first quadrant, we can substitute these values into the Pythagorean Identity from before to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ (\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2} & =1 \\\\ {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last expression is known as the **Pythagorean trigonometric identity,** and is arguably the most important equation in trigonometry.\n\nThe Pythagorean trigonometric identity holds for *every* angle $\\theta,$ not just if $\\theta$ is in the first quadrant. In this lesson, we're focusing on the first quadrant only. We'll learn how to deal with angles in other quadrants in a future lesson." "Recall that the coordinates of a point on the unit circle with a central angle $\\theta$ have the property\n\n$$\nx = \\cos\\theta, \\qquad y = \\sin\\theta.\n$$\n\nIf this point is in the first quadrant, we can substitute these values into the Pythagorean Identity from before to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ (\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2} & =1 \\\\ {\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta +{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last expression is known as the **Pythagorean trigonometric identity,** and is arguably the most important equation in trigonometry.\n\nThe Pythagorean trigonometric identity holds for *every* angle $\\theta,$ not just if $\\theta$ is in the first quadrant. In this lesson, we're focusing on the first quadrant only. We'll learn how to deal with angles in other quadrants in a future lesson."] [:db/cas 17592186111877 :tutorial/content "We can simplify the addition of two logarithms that share a base using the **product rule** for logarithms.\n\nThe **product rule** states that two logarithms which share a common base $\\color{blue}n$ satisfy\n\n$$\n\\log_{\\color{blue}n}(a) + \\log_{\\color{blue}n}(b) = \\log_{\\color{blue}n}\\left( ab\\right).\n$$\n\nSo, we can use the product rule to rewrite $\\log_{\\color{blue}5}(4) + \\log_{\\color{blue}5}(3)$ as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{5}⁡4+{log}_{5}⁡(3) & ={log}_{5}⁡(4\\cdot 3) \\\\  & ={log}_{5}⁡(12).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe product rule works exactly the same for the natural and common logarithms." "We can simplify the addition of two logarithms that share a base using the **product rule** for logarithms.\n\nThe **product rule** states that two logarithms which share a common base $\\color{blue}n$ satisfy\n\n$$\n\\log_{\\color{blue}n}(a) + \\log_{\\color{blue}n}(b) = \\log_{\\color{blue}n}\\left( ab\\right).\n$$\n\nSo, we can use the product rule to rewrite $\\log_{\\color{blue}5}(4) + \\log_{\\color{blue}5}(3)$ as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\log }_{5}⁡4+{\\log }_{5}⁡(3) & ={\\log }_{5}⁡(4\\cdot 3) \\\\  & ={\\log }_{5}⁡(12).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe product rule works exactly the same for the natural and common logarithms."] [:db/cas 17592186111878 :tutorial/content "Suppose we want to rewrite $\\log_3(4) - \\log_3(6)$ as a single logarithm. We can do this using the **quotient rule**. \n\nThe quotient rule is very similar to the product rule, except that this time we're dealing with the difference of two logarithms rather than the sum. \n\nThe quotient rule states that two logarithms which share a common base $\\color{blue}n$ satisfy\n\n$$\n\\log_{\\color{blue}n} (a) - \\log_{\\color{blue}n} (b) = \\log_{\\color{blue}n}\\left(\\dfrac a b\\right) .\n$$\n\nSo, we can use this rule to rewrite $\\log_{\\color{blue}3}(4) - \\log_{\\color{blue}3}(6)$ as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{3}⁡(4)-{log}_{3}⁡(6) & ={log}_{3}⁡\\left(\\frac{4}{6}\\right) \\\\  & ={log}_{3}⁡\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe quotient rule works exactly the same for the natural and common logarithms." "Suppose we want to rewrite $\\log_3(4) - \\log_3(6)$ as a single logarithm. We can do this using the **quotient rule**. \n\nThe quotient rule is very similar to the product rule, except that this time we're dealing with the difference of two logarithms rather than the sum. \n\nThe quotient rule states that two logarithms which share a common base $\\color{blue}n$ satisfy\n\n$$\n\\log_{\\color{blue}n} (a) - \\log_{\\color{blue}n} (b) = \\log_{\\color{blue}n}\\left(\\dfrac a b\\right) .\n$$\n\nSo, we can use this rule to rewrite $\\log_{\\color{blue}3}(4) - \\log_{\\color{blue}3}(6)$ as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\log }_{3}⁡(4)-{\\log }_{3}⁡(6) & ={\\log }_{3}⁡\\left(\\frac{4}{6}\\right) \\\\  & ={\\log }_{3}⁡\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe quotient rule works exactly the same for the natural and common logarithms."] [:db/cas 17592186111881 :tutorial/content "Now suppose we wish to find the inverse of the logarithmic function \n\n$$\nf(x)=3\\log_{2}(x-1)+1.\n$$\n\nWe can find $f^{-1}(x)$ using the same three steps as before.\n\n**Step 1:** We start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y{:}$\n\n$$\ny = 3\\log_{2}(x-1)+1\n$$\n\n**Step 2:** Then, we interchange $x$ and $y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\nx = 3\\log_{2}(y-1)+1\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Finally, we make $y$ the subject of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{log}_{2}⁡(y-1)+1 & =x \\\\ 3{log}_{2}⁡(y-1) & =x-1 \\\\ {log}_{2}⁡(y-1) & =\\frac{x-1}{3} \\\\ {2}^{{log}_{2}⁡(y-1)} & ={2}^{(x-1)/3} \\\\ y-1 & ={2}^{(x-1)/3} \\\\ y & ={2}^{(x-1)/3}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nTherefore, the inverse function is \n\n$$\nf^{-1}(x) = {2}^{(x-1)/3}+1.\n$$" "Now suppose we wish to find the inverse of the logarithmic function \n\n$$\nf(x)=3\\log_{2}(x-1)+1.\n$$\n\nWe can find $f^{-1}(x)$ using the same three steps as before.\n\n**Step 1:** We start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y{:}$\n\n$$\ny = 3\\log_{2}(x-1)+1\n$$\n\n**Step 2:** Then, we interchange $x$ and $y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\nx = 3\\log_{2}(y-1)+1\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Finally, we make $y$ the subject of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{\\log }_{2}⁡(y-1)+1 & =x \\\\ 3{\\log }_{2}⁡(y-1) & =x-1 \\\\ {\\log }_{2}⁡(y-1) & =\\frac{x-1}{3} \\\\ {2}^{{\\log }_{2}⁡(y-1)} & ={2}^{(x-1)/3} \\\\ y-1 & ={2}^{(x-1)/3} \\\\ y & ={2}^{(x-1)/3}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nTherefore, the inverse function is \n\n$$\nf^{-1}(x) = {2}^{(x-1)/3}+1.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186111905 :tutorial/content "Suppose we have the transformed exponential function \n\n$$\nf(x)=2^{5x}+1\n$$\n\nand we wish to find the inverse function $f^{-1}(x).$ We can do this using the following three steps.\n\n**Step 1:** We start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={2}^{5x}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2:** Then, we interchange $x$ and $y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={2}^{5y}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Finally, we make $y$ the subject of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={2}^{5y}+1 \\\\ x-1 & ={2}^{5y} \\\\ {log}_{2}⁡(x-1) & ={log}_{2}⁡({2}^{5y}) \\\\ {log}_{2}⁡(x-1) & =5y \\\\ 5y & ={log}_{2}⁡(x-1) \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{5}{log}_{2}⁡(x-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nTherefore, the inverse function is \n\n$$\nf^{-1}(x) = \\dfrac{1}{5}\\log_2{(x-1)}.\n$$" "Suppose we have the transformed exponential function \n\n$$\nf(x)=2^{5x}+1\n$$\n\nand we wish to find the inverse function $f^{-1}(x).$ We can do this using the following three steps.\n\n**Step 1:** We start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={2}^{5x}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2:** Then, we interchange $x$ and $y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={2}^{5y}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Finally, we make $y$ the subject of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={2}^{5y}+1 \\\\ x-1 & ={2}^{5y} \\\\ {\\log }_{2}⁡(x-1) & ={\\log }_{2}⁡({2}^{5y}) \\\\ {\\log }_{2}⁡(x-1) & =5y \\\\ 5y & ={\\log }_{2}⁡(x-1) \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{5}{\\log }_{2}⁡(x-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nTherefore, the inverse function is \n\n$$\nf^{-1}(x) = \\dfrac{1}{5}\\log_2{(x-1)}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186113372 :answer/value "det\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{bmatrix} = 0" "\\det \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{bmatrix} = 0"] [:db/cas 17592186114997 :question/worked-solution "Here, we are given that the area of the sector is\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A}=6\\pi\\,\\textrm{m}^2\n$$\n\nand the central angle is\n\n$$\n\\theta=\\dfrac{4}{3}\\pi\n$$\n\nradians.\n\nRearranging the formula for the area of a sector, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= \\frac{r^{2}θ}{2} \\\\\nr^{2}θ &= 2A \\\\\nr^{2} &= \\frac{2A}{θ}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr^{2} &= \\frac{2 \\cdot A}{θ} \\\\\n&= (2 \\cdot 6π)/((\\frac{4}{3}π)) \\\\\n&= 12π \\div \\frac{4π}{3} \\\\\n&= 12π \\cdot \\frac{3}{4π} \\\\\n&= \\frac{36π}{4π} \\\\\n&= 9\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\nr=\\sqrt{9} = 3\\,\\textrm{m}\n$$" "Here, we are given that the area of the sector is\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A}=6\\pi\\,\\textrm{m}^2\n$$\n\nand the central angle is\n\n$$\n\\theta=\\dfrac{4}{3}\\pi\n$$\n\nradians.\n\nRearranging the formula for the area of a sector, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= \\frac{r^{2}θ}{2} \\\\\nr^{2}θ &= 2A \\\\\nr^{2} &= \\frac{2A}{θ}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr^{2} &= \\frac{2 \\cdot A}{θ} \\\\\n&= (2 \\cdot 6π)/((\\frac{4}{3}π)) \\\\\n&= 12π \\operatorname{div}  \\frac{4π}{3} \\\\\n&= 12π \\cdot \\frac{3}{4π} \\\\\n&= \\frac{36π}{4π} \\\\\n&= 9\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\nr=\\sqrt{9} = 3\\,\\textrm{m}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186115019 :question/worked-solution "Here, we just take the constant out of the derivative and use the result for the derivative of $\\csc x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2csc⁡x) \\\\  & =2\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(csc⁡x) \\\\  & =2(-csc⁡xcot⁡x) \\\\  & =-2csc⁡xcot⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Here, we just take the constant out of the derivative and use the result for the derivative of $\\csc x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2\\csc ⁡x) \\\\  & =2\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\csc ⁡x) \\\\  & =2(-\\csc ⁡x\\cot ⁡x) \\\\  & =-2\\csc ⁡x\\cot ⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186115020 :question/worked-solution "We take the derivative of each term and then add them, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2x-3cot⁡x) \\\\  & =2-3(-{csc}^{2}⁡x) \\\\  & =2+3{csc}^{2}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We take the derivative of each term and then add them, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2x-3\\cot ⁡x) \\\\  & =2-3(-{\\csc }^{2}⁡x) \\\\  & =2+3{\\csc }^{2}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186115021 :question/worked-solution "We use the product rule, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(xsec⁡x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x)\\cdot sec⁡x+x\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(sec⁡x) \\\\  & =(1)\\cdot sec⁡x+x\\cdot (sec⁡xtan⁡x) \\\\  & =sec⁡x(1+xtan⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We use the product rule, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x\\sec ⁡x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x)\\cdot \\sec ⁡x+x\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\sec ⁡x) \\\\  & =(1)\\cdot \\sec ⁡x+x\\cdot (\\sec ⁡x\\tan ⁡x) \\\\  & =\\sec ⁡x(1+x\\tan ⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186115022 :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate the derivative using the quotient rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{-2cot⁡x}{sin⁡x}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-2cot⁡x)\\cdot sin⁡x-(-2cot⁡x)\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(sin⁡x)}{{[sin⁡x]}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2(-{csc}^{2}⁡x)\\cdot sin⁡x+2cot⁡x\\cdot (cos⁡x)}{{sin}^{2}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\frac{2csc⁡x+2cot⁡xcos⁡x}{{sin}^{2}⁡x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $x=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}$ in the derivative and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) & =\\frac{2csc⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)+2cot⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)cos⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)}{{sin}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\cdot (\\sqrt{2})+2\\cdot (1)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)}{{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)} \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we calculate the derivative using the quotient rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{-2\\cot ⁡x}{\\sin ⁡x}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-2\\cot ⁡x)\\cdot \\sin ⁡x-(-2\\cot ⁡x)\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\sin ⁡x)}{{[\\sin ⁡x]}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2(-{\\csc }^{2}⁡x)\\cdot \\sin ⁡x+2\\cot ⁡x\\cdot (\\cos ⁡x)}{{\\sin }^{2}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\csc ⁡x+2\\cot ⁡x\\cos ⁡x}{{\\sin }^{2}⁡x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $x=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}$ in the derivative and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) & =\\frac{2\\csc ⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)+2\\cot ⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)\\cos ⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)}{{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\cdot (\\sqrt{2})+2\\cdot (1)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)}{{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)} \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186115025 :question/worked-solution "Both logarithmic terms have a base of $2$ and an argument of $5.$ Therefore, we can combine them by adding their coefficients:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{log}_{2}⁡5+4{log}_{2}⁡5 & =(2+4){log}_{2}⁡5=6{log}_{2}⁡5\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Both logarithmic terms have a base of $2$ and an argument of $5.$ Therefore, we can combine them by adding their coefficients:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{\\log }_{2}⁡5+4{\\log }_{2}⁡5 & =(2+4){\\log }_{2}⁡5=6{\\log }_{2}⁡5\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186115026 :question/worked-solution "Both the first and the third logarithmic terms have a base of ${\\color{blue}4}$ and an argument of ${\\color{red}9}.$ Therefore, we can combine them by adding their coefficients:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{log}_{4}⁡(9)-3{log}_{3}⁡(9)+5{log}_{4}⁡(9) & =(2+5){log}_{4}⁡(9)-3{log}_{3}⁡(9) \\\\  & =7{log}_{4}⁡(9)-3{log}_{3}⁡(9)\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Both the first and the third logarithmic terms have a base of ${\\color{blue}4}$ and an argument of ${\\color{red}9}.$ Therefore, we can combine them by adding their coefficients:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{\\log }_{4}⁡(9)-3{\\log }_{3}⁡(9)+5{\\log }_{4}⁡(9) & =(2+5){\\log }_{4}⁡(9)-3{\\log }_{3}⁡(9) \\\\  & =7{\\log }_{4}⁡(9)-3{\\log }_{3}⁡(9)\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186115027 :question/worked-solution "Both the second and the third logarithmic terms have a base of ${\\color{blue}4}$ and an argument of ${\\color{red}2x}.$ Therefore, we can combine them by adding their coefficients:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{log}_{6}⁡(2x)-2{log}_{4}⁡(2x)-3{log}_{4}⁡(2x) & =9{log}_{6}⁡(2x)+(-2-3){log}_{4}⁡(2x) \\\\  & =9{log}_{6}⁡(2x)-5{log}_{4}⁡(2x)\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Both the second and the third logarithmic terms have a base of ${\\color{blue}4}$ and an argument of ${\\color{red}2x}.$ Therefore, we can combine them by adding their coefficients:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{\\log }_{6}⁡(2x)-2{\\log }_{4}⁡(2x)-3{\\log }_{4}⁡(2x) & =9{\\log }_{6}⁡(2x)+(-2-3){\\log }_{4}⁡(2x) \\\\  & =9{\\log }_{6}⁡(2x)-5{\\log }_{4}⁡(2x)\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186115040 :question/worked-solution "Since the logarithms on both sides of the equation have the same base, we can equate the arguments. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{2}⁡({x}^{2}-6) & ={log}_{2}⁡(10) \\\\ {x}^{2}-6 & =10 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =16 \\\\ x & =\\pm 4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now check for extraneous solutions by substituting back into the original equation: \n\n- Substituting $x=4$ back into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{2}⁡({x}^{2}-6) & ={log}_{2}⁡({4}^{2}-6) \\\\  & ={log}_{2}⁡(16-6) \\\\  & ={log}_{2}⁡(10).\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=4$ is a valid solution.\n\n- Substituting $x=-4$ back into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{2}⁡({x}^{2}-6) & ={log}_{2}⁡((-4)^{2}-6) \\\\  & ={log}_{2}⁡(16-6) \\\\  & ={log}_{2}⁡(10).\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=-4$ is a valid solution.\n\nSo, we conclude that the solutions are $x = \\pm 4.$" "Since the logarithms on both sides of the equation have the same base, we can equate the arguments. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\log }_{2}⁡({x}^{2}-6) & ={\\log }_{2}⁡(10) \\\\ {x}^{2}-6 & =10 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =16 \\\\ x & =\\pm 4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now check for extraneous solutions by substituting back into the original equation: \n\n- Substituting $x=4$ back into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\log }_{2}⁡({x}^{2}-6) & ={\\log }_{2}⁡({4}^{2}-6) \\\\  & ={\\log }_{2}⁡(16-6) \\\\  & ={\\log }_{2}⁡(10).\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=4$ is a valid solution.\n\n- Substituting $x=-4$ back into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\log }_{2}⁡({x}^{2}-6) & ={\\log }_{2}⁡((-4)^{2}-6) \\\\  & ={\\log }_{2}⁡(16-6) \\\\  & ={\\log }_{2}⁡(10).\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=-4$ is a valid solution.\n\nSo, we conclude that the solutions are $x = \\pm 4.$"] [:db/cas 17592186115106 :question/worked-solution "Clearly, $b \\neq 0.$ Substituting $x=\\dfrac1b$ into the equation of the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =k{log}_{5}⁡(b\\cdot \\frac{1}{b}) \\\\  & =k{log}_{5}⁡1 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the point $\\left(\\dfrac1b, 0\\right)$ lies on the curve. But from the diagram, we know that $(2,0)$ lies on the curve. Hence, \n\n$$\n(2,0)  = \\left(\\dfrac1b,0\\right)\\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad b=\\dfrac12.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute coordinates of the point $(10,3)$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k{log}_{5}⁡(bx) & =y \\\\ k{log}_{5}⁡(\\frac{1}{2}x) & =y \\\\ k{log}_{5}⁡(\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 10) & =3 \\\\ k{log}_{5}⁡5 & =3 \\\\ k & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\nb\\cdot k = \\dfrac12 \\cdot 3 = \\dfrac32.\n$$" "Clearly, $b \\neq 0.$ Substituting $x=\\dfrac1b$ into the equation of the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =k{\\log }_{5}⁡(b\\cdot \\frac{1}{b}) \\\\  & =k{\\log }_{5}⁡1 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the point $\\left(\\dfrac1b, 0\\right)$ lies on the curve. But from the diagram, we know that $(2,0)$ lies on the curve. Hence, \n\n$$\n(2,0)  = \\left(\\dfrac1b,0\\right)\\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad b=\\dfrac12.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute coordinates of the point $(10,3)$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k{\\log }_{5}⁡(bx) & =y \\\\ k{\\log }_{5}⁡(\\frac{1}{2}x) & =y \\\\ k{\\log }_{5}⁡(\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 10) & =3 \\\\ k{\\log }_{5}⁡5 & =3 \\\\ k & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\nb\\cdot k = \\dfrac12 \\cdot 3 = \\dfrac32.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186115531 :question/problem "Find the slope of $y=f(x)$ at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ if $f(x)=2x-\\sqrt{2}sec⁡x.$" "Find the slope of $y=f(x)$ at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ if $f(x)=2x-\\sqrt{2}\\sec ⁡x.$"] [:db/cas 17592186115531 :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate the derivative using the sum rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2x-\\sqrt{2}sec⁡x) \\\\  & =2-\\sqrt{2}sec⁡xtan⁡x\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ in the derivative and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) & =2-\\sqrt{2}sec⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)tan⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) \\\\  & =2-\\sqrt{2}(\\sqrt{2})(1) \\\\  & =2-2 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we calculate the derivative using the sum rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2x-\\sqrt{2}\\sec ⁡x) \\\\  & =2-\\sqrt{2}\\sec ⁡x\\tan ⁡x\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ in the derivative and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) & =2-\\sqrt{2}\\sec ⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)\\tan ⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) \\\\  & =2-\\sqrt{2}(\\sqrt{2})(1) \\\\  & =2-2 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186115727 :question/problem "Find ${f}^{′}(x)$ if $f(x)={x}^{2}-6csc⁡x.$" "Find ${f}^{′}(x)$ if $f(x)={x}^{2}-6\\csc ⁡x.$"] [:db/cas 17592186115727 :question/worked-solution "We take the derivative of each term and then add them, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}-6csc⁡x) \\\\  & =2x-6(-csc⁡xcot⁡x) \\\\  & =2x+6csc⁡xcot⁡x\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We take the derivative of each term and then add them, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}-6\\csc ⁡x) \\\\  & =2x-6(-\\csc ⁡x\\cot ⁡x) \\\\  & =2x+6\\csc ⁡x\\cot ⁡x\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186115770 :question/worked-solution "All of these vector-valued functions are defined for all $t\\in (0,\\pi ).$ \n\nA function is differentiable on the interval $(0,\\pi )$ if and only if each of \n its component functions are differentiable on that interval.\n\nLet's now consider the functions one-by-one.\n\n- We differentiate $f(t)$ to find ${f}^{′}(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t),\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince each component of ${f}^{′}(t)$ is defined for all  $t\\in (0,\\pi )$, we conclude that $f(t)$ is differentiable on this interval.\n\n- Next, we differentiate $g(t)$  to find ${g}^{′}(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1+{t}^{2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-t+2),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{t})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2t,\\,-1,\\,\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince each component of ${g}^{′}(t)$ is defined for all $(0,\\pi )$, we have that   $g(t)$ is also differentiable on this interval.\n\n- Finally, we differentiate $h(t)$ to find ${h}^{′}(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{1}{t}\\right),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{tan}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}},\\,{sec}^{2}⁡t,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the second component of this vector, $\\operatorname{sec}⁡t$, is undefined at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$,  we conclude that the function $h(t)$ is not differentiable on $(0,\\pi ).$\n\nTherefore, the answer is:\n\n$\\,$ $f(t)$ and $g(t)$ only" "All of these vector-valued functions are defined for all $t\\in (0,\\pi ).$ \n\nA function is differentiable on the interval $(0,\\pi )$ if and only if each of \n its component functions are differentiable on that interval.\n\nLet's now consider the functions one-by-one.\n\n- We differentiate $f(t)$ to find ${f}^{′}(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t),\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince each component of ${f}^{′}(t)$ is defined for all  $t\\in (0,\\pi )$, we conclude that $f(t)$ is differentiable on this interval.\n\n- Next, we differentiate $g(t)$  to find ${g}^{′}(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1+{t}^{2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-t+2),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{t})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2t,\\,-1,\\,\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince each component of ${g}^{′}(t)$ is defined for all $(0,\\pi )$, we have that   $g(t)$ is also differentiable on this interval.\n\n- Finally, we differentiate $h(t)$ to find ${h}^{′}(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{1}{t}\\right),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{tan}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}},\\,{\\sec }^{2}⁡t,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the second component of this vector, $\\operatorname{sec}⁡t$, is undefined at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$,  we conclude that the function $h(t)$ is not differentiable on $(0,\\pi ).$\n\nTherefore, the answer is:\n\n$\\,$ $f(t)$ and $g(t)$ only"] [:db/cas 17592186115826 :question/problem "Determine the range of the function $f(x)=2{log}_{5}⁡(2x-1)+1.$" "Determine the range of the function $f(x)=2{\\log }_{5}⁡(2x-1)+1.$"] [:db/cas 17592186115826 :question/worked-solution "The range of the function $y={log}_{5}⁡(x)$ is $y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ \n\nHorizontal and vertical shifts and stretches do not affect the range of a logarithmic function.\n\nTherefore, the range of the given function is $f(x)\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$" "The range of the function $y={\\log }_{5}⁡(x)$ is $y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ \n\nHorizontal and vertical shifts and stretches do not affect the range of a logarithmic function.\n\nTherefore, the range of the given function is $f(x)\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$"] [:db/cas 17592186116463 :tutorial/content "Suppose we want to solve the equation\n\n$$\n\\sec x={\\sqrt{2}}, \\qquad  0^\\circ\\leq x <   360^\\circ.\n$$\n\nMost calculators don't have a $\\textrm{arcsec}{x}$ button, so we can't get a principal value immediately. However, by rewriting the equation in terms of $\\cos{x}$, we can get a principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sec}x & =\\sqrt{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}x} & =\\sqrt{2} \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}x & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can proceed as usual. Our principal value is\n\n$$\nx = \\arccos \\left(\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\right)= 45^\\circ.\n$$\n\nSince $45^\\circ$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is $x_1=45^\\circ.$ It lies in the first quadrant and has a reference angle $\\theta_R = 45^\\circ.$\n\nSince $\\cos{x} > 0,$ the second solution must lie in the fourth quadrant, and it also has a reference angle $\\theta_R = 45^\\circ.$\n\n![](images/c7/c722d4fa2b77011e4df5b086c19bb09943446941484cfaf87559ca39a52183bc.png)\n\nSince the second solution $x_2$ lies in the $4$th quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\nx_2 = 360^\\circ - 45^\\circ = 315^\\circ .\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions are $x=45^\\circ, 315^\\circ.$" "Suppose we want to solve the equation\n\n$$\n\\sec x={\\sqrt{2}}, \\qquad  0^\\circ\\leq x <   360^\\circ.\n$$\n\nMost calculators don't have a $\\operatorname{arcsec} {x}$ button, so we can't get a principal value immediately. However, by rewriting the equation in terms of $\\cos{x}$, we can get a principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sec}x & =\\sqrt{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}x} & =\\sqrt{2} \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}x & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can proceed as usual. Our principal value is\n\n$$\nx = \\arccos \\left(\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\right)= 45^\\circ.\n$$\n\nSince $45^\\circ$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is $x_1=45^\\circ.$ It lies in the first quadrant and has a reference angle $\\theta_R = 45^\\circ.$\n\nSince $\\cos{x} > 0,$ the second solution must lie in the fourth quadrant, and it also has a reference angle $\\theta_R = 45^\\circ.$\n\n![](images/c7/c722d4fa2b77011e4df5b086c19bb09943446941484cfaf87559ca39a52183bc.png)\n\nSince the second solution $x_2$ lies in the $4$th quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\nx_2 = 360^\\circ - 45^\\circ = 315^\\circ .\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions are $x=45^\\circ, 315^\\circ.$"] [:db/cas 17592186116465 :tutorial/content "Suppose we want to solve the equation\n\n$$\n\\csc x = \\sqrt{2},  \\qquad 0^\\circ \\leq x < 360^\\circ.\n$$\n\nMost calculators don't have an $\\textrm{arccsc}$ button, so we can't get a principal value immediately. However, by rewriting the equation in terms of $\\sin x,$ we can get a principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{csc}x & =\\sqrt{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}x} & =\\sqrt{2} \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}x & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can proceed as usual. Our principal value is\n\n$$\nx = \\arcsin \\left(\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\right)= 45^\\circ.\n$$\n\nSince $45^\\circ$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is $x_1=45^\\circ.$ It is in the first quadrant and has a reference angle $\\theta_R = 45^\\circ.$\n\nSince $\\sin{x} > 0,$ the second solution must lie in the second quadrant, and it also has a reference angle $\\theta_R = 45^\\circ.$\n\n![](images/50/50f7efb47ec8c6dab76c82b77806b419a88b3944266346e32a8fefb05f5bd479.png)\n\nSince the second solution $x_2$ lies in the $2$nd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\nx_2 = 180^\\circ - 45^\\circ = 135^\\circ .\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions are $x=45^\\circ, 135^\\circ.$" "Suppose we want to solve the equation\n\n$$\n\\csc x = \\sqrt{2},  \\qquad 0^\\circ \\leq x < 360^\\circ.\n$$\n\nMost calculators don't have an $\\operatorname{arccsc} $ button, so we can't get a principal value immediately. However, by rewriting the equation in terms of $\\sin x,$ we can get a principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{csc}x & =\\sqrt{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}x} & =\\sqrt{2} \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}x & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can proceed as usual. Our principal value is\n\n$$\nx = \\arcsin \\left(\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\right)= 45^\\circ.\n$$\n\nSince $45^\\circ$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is $x_1=45^\\circ.$ It is in the first quadrant and has a reference angle $\\theta_R = 45^\\circ.$\n\nSince $\\sin{x} > 0,$ the second solution must lie in the second quadrant, and it also has a reference angle $\\theta_R = 45^\\circ.$\n\n![](images/50/50f7efb47ec8c6dab76c82b77806b419a88b3944266346e32a8fefb05f5bd479.png)\n\nSince the second solution $x_2$ lies in the $2$nd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\nx_2 = 180^\\circ - 45^\\circ = 135^\\circ .\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions are $x=45^\\circ, 135^\\circ.$"] [:db/cas 17592186116467 :tutorial/content "Suppose we want to solve the equation\n\n$$\n\\cot x = 1, \\qquad 0 \\leq x < 2\\pi.\n$$\n\nMost calculators don't have a $\\textrm{arccot}$ button, so we can't get a principal value immediately. However, recall that $\\cot x = \\dfrac{1}{\\tan x}.$ We can get a principal value if we rewrite the equation in terms of $\\tan x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\tan{x} = \\frac{1}{1} = 1\n$$\n\nNow, we can proceed as usual. Our principal value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\operatorname{arctan}(1)=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\dfrac\\pi 4$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is $x_1 = \\dfrac{\\pi}{4}.$\n\nSince we're dealing with the tangent function, we can get additional solutions by adding multiples of $\\pm \\pi$ to our principal value until we find all solutions. So we get another solution,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={x}_{1}+\\pi  \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\pi  \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are no more solutions in the desired domain. So, the solutions are $x=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}, \\dfrac{5\\pi}{4}.$" "Suppose we want to solve the equation\n\n$$\n\\cot x = 1, \\qquad 0 \\leq x < 2\\pi.\n$$\n\nMost calculators don't have a $\\operatorname{arccot} $ button, so we can't get a principal value immediately. However, recall that $\\cot x = \\dfrac{1}{\\tan x}.$ We can get a principal value if we rewrite the equation in terms of $\\tan x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\tan{x} = \\frac{1}{1} = 1\n$$\n\nNow, we can proceed as usual. Our principal value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\operatorname{arctan}(1)=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\dfrac\\pi 4$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is $x_1 = \\dfrac{\\pi}{4}.$\n\nSince we're dealing with the tangent function, we can get additional solutions by adding multiples of $\\pm \\pi$ to our principal value until we find all solutions. So we get another solution,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={x}_{1}+\\pi  \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\pi  \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are no more solutions in the desired domain. So, the solutions are $x=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}, \\dfrac{5\\pi}{4}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186116474 :tutorial/content "To see why the derivative of $\\sec x$ is $\\sec x \\tan x,$ we start by remembering that\n\n$$\n\\sec x = \\dfrac{1}{\\cos x}.\n$$\n\nIf we now differentiate the above relation using the quotient rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(1)\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡x-1\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}{{[\\operatorname{cos}⁡x]}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(0)\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡x-1\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}{{cos}^{2}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{{cos}^{2}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\underbrace{\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}_{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x}\\underbrace{\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}_{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can use the same method to find the derivative of the other two reciprocal trigonometric functions,\n\n$$\n\\csc x = \\dfrac{1}{\\sin x} \\qquad \\text{and} \\qquad \\cot x = \\dfrac{1}{\\tan x}.\n$$" "To see why the derivative of $\\sec x$ is $\\sec x \\tan x,$ we start by remembering that\n\n$$\n\\sec x = \\dfrac{1}{\\cos x}.\n$$\n\nIf we now differentiate the above relation using the quotient rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(1)\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡x-1\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}{{[\\operatorname{cos}⁡x]}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(0)\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡x-1\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}{{\\cos }^{2}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{{\\cos }^{2}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\underbrace{\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}_{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x}\\underbrace{\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}_{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can use the same method to find the derivative of the other two reciprocal trigonometric functions,\n\n$$\n\\csc x = \\dfrac{1}{\\sin x} \\qquad \\text{and} \\qquad \\cot x = \\dfrac{1}{\\tan x}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186116482 :tutorial/content "To solve an equation that has logarithms with the same base on both sides, we can exponentiate both sides of the equation by the base. For example, consider the equation\n\n$$\n\\log_3(2x-4)=\\log_3(x+3).\n$$\n\nSince both sides of the equation are represented as logarithms of the same base ($3$), we can exponentiate both sides of the equation by $3.$ This gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{{log}_{3}⁡(2x-4)} & ={3}^{{log}_{3}⁡(x+3)} \\\\ 2x-4 & =x+3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for $x\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-4 & =x+3 \\\\ x & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=7$ is the solution.\n\n**Caution:** Remember that, when solving logarithmic equations, it is sometimes possible to get *extraneous* solutions that don't actually satisfy the original equation. We can't take a logarithm of zero or a negative number.\n\nSo, once we've found the solutions, we need to substitute them back into the original equation and check that the arguments of the logarithms are positive. Substituting $x=7$ back into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{3}⁡(2x-4) & \\overset{?}{=}{log}_{3}⁡(x+3) \\\\ {log}_{3}⁡(2\\cdot 7-4) & \\overset{?}{=}{log}_{3}⁡(7+3) \\\\ {log}_{3}⁡(10) & ={log}_{3}⁡(10).\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=7$ is a valid solution." "To solve an equation that has logarithms with the same base on both sides, we can exponentiate both sides of the equation by the base. For example, consider the equation\n\n$$\n\\log_3(2x-4)=\\log_3(x+3).\n$$\n\nSince both sides of the equation are represented as logarithms of the same base ($3$), we can exponentiate both sides of the equation by $3.$ This gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{{\\log }_{3}⁡(2x-4)} & ={3}^{{\\log }_{3}⁡(x+3)} \\\\ 2x-4 & =x+3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for $x\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-4 & =x+3 \\\\ x & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=7$ is the solution.\n\n**Caution:** Remember that, when solving logarithmic equations, it is sometimes possible to get *extraneous* solutions that don't actually satisfy the original equation. We can't take a logarithm of zero or a negative number.\n\nSo, once we've found the solutions, we need to substitute them back into the original equation and check that the arguments of the logarithms are positive. Substituting $x=7$ back into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\log }_{3}⁡(2x-4) & \\overset{?}{=}{\\log }_{3}⁡(x+3) \\\\ {\\log }_{3}⁡(2\\cdot 7-4) & \\overset{?}{=}{\\log }_{3}⁡(7+3) \\\\ {\\log }_{3}⁡(10) & ={\\log }_{3}⁡(10).\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=7$ is a valid solution."] [:db/cas 17592186116483 :tutorial/content "When two logarithmic terms in an expression have the same base and argument, we can combine them by adding their coefficients.\n\nTo demonstrate, let's look at the expression\n\n$$\n5\\log_{\\color{blue}4}{\\color{red}7}+3\\log_{\\color{blue}4}{\\color{red}7}.\n$$\n\nBoth logarithmic terms have a base of $\\color{blue}4$ and an argument of ${\\color{red}7}.$ Therefore, we can combine them by adding their coefficients:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{log}_{4}⁡7+3{log}_{4}⁡7 & =(5+3){log}_{4}⁡7=8{log}_{4}⁡7\\end{aligned}\n$$" "When two logarithmic terms in an expression have the same base and argument, we can combine them by adding their coefficients.\n\nTo demonstrate, let's look at the expression\n\n$$\n5\\log_{\\color{blue}4}{\\color{red}7}+3\\log_{\\color{blue}4}{\\color{red}7}.\n$$\n\nBoth logarithmic terms have a base of $\\color{blue}4$ and an argument of ${\\color{red}7}.$ Therefore, we can combine them by adding their coefficients:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{\\log }_{4}⁡7+3{\\log }_{4}⁡7 & =(5+3){\\log }_{4}⁡7=8{\\log }_{4}⁡7\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186116524 :tutorial/content "Trigonometric functions appear very frequently in calculus, so it's extremely important to commit their derivatives to memory.\n\nConveniently, there are some mnemonic tricks that we can use to more easily remember the derivatives of trigonometric functions.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\, & \\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x) & ={sec}^{2}⁡x\\, & \\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x) & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{cot}⁡x) & =-{csc}^{2}⁡x\\, & \\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{csc}⁡x) & =-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\operatorname{cot}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIt's helpful to notice that the \"co\"-functions (cosine, cosecant, and cotangent) all have negative signs in their derivatives, while the other trigonometric functions (sine, secant, and tangent) do not.\n\nIt's also helpful to realize that functions come in pairs.\n\n- Sine and cosine are a pair because each appears in the other's derivative.\n\n- Tangent and secant are a pair because each appears in the other's derivative.\n\n- Cotangent and cosecant are a pair because each appears in the other's derivative.\n\nLastly, it's helpful to realize the following:\n\n- Ignoring the positive or negative sign, tangent and cotangent have a similar format for their derivatives: the square of the function they're paired with.\n\n- Ignoring the positive or negative sign, secant and cosecant have a similar format for their derivatives: the product of themself and the function they're paired with." "Trigonometric functions appear very frequently in calculus, so it's extremely important to commit their derivatives to memory.\n\nConveniently, there are some mnemonic tricks that we can use to more easily remember the derivatives of trigonometric functions.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\, & \\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x) & ={\\sec }^{2}⁡x\\, & \\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x) & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{cot}⁡x) & =-{\\csc }^{2}⁡x\\, & \\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{csc}⁡x) & =-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\operatorname{cot}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIt's helpful to notice that the \"co\"-functions (cosine, cosecant, and cotangent) all have negative signs in their derivatives, while the other trigonometric functions (sine, secant, and tangent) do not.\n\nIt's also helpful to realize that functions come in pairs.\n\n- Sine and cosine are a pair because each appears in the other's derivative.\n\n- Tangent and secant are a pair because each appears in the other's derivative.\n\n- Cotangent and cosecant are a pair because each appears in the other's derivative.\n\nLastly, it's helpful to realize the following:\n\n- Ignoring the positive or negative sign, tangent and cotangent have a similar format for their derivatives: the square of the function they're paired with.\n\n- Ignoring the positive or negative sign, secant and cosecant have a similar format for their derivatives: the product of themself and the function they're paired with."] [:db/cas 17592186119493 :question/worked-solution "Recall that we always round to $3$ decimal places unless stated otherwise.\n\nOur expression can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{e+7}{\\pi-1} = {\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{(}e+7\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{)}} \\div {\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{(}\\pi-1\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{)}}\n$$\n\nWe're now ready to enter this expression into a calculator. To do so, we note the following:\n\n- We enter the symbols from left to right as they appear.\n- Your calculator will have special $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,(\\,}$ and $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,)\\,}$ buttons for left and right parentheses.\n- Scientific and graphing calculators usually have special $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,e\\,}$ and $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,\\pi\\,}$ buttons. You may need to press $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\textrm{2nd}}$ or $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\textrm{SHIFT}}$ to access these.\n\nNotice the parentheses in the expression above.\n\nEvaluating this using a calculator, we obtain\n\n$$\n(e+7) \\div (\\pi-1) \\approx 4.538\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." "Recall that we always round to $3$ decimal places unless stated otherwise.\n\nOur expression can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{e+7}{\\pi-1} = {\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{(}e+7\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{)}} \\operatorname{div}  {\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{(}\\pi-1\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{)}}\n$$\n\nWe're now ready to enter this expression into a calculator. To do so, we note the following:\n\n- We enter the symbols from left to right as they appear.\n- Your calculator will have special $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,(\\,}$ and $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,)\\,}$ buttons for left and right parentheses.\n- Scientific and graphing calculators usually have special $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,e\\,}$ and $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,\\pi\\,}$ buttons. You may need to press $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\textrm{2nd}}$ or $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\textrm{SHIFT}}$ to access these.\n\nNotice the parentheses in the expression above.\n\nEvaluating this using a calculator, we obtain\n\n$$\n(e+7) \\operatorname{div}  (\\pi-1) \\approx 4.538\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."] [:db/cas 17592186119504 :question/worked-solution "Notice that the $1$st column contains three zeros. So, using Laplace expansion along the $1$st column, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & {\\color{lightgray}{5}} & {\\color{lightgray}{5}} & {\\color{lightgray}{4}} \\\\ {\\color{lightgray}{0}} & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ {\\color{lightgray}{0}} & 8 & 2 & 0 \\\\ {\\color{lightgray}{0}} & 13 & 19 & -3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-1)^{1+1}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 8 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 13 & 19 & -3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 8 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 13 & 19 & -3\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe determinant of a triangular matrix is equal to the product of the entries on the main diagonal. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 8 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 13 & 19 & -3\\end{vmatrix} & =1\\cdot 2\\cdot (-3)=-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\det(A) = 2 \\cdot (-6) = -12.\n$$" "Notice that the $1$st column contains three zeros. So, using Laplace expansion along the $1$st column, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\det (A) & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & {\\color{lightgray}{5}} & {\\color{lightgray}{5}} & {\\color{lightgray}{4}} \\\\ {\\color{lightgray}{0}} & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ {\\color{lightgray}{0}} & 8 & 2 & 0 \\\\ {\\color{lightgray}{0}} & 13 & 19 & -3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-1)^{1+1}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 8 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 13 & 19 & -3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 8 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 13 & 19 & -3\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe determinant of a triangular matrix is equal to the product of the entries on the main diagonal. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 8 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 13 & 19 & -3\\end{vmatrix} & =1\\cdot 2\\cdot (-3)=-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\det(A) = 2 \\cdot (-6) = -12.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186119506 :question/worked-solution "First, let's construct the matrix $M,$ whose columns are the coordinates of our vectors $\\mathbf{a},$ $\\mathbf{b},$ $\\mathbf{c},$ and  $\\mathbf{d}{:}$\n\n$$\nM =\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{cccc}2 & 3 & - 2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ - 4 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 5 & - 4 & 2 & 5\\end{array} \\right]}\n$$\n\nThe volume of the parallelepiped spanned by these vectors equals the absolute value of the determinant of $M{:}$\n\n$$\nV = | \\det(M) |\n$$\n\nNotice that the $2$nd row of the determinant contains three zeros. So, using Laplace expansion along the $2$nd row, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =\\begin{vmatrix}{\\color{lightgray}{2}} & 3 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & {\\color{lightgray}{0}} & {\\color{lightgray}{0}} & {\\color{lightgray}{0}} \\\\ {\\color{lightgray}{-4}} & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ {\\color{lightgray}{5}} & -4 & 2 & 5\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =3\\cdot (-1)^{2+1}\\begin{vmatrix}3 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 2 & 5\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-3\\begin{vmatrix}3 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 2 & 5\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the $2$nd row of the $3 \\times 3$ determinant contains two zeros. So, using Laplace expansion along the $2$nd row, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =-3\\begin{vmatrix}3 & {\\color{lightgray}{-2}} & 1 \\\\ {\\color{lightgray}{0}} & 1 & {\\color{lightgray}{0}} \\\\ -4 & {\\color{lightgray}{2}} & 5\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-3\\cdot 1\\cdot (-1)^{2+2}\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ -4 & 5\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-3\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ -4 & 5\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-3(15+4) \\\\  & =-57.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume is\n\n$$\nV = | \\det(M) | = |-57| = 57.\n$$" "First, let's construct the matrix $M,$ whose columns are the coordinates of our vectors $\\mathbf{a},$ $\\mathbf{b},$ $\\mathbf{c},$ and  $\\mathbf{d}{:}$\n\n$$\nM =\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{cccc}2 & 3 & - 2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ - 4 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 5 & - 4 & 2 & 5\\end{array} \\right]}\n$$\n\nThe volume of the parallelepiped spanned by these vectors equals the absolute value of the determinant of $M{:}$\n\n$$\nV = | \\det(M) |\n$$\n\nNotice that the $2$nd row of the determinant contains three zeros. So, using Laplace expansion along the $2$nd row, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\det (M) & =\\begin{vmatrix}{\\color{lightgray}{2}} & 3 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & {\\color{lightgray}{0}} & {\\color{lightgray}{0}} & {\\color{lightgray}{0}} \\\\ {\\color{lightgray}{-4}} & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ {\\color{lightgray}{5}} & -4 & 2 & 5\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =3\\cdot (-1)^{2+1}\\begin{vmatrix}3 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 2 & 5\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-3\\begin{vmatrix}3 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 2 & 5\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the $2$nd row of the $3 \\times 3$ determinant contains two zeros. So, using Laplace expansion along the $2$nd row, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\det (M) & =-3\\begin{vmatrix}3 & {\\color{lightgray}{-2}} & 1 \\\\ {\\color{lightgray}{0}} & 1 & {\\color{lightgray}{0}} \\\\ -4 & {\\color{lightgray}{2}} & 5\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-3\\cdot 1\\cdot (-1)^{2+2}\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ -4 & 5\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-3\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ -4 & 5\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-3(15+4) \\\\  & =-57.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume is\n\n$$\nV = | \\det(M) | = |-57| = 57.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186119507 :question/worked-solution "To determine if the relationship is linear or exponential, we need to determine how $y$ changes as $x$ increases at each step:\n\n- For a linear relationship, there should be a common *difference* between successive values of $y$.\n- For an exponential relationship, there should be a common *ratio* between successive values of $y$.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false, while statement II is true. Let's check for a common ratio by dividing successive values of $y$. We see that\n$1 \\div \\left(\\dfrac15\\right) = 5$,\nand similarly,\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{5}{1} = \\frac{25}{5} = 5.\n$$\nSince the *ratio* of any two successive terms is constant, the relationship is *exponential*. In this case, the common ratio is $5$.\n- Statement III is false. When $x$ increases by $1$, the respective value of $y$ increases by a factor of $5$, not $10$.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$\\color{SteelBlue}\\overset{+1}{\\longrightarrow}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$\\color{SteelBlue}\\overset{+1}{\\longrightarrow}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$\\color{SteelBlue}\\overset{+1}{\\longrightarrow}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac15$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$25$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$\\color{SandyBrown}\\underset{\\times 5}{\\longrightarrow}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$\\color{SandyBrown}\\underset{\\times 5}{\\longrightarrow}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$\\color{SandyBrown}\\underset{\\times 5}{\\longrightarrow}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" "To determine if the relationship is linear or exponential, we need to determine how $y$ changes as $x$ increases at each step:\n\n- For a linear relationship, there should be a common *difference* between successive values of $y$.\n- For an exponential relationship, there should be a common *ratio* between successive values of $y$.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false, while statement II is true. Let's check for a common ratio by dividing successive values of $y$. We see that\n$1 \\operatorname{div}  \\left(\\dfrac15\\right) = 5$,\nand similarly,\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{5}{1} = \\frac{25}{5} = 5.\n$$\nSince the *ratio* of any two successive terms is constant, the relationship is *exponential*. In this case, the common ratio is $5$.\n- Statement III is false. When $x$ increases by $1$, the respective value of $y$ increases by a factor of $5$, not $10$.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$\\color{SteelBlue}\\overset{+1}{\\longrightarrow}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$\\color{SteelBlue}\\overset{+1}{\\longrightarrow}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$\\color{SteelBlue}\\overset{+1}{\\longrightarrow}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac15$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$25$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$\\color{SandyBrown}\\underset{\\times 5}{\\longrightarrow}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$\\color{SandyBrown}\\underset{\\times 5}{\\longrightarrow}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$\\color{SandyBrown}\\underset{\\times 5}{\\longrightarrow}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""] [:db/cas 17592186119533 :question/worked-solution "Remember that in general, the principal normal vector is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf N(t)= \\dfrac{\\mathbf T'(t)}{\\|\\mathbf T'(t)\\|}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the unit tangent vector $\\mathbf T(t).$ To do this, we calculate $\\mathbf r'(t)$ and its magnitude as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{3}t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\sqrt{3},\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{(\\sqrt{3})^{2}+(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3+{sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the unit tangent vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(t) & =\\frac{{r}^{′}(t)}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}⟨\\sqrt{3},\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\,-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{2},\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the principal normal $\\mathbf{N}(t).$ To do this, we calculate $\\mathbf T'(t)$ and its magnitude as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{2}\\right),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{2}\\right)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}⟨0,\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩ \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{2}+{(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{{cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize $\\mathbf T'(t)$ and obtain the principal normal to the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(t) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(t)}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}=\\frac{\\frac{1}{2}⟨0,\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩}{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}=⟨0,\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo check our result, we can verify that $\\mathbf T$ and $\\mathbf N$ are perpendicular by computing their dot product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(t)\\cdot N(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\,-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{2},\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\cdot 0+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{2}-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{2} \\\\  & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Remember that in general, the principal normal vector is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf N(t)= \\dfrac{\\mathbf T'(t)}{\\|\\mathbf T'(t)\\|}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the unit tangent vector $\\mathbf T(t).$ To do this, we calculate $\\mathbf r'(t)$ and its magnitude as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{3}t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\sqrt{3},\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{(\\sqrt{3})^{2}+(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3+{\\sin }^{2}⁡t+{\\cos }^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the unit tangent vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(t) & =\\frac{{r}^{′}(t)}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}⟨\\sqrt{3},\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\,-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{2},\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the principal normal $\\mathbf{N}(t).$ To do this, we calculate $\\mathbf T'(t)$ and its magnitude as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{2}\\right),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{2}\\right)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}⟨0,\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩ \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{2}+{(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{{\\cos }^{2}⁡t+{\\sin }^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize $\\mathbf T'(t)$ and obtain the principal normal to the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(t) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(t)}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}=\\frac{\\frac{1}{2}⟨0,\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩}{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}=⟨0,\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo check our result, we can verify that $\\mathbf T$ and $\\mathbf N$ are perpendicular by computing their dot product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(t)\\cdot N(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\,-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{2},\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\cdot 0+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{2}-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{2} \\\\  & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186119544 :question/worked-solution "Orthogonal trajectories are families of curves that intersect another given family of curves at right angles. In other words, the tangent lines of curves from each family are perpendicular at every intersection point.\n\nLet's determine the slope of the family of curves given by $y^2 = 9C^2\\tan(x).$ First, we implicitly differentiate with respect to $x$ and solve for the derivative of $y{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{dx}({y}^{2}) & =\\frac{d}{dx}\\,(9{C}^{2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x)) \\\\ 2y{y}^{′} & =9{C}^{2}{sec}^{2}⁡(x) \\\\ {y}^{′} & =\\frac{9{C}^{2}{sec}^{2}⁡(x)}{2y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we must eliminate $C.$ From the original family of curves equation, we have\n\n$$\ny^2 = 9C^2\\tan(x)\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\nC^2 = \\dfrac{y^2}{9\\tan(x)}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our slope equation, we get\n\n$$\ny' = \\dfrac{9\\left(\\dfrac{y^2}{9\\tan(x)}\\right)\\sec^2(x)}{2y}\n= \\dfrac{y\\sec^2(x)}{2\\tan(x)}.\n$$\n\nFor a curve to intersect this family orthogonally, its slope $y'_\\perp$ must be the negative reciprocal of the slope $y'$ above. Hence,\n\n$$\ny'_\\perp = -\\dfrac{1}{y'} = -\\dfrac{1}{\\left(\\dfrac{y\\sec^2(x)}{2\\tan(x)}\\right)}\n= -\\dfrac{2\\tan(x)}{y\\sec^2(x)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the orthogonal trajectories must satisfy\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}= -\\dfrac{2\\tan(x)}{y\\sec^2(x)}.\n$$\n\n**Note**: Since we divide by $y$ when forming the orthogonal slope, the orthogonal-trajectory ODE is only valid where $y\\neq 0$ (and where $\\tan(x)$ is defined). In particular, the original family forces $y=0$ whenever $\\tan(x)=0$." "Orthogonal trajectories are families of curves that intersect another given family of curves at right angles. In other words, the tangent lines of curves from each family are perpendicular at every intersection point.\n\nLet's determine the slope of the family of curves given by $y^2 = 9C^2\\tan(x).$ First, we implicitly differentiate with respect to $x$ and solve for the derivative of $y{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{dx}({y}^{2}) & =\\frac{d}{dx}\\,(9{C}^{2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x)) \\\\ 2y{y}^{′} & =9{C}^{2}{\\sec }^{2}⁡(x) \\\\ {y}^{′} & =\\frac{9{C}^{2}{\\sec }^{2}⁡(x)}{2y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we must eliminate $C.$ From the original family of curves equation, we have\n\n$$\ny^2 = 9C^2\\tan(x)\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\nC^2 = \\dfrac{y^2}{9\\tan(x)}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our slope equation, we get\n\n$$\ny' = \\dfrac{9\\left(\\dfrac{y^2}{9\\tan(x)}\\right)\\sec^2(x)}{2y}\n= \\dfrac{y\\sec^2(x)}{2\\tan(x)}.\n$$\n\nFor a curve to intersect this family orthogonally, its slope $y'_\\perp$ must be the negative reciprocal of the slope $y'$ above. Hence,\n\n$$\ny'_\\perp = -\\dfrac{1}{y'} = -\\dfrac{1}{\\left(\\dfrac{y\\sec^2(x)}{2\\tan(x)}\\right)}\n= -\\dfrac{2\\tan(x)}{y\\sec^2(x)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the orthogonal trajectories must satisfy\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}= -\\dfrac{2\\tan(x)}{y\\sec^2(x)}.\n$$\n\n**Note**: Since we divide by $y$ when forming the orthogonal slope, the orthogonal-trajectory ODE is only valid where $y\\neq 0$ (and where $\\tan(x)$ is defined). In particular, the original family forces $y=0$ whenever $\\tan(x)=0$."] [:db/cas 17592186119554 :question/worked-solution "For a curve $\\mathbf r(t)= x(t) \\,\\mathbf i + y(t) \\,\\mathbf j,$ the unit tangent vector $\\mathbf T(t)$ at the point $P(x(t),y(t))$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\mathbf T(t)=\\dfrac{\\mathbf r'(t)}{\\left\\| \\mathbf r'(t) \\right\\|}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the derivative of $\\mathbf r(t)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({sin}^{3}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({cos}^{3}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3{sin}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,3{cos}^{2}⁡t(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the magnitude of $\\mathbf r'(t)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =|3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t|\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡t+(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}} \\\\ \\text{ } & =|3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t|\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =|3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t|\\sqrt{1} \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the last step, we were able to remove the absolute value bars because $\\sin{t}>0$ and $\\cos{t}>0$ for $0 < t < \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}.$\n\nFinally, we normalize $\\mathbf r'(t)$ and obtain the unit tangent vector to the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(t) & =\\frac{{r}^{′}(t)}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩}{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t} \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" "For a curve $\\mathbf r(t)= x(t) \\,\\mathbf i + y(t) \\,\\mathbf j,$ the unit tangent vector $\\mathbf T(t)$ at the point $P(x(t),y(t))$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\mathbf T(t)=\\dfrac{\\mathbf r'(t)}{\\left\\| \\mathbf r'(t) \\right\\|}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the derivative of $\\mathbf r(t)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({\\sin }^{3}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({\\cos }^{3}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3{\\sin }^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,3{\\cos }^{2}⁡t(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the magnitude of $\\mathbf r'(t)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =|3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t|\\sqrt{{\\sin }^{2}⁡t+(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}} \\\\ \\text{ } & =|3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t|\\sqrt{{\\sin }^{2}⁡t+{\\cos }^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =|3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t|\\sqrt{1} \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the last step, we were able to remove the absolute value bars because $\\sin{t}>0$ and $\\cos{t}>0$ for $0 < t < \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}.$\n\nFinally, we normalize $\\mathbf r'(t)$ and obtain the unit tangent vector to the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(t) & =\\frac{{r}^{′}(t)}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩}{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t} \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186119559 :question/worked-solution "First, we find $\\mathbf f'(t)$ and $\\|\\mathbf f'(t)\\|\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,4\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t⟩ \\\\ ‖{f}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}+(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9{cos}^{2}⁡t+16{cos}^{2}⁡2t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length of the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}‖{f}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }\\sqrt{9{cos}^{2}⁡t+16{cos}^{2}⁡2t}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we find $\\mathbf f'(t)$ and $\\|\\mathbf f'(t)\\|\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,4\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t⟩ \\\\ ‖{f}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}+(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9{\\cos }^{2}⁡t+16{\\cos }^{2}⁡2t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length of the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}‖{f}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }\\sqrt{9{\\cos }^{2}⁡t+16{\\cos }^{2}⁡2t}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186119560 :question/worked-solution "First, we find $\\mathbf g'(t)$ and $\\|\\mathbf g'(t)\\|\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t,\\,4\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t⟩ \\\\ ‖{g}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{(-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t)^{2}+(4\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16{cos}^{2}⁡2t+16{sin}^{2}⁡2t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16} \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length of the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}‖{g}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi }4\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4t\\,{|}_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =4(\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{8\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we find $\\mathbf g'(t)$ and $\\|\\mathbf g'(t)\\|\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t,\\,4\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t⟩ \\\\ ‖{g}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{(-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t)^{2}+(4\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16{\\cos }^{2}⁡2t+16{\\sin }^{2}⁡2t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16} \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length of the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}‖{g}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi }4\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4t\\,{|}_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =4(\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{8\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186119562 :question/worked-solution "First, we find $\\mathbf{r}'(t)$ and its magnitude $\\|\\mathbf{r}'(t)\\|\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =2\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,i+2\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,j \\\\  & =2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,i+2(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,j \\\\  & =-2t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+2t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =|2t|\\sqrt{(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+{cos}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =2t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that in the last step, we removed the absolute value bars since $t>0.$ \n\nNext, we calculate the arc length of the curve from $0$ to $t\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{t}‖{r}^{′}(u)‖\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{t}2u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={u}^{2}{|}_{0}^{t} \\\\  & ={t}^{2}-0 \\\\  & ={t}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $s=t^2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & =s \\\\ t & =\\pm \\sqrt{s} \\\\ t & =\\sqrt{s},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ntaking the positive root because $t>0.$  \n\nFinally, the vector function that parametrizes $\\mathbf r(t)$ by its arc length is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(s) & =r(\\sqrt{s}) \\\\  & =2(\\sqrt{s}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\sqrt{s}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\sqrt{s})\\,i+2(\\sqrt{s}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\sqrt{s}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\sqrt{s})\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we find $\\mathbf{r}'(t)$ and its magnitude $\\|\\mathbf{r}'(t)\\|\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =2\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,i+2\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,j \\\\  & =2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,i+2(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,j \\\\  & =-2t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+2t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =|2t|\\sqrt{(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+{\\cos }^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =2t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that in the last step, we removed the absolute value bars since $t>0.$ \n\nNext, we calculate the arc length of the curve from $0$ to $t\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{t}‖{r}^{′}(u)‖\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{t}2u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={u}^{2}{|}_{0}^{t} \\\\  & ={t}^{2}-0 \\\\  & ={t}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $s=t^2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & =s \\\\ t & =\\pm \\sqrt{s} \\\\ t & =\\sqrt{s},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ntaking the positive root because $t>0.$  \n\nFinally, the vector function that parametrizes $\\mathbf r(t)$ by its arc length is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(s) & =r(\\sqrt{s}) \\\\  & =2(\\sqrt{s}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\sqrt{s}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\sqrt{s})\\,i+2(\\sqrt{s}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\sqrt{s}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\sqrt{s})\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186119565 :question/worked-solution "Let $\\mathbf T(t)$ denote the unit tangent to the given curve as a function of the parameter $t.$ Since we are given the curve $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ parametrized by the parameter $t$ (not the arc length $s$), we will use the following formula for the curvature:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf \\kappa(t) = \\dfrac{\\left\\| \\mathbf T'(t) \\right\\|}{\\| \\mathbf r'(t) \\|}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate $\\mathbf r'(t)$ and find its magnitude:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}+(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4({cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t)} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the corresponding unit tangent vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(t) & =\\frac{{r}^{′}(t)}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}⟨2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate $\\mathbf T'(t)$ and find its magnitude:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩ \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the curvature is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(t) & =\\frac{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}=\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let $\\mathbf T(t)$ denote the unit tangent to the given curve as a function of the parameter $t.$ Since we are given the curve $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ parametrized by the parameter $t$ (not the arc length $s$), we will use the following formula for the curvature:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf \\kappa(t) = \\dfrac{\\left\\| \\mathbf T'(t) \\right\\|}{\\| \\mathbf r'(t) \\|}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate $\\mathbf r'(t)$ and find its magnitude:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}+(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4({\\cos }^{2}⁡t+{\\sin }^{2}⁡t)} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the corresponding unit tangent vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(t) & =\\frac{{r}^{′}(t)}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}⟨2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate $\\mathbf T'(t)$ and find its magnitude:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩ \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the curvature is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(t) & =\\frac{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}=\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186119566 :question/worked-solution "Let $\\mathbf T(s)$ denote the unit tangent to the given curve as a function of the arc length $s.$ Since we are given the curve $\\mathbf{r}(s)$ parametrized by the arc length $s,$ we will use the following formula for the curvature:\n\n$$\n\\kappa(s) = \\| \\mathbf T'(s) \\| = \\| \\mathbf{r}''(s) \\|\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate $\\mathbf r'(s)$ and $\\mathbf r''(s)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(s) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡s),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡s),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡s)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡s,\\,\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡s,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡s⟩ \\\\ {r}^{″}(s) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡s),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡s),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡s)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡s,\\,\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡s,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡s⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the curvature is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(s) & =‖{r}^{″}(s)‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡s)}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡s)}^{2}+{(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡s)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{2}{cos}^{2}⁡s+\\frac{1}{2}{cos}^{2}⁡s+{sin}^{2}⁡s} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{cos}^{2}⁡s+{sin}^{2}⁡s} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let $\\mathbf T(s)$ denote the unit tangent to the given curve as a function of the arc length $s.$ Since we are given the curve $\\mathbf{r}(s)$ parametrized by the arc length $s,$ we will use the following formula for the curvature:\n\n$$\n\\kappa(s) = \\| \\mathbf T'(s) \\| = \\| \\mathbf{r}''(s) \\|\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate $\\mathbf r'(s)$ and $\\mathbf r''(s)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(s) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡s),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡s),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡s)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡s,\\,\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡s,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡s⟩ \\\\ {r}^{″}(s) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡s),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡s),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡s)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡s,\\,\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡s,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡s⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the curvature is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(s) & =‖{r}^{″}(s)‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡s)}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡s)}^{2}+{(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡s)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{2}{\\cos }^{2}⁡s+\\frac{1}{2}{\\cos }^{2}⁡s+{\\sin }^{2}⁡s} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{\\cos }^{2}⁡s+{\\sin }^{2}⁡s} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186119574 :question/worked-solution "Since the curve $\\mathbf{r}(s)$ and its unit tangent vector $\\mathbf T(s)$ are both parametrized by arc length $s,$ we will use the following formula for the radius of curvature:\n\n$$\nR(s) = \\dfrac{1}{\\kappa(s)} = \\dfrac{1}{\\|\\mathbf T'(s)\\|}\n$$\n\nWe differentiate $\\mathbf T(s)$ with respect to $s$ and calculate its magnitude:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(s) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{3}\\right))i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{3}\\right))\\,j \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{3}\\right)\\,i-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{3}\\right)\\,j \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(s)‖ & =\\sqrt{(-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{3}\\right))^{2}+(-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{3}\\right))^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{9}({cos}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{3}\\right)+{sin}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{3}\\right))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the radius of curvature is\n\n$$\nR(s) = \\dfrac{1}{\\|\\mathbf T'(s)\\|} = \\dfrac{1}{\\:\\dfrac{1}{3}\\:} = 3\\,.\n$$" "Since the curve $\\mathbf{r}(s)$ and its unit tangent vector $\\mathbf T(s)$ are both parametrized by arc length $s,$ we will use the following formula for the radius of curvature:\n\n$$\nR(s) = \\dfrac{1}{\\kappa(s)} = \\dfrac{1}{\\|\\mathbf T'(s)\\|}\n$$\n\nWe differentiate $\\mathbf T(s)$ with respect to $s$ and calculate its magnitude:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(s) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{3}\\right))i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{3}\\right))\\,j \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{3}\\right)\\,i-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{3}\\right)\\,j \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(s)‖ & =\\sqrt{(-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{3}\\right))^{2}+(-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{3}\\right))^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{9}({\\cos }^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{3}\\right)+{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{3}\\right))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the radius of curvature is\n\n$$\nR(s) = \\dfrac{1}{\\|\\mathbf T'(s)\\|} = \\dfrac{1}{\\:\\dfrac{1}{3}\\:} = 3\\,.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186119577 :question/worked-solution "The curvature $\\kappa(t)$ of a plane curve $\\mathbf r(t) = x(t) \\, \\mathbf i + y(t)\\,\\mathbf j$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\kappa (t) = \\dfrac {|x'(t) y''(t) - y'(t) x''(t)|} {\\big([x'(t)]^2 + [y'(t)]^2\\big)^{3/2}}.\n$$\n\nThe radius of curvature $R$ is defined as\n$R(t) = \\dfrac 1 {\\kappa(t)}.$\n\nAlong our curve, we have\n\n$$\nx(t) = t, \\qquad y(t) = \\sin t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the derivatives of the components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) & =1,\\, & {y}^{′}(t) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ {x}^{″}(t) & =0,\\, & {y}^{″}(t) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these into the formula for the curvature, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(t) & =\\frac{|1\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot 0|}{{({1}^{2}+(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2})}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|\\operatorname{sin}⁡t|}{{(1+{cos}^{2}⁡t)}^{3/2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature at $t = \\dfrac{\\pi}2$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) & =\\frac{|\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)|}{{(1+{cos}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right))}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{(1+{0}^{2})}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the radius of curvature at $t = \\dfrac{\\pi}2$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) & =\\frac{1}{κ\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The curvature $\\kappa(t)$ of a plane curve $\\mathbf r(t) = x(t) \\, \\mathbf i + y(t)\\,\\mathbf j$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\kappa (t) = \\dfrac {|x'(t) y''(t) - y'(t) x''(t)|} {\\big([x'(t)]^2 + [y'(t)]^2\\big)^{3/2}}.\n$$\n\nThe radius of curvature $R$ is defined as\n$R(t) = \\dfrac 1 {\\kappa(t)}.$\n\nAlong our curve, we have\n\n$$\nx(t) = t, \\qquad y(t) = \\sin t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the derivatives of the components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) & =1,\\, & {y}^{′}(t) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ {x}^{″}(t) & =0,\\, & {y}^{″}(t) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these into the formula for the curvature, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(t) & =\\frac{|1\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot 0|}{{({1}^{2}+(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2})}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|\\operatorname{sin}⁡t|}{{(1+{\\cos }^{2}⁡t)}^{3/2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature at $t = \\dfrac{\\pi}2$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) & =\\frac{|\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)|}{{(1+{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right))}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{(1+{0}^{2})}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the radius of curvature at $t = \\dfrac{\\pi}2$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) & =\\frac{1}{κ\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186119579 :question/worked-solution "The signed curvature $k(t)$ of a plane curve $\\mathbf r(t) =  \\left \\langle x(t), y(t) \\right \\rangle$ is given by\n\n$$\nk (t) = \\dfrac {x'(t) y''(t) - y'(t) x''(t)} {\\big([x'(t)]^2 + [y'(t)]^2\\big)^{3/2}}.\n$$\n\nAlong our curve, we have\n\n$$\nx(t) = t, \\qquad y(t) = \\sin t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the derivatives of the components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) & =1,\\, & {y}^{′}(t) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ {x}^{″}(t) & =0,\\, & {y}^{″}(t) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these into the formula for the signed curvature, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k(t) & =\\frac{1\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)-0\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{{({1}^{2}+(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2})}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{{(1+{cos}^{2}⁡t)}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{\\sqrt{(1+{cos}^{2}⁡t)^{3}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The signed curvature $k(t)$ of a plane curve $\\mathbf r(t) =  \\left \\langle x(t), y(t) \\right \\rangle$ is given by\n\n$$\nk (t) = \\dfrac {x'(t) y''(t) - y'(t) x''(t)} {\\big([x'(t)]^2 + [y'(t)]^2\\big)^{3/2}}.\n$$\n\nAlong our curve, we have\n\n$$\nx(t) = t, \\qquad y(t) = \\sin t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the derivatives of the components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) & =1,\\, & {y}^{′}(t) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ {x}^{″}(t) & =0,\\, & {y}^{″}(t) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these into the formula for the signed curvature, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k(t) & =\\frac{1\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)-0\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{{({1}^{2}+(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2})}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{{(1+{\\cos }^{2}⁡t)}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{\\sqrt{(1+{\\cos }^{2}⁡t)^{3}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186119589 :question/worked-solution "Since swapping any two rows changes the sign of the determinant to the opposite sign, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(B) & =-det(A) \\\\  & =-(-16) \\\\  & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Since swapping any two rows changes the sign of the determinant to the opposite sign, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\det (B) & =-\\det (A) \\\\  & =-(-16) \\\\  & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186119590 :question/worked-solution "The row operation applied to $A$ consists of multiplying a row by the number $\\dfrac{1}{8}.$\n\nWhen we do this, the determinant is multiplied by the same number. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(B) & =\\frac{1}{8}det(A) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}\\cdot 32 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The row operation applied to $A$ consists of multiplying a row by the number $\\dfrac{1}{8}.$\n\nWhen we do this, the determinant is multiplied by the same number. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\det (B) & =\\frac{1}{8}\\det (A) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}\\cdot 32 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186119591 :question/worked-solution "First, let's figure out what each operation does to the determinant.\n\n- The operation $R_4 \\leftrightarrow R_5$ involves swapping two rows, so it changes the sign of the determinant to the opposite one.\n\n- The operation $R_6:= 4 R_6$ involves multiplying a row by $4,$ so the determinant is multiplied by $4$ as well.\n\n- The operation $R_8:= R_8 - \\dfrac{1}{2} R_7$ involves adding a multiple of one row to another row,  so it does not change the determinant.\n\nTherefore, we have to change the sign of the determinant to the opposite one and multiply by $4$ as well. Consequently, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(B) & =4\\cdot (-det(A)) \\\\  & =-4det(A) \\\\  & =-4\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =-20.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, let's figure out what each operation does to the determinant.\n\n- The operation $R_4 \\leftrightarrow R_5$ involves swapping two rows, so it changes the sign of the determinant to the opposite one.\n\n- The operation $R_6:= 4 R_6$ involves multiplying a row by $4,$ so the determinant is multiplied by $4$ as well.\n\n- The operation $R_8:= R_8 - \\dfrac{1}{2} R_7$ involves adding a multiple of one row to another row,  so it does not change the determinant.\n\nTherefore, we have to change the sign of the determinant to the opposite one and multiply by $4$ as well. Consequently, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\det (B) & =4\\cdot (-\\det (A)) \\\\  & =-4\\det (A) \\\\  & =-4\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =-20.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186119597 :question/worked-solution "We use the cofactor expansion along the first row:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & ={a}_{11}{C}_{11}+{a}_{12}{C}_{12}+{a}_{13}{C}_{13}+{a}_{14}{C}_{14} \\\\  & =0\\cdot {C}_{11}+0\\cdot {C}_{12}+2\\cdot {C}_{13}+0\\cdot {C}_{14} \\\\  & =2{C}_{13} \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}∗ & ∗ & 2 & ∗ \\\\ 5 & 1 & ∗ & 0 \\\\ 1 & 6 & ∗ & -1 \\\\ -6 & 3 & ∗ & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2(-1)^{1+3}\\begin{vmatrix}5 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 6 & -1 \\\\ -6 & 3 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\left(5\\begin{vmatrix}6 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{vmatrix}-1\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ -6 & 0\\end{vmatrix}+0\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 6 \\\\ -6 & 3\\end{vmatrix}\\right) \\\\  & =2(5(6\\cdot 0+1\\cdot 3)-(1\\cdot 0-(-1)\\cdot (-6))+0(1\\cdot 3-6\\cdot (-6))) \\\\  & =2(15+6+0) \\\\  & =2\\cdot 21 \\\\  & =42\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We use the cofactor expansion along the first row:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\det (A) & ={a}_{11}{C}_{11}+{a}_{12}{C}_{12}+{a}_{13}{C}_{13}+{a}_{14}{C}_{14} \\\\  & =0\\cdot {C}_{11}+0\\cdot {C}_{12}+2\\cdot {C}_{13}+0\\cdot {C}_{14} \\\\  & =2{C}_{13} \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}∗ & ∗ & 2 & ∗ \\\\ 5 & 1 & ∗ & 0 \\\\ 1 & 6 & ∗ & -1 \\\\ -6 & 3 & ∗ & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2(-1)^{1+3}\\begin{vmatrix}5 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 6 & -1 \\\\ -6 & 3 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\left(5\\begin{vmatrix}6 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{vmatrix}-1\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ -6 & 0\\end{vmatrix}+0\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 6 \\\\ -6 & 3\\end{vmatrix}\\right) \\\\  & =2(5(6\\cdot 0+1\\cdot 3)-(1\\cdot 0-(-1)\\cdot (-6))+0(1\\cdot 3-6\\cdot (-6))) \\\\  & =2(15+6+0) \\\\  & =2\\cdot 21 \\\\  & =42\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186119665 :question/worked-solution "Since $\\det(X)=\\det(Y)$, the row operation applied to $X$ must be to add or subtract a multiple of one row to another row. In other words, it is of the form \n\n$$\nR_j := R_j + (k)R_i, \\quad i \\neq j.\n$$\n\nBy considering the given row operations that are of the above form, we notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(X) & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{3} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =det(Y).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the required row operation is\n\n$$\nR_2:= R_2 + 3R_3.\n$$" "Since $\\det(X)=\\det(Y)$, the row operation applied to $X$ must be to add or subtract a multiple of one row to another row. In other words, it is of the form \n\n$$\nR_j := R_j + (k)R_i, \\quad i \\neq j.\n$$\n\nBy considering the given row operations that are of the above form, we notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\det (X) & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{3} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\det (Y).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the required row operation is\n\n$$\nR_2:= R_2 + 3R_3.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186119666 :question/worked-solution "Multiplying a $n\\times n$ matrix by a scalar $k$ is equivalent to multiplying each row by $k,$ and this occurs $n$ times. This leads to the result\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(k\\cdot A)={k}^{n}\\cdot det(A).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $k=2$ and $n=4.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(2A) & ={2}^{4}\\cdot det(A) \\\\  & =16\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =80.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Multiplying a $n\\times n$ matrix by a scalar $k$ is equivalent to multiplying each row by $k,$ and this occurs $n$ times. This leads to the result\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\det (k\\cdot A)={k}^{n}\\cdot \\det (A).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $k=2$ and $n=4.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\det (2A) & ={2}^{4}\\cdot \\det (A) \\\\  & =16\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =80.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186119675 :question/worked-solution "First, we find $\\mathbf r'(t)$ and $\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,1⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}+(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length of the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\text{ }\\sqrt{2}\\,t{|}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}(2\\pi -0) \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we find $\\mathbf r'(t)$ and $\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,1⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}+(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{\\cos }^{2}⁡t+{\\sin }^{2}⁡t+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length of the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\text{ }\\sqrt{2}\\,t{|}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}(2\\pi -0) \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186119703 :question/worked-solution "If $(\\rho, \\theta, \\phi)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx = \\rho\\cos\\theta\\sin\\phi, \\qquad\ny = \\rho\\sin\\theta\\sin\\phi, \\qquad \nz = \\rho\\cos\\phi\n$$\n\nNow, a point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface has the corresponding position vector given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{f} = \\big\\langle x, \\: y, \\: z \\big\\rangle = \\big\\langle \\rho\\cos\\theta\\sin\\phi, \\: \\rho\\sin\\theta\\sin\\phi, \\: \\rho\\cos\\phi \\big\\rangle.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $\\rho=\\theta \\sin{\\phi},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\theta ,ϕ) & =⟨\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡ϕ,\\,\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡ϕ,\\,\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the first component of $\\mathbf{f}$ is ${\\color{black}\\theta \\cos\\theta \\sin^2 \\phi}.$" "If $(\\rho, \\theta, \\phi)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx = \\rho\\cos\\theta\\sin\\phi, \\qquad\ny = \\rho\\sin\\theta\\sin\\phi, \\qquad \nz = \\rho\\cos\\phi\n$$\n\nNow, a point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface has the corresponding position vector given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{f} = \\big\\langle x, \\: y, \\: z \\big\\rangle = \\big\\langle \\rho\\cos\\theta\\sin\\phi, \\: \\rho\\sin\\theta\\sin\\phi, \\: \\rho\\cos\\phi \\big\\rangle.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $\\rho=\\theta \\sin{\\phi},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\theta ,ϕ) & =⟨\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {\\sin }^{2}⁡ϕ,\\,\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {\\sin }^{2}⁡ϕ,\\,\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the first component of $\\mathbf{f}$ is ${\\color{black}\\theta \\cos\\theta \\sin^2 \\phi}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186119734 :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arsinh}{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcosh}{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\artanh}{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arsech}{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\cosh}{\\mathop{\\rm cosh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\sinh}{\\mathop{\\rm sinh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits} y=\\sinh{x}\n$$?\n\n1. The function is the average of $y=e^x$ and $y=e^{-x}$.\n2. The function is decreasing over $(-\\infty,0)$ and increasing over $(0, \\infty)$.\n3. The function is odd." "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\newcommand{\\operatorname{sech} }{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{csch} }{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsinh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcosh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{artanh} }{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsech} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcsch} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcoth} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\cosh}{\\mathop{\\rm cosh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\sinh}{\\mathop{\\rm sinh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{csch} }{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits} y=\\sinh{x}\n$$?\n\n1. The function is the average of $y=e^x$ and $y=e^{-x}$.\n2. The function is decreasing over $(-\\infty,0)$ and increasing over $(0, \\infty)$.\n3. The function is odd."] [:db/cas 17592186119734 :question/worked-solution "Let's recall the graph of\n\n$$\n\\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arsinh}{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcosh}{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\artanh}{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arsech}{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\cosh}{\\mathop{\\rm cosh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\sinh}{\\mathop{\\rm sinh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits} y=\\sinh{x}\n$$\n\n![](images/be/bed96bcd7370c5e66c9cfb8137199a3dbc39ac5250f325f61e10190be3ef1e25.png)\n\nLet's now examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. The function $y=\\sinh{x}$ is the average of $y=e^x$ and $y=-e^{-x}{:}$\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\sinh{x} = \\dfrac{e^x+\\left(-e^{-x}\\right)}{2}= \\dfrac{e^x-e^{-x}}{2}\n$$\n- Statement II is false. The function $y=\\sinh{x}$ is increasing over the entire real line.\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, the function $y=\\sinh{x}$ is odd, i.e., $\\sinh(-x)=-\\sinh(x)$, and the graph has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" "Let's recall the graph of\n\n$$\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{sech} }{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{csch} }{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsinh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcosh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{artanh} }{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsech} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcsch} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcoth} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\cosh}{\\mathop{\\rm cosh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\sinh}{\\mathop{\\rm sinh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{csch} }{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits} y=\\sinh{x}\n$$\n\n![](images/be/bed96bcd7370c5e66c9cfb8137199a3dbc39ac5250f325f61e10190be3ef1e25.png)\n\nLet's now examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. The function $y=\\sinh{x}$ is the average of $y=e^x$ and $y=-e^{-x}{:}$\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\sinh{x} = \\dfrac{e^x+\\left(-e^{-x}\\right)}{2}= \\dfrac{e^x-e^{-x}}{2}\n$$\n- Statement II is false. The function $y=\\sinh{x}$ is increasing over the entire real line.\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, the function $y=\\sinh{x}$ is odd, i.e., $\\sinh(-x)=-\\sinh(x)$, and the graph has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""] [:db/cas 17592186119735 :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arsinh}{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcosh}{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\artanh}{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arsech}{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\cosh}{\\mathop{\\rm cosh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\sinh}{\\mathop{\\rm sinh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits} y=\\cosh{x}\n$$?\n\n1. The domain of the function is $[0, \\infty)$.\n2. The graph of the function is symmetric with respect to the $y$-axis.\n3. The minimum of the function occurs at $x=0$." "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\newcommand{\\operatorname{sech} }{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{csch} }{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsinh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcosh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{artanh} }{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsech} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcsch} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcoth} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\cosh}{\\mathop{\\rm cosh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\sinh}{\\mathop{\\rm sinh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{csch} }{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits} y=\\cosh{x}\n$$?\n\n1. The domain of the function is $[0, \\infty)$.\n2. The graph of the function is symmetric with respect to the $y$-axis.\n3. The minimum of the function occurs at $x=0$."] [:db/cas 17592186119735 :question/worked-solution "Let's recall the graph of\n\n$$\n\\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arsinh}{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcosh}{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\artanh}{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arsech}{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\cosh}{\\mathop{\\rm cosh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\sinh}{\\mathop{\\rm sinh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits} y=\\cosh{x}\n$$\n\n![](images/bd/bdcc58c5097062db8cbe5875a4af6f91db08f7e7c61a264f15f612f77d0e3350.png)\n\nLet's now examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. The domain of the function $y=\\cosh{x}$ is $(-\\infty,\\infty)$ and the range is $[1,\\infty)$.\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, the function $y=\\cosh{x}$ is even, i.e. $\\cosh(-x)=\\cosh(x)$, and the graph is symmetric about the $y$-axis.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, the minimum value of the function $y=\\cosh{x}$ is $y=1$ and occurs at $x=0$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" "Let's recall the graph of\n\n$$\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{sech} }{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{csch} }{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsinh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcosh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{artanh} }{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsech} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcsch} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcoth} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\cosh}{\\mathop{\\rm cosh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\sinh}{\\mathop{\\rm sinh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{csch} }{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits} y=\\cosh{x}\n$$\n\n![](images/bd/bdcc58c5097062db8cbe5875a4af6f91db08f7e7c61a264f15f612f77d0e3350.png)\n\nLet's now examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. The domain of the function $y=\\cosh{x}$ is $(-\\infty,\\infty)$ and the range is $[1,\\infty)$.\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, the function $y=\\cosh{x}$ is even, i.e. $\\cosh(-x)=\\cosh(x)$, and the graph is symmetric about the $y$-axis.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, the minimum value of the function $y=\\cosh{x}$ is $y=1$ and occurs at $x=0$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""] [:db/cas 17592186119736 :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arsinh}{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcosh}{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\artanh}{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arsech}{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\cosh}{\\mathop{\\rm cosh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\sinh}{\\mathop{\\rm sinh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits} y=\\tanh{x}\n$$?\n\n1. $\\tanh{x} \\to 1$ as $x \\to \\infty$.\n2. The range of the function is $(-\\infty,\\infty)$.\n3. The function has no zeros." "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\newcommand{\\operatorname{sech} }{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{csch} }{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsinh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcosh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{artanh} }{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsech} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcsch} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcoth} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\cosh}{\\mathop{\\rm cosh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\sinh}{\\mathop{\\rm sinh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{csch} }{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits} y=\\tanh{x}\n$$?\n\n1. $\\tanh{x} \\to 1$ as $x \\to \\infty$.\n2. The range of the function is $(-\\infty,\\infty)$.\n3. The function has no zeros."] [:db/cas 17592186119736 :question/worked-solution "Let's recall the graph of\n\n$$\n\\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arsinh}{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcosh}{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\artanh}{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arsech}{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\cosh}{\\mathop{\\rm cosh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\sinh}{\\mathop{\\rm sinh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits} y=\\tanh{x}\n$$\n\n![](images/fa/fa55375a8d37d72a82600c3d0ad4466578b67c919eef756d1abddc6ab9535e7f.png)\n\nLet's now examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, we have that $\\tanh{x} \\to 1$ as $x \\to \\infty$.\n- Statement II is false. The range of $\\tanh{x}$ is $(-1,1)$.\n- Statement III is false. The function $y=\\tanh{x}$ has a zero at $x=0$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" "Let's recall the graph of\n\n$$\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{sech} }{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{csch} }{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsinh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcosh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{artanh} }{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsech} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcsch} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcoth} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\cosh}{\\mathop{\\rm cosh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\sinh}{\\mathop{\\rm sinh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{csch} }{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits} y=\\tanh{x}\n$$\n\n![](images/fa/fa55375a8d37d72a82600c3d0ad4466578b67c919eef756d1abddc6ab9535e7f.png)\n\nLet's now examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, we have that $\\tanh{x} \\to 1$ as $x \\to \\infty$.\n- Statement II is false. The range of $\\tanh{x}$ is $(-1,1)$.\n- Statement III is false. The function $y=\\tanh{x}$ has a zero at $x=0$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""] [:db/cas 17592186120067 :question/worked-solution "If $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nNow, a point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface has the corresponding position vector given by\n\n$$\nf=⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩=⟨\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $\\rho =ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\theta ,ϕ) & =⟨ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨ϕ{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the first component of $f$ is $ϕ{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ.$" "If $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nNow, a point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface has the corresponding position vector given by\n\n$$\nf=⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩=⟨\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $\\rho =ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\theta ,ϕ) & =⟨ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨ϕ{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the first component of $f$ is $ϕ{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ.$"] [:db/cas 17592186120155 :question/worked-solution "First, let's construct the matrix $M,$ whose columns are the coordinates of our vectors $\\mathbf{a},$ $\\mathbf{b},$ $\\mathbf{c},$ and  $\\mathbf{d}:$\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -3 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe volume of the parallelepiped spanned by these vectors equals the absolute value of the determinant of $M:$\n\n$$\nV=|det(M)|\n$$\n\nThe determinant of a triangular matrix is equal to the product of the entries on the main diagonal. So, we have\n\n$$\ndet(M)=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -3 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}=5\\cdot 2\\cdot 3\\cdot (-1)=-30.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume is\n\n$$\nV=|det(M)|=|-30|=30.\n$$" "First, let's construct the matrix $M,$ whose columns are the coordinates of our vectors $\\mathbf{a},$ $\\mathbf{b},$ $\\mathbf{c},$ and  $\\mathbf{d}:$\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -3 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe volume of the parallelepiped spanned by these vectors equals the absolute value of the determinant of $M:$\n\n$$\nV=|\\det (M)|\n$$\n\nThe determinant of a triangular matrix is equal to the product of the entries on the main diagonal. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\det (M)=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -3 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}=5\\cdot 2\\cdot 3\\cdot (-1)=-30.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume is\n\n$$\nV=|\\det (M)|=|-30|=30.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186120156 :question/worked-solution "The curvature $κ(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t,\\,2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t,\\,2⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)}^{2}+{(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t)}^{2}+{(2)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4{sin}^{2}⁡2t+4{cos}^{2}⁡2t+4} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4({sin}^{2}⁡2t+{cos}^{2}⁡2t)+4} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+4} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we are told that\n\n$$\n{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)=8(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\,i-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,j+\\,k),\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖ & =8\\sqrt{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)^{2}+(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t)^{2}+(1)^{2}} \\\\  & =8\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡2t+{cos}^{2}⁡2t+1} \\\\  & =8\\sqrt{1+1} \\\\  & =8\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(t) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{8\\sqrt{2}}{{(2\\sqrt{2})}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{8\\sqrt{2}}{16\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The curvature $κ(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t,\\,2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t,\\,2⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)}^{2}+{(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t)}^{2}+{(2)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4{\\sin }^{2}⁡2t+4{\\cos }^{2}⁡2t+4} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4({\\sin }^{2}⁡2t+{\\cos }^{2}⁡2t)+4} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+4} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we are told that\n\n$$\n{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)=8(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\,i-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,j+\\,k),\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖ & =8\\sqrt{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)^{2}+(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t)^{2}+(1)^{2}} \\\\  & =8\\sqrt{{\\sin }^{2}⁡2t+{\\cos }^{2}⁡2t+1} \\\\  & =8\\sqrt{1+1} \\\\  & =8\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(t) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{8\\sqrt{2}}{{(2\\sqrt{2})}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{8\\sqrt{2}}{16\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186120164 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the unit tangent vector $T(t).$ To do this, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$ and its magnitude as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,4⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{(-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}+(4)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)+16} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25} \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the unit tangent vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(t) & =\\frac{{r}^{′}(t)}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}⟨-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,4⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\frac{4}{5}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the principal normal $N(t).$ To do this, we calculate ${T}^{′}(t)$ and its magnitude as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{4}{5}\\right)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{5}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,0⟩ \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(-\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{2}+{(-\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{9}{25}({cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize ${T}^{′}(t)$ and obtain the principal normal to the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(t) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(t)}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}=\\frac{-\\frac{3}{5}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,0⟩}{\\left(\\frac{3}{5}\\right)}=⟨-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we find the unit tangent vector $T(t).$ To do this, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$ and its magnitude as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,4⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{(-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}+(4)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9({\\sin }^{2}⁡t+{\\cos }^{2}⁡t)+16} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25} \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the unit tangent vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(t) & =\\frac{{r}^{′}(t)}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}⟨-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,4⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\frac{4}{5}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the principal normal $N(t).$ To do this, we calculate ${T}^{′}(t)$ and its magnitude as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{4}{5}\\right)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{5}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,0⟩ \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(-\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{2}+{(-\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{9}{25}({\\cos }^{2}⁡t+{\\sin }^{2}⁡t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize ${T}^{′}(t)$ and obtain the principal normal to the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(t) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(t)}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}=\\frac{-\\frac{3}{5}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,0⟩}{\\left(\\frac{3}{5}\\right)}=⟨-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186120207 :question/worked-solution "We use the cofactor expansion along the first row:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & ={a}_{11}{C}_{11}+{a}_{12}{C}_{12}+{a}_{13}{C}_{13}+{a}_{14}{C}_{14} \\\\  & =0\\cdot {C}_{11}+8\\cdot {C}_{12}+0\\cdot {C}_{13}+0\\cdot {C}_{14} \\\\  & =8{C}_{12} \\\\  & =8\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}{\\color{lightgray}∗} & {\\color{lightgray}8} & {\\color{lightgray}∗} & {\\color{lightgray}∗} \\\\ -1 & {\\color{lightgray}∗} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & {\\color{lightgray}∗} & 6 & 2 \\\\ 1 & {\\color{lightgray}∗} & -1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =8(-1)^{1+2}\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 6 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-8\\left((-1)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}6 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}-0\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}+0\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}3 & 6 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix}\\right) \\\\  & =8(6\\cdot 2-(-1)\\cdot 2)-0+0 \\\\  & =8\\cdot 14 \\\\  & =112\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We use the cofactor expansion along the first row:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\det (A) & ={a}_{11}{C}_{11}+{a}_{12}{C}_{12}+{a}_{13}{C}_{13}+{a}_{14}{C}_{14} \\\\  & =0\\cdot {C}_{11}+8\\cdot {C}_{12}+0\\cdot {C}_{13}+0\\cdot {C}_{14} \\\\  & =8{C}_{12} \\\\  & =8\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}{\\color{lightgray}∗} & {\\color{lightgray}8} & {\\color{lightgray}∗} & {\\color{lightgray}∗} \\\\ -1 & {\\color{lightgray}∗} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & {\\color{lightgray}∗} & 6 & 2 \\\\ 1 & {\\color{lightgray}∗} & -1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =8(-1)^{1+2}\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 6 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-8\\left((-1)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}6 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}-0\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}+0\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}3 & 6 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix}\\right) \\\\  & =8(6\\cdot 2-(-1)\\cdot 2)-0+0 \\\\  & =8\\cdot 14 \\\\  & =112\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186120287 :question/worked-solution "First, we find ${r}^{′}(t)$ and its magnitude $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3,\\,4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t),\\,-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t))^{2}+(-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9+16{cos}^{2}⁡(2t)+16{sin}^{2}⁡(2t)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9+16({cos}^{2}⁡(2t)+{sin}^{2}⁡(2t))} \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the arc length of the curve from $0$ to $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{t}‖{r}^{′}(u)‖\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{t}5\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =5u\\,{|}_{0}^{t} \\\\  & =5t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $s=5t$, we have that $t=\\frac{s}{5}.$\n\nTherefore, the vector function that parametrizes $r(t)$ by its arc length is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(s) & =r\\left(\\frac{s}{5}\\right) \\\\  & =⟨3\\cdot \\frac{s}{5},\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{s}{5}),\\,2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{s}{5})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{3s}{5},\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{2s}{5}\\right),\\,2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{2s}{5}\\right)⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we find ${r}^{′}(t)$ and its magnitude $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3,\\,4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t),\\,-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t))^{2}+(-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9+16{\\cos }^{2}⁡(2t)+16{\\sin }^{2}⁡(2t)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9+16({\\cos }^{2}⁡(2t)+{\\sin }^{2}⁡(2t))} \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the arc length of the curve from $0$ to $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{t}‖{r}^{′}(u)‖\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{t}5\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =5u\\,{|}_{0}^{t} \\\\  & =5t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $s=5t$, we have that $t=\\frac{s}{5}.$\n\nTherefore, the vector function that parametrizes $r(t)$ by its arc length is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(s) & =r\\left(\\frac{s}{5}\\right) \\\\  & =⟨3\\cdot \\frac{s}{5},\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{s}{5}),\\,2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{s}{5})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{3s}{5},\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{2s}{5}\\right),\\,2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{2s}{5}\\right)⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186120322 :question/worked-solution "Since the curve $r(s)$ and its unit tangent vector $T(s)$ are both parametrized by arc length $s$, we will use the following formula for the radius of curvature:\n\n$$\nR(s)=\\frac{1}{κ(s)}=\\frac{1}{‖{T}^{′}(s)‖}\n$$\n\nWe differentiate $T(s)$ with respect to $s$ and calculate its magnitude:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(s) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(-\\frac{4}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{2}\\right))i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{2}\\right))\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{2}\\right))k \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{5}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{2}\\right)\\,i+\\frac{3}{5}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{2}\\right)\\,j-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{2}\\right)\\,k \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{10}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{2}\\right)\\,i+\\frac{3}{10}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{2}\\right)\\,j-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{2}\\right)\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}[-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{2}\\right)\\,i+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{2}\\right)\\,j-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{2}\\right)\\,k] \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(s)‖ & =\\frac{1}{10}\\sqrt{(-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{2}\\right))^{2}+(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{2}\\right))^{2}+(-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{2}\\right))^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}\\sqrt{16{cos}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{2}\\right)+9{cos}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{2}\\right)+25{sin}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{2}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}\\sqrt{25({cos}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{2}\\right)+{sin}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{2}\\right))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}\\sqrt{25} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of curvature is\n\n$$\nR(s)=\\frac{1}{‖{T}^{′}(s)‖}=\\frac{1}{\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\,}=2.\n$$" "Since the curve $r(s)$ and its unit tangent vector $T(s)$ are both parametrized by arc length $s$, we will use the following formula for the radius of curvature:\n\n$$\nR(s)=\\frac{1}{κ(s)}=\\frac{1}{‖{T}^{′}(s)‖}\n$$\n\nWe differentiate $T(s)$ with respect to $s$ and calculate its magnitude:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(s) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(-\\frac{4}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{2}\\right))i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{2}\\right))\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{2}\\right))k \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{5}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{2}\\right)\\,i+\\frac{3}{5}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{2}\\right)\\,j-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{2}\\right)\\,k \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{10}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{2}\\right)\\,i+\\frac{3}{10}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{2}\\right)\\,j-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{2}\\right)\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}[-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{2}\\right)\\,i+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{2}\\right)\\,j-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{2}\\right)\\,k] \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(s)‖ & =\\frac{1}{10}\\sqrt{(-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{2}\\right))^{2}+(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{2}\\right))^{2}+(-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{2}\\right))^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}\\sqrt{16{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{2}\\right)+9{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{2}\\right)+25{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{2}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}\\sqrt{25({\\cos }^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{2}\\right)+{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{2}\\right))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}\\sqrt{25} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of curvature is\n\n$$\nR(s)=\\frac{1}{‖{T}^{′}(s)‖}=\\frac{1}{\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\,}=2.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186120326 :question/worked-solution "Let $T(s)$ denote the unit tangent to the given curve as a function of the arc length $s.$ Since we are given the curve $r(s)$ parametrized by the arc length $s$, we will use the following formula for the curvature:\n\n$$\nκ(s)=‖{T}^{′}(s)‖=‖{r}^{″}(s)‖\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{′}(s)$ and ${r}^{″}(s)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(s) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}\\left(\\frac{s}{\\sqrt{10}}\\right),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{\\sqrt{10}}\\right)),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{\\sqrt{10}}\\right))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}},\\,-3\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{\\sqrt{10}}\\right),\\,3\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{\\sqrt{10}}\\right)⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}⟨1,\\,-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{\\sqrt{10}}\\right),\\,3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{\\sqrt{10}}\\right)⟩ \\\\ {r}^{″}(s) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(1),\\,-3\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{s}{\\sqrt{10}}),\\,3\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\frac{s}{\\sqrt{10}})⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}⟨0,\\,-3\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{\\sqrt{10}}\\right),\\,-3\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{\\sqrt{10}}\\right)⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{10}⟨0,\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{\\sqrt{10}}\\right),\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{\\sqrt{10}}\\right)⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the curvature is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(s) & =‖{r}^{″}(s)‖ \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{10}\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{\\sqrt{10}}\\right))}^{2}+{(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{\\sqrt{10}}\\right))}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{10}\\sqrt{0+{cos}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{\\sqrt{10}}\\right)+{sin}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{\\sqrt{10}}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let $T(s)$ denote the unit tangent to the given curve as a function of the arc length $s.$ Since we are given the curve $r(s)$ parametrized by the arc length $s$, we will use the following formula for the curvature:\n\n$$\nκ(s)=‖{T}^{′}(s)‖=‖{r}^{″}(s)‖\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{′}(s)$ and ${r}^{″}(s)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(s) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}\\left(\\frac{s}{\\sqrt{10}}\\right),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{\\sqrt{10}}\\right)),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{\\sqrt{10}}\\right))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}},\\,-3\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{\\sqrt{10}}\\right),\\,3\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{\\sqrt{10}}\\right)⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}⟨1,\\,-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{\\sqrt{10}}\\right),\\,3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{\\sqrt{10}}\\right)⟩ \\\\ {r}^{″}(s) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(1),\\,-3\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{s}{\\sqrt{10}}),\\,3\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\frac{s}{\\sqrt{10}})⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}⟨0,\\,-3\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{\\sqrt{10}}\\right),\\,-3\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{\\sqrt{10}}\\right)⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{10}⟨0,\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{\\sqrt{10}}\\right),\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{\\sqrt{10}}\\right)⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the curvature is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(s) & =‖{r}^{″}(s)‖ \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{10}\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{\\sqrt{10}}\\right))}^{2}+{(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{\\sqrt{10}}\\right))}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{10}\\sqrt{0+{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{\\sqrt{10}}\\right)+{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{\\sqrt{10}}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186120381 :question/worked-solution "Since we are given only the curve $r(t)$, we will use the cross product formula to find the radius of curvature:\n\n$$\nR(t)=\\frac{1}{κ(t)}=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}}{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$, ${r}^{′}(\\pi ),$ and $‖{r}^{′}(\\pi )‖$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩ \\\\ {r}^{′}(\\pi ) & =⟨{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi ,\\,-{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-{e}^{0},\\,-{e}^{-1}\\cdot 0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,\\,0⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(\\pi )‖ & =\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+0}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{″}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=\\pi :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{″}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{cos}^{2}⁡t,\\,-{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{sin}^{2}⁡t⟩ \\\\ {r}^{″}(\\pi ) & =⟨-{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi +{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi }{cos}^{2}⁡\\pi ,\\,-{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi +{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡\\pi ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0+1,{e}^{-1}+0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\frac{1}{e}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the cross product by considering ${r}^{′}(\\pi )$ and ${r}^{″}(\\pi )$ as $3$-dimensional vectors with zero $z$-components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(\\pi )\\times {r}^{″}(\\pi ) & =⟨-1,\\,0,\\,0⟩\\times ⟨1,\\,\\frac{1}{e},\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ -1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1/e & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1/e\\end{vmatrix}\\,k \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{e}\\,k \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(\\pi )\\times {r}^{″}(\\pi )‖ & =\\frac{1}{e}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of curvature at $t=\\pi $ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(\\pi ) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(\\pi ){‖}^{3}}{‖{r}^{′}(\\pi )\\times {r}^{″}(\\pi )‖}=\\frac{1}{\\left(\\frac{1}{e}\\right)}=e.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Since we are given only the curve $r(t)$, we will use the cross product formula to find the radius of curvature:\n\n$$\nR(t)=\\frac{1}{κ(t)}=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}}{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$, ${r}^{′}(\\pi ),$ and $‖{r}^{′}(\\pi )‖$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩ \\\\ {r}^{′}(\\pi ) & =⟨{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi ,\\,-{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-{e}^{0},\\,-{e}^{-1}\\cdot 0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,\\,0⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(\\pi )‖ & =\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+0}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{″}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=\\pi :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{″}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{\\cos }^{2}⁡t,\\,-{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{\\sin }^{2}⁡t⟩ \\\\ {r}^{″}(\\pi ) & =⟨-{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi +{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi }{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\pi ,\\,-{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi +{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi }{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\pi ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0+1,{e}^{-1}+0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\frac{1}{e}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the cross product by considering ${r}^{′}(\\pi )$ and ${r}^{″}(\\pi )$ as $3$-dimensional vectors with zero $z$-components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(\\pi )\\times {r}^{″}(\\pi ) & =⟨-1,\\,0,\\,0⟩\\times ⟨1,\\,\\frac{1}{e},\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ -1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1/e & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1/e\\end{vmatrix}\\,k \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{e}\\,k \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(\\pi )\\times {r}^{″}(\\pi )‖ & =\\frac{1}{e}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of curvature at $t=\\pi $ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(\\pi ) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(\\pi ){‖}^{3}}{‖{r}^{′}(\\pi )\\times {r}^{″}(\\pi )‖}=\\frac{1}{\\left(\\frac{1}{e}\\right)}=e.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186120401 :question/worked-solution "We use the cofactor expansion along the first row:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & ={a}_{11}{C}_{11}+{a}_{12}{C}_{12}+{a}_{13}{C}_{13}+{a}_{14}{C}_{14} \\\\  & =(-3)\\cdot {C}_{11}+0\\cdot {C}_{12}+0\\cdot {C}_{13}+5\\cdot {C}_{14} \\\\  & =-3{C}_{11}+5{C}_{14} \\\\  & =(-3)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}{\\color{lightgray}-3} & {\\color{lightgray}∗} & {\\color{lightgray}∗} & {\\color{lightgray}∗} \\\\ {\\color{lightgray}∗} & 0 & 3 & -2 \\\\ {\\color{lightgray}∗} & -1 & 2 & -3 \\\\ {\\color{lightgray}∗} & 6 & 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix}+5\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}{\\color{lightgray}∗} & {\\color{lightgray}∗} & {\\color{lightgray}∗} & {\\color{lightgray}5} \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 & {\\color{lightgray}∗} \\\\ 4 & -1 & 2 & {\\color{lightgray}∗} \\\\ -1 & 6 & 0 & {\\color{lightgray}∗}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-3)(-1)^{1+1}\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 3 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 2 & -3 \\\\ 6 & 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix}+5(-1)^{1+4}\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 4 & -1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 6 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-3\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 3 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 2 & -3 \\\\ 6 & 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix}-5\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 4 & -1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 6 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-3\\left(0\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix}-3\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-1 & -3 \\\\ 6 & 0\\end{vmatrix}-2\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ 6 & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\right) \\\\  & -5\\left(0\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ 6 & 0\\end{vmatrix}-0\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}4 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}+3\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}4 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 6\\end{vmatrix}\\right) \\\\  & =-3\\cdot (-3\\cdot (0+18)+(-2)\\cdot (0-12))-5\\cdot (3\\cdot (24-1)) \\\\  & =90-345 \\\\  & =-255\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We use the cofactor expansion along the first row:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\det (A) & ={a}_{11}{C}_{11}+{a}_{12}{C}_{12}+{a}_{13}{C}_{13}+{a}_{14}{C}_{14} \\\\  & =(-3)\\cdot {C}_{11}+0\\cdot {C}_{12}+0\\cdot {C}_{13}+5\\cdot {C}_{14} \\\\  & =-3{C}_{11}+5{C}_{14} \\\\  & =(-3)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}{\\color{lightgray}-3} & {\\color{lightgray}∗} & {\\color{lightgray}∗} & {\\color{lightgray}∗} \\\\ {\\color{lightgray}∗} & 0 & 3 & -2 \\\\ {\\color{lightgray}∗} & -1 & 2 & -3 \\\\ {\\color{lightgray}∗} & 6 & 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix}+5\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}{\\color{lightgray}∗} & {\\color{lightgray}∗} & {\\color{lightgray}∗} & {\\color{lightgray}5} \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 & {\\color{lightgray}∗} \\\\ 4 & -1 & 2 & {\\color{lightgray}∗} \\\\ -1 & 6 & 0 & {\\color{lightgray}∗}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-3)(-1)^{1+1}\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 3 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 2 & -3 \\\\ 6 & 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix}+5(-1)^{1+4}\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 4 & -1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 6 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-3\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 3 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 2 & -3 \\\\ 6 & 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix}-5\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 4 & -1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 6 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-3\\left(0\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix}-3\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-1 & -3 \\\\ 6 & 0\\end{vmatrix}-2\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ 6 & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\right) \\\\  & -5\\left(0\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ 6 & 0\\end{vmatrix}-0\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}4 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}+3\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}4 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 6\\end{vmatrix}\\right) \\\\  & =-3\\cdot (-3\\cdot (0+18)+(-2)\\cdot (0-12))-5\\cdot (3\\cdot (24-1)) \\\\  & =90-345 \\\\  & =-255\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186120420 :question/problem "Given that $det(A)=det(B),$ which of the following elementary row operations transforms matrix $A$ to matrix $B,$ where\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 4 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -9 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$" "Given that $\\det (A)=\\det (B),$ which of the following elementary row operations transforms matrix $A$ to matrix $B,$ where\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 4 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -9 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186120420 :question/worked-solution "Since $det(A)=det(B)$, the row operation applied to $A$ must be to add or subtract a multiple of one row to another row. In other words, it is of the form \n\n$$\n{R}_{j}:={R}_{j}+(k){R}_{i},\\,i\\ne j.\n$$\n\nBy considering the given row operations that are of the above form, we notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 4 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -9 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =det(B).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the required row operation is\n\n$$\n{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1}.\n$$" "Since $\\det (A)=\\det (B)$, the row operation applied to $A$ must be to add or subtract a multiple of one row to another row. In other words, it is of the form \n\n$$\n{R}_{j}:={R}_{j}+(k){R}_{i},\\,i\\ne j.\n$$\n\nBy considering the given row operations that are of the above form, we notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\det (A) & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 4 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -9 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\det (B).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the required row operation is\n\n$$\n{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186120427 :question/problem "Given that $det({A}^{T})=5$, which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." "Given that $\\det ({A}^{T})=5$, which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186120427 :question/worked-solution "From the properties of determinants, we know that \n\n$$\n\\text{det}(A)=\\text{det}({A}^{T})=5\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\ndet(I)=1.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement $4\\text{det}(A)=20$ is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n4\\text{det}(A)=4\\cdot 5=20.\n$$\n\n- Statement $2\\text{det}({A}^{T})=25$ is false. We have\n\n$$\n2\\text{det}({A}^{T})=2\\cdot 5=10\\ne 25.\n$$\n\n- Statement $\\text{det}(A)-3\\text{det}(I)=8$ is false. We have\n\n$$\n\\text{det}(A)-3\\text{det}(I)=5-3\\cdot 1=2\\ne 8.\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is $4\\text{det}(A)=20$ only." "From the properties of determinants, we know that \n\n$$\n\\text{det}(A)=\\text{det}({A}^{T})=5\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\det (I)=1.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement $4\\text{det}(A)=20$ is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n4\\text{det}(A)=4\\cdot 5=20.\n$$\n\n- Statement $2\\text{det}({A}^{T})=25$ is false. We have\n\n$$\n2\\text{det}({A}^{T})=2\\cdot 5=10\\ne 25.\n$$\n\n- Statement $\\text{det}(A)-3\\text{det}(I)=8$ is false. We have\n\n$$\n\\text{det}(A)-3\\text{det}(I)=5-3\\cdot 1=2\\ne 8.\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is $4\\text{det}(A)=20$ only."] [:db/cas 17592186120428 :question/problem "Given that $A =-3I$, where $I$ is the $3 \\times 3$ identity matrix, what is the value of $det(A)$?" "Given that $A =-3I$, where $I$ is the $3 \\times 3$ identity matrix, what is the value of $\\det (A)$?"] [:db/cas 17592186120429 :question/worked-solution "Notice that the $3$rd column contains three zeros. So, using Laplace expansion along the $3$rd column, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(B) & =\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 0 & {\\color{lightgray}{0}} & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1 & {\\color{lightgray}{0}} & 0 \\\\ {\\color{lightgray}{5}} & {\\color{lightgray}{11}} & 1 & {\\color{lightgray}{19}} \\\\ 4 & 0 & {\\color{lightgray}{0}} & -3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-1)^{3+3}\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0 & -3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0 & -3\\end{vmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe determinant of a triangular matrix is equal to the product of the entries on the main diagonal. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0 & -3\\end{vmatrix} & =3\\cdot 1\\cdot (-3)=-9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\ndet(B)=-9.\n$$" "Notice that the $3$rd column contains three zeros. So, using Laplace expansion along the $3$rd column, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\det (B) & =\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 0 & {\\color{lightgray}{0}} & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1 & {\\color{lightgray}{0}} & 0 \\\\ {\\color{lightgray}{5}} & {\\color{lightgray}{11}} & 1 & {\\color{lightgray}{19}} \\\\ 4 & 0 & {\\color{lightgray}{0}} & -3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-1)^{3+3}\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0 & -3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0 & -3\\end{vmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe determinant of a triangular matrix is equal to the product of the entries on the main diagonal. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0 & -3\\end{vmatrix} & =3\\cdot 1\\cdot (-3)=-9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\det (B)=-9.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186120431 :question/problem "Suppose $A$ is a $4\\times 4$ matrix and $det(A)=12.$ The matrix $B$ is obtained from $A$ by applying the elementary row operations ${R}_{2}↔{R}_{3}$ and ${R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+2{R}_{3}.$ What is the value of $det(B)?$" "Suppose $A$ is a $4\\times 4$ matrix and $\\det (A)=12.$ The matrix $B$ is obtained from $A$ by applying the elementary row operations ${R}_{2}↔{R}_{3}$ and ${R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+2{R}_{3}.$ What is the value of $\\det (B)?$"] [:db/cas 17592186120431 :question/worked-solution "First, let's figure out what each operation does to the determinant.\n\n- The operation ${R}_{2}↔{R}_{3}$ involves swapping two rows, so it changes the sign of the determinant to the opposite one.\n\n- The operation ${R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+2{R}_{3}$ involves adding a multiple of one row to another row,  so it does not change the determinant.\n\nTherefore, we only have to change the sign of the determinant to the opposite one. Consequently, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(B) & =-det(A) \\\\  & =-(12) \\\\  & =-12.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, let's figure out what each operation does to the determinant.\n\n- The operation ${R}_{2}↔{R}_{3}$ involves swapping two rows, so it changes the sign of the determinant to the opposite one.\n\n- The operation ${R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+2{R}_{3}$ involves adding a multiple of one row to another row,  so it does not change the determinant.\n\nTherefore, we only have to change the sign of the determinant to the opposite one. Consequently, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\det (B) & =-\\det (A) \\\\  & =-(12) \\\\  & =-12.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186120432 :question/problem "Suppose $U$ is a $4\\times 4$ matrix and $det(U)=10.$ The matrix $V$ is obtained from $U$ by applying the elementary row operations ${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+6{R}_{4}$ and ${R}_{3}:=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{3}.$ What is the value of $det(V)?$" "Suppose $U$ is a $4\\times 4$ matrix and $\\det (U)=10.$ The matrix $V$ is obtained from $U$ by applying the elementary row operations ${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+6{R}_{4}$ and ${R}_{3}:=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{3}.$ What is the value of $\\det (V)?$"] [:db/cas 17592186120432 :question/worked-solution "First, let's figure out what each operation does to the determinant.\n\n- The operation ${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+6{R}_{4}$ involves adding a multiple of one row to another row,  so it does not change the determinant.\n\n- The operation ${R}_{3}:=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{3}$ involves multiplying a row by $\\frac{1}{2},$ so the determinant is multiplied by $\\frac{1}{2}$ as well.\n\nTherefore, we only have to multiply the determinant by $\\frac{1}{2}.$ Consequently, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(V) & =\\frac{1}{2}det(U) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 10 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, let's figure out what each operation does to the determinant.\n\n- The operation ${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+6{R}_{4}$ involves adding a multiple of one row to another row,  so it does not change the determinant.\n\n- The operation ${R}_{3}:=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{3}$ involves multiplying a row by $\\frac{1}{2},$ so the determinant is multiplied by $\\frac{1}{2}$ as well.\n\nTherefore, we only have to multiply the determinant by $\\frac{1}{2}.$ Consequently, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\det (V) & =\\frac{1}{2}\\det (U) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 10 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186120449 :question/problem "Given that $$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 10 & 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ find $det(A).$" "Given that $$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 10 & 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ find $\\det (A).$"] [:db/cas 17592186120449 :question/worked-solution "Given two $n\\times n$ matrices $M$ and $N,$ we have\n\n$$\ndet(MN)=det(M)\\cdot det(N).\n$$\n\nIn our case, this gives\n\n$$\ndet(A)=det\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 10 & 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot det\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNotice that both matrices are triangular. Therefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =(5\\cdot (-1)\\cdot 2)\\cdot (3\\cdot 2\\cdot (-2)) \\\\  & =(-10)\\cdot (-12) \\\\  & =120.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Given two $n\\times n$ matrices $M$ and $N,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\det (MN)=\\det (M)\\cdot \\det (N).\n$$\n\nIn our case, this gives\n\n$$\n\\det (A)=\\det \\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 10 & 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\det \\begin{bmatrix}3 & 9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNotice that both matrices are triangular. Therefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\det (A) & =(5\\cdot (-1)\\cdot 2)\\cdot (3\\cdot 2\\cdot (-2)) \\\\  & =(-10)\\cdot (-12) \\\\  & =120.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186120450 :question/problem "Given that $$\n\\displaystyle A = \\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{3} & -4 \\\\ -1 & 5\\sqrt{3}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 2 \\\\ 5 & 3\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ find $det(A)$." "Given that $$\n\\displaystyle A = \\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{3} & -4 \\\\ -1 & 5\\sqrt{3}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 2 \\\\ 5 & 3\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ find $\\det (A)$."] [:db/cas 17592186120451 :question/problem "Find $det({A}^{-1}),$ if $A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 6\\end{bmatrix}.$" "Find $\\det ({A}^{-1}),$ if $A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 6\\end{bmatrix}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186120451 :question/worked-solution "Given an invertible $n\\times n$ matrix $A,$ we have\n\n$$\ndet({A}^{-1})=\\frac{1}{det(A)}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find $det(A):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =det\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =12+3 \\\\  & =15\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain \n\n$$\ndet({A}^{-1})=\\frac{1}{det(A)}=\\frac{1}{15}.\n$$" "Given an invertible $n\\times n$ matrix $A,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\det ({A}^{-1})=\\frac{1}{\\det (A)}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find $\\det (A):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\det (A) & =\\det \\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =12+3 \\\\  & =15\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\det ({A}^{-1})=\\frac{1}{\\det (A)}=\\frac{1}{15}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186120452 :question/problem "Suppose $A$ is a $5\\times 5$ matrix and $det(A)=-28.$ The matrix $B$ is obtained from $A$ by applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{5}↔{R}_{3}$ (switching the third and the fifth rows). What is the value of $det(B)?$" "Suppose $A$ is a $5\\times 5$ matrix and $\\det (A)=-28.$ The matrix $B$ is obtained from $A$ by applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{5}↔{R}_{3}$ (switching the third and the fifth rows). What is the value of $\\det (B)?$"] [:db/cas 17592186120452 :question/worked-solution "Since swapping any two rows changes the sign of the determinant to the opposite sign, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(B) & =-det(A) \\\\  & =-(-28) \\\\  & =28.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Since swapping any two rows changes the sign of the determinant to the opposite sign, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\det (B) & =-\\det (A) \\\\  & =-(-28) \\\\  & =28.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186120453 :question/problem "Suppose $A$ is a $6\\times 6$ matrix and $det(A)=35.$ The matrix $B$ is obtained from $A$ by applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{1}:=-\\frac{3}{5}{R}_{1}.$ What is the value of $det(B)?$" "Suppose $A$ is a $6\\times 6$ matrix and $\\det (A)=35.$ The matrix $B$ is obtained from $A$ by applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{1}:=-\\frac{3}{5}{R}_{1}.$ What is the value of $\\det (B)?$"] [:db/cas 17592186120453 :question/worked-solution "The row operation applied to $A$ consists of multiplying a row by the number $-\\frac{3}{5}.$\n\nWhen we do this, the determinant is multiplied by the same number. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(B) & =-\\frac{3}{5}det(A) \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{5}\\cdot 35 \\\\  & =-21.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The row operation applied to $A$ consists of multiplying a row by the number $-\\frac{3}{5}.$\n\nWhen we do this, the determinant is multiplied by the same number. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\det (B) & =-\\frac{3}{5}\\det (A) \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{5}\\cdot 35 \\\\  & =-21.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186120454 :question/problem "Suppose $X$ is a $4\\times 4$ matrix and $det(X)=105.$ The matrix $Y$ is obtained from $X$ by applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{3}↔{R}_{1}$ (switching the first and the third rows). What is the value of $det(Y)?$" "Suppose $X$ is a $4\\times 4$ matrix and $\\det (X)=105.$ The matrix $Y$ is obtained from $X$ by applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{3}↔{R}_{1}$ (switching the first and the third rows). What is the value of $\\det (Y)?$"] [:db/cas 17592186120454 :question/worked-solution "Since swapping any two rows changes the sign of the determinant to the opposite sign, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(Y) & =-det(X) \\\\  & =-(105) \\\\  & =-105.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Since swapping any two rows changes the sign of the determinant to the opposite sign, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\det (Y) & =-\\det (X) \\\\  & =-(105) \\\\  & =-105.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186120455 :question/problem "Suppose $C$ is a $7\\times 7$ matrix and $det(C)=-126.$ The matrix $D$ is obtained from $C$ by applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{5}:=\\frac{8}{21}{R}_{5}.$ What is the value of $det(D)?$" "Suppose $C$ is a $7\\times 7$ matrix and $\\det (C)=-126.$ The matrix $D$ is obtained from $C$ by applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{5}:=\\frac{8}{21}{R}_{5}.$ What is the value of $\\det (D)?$"] [:db/cas 17592186120455 :question/worked-solution "The row operation applied to $C$ consists of multiplying a row by the number $\\frac{8}{21}.$\n\nWhen we do this, the determinant is multiplied by the same number. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(D) & =\\frac{8}{21}det(C) \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{21}\\cdot (-126) \\\\  & =-48.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The row operation applied to $C$ consists of multiplying a row by the number $\\frac{8}{21}.$\n\nWhen we do this, the determinant is multiplied by the same number. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\det (D) & =\\frac{8}{21}\\det (C) \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{21}\\cdot (-126) \\\\  & =-48.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186120456 :question/problem "Consider the matrices given below. Given that $X$ and $Y$ are row equivalent matrices and $det(X)=-8$, find $det(Y).$\n\n$$\nX=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -6 & 3 & 5\\end{bmatrix},\\,Y=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -6 & 3 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" "Consider the matrices given below. Given that $X$ and $Y$ are row equivalent matrices and $\\det (X)=-8$, find $\\det (Y).$\n\n$$\nX=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -6 & 3 & 5\\end{bmatrix},\\,Y=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -6 & 3 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186120456 :question/worked-solution "Notice that $Y$ can be obtained from $X$ by swapping the $2$nd and the $3$rd rows. \n\nThis changes the sign of the determinant to the opposite one.\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(Y) & =-det(X) \\\\  & =-(-8) \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Notice that $Y$ can be obtained from $X$ by swapping the $2$nd and the $3$rd rows. \n\nThis changes the sign of the determinant to the opposite one.\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\det (Y) & =-\\det (X) \\\\  & =-(-8) \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186120495 :question/worked-solution "Let $T(s)$ denote the unit tangent to the given curve as a function of the arc length $s.$ Since we are given the curve $r(s)$ parametrized by the arc length $s$, we will use the following formula for the curvature:\n\n$$\nκ(s)=‖{T}^{′}(s)‖=‖{r}^{″}(s)‖\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{′}(s)$ and ${r}^{″}(s)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(s) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡s),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡s),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡s)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡s,\\,\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡s,\\,\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡s⟩ \\\\ {r}^{″}(s) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡s),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡s),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡s)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\operatorname{cos}⁡s,\\,-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡s,\\,-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡s⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the curvature is  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(s) & =‖{r}^{″}(s)‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡s)}^{2}+{(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡s)}^{2}+{(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡s)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{cos}^{2}⁡s+\\frac{1}{2}{sin}^{2}⁡s+\\frac{1}{2}{sin}^{2}⁡s} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{cos}^{2}⁡s+{sin}^{2}⁡s} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let $T(s)$ denote the unit tangent to the given curve as a function of the arc length $s.$ Since we are given the curve $r(s)$ parametrized by the arc length $s$, we will use the following formula for the curvature:\n\n$$\nκ(s)=‖{T}^{′}(s)‖=‖{r}^{″}(s)‖\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{′}(s)$ and ${r}^{″}(s)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(s) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡s),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡s),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡s)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡s,\\,\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡s,\\,\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡s⟩ \\\\ {r}^{″}(s) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡s),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡s),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡s)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\operatorname{cos}⁡s,\\,-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡s,\\,-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡s⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the curvature is  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(s) & =‖{r}^{″}(s)‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡s)}^{2}+{(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡s)}^{2}+{(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡s)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{\\cos }^{2}⁡s+\\frac{1}{2}{\\sin }^{2}⁡s+\\frac{1}{2}{\\sin }^{2}⁡s} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{\\cos }^{2}⁡s+{\\sin }^{2}⁡s} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186120512 :question/problem "Suppose $A$ is a $3\\times3$ matrix and $det(A) = 4$. What is the value of $det(5A)$?" "Suppose $A$ is a $3\\times3$ matrix and $\\det (A) = 4$. What is the value of $\\det (5A)$?"] [:db/cas 17592186120513 :question/problem "Suppose $B$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix and $det(B)=-2.$ What is the value of $det(3B)?$" "Suppose $B$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix and $\\det (B)=-2.$ What is the value of $\\det (3B)?$"] [:db/cas 17592186120513 :question/worked-solution "Multiplying a $n\\times n$ matrix by a scalar $k$ is equivalent to multiplying each row by $k,$ and this occurs $n$ times. This leads to the result\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(k\\cdot B)={k}^{n}\\cdot det(B).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $k=3$ and $n=2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(3B) & ={3}^{2}\\cdot det(B) \\\\  & =9\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =-18.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Multiplying a $n\\times n$ matrix by a scalar $k$ is equivalent to multiplying each row by $k,$ and this occurs $n$ times. This leads to the result\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\det (k\\cdot B)={k}^{n}\\cdot \\det (B).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $k=3$ and $n=2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\det (3B) & ={3}^{2}\\cdot \\det (B) \\\\  & =9\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =-18.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186121001 :tutorial/content "Consider the curve $C$ parametrized by the vector function \n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t) = \\langle 2\\sin t, \\: 2\\cos t, \\: \\sqrt{5} t\\rangle, \\qquad t \\ge 0.\n$$\n\n \n\nFor any $t >0,$ the arc length $s$ of $C$ from $\\mathbf r(0)$ to $\\mathbf r(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{t}‖{r}^{′}(u)‖\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{t}‖⟨2\\operatorname{cos}⁡u,\\,-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡u,\\,\\sqrt{5}⟩‖\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{t}\\sqrt{(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡u)^{2}+(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡u)^{2}+(\\sqrt{5})^{2}}\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{t}\\sqrt{4({cos}^{2}⁡u+{sin}^{2}⁡u)+5}\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{t}\\sqrt{9}\\,du \\\\  & =3u{|}_{0}^{t} \\\\  & =3t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $s= 3t,$ or equivalently $t = \\dfrac{s}{3}.$ \n\nNow, we can define a new vector function $\\mathbf{R}$ as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf R(s)= \\mathbf r\\left(\\dfrac{s}{3}\\right) = \\left\\langle 2\\sin \\left(\\dfrac{s}{3}\\right), \\: 2\\cos \\left(\\dfrac{s}{3}\\right), \\: \\sqrt{5} \\left(\\dfrac{s}{3}\\right) \\right\\rangle .\n$$\n\nThe function $\\mathbf{R}(s)$ gives us the *same* curve $C,$ but this time it is parameterized by the arc length $s.$\n\nIn general, if $\\mathbf r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b]$ is a continuously differentiable vector function such that $\\mathbf r'(t )\\neq \\mathbf{0},$ then to parametrize it in terms of arc length we perform the following steps:\n\n1\\. Compute $$\n\\displaystyle s = \\displaystyle s(t) = \\int_a^t\\|\\mathbf r'(u)\\| \\, \\mathrm{d}u\n$$\n\n2\\. Write $s = s(t)$ in the form $t=t(s)$\n\n3\\. Substitute $t=t(s)$ back into $\\mathbf r(t)$ to get $\\mathbf R(s)=\\mathbf r(t(s))$\n\n**Note:** One advantage of parametrizing a curve by its arc length is that the tangent vector is always a unit vector:\n\n$$\n\\left\\|\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}\\mathbf R}{\\textrm{d} s}\\right\\| = 1\n$$" "Consider the curve $C$ parametrized by the vector function \n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t) = \\langle 2\\sin t, \\: 2\\cos t, \\: \\sqrt{5} t\\rangle, \\qquad t \\ge 0.\n$$\n\n \n\nFor any $t >0,$ the arc length $s$ of $C$ from $\\mathbf r(0)$ to $\\mathbf r(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{t}‖{r}^{′}(u)‖\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{t}‖⟨2\\operatorname{cos}⁡u,\\,-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡u,\\,\\sqrt{5}⟩‖\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{t}\\sqrt{(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡u)^{2}+(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡u)^{2}+(\\sqrt{5})^{2}}\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{t}\\sqrt{4({\\cos }^{2}⁡u+{\\sin }^{2}⁡u)+5}\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{t}\\sqrt{9}\\,du \\\\  & =3u{|}_{0}^{t} \\\\  & =3t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $s= 3t,$ or equivalently $t = \\dfrac{s}{3}.$ \n\nNow, we can define a new vector function $\\mathbf{R}$ as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf R(s)= \\mathbf r\\left(\\dfrac{s}{3}\\right) = \\left\\langle 2\\sin \\left(\\dfrac{s}{3}\\right), \\: 2\\cos \\left(\\dfrac{s}{3}\\right), \\: \\sqrt{5} \\left(\\dfrac{s}{3}\\right) \\right\\rangle .\n$$\n\nThe function $\\mathbf{R}(s)$ gives us the *same* curve $C,$ but this time it is parameterized by the arc length $s.$\n\nIn general, if $\\mathbf r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b]$ is a continuously differentiable vector function such that $\\mathbf r'(t )\\neq \\mathbf{0},$ then to parametrize it in terms of arc length we perform the following steps:\n\n1\\. Compute $$\n\\displaystyle s = \\displaystyle s(t) = \\int_a^t\\|\\mathbf r'(u)\\| \\, \\mathrm{d}u\n$$\n\n2\\. Write $s = s(t)$ in the form $t=t(s)$\n\n3\\. Substitute $t=t(s)$ back into $\\mathbf r(t)$ to get $\\mathbf R(s)=\\mathbf r(t(s))$\n\n**Note:** One advantage of parametrizing a curve by its arc length is that the tangent vector is always a unit vector:\n\n$$\n\\left\\|\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}\\mathbf R}{\\textrm{d} s}\\right\\| = 1\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186121023 :tutorial/content "Another useful property of determinants is that, for any invertible matrix $A,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\det(A^{-1}) = \\dfrac{1}{\\det(A)}.\n$$\n\nTo understand why the above property is true, remember that $A \\cdot A^{-1} = I$ for any invertible matrix $A.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A\\cdot {A}^{-1} & =I \\\\ det(A\\cdot {A}^{-1}) & =det(I) \\\\ det(A)\\cdot det({A}^{-1}) & =1 \\\\ det({A}^{-1}) & =\\frac{1}{det(A)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Important:** We can only apply the formula $\\det(A^{-1}) = \\dfrac{1}{\\det(A)}$ if $A$ is invertible, meaning that $\\det(A) \\neq 0.$" "Another useful property of determinants is that, for any invertible matrix $A,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\det(A^{-1}) = \\dfrac{1}{\\det(A)}.\n$$\n\nTo understand why the above property is true, remember that $A \\cdot A^{-1} = I$ for any invertible matrix $A.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A\\cdot {A}^{-1} & =I \\\\ \\det (A\\cdot {A}^{-1}) & =\\det (I) \\\\ \\det (A)\\cdot \\det ({A}^{-1}) & =1 \\\\ \\det ({A}^{-1}) & =\\frac{1}{\\det (A)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Important:** We can only apply the formula $\\det(A^{-1}) = \\dfrac{1}{\\det(A)}$ if $A$ is invertible, meaning that $\\det(A) \\neq 0.$"] [:db/cas 17592186121024 :tutorial/content "One final important property of the determinant is that it is **linear in each row and column**. For rows, for example, this means \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\det\\begin{bmatrix}⋮ & ⋮ & … & ⋮ \\\\ {{\\color{red}{◻}}}_{1}+{{\\color{blue}{△}}}_{1} & {{\\color{red}{◻}}}_{2}+{{\\color{blue}{△}}}_{2} & … & {{\\color{red}{◻}}}_{n}+{{\\color{blue}{△}}}_{n} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & … & ⋮\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & \\hspace{2em}\\hspace{2em}=\\det\\begin{bmatrix}⋮ & ⋮ & … & ⋮ \\\\ {{\\color{red}{◻}}}_{1} & {{\\color{red}{◻}}}_{2} & … & {{\\color{red}{◻}}}_{n} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & … & ⋮\\end{bmatrix}+\\det\\begin{bmatrix}⋮ & ⋮ & … & ⋮ \\\\ {{\\color{blue}{△}}}_{1} & {{\\color{blue}{△}}}_{2} & … & {{\\color{blue}{△}}}_{n} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & … & ⋮\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor instance, it's easy to check that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overbrace{det\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}}^{-2} & =det\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ {\\color{red}{0}}+{\\color{blue}{3}} & {\\color{red}{4}}+{\\color{blue}{0}}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\underbrace{det\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ {\\color{red}{0}} & {\\color{red}{4}}\\end{bmatrix}}_{4}+\\underbrace{det\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ {\\color{blue}{3}} & {\\color{blue}{0}}\\end{bmatrix}}_{-6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that this property also holds for columns:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det\\begin{bmatrix}⋯ & {{\\color{red}{◻}}}_{1}+{{\\color{blue}{△}}}_{1} & ⋯ \\\\ ⋯ & {{\\color{red}{◻}}}_{2}+{{\\color{blue}{△}}}_{2} & ⋯ \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ ⋯ & {{\\color{red}{◻}}}_{n}+{{\\color{blue}{△}}}_{n} & ⋯\\end{bmatrix}=det\\begin{bmatrix}⋯ & {{\\color{red}{◻}}}_{1} & ⋯ \\\\ ⋯ & {{\\color{red}{◻}}}_{2} & ⋯ \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ ⋯ & {{\\color{red}{◻}}}_{n} & ⋯\\end{bmatrix}+det\\begin{bmatrix}⋯ & {{\\color{blue}{△}}}_{1} & ⋯ \\\\ ⋯ & {{\\color{blue}{△}}}_{2} & ⋯ \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ ⋯ & {{\\color{blue}{△}}}_{n} & ⋯\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor example, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overbrace{det\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}}^{-2} & =det\\begin{bmatrix}{\\color{red}{1}}+{\\color{blue}{0}} & 2 \\\\ {\\color{red}{0}}+{\\color{blue}{3}} & 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\underbrace{det\\begin{bmatrix}{\\color{red}{1}} & 2 \\\\ {\\color{red}{0}} & 4\\end{bmatrix}}_{4}+\\underbrace{det\\begin{bmatrix}{\\color{blue}{0}} & 2 \\\\ {\\color{blue}{3}} & 4\\end{bmatrix}}_{-6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "One final important property of the determinant is that it is **linear in each row and column**. For rows, for example, this means \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\det\\begin{bmatrix}⋮ & ⋮ & … & ⋮ \\\\ {{\\color{red}{◻}}}_{1}+{{\\color{blue}{△}}}_{1} & {{\\color{red}{◻}}}_{2}+{{\\color{blue}{△}}}_{2} & … & {{\\color{red}{◻}}}_{n}+{{\\color{blue}{△}}}_{n} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & … & ⋮\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & \\hspace{2em}\\hspace{2em}=\\det\\begin{bmatrix}⋮ & ⋮ & … & ⋮ \\\\ {{\\color{red}{◻}}}_{1} & {{\\color{red}{◻}}}_{2} & … & {{\\color{red}{◻}}}_{n} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & … & ⋮\\end{bmatrix}+\\det\\begin{bmatrix}⋮ & ⋮ & … & ⋮ \\\\ {{\\color{blue}{△}}}_{1} & {{\\color{blue}{△}}}_{2} & … & {{\\color{blue}{△}}}_{n} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & … & ⋮\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor instance, it's easy to check that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overbrace{\\det \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}}^{-2} & =\\det \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ {\\color{red}{0}}+{\\color{blue}{3}} & {\\color{red}{4}}+{\\color{blue}{0}}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\underbrace{\\det \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ {\\color{red}{0}} & {\\color{red}{4}}\\end{bmatrix}}_{4}+\\underbrace{\\det \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ {\\color{blue}{3}} & {\\color{blue}{0}}\\end{bmatrix}}_{-6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that this property also holds for columns:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\det \\begin{bmatrix}⋯ & {{\\color{red}{◻}}}_{1}+{{\\color{blue}{△}}}_{1} & ⋯ \\\\ ⋯ & {{\\color{red}{◻}}}_{2}+{{\\color{blue}{△}}}_{2} & ⋯ \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ ⋯ & {{\\color{red}{◻}}}_{n}+{{\\color{blue}{△}}}_{n} & ⋯\\end{bmatrix}=\\det \\begin{bmatrix}⋯ & {{\\color{red}{◻}}}_{1} & ⋯ \\\\ ⋯ & {{\\color{red}{◻}}}_{2} & ⋯ \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ ⋯ & {{\\color{red}{◻}}}_{n} & ⋯\\end{bmatrix}+\\det \\begin{bmatrix}⋯ & {{\\color{blue}{△}}}_{1} & ⋯ \\\\ ⋯ & {{\\color{blue}{△}}}_{2} & ⋯ \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ ⋯ & {{\\color{blue}{△}}}_{n} & ⋯\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor example, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overbrace{\\det \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}}^{-2} & =\\det \\begin{bmatrix}{\\color{red}{1}}+{\\color{blue}{0}} & 2 \\\\ {\\color{red}{0}}+{\\color{blue}{3}} & 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\underbrace{\\det \\begin{bmatrix}{\\color{red}{1}} & 2 \\\\ {\\color{red}{0}} & 4\\end{bmatrix}}_{4}+\\underbrace{\\det \\begin{bmatrix}{\\color{blue}{0}} & 2 \\\\ {\\color{blue}{3}} & 4\\end{bmatrix}}_{-6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186121044 :tutorial/content "As we saw, swapping two rows does not preserve the determinant. However, the only difference is the sign of the determinant changes. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}{\\color{red}1} & {\\color{red}2} \\\\ {\\color{blue}3} & {\\color{blue}4}\\end{vmatrix} & =1\\cdot 4-2\\cdot 3=-2 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}{\\color{blue}3} & {\\color{blue}4} \\\\ {\\color{red}1} & {\\color{red}2}\\end{vmatrix} & =3\\cdot 2-1\\cdot 4=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, in the above example, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}{\\color{red}1} & {\\color{red}2} \\\\ {\\color{blue}3} & {\\color{blue}4}\\end{vmatrix} & =-\\begin{vmatrix}{\\color{blue}3} & {\\color{blue}4} \\\\ {\\color{red}1} & {\\color{red}2}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn general, swapping any two rows of a matrix changes the sign of the determinant to the opposite one. That is to say, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det\\begin{bmatrix}⋮ & ⋮ & … & ⋮ \\\\ {\\color{red}◻} & {\\color{red}◻} & … & {\\color{red}◻} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & … & ⋮ \\\\ {\\color{blue}△} & {\\color{blue}△} & … & {\\color{blue}△} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & … & ⋮\\end{bmatrix}=-det\\begin{bmatrix}⋮ & ⋮ & … & ⋮ \\\\ {\\color{blue}△} & {\\color{blue}△} & … & {\\color{blue}△} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & … & ⋮ \\\\ {\\color{red}◻} & {\\color{red}◻} & … & {\\color{red}◻} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & … & ⋮\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "As we saw, swapping two rows does not preserve the determinant. However, the only difference is the sign of the determinant changes. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}{\\color{red}1} & {\\color{red}2} \\\\ {\\color{blue}3} & {\\color{blue}4}\\end{vmatrix} & =1\\cdot 4-2\\cdot 3=-2 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}{\\color{blue}3} & {\\color{blue}4} \\\\ {\\color{red}1} & {\\color{red}2}\\end{vmatrix} & =3\\cdot 2-1\\cdot 4=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, in the above example, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}{\\color{red}1} & {\\color{red}2} \\\\ {\\color{blue}3} & {\\color{blue}4}\\end{vmatrix} & =-\\begin{vmatrix}{\\color{blue}3} & {\\color{blue}4} \\\\ {\\color{red}1} & {\\color{red}2}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn general, swapping any two rows of a matrix changes the sign of the determinant to the opposite one. That is to say, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\det \\begin{bmatrix}⋮ & ⋮ & … & ⋮ \\\\ {\\color{red}◻} & {\\color{red}◻} & … & {\\color{red}◻} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & … & ⋮ \\\\ {\\color{blue}△} & {\\color{blue}△} & … & {\\color{blue}△} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & … & ⋮\\end{bmatrix}=-\\det \\begin{bmatrix}⋮ & ⋮ & … & ⋮ \\\\ {\\color{blue}△} & {\\color{blue}△} & … & {\\color{blue}△} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & … & ⋮ \\\\ {\\color{red}◻} & {\\color{red}◻} & … & {\\color{red}◻} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & … & ⋮\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186121045 :tutorial/content "As we have seen, multiplying a row by a number does not preserve the determinant. However, it does affect the determinant in a predictable way: multiplying a row by a number scales the determinant by that number as well.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{vmatrix} & =1\\cdot 4-2\\cdot 3=-2 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}{\\color{red}2}\\cdot 1 & {\\color{red}2}\\cdot 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{vmatrix}=\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{vmatrix} & =2\\cdot 4-3\\cdot 4=-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, in the above example, we have\n$$\n\\left| {\\begin{array}{cc}{\\color{red}2} \\cdot  1 & {\\color{red}2} \\cdot  2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{array}} \\right| = {\\color{red}2} \\cdot \\left| {\\begin{array}{cc}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{array}} \\right|.\n$$\n\nIn general, multiplying a row of a matrix by a number multiplies the determinant by that number. That is to say, we can factor out the common multiplier from the row of the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det\\begin{bmatrix}⋮ & ⋮ & … & ⋮ \\\\ {\\color{red}k}△ & {\\color{red}k}△ & … & {\\color{red}k}△ \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & … & ⋮\\end{bmatrix}={\\color{red}k}\\cdot det\\begin{bmatrix}⋮ & ⋮ & … & ⋮ \\\\ △ & △ & … & △ \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & … & ⋮\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "As we have seen, multiplying a row by a number does not preserve the determinant. However, it does affect the determinant in a predictable way: multiplying a row by a number scales the determinant by that number as well.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{vmatrix} & =1\\cdot 4-2\\cdot 3=-2 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}{\\color{red}2}\\cdot 1 & {\\color{red}2}\\cdot 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{vmatrix}=\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{vmatrix} & =2\\cdot 4-3\\cdot 4=-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, in the above example, we have\n$$\n\\left| {\\begin{array}{cc}{\\color{red}2} \\cdot  1 & {\\color{red}2} \\cdot  2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{array}} \\right| = {\\color{red}2} \\cdot \\left| {\\begin{array}{cc}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{array}} \\right|.\n$$\n\nIn general, multiplying a row of a matrix by a number multiplies the determinant by that number. That is to say, we can factor out the common multiplier from the row of the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\det \\begin{bmatrix}⋮ & ⋮ & … & ⋮ \\\\ {\\color{red}k}△ & {\\color{red}k}△ & … & {\\color{red}k}△ \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & … & ⋮\\end{bmatrix}={\\color{red}k}\\cdot \\det \\begin{bmatrix}⋮ & ⋮ & … & ⋮ \\\\ △ & △ & … & △ \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & … & ⋮\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186121059 :tutorial/content "One way to find the determinant of a $3\\!\\times\\! 3$ matrix $A$ is to take the alternating sum of the entries of the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\det(A) = {\\color{red}a_{11}}{\\color{blue}M_{11}} - {\\color{red}a_{12}}{\\color{blue}M_{12}} + {\\color{red}a_{13}}{\\color{blue}M_{13}}\n$$\n\nRemember that we had to change the sign of the second product. When we multiply a minor of an entry by the respective sign in the formula like this, we get the cofactor of the entry. The cofactors in the above formula were ${\\color{blue}M_{11}},$ $-{\\color{blue}M_{12}},$ and ${\\color{blue}M_{13}}.$\n\nUsing this idea, we can write the formula for the determinant of a $3\\!\\times\\! 3$ matrix $A$ in terms of cofactors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\det(A) = {\\color{red}a_{11}}{\\color{blue}C_{11}} + {\\color{red}a_{12}}{\\color{blue}C_{12}} + {\\color{red}a_{13}}{\\color{blue}C_{13}}\n$$\n\nThis is called the **cofactor expansion** of the determinant across the first row.\n\nIn fact, we can use a similar method of multiplying the entries of the top row of the matrix by their respective cofactors to find the determinant for any $n\\!\\times\\! n$ matrix $A$ when $n \\ge 3$. The formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & ={\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}{a}_{1j}{C}_{1j} \\\\  & ={a}_{11}{C}_{11}+{a}_{12}{C}_{12}+⋯+{a}_{1n}{C}_{1n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's see how to use this formula in the next example." "One way to find the determinant of a $3\\!\\times\\! 3$ matrix $A$ is to take the alternating sum of the entries of the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\det(A) = {\\color{red}a_{11}}{\\color{blue}M_{11}} - {\\color{red}a_{12}}{\\color{blue}M_{12}} + {\\color{red}a_{13}}{\\color{blue}M_{13}}\n$$\n\nRemember that we had to change the sign of the second product. When we multiply a minor of an entry by the respective sign in the formula like this, we get the cofactor of the entry. The cofactors in the above formula were ${\\color{blue}M_{11}},$ $-{\\color{blue}M_{12}},$ and ${\\color{blue}M_{13}}.$\n\nUsing this idea, we can write the formula for the determinant of a $3\\!\\times\\! 3$ matrix $A$ in terms of cofactors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\det(A) = {\\color{red}a_{11}}{\\color{blue}C_{11}} + {\\color{red}a_{12}}{\\color{blue}C_{12}} + {\\color{red}a_{13}}{\\color{blue}C_{13}}\n$$\n\nThis is called the **cofactor expansion** of the determinant across the first row.\n\nIn fact, we can use a similar method of multiplying the entries of the top row of the matrix by their respective cofactors to find the determinant for any $n\\!\\times\\! n$ matrix $A$ when $n \\ge 3$. The formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\det (A) & ={\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}{a}_{1j}{C}_{1j} \\\\  & ={a}_{11}{C}_{11}+{a}_{12}{C}_{12}+⋯+{a}_{1n}{C}_{1n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's see how to use this formula in the next example."] [:db/cas 17592186121061 :tutorial/content "Let's check what happens if we multiply the entire matrix (not only one row) by a number. For example,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{vmatrix} & =1\\cdot 4-2\\cdot 3=-2 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}{\\color{red}3}\\cdot 1 & {\\color{red}3}\\cdot 2 \\\\ {\\color{red}3}\\cdot 3 & {\\color{red}3}\\cdot 4\\end{vmatrix}=\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 6 \\\\ 9 & 12\\end{vmatrix} & =3\\cdot 12-6\\cdot 9=-18\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, in the above example, we have\n\n$$\n\\left| {\\begin{array}{cc}{\\color{red}3} \\cdot  1 & {\\color{red}3} \\cdot  2 \\\\ {\\color{red}3} \\cdot  3 & {\\color{red}3} \\cdot  4\\end{array}} \\right| = {\\color{red}3}^2 \\cdot \\left| {\\begin{array}{cc}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{array}} \\right|.\n$$\n\nThe above is equivalent to multiplying both rows of the matrix by $3$. Multiplying the first row scales the determinant by $3$, and then multiplying the second row scales it by $3$ once more.\n\nIn general, multiplying an entire $n \\times n$ matrix by a number multiplies the determinant by that number to the power of $n$. That is to say, we can factor out the common multiplier from the entire matrix, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(k\\cdot A) &={k}^{n}\\cdot det(A),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an $n \\times n$ matrix." "Let's check what happens if we multiply the entire matrix (not only one row) by a number. For example,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{vmatrix} & =1\\cdot 4-2\\cdot 3=-2 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}{\\color{red}3}\\cdot 1 & {\\color{red}3}\\cdot 2 \\\\ {\\color{red}3}\\cdot 3 & {\\color{red}3}\\cdot 4\\end{vmatrix}=\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 6 \\\\ 9 & 12\\end{vmatrix} & =3\\cdot 12-6\\cdot 9=-18\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, in the above example, we have\n\n$$\n\\left| {\\begin{array}{cc}{\\color{red}3} \\cdot  1 & {\\color{red}3} \\cdot  2 \\\\ {\\color{red}3} \\cdot  3 & {\\color{red}3} \\cdot  4\\end{array}} \\right| = {\\color{red}3}^2 \\cdot \\left| {\\begin{array}{cc}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{array}} \\right|.\n$$\n\nThe above is equivalent to multiplying both rows of the matrix by $3$. Multiplying the first row scales the determinant by $3$, and then multiplying the second row scales it by $3$ once more.\n\nIn general, multiplying an entire $n \\times n$ matrix by a number multiplies the determinant by that number to the power of $n$. That is to say, we can factor out the common multiplier from the entire matrix, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\det (k\\cdot A) &={k}^{n}\\cdot \\det (A),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an $n \\times n$ matrix."] [:db/cas 17592186121111 :tutorial/content "$$\n\\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arsinh}{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcosh}{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\artanh}{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arsech}{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\cosh}{\\mathop{\\rm cosh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\sinh}{\\mathop{\\rm sinh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n$$\n\nSuppose we want to plot the graph of the function $y= \\sinh x$. To do this, we start with the definition:\n\n$$\n\\sinh x = \\dfrac{e^x-e^{-x}}{2}\n$$\n\nWe create a table of values by substituting some $x$-values into the definition of $y = \\sinh x$ and finding the corresponding (approximate) $y$-values:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>$x$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-1.5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-0.5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1.5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\sinh x$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-3.63$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-2.13$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-1.18$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-0.52$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.52$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1.18$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2.13$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3.63$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nPlotting these points, we get the following curve:\n\n![](images/1f/1f6ef568d6da4e7911d717c166cb699699dfad8188fda59a8f103837ef506717.png)\n\nThe key features of the graph are as follows:\n\n- The domain and range are $(-\\infty,\\infty)$.\n- It is an odd function.\n- It has a zero at $x=0$.\n- Its graph is increasing over the entire real line. Moreover,$\\qquad$ $\\sinh{x} \\to -\\infty$ as $x \\to -\\infty$, $\\qquad$ $\\sinh{x} \\to \\infty$ as $x \\to \\infty$.\n- It is the mean (average) of the functions $y=e^x$ and $y=-e^{-x}$.\n\n![](images/04/041792667d5783cb3c81a26dc508f14061a94e47bbc1d9ee342d55bb07f5d903.png)" "$$\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{sech} }{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{csch} }{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsinh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcosh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{artanh} }{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsech} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcsch} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcoth} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\cosh}{\\mathop{\\rm cosh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\sinh}{\\mathop{\\rm sinh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{csch} }{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n$$\n\nSuppose we want to plot the graph of the function $y= \\sinh x$. To do this, we start with the definition:\n\n$$\n\\sinh x = \\dfrac{e^x-e^{-x}}{2}\n$$\n\nWe create a table of values by substituting some $x$-values into the definition of $y = \\sinh x$ and finding the corresponding (approximate) $y$-values:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>$x$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-1.5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-0.5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1.5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\sinh x$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-3.63$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-2.13$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-1.18$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-0.52$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.52$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1.18$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2.13$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3.63$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nPlotting these points, we get the following curve:\n\n![](images/1f/1f6ef568d6da4e7911d717c166cb699699dfad8188fda59a8f103837ef506717.png)\n\nThe key features of the graph are as follows:\n\n- The domain and range are $(-\\infty,\\infty)$.\n- It is an odd function.\n- It has a zero at $x=0$.\n- Its graph is increasing over the entire real line. Moreover,$\\qquad$ $\\sinh{x} \\to -\\infty$ as $x \\to -\\infty$, $\\qquad$ $\\sinh{x} \\to \\infty$ as $x \\to \\infty$.\n- It is the mean (average) of the functions $y=e^x$ and $y=-e^{-x}$.\n\n![](images/04/041792667d5783cb3c81a26dc508f14061a94e47bbc1d9ee342d55bb07f5d903.png)"] [:db/cas 17592186121112 :tutorial/content "Let's now plot the graph of the hyperbolic cosine. As before, we start with the definition:\n\n$$\n\\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arsinh}{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcosh}{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\artanh}{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arsech}{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\cosh}{\\mathop{\\rm cosh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\sinh}{\\mathop{\\rm sinh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits} y= \\cosh x = \\dfrac{e^x+e^{-x}}{2}\n$$\n\nCreating a table of values (rounded to two decimal places where appropriate), we get the following:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>$x$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-1.5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-0.5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1.5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\cosh x$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3.76$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2.35$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1.54$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1.13$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1.13$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1.54$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2.35$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3.76$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nPlotting these points, we get the curve shown below:\n\n![](images/07/07836118fdbb6c7e737a3f9926ef65245994eb3d56138c76a64a70cfe6b544ab.png)\n\nThe key features of the graph are as follows:\n\n- The domain is $(-\\infty,\\infty)$ and the range is $[1,\\infty)$.\n- It is an even function (the graph is symmetric about the $y$-axis).\n- It has no zeros.\n- Its minimum value occurs at $x=0$.\n- Its graph is decreasing over $(-\\infty,0)$ and is increasing over $(0,\\infty)$. Moreover,$\\qquad$ $\\cosh{x} \\to \\infty$ as $x \\to \\pm\\infty$.\n- It is the mean (average) of the functions $y=e^x$ and $y=e^{-x}$.\n\n![](images/06/069613b68901d727d713bd50bbbe6a172348d41df12eabe9f0e735a2499d495a.png