$0$:\n\n| $\\,\\,{\\phantom{0}} {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 9 {\\,. \\,} 8 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 7 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 5$$8$$\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{7 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 9 {\\,. \\,} 0 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 0 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 0}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$7$$2$$7$$0$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$6$$4$$6$$0$$-\\!\\!\\!$$5$$6$$4$$0$$-\\!\\!\\!$$4$$0$$\\fbox{0}$ |\n| --- |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n79 \\operatorname{div}  8 = 9.875\\,\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186138255 :question/worked-solution "To determine the amount Cameron spent each day, we need to divide $607$ by $5$.\n\nWe start by going through the long division procedure as usual:\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{r@{\\,}r} & 121 \\\\ 5 & \\enclose{longdiv}{607} \\\\ & \\underline{-5\\phantom{00}} \\\\ & 10\\phantom{0} \\\\ & \\underline{-10\\phantom{0}} \\\\ & 07 \\\\ & \\underline{-5} \\\\ & \\color{red}{2}\\end{array}\n$$\n\nNotice that the remainder $\\color{red}2$ is less than the divisor $5$. But we can proceed further by adding a $\\color{blue}0$ after the decimal point in the dividend. We also add a decimal point in the quotient, as shown below:\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{r@{\\,}r} & 121\\bbox[2pt,lightgray]{\\color{blue}{.}} \\\\ 5 & \\enclose{longdiv}{607\\bbox[2pt,lightgray]{\\color{blue}{.}}\\color{blue}{0}} \\\\ & \\underline{-5\\phantom{00.0}}\\quad\\color{lightgray}{\\downarrow} \\\\ & 10\\phantom{0.0}\\quad\\color{lightgray}{\\downarrow} \\\\ & \\underline{-10\\phantom{0.0}}\\quad\\color{lightgray}{\\downarrow} \\\\ & 07\\phantom{.0}\\quad\\color{lightgray}{\\downarrow} \\\\ & \\underline{-5\\phantom{.0}}\\quad\\color{lightgray}{\\downarrow} \\\\ & {\\color{red}{2}}{\\color{lightgray}{0}}\\end{array}\n$$\n\nNow we complete the division, as usual, stopping once we obtain a remainder of $0$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{r@{\\,}r} & 121.4 \\\\ 5 & \\enclose{longdiv}{607.0} \\\\ & \\underline{-5\\phantom{00.0}} \\\\ & 10\\phantom{0.0} \\\\ & \\underline{-10\\phantom{0.0}} \\\\ & 07\\phantom{.0} \\\\ & \\underline{-5\\phantom{.0}} \\\\ & 20 \\\\ & \\underline{-20} \\\\ & \\boxed{0}\\end{array}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n607 \\div 5 = 121.4 \\,\n$$\n\nWe conclude that Cameron spent $\\$121.40$ each day." "To determine the amount Cameron spent each day, we need to divide $607$ by $5$.\n\nWe start by going through the long division procedure as usual:\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{r@{\\,}r} & 121 \\\\ 5 & \\enclose{longdiv}{607} \\\\ & \\underline{-5\\phantom{00}} \\\\ & 10\\phantom{0} \\\\ & \\underline{-10\\phantom{0}} \\\\ & 07 \\\\ & \\underline{-5} \\\\ & \\color{red}{2}\\end{array}\n$$\n\nNotice that the remainder $\\color{red}2$ is less than the divisor $5$. But we can proceed further by adding a $\\color{blue}0$ after the decimal point in the dividend. We also add a decimal point in the quotient, as shown below:\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{r@{\\,}r} & 121\\bbox[2pt,lightgray]{\\color{blue}{.}} \\\\ 5 & \\enclose{longdiv}{607\\bbox[2pt,lightgray]{\\color{blue}{.}}\\color{blue}{0}} \\\\ & \\underline{-5\\phantom{00.0}}\\quad\\color{lightgray}{\\downarrow} \\\\ & 10\\phantom{0.0}\\quad\\color{lightgray}{\\downarrow} \\\\ & \\underline{-10\\phantom{0.0}}\\quad\\color{lightgray}{\\downarrow} \\\\ & 07\\phantom{.0}\\quad\\color{lightgray}{\\downarrow} \\\\ & \\underline{-5\\phantom{.0}}\\quad\\color{lightgray}{\\downarrow} \\\\ & {\\color{red}{2}}{\\color{lightgray}{0}}\\end{array}\n$$\n\nNow we complete the division, as usual, stopping once we obtain a remainder of $0$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{r@{\\,}r} & 121.4 \\\\ 5 & \\enclose{longdiv}{607.0} \\\\ & \\underline{-5\\phantom{00.0}} \\\\ & 10\\phantom{0.0} \\\\ & \\underline{-10\\phantom{0.0}} \\\\ & 07\\phantom{.0} \\\\ & \\underline{-5\\phantom{.0}} \\\\ & 20 \\\\ & \\underline{-20} \\\\ & \\boxed{0}\\end{array}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n607 \\operatorname{div}  5 = 121.4 \\,\n$$\n\nWe conclude that Cameron spent $\\$121.40$ each day."] [:db/cas 17592186138263 :question/worked-solution "Here, we need to estimate the value of $997 \\div 19$.\n\nFirst, we round both numbers to the nearest ten:\n$$\n\\begin{array}{ccc}997 & ÷ & 19 \\\\ ↓ &  & ↓ \\\\ {\\color{blue}{1,000}} & ÷ & {\\color{blue}{20}}\\end{array}\n$$\n\nSo we need to find the value of $1,000 \\div 20$, which can be written as the following fraction:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1,000}{20}\n$$\n\nBy dividing the numerator and denominator by $10$, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1,000 \\div 10}{\\, \\, \\, \\, \\,\\, \\, \\, 20 \\div 10} = \\dfrac{100}{2}\n$$\n\nwhich is the same as\n\n$$\n100 \\div 2 = 50\n$$\n\nTherefore, $997 \\div 19$ is about $50$.\n\nSo, it will take about $50$ minutes to fill the pool." "Here, we need to estimate the value of $997 \\operatorname{div}  19$.\n\nFirst, we round both numbers to the nearest ten:\n$$\n\\begin{array}{ccc}997 & ÷ & 19 \\\\ ↓ &  & ↓ \\\\ {\\color{blue}{1,000}} & ÷ & {\\color{blue}{20}}\\end{array}\n$$\n\nSo we need to find the value of $1,000 \\operatorname{div}  20$, which can be written as the following fraction:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1,000}{20}\n$$\n\nBy dividing the numerator and denominator by $10$, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1,000 \\operatorname{div}  10}{\\, \\, \\, \\, \\,\\, \\, \\, 20 \\operatorname{div}  10} = \\dfrac{100}{2}\n$$\n\nwhich is the same as\n\n$$\n100 \\operatorname{div}  2 = 50\n$$\n\nTherefore, $997 \\operatorname{div}  19$ is about $50$.\n\nSo, it will take about $50$ minutes to fill the pool."] [:db/cas 17592186138264 :question/problem "Estimate the value of $52,284 \\div 841$ by rounding the dividend to the nearest thousand and rounding the divisor to the nearest hundred." "Estimate the value of $52,284 \\operatorname{div}  841$ by rounding the dividend to the nearest thousand and rounding the divisor to the nearest hundred."] [:db/cas 17592186138264 :question/worked-solution "We round the dividend to the nearest thousand:\n$52,284 \\rightarrow 52,000$\n\nAlso, we round the divisor to the nearest hundred:\n$841 \\rightarrow 800$\n\nSo we need to find the value of $52,000 \\div 800$, which can be written as the following fraction:\n\n$$\n\\df