cb43ffdafe13310912f4a2dd0b115cf151ee791eb.png)\n\nFind the value of $\\frac{1}{2} \\operatorname{div}  \\frac{2}{3}$ given the model above."] [:db/cas 17592186143550 :question/problem "What is the value of $6.324 \\div 0.31$?" "What is the value of $6.324 \\operatorname{div}  0.31$?"] [:db/cas 17592186143558 :question/problem "What is $28 \\div 3$?" "What is $28 \\operatorname{div}  3$?"] [:db/cas 17592186143580 :question/problem "What is $3 \\cdot 6 \\div 2$?" "What is $3 \\cdot 6 \\operatorname{div}  2$?"] [:db/cas 17592186143581 :question/problem "In the expression below, which part of the expression should be calculated first?\n\n${1 - 4 \\cdot [18 \\div 3(2 + 4)]} + 9$" "In the expression below, which part of the expression should be calculated first?\n\n${1 - 4 \\cdot [18 \\operatorname{div}  3(2 + 4)]} + 9$"] [:db/cas 17592186143975 :tutorial/content "We can create a numerical expression when information is given to us in words.\n\nFor example, let's create an expression that represents the sentence \"*Add $1$ and $11$, then divide by $4$*\".\n\nChecking our sentence carefully, we need to add $1$ and $11$ *before* dividing by $4$.\n\nFor the phrase \"*Add $1$ and $11$*\", this means:\n$1 + 11$\n\nSince this operation comes first, we use parentheses:\n$(1 + 11)$\n\nAdding parentheses will help us to avoid mistakes with the order of operations.\n\nFor the phrase \"*then divide by $4$*\", this means that we should divide the above expression by $4$. So we get:\n$(1 + 11) \\div 4$" "We can create a numerical expression when information is given to us in words.\n\nFor example, let's create an expression that represents the sentence \"*Add $1$ and $11$, then divide by $4$*\".\n\nChecking our sentence carefully, we need to add $1$ and $11$ *before* dividing by $4$.\n\nFor the phrase \"*Add $1$ and $11$*\", this means:\n$1 + 11$\n\nSince this operation comes first, we use parentheses:\n$(1 + 11)$\n\nAdding parentheses will help us to avoid mistakes with the order of operations.\n\nFor the phrase \"*then divide by $4$*\", this means that we should divide the above expression by $4$. So we get:\n$(1 + 11) \\operatorname{div}  4$"] [:db/cas 17592186143976 :tutorial/content "Let's take a look at the following expression:\n\n$$\n5 + (10\\div 2)\n$$\n\nHow do we create an expression that is $8$ times larger than this expression without evaluating the expression first?\n\nFirst, let's note the language. We want an expression that is \"$8$ *times larger*\" than the one above, which means that we should *multiply* by $8$.\n\nFirst, since the expression already has parentheses, we add square brackets.\n\n$$\n[5 + (10\\div 2)]\n$$\n\nWe add brackets to ensure that we're modifying the entire expression, not just part of it.\n\nNow, we multiply the given expression by $8$. This gives:\n\n$$\n8\\times[5 + (10\\div 2)]\n$$\n\nAnd that's our answer!" "Let's take a look at the following expression:\n\n$$\n5 + (10\\operatorname{div}  2)\n$$\n\nHow do we create an expression that is $8$ times larger than this expression without evaluating the expression first?\n\nFirst, let's note the language. We want an expression that is \"$8$ *times larger*\" than the one above, which means that we should *multiply* by $8$.\n\nFirst, since the expression already has parentheses, we add square brackets.\n\n$$\n[5 + (10\\operatorname{div}  2)]\n$$\n\nWe add brackets to ensure that we're modifying the entire expression, not just part of it.\n\nNow, we multiply the given expression by $8$. This gives:\n\n$$\n8\\times[5 + (10\\operatorname{div}  2)]\n$$\n\nAnd that's our answer!"] [:db/cas 17592186143977 :tutorial/content "Let's take a look at the following expression:\n\n$$\n6\\times(4+2)\n$$\n\nHow do we create an expression that is $3$ times smaller than this expression?\n\nFirst, we once again pay careful attention to the language. We want an expression that is \"$3$ *times smaller*\" than the one above. This means that we should *divide* by $3$.\n\nFirst, since the expression already has parentheses, we add square brackets:\n\n$$\n[6\\times(4+2)]\n$$\n\nNow, we divide the expression by $3$. This gives:\n\n$$\n[6\\times(4+2)]\\div 3\n$$" "Let's take a look at the following expression:\n\n$$\n6\\times(4+2)\n$$\n\nHow do we create an expression that is $3$ times smaller than this expression?\n\nFirst, we once again pay careful attention to the language. We want an expression that is \"$3$ *times smaller*\" than the one above. This means that we should *divide* by $3$.\n\nFirst, since the expression already has parentheses, we add square brackets:\n\n$$\n[6\\times(4+2)]\n$$\n\nNow, we divide the expression by $3$. This gives:\n\n$$\n[6\\times(4+2)]\\operatorname{div}  3\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186143981 :tutorial/content "In some division problems, we do not obtain an exact result because there is a remainder. For example,\n\n$$\n7 \\div 4 = {\\color{blue}{1}} \\, \\text{R} \\, {\\color{red}{3}}\n$$\n\nThis means that $7 \\div 4$ is *more* than $1$, but *less* than $2$. It is somewhere in between.\n\nSo what is $7\\div 4$ exactly?\n\nWe can write the exact result of this division problem as a mixed number, where the quotient (${\\color{blue}{1}}$) is the whole number part, and the remainder (${\\color{red}{3}}$) is expressed as a fraction.\n\nTo write the remainder as a fraction, we just need to divide it by the original divisor ($4$). Therefore, expressed as a mixed number, our result is\n\n$$\n7 \\div 4 = {\\color{blue}{1}} \\, \\dfrac{\\color{red}3}{4}\n$$" "In some division problems, we do not obtain an exact result because there is a remainder. For example,\n\n$$\n7 \\operatorname{div}  4 = {\\color{blue}{1}} \\, \\text{R} \\, {\\color{red}{3}}\n$$\n\nThis means that $7 \\operatorname{div}  4$ is *more* than $1$, but *less* than $2$. It is somewhere in between.\n\nSo what is $7\\operatorname{div}  4$ exactly?\n\nWe can write the exact result of this division problem as a mixed number, where the quotient (${\\color{blue}{1}}$) is the whole number part, and the remainder (${\\color{red}{3}}$) is expressed as a fraction.\n\nTo write the remainder as a fraction, we just need to divide it by the original divisor ($4$). Therefore, expressed as a mixed number, our result is\n\n$$\n7 \\operatorname{div}  4 = {\\color{blue}{1}} \\, \\dfrac{\\color{red}3}{4}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186143982 :tutorial/content "Multiplication and division are \"opposite\" operations. For example, since\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}2} \\times {\\color{red}3} = 6\n$$\n\nwe also know that\n\n$$\n6 \\div {\\color{red}3} = {\\color{blue}2}\\,\n$$\n\nWe can use this idea to figure out the solution to some division problems. For example, suppose we are told that the result of calculating $75 \\div 15$ is either\n\n$$\n4,\\quad 5,\\quad \\text{or} \\quad 6\n$$\n\nTo find which choice is correct, we start by writing the corresponding multiplication equation. If\n\n$$\n75 \\div 15 = \\square\n$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\n\\square \\times 15 = 75\n$$\n\nWe can represent this multiplication problem using an area model:\n\n![](images/93/93e6f7c4e26ef45b3e26ef22e26f12134467ce2682a587d8624e9bdd7f891b71.png)\n\nWe'll try each of the given numbers ($4,5,6$) until we find one that satisfies the multiplication equation.\n\n- $4 \\times 15 = 60 \\quad{\\color{red}{\\times}}$\n- $5 \\times 15 = 60 + 15 = 75 \\quad{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$\n\nSo we have:\n\n![](images/63/63cc965e3e080ab40379befc0f5d011fc52add0eac231917cadf928019b0da89.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n75 \\div 15 = 5\n$$" "Multiplication and division are \"opposite\" operations. For example, since\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}2} \\times {\\color{red}3} = 6\n$$\n\nwe also know that\n\n$$\n6 \\operatorname{div}  {\\color{red}3} = {\\color{blue}2}\\,\n$$\n\nWe can use this idea to figure out the solution to some division problems. For example, suppose we are told that the result of calculating $75 \\operatorname{div}  15$ is either\n\n$$\n4,\\quad 5,\\quad \\text{or} \\quad 6\n$$\n\nTo find which choice is correct, we start by writing the corresponding multiplication equation. If\n\n$$\n75 \\operatorname{div}  15 = \\square\n$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\n\\square \\times 15 = 75\n$$\n\nWe can represent this multiplication problem using an area model:\n\n![](images/93/93e6f7c4e26ef45b3e26ef22e26f12134467ce2682a587d8624e9bdd7f891b71.png)\n\nWe'll try each of the given numbers ($4,5,6$) until we find one that satisfies the multiplication equation.\n\n- $4 \\times 15 = 60 \\quad{\\color{red}{\\times}}$\n- $5 \\times 15 = 60 + 15 = 75 \\quad{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$\n\nSo we have:\n\n![](images/63/63cc965e3e080ab40379befc0f5d011fc52add0eac231917cadf928019b0da89.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n75 \\operatorname{div}  15 = 5\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186143983 :tutorial/content "We can apply our knowledge of adding and subtracting fractions with unlike denominators to real-world situations.\n\nFor example, suppose William went to the grocery store to buy flour. He purchased\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{3}\n$$\n\npounds of brown flour and\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{6}\n$$\n\npounds of white flour. He'd like to know how much flour he bought in total.\n\nTo find the total amount of flour that William bought, we need to add\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{3}\n$$\n\n(the amount of brown flour) to\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{6}\n$$\n\n(the amount of white flour). Therefore, we have the following sum:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{3} + \\dfrac{1}{6}\n$$\n\nTo add two fractions with unlike denominators, we need to express each fraction as an equivalent fraction with a common denominator.\n\nHere, we can make a common denominator of $6$.\n\nTo put\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nover a denominator of $6$, we multiply the numerator and denominator by $2{:}$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{3} = \\dfrac{1 \\times 2}{3 \\times 2} = \\dfrac{2}{6}\n$$\n\nWe can now add the fractions. We keep the denominator the same and we add the numerators:\n\n$$\n\\frac{2}{6} + \\frac{1}{6} = \\frac{3}{6}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify this fraction by dividing the numerator and denominator by $3$:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{6} = \\dfrac{3\\div 3}{6\\div 3} = \\dfrac12\n$$\n\nTherefore, William bought\n\n$$\n\\dfrac12\n$$\n\npounds of flour in total." "We can apply our knowledge of adding and subtracting fractions with unlike denominators to real-world situations.\n\nFor example, suppose William went to the grocery store to buy flour. He purchased\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{3}\n$$\n\npounds of brown flour and\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{6}\n$$\n\npounds of white flour. He'd like to know how much flour he bought in total.\n\nTo find the total amount of flour that William bought, we need to add\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{3}\n$$\n\n(the amount of brown flour) to\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{6}\n$$\n\n(the amount of white flour). Therefore, we have the following sum:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{3} + \\dfrac{1}{6}\n$$\n\nTo add two fractions with unlike denominators, we need to express each fraction as an equivalent fraction with a common denominator.\n\nHere, we can make a common denominator of $6$.\n\nTo put\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nover a denominator of $6$, we multiply the numerator and denominator by $2{:}$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{3} = \\dfrac{1 \\times 2}{3 \\times 2} = \\dfrac{2}{6}\n$$\n\nWe can now add the fractions. We keep the denominator the same and we add the numerators:\n\n$$\n\\frac{2}{6} + \\frac{1}{6} = \\frac{3}{6}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify this fraction by dividing the numerator and denominator by $3$:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{6} = \\dfrac{3\\operatorname{div}  3}{6\\operatorname{div}  3} = \\dfrac12\n$$\n\nTherefore, William bought\n\n$$\n\\dfrac12\n$$\n\npounds of flour in total."] [:db/cas 17592186143984 :tutorial/content "Mixed numbers are a common occurrence in everyday life. Therefore, we can apply our knowledge of adding and subtracting mixed numbers to real-world situations.\n\nFor example, suppose Daniela is sewing a costume. She needs\n\n$$\n2\\,\\dfrac{1}{6}\n$$\n\nyards of gold fabric and\n\n$$\n1\\,\\dfrac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nyards of purple fabric. How many yards of fabric does she need to make her costume?\n\nTo find the total yards of fabric that Daniela needs, we would need to add\n\n$$\n2\\,\\dfrac{1}{6}\n$$\n\n(the number of yards of gold fabric) to\n\n$$\n1\\,\\dfrac{1}{3}\n$$\n\n(the number of yards of purple fabric):\n\n$$\n2\\,\\dfrac{1}{6} + 1\\,\\dfrac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nTo add two mixed numbers, we add whole numbers to whole numbers and fractions to fractions. Adding the whole numbers, we get\n\n$$\n2+1 = {\\color{red}{3}}\n$$\n\nNow, we add the fractions:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{6} + \\dfrac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nTo add two fractions with unlike denominators, we need to express each fraction as an equivalent fraction with a common denominator. In this question, we can make a common denominator of $6$.\n\n- The fraction $\\dfrac{1}{6}$ already has a denominator of $6$.\n- To put $\\dfrac{1}{3}$ over a denominator of $6$, we multiply the numerator and denominator by $2$:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac{1}{3} = \\dfrac{1 \\times 2}{3 \\times 2} = \\dfrac{2}{6}\n$$\n\nWe can now add the fractions. We keep the denominator the same and we add the numerators:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{6} + \\frac{2}{6} = \\frac{3}{6}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify this fraction by dividing the numerator and denominator by $3$:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{6} = \\dfrac{3\\div 3}{6\\div 3} = {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac12}}\n$$\n\nAdding, we get\n\n$$\n2\\,\\dfrac{1}{6} + 1\\,\\dfrac{1}{3} = {\\color{red}{3}} + {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac12}} = 3\\,\\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, Daniela needs\n\n$$\n3\\,\\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nyards of fabric to make her costume." "Mixed numbers are a common occurrence in everyday life. Therefore, we can apply our knowledge of adding and subtracting mixed numbers to real-world situations.\n\nFor example, suppose Daniela is sewing a costume. She needs\n\n$$\n2\\,\\dfrac{1}{6}\n$$\n\nyards of gold fabric and\n\n$$\n1\\,\\dfrac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nyards of purple fabric. How many yards of fabric does she need to make her costume?\n\nTo find the total yards of fabric that Daniela needs, we would need to add\n\n$$\n2\\,\\dfrac{1}{6}\n$$\n\n(the number of yards of gold fabric) to\n\n$$\n1\\,\\dfrac{1}{3}\n$$\n\n(the number of yards of purple fabric):\n\n$$\n2\\,\\dfrac{1}{6} + 1\\,\\dfrac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nTo add two mixed numbers, we add whole numbers to whole numbers and fractions to fractions. Adding the whole numbers, we get\n\n$$\n2+1 = {\\color{red}{3}}\n$$\n\nNow, we add the fractions:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{6} + \\dfrac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nTo add two fractions with unlike denominators, we need to express each fraction as an equivalent fraction with a common denominator. In this question, we can make a common denominator of $6$.\n\n- The fraction $\\dfrac{1}{6}$ already has a denominator of $6$.\n- To put $\\dfrac{1}{3}$ over a denominator of $6$, we multiply the numerator and denominator by $2$:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac{1}{3} = \\dfrac{1 \\times 2}{3 \\times 2} = \\dfrac{2}{6}\n$$\n\nWe can now add the fractions. We keep the denominator the same and we add the numerators:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{6} + \\frac{2}{6} = \\frac{3}{6}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify this fraction by dividing the numerator and denominator by $3$:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{6} = \\dfrac{3\\operatorname{div}  3}{6\\operatorname{div}  3} = {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac12}}\n$$\n\nAdding, we get\n\n$$\n2\\,\\dfrac{1}{6} + 1\\,\\dfrac{1}{3} = {\\color{red}{3}} + {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac12}} = 3\\,\\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, Daniela needs\n\n$$\n3\\,\\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nyards of fabric to make her costume."] [:db/cas 17592186143993 :tutorial/content "When the two patterns both begin with $0$, we can compare them using just their rules.\n\nSo, say we know that:\n\n- *Both patterns start from zero*\n- *The rule for pattern A is \"add $10$\".*\n- *The rule for pattern B is \"add $5$\".*\n\nWe can make comparison statements without computing any terms in either pattern.\n\nPattern A is \"add $\\color{red}10$\" and pattern B is \"add ${\\color{blue}5}$.\" Therefore, pattern A is growing faster, by a factor of\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}{10}}\\div {\\color{blue}{5}} = 2 \\,\n$$\n\nConsequently, we have two equivalent statements:\n\n- The terms of pattern A are $2$ times larger than the corresponding terms in pattern B.\n- The terms of pattern B are $2$ times smaller than the corresponding terms in pattern A." "When the two patterns both begin with $0$, we can compare them using just their rules.\n\nSo, say we know that:\n\n- *Both patterns start from zero*\n- *The rule for pattern A is \"add $10$\".*\n- *The rule for pattern B is \"add $5$\".*\n\nWe can make comparison statements without computing any terms in either pattern.\n\nPattern A is \"add $\\color{red}10$\" and pattern B is \"add ${\\color{blue}5}$.\" Therefore, pattern A is growing faster, by a factor of\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}{10}}\\operatorname{div}  {\\color{blue}{5}} = 2 \\,\n$$\n\nConsequently, we have two equivalent statements:\n\n- The terms of pattern A are $2$ times larger than the corresponding terms in pattern B.\n- The terms of pattern B are $2$ times smaller than the corresponding terms in pattern A."] [:db/cas 17592186144002 :tutorial/content "When we divide a fraction by a whole number, say ${\\color{red}{2}},$ the idea is to \n\n- split the fraction into ${\\color{red}{2}}$ equal parts, and\n\n- determine the fraction that makes up each part.\n\nFor example, suppose we wish to find the value of \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4}{5} \\div 2.\n$$\n\nLet's start by drawing the fraction model for $\\dfrac45\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/d1/d159ff7e61ae308f27dd561cc8b219f0fc5b5fa46135ebff70ed15558186280b.png)\n\nWe need to divide this fraction by ${\\color{red}{2}}.$ To do this, we draw a horizontal line through our fraction that splits it into $\\color{red}2$ *equal parts*.\n\n![](images/87/87c0a9dad9ab63de7858e8857e412b4d6ce849dfc0b02a172a5debe420c5baf6.png)\n\nWe only need to determine the fraction that makes up one of the parts. Therefore, we can focus on only one row (i.e., the bottom row).\n\nNow, we remove all the shaded parts not in the bottom row. \n\n![](images/54/54a3760d69e7622afa800e4fe107cdafc7069a1fd31138a9ad6c63e8cf6f8f1f.png)\n\nFrom the picture on the right, we obtain that the shaded part represents $\\dfrac{4}{10}$ of the whole. Therefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4}{5} \\div 2 = \\dfrac{4}{10} \\, .\n$$" "When we divide a fraction by a whole number, say ${\\color{red}{2}},$ the idea is to \n\n- split the fraction into ${\\color{red}{2}}$ equal parts, and\n\n- determine the fraction that makes up each part.\n\nFor example, suppose we wish to find the value of \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4}{5} \\operatorname{div}  2.\n$$\n\nLet's start by drawing the fraction model for $\\dfrac45\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/d1/d159ff7e61ae308f27dd561cc8b219f0fc5b5fa46135ebff70ed15558186280b.png)\n\nWe need to divide this fraction by ${\\color{red}{2}}.$ To do this, we draw a horizontal line through our fraction that splits it into $\\color{red}2$ *equal parts*.\n\n![](images/87/87c0a9dad9ab63de7858e8857e412b4d6ce849dfc0b02a172a5debe420c5baf6.png)\n\nWe only need to determine the fraction that makes up one of the parts. Therefore, we can focus on only one row (i.e., the bottom row).\n\nNow, we remove all the shaded parts not in the bottom row. \n\n![](images/54/54a3760d69e7622afa800e4fe107cdafc7069a1fd31138a9ad6c63e8cf6f8f1f.png)\n\nFrom the picture on the right, we obtain that the shaded part represents $\\dfrac{4}{10}$ of the whole. Therefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4}{5} \\operatorname{div}  2 = \\dfrac{4}{10} \\, .\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186144004 :tutorial/content "We can also use the standard algorithm for division to divide four and five-digit decimals by whole numbers.\n\nFor instance, to find $50.04 \\div 12$, we start by writing down the problem using long division notation. We keep the decimal point in the dividend:\n\n| $12$$\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{5 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 0 {\\,\\bbox[2px, lightgray]{\\color{blue}.}\\,} 0 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 4}$ |\n| --- |\n\nWe also bring the decimal point up into the quotient, placing it directly above the dot in the dividend:\n\n| $\\:\\! \\phantom{0} {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} \\phantom{0} \\bbox[2px, lightgray]{\\color{blue}.}$$12$$\\! \\enclose{longdiv}{5 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 0 {\\,\\bbox[2px, lightgray]{\\color{blue}.}\\,} 0 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 4}$ |\n| --- |\n\nNow, сarrying out the long division process as usual, we get:\n\n| $4$$\\!\\!. 1$$7$$12$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{5 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 0 {\\,. \\,} 0 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 4}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$4$$8$$2$$0$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$1$$2$$8$$4$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$8$$4$$0$ |\n| --- |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n50.04 \\div 12 = 4.17\\,\n$$" "We can also use the standard algorithm for division to divide four and five-digit decimals by whole numbers.\n\nFor instance, to find $50.04 \\operatorname{div}  12$, we start by writing down the problem using long division notation. We keep the decimal point in the dividend:\n\n| $12$$\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{5 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 0 {\\,\\bbox[2px, lightgray]{\\color{blue}.}\\,} 0 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 4}$ |\n| --- |\n\nWe also bring the decimal point up into the quotient, placing it directly above the dot in the dividend:\n\n| $\\:\\! \\phantom{0} {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} \\phantom{0} \\bbox[2px, lightgray]{\\color{blue}.}$$12$$\\! \\enclose{longdiv}{5 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 0 {\\,\\bbox[2px, lightgray]{\\color{blue}.}\\,} 0 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 4}$ |\n| --- |\n\nNow, сarrying out the long division process as usual, we get:\n\n| $4$$\\!\\!. 1$$7$$12$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{5 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 0 {\\,. \\,} 0 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 4}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$4$$8$$2$$0$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$1$$2$$8$$4$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$8$$4$$0$ |\n| --- |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n50.04 \\operatorname{div}  12 = 4.17\\,\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186144005 :tutorial/content "To use the standard algorithm, the divisor must be a whole number.\n\nSo, to divide decimals by other decimals such as $0.832 \\div 0.13$, we must find an equivalent division problem by multiplying both the dividend and divisor by an appropriate power of ten.\n\nFirst, we write the division problem $0.832 \\div 0.13$ as a fraction:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{0.832}{0.13}\n$$\n\nMultiplying the numerator and denominator by $100$, we get a whole number in the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{0.832 \\times 100}{0.13 \\times 100} = \\dfrac{83.2}{13}\n$$\n\nSo the problem is equivalent to $83.2 \\div 13$.\n\nNext, we write down the problem using long division notation, where we keep the decimal point in the dividend:\n\n| $13$$\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{8 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 3 {\\,\\bbox[2px, lightgray]{\\color{blue}.}\\,} 2}$ |\n| --- |\n\nWe also bring the decimal point up into the quotient, placing it directly above the dot in the dividend:\n\n| $\\:\\:\\: \\phantom{0} {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} \\phantom{0} {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} \\bbox[2px, lightgray]{\\color{blue}.}$$13$$\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{8 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 3 {\\,\\bbox[2px, lightgray]{\\color{blue}.}\\,} 2}$ |\n| --- |\n\nNow, сarrying out the long division process as usual, we get:\n\n| $6$$\\!\\!\\!. 4$$13$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{8 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 3 {\\,. \\,} 2}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$7$$8$$5$$2$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$5$$2$$\\phantom{0}$$0$ |\n| --- |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n0.832 \\div 0.13 = 6.4\\,\n$$" "To use the standard algorithm, the divisor must be a whole number.\n\nSo, to divide decimals by other decimals such as $0.832 \\operatorname{div}  0.13$, we must find an equivalent division problem by multiplying both the dividend and divisor by an appropriate power of ten.\n\nFirst, we write the division problem $0.832 \\operatorname{div}  0.13$ as a fraction:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{0.832}{0.13}\n$$\n\nMultiplying the numerator and denominator by $100$, we get a whole number in the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{0.832 \\times 100}{0.13 \\times 100} = \\dfrac{83.2}{13}\n$$\n\nSo the problem is equivalent to $83.2 \\operatorname{div}  13$.\n\nNext, we write down the problem using long division notation, where we keep the decimal point in the dividend:\n\n| $13$$\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{8 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 3 {\\,\\bbox[2px, lightgray]{\\color{blue}.}\\,} 2}$ |\n| --- |\n\nWe also bring the decimal point up into the quotient, placing it directly above the dot in the dividend:\n\n| $\\:\\:\\: \\phantom{0} {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} \\phantom{0} {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} \\bbox[2px, lightgray]{\\color{blue}.}$$13$$\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{8 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 3 {\\,\\bbox[2px, lightgray]{\\color{blue}.}\\,} 2}$ |\n| --- |\n\nNow, сarrying out the long division process as usual, we get:\n\n| $6$$\\!\\!\\!. 4$$13$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{8 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 3 {\\,. \\,} 2}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$7$$8$$5$$2$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$5$$2$$\\phantom{0}$$0$ |\n| --- |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n0.832 \\operatorname{div}  0.13 = 6.4\\,\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186144013 :tutorial/content "We can apply the idea of representing a remainder as a fraction to any problem involving the division of whole numbers. To illustrate, let's write $85 \\div 7$ as a mixed number.\n\nWe start by going through the long division procedure as usual:\n\n| $\\color{blue}1$$\\color{blue}2$$7$$\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{8 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 5}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$7$$1$$5$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$1$$4$$\\color{red}1$ |\n| --- |\n\nFrom the long division, we conclude that\n\n$$\n85\\div 7 = {\\color{blue}{12}} \\,\\textrm{R}\\, {\\color{red}{1}}\n$$\n\nTo write the remainder as a fraction, we divide it by the original divisor ($7$). Therefore, expressed as a mixed number, our result is\n\n$$\n85 \\div 7 = {\\color{blue}12} \\, \\dfrac{\\color{red}1}{7}\\,\n$$" "We can apply the idea of representing a remainder as a fraction to any problem involving the division of whole numbers. To illustrate, let's write $85 \\operatorname{div}  7$ as a mixed number.\n\nWe start by going through the long division procedure as usual:\n\n| $\\color{blue}1$$\\color{blue}2$$7$$\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{8 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 5}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$7$$1$$5$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$1$$4$$\\color{red}1$ |\n| --- |\n\nFrom the long division, we conclude that\n\n$$\n85\\operatorname{div}  7 = {\\color{blue}{12}} \\,\\textrm{R}\\, {\\color{red}{1}}\n$$\n\nTo write the remainder as a fraction, we divide it by the original divisor ($7$). Therefore, expressed as a mixed number, our result is\n\n$$\n85 \\operatorname{div}  7 = {\\color{blue}12} \\, \\dfrac{\\color{red}1}{7}\\,\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186144015 :tutorial/content "**Box models** allow us to solve division problems by breaking them into smaller, easier steps.\n\nIn this approach, we repeatedly subtract easy multiples of the divisor from the dividend. Each multiple we subtract gives a partial quotient, and the sum of all partial quotients is the final quotient.\n\nThis strategy is called **partial quotients,** and the box model helps us visually track the steps.\n\nLet's consider the following division problem:\n\n$$\n36 \\div 2\n$$\n\nThe box model for this division problem looks as follows:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"right\" data-cell-valign=\"top\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"><div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td data-fill=\"#EBF5FB\">$2$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-cell-valign=\"top\"><div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$6$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-cell-valign=\"top\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nWe can solve this division problem by following four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Pick a multiple of $2$ that is easy to compute but is no larger than $36$. Let's pick\n\n$$\n2 \\times {\\color{blue}10} = 20\n$$\n\nWe write ${\\color{blue}10}$ above the box and subtract $2 \\times {\\color{blue}10}$ from $36$ inside the box.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#EBF5FB\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$\\color{blue}10$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"right\" data-cell-valign=\"top\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"><div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td data-fill=\"#EBF5FB\">$2$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-cell-valign=\"top\"><div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"sou